// Generated by tools/build.py — do not edit by hand.
export const CONTENT = {"chapters":[{"chapter":1,"title":"Understanding Marketing","topics":[{"id":"t01-1","order":1,"title":"What marketing is and how exchange works","summary":"Marketing is not just ads or selling. It is the process of discovering what people need and planning goods, services or ideas that satisfy those needs while meeting the organization's goals. At its heart is exchange: a trade that leaves both sides better off and that needs four conditions to happen. Marketers cannot create needs, but they do shape wants.","concepts":["c01-marketing-definition","c01-exchange","c01-needs-wants"]},{"id":"t01-2","order":2,"title":"Choosing customers and promising value","summary":"Not everyone is a potential buyer: a market is people with both the desire and the ability to buy. Because funds are limited, firms pick target markets to focus on. They then compete by delivering customer value and by stating a value proposition that sets their brand apart.","concepts":["c01-market","c01-target-market","c01-customer-value","c01-value-proposition"]},{"id":"t01-3","order":3,"title":"The 4 Ps, the environment and the marketing process","summary":"Marketers control four tools (product, price, place and promotion) but work inside five environmental forces they cannot control. They combine the 4 Ps into a marketing program, which is step 2 of the three-step marketing process: identify consumer needs, manage the marketing mix, realize profits (or objectives).","concepts":["c01-marketing-mix","c01-environmental-forces","c01-marketing-program","c01-marketing-process"]},{"id":"t01-4","order":4,"title":"Business philosophies and customer relationships","summary":"Businesses moved from a production focus, to a sales focus, to the marketing concept, and now to relationship and societal marketing. This topic covers the marketing concept and how firms put it into practice (market orientation), the societal marketing concept and CSR, and how firms build long-term relationships (CRM) and manage the customer experience.","concepts":["c01-business-philosophies","c01-marketing-concept","c01-market-orientation","c01-societal-csr","c01-crm","c01-customer-experience"]},{"id":"t01-5","order":5,"title":"Who buys, what marketing creates, and where it is heading","summary":"Buyers are either ultimate consumers (household use) or organizational buyers (business use or resale). Marketing creates value called utility, and it can be studied for a whole nation (macromarketing) or one organization (micromarketing). The chapter closes with seven evolving practices, ethics and the CMA Code, and the point that everyone markets and marketing touches every career.","concepts":["c01-consumers-org-buyers","c01-utility","c01-macro-micro","c01-evolving-practices","c01-ethics","c01-who-markets"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"c01-marketing-definition","topic":"t01-1","term":"Marketing (definitions)","definition":"Textbook: the process of planning goods, services or ideas to meet consumer needs and organizational objectives, including the conception of products and their pricing, promotion and distribution. CMA: a set of business practices designed to plan for and present an organization's products or services in ways that build effective customer relationships.","explain":"Marketing is much more than ads or selling: it starts with understanding what customers need and ends with offerings that satisfy those needs while meeting the organization's goals. The Chartered Institute of Marketing (UK) calls it the management process responsible for identifying, anticipating and satisfying customer requirements profitably; the AMA-style definition stresses creating, communicating, delivering and exchanging offerings that benefit the organization, its stakeholders and society. Effective marketing requires intimate knowledge of consumers and the marketplace, not just common sense, and most of it is invisible to the average consumer.","example":"When Lululemon faced flat sales, four CEOs in five years, planned layoffs of 1,100 people and skeptical customers, the marketer's job was not simply to buy more ads but to make key decisions (go back to basics, stress key positive attributes, leverage brand equity, change the operating model) to stop the decline.","contrast":"Advertising and personal selling are only parts of promotion, and promotion is only one of the 4 Ps. Marketing covers the whole process: researching needs, conceiving the product, pricing it, distributing it and communicating about it.","trap":"Distractors such as 'good common sense', 'selling unwanted things' or 'advertising' describe the popular misconception. Match source to wording: 'build effective customer relationships' = CMA; 'identifying, anticipating and satisfying customer requirements profitably' = Chartered Institute of Marketing (UK); 'creating, communicating, delivering and exchanging offerings ... society at large' = AMA/textbook style.","mnemonic":"CMA = Canada = Customer relationships. CIM = Identify, antICIpate, satisfy - profitably.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Three definitions of marketing","head":["Source","Key wording"],"rows":[["Canadian Marketing Association (CMA)","A set of business practices designed to plan for and present an organization's products or services in ways that build effective customer relationships"],["Chartered Institute of Marketing (UK)","The management process responsible for identifying, anticipating and satisfying customer requirements profitably"],["Textbook / AMA style","The activity for creating, communicating, delivering and exchanging offerings that benefit the organization, its stakeholders and society at large"]]}},{"id":"c01-exchange","topic":"t01-1","term":"Exchange and the four requirements for marketing","definition":"Exchange is the trade of things of value between a buyer and a seller so that each is better off after the trade. Marketing can occur only when there are (1) two or more parties with unsatisfied needs, (2) a desire and ability on their part to be satisfied, (3) a way for the parties to communicate, and (4) something to exchange.","explain":"Exchange is the heart of marketing: each side gives up something it values less to get something it values more. Money is NOT required; time, effort, blood, a vote or a good feeling can all be things of value. If any one of the four requirements is missing (for example, no way to communicate, or the buyer lacks the ability to pay), no marketing exchange can happen.","example":"After seeing a Canadian Red Cross ad, Amanda donates a pint of blood and goes home feeling she did a good deed. Blood was exchanged for a feeling of satisfaction, and the Red Cross gained blood to serve society, so this is a marketing exchange.","contrast":"An exchange leaves BOTH parties better off; a one-sided transfer does not. The 'desire and ability' requirement mirrors the definition of a market (people with both the desire and the ability to buy a specific product).","trap":"Answers like 'no, because no money changed hands' or 'no, because the Red Cross is a non-profit' are wrong. In 'which is NOT a requirement' questions, watch for fakes such as money as the medium of exchange, a written contract, a profit motive or a physical store.","mnemonic":"2-D-C-S: Two parties, Desire and ability, Communication, Something to exchange.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Four factors required for marketing to occur","items":["Two or more parties (individuals or organizations) with unsatisfied needs","A desire and ability on their part to be satisfied","A way for the parties to communicate","Something to exchange"]}},{"id":"c01-needs-wants","topic":"t01-1","term":"Needs vs wants","definition":"A need occurs when a person feels deprived of basic necessities such as food, water, clothing and shelter. A want is a need that is shaped by a person's knowledge, culture and personality.","explain":"A need is the basic deprivation (thirst); a want is the learned preference for a specific item that satisfies that need (a particular drink brand). Marketers do not create needs, but they do try to shape wants. That is why the answer to 'Who's in charge, the consumer or the marketer?' is both: consumers' needs come first, while marketing influences which products they want.","example":"A Tim Hortons ad on a cold Edmonton morning taps the need for warmth and food, and shapes the want for a steeped tea and a breakfast sandwich in particular.","contrast":"Need = generic deprivation of a basic necessity (hunger, thirst, shelter). Want = a specific product or brand preference shaped by knowledge, culture and personality. 'Preferences' is not the course term for either blank in the classic question.","trap":"In the official fill-in-the-blank, the ad 'appeals to consumers' NEEDS for liquid and attempts to shape consumers' WANTS for the advertised product' - the order is needs; wants. Any option claiming marketers 'create needs' is false.","mnemonic":"Need = Necessity. Want = Which brand.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-market","topic":"t01-2","term":"Market","definition":"A market is people with both the desire and the ability to buy a specific product.","explain":"Desire alone is not enough: someone who wants a product but cannot afford it is not part of the market, and neither is someone who could afford it but has no interest. Desire plus ability defines the market; marketers then choose which part of it to focus on (the target market).","example":"Many Winnipeg teenagers would love a $90,000 electric pickup but cannot pay for it, so they are not in its market. Established tradespeople who want an electric pickup and can afford one are.","contrast":"Market = everyone with the desire AND ability to buy a product. Target market = the specific segment(s) of that market the firm chooses to direct its marketing program toward. Mass market = treating everyone as one undifferentiated group.","trap":"Watch for 'people who want the product' (missing ability) or 'people who can afford it' (missing desire). Both halves must be present. Not being targeted does not remove someone from the market.","mnemonic":"Market = Motivation (desire) + Means (ability).","figure":null},{"id":"c01-target-market","topic":"t01-2","term":"Target market","definition":"One or more specific groups (segments) of existing and potential consumers toward which an organization directs its marketing program.","explain":"With limited funds, a firm gets more from channelling resources to the specific groups most interested in purchasing than from trying to reach everyone. The target market then drives every marketing mix decision: product features, price level, where it is sold and how it is promoted.","example":"Sound and Cinema, which turns ordinary basements into home theatres, advertises only in publications mailed to homeowners in subdivisions where the least expensive home costs $275,000.","contrast":"The target market is chosen from the market (people with desire and ability). A mass-market approach aims one offer at everyone, the opposite of targeting. The target market is the WHO; the marketing mix is WHAT the firm does for them.","trap":"Fake distractors from the official question: 'tangent market', 'market aggregation', 'promotional market'. Cue words: 'directs its marketing program/efforts toward', 'specific group', 'segment'. The definition includes existing AND potential consumers, and it can be more than one group.","mnemonic":"Limited funds? Don't spray - aim.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-customer-value","topic":"t01-2","term":"Customer value","definition":"The unique combination of benefits received by targeted buyers that includes quality, price, convenience, on-time delivery, and both before-sale and after-sale service.","explain":"Customer value is what buyers actually receive. Firms usually win by standing out on one route - the best price, the best product or the best service - while staying acceptable on the others. Consistently delivering value is what builds loyal customers.","example":"Costco competes mainly on best price, Canada Goose on best product, and a Calgary bike shop that offers expert fitting before the sale and free lifetime tune-ups after it competes on best service.","contrast":"Customer value = benefits RECEIVED by targeted buyers. Value proposition = benefits a brand PROMISES to deliver. Customer satisfaction = how well the experience met expectations (an outcome, not the bundle of benefits).","trap":"The official definition lists quality, price, convenience, on-time delivery, and before- and after-sale service; distractors are customer satisfaction, target marketing and benefit segmentation. 'Received' points to customer value; 'promises' points to value proposition.","mnemonic":"Q-P-C-D-S: Quality, Price, Convenience, Delivery on time, Service before and after.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Benefits in customer value (delivered via best price, best product or best service)","items":["Quality","Price","Convenience","On-time delivery","Before-sale and after-sale service"]}},{"id":"c01-value-proposition","topic":"t01-2","term":"Value proposition","definition":"The unique combination of benefits that a brand promises to deliver to consumers; it differentiates the brand from competing brands.","explain":"A value proposition answers 'Why should our target customer buy from us instead of a rival?' It is a promise made in advance, and it should appeal to the target market. The slides show a pizza promoting its Ancient Grains crust and MEC's Buyer's Guide stressing product quality as examples.","example":"Prairie Brew, an invented Edmonton coffee roaster, promises 'beans roasted within 48 hours of arriving at your door' - a benefit its supermarket rivals cannot match.","contrast":"Value proposition = the brand's PROMISE that differentiates it. Customer value = the benefits buyers actually RECEIVE. Marketing mix = the controllable tools (4 Ps) used to keep the promise.","trap":"Cue words 'promises to deliver' and 'differentiates the brand' point to value proposition; 'received by targeted buyers' plus the quality/price/convenience list points to customer value. A question pairing an ad promise with later survey results is testing exactly this order.","mnemonic":"Proposition = Promise.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-marketing-mix","topic":"t01-3","term":"Marketing mix (4 Ps)","definition":"The controllable factors - product, price, promotion and place - that a marketing manager can use to solve a marketing problem; they must be consistently coordinated and appeal to the target market.","explain":"Product is a good, service or idea to satisfy consumers' needs; price is what is exchanged for the product; promotion is a means of communication between seller and buyer; place is a means of getting the product to the consumer. The four elements are controllable and must work together: a premium product with a bargain price and discount-store distribution sends mixed signals.","example":"A new Edmonton lentil-chip brand: product = high-protein chips; price = $4.99 a bag; place = Loblaws and Shoppers Drug Mart; promotion = in-store sampling and Instagram ads.","contrast":"The 4 Ps are CONTROLLABLE; environmental forces (social, economic, technological, competitive, regulatory) are UNCONTROLLABLE. Advertising and selling are only part of promotion, which is one P of four.","trap":"Place means getting the product to consumers (distribution), not just a store address. Price is 'what is exchanged', which need not be money (time, trade-ins, volunteer hours). Any factor involving the economy, competitors, laws, technology or social trends is an environmental force, not a P.","mnemonic":"The 4 Ps are in your Pocket (controllable); the environment is the weather (uncontrollable).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"The 4 Ps","head":["Element","Meaning"],"rows":[["Product","A good, service or idea to satisfy consumers' needs"],["Price","What is exchanged for the product"],["Promotion","A means of communication between the seller and the buyer"],["Place","A means of getting the product to the consumer"]]}},{"id":"c01-environmental-forces","topic":"t01-3","term":"Environmental forces","definition":"The uncontrollable factors - social, economic, technological, competitive and regulatory forces - that affect a marketing decision.","explain":"Marketers cannot change these forces, but they must monitor them and adapt the 4 Ps. In the 'role of marketing' figure, the marketing department works alongside R&D, manufacturing, finance, HR, IT and senior management, and links the organization to customers (relationships), suppliers (partnerships), other organizations (alliances) and owners/shareholders, all surrounded by these five forces.","example":"Growing interest in sustainability (social), a recession (economic), the rise of smartphone shopping (technological), a rival opening next door (competitive) and a new federal privacy rule (regulatory).","contrast":"Environmental forces sit outside the firm's control; the 4 Ps sit inside it. A firm's response to a force (cutting prices during a recession) is a marketing mix decision, while the recession itself is the environmental force.","trap":"There are exactly five: social, economic, technological, competitive, regulatory. Distractors like 'promotional', 'pricing' or 'distribution' forces are 4 Ps in disguise. Competitors are uncontrollable even though they are in your industry.","mnemonic":"SECTR - read it as 'SECToR': Social, Economic, Competitive, Technological, Regulatory.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Five environmental forces (uncontrollable)","items":["Social","Economic","Technological","Competitive","Regulatory"]}},{"id":"c01-marketing-program","topic":"t01-3","term":"Marketing program","definition":"A plan that integrates the marketing mix to provide a good, service or idea to prospective buyers.","explain":"Once consumer needs have been discovered, marketing's next task is to translate that information into products that satisfy them. The marketing program is the plan that does this by combining the right product, price, promotion and place for the target market; it is the heart of step 2 of the marketing process.","example":"After research shows Regina shift workers want healthy late-night food, a caterer designs a program: $12 grain bowls (product, price) sold from a food truck near hospitals overnight (place) and promoted in a nurses' union newsletter (promotion).","contrast":"Marketing mix = the four controllable ingredients. Marketing program = the integrated plan (recipe) that combines them for prospective buyers. Marketing process = the three-step sequence (identify needs, manage the mix, realize profits) in which the program is created in step 2.","trap":"Official question: 'After discovering consumers' needs, marketing's next task is to...' - answer: translate information about consumer needs into products that satisfy them, NOT determine demographics, design prototypes or write a target-market mission statement.","mnemonic":"Mix = ingredients; Program = recipe.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-marketing-process","topic":"t01-3","term":"The marketing process (Figure 1-2)","definition":"A process that focuses on consumer needs: (1) identify consumer needs, (2) manage the marketing mix to meet consumer needs, and (3) realize profits for the company (or objectives for non-profit organizations).","explain":"Step 1 discovers needs by researching potential consumers; step 2 satisfies those needs by designing a marketing program with the right product, price, promotion and place; step 3 is the payoff - profit for a business or other objectives for a non-profit. Information about needs flows from the market (potential consumers) to the organization, and products, services and ideas flow back to the market.","example":"A Calgary meal-kit start-up surveys busy parents (step 1), launches 20-minute kits at $11 a serving sold online and promoted on Instagram (step 2), and becomes profitable in year two (step 3).","contrast":"The process is the overall three-step sequence; the marketing program is the plan built in step 2; the marketing concept is the philosophy behind it (satisfy needs while achieving organizational goals).","trap":"The process STARTS with consumer needs, never with the product or with promotion. For a non-profit, step 3 is realizing objectives, not profit. Information flows from consumers to the organization; offerings flow from the organization to consumers.","mnemonic":"Discover - Satisfy - Realize.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"The marketing process (Figure 1-2)","items":["1. Identify consumer needs (discover needs by researching consumers)","2. Manage the marketing mix to meet consumer needs (design a marketing program: product, price, promotion, place)","3. Realize profits for the company (or objectives for non-profit organizations)"]}},{"id":"c01-business-philosophies","topic":"t01-4","term":"Evolution of business philosophies","definition":"The progression of organizational orientations over time: production orientation, then sales orientation, then marketing orientation (the marketing concept), then relationship marketing / societal marketing orientation.","explain":"In the production era demand exceeded supply, so firms focused on producing goods efficiently. As competition grew, the sales orientation focused on selling and promotion to move output. The marketing orientation then put consumer needs first, and today's relationship/societal orientation adds long-term customer relationships and responsibility to society.","example":"A furniture maker that once sold whatever it built (production), then hired aggressive salespeople to clear its warehouse (sales), then surveyed customers before designing (marketing), and now offers lifetime repairs and sustainably sourced wood (relationship/societal).","contrast":"The sales orientation starts with products the firm has already made and pushes them; the marketing orientation starts with what customers need. The relationship/societal orientation goes further by stressing long-term relationships and society's well-being.","trap":"Order questions: production always comes first and relationship/societal last. Cues: 'demand exceeded supply' or 'efficiency' = production; 'aggressive selling to move inventory' = sales; 'find out what customers want first' = marketing; 'long-term relationships and social responsibility' = relationship/societal.","mnemonic":"Please Sell My Relationship: Production, Sales, Marketing, Relationship/Societal.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Evolution of business philosophies","head":["Orientation","Focus","Conditions"],"rows":[["Production","Producing goods efficiently","Early 1900s; demand exceeded supply"],["Sales","Selling and promotion to move output","Roughly 1930s-1950s; competition grew"],["Marketing (marketing concept)","Discovering and satisfying consumer needs","1950s-60s onward"],["Relationship / societal marketing","Long-term customer relationships plus society's well-being","Today"]]}},{"id":"c01-marketing-concept","topic":"t01-4","term":"Marketing concept","definition":"The idea that an organization should strive to satisfy the needs of consumers while also trying to achieve the organization's goals.","explain":"The marketing concept has two halves: satisfy consumers AND meet organizational goals such as profit. It is a guiding philosophy that starts with customers' needs. Firms that put great effort into implementing the marketing concept are said to have a market orientation.","example":"A Halifax brewery develops a low-alcohol line because customers asked for it, but launches it only after confirming it will meet the brewery's margin targets.","contrast":"Marketing concept = the idea (satisfy needs + achieve organizational goals). Market orientation = the practical implementation (collect, share and use customer and competitor information). Societal marketing concept = satisfy needs in a way that also provides for society's well-being.","trap":"An option that drops either half ('satisfy consumers at any cost' or 'maximize profit') is not the marketing concept. 'Society's well-being' signals the societal marketing concept; 'collecting and sharing information across departments' signals market orientation.","mnemonic":"Happy customers + company goals met = marketing concept.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-market-orientation","topic":"t01-4","term":"Market orientation","definition":"An organization that focuses its efforts on (1) continuously collecting information about customers' needs and competitors' capabilities, (2) sharing this information across departments, and (3) using the information to create customer value.","explain":"Market orientation is the marketing concept put into practice. Its three parts always appear together on exams: collect (about customers AND competitors), share (across departments, not just within marketing) and use (to create customer value).","example":"An invented pharmacy chain, Northway, reviews loyalty-card data and competitors' promotions every week, shares the dashboard with buying, store operations and finance, and uses it to redesign its in-store health clinics.","contrast":"Market orientation is about information flowing through the whole firm to create value; the marketing concept is the underlying idea; macromarketing is a nation's aggregate flow of goods; the societal marketing concept adds society's well-being.","trap":"Official distractors: societal marketing concept, macromarketing, nonprofit orientation, consumerism. Cue: 'continuously collecting information about customers' needs AND competitors' capabilities, sharing it across departments'. Keeping information inside marketing breaks the definition.","mnemonic":"Collect - Share - Use.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Three components of a market orientation","items":["Continuously collect information about customers' needs and competitors' capabilities","Share the information across departments","Use the information to create customer value"]}},{"id":"c01-societal-csr","topic":"t01-4","term":"Societal marketing concept and CSR","definition":"Societal marketing concept: the view that organizations should satisfy the needs of consumers in a way that also provides for society's well-being. Corporate social responsibility (CSR): the idea that organizations are part of a larger society and are accountable to that society for their actions.","explain":"The societal marketing concept extends the marketing concept by adding society as a third beneficiary. CSR is the accountability idea behind it, and the slides list three types of CSR initiatives: (1) sponsoring and/or spearheading community programs, (2) sponsoring and/or getting involved in fundraising for charitable organizations, and (3) a business philosophy that implements socially responsible business practices.","example":"A Calgary grocer sponsors a youth soccer league (community program), runs a checkout campaign for the food bank (charitable fundraising), and switches to compostable packaging across all stores (socially responsible business philosophy).","contrast":"Marketing concept = consumers + organizational goals. Societal marketing concept = consumers + organizational goals + society's well-being. CSR = the organization's accountability to society and the initiatives that express it. Ethics = moral principles guiding individual or group decisions.","trap":"Classify CSR scenarios carefully: running or sponsoring a local program = type 1; raising money for a charity = type 2; changing how the whole business operates (sourcing, packaging, wages, emissions) = type 3. Loyalty programs are CRM, not CSR. 'Society's well-being' = societal marketing concept.","mnemonic":"CSR's 3 Cs: Community programs, Charity fundraising, Company-wide philosophy.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Three types of CSR initiatives","items":["Sponsorship and/or spearheading of community programs","Sponsorship and/or involvement in fundraising initiatives for charitable organizations","A business philosophy that implements socially responsible business practices"]}},{"id":"c01-crm","topic":"t01-4","term":"Customer relationship management (CRM) and relationship marketing","definition":"CRM: the process of building and developing long-term relationships with customers by delivering customer value and satisfaction. Relationship marketing: linking the organization to its individual customers, employees, suppliers and other partners for their mutual long-term benefit.","explain":"Instead of chasing one-off sales, CRM aims to keep customers for years by consistently delivering value and satisfaction; exceptional ongoing service is a classic CRM tactic. Relationship marketing is broader in who it links: not just customers but also employees, suppliers and other partners, all for mutual long-term benefit.","example":"A Saskatoon accounting-software firm assigns each small-business client a dedicated advisor, tracks their needs, and offers free quarterly check-ins to keep them for years.","contrast":"CRM = long-term relationships with CUSTOMERS through value and satisfaction. Relationship marketing = links with customers, employees, suppliers and partners. Customer experience management = customers' internal response to every aspect of the organization. A transactional focus seeks only the single sale.","trap":"Official question: vendors providing 'exceptional, ongoing service to their business customers' hope to benefit from CRM, not the societal marketing concept or 'customer orientation'. Cue words: long-term, ongoing, loyalty, retain. If employees, suppliers and partners are named too, think relationship marketing.","mnemonic":"CRM = Customers Retained for the long run.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-customer-experience","topic":"t01-4","term":"Customer experience management","definition":"Managing the internal response that customers have to all aspects of an organization and its offering.","explain":"Customers form impressions at every touchpoint: website, store, packaging, staff, call centre, delivery and returns. Customer experience management deliberately designs and monitors all of them so the customer's overall internal response is positive and consistent. A great experience supports loyalty, so it works hand in hand with CRM.","example":"An Edmonton furniture retailer maps every touchpoint - app, showroom greeting, delivery-crew manners, follow-up email - and redesigns the delivery-window process after learning customers felt anxious waiting.","contrast":"Customer experience management = customers' internal response to ALL aspects of the organization and offering. CRM = building long-term relationships by delivering value and satisfaction. Experiential marketing = a promotional practice that creates hands-on brand experiences such as pop-ups.","trap":"Cue words: 'internal response', 'every touchpoint', 'all aspects of the organization'. Do not confuse it with experiential marketing just because both contain 'experience'. If the stem stresses keeping customers long-term through value and satisfaction, the answer is CRM.","mnemonic":"Experience = how it FEELS at every touchpoint.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-consumers-org-buyers","topic":"t01-5","term":"Ultimate consumers vs organizational buyers","definition":"Ultimate consumers are the people who use the goods and services purchased for a household. Organizational buyers are manufacturers, wholesalers, retailers and government agencies that buy goods and services for their own use or for resale.","explain":"The same product can be bought by either type of buyer; what decides the category is the PURPOSE of the purchase. Buying for personal or household use makes you an ultimate consumer; buying to run an organization or to resell makes you an organizational buyer.","example":"A parent buying milk at Sobeys for the family is an ultimate consumer; a store owner buying hand-painted slate signs to sell in her store is an organizational buyer.","contrast":"Ultimate consumer = household use. Organizational buyer = organizational use or resale. A professional buying something for a personal hobby is still an ultimate consumer, whatever their job title.","trap":"Distractors give buyers job titles (programmer, botanist, dentist, baseball player) to tempt you, but they are buying for household use. Look for 'to resell', 'for the store', 'for the clinic' or a government agency.","mnemonic":"Who uses it? Home = ultimate consumer; organization or resale = organizational buyer.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-utility","topic":"t01-5","term":"Utility","definition":"The benefits or customer value received by users of the product. The four types are form, place, time and possession utility.","explain":"Form utility is the production of the good or service itself; place utility is having the offering available where consumers need it; time utility is having it available when they need it; possession utility is getting the product to consumers so they can use it, for example by offering credit or financing. Marketing creates place, time and possession utility and influences form utility.","example":"A bakery turning flour into croissants (form), a coffee kiosk beside airport gates (place), a 24-hour pharmacy (time) and a furniture store's interest-free financing (possession).","contrast":"Place = WHERE; time = WHEN; possession = being able to acquire and use it (credit, payment options); form = making the product itself. Marketing creates place, time and possession directly but only influences form, which production creates.","trap":"Questions often ask which utility marketing does NOT create directly: form. Do not confuse place (location/availability) with possession (ability to acquire and use, e.g., financing). 'Open 24/7' or 'available at 3 a.m.' = time.","mnemonic":"Form-Place-Time-Possession = What, Where, When, Whose.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Four types of utility","head":["Utility","Meaning","Marketing's role"],"rows":[["Form","Production of the good or service","Influences it"],["Place","Having the offering available where consumers need it","Creates it"],["Time","Having the offering available when consumers need it","Creates it"],["Possession","Getting the product to consumers so they can use it (e.g., credit, delivery)","Creates it"]]}},{"id":"c01-macro-micro","topic":"t01-5","term":"Macromarketing vs micromarketing","definition":"Macromarketing is the aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services to benefit society. Micromarketing is how an individual organization directs its marketing activities and allocates its resources to benefit its customers.","explain":"The two terms differ in level of analysis. Macromarketing looks at the whole system - how all the goods and services in a country flow to consumers and benefit society. Micromarketing looks inside one firm or non-profit: its target markets, marketing mix and resource choices.","example":"A study of how Canada's entire food distribution system moves produce from farms to every province is macromarketing; Maple Leaf Foods deciding how to split its ad budget among its brands is micromarketing.","contrast":"Macro = nation, society, aggregate flow. Micro = one organization, its customers, its resources. Neither refers to company size: a giant firm's own national plan is still micromarketing.","trap":"Do not equate 'macro' with 'big company' or 'national advertising'. Cue words: 'aggregate', 'nation's', 'benefit society' = macro; 'an individual organization', 'allocates its resources' = micro.","mnemonic":"Macro = the whole Map; Micro = My company.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-evolving-practices","topic":"t01-5","term":"New and evolving marketing practices","definition":"Seven evolving areas highlighted in the chapter: (1) digital marketing, including content, mobile and social media marketing; (2) augmented reality; (3) experiential marketing; (4) influencer marketing; (5) partnership marketing; (6) metrics and analytics; and (7) new marketing regulations and ethical considerations.","explain":"Marketing keeps changing with technology and society. Digital tools let brands publish content and reach people on phones and social platforms; augmented reality overlays digital images on the real world; experiential marketing lets consumers interact directly with a brand; influencer marketing uses trusted personalities; partnership marketing forms formal associations between brands; metrics and analytics measure results; and new regulations and ethics shape what is acceptable.","example":"An Ottawa paint brand lets shoppers preview colours on their own walls through a phone app (augmented reality), pays a home-reno creator for videos (influencer marketing), and tracks which video drives the most app downloads (metrics and analytics).","contrast":"Content, mobile and social media marketing are sub-types INSIDE digital marketing, not separate items in the list of seven. Experiential = consumers interact with the brand hands-on; influencer = a personality promotes it; partnership = two brands formally associate.","trap":"'Which is NOT one of the new and evolving practices?' Distractors are old philosophies such as production or sales orientation or mass marketing. The seventh item is new regulations AND ethical considerations.","mnemonic":"D-A-E-I-P-M-R: 'Digital Apps Excite Influencers; Partners Measure Rules.'","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Seven new and evolving marketing practices","items":["Digital marketing (content marketing, mobile marketing, social media marketing)","Augmented reality","Experiential marketing","Influencer marketing","Partnership marketing","Metrics and analytics","New marketing regulations and ethical considerations"]}},{"id":"c01-ethics","topic":"t01-5","term":"Ethics and the CMA Code","definition":"Ethics are the moral principles and values that govern the actions and decisions of an individual or group. In Canada, the Canadian Marketing Association publishes the Canadian Marketing Code of Ethics and Standards.","explain":"Many companies, industries and professional associations publish guidelines and codes of ethics so that members know what conduct is expected even where the law is silent. 'New marketing regulations and ethical considerations' is one of the seven evolving practices, reflecting issues such as privacy, data use and honest advertising.","example":"A Calgary agency that follows the CMA Code refuses a client's request to hide automatic-renewal fees in fine print, even though the client argues no specific law forbids it.","contrast":"Ethics = moral principles about what is right. Laws/regulations = government rules about what is legal (a regulatory environmental force). CSR = the organization's accountability to society as a whole. An action can be legal yet unethical.","trap":"Codes of ethics come from companies, industries AND professional associations, not only government. 'Any legal practice is ethical' is false. The Canadian code comes from the CMA (Canadian Marketing Association), not the UK's Chartered Institute of Marketing.","mnemonic":"Legal is the floor; ethical is the standard.","figure":null},{"id":"c01-who-markets","topic":"t01-5","term":"Who markets, and marketing's reach (careers)","definition":"Every organization markets - for-profit firms, non-profits and governments, as well as individuals, places and causes - and what is marketed can be goods, services or ideas. Marketing affects all individuals, all organizations, all industries and all countries.","explain":"Any organization that needs people to engage in exchanges with it, or simply to think or behave in a certain way, is marketing - the Canadian Red Cross seeking blood donors and the Ministry of Transportation promoting road safety are examples. Because marketing touches everyone, understanding it makes us better customers, more informed citizens and better career planners.","example":"The City of Calgary's campaign urging residents to put food scraps in their green carts markets an idea, even though nothing is sold.","contrast":"Marketing is not limited to large companies with national ad budgets, or to physical goods: services (a dental clinic), ideas (don't drive distracted), people (a candidate) and places (a tourist town) are marketed too.","trap":"Options like 'only those that can afford national advertising', 'exclusively Fortune 1000 companies' or 'very large nonprofits' are wrong: every organization markets. A government campaign is still one organization's marketing, not macromarketing.","mnemonic":"Everyone markets everything: goods, services, ideas.","figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"a01-01","type":"order","concept":"c01-marketing-process","title":"Order the marketing process","prompt":"Put the three steps of the marketing process (Figure 1-2) in the correct order.","items":["Identify consumer needs","Manage the marketing mix to meet consumer needs","Realize profits for the company (or objectives for a non-profit)"],"explanation":"The process focuses on consumer needs: first discover them through research, then satisfy them with a marketing program combining the right product, price, promotion and place, and finally realize profits (or, for non-profits, other objectives)."},{"id":"a01-02","type":"order","concept":"c01-business-philosophies","title":"Order the business philosophies","prompt":"Put the business philosophies in the order in which they evolved, from earliest to today.","items":["Production orientation","Sales orientation","Marketing orientation (marketing concept)","Relationship / societal marketing orientation"],"explanation":"Production came first (demand exceeded supply, so efficiency mattered most); growing competition led to the sales orientation (push output); the marketing orientation then put consumer needs first; today firms add long-term relationships and society's well-being."},{"id":"a01-03","type":"sort","concept":"c01-environmental-forces","title":"Controllable or uncontrollable?","prompt":"Drag each factor into the correct bucket: a controllable marketing mix decision or an uncontrollable environmental force.","buckets":["Controllable: marketing mix (4 Ps)","Uncontrollable: environmental force"],"items":[{"text":"Setting a 20% back-to-school discount","bucket":0},{"text":"A recession cutting household spending","bucket":1},{"text":"Choosing to sell through Canadian Tire stores","bucket":0},{"text":"A new federal privacy law on customer data","bucket":1},{"text":"Launching an Instagram ad campaign","bucket":0},{"text":"A rival opening a store across the street","bucket":1},{"text":"Adding a gluten-free version of a snack","bucket":0},{"text":"Rapid growth in smartphone shopping","bucket":1},{"text":"Growing consumer concern about sustainability","bucket":1},{"text":"Accepting trade-ins as part payment","bucket":0}],"explanation":"The 4 Ps (product, price, place, promotion) are decisions the marketer controls - including accepting trade-ins, which is a price decision. The economy, laws, competitors, technology and social trends are the five environmental forces: marketers cannot control them, only monitor them and respond with the 4 Ps."},{"id":"a01-04","type":"match","concept":"c01-utility","title":"Match the utility","prompt":"Match each type of utility with its example.","pairs":[{"left":"Form utility","right":"A bakery turns flour and butter into fresh croissants"},{"left":"Place utility","right":"A coffee kiosk right beside the airport departure gates"},{"left":"Time utility","right":"A pharmacy that stays open 24 hours a day"},{"left":"Possession utility","right":"A furniture store offering 24 months of interest-free financing"}],"explanation":"Form = producing the good itself (marketing only influences it); place = available where needed; time = available when needed; possession = helping consumers acquire and use it, for example through credit. Marketing directly creates place, time and possession utility."},{"id":"a01-05","type":"sort","concept":"c01-societal-csr","title":"Classify the CSR initiative","prompt":"Sort each initiative into the type of corporate social responsibility (CSR) initiative it represents.","buckets":["Community programs","Charitable fundraising","Socially responsible business philosophy"],"items":[{"text":"An insurer runs free swimming lessons for inner-city kids","bucket":0},{"text":"A grocer organizes and sponsors a neighbourhood clean-up day","bucket":0},{"text":"A coffee chain sponsors a youth soccer league","bucket":0},{"text":"A hardware chain matches checkout donations to a cancer charity","bucket":1},{"text":"Bank staff run a charity walk for a children's hospital foundation","bucket":1},{"text":"A retailer gives 1% of sales to a food bank's fundraising drive","bucket":1},{"text":"An apparel maker pays all its suppliers a living wage","bucket":2},{"text":"A brewery switches every product to recyclable packaging","bucket":2},{"text":"A tech firm ties executive bonuses to emissions cuts","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Type 1 sponsors or spearheads community programs; type 2 sponsors or joins fundraising for charitable organizations; type 3 builds social responsibility into how the whole business operates (sourcing, packaging, pay, emissions)."},{"id":"a01-06","type":"match","concept":"c01-evolving-practices","title":"Match the evolving practice","prompt":"Match each of the seven new and evolving marketing practices with its example.","pairs":[{"left":"Digital marketing","right":"How-to blog videos, app notifications and social media posts"},{"left":"Augmented reality","right":"A phone app that places a life-sized 3D sofa in your real living room"},{"left":"Experiential marketing","right":"A festival pop-up where visitors test jackets in a -20°C cold room"},{"left":"Influencer marketing","right":"Paying a fitness creator with 400,000 followers to feature a water bottle"},{"left":"Partnership marketing","right":"A coffee chain and a bookseller co-brand cafés inside bookstores"},{"left":"Metrics and analytics","right":"Tracking which ad platform actually drives sales"},{"left":"Regulations and ethical considerations","right":"Following the CMA Canadian Marketing Code of Ethics and Standards"}],"explanation":"These are the seven new and evolving practices from Chapter 1. Content, mobile and social media marketing are grouped under digital marketing, and the seventh item covers both new regulations and ethical considerations."}],"questions":[{"id":"q01-001","concept":"c01-marketing-definition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Effective marketing requires:","options":["good common sense","all departments within an organization to work together","intimate knowledge and understanding of consumers and the marketplace","selling unwanted things","taking the customer's money"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The chapter stresses that effective marketing requires intimate knowledge and understanding of consumers and the marketplace, which goes well beyond common sense. Departments working together helps (it is part of a market orientation), but it is not the stated requirement, and 'common sense' is exactly the misconception the chapter corrects.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-002","concept":"c01-marketing-definition","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The Chartered Institute of Marketing (UK) defines marketing as 'the management process responsible for identifying, anticipating and satisfying customer requirements _____.'","options":["efficiently","profitably","through relationships","ethically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The CIM definition ends with 'profitably': marketing must identify, anticipate and satisfy customer requirements while making money for the organization. Relationships belong to the Canadian Marketing Association definition (practices that 'build effective customer relationships'), not the CIM one.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-003","concept":"c01-marketing-definition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pairing of a marketing definition and its source is correct?","options":["Chartered Institute of Marketing (UK): 'the management process responsible for identifying, anticipating and satisfying customer requirements profitably'","Canadian Marketing Association: 'the management process responsible for identifying, anticipating and satisfying customer requirements profitably'","Chartered Institute of Marketing (UK): 'a set of business practices ... in ways that build effective customer relationships'","Canadian Marketing Association: 'the aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services to benefit society'"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The CIM (UK) defines marketing as the management process responsible for identifying, anticipating and satisfying customer requirements profitably. The CMA definition is the one about business practices that 'build effective customer relationships', and 'aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services' defines macromarketing, not marketing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-004","concept":"c01-marketing-definition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"The new marketing manager at Northridge Outfitters, a Calgary jacket maker, tells the CEO: 'My job isn't just running ads. I research what hikers need, help shape the jackets, set prices, choose where they're sold and decide how we talk about them.' Which view of marketing is the manager describing?","options":["The popular view: a set of persuasive advertising and selling techniques used to move existing inventory","Corporate social responsibility: sponsoring community programs and fundraising for charitable organizations","Macromarketing: the aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services to benefit society as a whole","The textbook view: planning offerings and their pricing, promotion and distribution to meet needs and objectives"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The manager covers the full textbook definition: discovering needs, then conceiving the product and its price, promotion and distribution to meet consumer needs and organizational objectives. The 'ads and selling' view is the misconception the chapter corrects; promotion is only one of the four Ps.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-005","concept":"c01-marketing-definition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about marketing is NOT consistent with Chapter 1?","options":["Many marketing practices and theories are invisible to the average consumer","Marketing aims to benefit the organization, its stakeholders and society","Effective marketing can be done well using common sense alone","Marketing is often confused with advertising or selling techniques"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The chapter states that effective marketing requires intimate knowledge and understanding of consumers and the marketplace, so 'common sense alone' is wrong. The other statements all appear in the chapter: marketing is commonly mistaken for ads or selling, much of it is invisible to consumers, and the AMA-style definition stresses benefits to the organization, stakeholders and society.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-006","concept":"c01-marketing-definition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A well-known athletic-apparel brand has had four CEOs in five years, plans to lay off 1,100 people, and faces skeptical customers, changing preferences and unionization demands while sales stay flat. According to the intro lecture, what is the marketer's role?","options":["Increase the advertising budget, because marketing's job is mainly running ads and selling harder","Wait for environmental forces to improve, because marketers cannot influence performance","Leave the problem to finance and HR, since flat sales and layoffs are not marketing issues","Make key decisions to stop the decline, such as going back to basics and leveraging brand equity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In the Lululemon case, the marketer's role is to make key decisions to stop the business's declining performance: go back to basics, stress key positive attributes, leverage brand equity, or change the operating model. Simply buying more ads reflects the narrow 'marketing = advertising' misconception.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-007","concept":"c01-exchange","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"The Canadian Red Cross created a series of advertisements encouraging viewers to donate blood. After viewing the advertisement, Amanda went to the local Red Cross office and donated a pint of blood. Afterwards, Amanda returned home feeling satisfied that she had performed a good deed. Was this a marketing exchange?","options":["No, because the Red Cross is a non-profit organization","Yes, because the Red Cross ran an advertisement","Yes, because the donated blood was exchanged for a feeling of satisfaction","No, because no money was exchanged","No, because the Red Cross did not provide Amanda with a product"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Exchange is the trade of things of value between buyer and seller so that each is better off. Amanda gave blood and received a feeling of satisfaction; the Red Cross received blood to serve society, so an exchange occurred. Money is not required and non-profits market too, so the 'no money' and 'non-profit' options fail; running an ad alone does not make something an exchange.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-008","concept":"c01-exchange","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The trade of things of value between a buyer and a seller so that each is better off after the trade is called:","options":["exchange","customer value","utility","a value proposition"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is the definition of exchange, the core of marketing. Utility is the benefit users get from a product and customer value is the bundle of benefits buyers receive; both can result from an exchange, but neither is the trade itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-009","concept":"c01-exchange","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four factors required for marketing to occur?","options":["Something to exchange","A way for the parties to communicate","Money to serve as the medium of exchange","Two or more parties with unsatisfied needs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The four factors are: two or more parties with unsatisfied needs, a desire and ability to be satisfied, a way to communicate, and something to exchange. Money is not required; 'something to exchange' can be time, effort, blood or even a vote.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-010","concept":"c01-exchange","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kestrel Kayaks, a small Kelowna builder, makes a touring kayak that many Prairie paddlers would gladly buy and can afford. But Kestrel has no website, no retailers and no advertising, so paddlers have never heard of it. Which requirement for marketing to occur is missing?","options":["Two or more parties with unsatisfied needs","A way for the parties to communicate","A desire and ability to be satisfied","Something to exchange"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both sides have needs, the paddlers have the desire and ability to buy, and there is a kayak and money to trade, but the parties cannot communicate, so no exchange can happen. 'Desire and ability' is present because the paddlers want the kayak and can afford it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-011","concept":"c01-exchange","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"A group of Grade 10 students in Red Deer fall in love with a $90,000 electric pickup at an auto show. The dealer is eager to sell, its ads reach the students daily, and the truck is in stock. Why will a marketing exchange still not occur?","options":["Only one of the two parties in the situation has an unsatisfied need","The dealer has nothing of value to offer the students in exchange","They lack the ability to pay, so the 'desire and ability' requirement fails","There is no way for the dealer and the students to communicate with each other"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One of the four requirements is a desire AND ability to be satisfied; the students have the desire but not the ability to pay $90,000. Communication is clearly present because the dealer's ads reach them daily, and there is a truck to exchange.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-012","concept":"c01-exchange","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"The second requirement for marketing to occur ('a desire and ability to be satisfied') mirrors the definition of a market, which also requires people to have both the desire and the ability to buy.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. A market is people with both the desire and the ability to buy a specific product, and the second requirement for marketing is a desire and ability on the parties' part to be satisfied. Desire without ability (or ability without desire) fails both tests.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-013","concept":"c01-exchange","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which situation would NOT result in a marketing exchange?","options":["A volunteer gives up a Saturday at a food bank and leaves feeling proud of helping out","A Yukon carver's bowls are wanted by collectors who can afford them, but they have no way to reach him","A student trades two hours of calculus tutoring for a classmate's used marketing textbook","Residents vote for a city council candidate who promises to build the new park they want"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The carver's situation lacks a way for the parties to communicate, one of the four requirements, so no exchange can happen. The other three are genuine marketing exchanges even though no money changes hands: tutoring for a book, time for a feeling of pride, and a vote for a promised park.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-014","concept":"c01-needs-wants","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A television advertisement shows several teenagers searching through the refrigerator for something to quench their thirst. The refrigerator offers the youngsters many alternatives - soft drinks, fruit drinks, sport drinks and bottles of Sunny Delight drink. The ad, which shows the teens happily selecting Sunny Delight over all the other product offerings, appeals to the consumers' _____ for liquid and attempts to shape consumers' _____ for the advertised product.","options":["wants; needs","wants; preferences","preferences; needs","needs; wants","needs; preferences"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A need is felt deprivation of a basic necessity such as liquid (thirst); a want is a need shaped by knowledge, culture and personality into a preference for a specific item (Sunny Delight). The ad appeals to the need and shapes the want. 'Wants; needs' reverses them, and marketers do not create needs.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-015","concept":"c01-needs-wants","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In marketing terms, a want is best described as:","options":["a need that is shaped by a person's knowledge, culture and personality","a feeling of deprivation of basic necessities such as food, water and shelter","the unique combination of benefits a brand promises to deliver","the desire and the ability to buy a specific product"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A want is a need shaped by knowledge, culture and personality into a preference for a specific item. Feeling deprived of basic necessities is the definition of a need, the most tempting distractor; desire plus ability describes a market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-016","concept":"c01-needs-wants","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Montreal agency's creative brief says: 'Our campaign will make Canadians NEED our client's new premium sparkling water.' Using Chapter 1's terms, how should the brief be corrected?","options":["The campaign can create both needs and wants, provided its methods are ethical","The campaign should create a market for the product, which is the same as creating a need","The campaign can create a need, but only if it is aimed at a specific target market","The campaign can shape consumers' want for the brand; the underlying need (thirst) already exists"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Marketers do not create needs; a need is felt deprivation of a basic necessity such as liquid, which exists before any ad. What marketing can do is shape the want, the learned preference for a specific product. Targeting or ethics do not change this rule.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-017","concept":"c01-needs-wants","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"On a scorching Calgary Stampede afternoon, Rahim is badly thirsty. He walks past a free water fountain and lines up to buy a $7 Okanagan peach lemonade he saw on Instagram. Which element of this situation is a WANT?","options":["His preference for that specific peach lemonade","The Instagram post that promoted the lemonade","The $7 price charged for the lemonade","The thirst he feels in the afternoon heat"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rahim's thirst is the need; his learned preference for a specific product (that peach lemonade rather than free water) is the want. The price and the Instagram post are marketing mix elements (price and promotion), not needs or wants.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-018","concept":"c01-needs-wants","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"The instructor asks: 'Who's in charge - the consumer or the marketer?' Which answer best reflects Chapter 1?","options":["The consumer: marketers only react and have no influence on what people want","Neither: environmental forces alone decide what consumers will buy","The marketer: marketing creates the needs that consumers then try to satisfy","Both: consumers' needs come first, but marketers shape their wants for specific products"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The chapter's answer is both. Marketing starts by discovering consumer needs, which marketers cannot create, but it then shapes wants by influencing which products people prefer. 'The marketer creates needs' is the classic wrong belief.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-019","concept":"c01-needs-wants","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Clearbrook Springs, a Winnipeg bottled-water start-up, knows that everyone needs water. It wants to grow sales of its own brand. Based on the need-want distinction, where should it focus its marketing effort?","options":["Treating all Canadians as one group, since everyone shares the need for water","Avoiding promotion, since the need for water already exists naturally","Shaping consumers' wants so they prefer Clearbrook as the way to satisfy thirst","Creating a brand-new need for hydration that consumers did not have before"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because the need (thirst) already exists and cannot be created by marketers, the brand's job is to shape the want: make Clearbrook the specific product people prefer. 'Creating a need' is impossible by the chapter's logic, and skipping promotion or targeting everyone wastes the chance to influence wants.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-020","concept":"c01-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"People with both the desire and the ability to buy a specific product make up a:","options":["target market","market","market segment","mass market"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A market is defined by two conditions: desire and ability to buy a specific product. A target market is narrower - the specific group(s) of that market the firm chooses to direct its marketing program toward.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-021","concept":"c01-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Peaks Adventures sells $2,400 guided heli-skiing trips. Which group belongs to the MARKET for these trips?","options":["Wealthy retirees who have no interest in skiing of any kind","Everyone who lives within driving distance of the Rockies","Experienced skiers who want a heli-skiing trip and can afford $2,400","University students who dream of heli-skiing but cannot afford it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only the experienced skiers have both the desire and the ability to buy. The students have desire without ability and the retirees have ability without desire, so neither group is part of the market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-022","concept":"c01-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a market from a target market?","options":["A market includes only a firm's existing customers; a target market includes only its potential customers","A market is everyone with the desire and ability to buy; a target market is the segment(s) the firm chooses to aim its program at","A market and a target market become the same thing once a product has been launched and promoted","A market is the segment a firm chooses to pursue; a target market is everyone who could possibly buy the product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The market is the full pool of people with desire and ability; the target market is the part of it the firm deliberately aims its marketing program at. Calling the market the chosen segment and the target market everyone who could buy reverses the terms, and target markets include both existing and potential customers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-023","concept":"c01-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Thousands of young followers 'like' a Montreal jeweller's $8,000 watches on Instagram, yet very few ever buy one. Using the chapter's definition, why are most of these followers NOT part of the jeweller's market?","options":["They have the desire but lack the ability to buy the product","They have the ability but lack the desire to buy the product","They have not yet been selected as the jeweller's target market","They are organizational buyers rather than ultimate consumers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A market requires both desire and ability to buy. The likes show desire, but most young followers cannot afford an $8,000 watch. Not being chosen as a target market does not remove anyone from the market; the target market is a subset chosen afterward.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-024","concept":"c01-market","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A person who could easily afford a product but has no interest in buying it is still part of that product's market.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Corrected: a market consists of people with BOTH the desire and the ability to buy a specific product, so someone with ability but no desire is not part of the market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-025","concept":"c01-target-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Sound and Cinema is a company that will turn an ordinary basement into a home theatre. It advertises in publications that are mailed to homeowners in subdivisions in which the least expensive home costs $275,000. These homeowners are the _____ for Sound and Cinema.","options":["mass market","tangent market","market aggregation","target market","promotional market"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A target market is one or more specific groups of existing and potential consumers toward which an organization directs its marketing program. Sound and Cinema deliberately aims its ads at affluent homeowners rather than everyone, which is the opposite of a mass-market approach.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-026","concept":"c01-target-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Brookside Golf Club in Edmonton aims its new junior-league campaign at two groups: members' children and nearby families who have never golfed but attended its free summer clinic. A manager objects that people who have never bought from Brookside cannot be part of a target market. Is the manager right?","options":["Yes - potential consumers can belong to the market but never to a target market","Yes - a target market includes only existing customers who have already purchased","No - a target market automatically includes everyone in Edmonton who can afford golf","No - a target market includes both existing and potential consumers the program is aimed at"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The definition of a target market explicitly covers existing AND potential consumers toward which the organization directs its marketing program, so the clinic families qualify. A target market is still a specific chosen group, not everyone with the ability to buy.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-027","concept":"c01-target-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the chapter recommend that a firm with limited funds focus on a target market instead of trying to appeal to everyone?","options":["Choosing a target market removes the need to design a marketing mix","Targeting everyone always produces higher customer value per dollar spent","A target market includes only existing customers, who are cheaper to reach","Resources go further when focused on the groups most interested in buying"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The slide's rationale: with limited funds, it is better to channel resources to the specific groups most interested in purchasing than to target everyone. A target market still needs a tailored marketing mix, and it includes potential as well as existing customers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-028","concept":"c01-target-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"FreshFit Meals, an Edmonton start-up, has a $15,000 launch budget for its high-protein meal-prep service. Research shows that downtown gym members aged 20 to 35 are by far the most interested buyers. What should FreshFit do?","options":["Spread the budget evenly across every Edmonton household","Direct its marketing program at downtown gym members aged 20 to 35","Skip research and promote to whoever responds to TV ads","Wait until it can afford a national campaign before marketing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With limited funds, FreshFit should channel its resources toward the group most interested in buying, which is exactly what selecting a target market means. Spreading $15,000 across every household is a mass-market approach that dilutes the budget.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-029","concept":"c01-target-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about target markets is NOT correct?","options":["A target market can include both existing and potential customers","An organization may direct its program at more than one segment","A target market must include every person who is able to buy the product","The marketing mix should be designed to appeal to the target market"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A target market is a chosen subset - one or more specific groups - not everyone able to buy. The definition explicitly includes existing and potential consumers and allows 'one or more' segments, and the 4 Ps must appeal to it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-030","concept":"c01-target-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Glacier Gear sells winter boots across Canada. Plan A: one generic ad sent to all Canadians. Plan B: separate programs for urban commuters in Toronto and backcountry hunters in northern Alberta. Which statement is correct?","options":["Plan B uses target markets; Plan A treats everyone as one mass market","Both plans use target markets because both reach existing customers","Neither plan uses target markets because both sell the same boots","Plan A uses a target market; Plan B treats everyone as one mass market"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plan B directs distinct marketing programs at specific groups, which is targeting. Plan A aims one message at everyone, the mass-market approach. Reaching existing customers does not make something targeting; choosing specific groups does.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-031","concept":"c01-customer-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The unique combination of benefits received by targeted buyers that include quality, price, convenience, on-time delivery, and both before-sale and after-sale service is called:","options":["target marketing","benefit segmentation","customer value","customer satisfaction","product dissonance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"This is the textbook definition of customer value: the benefits targeted buyers receive, including quality, price, convenience, on-time delivery and before- and after-sale service. Customer satisfaction is a related outcome (how well expectations were met), not the bundle of benefits itself.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-032","concept":"c01-customer-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rocky Ridge Hardware in Cochrane cannot match big-box prices, so it offers free same-day delivery, expert project advice before you buy, and free tool repairs after you buy. Which of the three ways to deliver customer value is Rocky Ridge emphasizing?","options":["Best product","Best promotion","Best service","Best price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Firms typically deliver customer value through the best price, the best product or the best service. Rocky Ridge explicitly gives up on price and competes on before-sale and after-sale service. 'Best promotion' is not one of the three routes.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-033","concept":"c01-customer-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes customer value from a value proposition?","options":["Customer value is the benefits buyers actually receive; a value proposition is the benefits a brand promises to deliver","Customer value applies to non-profits; a value proposition applies only to for-profit firms","Customer value is a promise made in advertising; a value proposition is what buyers actually experience","Customer value refers only to low price; a value proposition refers only to product quality"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Customer value is defined by benefits 'received by targeted buyers', while a value proposition is the unique combination of benefits a brand 'promises to deliver'. Calling customer value the advertised promise and the value proposition what buyers actually experience simply reverses the two terms.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-034","concept":"c01-customer-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT part of the textbook definition of customer value?","options":["Before-sale and after-sale service","Market share","Convenience","On-time delivery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Customer value includes quality, price, convenience, on-time delivery, and before- and after-sale service. Market share measures the firm's performance; it is not a benefit that buyers receive.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-035","concept":"c01-customer-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two Calgary grocers sell identical products at identical prices. Grocer A also offers 30-minute curbside pickup and no-questions-asked returns. Why does Grocer A likely deliver greater customer value?","options":["Customer value depends only on price, so Grocer A must secretly be charging less overall","Customer value depends on how much a firm spends on advertising its value promise","Customer value is the same as satisfaction, which depends only on the product itself","Customer value includes benefits like convenience and after-sale service, not just price and quality"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Because customer value is a unique combination of benefits that includes convenience and after-sale service, Grocer A adds value even with equal prices and products. Advertising spending is not part of the definition; an advertised promise is a value proposition, not value received.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-036","concept":"c01-value-proposition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The unique combination of benefits that a brand promises to deliver to consumers, setting it apart from competing brands, is its:","options":["customer value","marketing program","value proposition","target market"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A value proposition is the brand's promise of a unique combination of benefits, and it differentiates the brand. Customer value is the benefits buyers actually receive, so 'promises' is the cue that separates the two.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-037","concept":"c01-value-proposition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Crust's box says: 'Ancient-grain crust, stone-baked, ready in nine minutes - the only frozen pizza that tastes like your favourite pizzeria.' In chapter terms, what is this statement communicating?","options":["Its market orientation","Its value proposition","Its customer relationship management","Its target market"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The statement promises a unique combination of benefits (ancient-grain crust, pizzeria taste, nine-minute convenience) that differentiates the brand - a value proposition, much like the slide's Ancient Grains Crust pizza example. It does not describe who the customers are (target market).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-038","concept":"c01-value-proposition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Brightline Telecom's ads promise 'No contracts, no surprise fees, 24/7 human support.' Six months later, a survey shows customers really did get reliable service, fair bills and fast help. The ad and the survey results illustrate, respectively:","options":["value proposition; customer value","target market; marketing mix","customer value; value proposition","marketing concept; market orientation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ad is a promise of benefits that differentiates the brand (value proposition); the survey measures the benefits customers actually received (customer value). Reversing the order is the classic trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-039","concept":"c01-value-proposition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A new Halifax coffee roaster discovers that shoppers cannot tell its beans apart from three competitors' beans. What should it develop to fix this problem?","options":["A production orientation focused on roasting more beans more cheaply","A macromarketing plan for Canada's national coffee supply chain","A larger market that includes people unable to afford specialty coffee","A clear value proposition promising a unique combination of benefits"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A value proposition differentiates a brand by promising a unique combination of benefits, which is exactly what the roaster lacks. People who cannot afford the product are not part of the market by definition, and a production focus ignores the differentiation problem.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-040","concept":"c01-value-proposition","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about a value proposition is NOT accurate?","options":["It should appeal to the brand's target market","It describes the benefits customers already received","It promises a unique combination of benefits","It helps differentiate a brand from competing brands"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A value proposition is a promise of benefits; benefits actually received after purchase describe customer value. Differentiation and appeal to the target market are exactly what a value proposition is for.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-041","concept":"c01-marketing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the marketing mix, which element is defined as 'a means of communication between the seller and the buyer'?","options":["Promotion","Price","Product","Place"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Promotion is the means of communication between seller and buyer. Place is the means of getting the product to the consumer, price is what is exchanged for the product, and product is the good, service or idea that satisfies needs.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-042","concept":"c01-marketing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Grove Cider decides to sell its new craft cider only at its own farm shop and in select independent liquor stores, rather than in every grocery chain. Which marketing mix decision is this?","options":["Product","Promotion","Price","Place"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Place is the means of getting the product to the consumer, so choosing which outlets carry the cider is a place decision. Promotion would be how the cidery communicates with buyers, such as ads or tastings.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-043","concept":"c01-marketing-mix","also":["c01-environmental-forces"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is a controllable marketing mix decision rather than an environmental force?","options":["A rival opening a store across the street","A recession that reduces household spending","Cutting a streaming subscription price from $15 to $12 per month","A new federal law limiting food advertising to children"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Price is one of the 4 Ps, which marketers control. A recession (economic), a new law (regulatory) and a rival's new store (competitive) are environmental forces, which the firm cannot control and can only respond to.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-044","concept":"c01-marketing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"A sporting-goods marketing manager lists decisions under her control. Which item does NOT belong on her list of marketing mix decisions?","options":["How quickly smartphone adoption grows among her customers","Whether to accept trade-in gear as part payment for new bikes","Whether to bundle free ski tuning with every pair of skis sold","Which delivery partners will get products to online buyers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Smartphone adoption is a technological environmental force the manager cannot control. Accepting trade-ins is a price decision (price is what is exchanged for the product), choosing delivery partners is place, and bundling ski tuning is part of the product offering - all controllable 4 P decisions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-045","concept":"c01-marketing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lumen Skincare launches a premium $95 serum in luxury packaging but sells it in dollar stores and promotes it with 'Cheapest in town!' flyers. Sales are poor. Which marketing mix principle did Lumen violate?","options":["The 4 Ps are uncontrollable, so marketers can only monitor them","Promotion matters more than the other three Ps combined","Price should always be set below competitors' prices","The 4 Ps must be consistently coordinated and appeal to the target market"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The slides stress that the 4 Ps must be consistently coordinated and appeal to the target market. A premium product and price clash with discount distribution and bargain promotion. The 4 Ps are controllable; 'uncontrollable' describes environmental forces.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-046","concept":"c01-marketing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Vancouver food bank's gift shop gives a reusable tote bag to anyone who contributes 30 minutes of volunteer time at its warehouse. In marketing mix terms, the 30 minutes of volunteer time is the:","options":["product","promotion","price","place"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Price is defined as what is exchanged for the product, and it need not be money - here it is time and effort. The tote is the product; the volunteer time is not a means of communication (promotion) or of getting the product to the consumer (place).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-047","concept":"c01-environmental-forces","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Social, economic, technological, competitive and regulatory factors are the _____ that affect marketing decisions.","options":["business philosophies","environmental forces","marketing mix elements","value propositions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"These five are the environmental forces: uncontrollable factors outside the marketer's direct control. The marketing mix elements (the 4 Ps) are the controllable factors marketers use to respond to them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-048","concept":"c01-environmental-forces","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bookings at Sunridge RV Rentals in Calgary dropped sharply after fuel prices and interest rates jumped and Canadians cut back on discretionary travel. Which environmental force is most directly at work?","options":["Economic","Regulatory","Technological","Social"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rising fuel costs, higher interest rates and reduced discretionary spending are economic conditions. A social force would involve changing values or lifestyles, and a regulatory force would involve new laws or rules.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-049","concept":"c01-environmental-forces","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"More Canadians are cutting back on meat for health and environmental reasons, so a Calgary burger chain adds plant-based patties to its menu. Which environmental force prompted the change?","options":["Competitive","Social","Economic","Regulatory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shifting consumer values and lifestyles (health and environmental concerns) are social forces. Nothing in the stem mentions rivals' actions (competitive), prices or incomes (economic), or new rules (regulatory).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-050","concept":"c01-environmental-forces","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the five environmental forces?","options":["Competitive","Technological","Regulatory","Promotional"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The five environmental forces are social, economic, technological, competitive and regulatory. Promotion is one of the controllable 4 Ps, so a 'promotional force' is a marketing mix element in disguise.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-051","concept":"c01-environmental-forces","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A new Health Canada rule bans certain health claims on supplement labels. In response, Vitality Labs redesigns its packaging and launches a new ad campaign. Which statement is correct?","options":["The rule is a competitive force because rival firms must also comply with it","The rule is a marketing mix decision; the redesign is an uncontrollable environmental force","The rule is a regulatory force; the packaging and ad changes are marketing mix decisions","The rule and the redesign are both marketing mix decisions controlled by the firm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Government rules are regulatory forces the firm cannot control; its responses (packaging, part of the product, and ads, part of promotion) are controllable marketing mix decisions. That rivals face the same rule does not make it a competitive force.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-052","concept":"c01-environmental-forces","also":["c01-marketing-definition"],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the textbook figure showing the role of marketing in an organization, which statement is accurate?","options":["Marketing operates apart from R&D, finance and manufacturing so that it can concentrate fully on advertising","Marketing works with other departments and links the firm to customers, suppliers, partners and owners","Environmental forces sit inside the marketing department and are directly controlled by the marketing manager","Marketing links the organization only to its customers; suppliers and shareholders are not part of the picture"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The figure shows the marketing department among R&D, manufacturing, finance, HR, IT and senior management, linking the organization to alliances, suppliers, customers and shareholders/owners, all surrounded by the uncontrollable environmental forces. Those forces sit outside the organization and cannot be controlled.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-053","concept":"c01-marketing-program","also":["c01-marketing-process"],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"After discovering consumers' needs, marketing's next task is to:","options":["create a target market mission statement","determine consumer demographics","translate information about consumer needs into products that satisfy them","design product prototypes","develop a value strategy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once needs are discovered (step 1 of the marketing process), marketing must translate that information into offerings that satisfy them, which is done through a marketing program: a plan that integrates the marketing mix to provide a good, service or idea to prospective buyers. Designing prototypes is a narrower R&D task, and demographics are part of discovering needs, not the next step.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-054","concept":"c01-marketing-program","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A plan that integrates the marketing mix to provide a good, service or idea to prospective buyers is a:","options":["marketing process","marketing concept","market orientation","marketing program"],"answer":3,"explanation":"That is the definition of a marketing program. The marketing process is the broader three-step sequence (identify needs, manage the mix, realize profits), and the marketing concept is a philosophy, not a plan.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-055","concept":"c01-marketing-program","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After learning that Regina shift workers want healthy late-night food, Harvest Bowl creates a $12 grain-bowl line, sells it from a food truck parked near hospitals from 10 p.m. to 3 a.m., and promotes it in a nurses' union newsletter. Together, these coordinated decisions form Harvest Bowl's:","options":["marketing program","market orientation","target market","value proposition"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Integrating product, price, place and promotion into one plan for prospective buyers is a marketing program. The shift workers are the target market the program is aimed at, not the program itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-056","concept":"c01-marketing-program","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the marketing mix from a marketing program?","options":["The mix applies only to physical goods; the program applies to services and ideas","The mix covers the uncontrollable forces; the program covers the controllable ones","The mix is the four controllable elements; the program is the plan that integrates them","The mix is the integrated plan; the program is the list of four controllable elements"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The marketing mix is the set of ingredients (product, price, promotion, place); the marketing program is the recipe that integrates them into a coherent plan. Uncontrollable factors are environmental forces, not part of the mix.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-057","concept":"c01-marketing-program","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which activity is NOT part of designing a marketing program?","options":["Deciding how to communicate with buyers","Measuring Canada's aggregate flow of goods","Choosing a price for the new offering","Deciding how to get the offering to buyers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A marketing program integrates the 4 Ps for one organization's prospective buyers, so pricing, distribution and communication all belong. The aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services is macromarketing, which is outside any single firm's program.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-058","concept":"c01-marketing-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the FIRST step in the marketing process shown in Figure 1-2?","options":["Promote the product to as many people as possible","Manage the marketing mix to meet consumer needs","Realize profits for the company","Identify consumer needs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The marketing process begins by identifying (discovering) consumer needs through research. Managing the marketing mix is step 2 and realizing profits is step 3; the process never starts with promotion.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-059","concept":"c01-marketing-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Calgary non-profit has researched what newcomers to Canada need and has launched free language workshops at public libraries, promoted through settlement agencies. In the marketing process, what comes next for this organization?","options":["Realize objectives, such as newcomers served","Identify consumer needs for the first time","Realize profits that it can pay out to owners","Manage the marketing mix to meet newcomers' needs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Step 1 (identify needs) and step 2 (manage the mix) are done, so step 3 follows; for a non-profit, step 3 is realizing objectives rather than profits. Paying profits to owners does not fit a non-profit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-060","concept":"c01-marketing-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After focus groups in Yellowknife and Edmonton revealed what tea drinkers dislike about current brands, Tundra Tea Co. is now choosing a $6 price, a recyclable tin, listings at Sobeys and an Instagram launch campaign. Which step of the marketing process is Tundra in?","options":["Realizing profits for the company","Building long-term relationships through CRM","Managing the marketing mix to meet consumer needs","Identifying consumer needs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Needs have already been identified (step 1), and Tundra is now setting product, price, place and promotion, which is step 2: managing the marketing mix to meet consumer needs. Profits (step 3) come only after the program reaches the market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-061","concept":"c01-marketing-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the marketing process in Figure 1-2 is NOT correct?","options":["The process begins by building a product and then searching for buyers","For a non-profit, the final step is realizing objectives rather than profits","Information about consumer needs flows from the market to the organization","Products, services and ideas flow from the organization to the market"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The marketing process is 'a process that focuses on consumer needs': it begins by identifying needs, not by building a product first (that is production-era thinking). The other three statements describe Figure 1-2 accurately.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-062","concept":"c01-marketing-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the chapter describe the marketing process as 'a process that focuses on consumer needs'?","options":["It ends with consumer needs only after the firm has maximized its production output","It replaces profit with consumer needs as the organization's one and only goal","It treats consumer needs as an uncontrollable environmental force to be monitored","It begins by discovering needs, then designs the mix to satisfy them before profits follow"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Needs drive the whole sequence: they are identified first, the marketing mix is managed to satisfy them, and profits (or objectives) result. The process does not drop profit - realizing profits is its third step.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-063","concept":"c01-marketing-process","also":["c01-marketing-concept"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A student writes: 'The marketing process and the marketing concept are the same thing.' Which correction is best?","options":["The process applies only to non-profit groups; the concept applies only to for-profit firms","The process is three steps: needs, mix, profits; the concept is satisfying needs while meeting goals","The process is a philosophy about serving customers; the concept is a three-step sequence of actions","The process is the 4 Ps of the mix; the concept is the five uncontrollable environmental forces"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The marketing process is a sequence of steps; the marketing concept is the guiding idea behind it. Calling the process a philosophy and the concept a three-step sequence simply swaps the two definitions, and both apply to for-profit and non-profit organizations.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-064","concept":"c01-business-philosophies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which sequence correctly shows the evolution of business philosophies?","options":["Production → marketing → sales → relationship/societal","Marketing → production → sales → relationship/societal","Production → sales → marketing → relationship/societal","Sales → production → marketing → relationship/societal"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Firms first focused on efficient production (demand exceeded supply), then on selling their output as competition grew, then on consumer needs (marketing orientation), and today on long-term relationships and society's well-being.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-065","concept":"c01-business-philosophies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"In 1912, a Montreal stove manufacturer could sell every stove it built because demand far exceeded supply, so its managers focused on making stoves as efficiently as possible. Which orientation did the firm have?","options":["Societal marketing orientation","Marketing orientation","Sales orientation","Production orientation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A production orientation focuses on producing goods efficiently and fits the early-1900s condition of demand exceeding supply. A sales orientation emerged later, once competition grew and firms had to push their output.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-066","concept":"c01-business-philosophies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Facing new competitors and warehouses full of unsold vacuum cleaners, Hartwell Appliances hires a large door-to-door sales force and pays big commissions to push its existing models. Which orientation does this reflect?","options":["Relationship marketing orientation","Sales orientation","Production orientation","Marketing orientation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pushing products the firm has already made through aggressive selling and promotion is the sales orientation. A marketing orientation would start by finding out what customers need before deciding what to make.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-067","concept":"c01-business-philosophies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What most clearly separates the marketing orientation from the sales orientation?","options":["Marketing emerged when demand exceeded supply; sales emerged once competition grew","Marketing stresses long-term relationships and society; sales stresses consumer needs","Marketing begins with consumer needs; sales begins with the products the firm has already made","Marketing relies on promotion; sales relies mainly on efficient production"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The key difference is the starting point: the marketing orientation starts by discovering consumer needs, while the sales orientation pushes output the firm has already produced. 'Demand exceeded supply' describes the production era, and long-term relationships plus society describe today's relationship/societal orientation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-068","concept":"c01-business-philosophies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the evolution of business philosophies, why did many firms shift from a production orientation to a sales orientation?","options":["Competition grew, so firms had to push their output to sell it","Consumers began demanding socially responsible business practices","Firms started sharing customer and competitor data across departments","Governments began banning efficient mass-production methods"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The production era worked because demand exceeded supply; as competition grew, firms needed selling and promotion to move what they produced. Demand for social responsibility belongs to today's societal era, and sharing data across departments describes a market orientation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-069","concept":"c01-business-philosophies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Ridgeline Credit Union in Calgary now measures success by how many members stay for ten years or more, and it publicly reports on its community investments and environmentally responsible lending. Which stage in the evolution of business philosophies does this reflect?","options":["Production orientation","Sales orientation","Relationship/societal orientation","Marketing orientation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Long-term relationships (members staying a decade) plus attention to society's well-being (community investment, responsible lending) mark today's relationship/societal orientation. The plain marketing orientation focuses on satisfying consumer needs without that added long-term relationship and societal emphasis.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-070","concept":"c01-marketing-concept","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The idea that an organization should strive to satisfy the needs of consumers while also trying to achieve the organization's goals is the:","options":["marketing concept","sales orientation","societal marketing concept","market orientation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This two-part idea (satisfy consumers + achieve organizational goals) is the marketing concept. The societal marketing concept adds a third element, society's well-being, and market orientation is the practical implementation of the marketing concept.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-071","concept":"c01-marketing-concept","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"At Boreal Bikes in Thunder Bay, managers launch only models that customer research shows riders want, and only if the model can also meet the company's 25% margin target. Which idea guides Boreal?","options":["Macromarketing","The marketing concept","Customer experience management","The production orientation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Boreal balances both halves of the marketing concept: satisfying consumer needs (research-based models) and achieving organizational goals (the margin target). A production orientation would focus on making bikes efficiently regardless of what riders want.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-072","concept":"c01-marketing-concept","also":["c01-societal-csr"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the societal marketing concept differ from the marketing concept?","options":["It focuses on pushing the firm's existing output through aggressive promotion","It drops the goal of satisfying consumer needs in favour of serving society","It requires collecting competitor information and sharing it across departments","It adds that needs be satisfied in a way that also provides for society's well-being"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The societal marketing concept keeps the marketing concept's goal of satisfying consumers but adds society's well-being. It does not abandon consumers; aggressive selling is the sales orientation, and sharing information across departments is market orientation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-073","concept":"c01-marketing-concept","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"A company claims to follow the marketing concept. Which practice is NOT consistent with that claim?","options":["Making whatever is cheapest to produce, then pushing it with heavy discounts","Rejecting a popular product idea that could never meet its profit goals","Researching customer needs before developing any new products","Updating its offering whenever customer needs change over time"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Making whatever is cheapest and then pushing it ignores consumer needs (production and sales thinking). Rejecting an idea that cannot meet profit goals IS consistent with the marketing concept, because the concept includes achieving the organization's goals, not satisfying customers at any cost.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-074","concept":"c01-marketing-concept","also":["c01-market-orientation"],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"Firms that put great effort into implementing the marketing concept are said to have a market orientation.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Market orientation is the marketing concept put into practice: continuously collecting information about customers' needs and competitors' capabilities, sharing it across departments, and using it to create customer value.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-075","concept":"c01-market-orientation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"An organization that focuses its efforts on continuously collecting information about customers' needs and competitors' capabilities, sharing this information across departments, and using the information to create customer values is said to:","options":["stress the societal marketing concept","have a focus on macromarketing","have a nonprofit orientation","have a market orientation","be utilizing consumerism"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Collecting customer and competitor information, sharing it across departments and using it to create customer value is the definition of market orientation, the result of implementing the marketing concept. The societal marketing concept would add society's well-being, and macromarketing concerns a nation's aggregate flow of goods.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-076","concept":"c01-market-orientation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Every Monday, Tidewater Grocers' analytics team compiles loyalty-card data on shoppers' changing needs and a report on competitors' new services, sends both to the buying, store-operations and finance teams, and each team uses them to improve what customers get. Tidewater displays:","options":["macromarketing","the societal marketing concept","a sales orientation","a market orientation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"All three components of market orientation are present: continuous collection of customer and competitor information, sharing across departments, and use of the information to create customer value. Nothing in the stem mentions society's well-being.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-077","concept":"c01-market-orientation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a component of a market orientation?","options":["Collecting information about competitors' capabilities","Keeping customer information inside the marketing department","Continuously collecting information about customers' needs","Using the gathered information to create customer value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A market orientation requires SHARING information across departments, not keeping it within marketing. Collecting customer and competitor information and using it to create value are the other components.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-078","concept":"c01-market-orientation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes market orientation from the marketing concept?","options":["Market orientation is the underlying idea; the marketing concept is how a firm puts it into practice","Market orientation is a production-era philosophy; the marketing concept is the philosophy used today","Market orientation is the practical implementation (collect, share, use information); the marketing concept is the idea","Market orientation focuses on society's well-being; the marketing concept largely ignores customers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The marketing concept is the idea (satisfy consumer needs while achieving organizational goals); firms that work hard at implementing it achieve a market orientation by collecting, sharing and using information. Calling market orientation the underlying idea and the marketing concept its implementation reverses the two.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-079","concept":"c01-market-orientation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cedar & Pine Furniture's marketing team surveys customers, but the design and production teams never see the results, and nobody tracks what competitors offer. What should the firm do to become market oriented?","options":["Sponsor a community program to adopt the societal marketing concept","Add competitor research and share all findings across departments so each can create customer value","Switch to a sales orientation to move existing inventory faster","Have the marketing team run customer surveys more often and keep the results in marketing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Market orientation has three parts: continuously collect information on customers' needs AND competitors' capabilities, share it across departments, and use it to create customer value. Cedar & Pine already collects customer data, so more surveys kept inside marketing miss both gaps: competitor information and cross-department sharing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-080","concept":"c01-societal-csr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The idea that organizations are part of a larger society and are accountable to that society for their actions is called:","options":["customer relationship management","the marketing concept","corporate social responsibility","macromarketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"That is the definition of corporate social responsibility (CSR). Macromarketing is also society-level, but it describes the aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services, not an organization's accountability for its actions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-081","concept":"c01-societal-csr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Every summer, Prairie Sky Insurance designs and runs a free swimming-lessons program for children in Regina's inner-city neighbourhoods. Which type of CSR initiative is this?","options":["Sponsorship and/or spearheading of community programs","Sponsorship of or involvement in fundraising for charitable organizations","A business philosophy that implements socially responsible practices","Customer relationship management aimed at policyholders"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Prairie Sky is spearheading a community program, CSR type 1 on the slide. No charity is raising money (type 2), and the firm's overall business practices are not changing (type 3).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-082","concept":"c01-societal-csr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"During April, Harbourfront Hardware in Halifax asks shoppers to donate at checkout to the Canadian Cancer Society and matches every dollar raised. Which type of CSR initiative is this?","options":["Sponsorship and/or spearheading of community programs","Experiential marketing at the point of sale","A business philosophy that implements socially responsible practices","Sponsorship of or involvement in fundraising for charitable organizations"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Raising and matching money for a charity is CSR type 2: fundraising initiatives for charitable organizations. It is not the firm running its own community program (type 1) or changing how the entire business operates (type 3).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-083","concept":"c01-societal-csr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Polaris Apparel overhauls how it operates: it pays all suppliers a living wage, switches every product line to recycled fabric, and ties executive bonuses to emissions cuts. Which type of CSR initiative is this?","options":["Partnership marketing with sustainable brands","A business philosophy that implements socially responsible practices","Sponsorship of or involvement in fundraising for charitable organizations","Sponsorship and/or spearheading of community programs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When social responsibility is built into the way the whole business runs (sourcing, materials, pay incentives), it is CSR type 3: a business philosophy that implements socially responsible practices. No specific community program or charity fundraiser is involved.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-084","concept":"c01-societal-csr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three types of CSR initiatives listed in the chapter?","options":["Sponsoring or spearheading community programs","Sponsoring or taking part in fundraising for charities","Running a points program that rewards repeat customers","Adopting a business philosophy of socially responsible practices"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The three CSR types are community programs, charitable fundraising, and a socially responsible business philosophy. A points program rewarding repeat customers is a customer relationship (loyalty) tactic, not a CSR initiative.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-085","concept":"c01-societal-csr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Glenbow Beverages wants to move from the marketing concept to the societal marketing concept. Which change would best accomplish this?","options":["Cutting sugar and using recyclable bottles while still meeting tastes and profit goals","Sharing competitor data across all departments every week to create customer value","Speeding up production lines to lower the cost of making each bottle","Hiring more salespeople to push existing inventory into more stores"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The societal marketing concept satisfies consumer needs (and organizational goals) in a way that also provides for society's well-being, which healthier drinks and recyclable packaging do. Faster production is production thinking, pushing inventory is sales thinking, and sharing competitor data is market orientation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-086","concept":"c01-societal-csr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Nordic Outfitters' CEO says: 'We design only what customers say they need, priced so we still hit our profit target, but we will not use a cheaper fabric that pollutes local rivers, even though customers would never notice.' The final part of her statement goes beyond the marketing concept to reflect the:","options":["market orientation","sales orientation","customer relationship management","societal marketing concept"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Satisfying customers while meeting profit goals is the marketing concept; refusing a cheaper input to protect society's well-being, even when customers would not notice, adds the societal dimension. Market orientation is about collecting, sharing and using customer and competitor information, which the statement does not describe.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-087","concept":"c01-crm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rajan's Wealth Software and Yi Capital Group, two top vendors of financial management software, have recently launched their own online trading exchange aimed at increasing the ease with which their customers can finance their new products and ideas. Both of these organizations provide exceptional, ongoing service to their business customers. These two organizations hope to benefit from:","options":["customer relationship management","societal marketing concept","customer orientation","product valuation","none of the above"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CRM is the process of building and developing long-term relationships with customers by delivering customer value and satisfaction, and exceptional, ongoing service to business customers aims at exactly that. The societal marketing concept would require a focus on society's well-being, which the stem never mentions.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-088","concept":"c01-crm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The process of building and developing long-term relationships with customers by delivering customer value and satisfaction is called:","options":["the marketing concept","market orientation","customer experience management","customer relationship management"],"answer":3,"explanation":"This is the definition of CRM. Customer experience management concerns the customer's internal response to all aspects of the organization; the cue words here are 'long-term relationships' delivered through 'value and satisfaction'.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-089","concept":"c01-crm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Linking an organization to its individual customers, employees, suppliers and other partners for their mutual long-term benefit is the definition of:","options":["customer relationship management","relationship marketing","partnership marketing","macromarketing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Relationship marketing links the organization not only to customers but also to employees, suppliers and other partners. CRM focuses on long-term relationships with customers specifically, so the mention of employees and suppliers is the cue. Partnership marketing is a formal association between brands.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-090","concept":"c01-crm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northwind Auto in Edmonton records each customer's service history, sends personalized maintenance reminders, and gives free winter-tire swaps to clients who have stayed five years or more. Northwind is practising:","options":["customer relationship management","macromarketing","the production orientation","experiential marketing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tracking individual customers and rewarding long-time clients builds long-term relationships by delivering value and satisfaction, which is CRM. Experiential marketing would involve creating hands-on brand experiences, not ongoing relationship building.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-091","concept":"c01-crm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do firms invest in customer relationship management rather than focusing only on individual sales?","options":["To replace the 4 Ps of the marketing mix with a single, cheaper promotional tool","To control environmental forces such as the state of the economy and competitors","To keep customers returning long-term by delivering value and satisfaction","To lower production costs by making manufacturing more efficient and automated"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CRM's purpose is long-term relationships built on delivering customer value and satisfaction, so customers keep coming back. Environmental forces cannot be controlled by any marketing tool, and CRM works alongside the marketing mix rather than replacing it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-092","concept":"c01-crm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which practice is NOT consistent with customer relationship management?","options":["Tracking each customer's preferences to personalize future offers","Using high-pressure tactics to maximize one-time sales, ignoring repeat business","Providing exceptional ongoing service to existing business clients","Rewarding long-time customers with loyalty perks and early access"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CRM is about long-term relationships, so one-off, high-pressure selling that ignores repeat business contradicts it. Ongoing service, personalization and loyalty rewards all build long-term relationships through value and satisfaction.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-093","concept":"c01-customer-experience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"Managing the internal response that customers have to all aspects of an organization and its offering is called:","options":["customer experience management","customer value","experiential marketing","customer relationship management"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is customer experience management; its cue words are 'internal response' and 'all aspects of an organization'. Experiential marketing is a promotional practice that creates hands-on brand experiences, and CRM is about long-term relationships built on value and satisfaction.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-094","concept":"c01-customer-experience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aurora Hotels hires a team to map every guest touchpoint - the booking app, check-in, room scent, staff greetings and checkout emails - and redesigns any point where guests say they felt frustrated or ignored. Which practice is this?","options":["Experiential marketing","Macromarketing","Customer experience management","Partnership marketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Managing guests' internal response across all aspects of the organization is customer experience management. Experiential marketing, the tempting look-alike, means creating hands-on brand events for consumers, not redesigning every service touchpoint.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-095","concept":"c01-customer-experience","also":["c01-crm"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes customer experience management from customer relationship management (CRM)?","options":["Experience management applies only to organizational buyers; CRM applies only to ultimate consumers buying for households","Experience management is a production-era practice; CRM is a sales-era practice used to push inventory","Experience management builds long-term relationships through delivering value; CRM manages customers' feelings at each touchpoint","Experience management targets customers' internal response to every aspect of the firm; CRM targets long-term relationships via value and satisfaction"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The definitions differ in focus: customer experience management targets the customer's internal response to all aspects of the organization, while CRM targets long-term relationships through value and satisfaction. Saying experience management builds relationships through value while CRM manages feelings at each touchpoint swaps the definitions; in practice the two support each other.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-096","concept":"c01-customer-experience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about customer experience management is NOT correct?","options":["It covers all aspects of the organization and its offering","It focuses only on the product itself, ignoring websites, staff and service","A positive experience can support long-term customer relationships","It concerns customers' internal response to the organization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Customer experience management covers ALL aspects of the organization and its offering - the website, staff, service and the product - so 'only the product' is wrong. A good experience supports the long-term relationships CRM aims for.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-097","concept":"c01-customer-experience","also":["c01-evolving-practices"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A student confuses customer experience management with experiential marketing. Which example is experiential marketing rather than customer experience management?","options":["An airline studies how travellers feel at each step from booking to baggage claim","A hotel retrains staff and updates its website so guests feel welcome at every touchpoint","A chip brand sets up a pop-up 'flavour lab' at a Toronto festival where visitors create and taste their own seasonings","A bank redesigns its app, branch greetings and follow-up calls after customers report feeling ignored"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Experiential marketing creates opportunities for consumers to interact directly with a brand, such as a hands-on festival pop-up. The airline, hotel and bank examples all manage customers' internal response across the organization's touchpoints, which is customer experience management.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-098","concept":"c01-consumers-org-buyers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is the best example of an organizational buyer?","options":["A mother buying milk for her young son","A computer programmer buying the latest game for his PlayStation","A store owner buying hand-painted slate signs to sell in her store","A botanist buying a rose bush for his home garden","A baseball player buying a t-ball set for his daughter"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Organizational buyers are manufacturers, wholesalers, retailers and government agencies that buy for their own use or for resale. The store owner is buying signs to resell; every other buyer is purchasing for personal or household use, which makes them ultimate consumers regardless of their occupation.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-099","concept":"c01-consumers-org-buyers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Priya, a dentist in Lethbridge, buys a laptop. In which situation is she acting as an organizational buyer?","options":["She buys it for her clinic's front desk","She buys it as a birthday gift for her sister","She buys it to stream movies in her living room","She buys it for her teenage son's homework"],"answer":0,"explanation":"What matters is the purpose of the purchase. Buying for the clinic's own use makes Priya an organizational buyer; the other purchases are for personal or household use, so she would be an ultimate consumer even though she is a professional.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-100","concept":"c01-consumers-org-buyers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"The same 24-pack of bottled water is bought by a Calgary family for a camping trip and by a convenience-store owner to sell from her cooler. What determines which buyer is the ultimate consumer?","options":["The size of the order: small versus bulk quantities","The price paid: full retail versus a discounted price","The type of product: consumer goods versus industrial goods","The purpose of the purchase: household use versus resale"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Ultimate consumers buy for household use; organizational buyers buy for their own organizational use or for resale. The product here is identical, so the only thing that separates the two buyers is the purpose of the purchase.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-101","concept":"c01-consumers-org-buyers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an organizational buyer?","options":["A dentist buying a gaming console for his den","A Canadian Tire dealer buying barbecues to resell","Alberta Health Services buying hospital beds","A Regina bakery buying flour to make bread"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The dentist is buying for household use, so he is an ultimate consumer despite his professional job - the classic job-title trap. A government agency buying for its own use, a manufacturer (bakery) buying inputs, and a retailer buying for resale are all organizational buyers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-102","concept":"c01-consumers-org-buyers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"A provincial ministry buys 500 snow shovels to sell at cost to seniors through community centres. How should the ministry be classified, and why?","options":["Not a buyer at all, because no profit is made on the resale","Ultimate consumer, because the shovels will end up being used in seniors' households","Ultimate consumer, because government agencies are not businesses","Organizational buyer, because a government agency is buying for resale rather than household use"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Organizational buyers include government agencies that buy for their own use or for resale. The ministry is reselling (even at cost), so it is an organizational buyer; the seniors who later buy the shovels for home use are the ultimate consumers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-103","concept":"c01-utility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The benefits or customer value received by users of a product is called:","options":["exchange","a value proposition","utility","customer satisfaction"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Utility is the benefit or customer value users receive from a product, and it comes in four types: form, place, time and possession. A value proposition is a promised set of benefits, not the benefit actually received through use.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-104","concept":"c01-utility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sunrise Coffee opens a kiosk beyond security in the Edmonton International Airport so departing travellers can get coffee right beside their gates. Which utility does this create most directly?","options":["Possession utility","Place utility","Time utility","Form utility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Place utility means having the offering available where consumers need it - here, right at the gates. Time utility would be about when it is available (for example, opening early for 5 a.m. flights).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-105","concept":"c01-utility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Northgate Pharmacy stays open 24 hours a day so customers can fill urgent prescriptions at 3 a.m. Which utility does this primarily provide?","options":["Possession utility","Place utility","Time utility","Form utility"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Being available WHEN consumers need the product is time utility. Place utility is about WHERE it is available, which the 24-hour schedule does not change.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-106","concept":"c01-utility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Harmon Furniture in Winnipeg lets shoppers take a $3,000 sofa home today with 24 months of interest-free financing. Which utility does the financing offer create?","options":["Possession utility","Time utility","Place utility","Form utility"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Possession utility is getting the product to consumers so they can own and use it, and offering credit or financing is the textbook example. Place utility concerns where the sofa is available, which the financing does not change.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-107","concept":"c01-utility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which type of utility does marketing NOT create directly, but only influences?","options":["Place utility","Form utility","Possession utility","Time utility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Form utility comes from producing the good or service, so production creates it; marketing influences it by identifying what consumers want. Marketing directly creates place, time and possession utility.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-108","concept":"c01-utility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Golden Wheat Bakery turns flour, eggs and butter into fresh croissants every morning. Which utility does this create, and what is marketing's role in it?","options":["Possession utility; marketing creates it directly by offering customers credit","Place utility; marketing creates it directly by choosing store locations","Form utility; marketing creates it directly through its promotion","Form utility; marketing influences it through insight into what consumers want"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Turning raw materials into a product is form utility, created by production. Marketing's role is to influence form utility by feeding consumer insights into product design, not to create it directly; promotion does not make the croissant.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-109","concept":"c01-macro-micro","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"How an individual organization directs its marketing activities and allocates its resources to benefit its customers is called:","options":["micromarketing","market orientation","a value proposition","macromarketing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is micromarketing: the marketing of one organization. Macromarketing looks at the aggregate flow of a whole nation's goods and services to benefit society.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-110","concept":"c01-macro-micro","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A University of Alberta economist studies how efficiently Canada's entire food system moves products from farms to consumers in every province, and whether that system benefits society. This is a study of:","options":["micromarketing","macromarketing","relationship marketing","market orientation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Examining the aggregate flow of a nation's goods to benefit society is macromarketing. Micromarketing would focus on how a single organization directs its own marketing activities.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-111","concept":"c01-macro-micro","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Loblaw, one of Canada's largest retailers, decides how to divide its national advertising budget among its store banners. Which term best applies?","options":["Neither, because budget decisions are financial rather than marketing activities","Macromarketing, because the company is very large and advertises nationally","Macromarketing, because the decision affects consumers across the whole nation","Micromarketing, because it is one organization allocating its marketing resources"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Micromarketing is about how an individual organization directs its marketing and allocates its resources, regardless of company size. Macromarketing is the aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services, not one large firm's national campaign.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-112","concept":"c01-macro-micro","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about macromarketing is NOT correct?","options":["It focuses on benefits to society as a whole","It concerns the aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services","It refers to marketing done by very large companies with national budgets","It looks at the overall system rather than a single organization"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Macromarketing is about the aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services to benefit society - a system-level view. Company size has nothing to do with it; even a giant firm's own plans are micromarketing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-113","concept":"c01-macro-micro","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"The aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services to benefit society is called _____.","options":["the societal marketing concept","macromarketing","corporate social responsibility","micromarketing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Macromarketing is the society-wide, aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services. The societal marketing concept and CSR also mention society, but they describe how an individual organization should behave, not the national flow of goods.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-114","concept":"c01-evolving-practices","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the seven new and evolving marketing practices identified in Chapter 1?","options":["Production orientation","Partnership marketing","Metrics and analytics","Augmented reality"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The seven practices are digital marketing (content, mobile, social media), augmented reality, experiential marketing, influencer marketing, partnership marketing, metrics and analytics, and new regulations and ethical considerations. Production orientation is the earliest business philosophy, not a new practice.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-115","concept":"c01-evolving-practices","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A Montreal furniture retailer's app lets shoppers point their phone at an empty corner of the living room and see a life-sized 3D sofa placed there. Which evolving practice is this?","options":["Partnership marketing","Influencer marketing","Experiential marketing","Augmented reality"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Overlaying digital images onto the real-world view through a phone is augmented reality. Experiential marketing involves hands-on, real-world brand experiences such as events or pop-ups.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-116","concept":"c01-evolving-practices","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Glacier Water pays a Calgary fitness creator with 400,000 TikTok followers to post videos featuring its bottles in her daily workout routine. Which evolving practice is this?","options":["Metrics and analytics","Experiential marketing","Influencer marketing","Partnership marketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Paying a personality with a large, trusting following to promote a brand is influencer marketing. Partnership marketing is a formal association between two brands, not a paid endorsement by an individual creator.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-117","concept":"c01-evolving-practices","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Canadian coffee chain and a national bookseller form a formal association: co-branded cafés inside the bookstores and a shared loyalty reward. Which evolving practice is this?","options":["Partnership marketing","Influencer marketing","Experiential marketing","Augmented reality"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Partnership marketing is the creation of formal associations between brands that contribute to each other's success, such as co-branded locations and shared rewards. Influencer marketing would rely on an individual personality's endorsement.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-118","concept":"c01-evolving-practices","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A student lists the seven new and evolving practices as: content marketing, mobile marketing, social media marketing, augmented reality, experiential marketing, influencer marketing and partnership marketing. What is wrong with this list?","options":["Partnership marketing belongs to the relationship era of business philosophy, not to evolving practices","Content, mobile and social media belong under digital marketing, and metrics/analytics and regulations/ethics are missing","Influencer marketing and partnership marketing are really the same practice and should be combined","Augmented reality is just a form of experiential marketing and should not be listed as a separate item"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The chapter groups content, mobile and social media marketing as parts of item 1, digital marketing. The full list of seven also includes metrics and analytics and new marketing regulations and ethical considerations, which the student left out.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-119","concept":"c01-evolving-practices","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Brightwater Cosmetics runs ads on three platforms but cannot tell which one actually drives sales. Which evolving marketing practice would most directly solve this problem?","options":["Experiential marketing","Augmented reality","Metrics and analytics","Partnership marketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Metrics and analytics measure marketing results, letting Brightwater compare which platform drives sales. The other practices are ways of reaching or engaging customers, not ways of measuring performance.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-120","concept":"c01-ethics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The moral principles and values that govern the actions and decisions of an individual or group are called:","options":["the societal marketing concept","corporate social responsibility","laws","ethics"],"answer":3,"explanation":"That is the definition of ethics. Laws are rules enforced by government; CSR is the idea that organizations are accountable to society, which is broader than the moral principles guiding individual decisions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-121","concept":"c01-ethics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Leaders at a Calgary marketing agency refuse a client's request to bury automatic-renewal fees in tiny print, saying it conflicts with the CMA Code even though no specific law clearly forbids it. Their decision is driven mainly by:","options":["a regulatory force","macromarketing","customer experience management","ethical principles"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The agency is acting on moral principles and a code of ethics, not on a legal requirement, so ethics is the driver. A regulatory force would be a law or government rule, and the stem says no law clearly forbids the practice.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-122","concept":"c01-ethics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about codes of ethics in marketing is NOT accurate, according to the chapter?","options":["Only government agencies issue codes of ethics for marketers","The CMA publishes a Canadian Marketing Code of Ethics and Standards","Many industries and professional associations have codes of ethics","Many companies have their own guidelines and codes of ethics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The slides note that many companies, industries and professional associations have guidelines and codes of ethics, including the CMA's Canadian Marketing Code of Ethics and Standards. Codes are not limited to government agencies.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-123","concept":"c01-ethics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Winnipeg supplement company's new ad is technically legal, but it implies results that the company's own studies do not support. Which statement best applies?","options":["The ad is a CSR initiative, because any form of advertising has some effect on society","The ad may be legal yet unethical; codes of ethics like the CMA Code guide conduct beyond the law","The ad is a regulatory environmental force, which the company itself cannot control","Because the ad is legal, it is automatically ethical, so no code of ethics applies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Laws set the legal minimum, while ethics are the moral principles governing what an individual or group believes is right, so an ad can satisfy the law and still mislead. That gap is why companies, industries and associations such as the CMA publish codes of ethics. The firm's own ad is a controllable decision, not an environmental force.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-124","concept":"c01-ethics","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The Canadian Marketing Code of Ethics and Standards is published by the Chartered Institute of Marketing.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Corrected: the Canadian Marketing Code of Ethics and Standards is published by the Canadian Marketing Association (CMA). The Chartered Institute of Marketing is the UK body whose definition of marketing appears on the slides.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-125","concept":"c01-who-markets","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"What kinds of organizations engage in marketing?","options":["only those that can afford national advertising","very large and established nonprofit organizations","exclusively Fortune 1000 companies","every organization markets","virtually no organizations"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Every organization markets, because each needs people to engage in exchanges with it or to think or behave in a certain way - including non-profits like the Canadian Red Cross and government agencies like the Ministry of Transportation. Marketing is not limited to big advertisers or large companies.","source":"official"},{"id":"q01-126","concept":"c01-who-markets","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Alberta's transportation ministry runs radio and billboard ads urging drivers to put their phones away while driving. Nothing is sold. Which statement best describes this campaign?","options":["It is macromarketing, because a government is doing it","It is not marketing, because no product or money is exchanged","It is marketing an idea, showing that government agencies market too","It is not marketing, because only for-profit firms can market"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ideas can be marketed, and governments market when they try to get people to think or behave in a certain way, like the chapter's Ministry of Transportation example. A government running a campaign is still one organization's marketing, not macromarketing (the aggregate flow of a nation's goods and services).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-127","concept":"c01-who-markets","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement about who markets is NOT correct?","options":["Non-profits such as the Canadian Red Cross engage in marketing","Services, such as dental care, can be marketed","Marketing applies only to physical goods sold for profit","Ideas, such as road safety, can be marketed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Everyone markets: goods, services and ideas, for-profit and non-profit. Limiting marketing to physical goods sold for profit contradicts the chapter's 'Who markets? Everyone' slide.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-128","concept":"c01-who-markets","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to Chapter 1, why is understanding marketing valuable even for students who will never work in a marketing department?","options":["Because marketing affects everyone, knowing it makes us better customers, citizens and career planners","Marketing is mainly common sense, so a marketing course offers an easy and useful credit","Marketing affects only consumer-goods firms, which employ most business graduates in Canada","Marketing knowledge is legally required to obtain every Canadian business licence"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The career slide states that marketing affects all individuals, organizations, industries and countries, and that knowing it makes us better customers, more informed citizens and better career planners. 'Common sense' is the misconception the chapter rejects.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q01-129","concept":"c01-who-markets","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Calgary food bank seeking donors, a city councillor seeking re-election and the Town of Banff promoting winter tourism all run campaigns. Which statement is most accurate?","options":["Only Banff is marketing, because tourism is a service that visitors pay money for","All three are marketing, since organizations, individuals and places can all be marketed","Only the food bank is marketing, because charities are the only non-business exception","None of them is marketing, because none of them sells a physical product for profit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Everyone markets: non-profits, individuals and places as well as firms, and the offering can be a good, service, idea, person or place. Requiring a physical product or a profit motive reflects the narrow view of marketing the chapter corrects.","source":"generated"}],"color":"#D4A834","short":"Marketing","emoji":"🏛️","tag":"Why marketing exists","province":"Italia","numeral":"I"},{"chapter":6,"title":"Segmentation, Targeting, and Positioning","topics":[{"id":"t06-1","order":1,"title":"STP: the big picture of segmentation","summary":"Marketers rarely try to sell to everyone. A customer-driven strategy has four steps: segmentation divides the market into groups, targeting chooses which groups to serve, differentiation makes the offering different and better, and positioning places it in target customers' minds. This topic defines segmentation, segments, target markets and product differentiation, and explains why firms segment at all.","concepts":["c06-stp-framework","c06-market-segmentation","c06-market-segments","c06-product-differentiation","c06-why-segment"]},{"id":"t06-2","order":2,"title":"How narrowly to target, and whether a segment is worth it","summary":"Firms can target broadly or narrowly, from mass marketing to niche and one-to-one marketing. Segmenting only makes sense when it adds more revenue than cost. Two separate checklists help: five criteria for whether a segment is properly FORMED, and five criteria for which segments to SELECT as targets.","concepts":["c06-continuum","c06-when-to-segment","c06-forming-criteria","c06-selecting-criteria"]},{"id":"t06-3","order":3,"title":"Segmentation variables: consumer and business markets","summary":"Consumer markets are segmented with four families of variables: geographic (where), demographic (census facts), psychographic (personality, values, lifestyle) and behaviouristic (how people use the product, including usage rate and the 80/20 rule). Business markets add bases such as operating characteristics, purchasing approaches, situational factors and personal characteristics.","concepts":["c06-consumer-variables","c06-geographic","c06-demographic","c06-psychographic","c06-behaviouristic","c06-usage-rate","c06-business-segmentation"]},{"id":"t06-4","order":4,"title":"From segments to targets: profiles, data, process and ethics","summary":"Marketers bring segments to life with target market profiles and personas, and buy data from firms such as Nielsen, Euromonitor and Environics Analytics. A six-step process moves from identifying needs to monitoring results, and a market-product grid shows where market size lies. Targeting also has to be socially responsible.","concepts":["c06-personas","c06-segmentation-analytics","c06-six-step-process","c06-market-product-grid","c06-ethics"]},{"id":"t06-5","order":5,"title":"Positioning the offering","summary":"Positioning is the place a brand holds in consumers' minds compared with competitors, built on image, product attributes and price. A firm can position head-to-head or look for a less-competitive niche (differentiation positioning), write the position down in a positioning statement, check it on a perceptual map, change it through repositioning, and build it on points-of-parity, points-of-difference and a USP.","concepts":["c06-product-positioning","c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation","c06-positioning-statement","c06-perceptual-map","c06-repositioning","c06-pop-pod","c06-usp"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"c06-stp-framework","topic":"t06-1","term":"STP framework (customer-driven marketing strategy)","definition":"A customer-driven marketing strategy has two decisions: select customers to serve (segmentation: divide the total market into smaller segments; targeting: select the segment or segments to enter) and decide on a value proposition (differentiation: differentiate the market offering to create superior customer value; positioning: position the offering in the minds of target customers).","explain":"Think of STP as a funnel from 'who could buy' to 'who we serve and how they see us'. First you slice the market into groups (segmentation), then pick the group(s) worth pursuing (targeting). Only then do you decide what will make your offering different (differentiation) and the place you want it to hold in those customers' minds (positioning). Everything aims at the centre of the model: create value for targeted customers.","example":"A Calgary craft brewery splits beer drinkers into segments, targets young adults who like sour beers, makes the only sours brewed with locally foraged fruit (differentiation), and positions itself as 'Calgary's adventurous brewery'.","contrast":"Segmentation = dividing the market; targeting = choosing which segment(s) to enter; differentiation = making the offering different and better; positioning = the place the offering holds in target customers' minds. Segmentation and targeting answer 'whom do we serve?'; differentiation and positioning answer 'with what value?'","trap":"Watch the verbs: 'divide/group' = segmentation, 'select/choose/enter' = targeting, 'make different/superior value' = differentiation, 'in the minds of' = positioning. Stems often describe a firm that has ALREADY divided the market and is now choosing a group, which is targeting, not segmentation.","mnemonic":"Slice, Select, Stand out, Settle in the mind (S-T-D-P).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Customer-driven marketing strategy","head":["Decision","Step","What it does"],"rows":[["Select customers to serve","Segmentation","Divide the total market into smaller segments"],["Select customers to serve","Targeting","Select the segment or segments to enter"],["Decide on a value proposition","Differentiation","Differentiate the market offering to create superior customer value"],["Decide on a value proposition","Positioning","Position the market offering in the minds of target customers"],["Centre of the model","Goal","Create value for targeted customers"]]}},{"id":"c06-market-segmentation","topic":"t06-1","term":"Market segmentation","definition":"Aggregating prospective buyers into groups (segments) that (1) have common needs and (2) will respond similarly to a marketing action.","explain":"Segmentation means grouping, not just splitting: you gather buyers who are alike in what they need and in how they react to marketing. The resulting groups are relatively homogeneous, with similar consumption behaviour, attitudes and target market profiles. Because each group wants something a bit different, segmentation leads to product differentiation.","example":"A Winnipeg hockey retailer groups customers into hockey parents, adult rec-league players and elite juniors, because each group needs different gear and responds differently to promotions.","contrast":"Segmentation groups the market; targeting chooses which group(s) to pursue. Segmentation is also the opposite of mass marketing, which treats the whole market as one group with one marketing mix.","trap":"Both conditions are required: common needs AND a similar response to a marketing action. If the stem says the firm 'decided which group to pursue', that is targeting, not segmentation.","mnemonic":"Same needs + same response = same segment.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-market-segments","topic":"t06-1","term":"Market segments and target market","definition":"Market segments are the relatively homogeneous groups of prospective buyers that segmentation produces. A target market is the specific group of existing and potential consumers to which marketers direct their marketing efforts.","explain":"Segmentation can produce many segments; the target market is the one (or few) the firm actually picks. Members of a segment are similar to each other in consumption behaviour, attitudes and profile, but they do not have to be identical. A target market includes both people who already buy and people the firm hopes will buy.","example":"Fjord Kayak Co. identifies five segments of paddlers in Western Canada but directs all its ads at one of them: retired outdoor enthusiasts on Vancouver Island. That group is its target market.","contrast":"A segment is any group identified in the market; a target market is the segment(s) chosen for marketing efforts. A persona is a character description of the typical customer in a target market, not the group itself.","trap":"'Existing AND potential': a target market is not just current customers. Also watch for options claiming segment members must be identical; they only need to be relatively homogeneous.","mnemonic":"Many segments, one bullseye.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-product-differentiation","topic":"t06-1","term":"Product differentiation","definition":"A strategy that uses different marketing mix activities, such as product features and advertising, to help consumers perceive a product as different from and better than competing products.","explain":"Once you know segments want different things, you can offer different versions of your product (or different messages) so each group sees your offering as the better choice for them. A firm selling two or more products with different features aimed at different segments is using product differentiation. It is the firm-side strategy that segmentation leads to.","example":"Hallmark sells several card brands, such as Shoebox, Mahogany and Tree of Life, each designed for a different segment.","contrast":"Product differentiation (offering distinct products or marketing so they are seen as different and better) is not differentiation positioning (placing ONE brand in a smaller, less-competitive niche by stressing unique attributes). It is also not behavioural or usage segmentation, which are ways of grouping buyers.","trap":"Several brands or models from one firm, each aimed at a different segment = product differentiation. Don't be pulled toward 'behavioural segmentation' or 'usage segmentation'; those describe how buyers are grouped, not the strategy of offering distinct products.","mnemonic":"Different features for different folks.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-why-segment","topic":"t06-1","term":"Why segment markets (three reasons)","definition":"Marketers segment because (1) consumers have a range of different needs, (2) companies have limited amounts of money, and (3) marketers need clear consumer insights on their target markets.","explain":"One product rarely satisfies everyone, budgets can't reach everyone, and good decisions need a clear picture of who you serve. Segmenting focuses money on groups whose needs you understand and can meet. Movies made for different audiences are a classic illustration.","example":"A Saskatoon bakery with an $8,000 annual budget spends it all reaching nearby office workers instead of advertising city-wide.","contrast":"These are reasons TO segment. Don't mix them up with the five criteria for FORMING segments (e.g., similarity within, difference among) or the five criteria for SELECTING target segments (e.g., market size, expected growth).","trap":"A distractor may present a forming criterion ('similarity of needs within a segment') or a selecting criterion ('market size') as a reason to segment. The three reasons are needs, money and insights.","mnemonic":"Needs differ, Money is limited, Insight matters (N-M-I).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Three reasons to segment","items":["Consumers have a range of different needs","Companies have limited amounts of money","Marketers need clear consumer insights on their target markets"]}},{"id":"c06-continuum","topic":"t06-2","term":"Target-market continuum: mass, segment, niche, individualized marketing","definition":"Approaches to segmentation range from broad to narrow: mass (undifferentiated) marketing uses one product and one marketing mix for the entire market; segment (differentiated) marketing designs different offerings for different segments; niche marketing focuses on one or a few narrowly defined, specialized segments; individualized (one-to-one) marketing tailors offerings to individual customers, often through mass customization (tailoring goods or services to individual tastes on a high-volume scale).","explain":"Picture a dial that runs from 'everyone' to 'just you'. Mass marketing treats the market as one big group. Segment marketing serves several groups with different offerings. Niche marketing zooms in on a small, specialized group. Individualized marketing treats each customer as a segment of one, and mass customization does this at high volume through build-to-order.","example":"Table salt sold the same way to everyone (mass); a hotel company with budget, business and luxury brands (segment); racing bikes only for competitive cyclists over 50 (niche); sneakers built to each buyer's chosen design (mass customization).","contrast":"Niche = one or a few narrow segments, which are still groups of people. Individualized marketing / mass customization = segments of one (each customer). Segment marketing serves several segments; mass marketing makes no distinction between segments.","trap":"'Build-to-order', 'each customer designs their own', 'high volume' = mass customization, not niche. 'Small, specialized group' = niche. 'One product, one mix, entire market' = mass. 'Differentiated marketing' is another name for segment marketing.","mnemonic":"Mass > Segment > Niche > Individual (Many, Some, Few, One).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Broad to narrow target-market continuum","items":["Mass marketing (undifferentiated): one product and one marketing mix for the entire market","Segment marketing (differentiated): different offerings for different segments","Niche marketing: one or a few narrowly defined, specialized segments","Individualized (one-to-one) marketing / mass customization: tailored to individual customers on a high-volume scale"]}},{"id":"c06-when-to-segment","topic":"t06-2","term":"When and how to segment","definition":"A firm segments its market only when doing so increases sales revenue, profit and ROI; if the added expenses more than offset the added revenue, it should not segment further. The ways to segment are one product with multiple market segments, multiple products with multiple market segments, and segments of one (mass customization).","explain":"Segmenting is a business decision, not an automatic good. One product for multiple segments (e.g., a TV show or magazine that several audiences enjoy) is cheap, because the main extra cost is promotion. Multiple products for multiple segments (different models for different groups) costs more but fits each segment better. Segments of one take this down to the individual customer. Stop when the extra cost outweighs the extra revenue.","example":"A family TV drama that appeals to young children, teens and older adults is one product serving multiple segments; a carmaker selling a hatchback, a minivan and a luxury sedan to different groups is using multiple products for multiple segments.","contrast":"One product/multiple segments = same product, different promotion (low cost). Multiple products/multiple segments = different products (higher cost, better fit). Segments of one = mass customization.","trap":"If a stem says added expenses exceed added revenue, the answer is 'do not segment further', not layoffs, cutting quality or dropping products. A single TV show or magazine aimed at several groups is ONE product with multiple segments, not mass customization.","mnemonic":"Keep segmenting only while revenue gained > cost added.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Ways to segment and when to stop","head":["Approach","What it means","Cost and fit"],"rows":[["One product, multiple market segments","The same product is marketed to several segments (e.g., a TV show or magazine)","Low added cost; mainly extra promotion"],["Multiple products, multiple market segments","Different products or models for different segments","Higher costs, but a better fit with each segment"],["Segments of one","Mass customization: tailoring to individual customers","Narrowest approach, done on a high-volume scale"],["The rule","Segment only if it increases sales revenue, profit and ROI","If added expenses more than offset added revenue, do not segment further"]]}},{"id":"c06-forming-criteria","topic":"t06-2","term":"Criteria for forming segments","definition":"Five criteria for forming market segments: (1) potential for increased profit and ROI; (2) similarity of needs of potential buyers within a segment; (3) difference of needs of buyers among segments; (4) feasibility of a marketing action to reach a segment; (5) simplicity and cost of assigning potential buyers to segments.","explain":"These criteria test whether a proposed segment is worth creating at all. Buyers inside the segment should be alike, the segments should differ from each other, a marketing action must be able to reach the segment, it must be simple and cheap to tell who belongs, and the whole thing must pay off in profit and ROI.","example":"A restaurant considering a separate salad bar for the 2-3% of people mildly allergic to preservatives fails 'potential for increased profit and ROI', because the segment is small and a duplicate salad bar is costly.","contrast":"Forming criteria judge whether segments are well built (similar within, different among, reachable, easy to assign, profitable). SELECTING criteria (market size, expected growth, competitive position, cost of reaching, compatibility with objectives and resources) choose which of the formed segments to target.","trap":"'Difference AMONG' versus 'similarity WITHIN': reversed wording is a common distractor. 'Simplicity and cost of assigning' (can we tell who belongs?) is different from 'feasibility of a marketing action' (can we reach them?). Expected growth and market size belong on the SELECTING list.","mnemonic":"PSDFS: Profit, Same within, Different among, Feasible to reach, Simple to assign.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Five criteria for FORMING segments","items":["Potential for increased profit and return on investment (ROI)","Similarity of needs of potential buyers within a segment","Difference of needs of buyers among segments","Feasibility of a marketing action to reach a segment","Simplicity and cost of assigning potential buyers to segments"]}},{"id":"c06-selecting-criteria","topic":"t06-2","term":"Criteria for selecting target segments","definition":"Five criteria for choosing which segments to target: market size, expected growth, competitive position, cost of reaching the segment, and compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources.","explain":"After segments are formed, the firm compares them. A big, growing segment is attractive, but not if strong rivals dominate it, if reaching it is too expensive, or if serving it doesn't fit what the organization wants to do and is able to do.","example":"A Regina software firm passes on a large, fast-growing craft-brewery segment because serving it would need hardware-installation skills the firm lacks and doesn't fit its mission.","contrast":"Selecting criteria answer 'which segment should we target?'; forming criteria answer 'is this a properly formed segment?'. 'Cost of reaching the segment' (selecting) is not the same as 'simplicity and cost of assigning buyers to segments' (forming).","trap":"Distractors slip forming criteria (similarity within, difference among, feasibility, assigning) into a selecting question, and the reverse. The word 'cost' appears on both lists, so check whether it is the cost of REACHING (selecting) or of ASSIGNING (forming).","mnemonic":"Size, Growth, Competition, Cost, Compatibility (SGC-CC).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Five criteria for SELECTING target segments","items":["Market size","Expected growth","Competitive position","Cost of reaching the segment","Compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources"]}},{"id":"c06-consumer-variables","topic":"t06-3","term":"Consumer segmentation variables (four families)","definition":"Consumer markets are segmented using four families of variables: geographic (where does the target market live and work?), demographic (what is its basic census-type information?), psychographic (what attitudes, values, interests, habits and approaches to life does it share?) and behaviouristic (how does it use and interact with the product?).","explain":"Each family answers a different question about the customer. Real segments usually combine families, for example a region (geographic) plus a music preference (psychographic). Classify each piece of a description separately.","example":"'Women aged 25-34 (demographic) in suburban Calgary (geographic) who value sustainability (psychographic) and use a reusable cup five times a week (behaviouristic).'","contrast":"Demographic = statistical facts about who people are; psychographic = their inner life (personality, values, lifestyle, interests); behaviouristic = their relationship with the product (usage, benefits sought, loyalty, occasion); geographic = location and climate. Business markets add other bases, such as operating characteristics.","trap":"Stems often hide two families in one scenario (East vs West = geographic; liking country music = psychographic). Interests and music taste are psychographic, NOT demographic.","mnemonic":"Where, Who on paper, Who inside, How they use (G-D-P-B).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Segmentation variables in consumer markets","head":["Family","Guiding question","Example variables"],"rows":[["Geographic","Where does the target market live and work?","Region, city size, urban/suburban/rural, climate"],["Demographic","What is the basic census-type information on the target market as a whole?","Age, gender, income, education, occupation, ethnicity, family life cycle, household size, generation"],["Psychographic","What prevailing attitudes, values, interests, habits and approaches to life does the target market share?","Lifestyle (activities, interests, opinions), personality, values, attitudes"],["Behaviouristic","How does this target market use and interact with the product?","Benefits sought, usage rate, user status, loyalty status, purchase occasion"]]}},{"id":"c06-geographic","topic":"t06-3","term":"Geographic segmentation","definition":"Segmenting by where the target market lives and works, using variables such as region, city size, urban/suburban/rural location and climate.","explain":"Location shapes needs: winter climate drives snowblower sales, and tastes can differ between regions. Geographic variables help decide where to sell and where to concentrate distribution and advertising.","example":"A hardware chain stocks more block heaters and snowblowers in Thunder Bay than in Victoria, BC, because of the climate.","contrast":"Geographic is about place (region, city size, climate). It is not demographic (age, income), even though census data often reports both together.","trap":"'Eastern vs Western Canada', 'urban vs rural', 'cold climates' = geographic. If the stem also mentions tastes or interests, the answer is often a two-family combination.","mnemonic":"Geo = where on the globe.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-demographic","topic":"t06-3","term":"Demographic segmentation","definition":"Segmenting by basic census-type information about the target market: age, gender, income, education, occupation, ethnicity, family life cycle, household size and generation.","explain":"Demographics are the easy-to-measure statistical facts about who customers are. They are widely available and often the starting point, but people with identical demographics can still differ greatly in values and behaviour.","example":"A daycare-supply company targets households with children under five and incomes over $90,000.","contrast":"Demographic = who you are on paper (age, income, life stage). Psychographic = who you are inside (personality, values, lifestyle). 'Generation' (e.g., Gen Z) is demographic; 'adventurous' is psychographic.","trap":"Lifestyle and interests are NOT demographic. Family life cycle and household size ARE demographic, even though they sound like lifestyle.","mnemonic":"Demographic = data from a census form.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-psychographic","topic":"t06-3","term":"Psychographic segmentation","definition":"Segmenting by the prevailing attitudes, values, interests, habits and approaches to life a target market shares, including lifestyle (activities, interests and opinions, or AIO), personality and values.","explain":"Psychographics capture the 'why' inside the customer. Two people of the same age and income may buy very differently because one is a thrill-seeker and the other a careful planner. Music preference, a collector's personality and personal values all count as psychographic.","example":"Nike targets 'keepers': people who buy Air Jordans, keep them under the bed and show them off to friends. That is a personality/lifestyle segment.","contrast":"Psychographic (personality, values, lifestyle, interests) vs behaviouristic (how people use the product: usage rate, benefits sought, loyalty, occasion, user status) vs demographic (age, income, gender).","trap":"'Keepers', 'collectors', 'adventurous', 'country-music fans', 'value sustainability' = psychographic. Don't pick 'behaviour' or 'usage' just because the stem describes something people do; ask whether it reflects personality and lifestyle or how the product is used.","mnemonic":"Psycho-graphic = a graph of the psyche (AIO + personality + values).","figure":null},{"id":"c06-behaviouristic","topic":"t06-3","term":"Behaviouristic segmentation","definition":"Segmenting by how the target market uses and interacts with the product: product features or benefits sought, usage rate, user status, loyalty status and purchase occasion.","explain":"Behaviouristic variables look at the customer's relationship with the product itself: what benefit they want from it, how much they use it, whether they are a non-user, first-time user or ex-user, how loyal they are, and when or why they buy.","example":"A toothpaste brand offers whitening, cavity-protection and sensitivity versions, which is segmentation by benefits sought.","contrast":"Behaviouristic = product use and interaction. Psychographic = personality, values and lifestyle in general. 'Buys only our brand' (loyalty status) is behaviouristic, even though it sounds like an attitude.","trap":"Benefits sought, user status, loyalty, occasion and usage rate are all behaviouristic. 'Ex-user / first-time user' is user status, not loyalty. 'Valentine's Day buyers' is purchase occasion.","mnemonic":"B-U-U-L-O: Benefits sought, Usage rate, User status, Loyalty status, Occasion.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-usage-rate","topic":"t06-3","term":"Usage rate and the 80/20 rule","definition":"Usage rate is the quantity consumed or patronage (store visits) during a specific period. The 80/20 rule is a concept that suggests 80 percent of a firm's sales are obtained from 20 percent of its customers.","explain":"Usage rate sorts customers into heavy, medium, light and non-users. Because a small group of heavy users often produces most of the sales, firms protect and reward them, which is why frequency and loyalty programs exist.","example":"If a pizzeria's sales follow the 80/20 rule, its top 400 of 2,000 customers produce $400,000 of its $500,000 in sales.","contrast":"Usage rate (how much or how often) differs from loyalty status (how committed a buyer is to one brand). The 80/20 rule links a share of CUSTOMERS (20%) to a share of SALES (80%).","trap":"Watch for flipped numbers: '80% of sales from 80% of customers' is wrong, while 'the other 80% of customers produce about 20% of sales' is simply the complement and is consistent with the rule. Usage rate is behaviouristic, not demographic or psychographic.","mnemonic":"A few heavy users carry the load: 20 percent of people, 80 percent of sales.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-business-segmentation","topic":"t06-3","term":"Segmenting business (B2B) markets","definition":"Business markets can be segmented by demographics (NAICS industry code, number of employees, sales, location) plus additional bases: operating characteristics (technology, user status, capabilities), purchasing approaches (centralized vs decentralized buying, buying criteria, relationships), situational factors (urgency, order size, specific application) and personal characteristics (buyer-seller similarity, attitude toward risk, loyalty).","explain":"Organizations buy differently from consumers, so B2B marketers add bases that describe how a company operates and buys. These help match sales approaches, service levels and products to each type of business customer.","example":"A Calgary pipe-fitting distributor offers premium rush delivery to oil-field clients who need emergency parts. That is segmentation by situational factors (urgency, order size).","contrast":"Operating characteristics = what the company uses and can do; purchasing approaches = how it buys; situational factors = the circumstances of the order; personal characteristics = traits of the individual buyers (risk attitude, similarity, loyalty). Lifestyle (AIO) is a consumer psychographic variable, not a B2B base.","trap":"Urgency and order size = situational factors, not purchasing approaches. Centralized vs decentralized buying = purchasing approaches. User status sits under operating characteristics in B2B, but is behaviouristic in consumer markets.","mnemonic":"D-O-P-S-P: Demographics, Operating, Purchasing, Situational, Personal.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Bases for segmenting business markets","head":["Base","Example variables"],"rows":[["Demographics","NAICS industry code, number of employees, annual sales, location"],["Operating characteristics","Technology, user status, capabilities"],["Purchasing approaches","Centralized vs decentralized buying, buying criteria, buyer-seller relationships"],["Situational factors","Urgency, order size, specific application"],["Personal characteristics","Buyer-seller similarity, attitude toward risk, loyalty"]]}},{"id":"c06-personas","topic":"t06-4","term":"Target market profiles and personas","definition":"A target market profile is a detailed description of the typical customer (demographics, psychographics, media habits and needs). Personas are character descriptions of a brand's typical customer, often with a name, photo and back-story.","explain":"Personas turn segment data into a believable person, so everyone on the team designs products, messages and media plans for 'someone' real. A persona might be 'Kayla, the Planner', complete with her age, job, goals, worries and favourite apps.","example":"Bow River Bank's persona 'Kayla, the Planner': 29, works in HR in Calgary, saving for a condo, reads personal-finance blogs, values security.","contrast":"A segment is a GROUP of buyers; a persona is a single fictional CHARACTER representing its typical member. A perceptual map plots brands, not customers.","trap":"A named, photographed, story-like description of one 'typical customer' = persona. A persona doesn't replace segmentation; it is built from it.","mnemonic":"Persona = a person standing in for the segment.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-segmentation-analytics","topic":"t06-4","term":"Segmentation analytics","definition":"Research companies such as Nielsen or Euromonitor provide general consumer and lifestyle trend data, while companies such as Environics Analytics (PRIZM) provide more granular data that pinpoints population clusters (e.g., 'Middle-Class Mosaic').","explain":"Marketers buy data to build segments. Broad trend reports show where the overall market is heading; cluster systems like PRIZM sort neighbourhoods into named lifestyle groups down to small geographic areas, which helps with store locations and local targeting.","example":"A Toronto retailer checks which PRIZM clusters live within 5 km of a proposed store before signing a lease.","contrast":"Nielsen / Euromonitor = general, broad consumer and lifestyle trends. Environics Analytics / PRIZM = granular, pinpointed population clusters.","trap":"'Granular', 'population clusters', 'neighbourhood', or named clusters like 'Middle-Class Mosaic' point to Environics/PRIZM. 'General trends' points to Nielsen or Euromonitor.","mnemonic":"Environics = Exact neighbourhoods; Nielsen/Euromonitor = the big picture.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-six-step-process","topic":"t06-4","term":"Six-step process for segmenting a market","definition":"(1) Identify consumer/customer needs and common characteristics in the market; (2) cluster common consumer/customer variables to create meaningful market segments; (3) estimate the size and feasibility of each segment; (4) identify the segment(s) to be targeted; (5) take actions with marketing programs to reach the segment(s); (6) monitor and evaluate the success of these programs compared with objectives.","explain":"The steps move from understanding (needs) to grouping (clusters) to sizing (how big and how realistic) to choosing (targets) to doing (marketing programs) to checking (results against objectives). Each step needs the output of the one before it.","example":"A Kelowna winery identifies buyers' needs, groups them into four segments, sizes each with census and sales data, picks two to target, launches campaigns, then compares results with its sales objectives.","contrast":"Estimating size and feasibility (step 3) comes BEFORE choosing targets (step 4). Taking action (step 5) comes before monitoring (step 6). Clustering (step 2) is the segmentation step; step 4 is targeting.","trap":"Watch 'immediately after' questions: after clustering comes estimating size and feasibility, not targeting. 'Compared with objectives' always signals step 6.","mnemonic":"I Can Estimate If Teams Measure (Identify, Cluster, Estimate, Identify targets, Take action, Monitor).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Six-step process for segmenting a market","items":["1. Identify consumer/customer needs and common characteristics in the market","2. Cluster common consumer/customer variables to create meaningful market segments","3. Estimate the size and feasibility of each segment","4. Identify the segment(s) to be targeted","5. Take actions with marketing programs to reach the segment(s)","6. Monitor and evaluate the success of these programs compared with objectives"]}},{"id":"c06-market-product-grid","topic":"t06-4","term":"Market-product grid","definition":"A framework relating the market segments of potential buyers (rows) to the products offered or potential marketing actions (columns); each cell shows the estimated size of the market.","explain":"The grid shows at a glance which product-segment combinations are big enough to pursue. Large cells suggest where to focus; small or empty cells suggest where not to.","example":"Polar Bikes lists commuters, mountain riders and kids as rows and its bike models as columns, writing the estimated market size in each cell.","contrast":"Market-product grid = segments x products, with market-size estimates. Perceptual map = brands plotted by consumer perception on two attributes. Persona = a description of one typical customer.","trap":"Rows and columns of segments and products, or 'estimated market size in each cell', means a market-product grid. Two perceptual dimensions means a perceptual map.","mnemonic":"Grid = Segments down, Products across, Size inside.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-ethics","topic":"t06-4","term":"Socially responsible and ethical segmentation","definition":"Segmentation and targeting raise legal and ethical issues: marketers must avoid consumer backlash and take special care with vulnerable groups, disadvantaged groups and potentially harmful products. Social responsibility can be more important than profits if marketing is viewed as exploitative or irresponsible.","explain":"A profitable segment is not automatically one you should target. Aiming risky products at children, or high-cost offers at people in financial hardship, can cause harm, public outrage and long-term damage to the brand.","example":"An energy-drink brand decides not to target 13- to 15-year-olds even though that segment is growing fast.","contrast":"An ethical screen asks 'SHOULD we target this group?'; the selecting criteria ask 'is this group ATTRACTIVE?'. A segment can pass the size and growth criteria and still be inappropriate to target.","trap":"Know all four slide items: consumer backlash, vulnerable groups (e.g., children), disadvantaged groups (e.g., low-income people) and potentially harmful products. The course says responsibility can outweigh profits when marketing looks exploitative.","mnemonic":"Back-off list: Backlash, Vulnerable, Disadvantaged, Harmful.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-product-positioning","topic":"t06-5","term":"Product positioning","definition":"The impression of the branded product a marketer wants to establish in consumers' minds relative to their needs and in contrast to the competition; that is, the place a product occupies in consumers' minds on important attributes relative to competitive products.","explain":"Positioning happens in the customer's head; the marketer's job is to shape it. It rests on three basic factors (image, product attributes and price), and every element of the marketing mix must support it. Once established, the position must be maintained through consistent performance and communication.","example":"Chiquita put its brand on bananas, a commodity, and promoted them as consistently high-quality fruit, giving the brand a distinct place in shoppers' minds.","contrast":"Positioning (the place in consumers' minds) vs product differentiation (using features and advertising to make products seem different and better) vs repositioning (changing a position that already exists).","trap":"A mix decision that contradicts the position (a premium brand sold in discount bins) is a positioning failure. Market size and expected growth are targeting criteria, not positioning factors.","mnemonic":"IAP: Image, Attributes, Price, with every mix arrow pointing the same way.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Keys to successful positioning","items":["All marketing mix efforts must support the positioning strategy","Maintain the position through consistent performance and communication","Build on three basic factors: image, product attributes, price"]}},{"id":"c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation","topic":"t06-5","term":"Head-to-head vs differentiation positioning","definition":"Head-to-head positioning means competing directly with competitors on similar product attributes in the same target market. Differentiation positioning means seeking a less-competitive, smaller market niche in which to locate a brand by stressing its unique attributes.","explain":"Head-to-head is a direct fight: same customers, same attributes, 'we're better, faster or cheaper'. Differentiation positioning steps away from the crowd: find a small niche where your unique features matter and big rivals aren't fighting.","example":"Children's Advil claiming it works faster and longer than Children's Tylenol (head-to-head); BMW's $495 Streetcarver skateboard with unique car-inspired stabilizers (differentiation positioning).","contrast":"Head-to-head = same market, similar attributes, direct comparison. Differentiation positioning = smaller, less-competitive niche, unique attributes. Product differentiation (several distinct products for different segments) is a different idea.","trap":"Comparison claims against a named rival on the same attribute = head-to-head. 'The only one with...' plus a small premium niche = differentiation positioning. Options like 'lateral', 'cognitive', 'distinction' or 'parallel market' positioning are not course terms.","mnemonic":"Head-to-head = nose to nose; differentiation = find your own corner.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-positioning-statement","topic":"t06-5","term":"Positioning statement","definition":"A formalized statement that identifies the image a branded product represents in the market and what sets it apart from the competition. Template: 'For (target market) who desire (target market need), (brand) is the (product category) that offers (product benefits).'","explain":"The statement is an internal guide that keeps everyone aligned on whom the brand serves, what need it meets, what category it competes in and which benefits set it apart. It is usually not the ad slogan itself.","example":"For busy Calgary parents who desire healthy dinners in 15 minutes, FreshFork is the meal-kit service that offers pre-chopped, dietitian-approved ingredients.","contrast":"Positioning statement = a full sentence with target, need, brand, category and benefits. USP = the single competitive advantage the positioning may rest on. Slogan = a short consumer-facing line.","trap":"Know the template's five slots in order: target market, need ('who desire'), brand, product category, product benefits. If the 'who desire' clause is missing, the need is missing.","mnemonic":"For WHO, who WANT what, BRAND is the CATEGORY that GIVES benefits.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-perceptual-map","topic":"t06-5","term":"Perceptual map","definition":"A means of displaying or graphing, in two dimensions, the location of products or brands in the minds of consumers.","explain":"Researchers ask consumers how they see competing brands on two important attributes (e.g., price and luxury) and plot the results. The map shows which brands compete most closely, where gaps exist and where a brand might move. Comparative claims only work against brands consumers already see as worse on the chosen attribute.","example":"On a drinks map with 'nutritious' on the vertical axis, coffee sits above sugared soft drinks but below skinny lattes and fruit-flavoured drinks, so a 'healthier than' coffee ad should compare coffee only with sugared soft drinks.","contrast":"Perceptual map = the tool that shows perceived positions. Repositioning = the strategy of changing a brand's place (a move on the map). Market-product grid = segments x products with market sizes.","trap":"On comparison questions, pick only the brands positioned LOWER than yours on the relevant axis. Two dimensions + consumer perceptions = perceptual map, not market-product grid.","mnemonic":"Map the mind on two lines.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-repositioning","topic":"t06-5","term":"Repositioning","definition":"Changing the place a product occupies in consumers' minds relative to competitive products, to more accurately meet consumer needs.","explain":"Repositioning is for products that already have a position that no longer works: the image is dated, the use is too narrow, or needs have shifted. The brand gives consumers new reasons, uses or associations, moving its dot on the perceptual map.","example":"Post Grape-Nuts, seen as 'something grandfather ate', promoted the cereal as a crunchy topping for yogurt, salad and soup, expanding its use from breakfast to all day.","contrast":"Positioning creates the initial place; repositioning changes an existing one. A perceptual map is the tool that can show the move, not the strategy itself.","trap":"Declining share + dated image + new uses or a new audience = repositioning, not 'product positioning' or 'perceptual mapping'. A brand-new product's first image is positioning.","mnemonic":"RE-position = position AGAIN.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-pop-pod","topic":"t06-5","term":"Points-of-parity vs points-of-difference","definition":"Points-of-parity (POPs) are attribute or benefit associations that are not necessarily unique to the brand but may be shared with other brands. Points-of-difference (PODs) are attributes or benefits consumers strongly associate with a brand, positively evaluate, and believe they could not find to the same extent with a competitive brand.","explain":"POPs are the basics a brand needs in order to be considered in its category: its 'ticket to play'. PODs are why customers choose it over rivals: its 'reason to win'. A POD must pass all three tests: strong association, positive evaluation, and not available to the same extent elsewhere.","example":"The course uses Sobeys to illustrate POPs (features shared with other grocers) and Tim Hortons to illustrate PODs (associations Canadians feel no rival matches).","contrast":"POP = shared and expected (ticket to play). POD = unique, valued and hard to match (reason to win). A well-liked attribute that every competitor also offers is still a POP.","trap":"'Shared with other brands' = POP, even if customers love it. 'Could not find to the same extent with a competitor' = POD. A USP is a single strong POD used as the basis for positioning.","mnemonic":"Parity = par for the course; Difference = distinctive.","figure":null},{"id":"c06-usp","topic":"t06-5","term":"Unique selling proposition (USP)","definition":"When choosing a competitive advantage on which to base positioning, a firm can build on one unique selling proposition (USP), a single distinctive benefit that sets the brand apart, or on multiple differences.","explain":"A USP is the one clear reason to buy that the brand repeats everywhere. It should be something customers value and competitors can't easily match; in effect, a single strong point-of-difference. Some brands instead position on several differences at once.","example":"Swift Pizza's only message, on every ad and box: 'Hot at your door in 20 minutes or it's free.'","contrast":"USP = one distinctive advantage. Multiple-differences positioning = several advantages. Positioning statement = the full template sentence. POP = a shared attribute, which makes a poor USP.","trap":"An attribute every competitor offers equally well can't be a USP (it's a POP). 'One single reason or benefit, promoted consistently' = USP.","mnemonic":"USP = Unique, Single, Promoted.","figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"a06-01","type":"order","concept":"c06-six-step-process","title":"Order the six segmentation steps","prompt":"Put the six steps for segmenting a market in the correct order.","items":["Identify consumer/customer needs and common characteristics in the market","Cluster common consumer/customer variables to create meaningful market segments","Estimate the size and feasibility of each segment","Identify the segment(s) to be targeted","Take actions with marketing programs to reach the segment(s)","Monitor and evaluate the success of these programs compared with objectives"],"explanation":"You must understand needs before you can cluster buyers, and size the clusters before you can sensibly pick targets. Only after choosing targets can you build marketing programs, and you can only evaluate those programs against objectives after they run."},{"id":"a06-02","type":"sort","concept":"c06-consumer-variables","title":"Sort the segmentation variables","prompt":"Drag each variable into the consumer segmentation family it belongs to.","buckets":["Geographic","Demographic","Psychographic","Behaviouristic"],"items":[{"text":"Climate (snowy vs mild winters)","bucket":0},{"text":"Urban, suburban or rural location","bucket":0},{"text":"City size","bucket":0},{"text":"Household income","bucket":1},{"text":"Family life cycle","bucket":1},{"text":"Generation (e.g., Gen Z)","bucket":1},{"text":"Personality (e.g., Nike's 'keepers')","bucket":2},{"text":"Lifestyle: activities, interests and opinions","bucket":2},{"text":"Values and attitudes","bucket":2},{"text":"Usage rate (heavy, medium, light users)","bucket":3},{"text":"Benefits sought","bucket":3},{"text":"Purchase occasion (e.g., Valentine's Day)","bucket":3}],"explanation":"Geographic = where people live and work; demographic = census-type facts such as income, life stage and generation; psychographic = personality, values and lifestyle (AIO); behaviouristic = how people use and interact with the product (usage rate, benefits sought, occasion, loyalty, user status)."},{"id":"a06-03","type":"match","concept":"c06-product-positioning","title":"Match the positioning terms","prompt":"Match each positioning term with its best example or description.","pairs":[{"left":"Head-to-head positioning","right":"Children's Advil claims it works faster and longer than Children's Tylenol"},{"left":"Differentiation positioning","right":"BMW Streetcarver: the only skateboard with car-inspired stabilizers, sold to a small premium niche"},{"left":"Repositioning","right":"Grape-Nuts promoted as a topping for yogurt, salad and soup"},{"left":"Perceptual map","right":"A two-dimensional graph of where brands sit in consumers' minds"},{"left":"Point-of-parity","right":"An attribute shared with competing brands, such as a grocer's loyalty card"},{"left":"Point-of-difference","right":"An attribute consumers strongly link to the brand, value, and can't find to the same extent elsewhere"},{"left":"Unique selling proposition","right":"One single distinctive advantage the positioning is built on"},{"left":"Positioning statement","right":"For (target market) who desire (need), (brand) is the (category) that offers (benefits)"}],"explanation":"Head-to-head attacks rivals on the same attributes in the same market, while differentiation positioning moves to a less-competitive niche. Repositioning changes an existing position, and a perceptual map is the tool that displays positions. POPs are shared, PODs are unique and valued, a USP is one chosen advantage, and the positioning statement puts the whole position into one formal sentence."},{"id":"a06-04","type":"sort","concept":"c06-selecting-criteria","title":"Forming vs selecting criteria","prompt":"Is each item a criterion for FORMING segments or for SELECTING target segments?","buckets":["Criterion for FORMING segments","Criterion for SELECTING target segments"],"items":[{"text":"Potential for increased profit and ROI","bucket":0},{"text":"Similarity of needs of potential buyers within a segment","bucket":0},{"text":"Difference of needs of buyers among segments","bucket":0},{"text":"Feasibility of a marketing action to reach a segment","bucket":0},{"text":"Simplicity and cost of assigning potential buyers to segments","bucket":0},{"text":"Market size","bucket":1},{"text":"Expected growth","bucket":1},{"text":"Competitive position","bucket":1},{"text":"Cost of reaching the segment","bucket":1},{"text":"Compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources","bucket":1}],"explanation":"Forming criteria check whether a segment is properly built: profitable, similar within, different among, reachable by a marketing action, and easy and cheap to assign buyers to. Selecting criteria compare formed segments to choose targets: size, growth, competition, cost of reaching, and fit with objectives and resources. Note the two different 'cost' items: assigning (forming) vs reaching (selecting)."},{"id":"a06-05","type":"order","concept":"c06-continuum","title":"Broad to narrow targeting","prompt":"Order these approaches from the broadest target market to the narrowest.","items":["Mass marketing","Segment marketing","Niche marketing","Individualized (one-to-one) marketing"],"explanation":"Mass marketing treats the whole market as one group; segment marketing serves several segments with different offerings; niche marketing focuses on one or a few narrow, specialized segments; individualized marketing (often mass customization) tailors offerings to each customer, a segment of one."},{"id":"a06-06","type":"match","concept":"c06-business-segmentation","title":"Match the B2B segmentation bases","prompt":"Match each business-market segmentation base with its example variables.","pairs":[{"left":"Demographics","right":"NAICS industry code, number of employees, location"},{"left":"Operating characteristics","right":"Technology used, user status, capabilities"},{"left":"Purchasing approaches","right":"Centralized vs decentralized buying, buying criteria"},{"left":"Situational factors","right":"Urgency, order size, specific application"},{"left":"Personal characteristics","right":"Buyer-seller similarity, attitude toward risk, loyalty"}],"explanation":"Business demographics describe the firm itself; operating characteristics describe what it uses and can do; purchasing approaches describe how it buys; situational factors describe the circumstances of an order; personal characteristics describe the individual buyers."}],"questions":[{"id":"q06-001","concept":"c06-stp-framework","also":["c06-market-segments"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northline Outfitters, an Edmonton outdoor-gear retailer, has already divided Alberta shoppers into groups such as weekend campers, backcountry skiers and urban commuters. Its managers are now meeting to decide which one or two of these groups the company will actually pursue. Which step of the STP framework are they performing?","options":["Targeting","Segmentation","Differentiation","Positioning"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Targeting is selecting the segment or segments to enter. The dividing (segmentation) is already done, and differentiation and positioning come only after the targets are chosen.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-002","concept":"c06-stp-framework","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the customer-driven marketing strategy model, which two steps make up the decision to 'select customers to serve'?","options":["Differentiation and positioning","Segmentation and positioning","Segmentation and targeting","Targeting and differentiation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Selecting customers to serve = segmentation (divide the total market into smaller segments) + targeting (select the segment or segments to enter). Differentiation and positioning together make up the other decision: deciding on a value proposition.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-003","concept":"c06-stp-framework","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pairing correctly matches an STP step with what it does?","options":["Positioning: dividing the total market into smaller groups of buyers","Targeting: selecting the segment or segments to enter","Differentiation: placing the offering in the minds of target customers","Segmentation: making the offering different to create superior value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Targeting = selecting the segment(s) to enter. The other pairings swap definitions: dividing the market is segmentation, placing the offering in customers' minds is positioning, and making the offering different to create superior value is differentiation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-004","concept":"c06-stp-framework","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Calgary meal-kit start-up is working through the four steps of the customer-driven marketing strategy model in the order the course presents them. It has segmented the market and has just chosen young professional couples as its target. Which step comes next?","options":["Positioning","Differentiation","Segmentation","Mass customization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The model runs segmentation -> targeting -> differentiation -> positioning. After choosing a target, the firm decides how to differentiate its offering to create superior value, and then positions that offering in target customers' minds. Positioning is tempting, but it comes after differentiation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-005","concept":"c06-stp-framework","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"At the centre of the customer-driven marketing strategy model, what overall goal do segmentation, targeting, differentiation and positioning all serve?","options":["Create value for targeted customers","Maximize market share across the total market","Minimize the cost of reaching every possible buyer","Offer the largest possible product line"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The centre of the model reads 'create value for targeted customers'. STP deliberately focuses on chosen segments instead of trying to win the total market or reach every buyer as cheaply as possible.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-006","concept":"c06-market-segmentation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Aggregating prospective buyers into groups that have common needs and will respond similarly to a marketing action is called:","options":["targeting","product positioning","product differentiation","market segmentation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"This is the textbook definition of market segmentation. Targeting comes afterward (choosing which group to pursue), and product differentiation is the strategy that segmentation leads to.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-007","concept":"c06-market-segmentation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Rink Supply groups its customers into hockey parents, adult rec-league players and elite junior players. People within each group need similar gear and react the same way to price promotions, while the groups differ from one another. The company has not yet decided which group to focus on. What is it doing?","options":["Targeting","Market segmentation","Positioning","Mass marketing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Grouping buyers who have common needs and respond similarly to marketing is market segmentation. It is not targeting yet, because no group has been chosen.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-008","concept":"c06-market-segmentation","also":["c06-product-differentiation"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the course, what does market segmentation ultimately lead to?","options":["Mass marketing","Repositioning","A single marketing mix for all buyers","Product differentiation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Once buyers are grouped by their distinct needs, the firm can offer different features and marketing to each group, which is product differentiation. Mass marketing and a single marketing mix are the opposite of segmentation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-009","concept":"c06-market-segmentation","also":["c06-stp-framework"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A classmate says, 'Segmentation and targeting are the same thing; both are about picking the customers you sell to.' Which correction is best?","options":["Segmentation chooses the group to pursue; targeting divides the market into groups","Segmentation divides the market into groups; targeting chooses which group(s) to pursue","Segmentation places the brand in consumers' minds; targeting divides the market","Segmentation sets the price for each group; targeting designs the product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Segmentation forms groups of buyers with common needs; targeting selects the segment(s) to enter. The reversed version is the classic distractor, and placing a brand in consumers' minds is positioning.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-010","concept":"c06-market-segmentation","also":["c06-continuum"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about market segmentation is NOT correct?","options":["It groups buyers who have common needs","It groups buyers who will respond similarly to a marketing action","It produces relatively homogeneous groups of buyers","It requires the firm to use one marketing mix for the entire market"],"answer":3,"explanation":"One marketing mix for the entire market is mass (undifferentiated) marketing, the opposite of segmentation. Common needs, a similar response and relatively homogeneous groups all describe segmentation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-011","concept":"c06-market-segments","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The specific group of existing and potential consumers to which marketers direct their marketing efforts is called the:","options":["market segment","persona","market-product grid","target market"],"answer":3,"explanation":"That is the definition of a target market. A market segment is any relatively homogeneous group identified through segmentation; the target market is the segment(s) the firm chooses to aim at. A persona describes one typical customer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-012","concept":"c06-market-segments","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Fjord Kayak Co. identifies five groups of paddlers in Western Canada. After analysis, it directs all of its advertising and product development at 'retired outdoor enthusiasts on Vancouver Island', both current customers and people who have never bought from it. That chosen group is Fjord's:","options":["target market","persona","segment of one","point-of-difference"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The group a firm chooses to direct its marketing efforts toward, including existing and potential customers, is its target market. A persona is a character description of one typical customer, not the group itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-013","concept":"c06-market-segments","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the key difference between a market segment and a target market?","options":["A segment includes only existing customers, while a target market includes only potential customers","A segment is any relatively homogeneous group identified in the market; a target market is the segment(s) the firm chooses to pursue","A segment is chosen by the firm, while a target market is identified by outside research companies","A segment is defined only by geographic variables, while a target market is defined only by psychographics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Segmentation can identify many segments; the target market is the one(s) selected for marketing efforts. Both can contain existing and potential customers, and either can be defined by any variable family.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-014","concept":"c06-market-segments","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Members of a well-defined market segment should share all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["similar consumption behaviour patterns","identical purchase histories with the firm","similar attitudes toward the product category","similar target market profiles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Segments are relatively homogeneous, with similar consumption behaviour, attitudes and target market profiles; they are not identical. Many members may never have bought from the firm at all, because segments include potential buyers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-015","concept":"c06-market-segments","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"After grouping Edmonton pet owners into four segments, Pawsitive Foods' managers ask what would turn one of these segments into their target market. The correct answer is that the segment:","options":["is the group the firm chooses to direct its marketing efforts toward","has the largest number of people of any segment in the market","has been plotted on a perceptual map against competing brands","contains only consumers who have already purchased from the firm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A segment becomes a target market when the firm chooses it and directs its marketing efforts toward it. Size alone doesn't make it a target (size is only one selecting criterion), and target markets include potential as well as current customers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-016","concept":"c06-product-differentiation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a Hallmark store you can find several different brands of greeting cards, including Fresh Ink, Nature's Sketchbook, Shoebox, Maxine, Mahogany, and Tree of Life cards - all made by Hallmark for sale in its stores. Hallmark uses a _____ strategy to reach several different market segments.","options":["product sampling","product extrapolation","behavioural segmentation","usage segmentation","product differentiation"],"answer":4,"explanation":"A firm selling two or more products with different features targeted to different market segments is using product differentiation. Behavioural and usage segmentation are ways of grouping buyers, not the strategy of offering a distinct brand to each group.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-017","concept":"c06-product-differentiation","also":["c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation"],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"A strategy that uses different marketing mix activities, such as product features and advertising, to help consumers perceive a product as being different from and better than competing products is:","options":["differentiation positioning","repositioning","product differentiation","niche marketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"This is the definition of product differentiation. Differentiation positioning is narrower: it means locating one brand in a smaller, less-competitive niche by stressing its unique attributes.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-018","concept":"c06-product-differentiation","also":["c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Rocky Ridge Brewing sells three beers with different recipes and labels: a light lager for young adults, a hoppy IPA for craft enthusiasts and a non-alcoholic brew for health-conscious drinkers. A classmate calls this 'differentiation positioning.' Why is 'product differentiation' the better label?","options":["Differentiation positioning applies only to services, while product differentiation applies only to physical goods","Differentiation positioning requires comparison ads against a named rival brand, which Rocky Ridge is not running","Differentiation positioning can only be used after a product has already been repositioned in consumers' minds","Distinct products aimed at different segments is product differentiation; differentiation positioning puts one brand in a niche"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Several products with different features aimed at different segments = product differentiation, like Hallmark's card brands. Differentiation positioning is about where ONE brand sits: a smaller, less-competitive niche. Comparison ads against a named rival describe head-to-head positioning.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-019","concept":"c06-product-differentiation","also":["c06-market-segmentation"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does market segmentation naturally lead to product differentiation?","options":["Segmentation forces the firm to sell one product to all buyers at the lowest possible price","Segmentation removes competitors from each segment, so products no longer need to differ","Grouping buyers by distinct needs lets the firm tailor features and marketing so each group sees its offering as better","Differentiation must come first, because segments cannot be identified until products already differ"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Different segments have different needs, so the firm tailors features and advertising to each one, which is product differentiation. Selling one product to everyone is mass marketing, and differentiation follows segmentation rather than coming before it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-020","concept":"c06-product-differentiation","also":["c06-continuum"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of product differentiation?","options":["A salt producer sells one product in one package with one ad campaign to all Canadians","A cereal maker offers a high-fibre line for adults and a sweetened line for kids, each with its own ads","A phone maker sells a rugged model for trades workers and a slim model for students","A card company sells several card brands designed for different customer groups"],"answer":0,"explanation":"One product, one package and one campaign for everyone is mass (undifferentiated) marketing. The other examples offer different products or marketing to different segments, which is product differentiation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-021","concept":"c06-why-segment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three reasons for segmenting a market given in the course?","options":["Consumers have a range of different needs","Segmenting guarantees the firm faces no competitors in each segment","Companies have limited amounts of money","Marketers need clear consumer insights on their target markets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The three reasons are: consumers have different needs, companies have limited money, and marketers need clear consumer insights. Segmenting doesn't remove competition; competitive position is something firms still have to weigh when they select targets.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-022","concept":"c06-why-segment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aurora Skincare learns that teenagers want acne control, new mothers want gentle fragrance-free products and retirees want anti-aging benefits. No single all-purpose cream can satisfy such different wants, so it plans separate products. Which reason for segmenting does this situation most directly reflect?","options":["Companies have limited amounts of money","Marketers need clear consumer insights on their target markets","Consumers have a range of different needs","Segments must show potential for increased profit and ROI"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The core issue is that the groups want different things: consumers have a range of different needs. 'Potential for increased profit and ROI' is a criterion for forming segments, not one of the three reasons to segment.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-023","concept":"c06-why-segment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"A small Saskatoon bakery has a marketing budget of only $8,000 for the year. Instead of advertising city-wide, the owner spends it all reaching office workers within a few blocks of the shop. Which reason for segmenting best justifies this decision?","options":["Companies have limited amounts of money","Consumers have a range of different needs","Marketers need clear consumer insights on their target markets","Segments must differ in their needs from one another"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A limited budget can't reach everyone, so the bakery concentrates its spending on one group: companies have limited amounts of money. 'Segments must differ in their needs' is a forming criterion (difference among segments), not a reason to segment.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-024","concept":"c06-why-segment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before launching a new sparkling water, Kelso Beverages commissions research to learn exactly who buys flavoured water, why, where and how often, so that its product and advertising decisions rest on a clear picture of those buyers instead of guesswork. Which reason for segmenting does this best reflect?","options":["Marketers need clear consumer insights on their target markets","Companies have limited amounts of money","Consumers have a range of different needs","Segments must be large enough to justify a separate program"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Seeking a clear, research-based picture of buyers reflects the need for clear consumer insights on target markets. Segment size relates to the selecting criterion 'market size', not to the three reasons for segmenting.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-025","concept":"c06-why-segment","also":["c06-forming-criteria"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A manager lists 'similarity of needs of potential buyers within a segment' as one of the three reasons to segment a market. What is wrong with this?","options":["Nothing; it is the first of the three reasons to segment","It is a selecting criterion, and the selecting criteria are the same list as the reasons to segment","It is a step in positioning rather than in segmentation","It is a forming criterion; the reasons to segment are different needs, limited money and the need for consumer insights"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Similarity within a segment is one of the five criteria for FORMING segments. The three reasons TO segment are: consumers have different needs, companies have limited money, and marketers need clear consumer insights. The selecting criteria (size, growth and so on) are a third, separate list.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-026","concept":"c06-continuum","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"On the target-market continuum from broadest to narrowest, which approach comes immediately after segment marketing?","options":["Mass marketing","Individualized marketing","Niche marketing","Differentiated marketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The continuum runs mass -> segment -> niche -> individualized. 'Differentiated marketing' is just another name for segment marketing, and individualized (one-to-one) marketing is the narrowest end, after niche.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-027","concept":"c06-continuum","also":["c06-when-to-segment"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Through its website, True North Sneakers lets each buyer choose the sole, colours, laces and stitched initials, then builds that exact pair to order. It does this for tens of thousands of customers a year. This is an example of:","options":["niche marketing","mass customization","segment marketing","product sampling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tailoring goods to individual customers' tastes on a high-volume, build-to-order basis is mass customization (individualized marketing). Niche marketing serves a small, specialized group, not each customer individually.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-028","concept":"c06-continuum","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What best distinguishes niche marketing from individualized (one-to-one) marketing?","options":["Niche marketing tailors each product to one customer; individualized marketing focuses on a small specialized segment","Niche marketing uses one marketing mix for the whole market; individualized marketing uses several mixes","Niche marketing is used only by small firms; individualized marketing is used only by large firms","Niche marketing focuses on one or a few narrow segments; individualized marketing tailors the offering to each customer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A niche is still a group of people: one or a few narrowly defined, specialized segments. Individualized marketing (often through mass customization) treats each customer as a segment of one. The reversed version is the trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-029","concept":"c06-continuum","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pairing of continuum approach and description is NOT correct?","options":["Mass marketing: one product and one marketing mix for the entire market","Segment marketing: different offerings designed for different segments","Individualized marketing: one-to-one marketing tailored to each customer","Niche marketing: tailoring each product to an individual customer's tastes"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Niche marketing focuses on one or a few narrowly defined, specialized segments; tailoring to each individual customer is individualized marketing or mass customization. The other three pairings are correct.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-030","concept":"c06-continuum","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A small Alberta organic-fertilizer company has a tiny budget and faces large national competitors in every major segment. It discovers a small, specialized group, rooftop urban gardeners in Calgary, whose needs no competitor serves well. Which approach on the continuum best fits its situation?","options":["Niche marketing","Mass marketing","Segment marketing","Individualized marketing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Focusing limited resources on one narrowly defined, specialized segment that rivals ignore is niche marketing. Segment marketing would mean serving several segments with different offerings, which its budget and the competition make unrealistic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-031","concept":"c06-when-to-segment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"A business firm segments its markets when this strategy increases its sales revenue, profit, and ROI. When its increases in expenses more than offset the potentially increased revenues from segmentation, it should:","options":["terminate as many employees as possible to save money.","reduce production costs, which will lower quality.","act as its own distributor.","not attempt to segment its market any further.","discontinue manufacturing any products that are not in the mature stage of their product life cycle."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Segmentation is worthwhile only when it increases sales revenue, profit and ROI. If the added expenses more than offset the added revenue, the firm should stop segmenting. Cost-cutting moves such as layoffs or lower quality don't address the segmentation decision at all.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-032","concept":"c06-when-to-segment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Seventh Heaven is a television show that is designed to have a broad appeal to a wide number of groups. It is a show that is appropriate to watch with young children without having to worry about being embarrassed. It is a show that teaches the importance of moral values by showing teens on the show learning to cope with real-life problems without being too moralistic. It appeals to an older market because it is reminiscent of TV dramas of previous decades. This WB television show is an example of:","options":["one product with multiple market segments.","multiple products with multiple market segments.","synergy.","mass customization.","none of these answers are correct."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Television shows are single products that are often directed to two or more distinct segments; here, children, teens and older viewers. 'Multiple products with multiple segments' would need a different show for each group, and mass customization tailors the product to each individual.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-033","concept":"c06-when-to-segment","also":["c06-product-differentiation"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kodiak Motors sells a compact hatchback designed for students, a minivan designed for young families and a luxury sedan designed for executives. These are three different models, each built and promoted for its own group. Which way of segmenting is this?","options":["Multiple products and multiple market segments","One product and multiple market segments","Segments of one","Mass marketing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Different products (models) for different segments = multiple products and multiple market segments. 'One product and multiple segments' would be a single model promoted differently to several groups, like one TV show watched by several audiences.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-034","concept":"c06-when-to-segment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Compared with a 'one product and multiple market segments' approach, a 'multiple products and multiple market segments' approach typically involves:","options":["lower costs, because one product is promoted to every segment at once","higher costs, but a better fit between each product and its segment's needs","no added promotion costs, because every segment buys the same product","identical costs, since only the advertising message changes by segment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Creating different products for different segments raises production and marketing costs, but each product fits its segment's needs better. One product for multiple segments is the low-cost option, with promotion as the main extra expense.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-035","concept":"c06-when-to-segment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeview Marine estimates that splitting its boat market into a fishing segment and a water-sports segment would raise annual revenue by $240,000 but add $275,000 in product, promotion and distribution expenses. Applying the 'when to segment' rule, what should it do?","options":["Segment, because revenue would rise by $240,000","Segment, because profit would rise by $35,000","Segment, because any increase in sales justifies segmentation","Not segment further, because added expenses exceed added revenue by $35,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Added revenue $240,000 - added expenses $275,000 = -$35,000, so profit would FALL by $35,000. A firm segments only when doing so increases revenue, profit and ROI; when the extra expenses more than offset the extra revenue, it should not segment further.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-036","concept":"c06-forming-criteria","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Between two and three percent of the population have some degree of allergic reaction, usually mild, to preservatives used in salad bars. Restaurants might consider people with these 'allergies' as a separate segment. So these restaurants will need to have their regular salad bar plus a special salad bar for the allergies segment. This multiple product and multiple market segment strategy would have the greatest difficulty meeting which of the following criteria used to form market segments?","options":["instrumentality of sufficient marketing objectives to ignore that segment","different needs of buyers among different segments","similarity of needs of potential buyers within a segment","simplicity and cost of assigning potential buyers to segments","potential for increased profit and ROI"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The segment is tiny (2-3% of the population), its members benefit only modestly, and running a duplicate salad bar is expensive, so it is unlikely to be profitable. That fails 'potential for increased profit and ROI'. Needs really do differ among the segments here, so that criterion is met.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-037","concept":"c06-forming-criteria","also":["c06-selecting-criteria"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the five criteria for FORMING market segments?","options":["Expected growth of the segment","Similarity of needs of potential buyers within a segment","Difference of needs of buyers among segments","Feasibility of a marketing action to reach a segment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Expected growth is a criterion for SELECTING target segments. The five forming criteria are profit/ROI potential, similarity within, difference among, feasibility of a marketing action, and simplicity and cost of assigning buyers to segments.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-038","concept":"c06-forming-criteria","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Winnipeg gym considers creating an 'afternoon exercisers' segment, but research shows that morning and afternoon exercisers want exactly the same equipment, classes and prices. Which criterion for forming segments does this proposed split fail?","options":["Similarity of needs of potential buyers within a segment","Potential for increased profit and ROI","Simplicity and cost of assigning potential buyers to segments","Difference of needs of buyers among segments"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Segments should differ in their needs from one another. If afternoon and morning exercisers want the same things, the split fails 'difference of needs among segments'. Similarity within the segment is actually met; the problem is that the groups aren't different from each other.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-039","concept":"c06-forming-criteria","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A bank wants to target customers who are 'privately planning to switch banks within six months', but there is no inexpensive way to identify which customers belong to this group without lengthy one-on-one interviews. Which forming criterion is the main problem?","options":["Feasibility of a marketing action to reach the segment","Simplicity and cost of assigning potential buyers to segments","Similarity of needs within the segment","Difference of needs among segments"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bank can't cheaply tell who is in the segment, so it fails 'simplicity and cost of assigning potential buyers to segments'. Feasibility of a marketing action is about whether a marketing program can reach the segment once its members are known.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-040","concept":"c06-forming-criteria","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts two of the criteria for forming market segments?","options":["Needs should differ within a segment and be similar among segments","Needs should be similar both within and among segments, so one marketing mix fits all","Needs should be similar WITHIN a segment and different AMONG segments","Needs should differ both within and among segments, to maximize variety"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Good segments are internally similar (members want the same things) and externally different (each segment wants something different from the others). The reversed wording is a common distractor.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-041","concept":"c06-forming-criteria","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why must a proposed segment show 'potential for increased profit and ROI' before a firm builds a separate marketing program for it?","options":["Because only the single largest segment in any market can ever generate a profit","Because ROI determines which region of the country the segment's members must live in","Because segmenting only pays if a segment's extra revenue more than covers the extra cost of serving it","Because profit potential is the only criterion used later when choosing a brand's positioning"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Separate programs cost money, so a segment is worth forming only if it adds more revenue than cost and improves ROI. Size alone doesn't guarantee this; a large segment can still be unprofitable if it is expensive to serve.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-042","concept":"c06-selecting-criteria","also":["c06-forming-criteria"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the five criteria for SELECTING target segments?","options":["Compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources","Similarity of needs of buyers within a segment","Competitive position","Cost of reaching the segment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Similarity of needs within a segment is a criterion for FORMING segments. The selecting criteria are market size, expected growth, competitive position, cost of reaching the segment, and compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-043","concept":"c06-selecting-criteria","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Regina accounting-software firm finds a large, fast-growing segment of craft breweries. Serving them, however, would require on-site hardware installation, a service the firm has no staff or skills for and that does not fit its mission. Which selection criterion argues most strongly against targeting this segment?","options":["Expected growth","Market size","Simplicity and cost of assigning potential buyers to segments","Compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Missing skills, missing staff and a poor fit with the mission all point to 'compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources'. Size and growth actually favour this segment, and 'simplicity and cost of assigning potential buyers to segments' is a criterion for forming segments, not for selecting them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-044","concept":"c06-selecting-criteria","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two large, well-established rivals already dominate the premium dog-food segment in Ontario, and a newcomer has no advantage over them in price, quality or distribution. Which criterion for selecting a target segment is the newcomer's biggest concern?","options":["Cost of reaching the segment","Expected growth","Competitive position","Difference of needs among segments"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Facing entrenched rivals with no edge is a competitive-position problem. 'Difference of needs among segments' is a forming criterion and says nothing about rivals.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-045","concept":"c06-selecting-criteria","also":["c06-forming-criteria"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Yellowknife wilderness outfitter's most promising segment is wealthy European adventure travellers. Getting its message in front of them, however, would require costly overseas trade shows and foreign-language advertising that would eat up most of its budget. Which criterion for selecting a target segment is the main obstacle?","options":["Cost of reaching the segment","Market size","Simplicity and cost of assigning buyers to segments","Similarity of needs within the segment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Expensive trade shows and ads needed to get in front of the segment are a 'cost of reaching the segment' problem, which is a selecting criterion. 'Simplicity and cost of assigning buyers' is a forming criterion about identifying who belongs to a segment, not about the cost of communicating with them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-046","concept":"c06-selecting-criteria","also":["c06-forming-criteria"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which criterion appears on the list for FORMING segments but NOT on the list for SELECTING target segments?","options":["Difference of needs of buyers among segments","Compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources","Expected growth","Cost of reaching the segment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Difference of needs among segments is a forming criterion. Compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources, expected growth and cost of reaching the segment are all selecting criteria.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-047","concept":"c06-selecting-criteria","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"A marketer is comparing two segments that are similar in size, competition and cost to reach. Segment A is growing 2 percent a year and Segment B is growing 15 percent a year. Which selection criterion favours Segment B?","options":["Market size","Competitive position","Expected growth","Feasibility of a marketing action"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Faster growth means a bigger future market, which is the 'expected growth' criterion. Market size is equal here, and feasibility of a marketing action is a forming criterion.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-048","concept":"c06-consumer-variables","also":["c06-geographic","c06-psychographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Universal Concerts is planning on bringing a series of concerts to Canada next year. In order for its concerts to be a success it needs to know that Western Canadians like country music and that a country music event in Eastern Canada is much more likely to have empty seats. This consumer behaviour requires Universal Concerts to segment its Canadian market according to:","options":["geographic and psychographic characteristics.","demographic characteristics.","lifestyle and demographic characteristics.","usage rate and demographic characteristics.","demographic and geographic characteristics."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Music preference is an interest, which makes it psychographic, and East versus West is geographic. Nothing in the stem involves census-type data such as age or income, so every option that includes 'demographic' is wrong.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-049","concept":"c06-consumer-variables","also":["c06-demographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which family of segmentation variables answers the question 'What is the basic census-type information on the target market as a whole?'","options":["Geographic","Demographic","Psychographic","Behaviouristic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Demographic variables (age, gender, income, education, occupation, family life cycle and so on) are basic census-type information. Geographic variables answer where the market lives and works, and behaviouristic variables answer how it uses the product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-050","concept":"c06-consumer-variables","also":["c06-business-segmentation"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four families of variables used to segment consumer markets?","options":["Psychographic","Behaviouristic","Geographic","Operating characteristics"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Operating characteristics (technology, user status, capabilities) is a base for segmenting BUSINESS markets. The four consumer families are geographic, demographic, psychographic and behaviouristic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-051","concept":"c06-consumer-variables","also":["c06-usage-rate"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A reusable-cup brand describes its target as 'women aged 25-34 living in suburban Calgary who value sustainability and use a reusable cup at least five times a week.' Which variable families does this description use?","options":["Geographic, demographic, psychographic and behaviouristic","Demographic and geographic only","Demographic, psychographic and behaviouristic only","Geographic, psychographic and behaviouristic only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Women aged 25-34 = demographic; suburban Calgary = geographic; values sustainability = psychographic; uses a cup at least five times a week = usage rate, which is behaviouristic. All four families appear.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-052","concept":"c06-consumer-variables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do marketers often combine several variable families (for example, demographic plus psychographic) instead of relying on just one?","options":["Because using only one variable family is not permitted under Canadian privacy law","Because psychographic data is always cheaper and more accurate to collect than demographic or geographic data","Because people with the same census profile can differ in values and product use, so combining variables gives more homogeneous segments","Because combining several variable families guarantees that every resulting segment will be roughly the same size"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Segments should be relatively homogeneous in needs and response. Two people with identical demographics may behave very differently, so layering psychographic or behaviouristic variables sharpens the segments, as in the concert example that combines geography with music taste.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-053","concept":"c06-geographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Northern Hardware stocks far more snowblowers and block heaters in its Thunder Bay store than in its Victoria, BC, store because of the difference in winter weather. This is segmentation based on:","options":["geographic variables","demographic variables","psychographic variables","behaviouristic variables"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Climate and region are geographic variables. Demographic variables would be things like age, income or family life cycle, none of which the stem mentions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-054","concept":"c06-geographic","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Region, city size, urban/suburban/rural location and climate are all _____ segmentation variables.","options":["demographic","behaviouristic","geographic","psychographic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"These variables describe where the target market lives and works, so they are geographic. City size can feel like a census statistic, but it describes a place rather than a person, so it is not demographic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-055","concept":"c06-geographic","also":["c06-usage-rate"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A transit-app company targets 'residents of large urban centres such as Toronto and Montreal' and, within that group, 'commuters who ride transit more than 10 times a week.' Which variable types are these, respectively?","options":["Demographic; psychographic","Geographic; demographic","Geographic; behaviouristic","Psychographic; behaviouristic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Large urban centres = city size and urban location, which are geographic. Riding more than 10 times a week = usage rate, a behaviouristic variable; it describes how they use the service, not who they are demographically.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-056","concept":"c06-geographic","also":["c06-demographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a geographic segmentation variable?","options":["Climate","Family life cycle","City size","Urban, suburban or rural area"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Family life cycle (single, young couple, family with children, empty nesters) is a demographic variable. Climate, city size and urban/suburban/rural location are geographic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-057","concept":"c06-geographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Calgary ice-melt manufacturer with a limited distribution budget must decide which parts of Canada to supply first. Which segmentation variable should be its starting point?","options":["Personality (psychographic)","Education level (demographic)","Loyalty status (behaviouristic)","Climate and region (geographic)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Demand for ice melt depends on winter conditions, and the decision is about WHERE to distribute, which is a geographic question about climate and region. Personality and education have nothing to do with whether a place gets icy.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-058","concept":"c06-demographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Little Sprouts, an Edmonton daycare-supply company, aims its ads at households with children under five and annual household incomes over $90,000. This is segmentation by:","options":["psychographic variables","demographic variables","behaviouristic variables","geographic variables"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Family life cycle (children under five) and income are demographic, census-type variables. Edmonton is mentioned only as the company's home base, not as the variable used to segment.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-059","concept":"c06-demographic","also":["c06-psychographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a demographic segmentation variable?","options":["Occupation","Generation","Household size","Lifestyle"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Lifestyle (activities, interests, opinions) is psychographic. Occupation, generation and household size are basic census-type information, so they are demographic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-060","concept":"c06-demographic","also":["c06-psychographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A brand targets 'Gen Z university students' and, within that group, 'people who see themselves as adventurous risk-takers.' Which pair correctly classifies these two descriptions?","options":["Psychographic; demographic","Demographic; behaviouristic","Demographic; psychographic","Behaviouristic; psychographic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Generation and education level are demographic. Seeing oneself as an adventurous risk-taker is personality, which is psychographic; it isn't behaviouristic because it says nothing about how they use the product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-061","concept":"c06-demographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The course describes demographic variables as the target market's:","options":["prevailing attitudes, values and interests","use of and interaction with the product","place of residence and work","basic census-type information"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Demographic = basic census-type information such as age, gender, income and education. Attitudes and values are psychographic, product use is behaviouristic, and where people live and work is geographic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-062","concept":"c06-demographic","also":["c06-psychographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Two 40-year-old Calgary accountants with identical incomes and family situations buy very different vehicles: one a sporty convertible, the other a practical hybrid. Why can't demographic segmentation alone explain this?","options":["Demographic variables measure only usage rate, so they cannot explain which product a person chooses","Demographic data cannot legally be used in Canada to market vehicles to individual consumers","Demographics describe who people are statistically, not the personalities, values or lifestyles that psychographics capture","Demographic variables always predict purchases perfectly, so the survey data must be inaccurate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Demographics capture census-type facts; differences in values and personality (thrill-seeking versus practicality) are psychographic. Usage rate is a behaviouristic variable, not a demographic one.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-063","concept":"c06-psychographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nike recently debuted Air Jordan XVII with a price tag of $200. The shoes arrived at shoe stores in a metallic silver briefcase instead of a shoebox. Nike is targeting a market it identifies as 'keepers,' people who buy the shoes, keep them under their bed and when friends come over, show them the shoes. Selling to the market segment called 'keepers' probably means that Nike is using which of the following types of customer characteristic variables to segment this market?","options":["demographics","psychographics","usage","benefits sought","behaviour"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'Keepers' are defined by personality and lifestyle (collecting and showing off), which are psychographic variables. 'Usage' and 'behaviour' are tempting because the stem describes what people do, but keeping shoes unworn reflects personality, not how much or how they use the product.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-064","concept":"c06-psychographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Lifestyle, measured through consumers' activities, interests and opinions (AIO), together with personality and values, makes up which family of segmentation variables?","options":["Demographic","Behaviouristic","Geographic","Psychographic"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Lifestyle (AIO), personality and values are psychographic variables. Behaviouristic variables cover product-related behaviour such as usage rate and benefits sought.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-065","concept":"c06-psychographic","also":["c06-behaviouristic"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A fitness club is building four segments. Which one is based on a PSYCHOGRAPHIC variable rather than a behaviouristic one?","options":["Members who visit the club five or more times a week","Former members who did not renew their membership last year","People who describe themselves as competitive achievers who love a challenge","People who sign up during the club's January New Year promotion"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Seeing oneself as a competitive achiever is personality/lifestyle, which is psychographic. Visit frequency is usage rate, former members is user status, and January sign-ups is purchase occasion; all three are behaviouristic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-066","concept":"c06-psychographic","also":["c06-usage-rate"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following would NOT be classified as a psychographic variable?","options":["Personality","Usage rate","Values","Attitudes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Usage rate (heavy, medium, light and non-users) is behaviouristic. Personality, values and attitudes all describe the consumer's inner life, which is psychographic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-067","concept":"c06-psychographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Arctic Trail Outfitters wants to build its next campaign around psychographic segments. Which information should its research team collect?","options":["Customers' ages, incomes and education levels","How often customers buy and which benefits they seek","Customers' provinces, city sizes and climates","Customers' activities, interests, opinions and values"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Psychographic segmentation uses lifestyle (activities, interests, opinions), personality and values. Ages and incomes are demographic, purchase frequency and benefits sought are behaviouristic, and provinces and climates are geographic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-068","concept":"c06-behaviouristic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Brightsmile toothpaste offers one version for people who want whiter teeth, one for people who want cavity protection, and one for people with sensitive teeth who want pain relief. Brightsmile is segmenting on:","options":["personality","benefits sought","family life cycle","usage rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Grouping buyers by what they want the product to do for them is segmentation by benefits sought, a behaviouristic variable. Usage rate would group them by how much toothpaste they use.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-069","concept":"c06-behaviouristic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A telecom company creates different offers for three groups: people who have never had its service, first-time subscribers, and customers who cancelled last year. Which behaviouristic variable is it using?","options":["Loyalty status","User status","Usage rate","Benefits sought"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Non-users, first-time users and ex-users are categories of user status. Loyalty status measures how committed current buyers are to the brand, and usage rate measures how much they use it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-070","concept":"c06-behaviouristic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The course describes behaviouristic variables as answering which question about a target market?","options":["Where does it live and work?","What is its basic census-type information?","What attitudes, values and interests does it share?","How does it use and interact with the product?"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Behaviouristic = how the target market uses and interacts with the product (benefits sought, usage rate, user status, loyalty, occasion). The other questions describe geographic, demographic and psychographic variables.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-071","concept":"c06-behaviouristic","also":["c06-psychographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A coffee chain notices that some customers always buy its brand and nothing else, while others buy whichever brand is on sale. Segmenting customers on this difference uses which variable?","options":["Personality (psychographic)","Loyalty status (behaviouristic)","Income (demographic)","Values (psychographic)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"How committed a buyer is to one brand is loyalty status, a behaviouristic variable. It can sound like an attitude, but it describes the customer's buying relationship with the product, not their general personality or values.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-072","concept":"c06-behaviouristic","also":["c06-psychographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a behaviouristic segmentation variable?","options":["Personality","Benefits sought","Purchase occasion","Loyalty status"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Personality is psychographic. Benefits sought, purchase occasion, loyalty status, user status and usage rate are behaviouristic because they describe how people use and interact with the product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-073","concept":"c06-usage-rate","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The concept suggesting that 80 percent of a firm's sales are obtained from 20 percent of its customers is the:","options":["80/20 rule","usage rate","loyalty status","market-product grid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is the 80/20 rule. Usage rate is the related variable (quantity consumed or store visits during a period) used to find the heavy users who produce most of the sales.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-074","concept":"c06-usage-rate","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeshore Pizza has 2,000 customers and annual sales of $500,000. If its sales follow the 80/20 rule exactly, about how much revenue comes from its 400 heaviest customers?","options":["$400,000","$100,000","$250,000","$80,000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"400 / 2,000 = 20% of customers. Under the 80/20 rule, they produce 80% of sales: 0.80 x $500,000 = $400,000. The $100,000 figure is the 20% of sales that comes from the other 1,600 customers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-075","concept":"c06-usage-rate","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"An Edmonton car wash learns that a small group of heavy users who wash their cars weekly generates most of its revenue. Following the logic of usage-rate segmentation and the 80/20 rule, what should it do?","options":["Create a frequency or loyalty program that rewards and retains the heavy users","Stop marketing to heavy users, since they will keep coming anyway","Shift all of its spending to non-users who live outside the city","Replace its usage data with census data on customers' ages and incomes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because a small share of heavy users produces most of the sales, protecting and rewarding them with frequency or loyalty programs is the classic 80/20 response. Ignoring them risks losing the customers who matter most.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-076","concept":"c06-usage-rate","also":["c06-behaviouristic"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Montreal grocery chain sorts shoppers into heavy, medium, light and non-users of its in-store bakery, based on how many times they buy bakery items each month. This is segmentation by:","options":["benefits sought","lifestyle","usage rate","family life cycle"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Usage rate is the quantity consumed or patronage during a specific period, usually classified as heavy, medium, light and non-users. Benefits sought would group shoppers by what they want from the bakery, not by how often they buy.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-077","concept":"c06-usage-rate","also":["c06-behaviouristic"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts usage rate with loyalty status?","options":["Usage rate measures a buyer's commitment to one brand; loyalty status measures the quantity consumed in a period","Usage rate is how much or how often a product is used in a period; loyalty status is commitment to one brand","Both measure the number of store visits in a period, so the two variables are interchangeable","Usage rate is a psychographic variable, while loyalty status is a demographic variable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Usage rate = quantity or frequency in a period (heavy versus light users); loyalty status = commitment to a brand. A heavy user can still switch brands freely, and both variables are behaviouristic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-078","concept":"c06-business-segmentation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"An industrial-cleaning supplier uses one sales approach for clients whose head office buys for every location, and a different approach for clients where each branch does its own buying. This business-market segmentation is based on:","options":["operating characteristics","situational factors","personal characteristics","purchasing approaches"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Centralized versus decentralized buying is a purchasing-approaches base. Operating characteristics describe the technology or capabilities a client has, not how it organizes its buying.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-079","concept":"c06-business-segmentation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Calgary pipe-fitting distributor creates a premium service for oil-field customers who need emergency parts delivered within hours, and it prices small rush orders differently from large planned orders. Which business segmentation base is this?","options":["Purchasing approaches","Demographics","Operating characteristics","Situational factors"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Urgency, order size and specific application are situational factors. Purchasing approaches would focus on how the customer organizes its buying (centralized or decentralized, buying criteria), not on the urgency of a particular order.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-080","concept":"c06-business-segmentation","also":["c06-behaviouristic"],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"In business markets, the technology a customer uses, whether it is a user or non-user of a product, and its capabilities all fall under which segmentation base?","options":["Operating characteristics","Personal characteristics","Situational factors","Purchasing approaches"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Technology, user status and capabilities are operating characteristics. Note that user status is behaviouristic in consumer markets but sits under operating characteristics in business markets.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-081","concept":"c06-business-segmentation","also":["c06-psychographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the additional bases that businesses use to segment business markets?","options":["Lifestyle and personality","Operating characteristics","Purchasing approaches","Personal characteristics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Lifestyle and personality are psychographic variables used to segment CONSUMER markets. The additional B2B bases are operating characteristics, purchasing approaches, situational factors and personal characteristics (which describe individual buyers' traits such as risk attitude, not their lifestyle).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-082","concept":"c06-business-segmentation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A software vendor groups its corporate clients by NAICS industry code and number of employees, and separately by how risk-averse their individual buyers are. Which classification is correct?","options":["Both groupings are operating characteristics, because both describe how the client company runs","NAICS code and employees are demographic bases; buyers' attitude toward risk is a personal-characteristics base","NAICS code is a situational factor; buyers' attitude toward risk is a purchasing approach","Both groupings are psychographic variables borrowed directly from consumer markets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Industry code, number of employees, sales and location are business demographics. Attitude toward risk, buyer-seller similarity and loyalty are personal characteristics of the individuals who do the buying.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-083","concept":"c06-personas","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Character descriptions of a brand's typical customer are called:","options":["perceptual maps","positioning statements","personas","market segments"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Personas are character descriptions of a brand's typical customer. A market segment is the group itself; the persona brings its typical member to life.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-084","concept":"c06-personas","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow River Bank's marketing team writes a one-page profile of a fictional customer named Kayla: 29, works in HR in Calgary, is saving for a condo, reads personal-finance blogs and values security. The write-up includes a photo and a personal quote and is meant to stand for the bank's typical customer. What has the team created?","options":["A market-product grid","A positioning statement","A persona","A perceptual map"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A named, story-like character description of the typical customer is a persona. A positioning statement describes the brand ('For ... who desire ..., [brand] is the ...'), not the customer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-085","concept":"c06-personas","also":["c06-market-segments"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does a persona differ from a market segment?","options":["A persona is a group of buyers with common needs, while a segment is a single fictional customer","A segment is a group of buyers with common needs; a persona is a character representing its typical customer","A persona plots competing brands on two perceptual dimensions, while a segment lists products","A persona states the brand's single competitive advantage, while a segment states its price"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The segment is the group; the persona is a single character representing its typical member. The reversed version is the trap, and plotting brands on two dimensions describes a perceptual map.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-086","concept":"c06-personas","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do marketers build personas for their target markets?","options":["To replace the need to identify and size market segments at all","To display competing brands on a two-dimensional graph of consumer perceptions","To make the typical customer concrete so product, message and media decisions fit that person","To calculate how the firm's sales split between heavy users and light users"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Personas turn segment data into a relatable person, so teams design offerings and communications for that customer's needs and media habits. They build on segmentation instead of replacing it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-087","concept":"c06-personas","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"A detailed target market profile or persona would typically include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["demographic details such as age and occupation","psychographic details such as values and interests","a competitor's production costs per unit","media habits such as favourite apps and podcasts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Profiles and personas describe the customer: demographics, psychographics, media habits and needs. A competitor's production costs describe the competitor, not the target customer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-088","concept":"c06-segmentation-analytics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Toronto retailer wants to know which specific neighbourhood population clusters, with names like 'Middle-Class Mosaic', live near a proposed store site. Which source best fits this need?","options":["Nielsen general consumer trend data","Euromonitor global lifestyle trend reports","Environics Analytics PRIZM cluster data","A market-product grid of its own products"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Environics Analytics (PRIZM) provides granular data that pinpoints population clusters such as 'Middle-Class Mosaic'. Nielsen and Euromonitor provide broader, general consumer and lifestyle trends.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-089","concept":"c06-segmentation-analytics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"According to the course, how does data from Nielsen or Euromonitor differ from Environics Analytics' data?","options":["Nielsen and Euromonitor provide granular neighbourhood clusters; Environics provides only global trends","All three companies provide only perceptual maps of competing brands","Nielsen and Euromonitor provide general consumer and lifestyle trends; Environics pinpoints population clusters","Environics provides positioning statements; Nielsen and Euromonitor provide personas"],"answer":2,"explanation":"General trends come from research companies like Nielsen and Euromonitor; Environics Analytics works at a more granular level, identifying population clusters. The reversed version is the trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-090","concept":"c06-segmentation-analytics","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Companies such as Environics Analytics provide data on a more _____ basis, pinpointing information on population clusters.","options":["general","national","historical","granular"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Environics data is granular: fine-grained enough to pinpoint population clusters. 'General' describes the consumer and lifestyle trend data from Nielsen or Euromonitor.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-091","concept":"c06-segmentation-analytics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A national beverage brand wants a broad picture of how Canadian consumer and lifestyle trends are shifting before it plans the next three years. Which source does the course suggest for this kind of overview?","options":["A PRIZM cluster profile for one postal code","A persona written by its own sales team","The brand's own positioning statement","A research company such as Nielsen or Euromonitor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For general consumer and lifestyle trends, the course points to research companies such as Nielsen or Euromonitor. A single PRIZM cluster is granular and local: useful for pinpointing neighbourhoods, not for a national trend overview.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-092","concept":"c06-segmentation-analytics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of segmentation analytics as described in the course?","options":["Nielsen consumer trend data","Euromonitor lifestyle trend data","A brand's positioning statement","Environics Analytics PRIZM population clusters"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A positioning statement is a formal description of a brand's image and what sets it apart; it isn't segmentation data. Nielsen, Euromonitor and Environics Analytics are the data sources the course names.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-093","concept":"c06-six-step-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the FIRST step in the six-step process for segmenting a market?","options":["Cluster common consumer variables to create meaningful segments","Identify consumer/customer needs and common characteristics in the market","Estimate the size and feasibility of each segment","Identify the segment(s) to be targeted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The process starts by identifying customer needs and common characteristics. Clustering (step 2) can't happen until the firm knows which needs and characteristics to group buyers on.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-094","concept":"c06-six-step-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the six-step segmentation process, which step comes immediately AFTER 'cluster common consumer/customer variables to create meaningful market segments'?","options":["Estimate the size and feasibility of each segment","Identify the segment(s) to be targeted","Take actions with marketing programs to reach the segment(s)","Identify consumer needs and common characteristics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Step 2 (cluster) is followed by step 3: estimate the size and feasibility of each segment. Choosing targets is step 4, because firms must size segments before deciding which to pursue.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-095","concept":"c06-six-step-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the FIFTH step in the six-step process for segmenting a market?","options":["Monitor and evaluate the success of these programs compared with objectives","Take actions with marketing programs to reach the segment(s)","Identify the segment(s) to be targeted","Estimate the size and feasibility of each segment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Step 5 is taking action with marketing programs to reach the chosen segment(s). Monitoring and evaluating against objectives is step 6, the final step.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-096","concept":"c06-six-step-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sunridge Cycles launched ads and new store displays aimed at its 'family weekend rider' segment. Six months later, it compares family-bike sales against the targets it set at the start. Which step of the segmentation process is it in?","options":["Step 6: monitor and evaluate the success of these programs compared with objectives","Step 5: take actions with marketing programs to reach the segment(s)","Step 3: estimate the size and feasibility of each segment","Step 4: identify the segment(s) to be targeted"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Comparing results with objectives is step 6. The campaign launch was step 5; this is the evaluation that follows it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-097","concept":"c06-six-step-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Kelowna winery has interviewed buyers about what they look for in wine. It now groups people who share similar characteristics into meaningful categories such as 'gift buyers', 'weekend entertainers' and 'serious collectors'. It has not yet estimated how big each group is. Which step is the winery in?","options":["Step 2: cluster common variables to create meaningful market segments","Step 1: identify needs and common characteristics","Step 3: estimate the size and feasibility of each segment","Step 4: identify the segment(s) to be targeted"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Grouping buyers who share variables into meaningful segments is step 2 (cluster). The interviews about needs were step 1, and estimating each group's size comes next, in step 3.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-098","concept":"c06-six-step-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Using census data and sales records, the Kelowna winery estimates that its 'serious collectors' and 'gift buyers' segments are both large and realistic to serve, while 'weekend entertainers' is small. It now decides to pursue collectors and gift buyers. Which step is this decision?","options":["Step 4: identify the segment(s) to be targeted","Step 2: cluster variables into segments","Step 5: take actions with marketing programs","Step 6: monitor and evaluate results"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Choosing which segments to pursue is step 4. The sizing work just completed was step 3, and designing campaigns to reach the chosen segments will be step 5.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-099","concept":"c06-six-step-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six steps in the process for segmenting a market?","options":["Estimate the size and feasibility of each segment","Write a positioning statement for each competitor","Monitor and evaluate the success of these programs compared with objectives","Cluster common consumer variables to create meaningful market segments"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Positioning statements are written for one's own brand during positioning; they aren't a step in segmenting. The six steps are: identify needs, cluster, estimate size and feasibility, identify targets, take action, and monitor/evaluate.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-100","concept":"c06-six-step-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does 'estimate the size and feasibility of each segment' come BEFORE 'identify the segment(s) to be targeted'?","options":["Because targeting must be finished before any segments can be formed or clustered","Because a firm can't sensibly choose targets until it knows how large and reachable each segment is","Because the marketing program for each segment is launched during the estimating step","Because feasibility can only be judged after results are monitored against objectives"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Size and feasibility are the evidence for choosing targets; without them, the choice is guesswork. The marketing program is launched in step 5 and monitoring happens in step 6, both after targeting.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-101","concept":"c06-market-product-grid","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A framework that relates the market segments of potential buyers to the products offered or potential marketing actions is a:","options":["perceptual map","market-product grid","persona","positioning statement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is a market-product grid: segments on one side (rows), products or marketing actions on the other (columns), and estimated market size in the cells. A perceptual map plots brands by consumer perceptions instead.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-102","concept":"c06-market-product-grid","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Polar Bikes builds a table with buyer segments (commuters, mountain riders, kids) as rows and its bike models as columns. It writes the estimated market size in each cell to see where to focus. What tool is this?","options":["A perceptual map","A positioning statement","A market-product grid","A segmentation analytics cluster"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Segments in rows, products in columns and estimated market size in each cell make a market-product grid. A perceptual map would plot brands on two consumer-perception dimensions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-103","concept":"c06-market-product-grid","also":["c06-perceptual-map"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How does a market-product grid differ from a perceptual map?","options":["A grid plots brands on two perceptual dimensions, while a perceptual map lists segments against products","A grid relates segments to products with estimated market sizes; a perceptual map plots how consumers see brands","Both show consumers' perceptions of brands; they differ only in how the chart is formatted","A grid is used only for positioning, while a perceptual map is used only for segmentation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The grid is a segmentation/targeting tool that shows where market volume lies across segment-product combinations. The perceptual map is a positioning tool that shows where brands sit in consumers' minds. The swapped version is the trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-104","concept":"c06-market-product-grid","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"On Polar Bikes' market-product grid, the 'kids' row shows a large estimated market in the 'balance bike' column but a tiny one in the 'carbon road bike' column. What does this suggest Polar should do?","options":["Reposition its carbon road bikes as premium children's toys","Drop the kids' segment entirely and focus on adult riders","Focus its kids' marketing on balance bikes, not carbon road bikes","Cut carbon road bike prices for kids until that cell grows"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cells show estimated market size, so the large kids/balance-bike cell is where the opportunity lies. Dropping the whole segment would ignore that strong cell, and nothing in the grid calls for repositioning carbon bikes.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-105","concept":"c06-market-product-grid","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about a market-product grid is NOT correct?","options":["Rows typically represent market segments","Columns typically represent products or marketing actions","It plots brands by how consumers perceive them on two attributes","Cells typically show the estimated size of the market"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Plotting brands by consumer perception on two attributes describes a perceptual map. The grid relates segments (rows) to products or marketing actions (columns), with estimated market sizes in the cells.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-106","concept":"c06-ethics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A payday-loan company runs flashy ads concentrated in low-income neighbourhoods, aimed at people who are already struggling with debt. Critics call the campaign exploitative. Which ethical segmentation concern does this most directly raise?","options":["Targeting a segment with poor expected growth","Failing to differentiate the product","Targeting disadvantaged groups","Relying on an inaccurate perceptual map"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aiming high-cost loans at people in financial hardship is targeting a disadvantaged group, one of the legal and ethical issues the course lists. Expected growth is a selecting criterion, not an ethical concern.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-107","concept":"c06-ethics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"The course lists several legal and ethical issues marketers must consider when segmenting and targeting. Which is NOT one of them?","options":["Avoiding consumer backlash","Using the 80/20 rule to reward heavy users","Targeting vulnerable groups","Marketing potentially harmful products"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The listed issues are consumer backlash, vulnerable groups, disadvantaged groups and potentially harmful products. Rewarding heavy users through loyalty programs is a standard usage-rate tactic, not one of the listed ethical issues.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-108","concept":"c06-ethics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"According to the course, when can social responsibility be more important than profits in segmentation and targeting?","options":["Only when a segment fails to meet the firm's ROI target","Only in not-for-profit and government organizations","Whenever a firm uses mass marketing instead of segmenting","When the marketing is viewed as exploitative or irresponsible"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The slides state that social responsibility can be more important than profits if marketing is viewed as exploitative or irresponsible. This applies to all firms, not just not-for-profits, and isn't tied to ROI targets.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-109","concept":"c06-ethics","also":["c06-selecting-criteria"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Canadian energy-drink brand discovers that its fastest-growing segment is 13- to 15-year-olds. Based on the course's guidance on socially responsible segmentation, what should it do?","options":["Avoid targeting this vulnerable group with a potentially harmful product","Target the group aggressively, because its expected growth is so high","Reposition the drink as a breakfast product for children and teens","Run head-to-head ads against rival energy drinks aimed at young teens"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Young teens are a vulnerable group and energy drinks are potentially harmful, so targeting them risks harm and consumer backlash. High expected growth is a selecting criterion, but the course says social responsibility can outweigh profit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-110","concept":"c06-ethics","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Because segmentation is legal, marketers never need to worry about consumer backlash when they choose which groups to target.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Corrected statement: marketers must avoid consumer backlash, especially when targeting vulnerable or disadvantaged groups or marketing potentially harmful products, and social responsibility can matter more than profits.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-111","concept":"c06-product-positioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The impression of a branded product that a marketer wants to establish in consumers' minds, relative to their needs and in contrast to the competition, is called:","options":["product differentiation","market segmentation","targeting","product positioning"],"answer":3,"explanation":"That is product positioning: the place the product occupies in consumers' minds relative to competitors. Product differentiation is using features and advertising to make products seem different and better.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-112","concept":"c06-product-positioning","also":["c06-selecting-criteria"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three basic factors on which product positioning is built?","options":["Market size","Image","Product attributes","Price"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The three basic positioning factors are image, product attributes and price. Market size is a criterion for selecting target segments.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-113","concept":"c06-product-positioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Summit Skincare positions its new serum as a premium, dermatologist-grade luxury product. Which marketing decision would most UNDERMINE that position?","options":["Featuring clinical test results in its ads","Using heavy glass packaging with a minimalist design","Pricing it above mass-market rivals","Selling it in discount bins at dollar stores"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For positioning to succeed, all marketing mix efforts must support it. Discount-bin distribution contradicts a premium image, while clinical test results, premium packaging and an above-market price all reinforce it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-114","concept":"c06-product-positioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"Once a brand has established its position, how does the course say that position should be maintained?","options":["By changing the advertising message every month to stay fresh","By matching every competitor's price cut immediately","By targeting every segment in the market","Through consistent performance and communication"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Positions are maintained through consistent performance and communication. Constantly changing the message or chasing every segment blurs the place the brand holds in consumers' minds.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-115","concept":"c06-product-positioning","also":["c06-repositioning"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Chiquita put its brand sticker on bananas, a fruit most shoppers saw as an unbranded commodity, and promoted them as consistently high-quality. This created a distinct place for the brand in consumers' minds where none had existed before. This best illustrates:","options":["product positioning","repositioning","mass customization","usage-rate segmentation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Creating a brand's first distinct place in consumers' minds is product positioning. Repositioning changes a place a product already occupies, but Chiquita was establishing a position for a previously unbranded commodity.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-116","concept":"c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Children's Advil pain reliever positioned itself against Children's Tylenol pain reliever by running ads, which claimed that the Advil brand is superior because it works faster and longer than the Tylenol brand. This is an example of:","options":["head-to-head positioning.","lateral positioning.","differentiation.","consumer positioning.","cognitive positioning."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Head-to-head positioning means competing directly with competitors on similar product attributes in the same target market; here, speed and duration of relief for the same parents. 'Differentiation' is tempting, but differentiation positioning avoids rivals by seeking a smaller, less-competitive niche, whereas Advil attacks Tylenol directly. 'Lateral', 'consumer' and 'cognitive' positioning are not course terms.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-117","concept":"c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"There are a lot of skateboard manufacturers on the market, but the BMW Streetcarver is the only one with stabilizers and wheel design based on BMW's automobile. This technology gives the BMW Streetcarver better control at high speeds and around sharp turns than any other brand. What type of positioning is BMW using with its skateboard priced at $495?","options":["parallel market positioning","distinction positioning","repositioning","differentiation positioning","head-to-head positioning"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Differentiation positioning means seeking a smaller, less-competitive niche by stressing unique attributes: the only board with BMW-based stabilizers, at a premium price. It isn't head-to-head, because BMW isn't fighting mainstream boards on the same attributes for the same buyers.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-118","concept":"c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Competing directly with competitors on similar product attributes in the same target market is called:","options":["differentiation positioning","repositioning","head-to-head positioning","niche marketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"That is head-to-head positioning. Differentiation positioning does the opposite: it avoids direct competition by seeking a smaller, less-competitive niche.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-119","concept":"c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation","also":["c06-product-differentiation"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts head-to-head positioning with differentiation positioning?","options":["Head-to-head competes on similar attributes in the same market; differentiation positioning seeks a smaller, less-competitive niche","Head-to-head seeks a smaller, less-competitive niche; differentiation positioning attacks the market leader directly","Head-to-head changes an existing position; differentiation positioning creates a brand's very first position","Both terms mean selling several different products to several different market segments"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Head-to-head = same market, similar attributes, direct comparison. Differentiation positioning = a niche, unique attributes, less competition. Changing an existing position is repositioning, and selling different products to different segments is product differentiation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-120","concept":"c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kestrel Audio stays out of the crowded wireless-earbud market dominated by large brands. Instead, it makes the only earbuds designed for competitive swimmers, waterproof to 10 metres with bone-conduction sound, and sells them to that small group at a premium price. This is:","options":["head-to-head positioning","differentiation positioning","repositioning","mass marketing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Seeking a smaller, less-competitive niche and stressing unique attributes is differentiation positioning. It isn't head-to-head, because Kestrel deliberately avoids competing with the big brands on similar attributes in the mainstream market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-121","concept":"c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why might a small firm with a genuinely unique product feature choose differentiation positioning instead of head-to-head positioning?","options":["It requires the firm to match the market leader's attributes exactly and then win on price in the same market","Head-to-head positioning is permitted only for products that have already reached the decline stage","It guarantees the firm the largest possible share of the total market within its first year","It avoids direct battles with larger rivals by placing the brand in a smaller, less-competitive niche that values its unique attributes"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Differentiation positioning means seeking a less-competitive, smaller niche by stressing unique attributes, which suits a small firm that can't win a direct fight. Matching the leader's attributes in the same market describes head-to-head positioning.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-122","concept":"c06-positioning-statement","also":["c06-usp"],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A formalized statement that identifies the image a branded product represents in the market and what sets it apart from the competition is a:","options":["unique selling proposition","positioning statement","persona","perceptual map"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is a positioning statement. A USP is the single competitive advantage a brand may build its positioning on; the positioning statement is the full sentence naming target, need, brand, category and benefits.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-123","concept":"c06-positioning-statement","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Complete the course's positioning statement template: 'For (target market) who desire (target market need), (brand) is the _____ that offers (product benefits).'","options":["(competitor's name)","(price point)","(distribution channel)","(product category)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The template is: For (target market) who desire (target market need), (brand) is the (product category) that offers (product benefits). Competitors, price points and channels are not slots in the template.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-124","concept":"c06-positioning-statement","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following follows the course's positioning statement format?","options":["For busy parents who desire quick dinners, FreshFork is the meal kit that offers pre-chopped ingredients","FreshFork: quick, healthy dinners made easy for busy Calgary families every night!","FreshFork aims to increase its market share in Calgary by 10 percent by the end of next year","FreshFork earns 80 percent of its sales from the 20 percent of customers who order weekly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Only the 'For busy parents who desire quick dinners...' sentence fills every slot of the template: target market, need ('who desire'), brand, product category and benefits. The others are a slogan, a sales objective and an 80/20 observation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-125","concept":"c06-positioning-statement","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Arbor Tea's draft positioning statement reads: 'For health-conscious professionals, Arbor is the tea brand that offers organic, antioxidant-rich blends.' Which element of the course template is missing?","options":["The target market","The product category","The product benefits","The target market need"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The draft names the target (health-conscious professionals), the brand (Arbor), the category (tea brand) and the benefits (organic, antioxidant-rich blends), but it skips the 'who desire (need)' clause.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-126","concept":"c06-positioning-statement","also":["c06-usp"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How does a positioning statement differ from a unique selling proposition (USP)?","options":["A positioning statement names the target, need, brand, category and benefits; a USP is the one advantage it may rest on","A USP names the target, need, category and benefits, while a positioning statement is one single advantage","A positioning statement plots brands on two axes, while a USP lists market segments against products","A USP is used only during segmentation, while a positioning statement is used only during targeting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The positioning statement is the full template sentence; the USP is one distinctive advantage that may appear inside it as the product benefit. The reversed version is the trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-127","concept":"c06-perceptual-map","also":["c06-head-to-head-vs-differentiation"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A perceptual map shows how various drinks are seen in the minds of adult consumers. On its vertical axis (more nutritious at the top, less nutritious at the bottom), coffee sits above sugared soft drinks, while skinny lattes and fruit-flavoured drinks sit above coffee. Suppose a coffee producer wants to increase sales. Its advertising agency recommends that it run ads showing that coffee is healthier than one or more competitive drinks shown on the map. In the ads, coffee should be compared with:","options":["skinny lattes.","fruit-flavoured drinks.","sugared soft drinks.","fruit-flavoured drinks and sugared soft drinks.","both sugared soft drinks and skinny lattes."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The coffee producer should compare itself only with drinks that are lower than coffee on the vertical nutrition axis, which consumers see as less nutritious. The only such drink is sugared soft drinks; skinny lattes and fruit-flavoured drinks are perceived as more nutritious than coffee, so claiming to beat them would not be believed.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-128","concept":"c06-perceptual-map","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A means of displaying or graphing, in two dimensions, the location of products or brands in the minds of consumers is a:","options":["market-product grid","positioning statement","perceptual map","persona"],"answer":2,"explanation":"That is a perceptual map. A market-product grid relates segments to products with estimated market sizes; it doesn't show consumer perceptions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-129","concept":"c06-perceptual-map","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Huron Hotels surveys travellers about six hotel brands and plots each brand on a chart with 'price' on one axis and 'perceived luxury' on the other. It then spots an empty area for an affordable-but-luxurious hotel. What tool did Huron use?","options":["A perceptual map","A market-product grid","A segmentation analytics cluster","A positioning statement"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plotting brands on two dimensions based on consumer perceptions is a perceptual map, and spotting empty spaces is one of its main uses. A market-product grid shows segments and products, not perceived brand positions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-130","concept":"c06-perceptual-map","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is a primary reason marketers use perceptual maps?","options":["To calculate the market size of each segment-product combination","To see how consumers perceive competing brands and to find positioning gaps","To assign each customer to a segment at the lowest possible cost","To estimate how the firm's sales split between heavy and light users"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Perceptual maps show where brands sit in consumers' minds relative to rivals, revealing close competitors, open gaps and possible moves. Estimating market size for each segment-product cell is the job of a market-product grid.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-131","concept":"c06-perceptual-map","also":["c06-repositioning"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Students often confuse perceptual maps with repositioning. Which statement is accurate?","options":["Repositioning is the display tool, while the perceptual map is the strategy that changes a brand's image","A perceptual map shows sales by segment, while repositioning shows changes in a product's price","A perceptual map shows where brands sit in consumers' minds; repositioning is the strategy of changing that place","Both are steps within the six-step process for segmenting a market"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The perceptual map is a display tool; repositioning is a strategy that changes the place a product occupies in consumers' minds, which can show up as a move on the map. That is why a campaign like Post Grape-Nuts' new-uses push is repositioning, not 'perceptual mapping'.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-132","concept":"c06-repositioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Post Grape-Nuts cereal has been marketed since early in the 20th century. Its market share has been steadily declining as consumers began associating it with something their grandfather ate. Post recently launched a campaign which invited consumers to try the crunchy cereal on top of their yogurt, their salad, or their bowl of soup as a delicious addition. This is an example of a _____ strategy.","options":["perceptual mapping","product positioning","product differentiation","repositioning","psychographics"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Repositioning changes the place an existing product occupies in consumers' minds; here, from an old-fashioned breakfast cereal to an all-day topping. 'Product positioning' is tempting, but the brand already had a position and Post is changing it.","source":"official"},{"id":"q06-133","concept":"c06-repositioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Changing the place a product occupies in consumers' minds relative to competitive products, to more accurately meet consumer needs, is called:","options":["product positioning","perceptual mapping","repositioning","product differentiation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"That is repositioning. Product positioning establishes a product's place in the first place; repositioning changes a place that already exists.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-134","concept":"c06-repositioning","also":["c06-product-positioning"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What separates repositioning from product positioning?","options":["Repositioning is done before launch, while positioning is done only after sales decline","Repositioning applies only to services, while positioning applies only to goods","Positioning changes a product's price, while repositioning changes only its packaging","Positioning establishes a brand's place in consumers' minds; repositioning changes a place it already holds"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Repositioning assumes an existing position that no longer fits consumer needs. The timing in the 'before launch' option is reversed: a brand-new product gets positioned, and an established one gets repositioned.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-135","concept":"c06-repositioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ridgeline Games was long seen as a maker of dusty family board games that only grandparents bought. It launched strategy-heavy games, streamer partnerships and sleek packaging, and young adults now see it as a cool brand for game nights. This is:","options":["niche marketing","mass customization","head-to-head positioning","repositioning"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Changing an established, dated image so the brand occupies a new place in consumers' minds is repositioning. It isn't head-to-head positioning, because the stem describes no direct comparison with a rival on similar attributes.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-136","concept":"c06-repositioning","also":["c06-product-positioning"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of repositioning?","options":["A budget hotel chain upgrades its rooms and image so travellers now see it as a stylish mid-priced choice","A dated department store refreshes its image to attract younger shoppers","A start-up launches its first-ever product and creates its initial brand image","A breakfast cereal is promoted as an all-day snack"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A brand-new product's first image is positioning, not repositioning, because there is no existing place to change. The other examples change how an existing product is seen or used.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-137","concept":"c06-pop-pod","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Every major grocery chain in Canada, including Harvest Basket, offers fresh produce, an in-store bakery, free parking and a loyalty card. For Harvest Basket, these features are best described as:","options":["points-of-difference","points-of-parity","a unique selling proposition","differentiation positioning"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Associations shared with other brands are points-of-parity: the basics needed to compete in the category, as in the course's Sobeys example. They can't be points-of-difference, because shoppers can find them to the same extent at rival chains.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-138","concept":"c06-pop-pod","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Attributes or benefits that consumers strongly associate with a brand, positively evaluate, and believe they could not find to the same extent with a competitive brand are:","options":["points-of-difference","points-of-parity","product attributes","market segments"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is the definition of points-of-difference (PODs). Points-of-parity are associations that may be shared with other brands.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-139","concept":"c06-pop-pod","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Coffee drinkers strongly associate Northern Grind with its signature maple-cinnamon latte and its community hockey sponsorships, rate both very positively, and say no other chain offers anything quite like them. These associations are best described as:","options":["points-of-parity","head-to-head positioning","product differentiation","points-of-difference"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Strong association, positive evaluation and a belief that rivals can't match it are the three marks of a point-of-difference, as in the course's Tim Hortons example. Points-of-parity are shared with competitors.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-140","concept":"c06-pop-pod","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Customers of Glacier Cafe really like its free Wi-Fi, but every nearby competitor offers equally fast free Wi-Fi. Why is the Wi-Fi a point-of-parity rather than a point-of-difference for Glacier?","options":["Because customers evaluate the free Wi-Fi negatively overall","Because although valued, it is available to the same extent at competing brands","Because Wi-Fi is a price factor rather than a product attribute","Because points-of-difference must always relate to the brand's price"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A POD must be strongly associated with the brand, positively evaluated AND not available to the same extent from rivals. The Wi-Fi is liked, but it fails the uniqueness test, so it is a POP.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-141","concept":"c06-pop-pod","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does a brand need both points-of-parity and points-of-difference?","options":["POPs show it meets the category basics (ticket to play); PODs give a reason to choose it over rivals (reason to win)","POPs make the brand unique, while PODs show it is similar to brands consumers already know","POPs are needed only by service brands, while PODs are needed only by brands of physical goods","Only PODs matter for positioning; POPs are features consumers simply ignore when choosing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without POPs, consumers may not see the brand as a real option in the category; without PODs, they have no reason to prefer it. The reversed version (POPs = unique) is the trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-142","concept":"c06-usp","also":["c06-pop-pod"],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When a firm bases its positioning on one single, distinctive competitive advantage, that advantage is called its:","options":["point-of-parity","positioning statement","unique selling proposition (USP)","market segment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Choosing one competitive advantage as the basis for positioning means building on a unique selling proposition; the alternative is positioning on multiple differences. A point-of-parity is shared with rivals, so it can't serve as a USP.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-143","concept":"c06-usp","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Swift Pizza repeats one message on every ad, menu and box: 'Hot at your door in 20 minutes or it's free.' It does not promote taste, price or variety. Swift is basing its positioning on:","options":["points-of-parity","a unique selling proposition","multiple differences","mass customization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Building positioning around one distinctive benefit and communicating it consistently is a USP strategy. A multiple-differences strategy would promote several advantages at once.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-144","concept":"c06-usp","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"When choosing a competitive advantage on which to base positioning, how does a USP approach differ from a multiple-differences approach?","options":["A USP approach promotes several shared benefits, while a multiple-differences approach promotes just one","A USP is always based on price, while multiple differences are always based on image","A USP is used for segmentation, while multiple differences are used for targeting","A USP approach promotes one distinctive advantage; a multiple-differences approach promotes several ways the brand differs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The course frames the choice as one unique selling proposition versus multiple differences. Both are positioning decisions, and neither is limited to price or image.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-145","concept":"c06-usp","also":["c06-pop-pod"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Slumber North Mattress's research shows that the one advantage consumers truly value, and that rivals cannot match, is a patented cooling layer. If it wants a single, clear basis for its positioning, what should it do?","options":["Make the cooling layer its USP and communicate it consistently","Promote that it offers the same features as every competitor","Reposition as a discount brand and compete mainly on price","Abandon positioning and switch to an undifferentiated mass-market approach"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A valued advantage that rivals can't match is ideal as a USP, and positions are maintained through consistent communication. Promoting shared features only stresses points-of-parity, which give no reason to choose the brand.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q06-146","concept":"c06-usp","also":["c06-pop-pod"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following would NOT make a good unique selling proposition?","options":["A distinctive benefit that customers value highly","An advantage that competitors cannot easily match","A benefit that every competitor offers equally well","A single, clear reason to choose the brand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A benefit every competitor offers equally well is a point-of-parity; it gives no reason to choose one brand over another. A good USP is distinctive, valued and hard to copy.","source":"generated"}],"color":"#C8683A","short":"STP","emoji":"🎯","tag":"Segment, target, position","province":"Hispania","numeral":"VI"},{"chapter":7,"title":"Products and Brands","topics":[{"id":"t07-1","order":1,"title":"What a product really is","summary":"In marketing, a product is anything - a good, a service or even an idea - that satisfies a need in exchange for something of value. Marketers view every product as having three layers: the core benefit the customer is really buying, the actual product (brand, design, features), and the augmented extras such as warranties and repair service. Exam questions love to describe a feature and ask which layer it belongs to.","concepts":["c07-product","c07-core-product","c07-actual-product","c07-augmented-product"]},{"id":"t07-2","order":2,"title":"Classifying consumer and business products","summary":"Consumer products are grouped by how much effort and risk the shopper puts in: convenience, shopping, specialty and unsought - and the class depends on the individual consumer's behaviour, not the item. Each class calls for a different marketing mix. Business products are bought to make other products or run a business: production goods become part of the final product, support goods help produce it, and demand for all of them is derived from consumer demand.","concepts":["c07-convenience","c07-shopping","c07-specialty","c07-unsought","c07-business-products","c07-derived-demand"]},{"id":"t07-3","order":3,"title":"Product items, lines and the product mix","summary":"Companies organize what they sell from the smallest unit up: a product item (tracked by a SKU), product lines of closely related items, and the product mix made up of all the lines. Depth counts the items within a line, length the total number of items, and width (breadth) the number of different lines.","concepts":["c07-product-line","c07-product-mix"]},{"id":"t07-4","order":4,"title":"Brands and brand value","summary":"A brand is the name, phrase, symbol or design that distinguishes a product from competitors, and it must be developed and nurtured by marketing. The spoken part is the brand name and the unspoken part is the brand mark; trademarks, copyrights and patents protect different assets. Strong brands earn associations, equity, personality, loyalty and advocates, are managed as assets that need ongoing investment, and are often built through experiences and storytelling.","concepts":["c07-brand","c07-brand-image","c07-legal-protection","c07-brand-equity","c07-brand-personality","c07-brand-loyalty","c07-brand-advocate","c07-experiential-branding"]},{"id":"t07-5","order":5,"title":"Branding strategies and packaging","summary":"Firms decide who owns the brand (manufacturer, retailer's private label, no brand at all, or a mix) and how names are used across products (one family name, many separate brands, extensions, sub-brands, co-brands or licensing). Packaging and labelling then add functional, communication and perceptual benefits.","concepts":["c07-brand-types","c07-family-multibranding","c07-brand-extension","c07-co-branding","c07-brand-licensing","c07-packaging"]},{"id":"t07-6","order":6,"title":"Services and the four I's","summary":"Services are intangible activities or benefits, and they differ from goods in four ways: intangibility, inconsistency, inseparability and inventory. Each 'I' creates a specific marketing problem with a matching fix - tangible cues, standardization and training, managing the person-to-person service encounter, and smoothing demand to avoid costly idle production capacity.","concepts":["c07-intangibility","c07-inconsistency","c07-inseparability","c07-inventory"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"c07-product","topic":"t07-1","term":"Product (goods, services and ideas)","definition":"A good, a service, or an idea consisting of a bundle of tangible and intangible attributes that satisfies consumers' needs and is received in exchange for money or something else of value. A service is an intangible activity or benefit an organization provides to satisfy needs in exchange for money or something else of value.","explain":"In marketing, 'product' is much broader than a physical item. A bag of coffee (good), a WestJet flight (service) and a MADD campaign urging people not to drive impaired (idea) are all products, because each delivers attributes that satisfy a need in exchange for something of value. Some attributes can be touched (colour, size); others cannot (reputation, convenience, peace of mind).","example":"MADD Canada markets the idea of sober driving; Tim Hortons sells goods (coffee, doughnuts) bundled with a service (a fast drive-thru).","contrast":"A product is the whole bundle of attributes that satisfies a need; a brand is the name, phrase, symbol or design that distinguishes one company's product from competitors'. Goods are tangible, services are intangible activities or benefits, and ideas are causes or ways of thinking - all three count as products.","trap":"Watch for options that limit 'product' to physical goods, to tangible features only, or to things sold for money. Ideas (anti-impaired-driving, anti-smoking) and services (banking, haircuts) are products, and 'something else of value' means the exchange can be for time, a donation or a change in behaviour.","mnemonic":"G-S-I: Goods, Services and Ideas are all products.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-core-product","topic":"t07-1","term":"Total product concept and the core product","definition":"The total product concept holds that marketers view products as having three layers: the core product (the fundamental benefit the consumer derives from the product), the actual product (the physical good or delivered service, including branding, design and features), and the augmented product (additional features and benefits that accompany the actual product).","explain":"Think of the layers as an onion. At the centre is why the customer buys at all - the benefit (a bicycle provides transportation and leisure). The actual product wraps that benefit in a specific branded, designed, featured item, and the augmented layer adds extras such as a warranty and repair service. Under this concept, what customers ultimately buy is the BENEFITS of the product, not just its tangible features.","example":"Bicycle (Figure 7-2): core = provides transportation and leisure activity; actual = branded bicycle with a metal unisex frame, 10 speeds, aluminum alloy wheels and a padded seat; augmented = warranty, repair facilities, online FAQs and service videos.","contrast":"Core = the benefit or need solved ('getting to work', 'peace of mind', 'clean teeth'); actual = what you can see, touch or specify (brand name, design, features, packaging, quality level); augmented = what comes WITH it (warranty, repairs, delivery, installation, FAQs, customer support).","trap":"A question asking 'what do customers buy?' wants 'the benefits of the product' - not 'only the tangible features' and not 'the complete package of benefits and features'. If a stem describes the fundamental benefit or need solved (transportation, peace of mind, a healthy smile), it is CORE; the built-in feature that delivers it (10 speeds, a fluoride formula) is ACTUAL. Don't confuse this with packaging's 'functional benefit', which is part of the actual product. 'Formal product' and 'future product' are distractors, not layers in this course.","mnemonic":"C-A-A from the inside out: Core (why), Actual (what), Augmented (what else).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Total product concept (Figure 7-2, bicycle)","head":["Layer","What it is","Bicycle example","Typical cues"],"rows":[["Core","Fundamental benefit the consumer derives","Provides transportation and leisure activity","benefit, need solved, peace of mind"],["Actual","Physical product including branding, design and features","Branded bicycle, metal unisex frame, 10 speeds, aluminum alloy wheels, padded seat","brand name, design, features, packaging, quality level"],["Augmented","Additional features or benefits that come with the actual product","Warranty, repair facilities, online FAQs, service videos","warranty, repairs, delivery, installation, customer support"]]}},{"id":"c07-actual-product","topic":"t07-1","term":"Actual product","definition":"The middle layer of the total product concept: the physical good or the delivered service that supplies the core benefit, including its branding, design and features.","explain":"This is the product as you would describe it on a spec sheet - the brand name, the styling, the features, the quality level and the packaging. It is how a company turns an abstract benefit into a specific offering customers can compare, so most differentiation on features and design happens at this layer.","example":"A Canada Goose parka's actual product is its brand name, down fill, hood design, colours and arctic-rated shell; its core product is staying warm, and its augmented product includes the warranty that comes with it.","contrast":"Actual vs core: a feature (10 speeds, a 4K screen) is actual; the benefit it delivers (transportation, entertainment) is core. Actual vs augmented: anything built into or part of the item itself is actual; services or promises that come along with it (warranty, free installation, repairs) are augmented.","trap":"Brand name, packaging, design, colour and quality level are ACTUAL even though they can feel like 'extras'. Don't classify an impressive built-in feature as augmented - augmented items accompany the product rather than being part of it. 'Formal product' is another textbook's label and is not used in this course.","mnemonic":"Actual = what's in the box (and the box itself).","figure":null},{"id":"c07-augmented-product","topic":"t07-1","term":"Augmented product (including warranty)","definition":"The outer layer of the total product concept: additional features and benefits that accompany the actual product, such as a warranty, service contract, repair facilities, delivery, installation assistance, or a website and FAQs, used to distinguish it from competitive offerings. A warranty is a statement indicating the liability of the manufacturer for product deficiencies.","explain":"Augmentation is how companies stand out when actual products are similar. A warranty tells buyers the maker will stand behind the product; repair programs, free delivery, installation, tutorials and online support all sit in this layer. These extras accompany the product rather than being built into it.","example":"Patagonia repairs broken zippers and torn clothing long after purchase; a car maker gives its first 50 buyers a three-year warranty; an appliance store offers free installation and a 24/7 support line.","contrast":"Augmented vs actual: a feature built into the product (heated seats) is actual; a promise or service delivered alongside it (a three-year warranty, free tire changeovers) is augmented. Augmented vs core: the core is the basic benefit sought, not the extras around it.","trap":"Warranty, repair service, delivery, installation, online FAQs, service videos and support lines = AUGMENTED every time. Promotional framing ('the first 50 buyers get...') does not change the layer. Watch for the 'formal' and 'future' product distractors.","mnemonic":"Augmented = Additional, After-sale, Assurance.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-convenience","topic":"t07-2","term":"Convenience products","definition":"Consumer products that are purchased frequently, are inexpensive, and require minimal shopping effort; they have widespread distribution and mass advertising, and consumers are aware of brands but will accept substitutes.","explain":"Consumer products are classified by the effort and risk a shopper puts into buying them. Convenience items are the low-effort end - toothpaste, snacks, gum. Because buyers will not go out of their way, the marketer must place the product almost everywhere and keep the brand top of mind with mass advertising. The manner in which a consumer product is classified depends on the individual.","example":"A chocolate bar beside the till at Shoppers Drug Mart, or a toothpaste brand stocked in every grocery, drug and convenience store in Canada.","contrast":"Convenience vs shopping: convenience = buy with little thought and accept substitutes; shopping = compare price, quality and style across a few outlets first. Convenience vs unsought: convenience products are wanted and bought routinely; unsought products are unknown or not initially wanted.","trap":"Cue words: 'frequently', 'inexpensive', 'minimal effort', 'grabs whatever is available', 'widespread distribution', 'mass advertising'. Remember the classification depends on the individual - the same item can be a convenience product for one person and a shopping product for another.","mnemonic":"Convenience = Cheap, Common, Close at hand.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Consumer product classes (classification depends on the individual)","head":["Class","Consumer effort","Price","Distribution","Promotion","Brand loyalty","Examples"],"rows":[["Convenience","Minimal; bought frequently","Inexpensive","Widespread","Mass advertising","Aware of brands but will accept substitutes","Toothpaste, snacks, gum"],["Shopping","Compare alternatives on price, quality, style","Moderate","Fewer outlets","Emphasize differentiation","Limited brand preference","Clothing, appliances, cameras, furniture"],["Specialty","Significant effort to search for and buy","High","Exclusive or very limited","Stress uniqueness to a targeted audience","Very brand loyal; will not accept substitutes","Rolex, luxury cars, designer goods"],["Unsought","Doesn't know about it, or knows but doesn't initially want it","Varies","Varies","Aggressive promotion and personal selling","Will accept substitutes","Life insurance, burial plots, radical new products"]]}},{"id":"c07-shopping","topic":"t07-2","term":"Shopping products","definition":"Consumer products for which the consumer compares several alternatives on criteria such as price, quality and style; they are sold through fewer outlets at moderate prices, marketers emphasize differentiation, and brand preference is limited.","explain":"Shopping products deserve some homework: people visit a few stores or websites, read reviews and compare before buying a winter jacket, a fridge or a sofa. Because buyers compare, the marketer's job is to show how its offering differs on quality, style or value. Distribution doesn't need to be everywhere - just where comparison shoppers go.","example":"A couple in Edmonton compares three dishwashers at different retailers on price, energy rating and noise level before buying.","contrast":"Shopping vs specialty: shopping buyers compare alternatives and will switch brands; specialty buyers have already decided on one unique brand and will search hard for it, refusing substitutes. Shopping vs convenience: shopping involves deliberate comparison; convenience involves almost none.","trap":"Cue words: 'compares', 'visits several stores', 'weighs price, quality and style', 'reads reviews before deciding'. If the shopper insists on ONE brand only and travels far for it, it is specialty, not shopping - even for the same item. Price alone never decides the class.","mnemonic":"Shopping = Side-by-side comparison.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-specialty","topic":"t07-2","term":"Specialty products","definition":"Consumer products with unique characteristics for which the consumer makes a significant effort to search for and buy a specific brand; they have exclusive or very limited distribution, high prices, and very brand-loyal buyers who will not accept substitutes.","explain":"With specialty products the brand decision is made before the shopping starts - the buyer wants THIS watch or THIS car and will travel to get it. Marketers protect the brand's uniqueness: few outlets, premium prices and image-focused promotion.","example":"A Calgary collector waits months and travels to Vancouver to buy one specific Rolex model from an authorized dealer, refusing similar watches.","contrast":"Specialty vs shopping: specialty buyers do not compare alternatives - they search for one brand; shopping buyers compare several. An expensive item bought after comparison shopping is a shopping product for that buyer.","trap":"Cue words: 'will accept no substitute', 'significant effort to find', 'exclusive dealer', 'very brand loyal', 'unique'. Don't equate specialty with 'expensive'. The same product can be specialty for one person and shopping for another - classification depends on the individual.","mnemonic":"Specialty = 'Only this one will do.'","figure":null},{"id":"c07-unsought","topic":"t07-2","term":"Unsought products","definition":"Consumer products that the consumer either does not know about or knows about but does not initially want; they require aggressive promotion and personal selling.","explain":"Few people wake up wanting to buy life insurance or a burial plot, and nobody can want a radically new product they have never heard of. Because demand isn't there to begin with, marketers must create awareness and desire through aggressive promotion and personal selling.","example":"A life insurance adviser in Winnipeg contacts new parents; a start-up launching a never-before-seen home device must first make people aware the category exists.","contrast":"Unsought vs convenience: both may accept substitutes, but convenience goods are wanted and bought routinely, while unsought goods are unknown or unwanted until the marketer creates the need. Unsought vs specialty: specialty buyers actively hunt for the product; unsought buyers are unaware of it or avoid it.","trap":"Two triggers: 'doesn't know about it' (radical new products) OR 'knows about it but doesn't want it yet' (life insurance, burial plots). The marketing-mix cue is aggressive promotion and personal selling.","mnemonic":"Unsought = Unknown or Unwanted.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-business-products","topic":"t07-2","term":"Business products: production and support goods","definition":"Products organizations buy to make other products or run their operations. Production goods become part of the final product (raw materials such as grain or lumber; component parts such as engines or microchips). Support goods assist in producing other products (installations, accessory equipment, supplies and services).","explain":"The test is simple: does the item end up INSIDE the final product? If yes, it is a production good - a raw material if it is basically unprocessed, a component part if it is already manufactured. If it only helps the business make things or operate, it is a support good: installations are buildings and fixed equipment, accessory equipment is tools and office equipment, supplies are consumables (stationery, paper clips, brooms), and services are things like maintenance, repair, legal and consulting.","example":"For an Alberta truck maker: steel = raw material; engines = component parts; the plant and fixed assembly line = installations; power tools and office computers = accessory equipment; paper and brooms = supplies; an outside maintenance contractor = services.","contrast":"Production vs support: production goods become part of the finished product; support goods never do. Installations vs accessory equipment: installations are buildings and fixed equipment; accessory equipment is tools and office equipment. Supplies vs accessory equipment: supplies are used up, equipment is reused.","trap":"A microchip inside a phone is a COMPONENT PART (production), but a computer used in the accounting office is ACCESSORY EQUIPMENT (support) - what decides it is whether the item becomes part of the product. 'Services' here means business services such as maintenance, repair, legal and consulting.","mnemonic":"Production = in the product; Support = I-A-S-S (Installations, Accessory equipment, Supplies, Services).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Business products","head":["Group","Type","What it is","Examples"],"rows":[["Production","Raw materials","Basic inputs that become part of the final product","Grain, lumber"],["Production","Component parts","Manufactured items assembled into the final product","Engines, microchips"],["Support","Installations","Buildings and fixed equipment","Factory, fixed assembly line"],["Support","Accessory equipment","Tools and office equipment","Power tools, office computers"],["Support","Supplies","Items used up in running the business","Stationery, paper clips, brooms"],["Support","Services","Business services that help the firm operate","Maintenance, repair, legal, consulting"]]}},{"id":"c07-derived-demand","topic":"t07-2","term":"Derived demand","definition":"Demand for business products is derived from (driven by) demand for the consumer products they help produce.","explain":"Businesses don't buy paint-spraying equipment or steel for their own sake; they buy them because consumers want cars. When consumer demand rises, demand for the business inputs rises too, and when it falls, B2B suppliers feel it. A business marketer must therefore watch demand in the final consumer market.","example":"If consumer demand for Ford cars increases, Ford may increase its demand for paint-spraying equipment. If Canadians buy more oat milk, Prairie oat processors see orders rise.","contrast":"Derived demand vs consumer demand: consumer demand starts with end users; derived demand is the ripple effect on suppliers of business products. Derived demand is a characteristic of BUSINESS products, not a class of consumer products.","trap":"Distractors include 'primary demand', 'secondary demand', 'unit demand' and 'contrived demand'. Any stem linking a change in consumer purchases to a change in a business's purchases of inputs or equipment = derived demand.","mnemonic":"Derived = Downstream drives upstream.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-product-line","topic":"t07-3","term":"Product item (SKU), product line, line depth and length","definition":"A product item is a specific product with a unique brand, size or price, identified by a stock keeping unit (SKU). A product line is a group of products closely related because they satisfy a class of needs, are used together, are sold to the same customer group, are distributed through the same outlets, or fall within a given price range. Line depth is the number of product items within a line; line length is the total number of items.","explain":"Think shelf level first: the SKU is one specific item (one detergent scent in one bottle size). Group related items and you get a product line (all of a brand's laundry detergents). Depth tells you how many variations - sizes, scents, formats - a line carries; length counts the total number of items. Deepening a line lets a firm serve more needs without entering a new category.","example":"Build-a-Bear Workshop's teddy bears, designed as gifts for almost every occasion, form a product line because they satisfy one class of need - gift-giving. One specific birthday bear in one outfit and size is a product item with its own SKU.","contrast":"Item/SKU = one specific version; product line = a related group of items; product mix = ALL of a company's lines. Depth = variety inside one line; width = number of different lines in the mix.","trap":"'Satisfies a class of needs', 'used together', 'same customer group', 'same outlets' and 'same price range' all point to PRODUCT LINE. Distractors: product class, product category, product mix, marketing mix. A unique brand, size or price = product item; its unique identifier = SKU. Adding a new size or flavour deepens a line - it does not create a new line.","mnemonic":"Depth goes down one line; width goes across lines.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"From item to mix","head":["Term","Meaning","Example"],"rows":[["Product item / SKU","One specific product with a unique brand, size or price; the SKU is its unique identifier","One scent of a detergent in one bottle size"],["Product line","Closely related products (class of needs, used together, same customers, same outlets or same price range)","Build-a-Bear's gift teddy bears"],["Line depth","Number of product items within a line","A pasta-sauce line offered in 14 flavours and sizes"],["Line length","Total number of items","Every item the firm offers, counted together"],["Product mix","All the product lines a company offers","P&G: baby care, fabric and home care, beauty, health and grooming"],["Mix width (breadth)","Number of different product lines","Adding a pet-food line would make P&G's mix wider"]]}},{"id":"c07-product-mix","topic":"t07-3","term":"Product mix and mix width (breadth)","definition":"The product mix is all the product lines offered by a company; product mix width (or breadth) is the number of different product lines the company offers.","explain":"Step back from one line and look at everything a company sells. Procter & Gamble's mix includes baby care (Pampers), fabric and home care (Tide, Bounce), beauty, and health and grooming (Head & Shoulders, Old Spice) lines. Adding a brand-new line widens the mix; adding variations within an existing line only deepens that line.","example":"P&G's product mix spans baby care, fabric and home care, beauty, and health and grooming lines. If P&G added a pet-food line, its product mix would become wider.","contrast":"Product mix (all lines; measured by width) vs product line (one related group; measured by depth and length) vs marketing mix (the 4 Ps: product, price, place, promotion). Width counts lines; depth counts items within a line.","trap":"'Product mix' and 'marketing mix' sound alike - the marketing mix is the 4 Ps, not the assortment of products. Width = breadth = number of lines. A new flavour of an existing product does NOT widen the mix. In a count question, count lines for width, not items.","mnemonic":"Mix = Many lines; width = how many lines wide.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-brand","topic":"t07-4","term":"Brand, brand name and brand mark (logo)","definition":"A brand is a name, phrase, symbol or design, or a combination of these, uniquely given by a company to a product to distinguish it from the competition. The brand name is the part that can be spoken (words, letters, numbers); the brand mark or logo is the part that cannot be spoken (a symbol or design).","explain":"A brand is the identity that separates one company's offering from look-alike competitors, and it doesn't build itself - it needs to be developed and nurtured by marketing activity. Split it into what you can say ('Lululemon') and what you can only see (its stylized logo). Non-profits such as MADD have brands too.","example":"Lululemon's name (brand name) and its stylized logo (brand mark); MADD's name and lettering identify the charity and distinguish it from other causes.","contrast":"Brand name (spoken: 'Roots', 'WestJet', '7-Eleven') vs brand mark or logo (unspoken: the Nike swoosh, the Roots beaver). Brand vs product: the product is the bundle of attributes; the brand is the identity that distinguishes it. Brand vs trademark: the trademark is the LEGAL protection of a brand name or mark.","trap":"Numbers and letters that can be pronounced ('3M', 'H&M', '7-Eleven') are brand NAMES, not marks. A symbol with no pronounceable words is a brand mark. Brands need ongoing marketing - they are never created once and left alone.","mnemonic":"If you can SAY it, it's the name; if you can only SEE it, it's the mark.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-brand-image","topic":"t07-4","term":"Brand associations and brand image","definition":"Brand associations are the thoughts, feelings and perceptions linked to a brand in a consumer's memory; together they form the brand image and reflect what the brand promises to deliver.","explain":"When you finish the sentence 'Apple is ___' or 'Tim Hortons is ___', the word that pops into your head is a brand association. What makes a brand more than a product is its substance - authenticity - so marketers must understand a brand's key associations (compare how H&M, Gap and Zara are seen). Brands also offer an 'educational' opportunity: teach people why they should care, because perceptions shape brand relationships.","example":"Tim Hortons is associated with 'Canadian', 'hockey' and 'Roll Up To Win'; Endy worked to build perceptions of a comfortable, hassle-free Canadian mattress delivered in a box.","contrast":"Brand image/associations = what consumers think and feel about the brand; brand personality = the human traits linked to it (one kind of association); brand equity = the added VALUE those associations create; brand loyalty = favourable attitude plus repeat purchase over time.","trap":"Fill-in-the-blank exercises ('Apple is ___') and 'what comes to mind' questions point to associations/image. The difference between a brand and a product is substance (authenticity) and associations, not physical features. Perceptions matter even when they don't match the technical facts.","mnemonic":"Image = what's in the consumer's head.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-legal-protection","topic":"t07-4","term":"Trademark, copyright and patent","definition":"A trademark is legal protection identifying that the owner has the exclusive right to use a brand name or brand mark (registered with the Canadian Intellectual Property Office for 10 years, renewable). A copyright legally protects original written works, sound recordings or forms of communication from being copied by others (music, literature, performances). A patent protects inventions.","explain":"Each tool protects a different kind of asset. Brand identity (names and logos) is protected by trademarks; creative expression (songs, books, recordings, performances) by copyright; and new inventions (a new mechanism or device) by patents. Marketers rely on trademarks to stop competitors from using their names and marks.","example":"A Calgary brewery registers its name and grizzly logo as trademarks; a jingle written for its ads is protected by copyright; the new canning mechanism its engineers invented is protected by a patent.","contrast":"Trademark = brand name or mark (identity); copyright = written word, sound recording or form of communication (creative works); patent = invention (how something works). Brand loyalty and brand personality are marketing outcomes, not legal protections.","trap":"Copyright in this course lasts the creator's lifetime plus 50 YEARS - the exam answer is 50. (Canadian law actually extended copyright to life plus 70 years effective December 30, 2022, but the course material and test bank use lifetime plus 50.) Trademarks are registered for 10 years and are renewable. 'Written word, sound recording or form of communication' always = copyright.","mnemonic":"TM = Trade name or mark; copyright = Creative work; patent = Product invention.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Legal protection","head":["Protection","What it protects","Duration (course answer)","Example"],"rows":[["Trademark","Exclusive right to use a brand name or brand mark","10 years, renewable (CIPO)","A brewery's name and logo"],["Copyright","Original written works, sound recordings, forms of communication","Creator's lifetime + 50 years (exam answer)","A song, a novel, an ad jingle"],["Patent","Inventions","Not specified in course material","A new canning mechanism"]]}},{"id":"c07-brand-equity","topic":"t07-4","term":"Brand equity and brands as assets","definition":"Brand equity is the added value a brand name gives to a product beyond its functional benefits; it provides a competitive advantage and allows higher prices. Because brands are assets, marketers must invest money to maintain their value.","explain":"Picture two identical hoodies, one plain and one with a Roots logo - the extra amount people will pay for the Roots one is brand equity. Like a building or a machine, a brand is an asset that loses value without upkeep, so companies keep investing in quality, advertising and experiences. Rankings such as Interbrand's best global brands (Apple first in 2025) and Leger's most admired brands in Canada show how valuable strong brands become; they are examples, not lists to memorize.","example":"Interbrand's 2025 ranking places Apple, Microsoft, Amazon and Google at the top in financial brand value; a branded battery commands a higher price than an unbranded one with the same specifications.","contrast":"Brand equity (the value or premium the name adds) vs brand loyalty (an individual consumer's favourable attitude and repeat purchase) vs brand image (the associations). Loyalty and positive associations are SOURCES of equity; equity is the resulting value.","trap":"'Added value beyond functional benefits', 'charge a premium', 'worth more than the physical product' and 'competitive advantage' = brand equity. If brands are assets, the correct action is to INVEST money to maintain their value - not sell them, not build new ones regularly, not balance them against liabilities.","mnemonic":"Equity = Extra value; assets need upkeep.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-brand-personality","topic":"t07-4","term":"Brand personality","definition":"A set of human characteristics associated with a brand name.","explain":"If the brand were a person, who would it be? Marketers deliberately give brands traits - rugged, sophisticated, sincere, exciting, competent - through ads, characters, tone of voice and design, because consumers make emotional connections with brands much as they do with people.","example":"Roots projects a down-to-earth, outdoorsy, friendly personality; a luxury jeweller projects sophistication.","contrast":"Brand personality (human traits) vs brand image/associations (all the thoughts and feelings about the brand, of which personality is one kind) vs brand equity (the value added) vs brand loyalty (favourable attitude and repeat purchase over time).","trap":"Look for human adjectives - 'adventurous', 'sincere', 'playful', 'rugged', 'sophisticated' - or the phrase 'if the brand were a person'. Don't confuse personality with a brand advocate, who is a real person recommending the brand.","mnemonic":"Personality = the brand as a Person.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-brand-loyalty","topic":"t07-4","term":"Brand loyalty","definition":"A favourable attitude toward and consistent purchase of a single brand over time.","explain":"Loyalty has two parts: the attitude (the consumer likes the brand) and the behaviour (the consumer keeps buying it over time). Marketers work to associate brands with specific personality traits and to help consumers make emotional connections with their brands, because loyal customers keep coming back.","example":"A Calgary driver has bought only Toyota vehicles for 20 years and says she wouldn't consider another make.","contrast":"Loyalty = the customer's own favourable attitude + repeat buying; advocacy = actively telling others; equity = the value the brand earns as a result. Repeat buying driven only by discounts, without a favourable attitude, is not true loyalty.","trap":"Cues: 'over time', 'consistently buys', 'favourable attitude', 'emotional connection'. If the stem says the person RECOMMENDS the brand to others, the answer is brand advocate, not brand loyalty. Specialty products feature very high brand loyalty.","mnemonic":"Loyalty = Like it + keep buying it.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-brand-advocate","topic":"t07-4","term":"Brand advocate","definition":"A person who speaks well of a product by passing along positive comments to other people.","explain":"Advocates spread word of mouth - telling friends, posting reviews, defending the brand online. Because their comments come from fellow consumers rather than the company, marketers try to turn satisfied, loyal customers into advocates.","example":"An Edmonton runner who posts glowing reviews of her running shoes and convinces three teammates to buy the same brand.","contrast":"Brand advocate (tells others) vs brand loyalty (keeps buying with a favourable attitude) vs brand personality (human traits of the brand itself). 'Brand associate', 'brand preceptor' and 'personal brand' are distractors, not this concept.","trap":"Key cue: 'passes along positive comments to others' or 'recommends'. A loyal customer who never tells anyone is not an advocate, and an advocate need not be a long-time buyer.","mnemonic":"Advocate = Adds a voice.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-experiential-branding","topic":"t07-4","term":"Experiential branding and storytelling","definition":"Branding whose aim is not to sell you something, but to show how a brand can enrich a consumer's life; storytelling is essential to it.","explain":"Instead of 'buy this', experiential branding says 'here is what life with this brand feels like'. Events, pop-ups, community activities and brand stories let people live the brand. Storytelling gives the experience meaning and emotion, making the brand memorable and building relationships.","example":"Lululemon's free community yoga classes and run clubs; an outdoor brand's Banff pop-up offering guided snowshoe walks and founder stories with no sales pitch.","contrast":"Experiential branding (showing how the brand enriches life; no hard sell) vs a traditional sales promotion (a direct pitch, discount or feature list) vs brand licensing (renting the name to another firm).","trap":"Cues: 'not to sell you something', 'enrich the consumer's life', 'experience', 'storytelling'. If the stem focuses on a discount, a feature list or closing the sale, it is not experiential branding.","mnemonic":"Show, don't sell - and tell a story.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-brand-types","topic":"t07-5","term":"Manufacturer, private-label, generic and mixed branding","definition":"Manufacturer (national) branding: the producer owns the brand name. Private-label (store or house) branding: a retailer or wholesaler owns the brand, usually made for it by a manufacturer. Generic branding: no brand name, plain packaging, low price. Mixed branding: a firm markets products under its own name and under a reseller's (private-label) name.","explain":"The key question is WHO OWNS the brand name. Tide and Nike belong to their manufacturers. President's Choice (Loblaw), Kirkland (Costco) and Canadian Tire's Mastercraft and Motomaster belong to retailers even though another company makes them. Generic products skip branding to keep costs and prices low. Mixed branding lets a producer sell under its own label AND supply a retailer's private label.","example":"Canadian Tire carries manufacturer brands, its own private labels (Mastercraft tools, Motomaster batteries) and some unbranded basics; a cracker maker that sells under its own label and also makes a supermarket's store-brand crackers uses mixed branding.","contrast":"Private-label vs manufacturer: the brand owner is the retailer or wholesaler vs the producer - not who physically makes it. Private-label vs generic: private labels DO carry a brand name (PC, Kirkland); generics carry none. Mixed branding vs multibranding: mixed = the firm's own name + a reseller's name; multibranding = several of the firm's OWN names.","trap":"A store brand is still a brand (private-label), not a generic. A manufacturer brand is also called a national brand. 'Made by an outside manufacturer' does not make it a manufacturer brand - ownership of the name decides.","mnemonic":"Who owns the name? Maker = manufacturer; merchant = private label; nobody = generic; both = mixed.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Branding strategies at a glance","items":["Manufacturer (national) branding - producer owns the brand (Tide, Nike)","Private-label (store/house) branding - retailer or wholesaler owns the brand (President's Choice, Kirkland, Mastercraft, Motomaster)","Generic branding - no brand name, plain packaging, low price","Mixed branding - products sold under the firm's own name and a reseller's name","Multiproduct (family/umbrella/corporate) branding - one name for all products (Samsung, Heinz)","Brand extension - current brand name used to enter a new product class (Dove soap to Dove shampoo)","Sub-branding - corporate or family name combined with a new name (Porsche Cayenne, Gatorade G2)","Multibranding - a different name for each product or segment (Tide, Gain, Cheer; Black & Decker vs DeWalt)","Co-branding - two manufacturers' brand names on one product (Tim Hortons + Oreo; Nike + Apple)","Brand licensing - another firm uses the brand for a royalty or fee"]}},{"id":"c07-family-multibranding","topic":"t07-5","term":"Multiproduct (family) branding vs multibranding","definition":"Multiproduct branding (also called family, umbrella or corporate branding) uses one name for all of a company's products. Multibranding gives each product or market segment a different, distinct brand name.","explain":"One name or many? Samsung and Heinz put a single name on everything, so every product benefits from the same reputation and new products launch more easily - multiproduct branding makes brand extensions and sub-branding possible. Procter & Gamble instead sells Tide, Gain and Cheer under separate names so each detergent can target a different segment, and Stanley Black & Decker uses DeWalt for professionals and Black & Decker for homeowners.","example":"Multiproduct: Samsung phones, TVs and refrigerators; Heinz ketchup, beans and relish. Multibranding: P&G's Tide, Gain and Cheer; Black & Decker vs DeWalt.","contrast":"Family branding = one shared name (new products borrow its reputation, but a failure can reflect on every product); multibranding = separate names (each targets its own segment and is insulated from the others, but each must be built separately). Multibranding vs mixed branding: multibranding uses the firm's own names; mixed adds a reseller's private label.","trap":"Cues for family/multiproduct: 'all products carry the company name', 'umbrella', 'corporate'. Cues for multibranding: 'a different brand name for each product or segment', 'shoppers may not know the brands share an owner'. Don't confuse multibranding with multiproduct branding just because both start with 'multi'.","mnemonic":"Family = one family name; multibranding = many names.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-brand-extension","topic":"t07-5","term":"Brand extension and sub-branding","definition":"A brand extension uses a current brand name to enter a completely new product class. Sub-branding combines a corporate or family brand name with a new brand name.","explain":"Both strategies borrow an established name. A brand extension stretches the SAME name into a new category (Dove soap to Dove shampoo; Tide detergent to Tide stain remover), betting that the name's reputation will transfer. Sub-branding pairs the parent name with a new name to signal something distinct yet related (Porsche Cayenne, Gatorade G2).","example":"Brand extension: Dove moving from bar soap into shampoo. Sub-branding: Porsche Cayenne; Gatorade G2.","contrast":"Brand extension (same name, new product CLASS) vs sub-branding (parent name + a NEW name) vs co-branding (two different companies' names on one product) vs adding a size or flavour to an existing line (that deepens the line; it is not entering a new class).","trap":"If a new name appears next to the parent name, think sub-branding; if the parent name alone moves into a different category, think brand extension. A new scent of the same candle stays in the same product class, so it is not a brand extension.","mnemonic":"Extension = same name, new class; sub-brand = family name + new name.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-co-branding","topic":"t07-5","term":"Co-branding","definition":"Pairing two brand names of two manufacturers on a single product.","explain":"Co-branding lets each partner borrow the other's equity and reach the other's customers, because one product carries both names. It works best when the brands fit together and each adds something the other lacks.","example":"Tim Hortons and Oreo on a co-branded doughnut; Nike and Apple on running products.","contrast":"Co-branding (two companies' brands together on ONE product) vs brand licensing (one company lets another use its brand for a royalty or fee) vs sub-branding (one company's parent name + a new name) vs mixed branding (one firm sells under its own name and a reseller's name).","trap":"Count the companies: two different manufacturers' names on the same product = co-branding. Only one company's names = sub-branding. One firm paying a royalty to use another's name = licensing.","mnemonic":"Co = two brands Cooperating on one product.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-brand-licensing","topic":"t07-5","term":"Brand licensing","definition":"A contractual agreement whereby a company allows another firm to use its brand name, patent, trade secret or other property for a royalty or fee.","explain":"Licensing lets a brand owner earn income and extend its reach without making the product itself, while the licensee gets instant recognition. Because someone else makes the product, the owner must protect its brand by controlling quality - a poor licensed product can damage the name.","example":"A junior hockey team lets an apparel maker produce and sell team-logo toques in exchange for a royalty on each one sold.","contrast":"Licensing (another firm pays a royalty or fee to use the property) vs co-branding (two manufacturers' names jointly on one product) vs brand extension (the owner itself uses its name in a new product class).","trap":"Cues: 'royalty', 'fee', 'contractual agreement', 'allows another firm to use'. Licensing can cover patents and trade secrets, not only brand names.","mnemonic":"Licensing = leasing your name.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-packaging","topic":"t07-5","term":"Packaging and labelling benefits","definition":"Packaging is any container in which a product is offered for sale and on which label information is conveyed. It provides functional benefits (convenience, storage, protection, usability), communication benefits (label information, directions, nutrition facts, brand benefits) and perceptual benefits (shaping perceptions of quality and helping the brand stand out on the shelf). Labelling is an integral part of packaging.","explain":"Packaging first has to work (functional), then inform (communication), then persuade through its look and feel (perceptual). Tide PODS' single-use pods are a functional innovation; nutrition facts and directions communicate; a premium bottle or bold design signals quality and grabs attention on a crowded shelf.","example":"Tide PODS (functional: no measuring or pouring); a resealable cheese bag (functional: storage); a nutrition-facts table (communication); a sculpted glass bottle that looks premium (perceptual).","contrast":"Functional = makes the product easier to use, store or protect; communication = tells you something (information, directions, nutrition, brand benefits); perceptual = changes how you perceive the brand (quality impression, standing out). Packaging is part of the ACTUAL product layer, not the augmented layer.","trap":"If the packaging changes how the product is used (no measuring, resealable, easy-pour, protective), it is FUNCTIONAL - even if it also looks good. 'Communicate information about the product' and 'help the brand stand out' are the most common distractors.","mnemonic":"F-C-P: Function first, then Communicate, then Perception.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Benefits of packaging","items":["Functional - convenience, storage, protection, usability (Tide PODS single-use pods)","Communication - label information, directions, nutrition facts, brand benefits","Perceptual - shapes perceptions of quality and helps the brand stand out on the shelf"]}},{"id":"c07-intangibility","topic":"t07-6","term":"Intangibility (services)","definition":"Services are intangible activities or benefits; intangibility means they cannot be touched, seen or felt before purchase, which makes them hard for consumers to evaluate in advance.","explain":"You can test-drive a car, but you can't try a massage, a tax return or a haircut before buying it. Marketers reduce this uncertainty by making the service tangible: testimonials, photos and videos, credentials, well-designed facilities and uniforms act as tangible cues.","example":"A massage centre posts customer testimonials on its website; an Edmonton law firm displays its credentials in a professional office.","contrast":"Intangibility (can't see or touch before buying - add tangible cues) vs inconsistency (quality varies - standardize and train) vs inseparability (produced and consumed at the same time; the deliverer is part of the service) vs inventory (can't be stored - manage capacity and demand).","trap":"Testimonials, reviews, photos, virtual tours, credentials, attractive facilities and 'make the service visible' = intangibility. 'Incongruity' is a made-up distractor, not one of the four I's.","mnemonic":"Intangible = invisible, so make it visible.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"The four I's of services","head":["Characteristic","What it means","Typical marketing response"],"rows":[["Intangibility","Cannot be touched or seen before purchase","Tangible cues: testimonials, facilities, photos, credentials"],["Inconsistency","Quality varies with the people who deliver it","Standardization and training"],["Inseparability","Consumer cannot separate the deliverer from the service; produced and consumed at the same time","Focus on the people and the service encounter"],["Inventory","Cannot be stored; demand fluctuates; idle production capacity when supply exceeds demand (expensive)","Off-peak pricing, reservations, part-time staff"]]}},{"id":"c07-inconsistency","topic":"t07-6","term":"Inconsistency (services)","definition":"The quality of a service varies because it depends on the people who provide it, as well as when and where it is delivered.","explain":"Two baristas, two tellers, two physiotherapy sessions - the same service can be excellent one day and poor the next because people aren't machines. Firms fight inconsistency with standardization (procedures, checklists, technology) and training.","example":"A Calgary car-wash chain writes a 12-step checklist and trains every attendant on it so each wash meets the same standard.","contrast":"Inconsistency (quality varies across employees or occasions) vs inseparability (production and consumption happen together, so the provider is part of the service). Training to make quality uniform targets inconsistency.","trap":"Cues: 'varies', 'depends on which employee', 'different every visit', 'standardize', 'training program'. Don't pick inseparability just because a person is involved - ask whether the problem is VARIATION in quality.","mnemonic":"Inconsistency = 'It depends who you get.'","figure":null},{"id":"c07-inseparability","topic":"t07-6","term":"Inseparability (services)","definition":"Consumers cannot separate the deliverer of the service from the service itself; the service is produced and consumed at the same time.","explain":"A haircut is created while you sit in the chair, and a concert happens as you listen. The customer is usually present, and the person delivering the service is, in the customer's eyes, the service - so the person-to-person encounter shapes how the whole service is judged.","example":"A client's opinion of a dental clinic is inseparable from the hygienist who cleans her teeth; a comedian's show is produced and consumed at the same moment.","contrast":"Inseparability (simultaneous production and consumption; the provider is the service) vs inconsistency (quality varies between deliveries) vs intangibility (cannot be seen before purchase).","trap":"Cues: 'at the same time', 'simultaneously', 'customer must be present', 'clients judge the service by the person delivering it', 'can't separate'. If the stem is about variation in quality from one employee to another, it is inconsistency instead.","mnemonic":"Inseparable = provider and service are glued together.","figure":null},{"id":"c07-inventory","topic":"t07-6","term":"Inventory (services) and idle production capacity","definition":"Services cannot necessarily be stored and accessed when in demand; fluctuating demand makes it hard to assess the capacity needed at peak times, and idle production capacity - when the supply of a service exceeds demand - is expensive.","explain":"An empty airline seat or an unused hotel room tonight is revenue lost forever; it can't be put on a shelf for next weekend. Service firms smooth demand and capacity with off-peak pricing, reservations and appointments, and part-time staff for peak periods.","example":"A Banff hotel cuts weekday rates in November to fill rooms that would otherwise sit empty; a tax-preparation firm hires part-time staff for March and April.","contrast":"Inventory (perishability; mismatched supply and demand; idle capacity) vs intangibility (can't see or touch). Idle production capacity = supply EXCEEDS demand - not demand exceeding supply.","trap":"Cues: 'empty seats', 'unused appointments', 'off-peak pricing', 'reservations', 'peak demand', 'idle capacity'. Idle production capacity means the supply of the service exceeds demand, and it is expensive because costs continue.","mnemonic":"Unsold service time can't be inventoried.","figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"a07-01","type":"sort","concept":"c07-core-product","title":"Core, actual or augmented?","prompt":"A Calgary family buys a minivan from Bow Valley Motors. Drag each item to the layer of the total product concept it belongs to.","buckets":["Core product","Actual product","Augmented product"],"items":[{"text":"Safe, reliable transportation for the whole family","bucket":0},{"text":"Peace of mind on long family road trips","bucket":0},{"text":"Sliding doors, third-row seating and all-wheel drive","bucket":1},{"text":"The brand name and sleek exterior design","bucket":1},{"text":"Heated seats built into the front row","bucket":1},{"text":"Quality level of the interior materials","bucket":1},{"text":"Five-year powertrain warranty","bucket":2},{"text":"Free roadside assistance for three years","bucket":2},{"text":"Online owner FAQs and how-to service videos","bucket":2},{"text":"Free first oil change at the dealership","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Core = the fundamental benefit sought (transportation, peace of mind). Actual = the product itself, including brand name, design, built-in features and quality level. Augmented = extras that accompany the product: warranty, roadside assistance, FAQs and service videos, free service - exactly like the warranty, repair facilities and service videos in the bicycle example."},{"id":"a07-02","type":"sort","concept":"c07-intangibility","title":"Which of the four I's?","prompt":"Each card describes a service challenge or a marketer's response to one. Drag it to the characteristic of services it reflects.","buckets":["Intangibility","Inconsistency","Inseparability","Inventory"],"items":[{"text":"A massage centre posts customer testimonials on its website","bucket":0},{"text":"A law firm displays its credentials in a polished office to reassure prospects","bucket":0},{"text":"A daycare offers virtual tours so parents can 'see' the care before enrolling","bucket":0},{"text":"Car-wash quality depends on which attendant is on shift","bucket":1},{"text":"A coffee chain trains every barista on the same 10-step recipe","bucket":1},{"text":"A hotel chain writes standard check-in procedures for every location","bucket":1},{"text":"A haircut is produced while the client sits in the chair","bucket":2},{"text":"Patients judge a clinic largely by the hygienist who treats them","bucket":2},{"text":"A comedian's show is created and enjoyed at the same moment","bucket":2},{"text":"Empty seats on a flight that has departed can never be sold","bucket":3},{"text":"A golf course offers cheaper tee times on weekday mornings","bucket":3},{"text":"A tax-preparation firm hires part-time staff for March and April","bucket":3}],"explanation":"Intangibility is handled with tangible cues (testimonials, credentials, virtual tours). Inconsistency means quality varies with the people delivering it, so firms standardize and train. Inseparability means the service is produced and consumed at the same time and customers can't separate the deliverer from the service. Inventory means services can't be stored, so firms fight idle capacity with off-peak pricing and flexible staffing."},{"id":"a07-03","type":"match","concept":"c07-family-multibranding","title":"Match the branding strategy","prompt":"Match each branding strategy with its example.","pairs":[{"left":"Private-label branding","right":"President's Choice products sold at Loblaws"},{"left":"Mixed branding","right":"A cracker maker sells under its own label and also makes a supermarket's store-brand crackers"},{"left":"Multiproduct (family) branding","right":"Samsung's name on its phones, TVs and refrigerators"},{"left":"Multibranding","right":"P&G sells Tide, Gain and Cheer to different segments"},{"left":"Brand extension","right":"Dove moves from bar soap into shampoo"},{"left":"Sub-branding","right":"Porsche Cayenne"},{"left":"Co-branding","right":"A doughnut carrying both the Tim Hortons and Oreo names"},{"left":"Brand licensing","right":"A hockey team lets an apparel firm use its logo for a royalty"}],"explanation":"Ask two questions: who owns the name (manufacturer, retailer's private label, or both = mixed), and how names are used (one name for everything = family; separate names = multibranding; same name into a new class = extension; parent name + new name = sub-brand; two manufacturers' names on one product = co-branding; renting the name for a fee = licensing)."},{"id":"a07-04","type":"sort","concept":"c07-convenience","title":"Classify the purchase","prompt":"Classify each consumer's behaviour as a convenience, shopping, specialty or unsought purchase. Remember: the class depends on the individual's behaviour.","buckets":["Convenience","Shopping","Specialty","Unsought"],"items":[{"text":"Grabbing whatever gum is at the checkout","bucket":0},{"text":"Buying any toothpaste brand that's in stock at the corner store","bucket":0},{"text":"Picking up a snack bar at a gas station","bucket":0},{"text":"Comparing three sofas at different stores on price and style","bucket":1},{"text":"Reading reviews and comparing fridges at two retailers","bucket":1},{"text":"Travelling to Vancouver for one specific Rolex model, accepting no substitute","bucket":2},{"text":"Waiting months for one luxury car brand from its exclusive dealer","bucket":2},{"text":"Being approached by an adviser about life insurance you hadn't planned to buy","bucket":3},{"text":"Hearing about a radically new gadget you never knew existed","bucket":3},{"text":"Pre-planning a burial plot after a salesperson's call","bucket":3}],"explanation":"Convenience = frequent, low-effort purchases where substitutes are fine. Shopping = comparing alternatives on price, quality and style. Specialty = significant effort for one specific brand with no substitutes. Unsought = products the consumer doesn't know about or doesn't initially want, which require aggressive promotion and personal selling."},{"id":"a07-05","type":"sort","concept":"c07-business-products","title":"Production or support good?","prompt":"Rocky Mountain Motors builds pickup trucks. Sort each purchase into production goods or support goods.","buckets":["Production goods","Support goods"],"items":[{"text":"Steel for the truck bodies","bucket":0},{"text":"Engines installed in each truck","bucket":0},{"text":"Microchips for the dashboard displays","bucket":0},{"text":"Tires mounted on every finished truck","bucket":0},{"text":"Paint applied to every truck body","bucket":0},{"text":"The assembly plant building","bucket":1},{"text":"Power tools used on the line","bucket":1},{"text":"Computers in the accounting office","bucket":1},{"text":"Paper, paper clips and brooms","bucket":1},{"text":"Outside maintenance and legal services","bucket":1}],"explanation":"Production goods (raw materials such as steel; component parts such as engines, chips and tires) become part of the finished truck. Support goods help make the truck but never become part of it: installations (the plant), accessory equipment (tools, office computers), supplies (paper, brooms) and services (maintenance, legal)."},{"id":"a07-06","type":"match","concept":"c07-brand-equity","title":"Brand concepts in action","prompt":"Match each brand concept with the example that illustrates it.","pairs":[{"left":"Brand equity","right":"Shoppers pay $55 more for a branded hoodie than for an identical unbranded one"},{"left":"Brand personality","right":"A brand described as rugged, honest and outdoorsy"},{"left":"Brand loyalty","right":"Buying only one toothpaste brand for 15 years because you like it"},{"left":"Brand advocate","right":"Posting a five-star review and urging friends to buy"},{"left":"Brand associations","right":"Completing 'Tim Hortons is ___' with 'Canadian'"},{"left":"Trademark","right":"Registering a name and logo with CIPO for exclusive use"},{"left":"Copyright","right":"Protecting a song written for an ad from being copied"},{"left":"Brand mark","right":"A symbol that is part of the brand but cannot be spoken"}],"explanation":"Equity = added value beyond function; personality = human traits; loyalty = favourable attitude plus repeat purchase over time; advocate = passes positive comments to others; associations = thoughts and feelings in memory; trademark = legal right to a brand name or mark; copyright = protection for creative works (course answer: lifetime plus 50 years); brand mark = the unspoken part of the brand."}],"questions":[{"id":"q07-001","concept":"c07-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In marketing, a product is best defined as:","options":["A good, service or idea consisting of a bundle of tangible and intangible attributes that satisfies needs in exchange for something of value","A physical good with tangible attributes that a manufacturer sells to consumers in exchange for money","A name, phrase, symbol or design that distinguishes one company's offering from those of competitors","The fundamental benefit a consumer derives from buying and using a good"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A product is a good, a service or an idea - a bundle of tangible and intangible attributes received in exchange for money or something else of value. Limiting it to physical goods sold for money is too narrow; 'name, phrase, symbol or design' defines a brand, and 'fundamental benefit' is only the core layer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-002","concept":"c07-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"MADD Canada runs a campaign persuading young Albertans to plan a safe ride home instead of driving impaired. Nothing is sold and no fee is charged. From a marketing perspective, what MADD is offering is:","options":["Not a product, because no money changes hands","Not a product, because nothing tangible is exchanged","A product - an idea exchanged for something of value other than money","A product line, because the campaign satisfies a class of needs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Products include goods, services AND ideas, and the exchange can be for 'something else of value' - here, a change in behaviour. Lacking tangibility or money does not disqualify it; a product line is a group of closely related products, not a single campaign idea.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-003","concept":"c07-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT consistent with the marketing definition of a product?","options":["A product includes intangible attributes such as reputation and convenience","A product can be an idea, such as a campaign against texting while driving","A product can be exchanged for something of value other than money","A product must be a tangible item that can be examined before purchase"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The definition covers services and ideas, which can't be touched, so 'must be tangible' contradicts it. A product is a bundle of tangible AND intangible attributes, and the exchange can be for money or something else of value.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-004","concept":"c07-product","also":["c07-brand"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ravi tells his study group, 'Products and brands are the same thing - Lululemon is a product.' Which response best corrects him?","options":["A product needs to be developed and nurtured by marketing, while a brand forms on its own once sales begin","A product is the bundle of attributes that satisfies a need; a brand is the name, symbol or design that distinguishes it from competitors","A brand is the bundle of tangible attributes that satisfies a need; a product is the image consumers hold of it","A product is always a physical good that can be touched, while a brand is always a service or an idea"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The product is the good, service or idea (the leggings) with its bundle of attributes; the brand (Lululemon) is the name, symbol or design that distinguishes it and must be developed and nurtured by marketing. Products aren't limited to physical goods, and brands don't arise on their own.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-005","concept":"c07-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"Why does a marketer treat a haircut as a 'product' even though the customer takes nothing tangible home?","options":["Because services - intangible activities or benefits provided in exchange for something of value - are one kind of product","Because the barber uses tangible tools such as scissors and clippers to deliver it","Because the salon's name and logo make it a brand rather than a service","Because anything sold for money is classified as a good under the total product concept"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A service is an intangible activity or benefit that satisfies needs in exchange for money or something else of value, and services are one of the three kinds of products (goods, services, ideas). The tools used are not what the customer buys, and a brand name doesn't change what the offering is.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-006","concept":"c07-core-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"Knowing what you do about the total product concept, what do customers buy when purchasing a product?","options":["Only the tangible features.","The complete package of benefits and features.","The additional services and benefits.","The benefits of the product."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Marketers view products as having three layers (core, actual, augmented), and the core product - the fundamental benefit the consumer derives - is what customers ultimately buy. 'The complete package of benefits and features' is the tempting distractor, but the course answer is the benefits; 'additional services' describes the augmented layer and 'tangible features' the actual layer.","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-007","concept":"c07-core-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"SnugNest sells baby monitors with HD video, a crying-detection alert and a two-year warranty. In focus groups, parents say what they really want is 'to sleep knowing my baby is safe.' Under the total product concept, this peace of mind is the:","options":["Augmented product","Product item","Core product","Actual product"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The core product is the fundamental benefit the consumer derives - here, reassurance that the baby is safe. The HD video and alert are features of the actual product, and the warranty is part of the augmented product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-008","concept":"c07-core-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Moving from the innermost layer outward, the three layers of the total product concept are:","options":["Core, augmented, actual","Actual, core, augmented","Augmented, actual, core","Core, actual, augmented"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The core benefit sits at the centre, the actual product (branding, design, features) wraps it, and the augmented product (warranty, repairs, FAQs) forms the outer layer. Putting 'actual' first confuses the physical product with the benefit it delivers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-009","concept":"c07-core-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"In the textbook's bicycle example of the total product concept, which pairing of item and layer is correct?","options":["Padded seat and branded metal frame - core product","Provides transportation and leisure activity - core product","Ten speeds and aluminum alloy wheels - augmented product","Online FAQs and service videos - actual product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In Figure 7-2 the core is the benefit (transportation and leisure); features such as 10 speeds, alloy wheels, the padded seat and branding make up the actual product; and the warranty, repair facilities, online FAQs and service videos are augmented. The other pairings each put an item one layer off: the seat and frame and the speeds and wheels are actual, and the FAQs and videos are augmented.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-010","concept":"c07-core-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Calgary toothpaste maker's ads list its fluoride formula, mint gel and new pump dispenser, yet it keeps losing share to a rival whose ads promise 'a confident, healthy smile.' Based on the total product concept, what should the maker change?","options":["Add more technical features to the actual product so its ads have more to list","Widen its product mix by launching a new line of mouthwash","Lead its messaging with the core benefit customers seek, such as healthy teeth and confidence","Offer a longer warranty, since augmented features are what customers ultimately buy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Under the total product concept, customers ultimately buy the benefits (the core), so a features-only message misses why people buy. More features (actual) or extras (augmented) don't fix a message that never connects to the core benefit, and widening the mix doesn't address the problem.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-011","concept":"c07-core-product","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Under the total product concept, a customer buying a power drill is ultimately buying its tangible features, such as the motor and battery, so marketers should focus only on the actual product layer.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Customers ultimately buy the benefits of a product - the core layer (for a drill, the ability to make holes and finish the job). Features belong to the actual layer, and marketers manage all three layers but must connect them to the core benefit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-012","concept":"c07-actual-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northline Bikes advertises its new e-bike's carbon frame, 500-watt motor, integrated lights and matte-black finish, all sold under the Northline name. These elements make up the e-bike's:","options":["Actual product","Core product","Augmented product","Product mix"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The actual product is the physical product including its branding, design and features - the frame, motor, lights, finish and brand name. The core would be the benefit (easy, fun transportation), and augmented would be extras such as a warranty or service plan.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-013","concept":"c07-actual-product","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the total product concept, the physical good or delivered service - including its branding, design and features - is called the _____ product.","options":["augmented","actual","future","core"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The actual layer is the product itself - brand, design, features, packaging and quality level. The core is the underlying benefit, the augmented layer is the extras that accompany it (warranty, repairs), and 'future product' is not one of the three layers used in this course.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-014","concept":"c07-actual-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Canadian Tire customer buys a snow blower. Which item belongs to the ACTUAL product rather than the augmented product?","options":["A how-to video library on the retailer's website","Free assembly and delivery to the customer's home","The two-year parts-and-labour warranty","The heated hand grips built into the handles"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Heated grips are a built-in feature, so they are part of the actual product. A warranty, free assembly and delivery, and online how-to videos accompany the product rather than being part of it, so they are augmented.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-015","concept":"c07-actual-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a new smartphone, which of the following is NOT part of the actual product?","options":["The brand name and logo on the back","A one-year extended repair plan","The phone's colour and design","The 6.5-inch display and triple camera"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A repair plan is an additional service that comes with the phone, which makes it augmented. Brand name, features such as the display and camera, and design or colour are all elements of the actual product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-016","concept":"c07-actual-product","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A premium chocolate maker's elegant gold-foil box is part of the augmented product because it is an extra the customer doesn't eat.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Packaging, like branding, design and features, is part of the ACTUAL product. The augmented product is the additional services and benefits that accompany it - a warranty, delivery, installation or customer support.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-017","concept":"c07-actual-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two Saskatoon cafes sell nearly identical lattes. Cafe Prairie wants to differentiate at the actual-product layer. Which move does that?","options":["Introduce a signature oat-honey latte served in a distinctive branded cup","Offer a satisfaction guarantee that replaces any drink for free","Promote coffee as 'the energy you need to start your day'","Add a free delivery service for orders placed through its app"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing the drink's features, design and branded packaging works on the actual product. A satisfaction guarantee and free delivery are extras that accompany the product (augmented), and 'energy to start your day' describes the core benefit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-018","concept":"c07-augmented-product","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fast & Swift, a sedan manufacturer, has introduced Spectra, a leisure car that boasts of an eco-friendly model at a comparatively lower price. The first 50 buyers are given a three-year warranty on their purchase. The three-year warranty provided to the first 50 buyers refers to the _____ product.","options":["formal","core","augmented","future"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A warranty is an additional feature that accompanies the actual product, so it belongs to the augmented product (along with service contracts, delivery, installation and support websites). The core would be the car's basic benefit (transportation), and 'formal' and 'future' are not layers of the total product concept in this course.","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-019","concept":"c07-augmented-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Patagonia is known for their focus on sustainability; they stand behind their products and will repair broken zippers or torn clothing long after you've purchased to keep it out of the landfill. This forms part of their:","options":["core product","actual product","augmented product","future product","none of the above"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Repairing zippers and torn clothing long after purchase is an after-sale service that accompanies the product - the augmented layer. It isn't part of the garment itself (actual) or the basic benefit of staying warm and covered (core).","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-020","concept":"c07-augmented-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement best describes a warranty?","options":["Label information that describes directions, ingredients and nutrition","Legal protection giving the owner the exclusive right to use a brand name or mark","A statement indicating the liability of the manufacturer for product deficiencies","A contractual agreement allowing another firm to use a brand name for a fee"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A warranty states the manufacturer's liability for product deficiencies and is a classic augmented-product element. Exclusive rights to a brand name or mark describe a trademark, label information is a communication benefit of packaging, and the fee arrangement is brand licensing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-021","concept":"c07-augmented-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Maple Home Appliances sells a dishwasher with a stainless-steel tub, a quiet 42-decibel motor, free installation and a 24/7 support line. Which pair lists ONLY augmented-product elements?","options":["The quiet motor and free installation","The stainless-steel tub and the 24/7 support line","The stainless-steel tub and the quiet motor","Free installation and the 24/7 support line"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Installation and customer support accompany the product, so they are augmented. The tub and the motor are built-in features of the actual product, so any pair that includes them mixes layers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-022","concept":"c07-augmented-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why would a company invest in the augmented layer - extended warranties, free repairs, online support - when its actual product already performs well?","options":["To reduce the number of items in its product line","To distinguish its offering from competitors whose actual products are similar","To change the core benefit that customers are seeking","To move the product from the shopping class to the convenience class"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Augmented features are additional benefits used to distinguish a product from competitive offerings, especially when actual products are hard to tell apart. They don't change the core benefit, and they have nothing to do with line length or the consumer product class.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-023","concept":"c07-augmented-product","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following would NOT be part of a new laptop's augmented product?","options":["A backlit keyboard built into the laptop","A three-year on-site repair plan","Free next-day home delivery","Online FAQs and setup tutorial videos"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A backlit keyboard is a built-in feature, so it belongs to the actual product. Delivery, a repair plan and online FAQs or videos are extras that accompany the product - augmented, just like the warranty and service videos in the bicycle example.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-024","concept":"c07-convenience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Every morning on her way to work in Regina, Mei grabs whatever brand of chewing gum is at the gas-station till without comparing options. For Mei, gum is a:","options":["Specialty product","Unsought product","Convenience product","Shopping product"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Frequent, inexpensive purchases made with minimal effort, where substitutes are acceptable, are convenience products. A shopping product would involve comparing price, quality or style before buying.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-025","concept":"c07-convenience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A new Canadian granola-bar brand will be marketed as a convenience product. Which marketing-mix plan fits that classification?","options":["Moderate price, a few selected outlets, and messages explaining how it differs from rivals","Premium price, exclusive distribution through one flagship store, and image advertising","Aggressive personal selling to make consumers aware that they need the product","Low price, widespread distribution in grocery, drug and convenience stores, and mass advertising"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Convenience products are inexpensive, need widespread distribution because buyers won't search for them, and rely on mass advertising. The exclusive plan fits specialty products, selected outlets with differentiation fits shopping products, and aggressive personal selling fits unsought products.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-026","concept":"c07-convenience","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Convenience products are bought frequently with minimal shopping effort; consumers are aware of brands but will usually accept a substitute.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Convenience products are frequent, inexpensive purchases with minimal effort, widespread distribution and mass advertising; buyers know the brands but accept substitutes - unlike specialty buyers, who refuse them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-027","concept":"c07-convenience","also":["c07-shopping"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Liam buys whatever paper towels are on sale at the nearest grocery store. His neighbour Grace visits three stores comparing price, absorbency and sheet count before buying paper towels in bulk. How should paper towels be classified?","options":["Convenience for Liam and shopping for Grace, because classification depends on the individual","Specialty for Grace, because she makes a significant effort before buying","Shopping for both, because at least one consumer compares alternatives","Convenience for both, because paper towels are inexpensive and bought frequently"],"answer":0,"explanation":"How a consumer product is classified depends on the individual's effort and behaviour. Grace compares price and quality across stores, which is shopping behaviour; specialty would require insisting on one unique brand and refusing substitutes, which she doesn't do.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-028","concept":"c07-convenience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which characteristic does NOT describe a convenience product?","options":["It is purchased frequently and is relatively inexpensive","It is supported by mass advertising","Consumers are very brand loyal and will not accept substitutes","It is distributed widely through many outlets"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Refusing substitutes and very high brand loyalty describe specialty products. Convenience products are frequent, inexpensive, widely distributed and mass advertised, and their buyers will accept substitutes.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-029","concept":"c07-shopping","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before buying a winter coat, Aisha visits four Edmonton stores, comparing price, warmth ratings and style, and she is open to whichever brand offers the best combination. For Aisha, the coat is a:","options":["Convenience product","Shopping product","Unsought product","Specialty product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Comparing several alternatives on price, quality and style while staying open to different brands is shopping-product behaviour. A specialty product would mean she wanted one specific brand and would accept no substitute.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-030","concept":"c07-shopping","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Products such as clothing, appliances and furniture, for which consumers compare several alternatives on price, quality and style, are called _____ products.","options":["convenience","unsought","specialty","shopping"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Shopping products are compared on price, quality and style and are sold through fewer outlets at moderate prices. Convenience products involve minimal comparison, specialty products involve searching for one brand, and unsought products aren't initially wanted.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-031","concept":"c07-shopping","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Breeze Appliances sells mid-priced refrigerators through a few major retailers. Knowing its fridges are shopping products, what should Breeze emphasize in its marketing?","options":["Persuasive personal selling to convince people they need a fridge at all","How its fridges differ from competitors' on features, quality and value","Availability in every corner store so buyers never need to search","Exclusivity and a refusal to discount, so buyers seek out one authorized dealer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because shopping-product buyers compare alternatives, marketers emphasize differentiation and sell through fewer outlets at moderate prices. Everywhere distribution suits convenience products, exclusivity suits specialty products, and creating the need suits unsought products.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-032","concept":"c07-shopping","also":["c07-specialty"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Daniel and Priya both buy high-end cameras. Daniel compared five models online and in stores before choosing one. Priya would only buy a Leica and drove from Red Deer to Calgary's sole authorized dealer to get it. Which classification is correct?","options":["Shopping product for both, because cameras are listed as shopping goods","Specialty product for Daniel; shopping product for Priya","Shopping product for Daniel; specialty product for Priya","Specialty product for both, because the cameras are expensive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Classification depends on the individual's behaviour, not the price tag or a textbook list. Daniel compared alternatives (shopping); Priya insisted on one brand and made a significant effort to get it (specialty).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-033","concept":"c07-shopping","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Shopping products need the widest possible distribution because consumers will not go out of their way to find them.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. That describes convenience products. Shopping products are sold through fewer outlets because consumers are willing to visit several stores to compare price, quality and style before buying.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-034","concept":"c07-specialty","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Marcel has wanted one particular Swiss watch model for years. When no Calgary jeweller carried it, he flew to Toronto to buy it at the brand's boutique and turned down the salesperson's suggestion of a similar model. For Marcel, the watch is a:","options":["Convenience product","Shopping product","Unsought product","Specialty product"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Unique characteristics, a significant effort to search, and refusal to accept substitutes define a specialty product. Marcel never compared alternatives, so it isn't a shopping product for him.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-035","concept":"c07-specialty","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which set of characteristics describes specialty products?","options":["Unique characteristics, very brand-loyal buyers, exclusive distribution and high prices","Little consumer awareness or desire, supported by aggressive personal selling","Comparison on price, quality and style, fewer outlets and moderate prices","Frequent purchase, low prices, widespread distribution and mass advertising"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Specialty products have unique characteristics, exclusive or very limited distribution, high prices and very brand-loyal buyers. The 'frequent purchase, low price' set describes convenience products, the 'comparison on price, quality and style' set describes shopping products, and 'little awareness or desire' describes unsought products.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-036","concept":"c07-specialty","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Aurora Timepieces, a Canadian luxury watchmaker with fiercely loyal buyers, is offered a deal to stock its watches in every discount department store in Canada. Given its products' classification, what is the best response?","options":["Decline, because specialty products depend on aggressive personal selling to create awareness","Decline, because specialty products rely on exclusive or very limited distribution that protects their uniqueness","Accept, but cut prices so the watches can compete with convenience goods","Accept, because wider distribution always strengthens products with brand-loyal buyers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Specialty products use exclusive or very limited distribution and high prices; mass distribution would undermine the uniqueness loyal buyers seek. Aggressive personal selling to create awareness describes unsought products, not specialty products.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-037","concept":"c07-specialty","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about specialty products is NOT accurate?","options":["Distribution is typically exclusive or very limited","Consumers make a significant effort to search for the product","Buyers tend to be very brand loyal","Consumers compare several brands on price before deciding which to buy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Comparing brands on price is shopping-product behaviour. Specialty buyers have already settled on a specific brand, make a significant effort to find it, and are very brand loyal.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-038","concept":"c07-specialty","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Any product with a high price, such as a luxury SUV, is automatically a specialty product.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Classification depends on the individual's behaviour. A buyer who compares several luxury SUVs on price, quality and style is treating it as a shopping product; it is a specialty product only for a buyer who insists on one brand and makes a significant effort to get it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-039","concept":"c07-unsought","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Evergreen Memorial Gardens has salespeople call Winnipeg families to discuss pre-planning burial plots - something most people know about but would rather not think about. Burial plots are an example of:","options":["Unsought products","Convenience products","Shopping products","Specialty products"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Unsought products are those the consumer doesn't know about, or knows about but doesn't initially want; burial plots and life insurance are classic examples, and they need aggressive promotion and personal selling. Specialty products, by contrast, are actively sought out.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-040","concept":"c07-unsought","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Products that the consumer either does not know about or knows about but does not initially want are called _____ products.","options":["specialty","convenience","unsought","shopping"],"answer":2,"explanation":"That is the definition of unsought products, which require aggressive promotion and personal selling. Convenience, shopping and specialty products are all ones consumers already want, with low, moderate and high buying effort.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-041","concept":"c07-unsought","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"AirPure, an Edmonton start-up, has invented a radically new home device that consumers have never heard of. Which marketing approach best fits the product's current classification?","options":["Aggressive promotion and personal selling to create awareness and desire","Selective distribution with messages comparing its price to competitors","Mass distribution through convenience stores with minimal advertising","Exclusive distribution and premium pricing for very brand-loyal buyers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Radical new products are unsought because consumers don't yet know about them, so marketers must use aggressive promotion and personal selling. Exclusive distribution for loyal buyers fits specialty products, which require an existing strong preference that AirPure doesn't have yet.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-042","concept":"c07-unsought","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What best distinguishes unsought products from convenience products?","options":["Unsought products are always cheaper than convenience products","Unsought products are business products; convenience products are consumer products","Unsought products are unknown or not initially wanted; convenience products are wanted and bought routinely with little effort","Convenience products need aggressive personal selling; unsought products rely on mass advertising alone"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both are consumer products; the difference is awareness and desire. Convenience goods are routinely wanted and need only mass advertising and wide availability, while unsought goods need aggressive promotion and personal selling because demand doesn't exist yet.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-043","concept":"c07-unsought","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why do unsought products such as life insurance typically require personal selling?","options":["Consumers are very brand loyal and need a salesperson to locate their preferred brand","Their demand is derived from demand for other consumer products","They are purchased so frequently that self-service cannot handle the volume","Consumers don't initially want them, so a salesperson must build awareness and persuade them of the need"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Because consumers either don't know about unsought products or don't initially want them, marketers must create the need through aggressive promotion and personal selling. Very brand-loyal buyers describe specialty products, and derived demand applies to business products.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-044","concept":"c07-business-products","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Polar Ridge, a Lethbridge furniture maker, buys rough lumber that it cuts and finishes into dining tables. For Polar Ridge, the lumber is:","options":["Accessory equipment (support good)","A raw material (production good)","A supply (support good)","A component part (production good)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Raw materials such as grain and lumber are production goods that become part of the final product. Component parts are already-manufactured items assembled into the product (engines, microchips), and supplies and accessory equipment never become part of the product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-045","concept":"c07-business-products","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does a boat builder classify the outboard engines it buys as production goods rather than support goods?","options":["Because the engines become part of the final product the builder sells","Because the engines are used up during the production process","Because the engines are major, long-term investments like buildings","Because demand for the engines is unrelated to consumer demand for boats"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Production goods (raw materials and component parts) become part of the final product; engines installed in boats are component parts. Buildings and fixed equipment describe installations, and items used up in operations describe supplies - both support goods.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-046","concept":"c07-business-products","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Rocky Mountain Motors builds pickup trucks in Alberta. Which purchase is a SUPPORT good rather than a production good?","options":["Tires mounted on every finished truck","Steel used to form the truck bodies","Laptops and printers for the accounting department","Engines installed in each truck"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Support goods assist production but don't become part of the product; office equipment is accessory equipment. Engines and tires are component parts and steel is a raw material - all production goods that end up in the truck.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-047","concept":"c07-business-products","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which group lists the four types of support goods?","options":["Raw materials, component parts, accessory equipment and services","Convenience, shopping, specialty and unsought products","Raw materials, component parts, installations and supplies","Installations, accessory equipment, supplies and services"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Support goods are installations, accessory equipment, supplies and services. Raw materials and component parts are production goods, and convenience/shopping/specialty/unsought are consumer product classes.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-048","concept":"c07-business-products","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Calgary craft brewery makes and sells beer. Which of its purchases is NOT a production good?","options":["Hops added to every batch during the boil","A new brewhouse building with fixed brewing tanks","Brewer's yeast used to ferment the beer","Malted barley that is brewed into the beer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The building and fixed tanks are an installation - a support good that assists production. Barley, hops and yeast become part of the beer, so they are raw materials, which are production goods.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-049","concept":"c07-business-products","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pairing of purchase and support-good category is correct for a Halifax accounting firm?","options":["Desktop computers for every accountant - installations","A new office building the firm constructs - accessory equipment","An outside IT repair contract - supplies","Printer paper and paper clips - supplies"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Supplies are consumables such as stationery, paper clips and brooms. A building is an installation, computers and other office equipment are accessory equipment, and repair or maintenance contracts are services.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-050","concept":"c07-derived-demand","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"If consumer demand for Ford cars (a consumer product) increases, the company may increase its demand for paint-spraying equipment (a business good). This illustrates _____.","options":["unit demand","contrived demand","derived demand","primary demand","secondary demand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Derived demand means demand for business products is derived from demand for the consumer products they help produce: more car buyers means Ford needs more paint-spraying equipment. None of the other options describes business demand driven by consumer purchases.","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-051","concept":"c07-derived-demand","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After Canadians began buying far more oat-based milk, Prairie Oat Mills in Saskatchewan saw orders from beverage makers for its processed oats double. This is an example of:","options":["Brand equity","Brand loyalty","Derived demand","Primary demand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The mill sells a business product, and its demand rose because consumer demand for oat milk rose - derived demand. Primary demand is demand for a product category as a whole rather than a specific brand; it doesn't describe a supplier's business sales rising because consumers bought more of the final product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-052","concept":"c07-derived-demand","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Derived demand means that the demand for business products depends on the demand for the consumer products they help make.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Business buyers purchase inputs and equipment because consumers want the final products; when consumer demand changes, demand for those business products follows.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-053","concept":"c07-derived-demand","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kodiak Fasteners sells specialty bolts to Canadian RV manufacturers. Economists forecast a sharp drop in consumer RV purchases next year. What should Kodiak expect and do?","options":["Expect orders to rise, because manufacturers stockpile parts when consumer demand falls","Expect orders to fall, since its demand is derived from RV demand, and plan production and marketing accordingly","Expect no change, because business buyers' demand is independent of consumer demand","Reclassify its bolts as supplies so its sales are no longer tied to RV demand"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Demand for business products is derived from consumer demand, so fewer RV sales means fewer bolts ordered. Business demand is not independent of consumers, and relabelling the product's category doesn't change what drives its demand.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-054","concept":"c07-derived-demand","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about derived demand is NOT correct?","options":["It describes how consumer demand for a product rises after it receives positive reviews","It applies to business products such as raw materials, component parts and equipment","B2B marketers watch consumer markets because their own sales depend on them","A drop in consumer demand for cars can reduce demand for paint-spraying equipment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Derived demand is business demand flowing from consumer demand - not consumer demand rising after good reviews (that's word of mouth). The other three statements accurately describe derived demand.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-055","concept":"c07-product-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"The Build a Bear Workshop in West Edmonton Mall sells custom made Teddy bears designed to be given as gifts for almost every occasion imaginable. For the Build a Bear Workshop, Teddy bears are an example of a:","options":["product class","product mix","product line","marketing mix","product category"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A product line is a group of closely related products that satisfy a class of needs - here, gift-giving. The product mix would be ALL of Build-a-Bear's lines, and the marketing mix is the 4 Ps (product, price, place, promotion), not a group of products.","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-056","concept":"c07-product-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A specific product with a unique brand, size or price, identified by its own stock keeping unit (SKU), is called a:","options":["Product class","Product mix","Product line","Product item"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A product item is one specific version of a product with a unique brand, size or price, and the SKU is its unique identifier. A product line is a group of closely related items, and the product mix is all of a company's lines.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-057","concept":"c07-product-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maplewood Kitchen offers its pasta-sauce line in 14 variations - different flavours, jar sizes and organic versions. The number of items within this one line describes the line's:","options":["Brand equity","Depth","Width","Product mix"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Line depth is the number of product items within a line - here, 14 variations of pasta sauce. Width (breadth) counts how many different lines a company offers, which is a property of the product mix, not of a single line.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-058","concept":"c07-product-line","also":["c07-product-mix"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Glacier Outfitters has three product lines: jackets, footwear and backpacks. It adds six new colours to its existing jacket line. What has changed?","options":["The product mix has become wider because more items were added","The width of the product mix has increased from three to nine","The jacket line has become deeper, while the width of the product mix is unchanged","The company has created a fourth product line"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Adding variations within an existing line increases that line's depth (and the total number of items), while width - the number of different lines - stays at three. Only adding a new line, such as tents, would widen the mix.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-059","concept":"c07-product-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeshore Foods wants to serve more of its frozen-pizza customers' needs without entering a new product category. Which move deepens its product line?","options":["Add gluten-free, family-size and spicy versions of its frozen pizzas","Drop two pizza varieties to simplify its offering","Launch a new line of frozen desserts","License its brand name to a cookware manufacturer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Deepening a line means adding items within the existing line, such as new versions of frozen pizza. Launching frozen desserts would add a new line and widen the product mix, licensing is a branding strategy, and dropping varieties makes the line shallower.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-060","concept":"c07-product-line","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":3,"stem":"Line depth is the number of product items within a line, whereas the total number of items is referred to as product line _____.","options":["length","width","mix","breadth"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Per the course definitions, depth = number of items within a line and length = the total number of items. Width and breadth both mean the number of different lines in the product mix, not a count of items.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-061","concept":"c07-product-mix","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"All of the product lines offered by a company make up its _____.","options":["product line","product class","marketing mix","product mix"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The product mix is all the product lines a company offers, measured by its width (breadth). The marketing mix is the 4 Ps, and a product line is just one group of closely related products within the mix.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-062","concept":"c07-product-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Procter & Gamble sells Pampers (baby care), Tide and Bounce (fabric and home care), and Head & Shoulders and Old Spice (beauty, health and grooming). Counting how many different lines P&G offers measures its product mix:","options":["Equity","Length","Width","Depth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Product mix width (breadth) is the number of different product lines a company offers. Depth is the number of items within a single line, and length is the total number of items - neither counts lines.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-063","concept":"c07-product-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northern Pantry Foods sells 4 cereals, 6 soups and 3 sauces, each group forming its own product line. It is about to launch a new line of 5 frozen pizzas. After the launch, what is the width of its product mix?","options":["5 items","4 lines","18 items","3 lines"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Width = number of different product lines. Before the launch there are 3 lines (cereals, soups, sauces); adding frozen pizzas makes 3 + 1 = 4. The figure 18 (4 + 6 + 3 + 5) counts items, not lines, so it is not width.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-064","concept":"c07-product-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A student writes: 'Tim Hortons' product mix is its product, price, place and promotion.' What is wrong with this statement?","options":["That describes brand equity; the product mix is the value a brand name adds","Nothing is wrong, because the product mix and the marketing mix are the same concept","That describes a product line; the product mix is the number of items within one line","That describes the marketing mix; the product mix is all the product lines a company offers"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The 4 Ps are the marketing mix. The product mix is the full set of product lines a company offers (for Tim Hortons, lines such as hot beverages, cold beverages, baked goods and lunch items). The number of items within one line is depth, not the mix.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-065","concept":"c07-product-mix","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Adding a new scent of Old Spice deodorant widens Procter & Gamble's product mix.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. A new scent is another item within an existing line, so it deepens that line. The product mix becomes wider only when the company adds a new product line.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-066","concept":"c07-brand","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A name, phrase, symbol or design, or a combination of these, uniquely given by a company to a product to distinguish it from the competition is a:","options":["Brand personality","Brand","Trademark","Product line"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is the definition of a brand, which needs to be developed and nurtured by marketing activity. A trademark is the LEGAL protection of a brand name or mark - not the brand itself - and brand personality is the set of human traits linked to a brand.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-067","concept":"c07-brand","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Customers recognize both the spoken name 'Aurora Air' and the airline's symbol of three green northern-lights waves. The three-wave symbol is the airline's:","options":["Brand mark (logo)","Brand name","Brand personality","Copyright"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The brand mark or logo is the part of a brand that cannot be spoken, such as a symbol or design. 'Aurora Air' is the brand name because it can be spoken.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-068","concept":"c07-brand","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which of the following is a brand NAME rather than a brand mark?","options":["Apple's bitten-apple silhouette","The Nike swoosh","'7-Eleven'","A beaver symbol on a clothing label"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A brand name is the part of a brand that can be spoken, including words, letters and numbers - so '7-Eleven' is a name. The swoosh, the beaver symbol and the apple silhouette are designs that can't be spoken, which makes them brand marks.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-069","concept":"c07-brand","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Once a company has created a brand name and logo, the brand will hold its strength on its own without further marketing activity.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. A brand needs to be developed and nurtured by marketing activity on an ongoing basis; because brands are assets, marketers must keep investing to maintain their value.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-070","concept":"c07-brand","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about brands is NOT accurate?","options":["A brand must be developed and nurtured by marketing activity","A brand can combine a name and a symbol","Only for-profit companies can have brands","A brand distinguishes a product from competitors' products"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Non-profits brand themselves too - MADD is one of the course examples. The other statements match the definition: a name, phrase, symbol or design (or a combination) that distinguishes a product and must be nurtured by marketing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-071","concept":"c07-brand-image","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"In class, students complete the sentence 'Tim Hortons is ____.' Most write 'Canadian', 'hockey' or 'Roll Up To Win'. These responses reveal Tim Hortons':","options":["Brand equity","Brand loyalty","Brand mark","Brand associations (brand image)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Brand associations are the thoughts and feelings linked to a brand in memory, and together they form the brand image. Brand equity is the added value those associations help create - a result, not the associations themselves.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-072","concept":"c07-brand-image","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The thoughts, feelings and perceptions consumers link to a brand in memory are called:","options":["Brand advocates","Brand associations","Brand marks","Brand extensions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brand associations - together, the brand image - are what comes to mind when consumers think of the brand. A brand advocate is a person who recommends the brand, and a brand mark is the unspoken symbol.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-073","concept":"c07-brand-image","also":["c07-brand"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"According to the course, what most fundamentally makes a brand different from a product?","options":["Its substance - authenticity - and the key associations people hold about it","Its physical features, such as size, colour and materials","Its registration with the Canadian Intellectual Property Office","Its price, since branded items always cost more than products"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The course stresses that the difference between brand and product is substance (authenticity) and the brand's key associations. Physical features belong to the product itself (the actual layer), and registration is a trademark, which protects a brand but doesn't define it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-074","concept":"c07-brand-image","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Endy, an online mattress company, finds many shoppers doubt that a mattress shipped in a box can be comfortable. Using the idea that brands provide an 'educational' opportunity, Endy should:","options":["Teach shoppers why they should care by explaining how the mattress is made and performs","License its name to a furniture retailer to escape the negative perceptions","Stop advertising and let the mattress's features speak for themselves","Cut its price below every competitor so that perceptions no longer matter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Brands provide an 'educational' opportunity: teach people why they should care, because perceptions are important to brand relationships. Ignoring perceptions, competing on price alone, or handing the name to a licensee doesn't build the relationship.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-075","concept":"c07-brand-image","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Marketers need to understand a brand's key associations because consumer perceptions shape their relationships with the brand, even when those perceptions differ from the product's technical facts.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Perceptions are important to brand relationships, so marketers study a brand's key associations (for example, how H&M, Gap and Zara are each seen) and use marketing to educate people about why they should care.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-076","concept":"c07-legal-protection","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ is/are used to legally protect the written word, a sound recording, or a form of communication from being copied by another.","options":["Trademarks","Brand loyalty","Copyrights","Brand personality","All of the above"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Copyrights protect original written works, sound recordings and forms of communication (music, literature, performances) from being copied; per the course material, protection lasts the creator's lifetime plus 50 years. Trademarks protect brand names and marks, while brand loyalty and brand personality are marketing outcomes, not legal protections.","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-077","concept":"c07-legal-protection","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow River Brewing registers its brand name and grizzly-bear logo with the Canadian Intellectual Property Office so competitors cannot use them. The brewery is obtaining a:","options":["Copyright","Trademark","Brand licence","Patent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A trademark gives the owner the exclusive legal right to use a brand name or brand mark; in Canada it is registered with CIPO for 10 years and is renewable. Copyright protects creative works such as songs or text, and a patent protects inventions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-078","concept":"c07-legal-protection","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the course material, how long does copyright protection last in Canada?","options":["The creator's lifetime plus 70 years","10 years, renewable","20 years from the date of filing","The creator's lifetime plus 50 years"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The course material and test bank state lifetime plus 50 years, so that is the exam answer. (Canadian law moved to life plus 70 years effective December 30, 2022, but the course has not adopted that figure.) '10 years, renewable' is the trademark registration period.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-079","concept":"c07-legal-protection","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kestrel Labs in Calgary invents a new battery-cooling mechanism, writes a training manual about it, and names the product 'CoolCell'. Which match of asset to legal protection is correct?","options":["Mechanism - trademark; manual - copyright; name - patent","Mechanism - patent; manual - trademark; name - copyright","Mechanism - patent; manual - copyright; name - trademark","Mechanism - copyright; manual - patent; name - trademark"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Patents protect inventions, copyright protects original written works, and trademarks protect brand names and marks. Each wrong option swaps at least one protection onto the wrong kind of asset.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-080","concept":"c07-legal-protection","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following would NOT be protected by copyright?","options":["A song written for a radio commercial","A novel by a Canadian author","A sound recording of a live comedy performance","A company's brand name"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A brand name is protected by a trademark, which gives the exclusive right to use a brand name or mark. Songs, written works, sound recordings and performances are exactly what copyright protects.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-081","concept":"c07-brand-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"If we consider brands to be assets, then it is critical that marketers:","options":["Sell them whenever possible.","Invest money to maintain their value.","Build new ones regularly.","Balance them against liabilities."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because brands are assets, they lose value without upkeep, so marketers must invest money to maintain their value - which is why brands like those on Interbrand's ranking keep spending on quality and marketing. Selling them or constantly building new ones throws away value already built, and balancing against liabilities is an accounting idea, not a marketing action.","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-082","concept":"c07-brand-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The added value a brand name gives to a product beyond its functional benefits is called:","options":["Brand loyalty","Brand licensing","Brand equity","Brand personality"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Brand equity is the added value a brand name brings beyond functional benefits, giving a competitive advantage and allowing higher prices. Brand loyalty is an individual's favourable attitude and repeat purchase - a source of equity, not the equity itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-083","concept":"c07-brand-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Shoppers at a Calgary outlet will pay $95 for a Roots hoodie but only $40 for an unbranded hoodie made in the same factory from the same fabric. The $55 difference reflects:","options":["Brand loyalty","Brand equity","Co-branding","Brand licensing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brand equity is the added value a brand name brings beyond functional benefits, which allows a higher price. Loyalty (an individual's repeat purchase and favourable attitude) can contribute to equity, but the price premium itself is the equity.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-084","concept":"c07-brand-equity","also":["c07-brand-loyalty"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes brand equity from brand loyalty?","options":["Equity is the added value a brand name brings to a product; loyalty is a consumer's favourable attitude and repeat purchase over time","Equity is a consumer's repeated purchase of a brand; loyalty is the legal value of a registered trademark","Equity applies only to services; loyalty applies only to physical goods","Equity is the set of human traits linked to a brand; loyalty is the number of brands a firm owns"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Brand equity is the value the brand name adds (a property of the brand); brand loyalty is a favourable attitude toward and consistent purchase of a brand over time (a behaviour of consumers). Human traits describe brand personality, and legal value relates to trademarks.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-085","concept":"c07-brand-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does strong brand equity matter to a company?","options":["It guarantees that no new competitors will enter the market","It legally prevents other firms from copying the product's design","It provides a competitive advantage and allows the company to charge higher prices","It removes the need for any further marketing investment in the brand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Brand equity creates competitive advantage and price premiums. It does not remove the need for investment - brands are assets that must be maintained - and legal protection comes from trademarks and patents, not from equity.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-086","concept":"c07-brand-personality","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A set of human characteristics associated with a brand name is called brand _____.","options":["licensing","personality","equity","loyalty"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brand personality is the set of human characteristics linked to a brand (rugged, sincere, sophisticated). Equity is the added value of the name, loyalty is a consumer's attitude and repeat purchase, and licensing is renting the name to another firm.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-087","concept":"c07-brand-personality","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Trailhead Gear's ads feature a weathered, adventurous character who laughs at bad weather, and the company describes its brand as 'rugged, honest and outdoorsy.' Trailhead is building its:","options":["Brand personality","Brand licensing","Brand equity","Brand loyalty"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Describing a brand in human terms - rugged, honest, outdoorsy - is building brand personality. Equity and loyalty may follow from a strong personality, but the human traits themselves are the personality.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-088","concept":"c07-brand-personality","also":["c07-brand-image"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Survey respondents describe Velvet & Co. cosmetics as 'sophisticated, glamorous and confident' and say they think of 'Paris' and 'luxury department stores' when they hear the name. Which statement is most accurate?","options":["The responses describe the consumers' own personalities, so they say nothing about the brand","All of the responses are brand equity, because they describe the brand's financial value","'Paris' and 'luxury department stores' are brand personality traits because they are associations","'Sophisticated, glamorous and confident' are brand personality traits, which form part of the broader brand image"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Brand personality is the set of HUMAN characteristics linked to a brand, and it is one kind of brand association. 'Paris' and 'luxury department stores' are associations but not human traits, and equity is the added value those associations create.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-089","concept":"c07-brand-personality","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lumen Bank wants young Canadians to feel an emotional connection to it, the way they would to a friend. Which tactic most directly builds brand personality?","options":["Add a new line of mortgage products to widen its product mix","Give the brand a consistent warm, witty, approachable voice across ads and social media","Lower account fees below those charged by the largest Canadian banks","Register the bank's name and logo as trademarks with CIPO"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brand personality is built by associating the brand with human traits - tone of voice, characters, spokespeople - which helps consumers make emotional connections. Fee cuts (price), trademarks (legal protection) and new lines (product mix) don't give the brand human characteristics.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-090","concept":"c07-brand-personality","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of a brand personality trait?","options":["Sincere","Rugged","Available in 2,000 stores","Sophisticated"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Brand personality traits are human characteristics such as sincere, rugged or sophisticated. Being available in 2,000 stores describes distribution, not a human trait.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-091","concept":"c07-brand-loyalty","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A favourable attitude toward and consistent purchase of a single brand over time is:","options":["Brand loyalty","Brand equity","Brand advocacy","Brand awareness"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Brand loyalty combines a favourable attitude with consistent purchase over time. Brand equity is the added value of the name, and advocacy means passing positive comments to others.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-092","concept":"c07-brand-loyalty","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"For 15 years, Denise in Moncton has bought only one brand of toothpaste. She feels it suits her family and never considers other brands, though she rarely talks about it with anyone. Denise demonstrates:","options":["Brand personality","Brand licensing","Brand advocacy","Brand loyalty"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A favourable attitude plus consistent purchase over time is brand loyalty. Denise isn't a brand advocate because she doesn't pass along comments to other people.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-093","concept":"c07-brand-loyalty","also":["c07-brand-advocate"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which customer is showing brand loyalty but NOT acting as a brand advocate?","options":["Tara buys whichever yogurt brand happens to be on sale each week","Sana posts an enthusiastic review of a cafe she has visited only once","Omar tells everyone at work how much he loves his new Roots jacket","Ken buys only Lululemon running gear but never discusses it with friends or online"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Loyalty is a favourable attitude plus consistent purchase over time; advocacy is passing positive comments to others. Ken buys consistently but tells no one, Sana and Omar are advocating, and Tara shows no loyalty at all.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-094","concept":"c07-brand-loyalty","also":["c07-brand-personality"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the course, why do marketers work to associate brands with specific personality traits?","options":["To help consumers make emotional connections with their brands, which builds loyalty","To make the core benefit tangible so it can be evaluated before purchase","To register those traits as trademarks with the Canadian government","To reduce the number of product lines the company must manage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The course notes that marketers associate brands with personality traits and help consumers make emotional connections - the foundation of brand loyalty. Traits can't be registered as trademarks, and they have nothing to do with the number of lines.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-095","concept":"c07-brand-loyalty","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hearth Coffee Roasters notices that many customers buy from it repeatedly only when it runs discounts, switching to rivals the rest of the time. What does this suggest about their brand loyalty?","options":["It is strong, because any repeat purchase demonstrates brand loyalty","It is weak, because loyalty needs a favourable attitude as well as repeat purchase over time","It is strong, because price-sensitive buyers are the most loyal customers","It is irrelevant, because coffee is always classified as a specialty product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brand loyalty requires a favourable attitude toward the brand and consistent purchase over time. Buying only on discount and switching otherwise shows price sensitivity, not loyalty - so Hearth should work on emotional connections, not just deals.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-096","concept":"c07-brand-advocate","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A _____ is a person who speaks well of a product by passing along comments to other people.","options":["brand preceptor","brand associate","personal brand","brand advocate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A brand advocate speaks well of a product and passes positive comments along to others through word of mouth, reviews and social posts. 'Brand associate' sounds similar but isn't the term, and 'brand preceptor' is not a marketing concept.","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-097","concept":"c07-brand-advocate","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a great trip, Jordan from Kelowna posts a five-star review of Okanagan Kayak Tours and convinces three friends to book their own tours. Jordan is acting as a:","options":["Brand personality","Brand licensee","Brand advocate","Brand associate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Speaking well of a product and passing positive comments to other people is what a brand advocate does. A licensee pays to use a brand name, and brand personality refers to the brand's human traits, not a customer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-098","concept":"c07-brand-advocate","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the key difference between a brand advocate and a brand-loyal customer?","options":["An advocate passes along positive comments to others; a loyal customer consistently buys the brand but may tell no one","An advocate owns the rights to the brand's trademark; a loyal customer only buys the product","An advocate is always paid by the company to promote it; a loyal customer is never paid","An advocate always buys the brand more often and in larger amounts than a loyal customer does"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Advocacy is about talking - passing positive comments to others - while loyalty is about attitude and repeat purchase. Advocates are not defined by being paid or by how often they buy.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-099","concept":"c07-brand-advocate","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Pedal, a Saskatoon bike shop, has hundreds of satisfied repeat customers but almost no online reviews or referrals. Which goal should its next campaign pursue?","options":["Convert its bikes from shopping products into convenience products","Register its store name as a copyright to protect its reputation","Turn satisfied, loyal customers into brand advocates who pass positive comments to others","Widen its product mix by adding a line of coffee drinks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prairie Pedal already has loyalty; what's missing is customers speaking well of it to others - brand advocacy. Changing the product class or the mix doesn't address word of mouth, and store names are protected by trademarks, not copyright.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-100","concept":"c07-brand-advocate","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why are brand advocates especially valuable to marketers?","options":["They remove the need for the brand to be nurtured by marketing activity","They create derived demand for the brand's business products","They guarantee that the brand's equity can never decline","Their positive comments reach other consumers from a fellow customer rather than from the company"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Advocates spread positive word of mouth that comes from fellow consumers, which other buyers tend to find credible. They don't guarantee equity or replace the ongoing marketing a brand needs, and derived demand is a business-product concept.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-101","concept":"c07-experiential-branding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the course, the purpose of experiential branding is:","options":["To pair two manufacturers' brand names on one product","Not to sell you something, but to show how a brand can enrich a consumer's life","To list a product's features and price as clearly as possible","To rent the brand name to another firm in exchange for a royalty"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Experiential branding aims not to sell you something but to show how a brand can enrich your life, and storytelling is essential. Renting the name is licensing, pairing two names is co-branding, and listing features is a traditional sales message.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-102","concept":"c07-experiential-branding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lumi Outdoor opens a pop-up in Banff where visitors join free guided snowshoe walks and hear the founders' stories about growing up in the Rockies - and no one is asked to buy anything. This is an example of:","options":["Mixed branding","Brand licensing","Experiential branding","Co-branding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Showing how the brand fits into and enriches people's lives through an experience and storytelling, without a sales pitch, is experiential branding. Licensing, co-branding and mixed branding are branding strategies about who uses or owns a name.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-103","concept":"c07-experiential-branding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which campaign is the clearest example of experiential branding rather than a traditional sales pitch?","options":["A TV ad that lists the running shoe's weight, cushioning technology and price","A free weekly run club where participants experience how the brand fits an active, social lifestyle","A cashier asking every customer at checkout to add a pair of socks to their purchase","A flyer announcing 30% off all running shoes in the store this weekend only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Experiential branding shows how the brand enriches life through experiences rather than pushing a sale. Discounts, feature-and-price ads and add-on requests are direct selling tactics.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-104","concept":"c07-experiential-branding","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Storytelling is essential to experiential branding because stories help consumers see how a brand can enrich their lives.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The course states that storytelling is essential to experiential branding, whose goal is not to sell something but to show how the brand can enrich the consumer's life.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-105","concept":"c07-experiential-branding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"A brand manager is planning an experiential branding campaign. Which element would NOT fit this approach?","options":["Activities that show how the brand enriches everyday life","An immersive event that lets visitors experience the brand first-hand","A founder's story that connects the brand to customers' values","A script focused on closing sales and maximizing units sold at each event"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Experiential branding is explicitly not about selling you something; a hard-close sales script contradicts its purpose. Immersive experiences, storytelling and showing how the brand enriches life are its core elements.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-106","concept":"c07-brand-types","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Canadian Tire's Mastercraft tools are made for Canadian Tire by outside manufacturers but sold only under the Mastercraft name in its stores. Mastercraft is an example of a:","options":["Private-label brand","Co-brand","Generic brand","Manufacturer brand"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A private-label (store) brand is owned by the retailer or wholesaler, even though another company manufactures it. Who makes the product doesn't matter - ownership of the name does - and a generic product would carry no brand name at all.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-107","concept":"c07-brand-types","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A brand owned by the producer of the product, such as Tide or Nike, is called a _____ brand.","options":["mixed","manufacturer (national)","generic","private-label"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Manufacturer (also called national) brands are owned and marketed by the producer. Private-label brands are owned by retailers or wholesalers, generic products have no brand name, and mixed branding means selling under both the firm's own and a reseller's name.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-108","concept":"c07-brand-types","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What distinguishes a private-label brand from a generic product?","options":["There is no difference, because both terms describe store brands","A private-label product has no brand name; a generic product carries the retailer's brand","A private-label product is owned by the manufacturer; a generic product is owned by the retailer","A private-label product carries a brand name owned by a retailer or wholesaler; a generic product has no brand name"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Private labels such as President's Choice and Kirkland are real brands owned by resellers, while generics are unbranded, plainly packaged and low priced. Manufacturer ownership describes national brands, not private labels.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-109","concept":"c07-brand-types","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Maritime Biscuit Co. sells crackers under its own Maritime label and also produces crackers that a national supermarket chain sells under the chain's store brand. Maritime Biscuit is using:","options":["Multibranding","Brand licensing","Mixed branding","Co-branding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mixed branding means a firm markets products under its own name and under a reseller's private-label name. Multibranding would mean using several of Maritime's OWN brand names, and co-branding would put two manufacturers' names on a single package.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-110","concept":"c07-brand-types","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of a private-label brand?","options":["Tide laundry detergent","Motomaster at Canadian Tire","Kirkland Signature at Costco","President's Choice at Loblaws"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tide is owned and marketed by its producer, Procter & Gamble, so it is a manufacturer (national) brand. President's Choice, Kirkland Signature and Motomaster are owned by the retailers that sell them - private labels.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-111","concept":"c07-family-multibranding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Samsung puts its name on its phones, televisions, refrigerators and washing machines. This is an example of:","options":["Multibranding","Co-branding","Mixed branding","Multiproduct (family) branding"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Using one name for all of a company's products is multiproduct (family, umbrella or corporate) branding. Multibranding would give each product or segment a different name.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-112","concept":"c07-family-multibranding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Procter & Gamble sells laundry detergent under three different names - Tide, Gain and Cheer - each aimed at a different segment. This strategy is:","options":["Brand extension","Multibranding","Multiproduct branding","Sub-branding"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Multibranding gives each product or segment its own distinct brand name. Multiproduct branding would put one name on everything, and sub-branding would combine the P&G name with a new name.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-113","concept":"c07-family-multibranding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts multiproduct branding and multibranding?","options":["Multiproduct branding uses one name for all products; multibranding uses a different name for each product or segment","Multiproduct branding sells under the firm's own and a reseller's names; multibranding uses no brand names","Multiproduct branding and multibranding are two names for the same strategy","Multiproduct branding puts two manufacturers' names on one product; multibranding uses one name for all products"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Multiproduct (family) branding = one name across all products; multibranding = separate names for each product or segment. Two manufacturers' names on one product is co-branding, and own-plus-reseller names is mixed branding.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-114","concept":"c07-family-multibranding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stanley Black & Decker sells tools to homeowners under the Black & Decker name and to professional contractors under the DeWalt name. Why use multibranding here instead of one family brand?","options":["Multibranding lets the company collect royalties from its own divisions","Separate names are required because tools are classified as business products","Separate names let each brand target its own segment without one brand's image diluting the other's","Multibranding lets the company avoid investing in either brand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Multibranding gives each segment a brand built for it, so the professional image of DeWalt isn't blurred by the homeowner positioning of Black & Decker (and vice versa). Each brand still needs investment, nothing requires separate names, and royalties belong to licensing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-115","concept":"c07-family-multibranding","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Multiproduct branding - also called family, umbrella or corporate branding - uses one name for all of a company's products, which makes brand extensions and sub-branding easier.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. With one name across all products (Samsung, Heinz), the company can extend that name into new product classes or combine it with new names (sub-brands).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-116","concept":"c07-brand-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dove, long known for its beauty bar soap, launches Dove shampoo and conditioner. This is an example of:","options":["Sub-branding","Brand extension","Multibranding","Co-branding"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A brand extension uses a current brand name to enter a new product class - soap to hair care. Sub-branding would pair the Dove name with a new brand name, which doesn't happen here.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-117","concept":"c07-brand-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Gatorade introduces a lower-calorie drink called Gatorade G2, combining the parent brand name with a new name. This strategy is:","options":["Mixed branding","Co-branding","Sub-branding","Brand extension"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sub-branding combines a corporate or family brand name with a new brand name (Gatorade + G2, Porsche + Cayenne). A brand extension uses the existing name alone to enter a new product class, and co-branding requires two different manufacturers' names.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-118","concept":"c07-brand-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Northern Glow Candles is considering three launches: (1) a lavender scent added to its existing candle line, (2) Northern Glow hand soap, and (3) a premium candle range called 'Northern Glow Ember'. Which label is correct?","options":["(2) is a brand extension, because the existing name enters a new product class","(1) is a brand extension, because a new item carries the existing name","(3) is a brand extension, because a new name is combined with the parent name","(2) is sub-branding, because the existing name appears on a new product"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A brand extension takes the current name into a NEW product class (candles to hand soap). A new scent stays in the same class and simply deepens the line, and (3) pairs the family name with a new name, which is sub-branding.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-119","concept":"c07-brand-extension","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Using a current brand name to enter a completely new product class is called _____.","options":["multibranding","sub-branding","brand licensing","brand extension"],"answer":3,"explanation":"That is the definition of a brand extension (Dove soap to Dove shampoo; Tide detergent to Tide stain remover). Sub-branding adds a new name to the parent name, multibranding uses separate names, and licensing lets another firm use the name for a fee.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-120","concept":"c07-brand-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do firms that use multiproduct (family) branding find brand extensions attractive?","options":["Brand extensions let the firm sell its products under a reseller's private label","Brand extensions eliminate the risk that a weak new product could harm the brand","Consumers' existing knowledge of and trust in the brand name can carry over to the new product class","Brand extensions require a completely new brand name for each new product"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A brand extension borrows an established name's reputation, which makes entering a new class easier. It doesn't eliminate risk - a weak extension can hurt the family name - selling under a reseller's label is mixed branding, and a new name for each product is multibranding.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-121","concept":"c07-co-branding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Tim Hortons partners with Oreo to sell a doughnut that features both brand names. This is an example of:","options":["Mixed branding","Brand licensing","Sub-branding","Co-branding"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Co-branding pairs two brand names of two manufacturers on a single product. Sub-branding would combine one company's parent name with a new name, and licensing involves one firm paying to use another's name.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-122","concept":"c07-co-branding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Co-branding is best defined as:","options":["Marketing products under the firm's own name and a reseller's name","Pairing two brand names of two manufacturers on a single product","Allowing another firm to use a brand name in exchange for a royalty","Using one brand name for all of a company's products"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Co-branding = two manufacturers' brand names on one product. Selling under the firm's own and a reseller's name is mixed branding, letting another firm use the name for a royalty is brand licensing, and one name for all products is multiproduct (family) branding.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-123","concept":"c07-co-branding","also":["c07-brand-licensing"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario is co-branding rather than brand licensing or sub-branding?","options":["A Canadian snack maker and a national chocolate brand jointly launch a granola bar carrying both companies' names","A clothing company pays a hockey team a royalty to put the team's logo on hoodies it makes and sells","A cookware firm pays a celebrity chef's company a fee to put the chef's name on its pans","A coffee chain launches 'Summit Cold Brew', combining its own name with a new product name"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Two manufacturers' names jointly on one product is co-branding. Paying a royalty or fee to use another party's name (the hockey team, the celebrity chef) is licensing, and one company's name plus a new name is sub-branding.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-124","concept":"c07-co-branding","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why might two manufacturers choose co-branding?","options":["Each brand can borrow the other's equity and reach the other's customers","It lets a retailer own the brand name instead of the manufacturers","It lets one firm earn royalties without making any product","It removes the need for either firm to protect its trademark"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Co-branding lets partners combine their brand equity on one product and reach each other's customers. Earning royalties without making the product is licensing, and retailer ownership of the name is private-label branding.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-125","concept":"c07-co-branding","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"When Nike and Apple put both of their brand names on the same running product, they are using co-branding.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Co-branding pairs two manufacturers' brand names on a single product, as in the course's Nike + Apple and Tim Hortons + Oreo examples.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-126","concept":"c07-brand-licensing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A contractual agreement whereby a company allows another firm to use its brand name, patent, trade secret or other property for a royalty or fee is called:","options":["Brand extension","Co-branding","Mixed branding","Brand licensing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"That is brand licensing. Co-branding pairs two manufacturers' names on one product, mixed branding sells under the firm's own and a reseller's names, and a brand extension is the owner itself using its name in a new product class.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-127","concept":"c07-brand-licensing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"The Calgary Chinooks, a junior hockey team, allow Northern Threads to make and sell Chinooks-logo toques; Northern Threads pays the team 10% of every sale. This arrangement is:","options":["Co-branding","Brand extension","Brand licensing","Private-label branding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Allowing another firm to use your brand name or logo in exchange for a royalty is brand licensing. It isn't a brand extension because the team itself isn't making the toques, and it isn't co-branding because two manufacturers' names aren't being paired.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-128","concept":"c07-brand-licensing","also":["c07-brand-extension"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes brand licensing from brand extension?","options":["Licensing always puts two manufacturers' names on one product, while an extension never does","In licensing, another firm pays to use the brand; in an extension, the brand owner itself uses its name in a new product class","Licensing applies only to patents, while an extension applies only to trademarks","In licensing, the owner uses its name in a new product class; in an extension, another firm pays a royalty"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Licensing is a contract letting another firm use the brand (or patent, trade secret) for a royalty or fee; a brand extension is the owner stretching its own name into a new class. Two manufacturers' names on one product is co-branding.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-129","concept":"c07-brand-licensing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"From the brand owner's perspective, what is a main benefit of brand licensing?","options":["Eliminating the need to register the brand as a trademark","Removing any risk that the licensed product could affect the brand's image","Turning the brand into a generic product with no name","Earning royalty or fee income while another firm makes and sells the product"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Licensing generates royalty or fee income and extends the brand's reach without the owner producing the item. It doesn't replace trademark protection, and a poor licensed product can still damage the brand.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-130","concept":"c07-brand-licensing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Alpine Cafe, famous in Canmore for its maple granola, is offered a deal: a national food company will make and sell 'Alpine Cafe Granola' in grocery stores and pay Alpine a 6% royalty. What should Alpine insist on before signing?","options":["That the granola be sold as a generic product with no brand name","That the food company also print its own brand name on the package, making it co-branding","Quality controls in the licensing contract, since a poor licensed product could damage its brand","That the deal be structured as derived demand so its cafe sales rise automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In licensing, someone else makes the product under your name, so the owner must protect brand equity through quality requirements in the contract. Adding a second name, going generic or invoking derived demand doesn't protect the brand.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-131","concept":"c07-packaging","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Tide released Tide PODS; this innovation in packaging resulted in convenient, single-use pods, eliminated the need for measuring and pouring liquid detergent, and provided a hassle-free laundry experience. This demonstrates:","options":["How packaging can provide a functional benefit.","How packaging can communicate the brand benefits.","How packaging can help a brand stand out.","How packaging can communicate information about the product."],"answer":0,"explanation":"First and foremost, packaging has functional benefits - it allows a product to be conveniently used, and single-use pods remove measuring and pouring. Communicating information or brand benefits is a communication benefit, and standing out on the shelf is a perceptual benefit.","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-132","concept":"c07-packaging","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Harvest Table's redesigned cereal box adds a large nutrition-facts table, allergy warnings and step-by-step serving suggestions. These changes mainly provide which packaging benefit?","options":["Communication benefit","Augmented benefit","Perceptual benefit","Functional benefit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Label information such as nutrition facts, warnings and directions is a communication benefit of packaging. A functional benefit would change how the product is used, stored or protected, such as a resealable liner.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-133","concept":"c07-packaging","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Glacier Spring switches from a plain plastic bottle to a frosted, sculpted glass bottle so its water looks premium and catches shoppers' eyes on a crowded shelf. This change mainly delivers a:","options":["Functional benefit","Perceptual benefit","Core benefit","Communication benefit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shaping perceptions of quality and helping the brand stand out on the shelf are perceptual benefits. The bottle doesn't make the water easier to use (functional) or add label information (communication).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-134","concept":"c07-packaging","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Packaging is best defined as:","options":["A written statement of the manufacturer's liability for product deficiencies","The additional services that accompany a product after purchase","Any container in which a product is offered for sale and on which label information is conveyed","The part of a brand that cannot be spoken, such as a symbol"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Packaging is any container in which a product is offered for sale and on which label information is conveyed; labelling is an integral part of it. A statement of the manufacturer's liability is a warranty, services that accompany the product are the augmented product, and the unspoken part of a brand is the brand mark.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-135","concept":"c07-packaging","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A yogurt brand redesigns its tub with a resealable lid, a bold new colour scheme and clearer serving directions. Which pairing of change and benefit is correct?","options":["Resealable lid - perceptual; bold colours - communication; serving directions - functional","Resealable lid - functional; bold colours - perceptual; serving directions - communication","Resealable lid - functional; bold colours - communication; serving directions - perceptual","Resealable lid - communication; bold colours - functional; serving directions - perceptual"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A resealable lid improves storage and usability (functional), bold colours shape perception and help the brand stand out (perceptual), and directions are label information (communication). The option pairing bold colours with communication is tempting because it gets the lid right, but colours shape perception rather than convey information.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-136","concept":"c07-packaging","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Canadian juice brand wants its packaging to signal higher quality so it can justify a premium price on a crowded shelf. Which packaging benefit should it focus on?","options":["Perceptual benefit","Functional benefit","Augmented-product benefit","Communication benefit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Perceptual benefits shape how consumers perceive quality and help a brand stand out. Functional changes improve usability, communication adds information, and packaging belongs to the actual product, not the augmented layer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-137","concept":"c07-intangibility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ease and Relax Centre, a specialty unit providing high-end massaging services, has customer testimonials on its website. By doing this, Ease and Relax Centre is dealing with the service characteristic of:","options":["incongruity","inconsistency","intangibility","inseparability"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A massage can't be seen or touched before purchase, so testimonials give prospective clients tangible evidence of the experience - a response to intangibility. Inconsistency is about variable quality between deliveries, and 'incongruity' is not one of the four I's.","source":"official"},{"id":"q07-138","concept":"c07-intangibility","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The service characteristic meaning that services cannot be touched, seen or tried before they are purchased is _____.","options":["inseparability","inconsistency","inventory","intangibility"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Intangibility means a service can't be touched or seen before purchase, so marketers add tangible cues. Inseparability is simultaneous production and consumption, inconsistency is variable quality, and inventory is the inability to store services.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-139","concept":"c07-intangibility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Clearview Financial Planning in Calgary finds that prospects hesitate because they can't evaluate its advice before signing up. Which tactic best addresses this characteristic of services?","options":["Hire part-time planners for the busy RRSP season","Offer lower fees during slow months to fill unused appointment slots","Show tangible cues such as client testimonials, credentials and a professional office","Write a standard script so every planner delivers advice the same way"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Not being able to evaluate a service before purchase is intangibility, which tangible cues address. Off-peak pricing and part-time staff address inventory, and standard scripts address inconsistency.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-140","concept":"c07-intangibility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Rocky Lodge Hotels takes four actions. Which one targets intangibility rather than another of the four I's?","options":["Posting 360-degree virtual tours and guest reviews of its rooms online","Discounting rooms on weeknights when occupancy is low","Taking online reservations to manage peak-season demand","Training all front-desk staff to follow the same check-in standards"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Virtual tours and reviews let guests 'see' an intangible stay before buying. Weeknight discounts and reservations manage inventory (perishable capacity), and standardized training addresses inconsistency.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-141","concept":"c07-intangibility","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four I's of services?","options":["Inseparability","Incongruity","Intangibility","Inventory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The four I's are intangibility, inconsistency, inseparability and inventory. 'Incongruity' appears as a distractor in test-bank questions but is not one of them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-142","concept":"c07-inconsistency","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Customers of Summit Hair Studio rave about the senior stylist but complain that cuts from the newer stylists vary a lot from visit to visit. This problem reflects which characteristic of services?","options":["Intangibility","Inventory","Inseparability","Inconsistency"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Inconsistency means service quality varies because it depends on the people who deliver it. Inseparability is tempting because a stylist is involved, but the problem described is VARIATION in quality, not simultaneous production and consumption.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-143","concept":"c07-inconsistency","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peak Fitness, an Edmonton gym chain, wants every personal-training session to meet the same standard no matter which trainer leads it. Which response targets the inconsistency of services?","options":["Post member testimonials and before-and-after photos online","Build a more attractive, modern gym facility","Offer discounted memberships for off-peak hours","Standardize session plans and train all trainers on them"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Standardization and training reduce the variation that comes from different people delivering the service. Testimonials and attractive facilities are tangible cues (intangibility), and off-peak discounts manage inventory.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-144","concept":"c07-inconsistency","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Inconsistency means the quality of a service can vary because it depends on the people who provide it.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Because services are delivered by people, quality can differ from one employee or occasion to the next, which is why firms rely on standardization and training.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-145","concept":"c07-inconsistency","also":["c07-inseparability"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which situation illustrates inconsistency rather than inseparability?","options":["The same restaurant meal is excellent on Monday and poorly prepared on Friday, depending on the cook on shift","A patient must be present in the dentist's chair while the cleaning is performed","A concert is produced by the band at the same moment the audience consumes it","A client's view of a law firm is shaped by the lawyer who personally handles her case"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Quality that varies with who is working is inconsistency. Being present during delivery, simultaneous production and consumption, and judging the service by its deliverer all describe inseparability.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-146","concept":"c07-inconsistency","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why are training and standardization the main tools for managing inconsistency?","options":["Services cannot be stored, so training builds up inventory for peak periods","Services are produced by machines, so standardization keeps them calibrated","Service quality depends on the people delivering it, so making their performance uniform reduces variation","Services cannot be seen before purchase, so training makes them tangible"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Inconsistency comes from the human element - different people deliver at different quality levels - so standard procedures and training narrow that variation. Storage problems are inventory, and invisibility before purchase is intangibility.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-147","concept":"c07-inseparability","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"At Cedar Spa, each massage is produced by the therapist at the same moment the client experiences it, and clients judge the spa largely by the therapist who treats them. This illustrates:","options":["Intangibility","Inseparability","Inconsistency","Inventory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inseparability means consumers can't separate the deliverer of the service from the service itself, and production and consumption happen at the same time. Inconsistency would require the stem to describe quality varying between therapists or visits.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-148","concept":"c07-inseparability","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The service characteristic in which consumers cannot separate the deliverer of the service from the service itself is:","options":["Idle production capacity","Inseparability","Inconsistency","Intangibility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is inseparability - the service is produced and consumed simultaneously, so the provider is part of the experience. Idle production capacity is part of the inventory characteristic (supply exceeding demand).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-149","concept":"c07-inseparability","also":["c07-inconsistency"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Calgary tutoring company notices two issues: (A) students judge the company almost entirely by the individual tutor sitting with them, and (B) lesson quality differs noticeably from tutor to tutor. Which matching is correct?","options":["A = inconsistency; B = inseparability","A = intangibility; B = inventory","A = inseparability; B = intangibility","A = inseparability; B = inconsistency"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Judging the service by the person delivering it is inseparability; quality differing from one tutor to another is inconsistency. Matching A to inconsistency and B to inseparability reverses the two - the classic trap. Ask whether the issue is 'who delivers it' or 'how much it varies'.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-150","concept":"c07-inseparability","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Because of inseparability, a dental clinic's patients see the hygienist as the service itself. What should the clinic prioritize?","options":["Removing its brand name so patients focus only on the hygienists","Offering discounts on slow weekday afternoons to fill empty appointment slots","Hiring and preparing hygienists who create a positive experience in every patient interaction","Posting more photos of the clinic online so patients can see it before booking"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When the deliverer can't be separated from the service, the person-to-person encounter IS the service, so the people delivering it matter most. Photos address intangibility, off-peak discounts address inventory, and dropping the brand name helps nothing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-151","concept":"c07-inseparability","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"Inseparability means a service is produced first and stored, then consumed later when the customer needs it.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Inseparability means the service is produced and consumed at the same time, and the consumer can't separate the deliverer from the service. The fact that services can't be stored relates to the inventory characteristic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-152","concept":"c07-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"On weekday afternoons, Lakeview Bowling in Regina has most of its lanes empty while staff are on shift and the lights and heat are on. This costly situation is an example of:","options":["Idle production capacity","Derived demand","Inconsistency","Inseparability"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Idle production capacity occurs when the supply of a service exceeds demand, and it's expensive because costs continue while capacity goes unused - part of the inventory characteristic. Inconsistency is about varying quality, not unused capacity.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-153","concept":"c07-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Banff ski-rental shop has long line-ups on holiday weekends but sits mostly idle midweek. Which set of tactics addresses the inventory characteristic of services?","options":["Write a standard fitting checklist for every rental technician","Post customer testimonials and photos of happy skiers on its website","Offer midweek discounts, take online reservations and add part-time staff on weekends","Change its name and logo to appear more premium"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Services can't be stored, so firms smooth demand and capacity with off-peak pricing, reservations and part-time staff. Testimonials address intangibility, and checklists address inconsistency.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-154","concept":"c07-inventory","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Idle production capacity occurs when the supply of a service exceeds demand, and it is expensive for the service provider.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. When supply exceeds demand, the provider still pays for staff, space and equipment while capacity goes unused - and that unused capacity can't be stored for later.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-155","concept":"c07-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement about the inventory characteristic of services is NOT correct?","options":["Unused capacity, such as an empty airline seat, is costly to the provider","Idle production capacity occurs when demand for a service exceeds the supply available","Fluctuating demand makes it hard to judge the capacity needed at peak times","Services cannot necessarily be stored and accessed when they are in demand"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Idle production capacity is the reverse: the SUPPLY of a service exceeds demand. The other statements correctly describe inventory - services can't be stored, fluctuating demand complicates peak planning, and unused capacity is expensive.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q07-156","concept":"c07-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why is an empty seat on a Prairie Air flight that has already departed considered a service-inventory problem?","options":["The seat's capacity perished when the plane left; it cannot be stored and sold later","Passengers cannot separate the pilot from the flight they are taking","The flight attendants' service quality varied on that particular flight","Passengers could not see or touch the flight before buying a ticket"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Services can't be stored, so capacity that goes unused at departure is lost forever - an inventory problem. Varying quality is inconsistency, not seeing the flight beforehand is intangibility, and the pilot being part of the service is inseparability.","source":"generated"}],"color":"#8FAA5A","short":"Products","emoji":"🏺","tag":"Products, brands, services","province":"Gallia","numeral":"VII"},{"chapter":8,"title":"New Product Development","topics":[{"id":"t08-1","order":1,"title":"The product life cycle","summary":"Every product moves through four stages: introduction, growth, maturity and decline. The right marketing mix changes at each stage. This topic covers how the sales and profit curves behave, the stage-by-stage management table (Figure 8-3) you must memorize, primary vs selective demand, and what to do when a product declines (deletion vs harvesting).","concepts":["c08-plc","c08-plc-stage-table","c08-introduction","c08-primary-selective-demand","c08-growth","c08-maturity","c08-decline","c08-deletion-harvesting"]},{"id":"t08-2","order":2,"title":"PLC shapes","summary":"Not every product follows the textbook curve. How much a consumer must learn changes the length of the introduction stage (high-learning vs low-learning products), and fashions and fads have their own distinctive patterns. Exam questions describe a product's sales pattern and ask you to name the shape.","concepts":["c08-high-learning","c08-low-learning","c08-fashion-fad"]},{"id":"t08-3","order":3,"title":"Extending the PLC","summary":"Instead of letting a mature product slide into decline, marketers can stretch its life: modify the product, modify the market, reposition it, or introduce a new product. Know each tactic's sub-types (product improvements, line extensions, new customers, increased use, new use situations, trading up and down) and the cannibalization risk that comes with line extensions.","concepts":["c08-plc-extension","c08-product-modification","c08-line-extension","c08-cannibalization","c08-market-modification","c08-repositioning"]},{"id":"t08-4","order":4,"title":"Innovation, adoption and failure","summary":"New products are the lifeblood of a company, but they are costly and risky to develop. This topic covers how new an innovation is (minor, continuous, radical), who adopts it and when (the adoption curve and its five categories), the four barriers that stop people adopting, and the five reasons new products fail.","concepts":["c08-degree-innovation","c08-adoption-curve","c08-adoption-barriers","c08-product-failure"]},{"id":"t08-5","order":5,"title":"Growth strategy and the NPD process","summary":"Firms choose where to grow using the strategic approaches to growth matrix (market penetration, product development, market development, diversification). They then follow a seven-step new product development process from strategy to commercialization. Know each step's purpose, methods and position in the sequence.","concepts":["c08-ansoff","c08-npd-process","c08-npd-strategy-ideas","c08-screening","c08-business-analysis","c08-development","c08-test-marketing","c08-commercialization"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"c08-plc","topic":"t08-1","term":"Product life cycle (PLC)","definition":"The stages a new product goes through in the marketplace (introduction, growth, maturity and decline), used to help manage a product from its initial launch to its eventual decline.","explain":"Think of the PLC as a product's biography. Sales (in units) rise slowly at launch, climb fast in growth, peak and plateau in maturity, and fall in decline. The profit curve lags behind sales: it is negative or minimal at introduction, reaches its maximum late in growth, levels off in maturity and then falls. The length of the cycle varies by product, and technology products tend to have much shorter cycles.","example":"Smartphones moved from introduction (few buyers, high prices) through rapid growth to maturity, where Rogers, Bell and Telus now fight over subscribers with discounts and promotions.","contrast":"The PLC tracks a PRODUCT's sales and profits over time; the adoption curve tracks the PEOPLE (innovators to laggards) who buy it over time. The NPD process is the seven-step internal process that happens BEFORE the PLC begins at commercialization.","trap":"Watch the timing of the peaks: profits reach their maximum in GROWTH and level off in maturity, while SALES peak in maturity. A statement that 'profits peak in maturity' or 'sales peak in growth' is wrong for this course.","mnemonic":"I-G-M-D: 'I Grow, Mature, Decline.' Profit peaks first (growth), sales peak second (maturity).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"PLC curve facts (Figure 8-1)","items":["Introduction: sales low and slow; profit negative or minimal","Growth: sales rise rapidly; profits increase and reach their maximum","Maturity: sales peak and plateau; profits are maximized and level off","Decline: sales fall; profits decrease to minimal","The profit curve lags the sales curve; technology products have shorter PLCs"]}},{"id":"c08-plc-stage-table","topic":"t08-1","term":"PLC stage table (Figure 8-3: managing the stages of the PLC)","definition":"The course's summary of how competition, the general marketing objective, the 4 Ps (product, price, place, promotion) and profit change across the introduction, growth, maturity and decline stages.","explain":"This table is a must-memorize grid: seven rows by four stages. Read each row as a story. Competitors go few, more, many, fewer. Objectives go awareness, product differentiation, brand loyalty, product rationalization. Distribution goes limited, increased, full, reduced. Most exam questions give you one or two cells and ask for the stage, or name a stage and ask for the right action.","example":"A Calgary start-up launching a smart bike lock with limited distribution, awareness advertising and a skimming price is in introduction. Once rivals copy it and the company adds features and expands into more stores, it has moved to growth.","contrast":"Don't mix up the objective row and the promotion row. Growth's OBJECTIVE is product differentiation and its PROMOTION emphasizes points of difference versus competitors. Maturity's objective is brand loyalty, but its promotion focuses on pricing and sales promotion.","trap":"Each price cue maps to one stage: 'skimming or penetration' = introduction; 'slowly reduced' = growth; discounts 'used frequently' = maturity; 'very low prices' = decline. 'Full distribution is achieved' = maturity, NOT growth (growth = distribution is increased).","mnemonic":"Objectives A-D-L-R: 'A Dog Loves Running' (Awareness, Differentiation, Loyalty, Rationalization).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 8-3 Managing the stages of the product life cycle","head":["Row","Introduction","Growth","Maturity","Decline"],"rows":[["Competition","Few competitors exist","More competitors enter the market","Many competitors in the market","Reduced competition, with some competitors leaving the market"],["General marketing objective","Awareness","Product differentiation","Brand loyalty","Product rationalization"],["Product","Focus on a new product or brand","Introduce more features","Ensure full product line is available and innovative with new ideas","Retain only best sellers or discontinue"],["Price","Use a skimming or penetration strategy","Prices are slowly reduced","Price discounts are used frequently","Very low prices"],["Place","Limited distribution","Distribution is increased","Full distribution is achieved","Distribution is reduced"],["Promotion","Focus on building awareness with advertising","Emphasize points of difference versus the competition","Focus on pricing and sales promotion","Only minimal promotion, if any"],["Profit","Minimal profits, if any","Increased profits that reach their maximum","Maximized profits that level off","Decreasing and minimal profits"]]}},{"id":"c08-introduction","topic":"t08-1","term":"Introduction stage","definition":"The first PLC stage, when a product is launched; the marketing objective is to create consumer awareness and stimulate trial, with few competitors, limited distribution and minimal profits, if any.","explain":"At launch nobody knows the product, so the job is awareness and trial. Costs are high (R&D, launch advertising) and sales are low, so profits are minimal or negative. Price is set with either skimming (a high price to recover R&D) or penetration (a low price to build volume), distribution is limited, and advertising focuses on building awareness. The firm must first stimulate primary demand for the product class, and later selective demand for its brand.","example":"When an Edmonton firm launches the first app-controlled snow-melting driveway mat, it advertises heavily to explain what the product is, sells it through only a few retailers, and earns little or no profit.","contrast":"Introduction vs growth: in introduction there are FEW competitors and the objective is AWARENESS; in growth MORE competitors enter, repeat buyers appear and the objective shifts to PRODUCT DIFFERENTIATION.","trap":"'Create awareness and stimulate trial' is the introduction signal. Skimming OR penetration pricing both belong to introduction, so don't let 'low price' push you to decline ('very low prices') or maturity ('frequent discounts').","mnemonic":"Introduction = 'Hello, world': awareness, trial, few rivals, little profit.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-primary-selective-demand","topic":"t08-1","term":"Primary vs selective demand","definition":"Primary demand is the desire for the product class rather than for a specific brand; selective demand is the desire for a specific brand within the product class.","explain":"When a product category is brand new, consumers don't yet want the category at all, so the pioneer must first sell the idea of the product class (primary demand). As competitors enter and the category becomes familiar, marketing shifts to persuading buyers to choose one brand over the others (selective demand). In the introduction stage the firm stimulates primary demand first and selective demand later.","example":"Early ads for oat milk explained why anyone would drink oat milk at all (primary demand); today oat-milk brands argue that their brand tastes better or is more sustainable than rivals (selective demand).","contrast":"Primary = 'buy this KIND of product'; selective = 'buy OUR brand of it'. Don't confuse selective demand with selective distribution (a place decision) or with brand loyalty (the maturity-stage objective).","trap":"A pioneering firm explaining what a new category does = primary demand. If the ad compares brands or stresses points of difference versus rivals, it is selective demand.","mnemonic":"Primary = Product class first; Selective = Specific brand second.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-growth","topic":"t08-1","term":"Growth stage","definition":"The second PLC stage, marked by rapidly increasing sales, the entry of more competitors, repeat purchasers and an objective of product differentiation; profits increase and reach their maximum.","explain":"Word spreads, early buyers become repeat buyers, and sales climb fast. Competitors see the opportunity and enter, so the firm differentiates: it introduces more features and versions, slowly reduces prices, increases distribution, and its promotion emphasizes points of difference versus the competition. Profits increase and reach their peak late in this stage.","example":"As e-bikes caught on in Canada, early brands added longer-range batteries and cargo versions, expanded from specialty shops into big sporting-goods chains, and advertised what made their bike better than the new rivals.","contrast":"Growth vs maturity: growth = MORE competitors entering, distribution INCREASED, prices SLOWLY REDUCED, profits REACH their maximum; maturity = MANY competitors, FULL distribution, FREQUENT discounts, profits LEVEL OFF.","trap":"Profits PEAK in growth (per the course table), even though sales keep rising into maturity. 'Emphasize points of difference versus the competition' is growth's promotion, not introduction's (awareness) or maturity's (pricing and sales promotion).","mnemonic":"Growth = 'Get different': more features, more rivals, more stores, max profit.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-maturity","topic":"t08-1","term":"Maturity stage","definition":"The third PLC stage, when sales growth slows and sales peak, many competitors fight for share, marginal competitors drop out and the objective is brand loyalty; profits are maximized and level off.","explain":"Most people who will buy the product already have, so growth comes from taking share. With many competitors, firms try to hold market share and build brand loyalty: they ensure the full product line is available and innovative (often adding line extensions), use price discounts frequently, achieve full distribution and promote through pricing and sales promotion. Profits level off and eventually start to decline. Most PLC extension strategies are used here.","example":"Canadian soft drinks are mature: the big brands offer many flavours and sizes, are sold everywhere from Loblaws to gas stations, and constantly run flyer deals.","contrast":"Maturity vs decline: maturity = MANY competitors and FREQUENT discounts with FULL distribution; decline = competitors LEAVING, VERY LOW prices, REDUCED distribution and minimal promotion.","trap":"'Full distribution is achieved' and 'price discounts are used frequently' are maturity cues. 'Brand loyalty' is maturity's objective, so don't pick growth (product differentiation). Sales peak in maturity, while profits peaked in growth and level off here.","mnemonic":"Maturity = 'Many rivals, loyal fans, everywhere, always on sale.'","figure":null},{"id":"c08-decline","topic":"t08-1","term":"Decline stage","definition":"The final PLC stage, when sales fall (usually because of environmental change or new technology), competition is reduced as some competitors leave, and the objective is product rationalization.","explain":"When a better technology or changing tastes arrive, sales drop. Firms rationalize: they retain only best sellers or discontinue items, cut prices very low, reduce distribution and do only minimal promotion, if any. Profits are decreasing and minimal. Management then chooses between deletion (drop the product) and harvesting (keep it with reduced support).","example":"DVD players declined as streaming took over; the remaining makers trimmed their lines to a couple of models, sold them at very low prices and stopped advertising.","contrast":"Decline vs maturity: both involve price pressure, but maturity uses FREQUENT DISCOUNTS while many competitors fight; decline has VERY LOW PRICES while competitors EXIT and distribution SHRINKS. Decline vs introduction: introduction has FEW competitors that EXIST; decline has REDUCED competition as rivals LEAVE.","trap":"'Product rationalization' is the decline objective; don't confuse it with 'brand loyalty' (maturity). 'Reduced competition' signals decline, not introduction ('few competitors exist').","mnemonic":"Decline = 'Drop or milk': trim the line, slash the price, shrink the shelves.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-deletion-harvesting","topic":"t08-1","term":"Deletion vs harvesting","definition":"Deletion is dropping a product from the company's product line; harvesting is keeping the product but reducing marketing support (and costs) to reap some minor profits from the remaining sales.","explain":"Both are decline-stage strategies. Deletion removes the product completely, freeing resources for better opportunities. Harvesting keeps selling to remaining buyers while cutting promotion and other costs, so each remaining sale is more profitable even as volume shrinks.","example":"A Winnipeg snack maker drops its slow-selling sour-cream crisps entirely (deletion) but keeps its old-fashioned toffee on shelves with no advertising because a loyal base still buys it (harvesting).","contrast":"Harvesting KEEPS the product and CUTS support; deletion DROPS the product. Neither is a PLC extension strategy: extension strategies (product or market modification, repositioning, a new product) try to sustain or revive sales, while harvesting accepts the decline.","trap":"Harvesting cues: 'drastically reduces promotion', 'maximize the remaining profit', 'keeps the product'. Deletion cues: 'discontinue', 'drop from the line', 'remove'. Distractors on the official question are line extension, radical innovation and commercialization.","mnemonic":"Harvest = keep the crop, stop the watering. Delete = pull it out.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-high-learning","topic":"t08-2","term":"High-learning product","definition":"A product for which significant consumer education is required, resulting in an extended introductory period in the PLC.","explain":"If consumers must learn what a product is, how to use it and why it's better, sales take a long time to build, so the introduction stage stretches out. Marketers spend heavily on education before growth kicks in.","example":"When the Swiffer Sweeper first launched, consumers had to understand the new product, how to use it and what to do with their old brooms and mops. Early microwave ovens and electric vehicles are other examples.","contrast":"High-learning = LONG introduction because of consumer education; low-learning = SHORT introduction because benefits are self-evident. Both describe the PLC SHAPE; they are not the degree-of-innovation categories (minor, continuous, radical).","trap":"The stem describes consumers needing to learn how to use something, then asks what 'type of product' it is in PLC terms. Don't pick 'fad' or vague options like 'learning' or 'new concept'. If the question asks about degree of INNOVATION instead, the answer set is minor/continuous/radical.","mnemonic":"High learning = high hurdle = long launch.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-low-learning","topic":"t08-2","term":"Low-learning product","definition":"A product whose benefits are self-evident and requires little learning, so it has a short introductory stage and sales begin immediately.","explain":"Because buyers instantly understand what the product does, it skips the long education phase and sales start right away. The downside is that such products are easily copied by competitors, so the firm needs strong, broad distribution to win share quickly.","example":"Dr. Oetker's new frozen pizza flavours sell well immediately with very little promotion; a new cereal under a well-known brand behaves the same way.","contrast":"Low-learning vs high-learning: SHORT introduction and immediate sales vs EXTENDED introduction and heavy education. Low-learning is about PLC SHAPE; the same product judged on degree of innovation would be a MINOR innovation.","trap":"'Sells well immediately with very little promotion' = low-learning for a PLC-shape question. Don't choose 'fashion' or 'fad': a fad also rises quickly, but its defining feature is the rapid FALL.","mnemonic":"Low learning = little lag.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-fashion-fad","topic":"t08-2","term":"Fashion and fad products","definition":"A fashion product is a style that becomes popular, declines and then returns in cycles over the years; a fad is a product with a rapid rise and an equally rapid fall, giving it a very short life cycle.","explain":"Both have unusual PLC shapes. Fashions are cyclical: a style falls out of favour and comes back years later. Fads explode in popularity and disappear almost as quickly, usually without returning.","example":"Fidget spinners and the Pet Rock were fads; wide-leg jeans and retro sneaker styles are fashions that keep coming back.","contrast":"Fashion = comes BACK (cyclical, over years); fad = up fast and DOWN fast (very short life, no comeback). A low-learning product also starts fast, but it can enjoy a long life afterward.","trap":"'Reappears', 'returns', 'comes back in style' = fashion. 'Craze', 'sudden spike then collapse', 'gone within a year' = fad. Don't equate 'fashion' with the clothing industry: a clothing item can be a fad.","mnemonic":"Fashion = Frequent comeback; Fad = Fast and finished.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-plc-extension","topic":"t08-3","term":"PLC extension strategies","definition":"Four ways marketers can extend a product's life cycle: (1) modifying the product, (2) modifying the market, (3) repositioning the product and (4) introducing a new product.","explain":"Rather than accept decline, a firm can change the product itself (improvements, line extensions), change who buys it or how it is used (new customers, more use, new use situations), change how it is positioned and the value it offers (repositioning, trading up or down), or replace it with a new product. These strategies are typically used in late growth and maturity.","example":"Arm & Hammer extended the life of its baking soda by promoting a new use situation (deodorizing the fridge) rather than just baking.","contrast":"Extension strategies try to SUSTAIN or REVIVE sales; harvesting and deletion are decline-stage strategies that ACCEPT falling sales. Don't confuse them with the strategic approaches to growth (Ansoff matrix), which classify growth by product and market newness.","trap":"Know each tactic's parent bucket: product improvements and line extensions = MODIFYING THE PRODUCT; new customers, increased use and new use situations = MODIFYING THE MARKET; trading up and trading down = REPOSITIONING.","mnemonic":"P-M-R-N: 'Please Make Revenue Now' (Product, Market, Reposition, New product).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Four PLC extension strategies","items":["1. Modifying the product: product improvements; line extensions","2. Modifying the market: finding new customers; increasing a product's use; creating a new use situation","3. Repositioning a product: meet changing consumer needs, react to competition or improve value (trading up / trading down)","4. Introducing a new product"]}},{"id":"c08-product-modification","topic":"t08-3","term":"Product modification (product improvements)","definition":"A PLC extension strategy that alters a product's characteristics, such as its quality, performance or appearance, to increase sales; product improvements and line extensions are its two forms.","explain":"Here the firm changes the PRODUCT, not the market. A product improvement upgrades the existing item (a better formula, longer battery life, a new design) so it stays competitive. A line extension adds a new variety, flavour or size to the existing line. Both aim to keep a mature product fresh.","example":"A Canadian detergent brand reformulates its existing liquid to clean better in cold water and relaunches it as 'new and improved', which is a product improvement.","contrast":"A product improvement modifies an EXISTING item; a line extension ADDS a new item to the line; market modification leaves the product alone and changes WHO uses it or HOW/WHEN it is used.","trap":"If the product itself is unchanged and only the label recipes or the target customer change, it is MARKET modification, not a product improvement. The official soup-recipe question uses 'product improvements' as a distractor.","mnemonic":"Improve the item or extend the line: either way you're changing the PRODUCT.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-line-extension","topic":"t08-3","term":"Line extension","definition":"The addition of a new item, such as a new flavour, size, variety or model, to an already existing product line under the same brand.","explain":"Line extensions are a product-modification strategy used heavily in maturity: they keep a brand fresh, fill shelf space and meet varied needs. Because the brand is already known and trusted, new items usually require little learning and sell quickly. The risk is that too many varieties confuse consumers and cannibalize the brand's existing items, which is the point of the slide asking 'Why all the varieties? Good idea?' about Tide.","example":"Kellogg's adds two new cereals to its well-known brands and sells over 3 million units in the first quarter because consumers already trust the name.","contrast":"Line extension = a new item in the SAME product line and category; brand extension (Chapter 7) = an existing brand name moved into a NEW product class; product improvement = changing an EXISTING item rather than adding one.","trap":"Line-extension scenarios come with distractors like test marketing, harvesting or dumping. If the brand enters a totally different category (a cereal brand launching frozen pizza), think brand extension, not line extension.","mnemonic":"Line extension = longer line, same brand, same category.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-cannibalization","topic":"t08-3","term":"Cannibalization","definition":"When a new product takes sales away from the firm's own existing products rather than from competitors.","explain":"If a company launches something its own customers buy INSTEAD of its current products, total company sales barely grow. Cannibalization is a key risk of adding too many line extensions, because new varieties mostly pull buyers from the brand's existing varieties.","example":"When a snack brand launches a 'lightly salted' version of its chips, many buyers simply switch from its regular chips, so the new item mostly eats into the company's own sales.","contrast":"Cannibalization = sales shifted from the firm's OWN products; normal competition = sales taken from or lost to RIVALS. A line extension is the action; cannibalization is a possible consequence.","trap":"'Sales of the company's existing product fell after it launched its own new version' = cannibalization, not the decline stage or harvesting. The cause is the firm's own new product, not an outside technology change or a competitor.","mnemonic":"Cannibal = eating your own.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-market-modification","topic":"t08-3","term":"Market modification","definition":"A PLC extension strategy that changes the market for a product rather than the product itself, by finding new customers, increasing a product's use, or creating a new use situation.","explain":"Finding new customers means selling the same product to a group that did not buy it before. Increasing a product's use means getting existing customers to use it more often or in larger amounts. Creating a new use situation means promoting a new occasion or purpose for the product.","example":"Suzanne's Soup Company added dinner recipes to its labels so people would eat its soup at dinner, not just lunch (a new use situation); Arm & Hammer promoting baking soda as a fridge deodorizer is another new use situation.","contrast":"New use situation = a new OCCASION or PURPOSE; increasing use = the SAME use more OFTEN or in larger amounts; finding new customers = a new GROUP of buyers. Product modification, by contrast, changes the product itself.","trap":"Recipes on labels and 'use it for X too' = new use situation, not increasing use. 'Three cans a day instead of one' = increasing use. 'Market our baby shampoo to adults' = finding new customers.","mnemonic":"Who, how much, what for: new customers (who), increased use (how much), new use situation (what for).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Three market modifications","items":["Finding new customers: a new group of users for the unchanged product","Increasing a product's use: current users use it more often or in larger amounts","Creating a new use situation: a new occasion or purpose for the product"]}},{"id":"c08-repositioning","topic":"t08-3","term":"Repositioning (incl. trading up and trading down)","definition":"Changing the place a product occupies in consumers' minds relative to competing products, in order to meet changing consumer needs, react to competition or improve value; it includes trading up and trading down.","explain":"A firm may reposition to react to a competitor's position, reach a new market, catch a rising trend or change the value it offers. Trading up adds value (features, higher quality) and raises the price. Trading down reduces features, quality or price, and also includes downsizing package contents while keeping the price the same.","example":"A Quebec cheese maker repositions its mild cheddar from a kids' lunchbox snack to a premium charcuterie-board cheese with new packaging and a higher price (trading up).","contrast":"Repositioning changes the product's POSITION and value in consumers' minds; product modification changes the PRODUCT itself (an improvement or a new item in the line); market modification targets new users or uses. Trading up = more for more; trading down = less for less, or less for the same price.","trap":"Shrinking package contents at the same price is trading DOWN, not a product improvement. Upgrading an existing item and raising its price is trading UP, not a line extension, unless a separate new item is added to the line.","mnemonic":"Up = more for more; Down = less for less (or less for the same).","figure":null},{"id":"c08-degree-innovation","topic":"t08-4","term":"Degree of innovation (minor, continuous, radical)","definition":"A classification of new products by how much new learning they require of consumers: minor innovations require no new learning, continuous innovations change the consumer's normal routine but do not require totally new learning, and radical innovations require new learning and consumption patterns.","explain":"'Newness' can be judged from the company's view (new to the firm), the market's view (new to the market) or the consumer's view (how much the consumer must learn). The more learning required, the more the marketing must educate: minor innovations need awareness and wide distribution; continuous innovations need advertising of points of difference and benefits; radical innovations need education through advertising, product trial and personal selling, with public relations playing a major role.","example":"New and improved toothpaste = minor; Swiffer WetJet or wearable technology = continuous; Amazon Alexa, self-driving cars or 3D printers = radical.","contrast":"Degree of innovation classifies the innovation by CONSUMER LEARNING; high- and low-learning describe the SHAPE of the PLC. One product can be both: Dr. Oetker's new pizza flavours are a low-learning product AND a minor innovation.","trap":"'Continuous improvement' and 'mini updates' are not course terms. 'Changes the routine but not totally new learning' = continuous, not radical. 'Public relations plays a major role', 'personal selling' and 'product trial' = radical. 'Wide distribution' as the emphasis = minor.","mnemonic":"Minor = no learning; Continuous = new routine; Radical = new everything.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 8-5 Degree of product innovation","head":["Degree","Consumer learning","Examples","Marketing emphasis"],"rows":[["Minor innovation","Requires no new learning by consumers","New and improved toothpaste or detergent","Gain consumer awareness and wide distribution"],["Continuous innovation","Changes the consumer's normal routine but does not require totally new learning","Swiffer WetJet; wearable technology","Advertise points of difference and benefits to consumers"],["Radical innovation","Requires new learning and consumption patterns by consumers","Amazon Alexa / Google Home; self-driving cars; 3D printers","Educate consumers through advertising, product trial and personal selling; public relations can play a major role"]]}},{"id":"c08-adoption-curve","topic":"t08-4","term":"Adoption curve (diffusion of innovation)","definition":"A model of how a new product is adopted over time by five adopter categories: innovators (2.5%), early adopters (13.5%), early majority (34%), late majority (34%) and laggards (16%).","explain":"People don't all buy a new product at once. Venturesome innovators try it first, then early adopters, the leaders in social settings whose approval influences the deliberate early majority. The skeptical late majority follows, and laggards, who fear debt and rely on neighbours and friends for information, come last. Marketers court early adopters because their influence drives the mass market.","example":"When a new smart-ring fitness tracker launches in Toronto, tech enthusiasts who read many review sources buy it first (innovators); popular fitness instructors who post about it are early adopters; most others follow over the next few years.","contrast":"Innovators are VENTURESOME, higher educated and use MULTIPLE information sources; early adopters are LEADERS in social settings with slightly above-average education. Early majority (deliberate, many informal social contacts) vs late majority (skeptical, below-average social status): both are 34%.","trap":"Percentages: 2.5 / 13.5 / 34 / 34 / 16. Laggards are 16%, not 2.5%. Innovators plus early adopters together = 16%; adoption reaches 50% once the early majority has adopted and 84% after the late majority. 'Leaders in social settings' = early adopters, NOT innovators.","mnemonic":"2.5-13.5-34-34-16: 'Venturesome, Leaders, Deliberate, Skeptical, Debt-fearing.'","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 8-6 The adoption curve","head":["Category","Share","Descriptors"],"rows":[["Innovators","2.5%","Venturesome; higher educated; use multiple information sources"],["Early adopters","13.5%","Leaders in social settings; slightly above average education"],["Early majority","34%","Deliberate; many informal social contacts"],["Late majority","34%","Skeptical; below average social status"],["Laggards","16%","Fear of debt; neighbours and friends are information sources"]]}},{"id":"c08-adoption-barriers","topic":"t08-4","term":"Barriers to adoption","definition":"Four barriers that deter consumers from adopting a new product: usage, value, risk and psychological barriers.","explain":"A usage barrier exists when the product isn't compatible with consumers' existing habits. A value barrier exists when the product offers no incentive to switch from what people already use. A risk barrier is the perceived physical, economic or social risk of adopting. A psychological barrier comes from cultural differences or image concerns. Marketers must identify which barrier is at work before they can remove it.","example":"Many Albertans hesitate to buy an electric vehicle because they fear being stranded far from a charger on a cold highway (a risk barrier), even though they understand how to drive one.","contrast":"Usage = 'it doesn't fit how I do things'; value = 'it's not better than what I have'; risk = 'what if it fails, costs me, or embarrasses me'; psychological = 'it clashes with my culture or my image'.","trap":"The four barriers are Usage, Value, Risk and Psychological; 'price', 'distribution' and 'awareness' are not on the list. A cost worry can be a value barrier (not worth switching) or a risk barrier (fear of an expensive mistake), so read the cue words.","mnemonic":"U-V-R-P: 'Useful? Valuable? Risky? Personal?'","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Four barriers to adoption","items":["Usage: the product is not compatible with existing habits","Value: the product offers no incentive to change","Risk: physical, economic or social risk of adopting","Psychological: cultural differences or image concerns"]}},{"id":"c08-product-failure","topic":"t08-4","term":"NPD challenges and why new products fail","definition":"New products are the lifeblood of a company, but new product development is expensive and risky; new products commonly fail because of an insignificant point of difference, an incomplete new concept definition, insufficient market attractiveness, poor execution of the marketing mix, or bad timing.","explain":"NPD is an expensive undertaking with a high risk of failure, high research costs and extensive time and effort spent on prototypes and marketing materials. A failure means expensive product write-offs (labour, materials and expenses to fix the problem) and a lack of future credibility in the market. Figure 8-7 pairs each failure issue with a solution; for example, an insignificant point of difference is fixed by determining a distinctive, meaningful POD through consumer research and competitor monitoring.","example":"The Amazon Fire Phone, Google Glass, Lululemon's sheer pants and New Coke are notable new-product failures shown in the slides.","contrast":"Insignificant POD (not meaningfully different) vs incomplete concept definition (features, benefits and positioning never clearly defined) vs insufficient market attractiveness (target market too small or not growing) vs poor marketing-mix execution (4 Ps misaligned, weak distribution) vs bad timing (launched when consumers weren't eager or conditions were wrong).","trap":"Match the SOLUTION to the issue: 'identify a target market large enough with growth potential' fixes insufficient market attractiveness; 'align the 4 Ps incl. brand name and package and gain sufficient distribution' fixes poor execution; 'launch when consumers are eager; monitor market conditions and competitors' fixes bad timing.","mnemonic":"Failure causes P-C-M-E-T: POD, Concept, Market, Execution, Timing.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 8-7 Why new products and services fail","head":["Issue","Potential solution"],"rows":[["Insignificant point of difference","Determine a distinctive, meaningful POD for the target audience; conduct research with consumers and monitor competitors"],["Incomplete new concept definition","Identify consumer insights and clearly define the product's features and benefits; develop a clear positioning"],["Insufficient market attractiveness","Identify a target market (with a need) that is large enough and has growth potential"],["Poor execution of the marketing mix","Ensure the 4 Ps (including brand name and package) are aligned and attractive; focus on gaining sufficient distribution"],["Bad timing","Launch when consumers are eager to purchase; monitor market conditions and competitor actions"]]}},{"id":"c08-ansoff","topic":"t08-5","term":"Strategic approaches to growth (Ansoff matrix)","definition":"A matrix that classifies growth strategies by whether the product and the market are current or new: market penetration (current product, current market), product development (new product, current market), market development (current product, new market) and diversification (new product, new market).","explain":"Ask two questions: is the product current or new, and is the market (the customers) current or new? Market penetration finds ways to make current products appeal more to current customers. Product development reaches current customers with a new product. Market development reaches new customers, such as a new segment or a new country, with a current product. Diversification reaches new customers with a new product and carries the most risk because nothing is familiar.","example":"A coffee chain running a loyalty promotion so regulars visit more often is market penetration; adding a new menu item for those regulars is product development; taking its existing menu to a new country is market development; launching an unrelated product for a new market is diversification.","contrast":"Market DEVELOPMENT = current product, NEW market; product DEVELOPMENT = NEW product, current market. The word before 'development' tells you what is new. Adapting an existing product line to a new country's culture is still market development, not product development or diversification.","trap":"Light adaptation of an existing line for a foreign market (the official Yummy Brand question) = market development. Call it diversification only when BOTH the product and the market are genuinely new. Selling more of the same product to the same customers, or winning share from rivals in the same market, = market penetration.","mnemonic":"The word before 'development' names what's NEW. Penetration = nothing new; diversification = everything new.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 8-8 Strategic approaches to growth","head":["Markets","Current products","New products"],"rows":[["Current markets","MARKET PENETRATION: finding ways to make current products appeal to current customers","PRODUCT DEVELOPMENT: reaching current customers with a new product"],["New markets","MARKET DEVELOPMENT: reaching new customers with a current product","DIVERSIFICATION: reaching new customers with a new product"]]}},{"id":"c08-npd-process","topic":"t08-5","term":"New product development (NPD) process","definition":"The seven sequential steps a firm uses to develop new products: (1) new product development strategy, (2) idea generation, (3) screening and evaluation, (4) business analysis, (5) development, (6) test marketing and (7) commercialization.","explain":"The process works like a funnel: many ideas enter, and at each step weak ideas are eliminated so money is spent only on the most promising ones. The early steps are cheap (strategy, ideas, screening with concept tests); the later steps (prototypes, test markets, full launch) are expensive. Test marketing is done only if necessary.","example":"A Calgary beverage company sets a goal of low-sugar drinks (strategy), collects 120 flavour ideas from staff and customers (idea generation), narrows them to five concepts tested with consumers (screening and evaluation), projects costs and profits (business analysis), brews prototypes (development), sells them only in Lethbridge (test marketing), then launches across Alberta (commercialization).","contrast":"Screening and evaluation narrows ideas and uses CONCEPT TESTS (descriptions); business analysis makes FINANCIAL projections; development builds PROTOTYPES; test marketing sells on a LIMITED scale in defined areas; commercialization is the FULL launch.","trap":"Know the positions: screening and evaluation is step 3; business analysis is step 4 and comes BEFORE development (step 5); test marketing is step 6, BEFORE commercialization. Concept tests happen in screening, not development.","mnemonic":"'Smart Ideas Survive Business Development Testing Completely': Strategy, Ideas, Screening, Business analysis, Development, Test marketing, Commercialization.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Figure 8-9 Steps in the NPD process","items":["1. New product development strategy: identify an NPD focus that meets company objectives (company objectives; SWOT analysis)","2. Idea generation: brainstorm new ideas (employees, co-workers, consumers)","3. Screening and evaluation: evaluate ideas and develop concepts (internal technical evaluation; external concept tests)","4. Business analysis: key features, marketing strategy, financial projections (production, legal, profitability)","5. Development: create and test the prototype internally and with consumers","6. Test marketing: test product and marketing strategy on a limited scale, if necessary","7. Commercialization: launch and fully market the product (all of the marketing mix; possible regional rollout)"]}},{"id":"c08-npd-strategy-ideas","topic":"t08-5","term":"NPD strategy and idea generation (steps 1-2)","definition":"Step 1, new product development strategy, identifies a new-product focus that meets company objectives, using the company's objectives and a SWOT analysis; step 2, idea generation, brainstorms new ideas from sources such as employees, co-workers, consumers, suppliers, competitors, universities and crowdsourcing.","explain":"Before inventing anything, the firm decides what kind of new products fit its goals and strengths (the strategic direction, often framed with the growth matrix). Then it casts a wide net for ideas: employees and co-workers, consumers' suggestions and complaints, suppliers, competitors (through reverse engineering), universities and inventors, and crowdsourcing or open innovation.","example":"A Halifax seafood company reviews its objectives and runs a SWOT analysis showing strength in frozen distribution (strategy), then runs an online contest inviting customers to suggest new frozen meals (idea generation through crowdsourcing).","contrast":"Strategy = deciding the FOCUS (objectives, SWOT); idea generation = BRAINSTORMING many ideas; screening and evaluation = NARROWING those ideas to a manageable list of promising concepts.","trap":"Reverse engineering a competitor's product and crowdsourcing are IDEA GENERATION sources, not screening or development. A SWOT analysis of the company, product or brand belongs to the STRATEGY step, not business analysis.","mnemonic":"First decide WHERE to look (strategy), then look EVERYWHERE (ideas).","figure":null},{"id":"c08-screening","topic":"t08-5","term":"Screening and evaluation (incl. concept tests)","definition":"The third NPD step, which internally and externally evaluates new-product ideas to eliminate those that warrant no further effort, reducing the array of brainstormed ideas to a manageable list of promising concepts.","explain":"Internally, the company checks technical requirements and fit. Externally, it runs concept tests: consumers see a description or sketch of the product idea (not an actual product) and say how interested they are. Ideas that fail are dropped cheaply, before money is spent on detailed financials or prototypes.","example":"A Vancouver kitchenware firm shows 300 consumers written descriptions of six gadget ideas and drops the four with the weakest purchase intent.","contrast":"Concept test (screening) = consumers react to a DESCRIPTION of an idea; prototype test (development) = consumers try an actual working MODEL; test market = consumers BUY the finished product in a limited area.","trap":"'Reduce the array of brainstorming ideas to a manageable list' = screening and evaluation (official question), not idea generation or development. If consumers react to descriptions or drawings, it is a concept test in screening.","mnemonic":"Screen = sieve: lots of ideas in, a few concepts out.","figure":null},{"id":"c08-business-analysis","topic":"t08-5","term":"Business analysis","definition":"The fourth NPD step, which specifies the product's key features and anticipated marketing strategy and makes financial projections, assessing production, legal and profitability issues.","explain":"Before spending on prototypes, the firm checks whether the concept makes business sense: what features it will have, how it will be priced, promoted and distributed, what it will cost to produce, whether there are legal issues, and whether it will be profitable.","example":"Before building any prototypes, a Saskatoon agri-tech firm forecasts sales of its soil sensor, estimates production costs, checks for patent conflicts and projects a three-year break-even.","contrast":"Business analysis = paper and spreadsheet work (features, marketing mix, finances, legal); screening and evaluation = narrowing ideas with concept tests; development = building and testing the physical PROTOTYPE.","trap":"'Financial projections', 'profitability', 'legal issues' and 'production' = business analysis. It comes BEFORE development (step 4 vs step 5), even though some students assume you build first and crunch numbers afterward.","mnemonic":"Business analysis = 'Will it make money?'","figure":null},{"id":"c08-development","topic":"t08-5","term":"Development (prototype step)","definition":"The fifth NPD step, which turns the idea on paper into a prototype and tests it internally at the company and externally with consumers.","explain":"Engineers and designers build working models. Internal company assessments (lab tests of safety, durability and performance) and external tests in which consumers use the prototype make sure the product works and that people like it before it goes anywhere near a store.","example":"A Montreal toy maker builds 50 working prototypes of a coding robot, stress-tests them in its lab and lends them to families for two weeks to gather feedback.","contrast":"Development = consumers test a working PROTOTYPE; concept tests in screening = consumers react only to a DESCRIPTION; test marketing = the finished product is actually SOLD in a limited area.","trap":"Don't confuse the NPD step 'development' with 'product development' in the growth matrix (a new product for current customers) or 'market development' (a current product for new customers). In the NPD process, development means building and testing prototypes.","mnemonic":"Development = 'Make a model and try it.'","figure":null},{"id":"c08-test-marketing","topic":"t08-5","term":"Test marketing","definition":"The sixth NPD step: offering a product for sale on a limited basis in a defined area to gauge consumer reactions and test the marketing strategy before a full launch.","explain":"Types include standard test markets (the product is sold in actual cities through regular channels), controlled test markets (retailers are paid to stock and display the product) and simulated or laboratory test markets (consumers see ads and shop in a simulated store). Test marketing is not always necessary: it is expensive, it alerts competitors, and it isn't practical for durable goods.","example":"A packaged-food company sells its new frozen perogies only in Regina and Saskatoon for three months to measure repeat purchases before going national.","contrast":"Test marketing sells the FINISHED product on a LIMITED scale to gather information; commercialization is the FULL-scale launch; development tests PROTOTYPES; a regional rollout (part of commercialization) is a sequenced full launch, not a test.","trap":"Firms may SKIP test marketing when it is too costly, when they don't want to tip off competitors, or for durable goods such as cars or appliances. 'Retailers are paid' = controlled test market; 'simulated store' = simulated test market; 'actual cities, regular channels' = standard test market.","mnemonic":"Test market = 'Try it in Regina before all of Canada.'","figure":null},{"id":"c08-commercialization","topic":"t08-5","term":"Commercialization (incl. regional rollout)","definition":"The seventh and final NPD step: positioning and launching a new product in full-scale production and sales with full marketing support, implementing all areas of the marketing mix.","explain":"This is the expensive full launch: production ramps up, the full marketing mix goes live, and the product enters the introduction stage of the PLC. Many firms use a regional rollout, introducing the product sequentially into geographical areas so that production and marketing can build up gradually.","example":"After a successful start in Ontario, a beverage start-up launches its sparkling tea in Quebec in spring, the Prairies in summer and B.C. in fall: a regional rollout during commercialization.","contrast":"Commercialization = FULL-scale launch (possibly region by region); test marketing = LIMITED sale to gather data before deciding whether to launch. A regional rollout is a launch schedule, not a test.","trap":"'Full-scale production, sales and marketing support' = commercialization (official question). Distractors include concept test, test marketing and business analysis. Commercialization is where the NPD process hands off to the PLC's introduction stage.","mnemonic":"Commercialization = 'Go big, or go region by region.'","figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"a08-01","type":"order","concept":"c08-npd-process","title":"Order the NPD steps","prompt":"Put the seven steps of the new product development process in the correct order.","items":["New product development strategy","Idea generation","Screening and evaluation","Business analysis","Development","Test marketing","Commercialization"],"explanation":"The process is a funnel. The firm first sets a strategic focus, then brainstorms ideas, screens them down to a manageable list (concept tests), runs the numbers on the survivors (business analysis), builds and tests prototypes (development), tests the finished product on a limited scale if necessary (test marketing), and finally launches with full marketing support (commercialization). Business analysis comes before development so money isn't spent on prototypes that won't be profitable."},{"id":"a08-02","type":"sort","concept":"c08-ansoff","title":"Sort into the growth matrix","prompt":"Drag each scenario into the correct strategic approach to growth.","buckets":["Market penetration","Product development","Market development","Diversification"],"items":[{"text":"A coffee chain launches a loyalty app so regulars buy their usual drinks more often","bucket":0},{"text":"A paint store cuts prices on its existing paint to win neighbourhood shoppers from rivals","bucket":0},{"text":"A bank creates a new travel-insurance product for its current account holders","bucket":1},{"text":"A yogurt maker adds a new high-protein yogurt for its existing grocery shoppers","bucket":1},{"text":"A Canadian snack brand adapts its existing line to local tastes and enters Costa Rica","bucket":2},{"text":"A children's sunscreen brand starts marketing the same sunscreen to adult golfers","bucket":2},{"text":"A dairy co-operative opens a chain of car washes in a new province","bucket":3},{"text":"A furniture maker launches electric scooters aimed at university students","bucket":3}],"explanation":"Ask two questions: is the product current or new, and is the market current or new? Same product, same customers = market penetration. New product, same customers = product development. Same product, new customers (a new segment or a new country, even with cultural adaptation) = market development. New product, new customers = diversification."},{"id":"a08-03","type":"sort","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","title":"Sort PLC stage characteristics","prompt":"Drag each characteristic from Figure 8-3 to the PLC stage where it belongs.","buckets":["Introduction","Growth","Maturity","Decline"],"items":[{"text":"Few competitors exist","bucket":0},{"text":"Use a skimming or penetration strategy","bucket":0},{"text":"Focus on building awareness with advertising","bucket":0},{"text":"Prices are slowly reduced","bucket":1},{"text":"Objective: product differentiation","bucket":1},{"text":"Increased profits that reach their maximum","bucket":1},{"text":"Full distribution is achieved","bucket":2},{"text":"Price discounts are used frequently","bucket":2},{"text":"Objective: brand loyalty","bucket":2},{"text":"Retain only best sellers or discontinue","bucket":3},{"text":"Distribution is reduced","bucket":3},{"text":"Objective: product rationalization","bucket":3}],"explanation":"Read each row of Figure 8-3 as a story. Competition: few, more, many, reduced. Objective: awareness, product differentiation, brand loyalty, product rationalization. Price: skimming or penetration, slowly reduced, frequent discounts, very low. Place: limited, increased, full, reduced. Profit: minimal, rising to its maximum (growth), maximized and levelling off (maturity), decreasing (decline)."},{"id":"a08-04","type":"match","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","title":"Match the adopter categories","prompt":"Match each adopter category with its share of adopters and its descriptors.","pairs":[{"left":"Innovators","right":"2.5%: venturesome; higher educated; use multiple information sources"},{"left":"Early adopters","right":"13.5%: leaders in social settings; slightly above average education"},{"left":"Early majority","right":"34%: deliberate; many informal social contacts"},{"left":"Late majority","right":"34%: skeptical; below average social status"},{"left":"Laggards","right":"16%: fear of debt; neighbours and friends are information sources"}],"explanation":"The percentages run 2.5, 13.5, 34, 34, 16 and add to 100. Innovators are the venturesome risk-takers; early adopters are the leaders in social settings whose influence moves the deliberate early majority; the skeptical late majority follows; laggards fear debt and rely on neighbours and friends."},{"id":"a08-05","type":"sort","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","title":"Sort by degree of innovation","prompt":"Drag each example, learning requirement or marketing emphasis to its degree of innovation (Figure 8-5).","buckets":["Minor innovation","Continuous innovation","Radical innovation"],"items":[{"text":"New and improved detergent","bucket":0},{"text":"Requires no new learning by consumers","bucket":0},{"text":"Emphasis: gain consumer awareness and wide distribution","bucket":0},{"text":"Swiffer WetJet","bucket":1},{"text":"Changes the normal routine but does not require totally new learning","bucket":1},{"text":"Emphasis: advertise points of difference and benefits","bucket":1},{"text":"Self-driving cars","bucket":2},{"text":"Requires new learning and consumption patterns","bucket":2},{"text":"Emphasis: educate through advertising, product trial and personal selling; PR plays a major role","bucket":2}],"explanation":"The more a consumer must learn, the more the marketing must educate. Minor innovations need no learning, so awareness and wide distribution are enough. Continuous innovations change routines, so ads stress points of difference and benefits. Radical innovations need new consumption patterns, so firms educate through advertising, trial, personal selling and public relations."},{"id":"a08-06","type":"match","concept":"c08-plc-extension","title":"Match the PLC extension tactic","prompt":"Match each PLC extension tactic with the scenario that illustrates it.","pairs":[{"left":"Product improvement","right":"A detergent is reformulated to clean better in cold water"},{"left":"Line extension","right":"Kellogg's adds two new cereals to its well-known brands"},{"left":"Finding new customers","right":"A baby shampoo is marketed to adults with sensitive scalps"},{"left":"Increasing a product's use","right":"A sparkling-water brand urges current drinkers to have three cans a day instead of one"},{"left":"Creating a new use situation","right":"Labels on a lunch soup carry dinner recipes"},{"left":"Trading up","right":"Basic candles get soy wax, designer jars and a higher price"},{"left":"Trading down","right":"A chip bag shrinks from 235 g to 200 g at the same price"},{"left":"Introducing a new product","right":"A sixth-generation tablet replaces a fifth generation whose sales have flattened"}],"explanation":"Product improvements and line extensions modify the PRODUCT. New customers, increased use and new use situations modify the MARKET (who, how much, and for what). Trading up and trading down are REPOSITIONING moves that change the value offered. Replacing an ageing product with a new one is the fourth strategy, introducing a new product."}],"questions":[{"id":"q08-001","concept":"c08-plc","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The _____ describes the stages a new product goes through in the marketplace and is used to help manage a product from its initial launch to its eventual decline.","options":["adoption curve","product life cycle","new product development process","product mix"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The product life cycle (PLC) tracks a product through introduction, growth, maturity and decline in the marketplace. The adoption curve is tempting, but it describes the categories of PEOPLE who adopt a product over time, not the product's stages.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-002","concept":"c08-plc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over a decade, sales of Northwind's home robot vacuums rose slowly at first, then surged, then flattened once most interested households owned one, and are now falling as a newer technology arrives. Which concept does this pattern illustrate?","options":["The adoption curve","The new product development process","The product life cycle","A fad"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sales that rise slowly, surge, flatten and then fall trace the four PLC stages: introduction, growth, maturity and decline. It is not a fad, which rises and collapses rapidly over a very short life rather than over a decade.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-003","concept":"c08-plc","also":["c08-plc-stage-table"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly compares the timing of the sales and profit curves in the product life cycle?","options":["Profits reach their maximum in growth, while sales peak in maturity","Sales and profits both reach their peak in the maturity stage","Sales peak in growth, while profits peak in maturity","Profits peak in introduction because there are few competitors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Per Figure 8-3, growth brings 'increased profits that reach their maximum' and maturity brings 'maximized profits that level off', while sales keep rising until they peak in maturity. The 'both peak in maturity' option is the classic mistake: profits top out earlier, late in growth.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-004","concept":"c08-plc","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Technology products tend to have longer product life cycles than most other products because consumers need time to learn how to use them.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. The length of the PLC varies by product, and technology products tend to have SHORTER life cycles because newer technology quickly replaces them. Corrected statement: technology products tend to have shorter PLCs.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-005","concept":"c08-plc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which sequence correctly lists the stages of the product life cycle?","options":["Introduction, maturity, growth, decline","Growth, introduction, maturity, decline","Introduction, growth, maturity, decline","Introduction, growth, decline, maturity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The PLC runs introduction, growth, maturity, decline. A common slip is putting maturity before growth; sales must grow rapidly before they can level off in maturity.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-006","concept":"c08-plc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement about the PLC profit curve (Figure 8-1) is NOT true?","options":["Profit is negative or minimal during the introduction stage","Profits reach their peak at the same moment sales peak","Profits increase and reach their maximum during growth","Profits decrease to minimal levels in the decline stage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Profits peak late in the growth stage and then level off in maturity, while sales peak later, in maturity. So profits and sales do NOT peak at the same moment; the other three statements match the course's profit row.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-007","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which sequence correctly shows the general marketing objective in each PLC stage, from introduction to decline?","options":["Awareness, product differentiation, brand loyalty, product rationalization","Awareness, brand loyalty, product differentiation, product rationalization","Product differentiation, awareness, brand loyalty, product rationalization","Awareness, product differentiation, product rationalization, brand loyalty"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Figure 8-3's objective row is awareness (introduction), product differentiation (growth), brand loyalty (maturity), product rationalization (decline). Swapping differentiation and loyalty is the classic error: you differentiate while competitors are still entering, then defend with loyalty once the market is crowded.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-008","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Across the PLC, how does distribution (place) change from introduction to decline according to Figure 8-3?","options":["Limited, full, increased, reduced","Full, increased, limited, reduced","Increased, limited, full, reduced","Limited, increased, full, reduced"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The place row reads: limited distribution (introduction), distribution is increased (growth), full distribution is achieved (maturity), distribution is reduced (decline). Full distribution is not reached until maturity, so 'limited, full, increased' is wrong.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-009","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pairing of a PLC stage with a Figure 8-3 entry is INCORRECT?","options":["Introduction, price: use a skimming or penetration strategy","Growth, place: full distribution is achieved","Maturity, promotion: focus on pricing and sales promotion","Decline, product: retain only best sellers or discontinue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In growth, distribution is INCREASED; full distribution is achieved only in maturity. The other three pairings are exactly as the table lists them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-010","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"A classmate made four flashcards from Figure 8-3. Which card contains an error?","options":["Introduction, competition: few competitors exist","Growth, profit: increased profits that reach their maximum","Maturity, objective: product differentiation","Decline, promotion: only minimal promotion, if any"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maturity's general marketing objective is brand loyalty; product differentiation is the growth objective. The other three cards are correct, including growth as the stage where profits reach their maximum.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-011","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"One row of Figure 8-3 reads 'focus on building awareness with advertising' in introduction and 'emphasize points of difference versus the competition' in growth. Which row is it?","options":["General marketing objective","Product","Promotion","Competition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"These are promotion entries; the row continues with 'focus on pricing and sales promotion' (maturity) and 'only minimal promotion, if any' (decline). The objective row is tempting because it uses related ideas, but its entries are single terms: awareness, product differentiation, brand loyalty, product rationalization.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-012","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","also":["c08-decline"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A brand manager learns that her product's general marketing objective is now 'product rationalization'. Which set of actions fits that stage?","options":["Introduce more features, slowly reduce prices, increase distribution, stress points of difference","Ensure a full line, use frequent discounts, achieve full distribution, focus on sales promotion","Retain only best sellers, use very low prices, reduce distribution, keep promotion minimal","Focus on a new brand, use skimming, limit distribution, advertise for awareness"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Product rationalization is the decline-stage objective, so the matching column is: retain only best sellers or discontinue, very low prices, reduced distribution and minimal promotion. The full-line and frequent-discount option describes maturity, whose objective is brand loyalty.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-013","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A student writes: 'In maturity, promotion emphasizes points of difference versus the competition.' Which correction is accurate?","options":["Maturity promotion focuses on pricing and sales promotion; emphasizing points of difference belongs to growth","Maturity promotion focuses on building awareness with advertising; points of difference belong to decline","Maturity promotion is minimal, if any; points of difference belong to introduction","No correction is needed; maturity promotion emphasizes points of difference"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Figure 8-3, growth promotion emphasizes points of difference versus competitors (matching its product differentiation objective), while maturity promotion focuses on pricing and sales promotion. Minimal promotion belongs to decline, and awareness advertising to introduction.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-014","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","also":["c08-plc"],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In Figure 8-3, the profit row reads: minimal profits, if any (introduction); increased profits that reach their maximum (growth); maximized profits that level off (maturity); decreasing and minimal profits (decline).","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. This is the exact profit row. Profits reach their maximum in growth and then level off in maturity, which is why 'profits peak in maturity' is a trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-015","concept":"c08-plc-stage-table","also":["c08-decline"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does Figure 8-3 show 'reduced competition, with some competitors leaving the market' in the decline stage?","options":["Strong brand loyalty built in maturity blocks new rivals from entering","Patents earned during development prevent competitors from selling","Full distribution leaves no shelf space for additional competitors","Falling sales and minimal profits make the market unattractive, so some firms exit"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In decline, sales fall (often because of new technology or environmental change) and profits become decreasing and minimal, so some competitors leave. Brand loyalty is the maturity objective and does not explain why rivals exit in decline.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-016","concept":"c08-introduction","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cybrex is in the business of making software packages for tax purposes. It is in the _____ stage if its marketing objective is to create consumer awareness and stimulate trial for its new software packages.","options":["growth","maturity","decline","introduction"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The marketing objective in the introduction stage is to create consumer awareness and stimulate trial for the new product (Figure 8-3 lists awareness as the introduction objective). Growth is tempting, but by then the objective has shifted to product differentiation as more competitors enter.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-017","concept":"c08-introduction","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Solaris Shades has just launched a patented self-tinting window film, the first of its kind in Canada, and must set its initial price. According to Figure 8-3, which pricing approach fits this stage?","options":["A skimming or penetration strategy","Prices slowly reduced as rivals enter","Frequent price discounts to hold share","Very low prices to clear remaining stock"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A just-launched product is in introduction, where Figure 8-3 says to use a skimming (high price to recover R&D) or penetration (low price to build volume) strategy. 'Prices slowly reduced' is the growth-stage entry, after competitors begin to enter.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-018","concept":"c08-introduction","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why are profits typically minimal, if any, during the introduction stage?","options":["Sales are still low while R&D and launch advertising costs are high","Intense competition forces the firm into frequent price discounts","Full distribution pushes channel and shipping costs to their peak","Very low prices are used to clear out the remaining inventory"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In introduction, few people know the product, so sales are low, while the firm is still recovering R&D and spending heavily on awareness advertising. Intense competition and frequent discounts describe maturity, not introduction, where few competitors exist.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-019","concept":"c08-introduction","also":["c08-growth"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pair of characteristics best distinguishes the introduction stage from the growth stage?","options":["Many competitors and full distribution vs reduced competition and reduced distribution","Brand loyalty objective vs product rationalization objective","Few competitors and limited distribution vs more competitors entering and increased distribution","Frequent price discounts vs very low prices"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Introduction has few competitors and limited distribution; in growth, more competitors enter and distribution is increased. The other pairs compare maturity with decline, not introduction with growth.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-020","concept":"c08-introduction","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT typical of the introduction stage?","options":["Advertising focused on building awareness of the new product","Limited distribution through a few selected retailers","A focus on introducing a new product or brand","Promotion that emphasizes points of difference versus the competition"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Emphasizing points of difference versus competitors is the growth-stage promotion, used once rivals have entered. Introduction promotion focuses on building awareness with advertising, because there are few competitors to compare against.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-021","concept":"c08-introduction","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the introduction stage, the general marketing objective is product differentiation.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. The introduction objective is awareness: create consumer awareness and stimulate trial. Product differentiation is the growth-stage objective, when more competitors enter the market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-022","concept":"c08-primary-selective-demand","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The desire for a product class, rather than for a specific brand, is called:","options":["primary demand","selective demand","derived demand","brand loyalty"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Primary demand is demand for the product class as a whole, which a pioneer must build first in the introduction stage. Selective demand is the opposite: demand for a specific brand within the class.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-023","concept":"c08-primary-selective-demand","also":["c08-introduction"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ferment Co., the first company to sell kombucha in Atlantic Canada, runs ads explaining what kombucha is and why fermented tea is good for you, without comparing its brand to any other. These ads aim to stimulate:","options":["selective demand","brand loyalty","primary demand","product differentiation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Explaining what a new product category is and why to buy it at all builds primary demand, the first task in the introduction stage. Selective demand would require persuading buyers to choose Ferment Co. over competing brands, which these ads don't do.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-024","concept":"c08-primary-selective-demand","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Eight kombucha brands now crowd Atlantic Canadian shelves. Ferment Co., the category pioneer, changes its ads from explaining what kombucha is to stressing that its tea is organic and locally brewed, unlike rivals. The ads have shifted from stimulating:","options":["selective demand to primary demand","primary demand to selective demand","brand loyalty to product differentiation","product rationalization to awareness"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The early ads built primary demand (desire for the kombucha category); now that competitors exist, the ads build selective demand (desire for Ferment Co.'s brand specifically). The reverse order is wrong: a pioneer moves from primary to selective demand.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-025","concept":"c08-primary-selective-demand","also":["c08-introduction"],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the textbook, how should a pioneering firm sequence its demand-building efforts in the introduction stage?","options":["Stimulate selective demand first, then primary demand","Stimulate only selective demand, since the category is already known","Stimulate any demand only after the growth stage begins","Stimulate primary demand first, then later selective demand"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In introduction, the firm must first stimulate primary demand (for the product class) and later selective demand (for its brand). Consumers can't prefer a brand of a product class they don't yet want, so selective demand comes second.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-026","concept":"c08-primary-selective-demand","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"A company that is the only seller in a brand-new product category gains little from ads comparing its brand with rivals; its first task is to build primary demand.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. With no rivals and an unfamiliar category, the firm must first create desire for the product class itself (primary demand). Brand-versus-brand messages build selective demand, which matters once competitors enter.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-027","concept":"c08-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sales of PeakTrail's avalanche beacons are climbing quickly, and many buyers are now repeat purchasers. Several new rivals have entered, so PeakTrail is adding features, slowly lowering prices and getting into more stores. Which PLC stage is PeakTrail in?","options":["Maturity","Introduction","Growth","Decline"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rapidly rising sales, repeat purchasers, more competitors entering, more features, slowly reduced prices and increased distribution are all growth-stage signs. Maturity is tempting, but maturity has many established competitors, full distribution and frequent discounts rather than slowly falling prices.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-028","concept":"c08-growth","also":["c08-plc-stage-table"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rivals have started copying VoltPup's popular dog-walking e-collar, and sales are rising rapidly. According to Figure 8-3, what should VoltPup's promotion focus on now?","options":["Emphasizing points of difference versus the competition","Building awareness of the product with advertising","Focusing on pricing and frequent sales promotions","Keeping promotion to a minimum, if any at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Competitors entering plus rapidly rising sales signals growth, where promotion emphasizes points of difference versus the competition. Building awareness was the introduction-stage focus; pricing and sales promotion is the maturity-stage focus.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-029","concept":"c08-growth","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the growth stage of the PLC, the general marketing objective is _____.","options":["awareness","brand loyalty","product differentiation","product rationalization"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Growth's objective is product differentiation, because new competitors are entering and the firm must stand apart. Brand loyalty is the maturity objective, used once the market is crowded and the goal shifts to holding share.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-030","concept":"c08-growth","also":["c08-maturity"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Two products are both profitable. Product A's distribution is still being increased and its profits are rising to their maximum. Product B has full distribution and maximized profits that have levelled off. Which is correct?","options":["A is in maturity; B is in growth","A is in introduction; B is in growth","Both are in maturity because both are profitable","A is in growth; B is in maturity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Increasing distribution and profits that reach their maximum are growth entries; full distribution and maximized profits that level off are maturity entries. Being profitable does not by itself place a product in maturity.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-031","concept":"c08-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do more competitors typically enter the market during the growth stage?","options":["Rapidly rising sales and profits signal an attractive opportunity","Sales have levelled off, so firms compete mainly on price","The pioneering firm has stopped advertising","Consumers have lost interest in the product class"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In growth, sales climb rapidly and profits rise toward their maximum, which attracts rivals. Levelled-off sales describe maturity, by which point many competitors are already in the market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-032","concept":"c08-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a growth-stage characteristic?","options":["Repeat purchasers begin to appear in the market","More features and versions of the product are introduced","Profits increase and reach their maximum level","Price discounts are used frequently to hold market share"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Frequent price discounts to hold share are a maturity-stage tactic; in growth, prices are only slowly reduced. Repeat purchasers, added features and peak profits are all growth characteristics.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-033","concept":"c08-maturity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Canadian frozen-pizza brands are sold in nearly every grocery store, offer dozens of varieties and fight for share with weekly flyer discounts. Sales growth has slowed and a few small brands have dropped out. Which PLC stage is this category in?","options":["Growth","Decline","Maturity","Introduction"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Full distribution, a full product line, frequent price discounts, slowing sales growth and marginal competitors dropping out are maturity signs. It is not decline: competition is still intense and distribution is full rather than reduced.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-034","concept":"c08-maturity","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Figure 8-3 lists _____ as the general marketing objective of the maturity stage.","options":["brand loyalty","product differentiation","awareness","product rationalization"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Maturity's objective is brand loyalty: with many competitors and slowing growth, firms focus on holding market share. Product differentiation is the earlier growth objective.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-035","concept":"c08-maturity","also":["c08-plc-stage-table"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Brewster Coffee's bagged coffee faces many competitors and already has full distribution across Canada. Which action best fits its PLC stage according to Figure 8-3?","options":["Cut distribution and keep only the best-selling items in the line","Use frequent price discounts and sales promotion while keeping a full product line","Focus advertising on building awareness of the product category","Slowly reduce prices while expanding into more retail stores"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Many competitors plus full distribution means maturity, where firms ensure the full product line is available and innovative, use frequent price discounts and focus promotion on pricing and sales promotion. Slowly reducing prices while expanding distribution is the growth-stage pattern.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-036","concept":"c08-maturity","also":["c08-decline"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What best distinguishes the maturity stage from the decline stage in Figure 8-3?","options":["Maturity: very low prices and reduced distribution; decline: frequent discounts and full distribution","Maturity: few competitors; decline: many competitors","Maturity: profits reach their maximum; decline: profits level off","Maturity: frequent price discounts and full distribution; decline: very low prices and reduced distribution"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Maturity uses frequent price discounts with full distribution; decline uses very low prices with reduced distribution. The profit option is wrong because maturity's profits are maximized and level off, while decline's are decreasing and minimal.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-037","concept":"c08-maturity","also":["c08-plc-extension"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why are most PLC extension strategies, such as line extensions and finding new customers, applied during the maturity stage?","options":["Sales growth has slowed and many rivals fight for share, so new sources of sales are needed","Profits are still negative, so the firm must recover its R&D costs quickly","Distribution is still limited, so the firm needs to recruit many more retailers","Consumers do not yet understand what the product class does or why to buy it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In maturity, most potential buyers already own the product and many competitors fight over them, so firms modify the product, the market or the positioning to keep sales from sliding into decline. Negative profits and limited distribution describe introduction, not maturity.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-038","concept":"c08-maturity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT characteristic of the maturity stage?","options":["Marginal competitors drop out of the market","Firms focus on holding market share","Distribution is reduced to cut costs","Line extensions fill out the product line"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reduced distribution belongs to decline; in maturity, full distribution is achieved. Marginal competitors dropping out, holding market share and adding line extensions are all maturity features.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-039","concept":"c08-decline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sales of HomeFax's desktop fax machines are falling as businesses switch to e-signatures. Two rivals have exited the market, HomeFax has cut its line to its two best sellers, and it sells them at very low prices through fewer retailers with no advertising. Which PLC stage is this?","options":["Maturity","Decline","Growth","Introduction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sales falling because of a new technology, competitors exiting, a line trimmed to best sellers, very low prices, fewer retailers and no advertising are all decline entries in Figure 8-3. Maturity also involves price pressure, but there discounts are frequent while distribution stays full.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-040","concept":"c08-decline","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"Sales in the decline stage typically fall because of environmental change or new technology, such as the arrival of a better substitute.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Decline is usually triggered by outside change, such as streaming replacing DVDs. This differs from cannibalization, where the firm's own new product takes sales from its existing products.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-041","concept":"c08-decline","also":["c08-introduction"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Both the introduction and decline stages can show relatively few competitors and minimal profits. Which pair of cues tells them apart?","options":["Introduction: few competitors exist; decline: some competitors are leaving","Introduction: limited distribution; decline: full distribution","Introduction: very low prices; decline: skimming prices","Introduction: brand loyalty objective; decline: awareness objective"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Introduction has few competitors because the market is new; decline has reduced competition because rivals are leaving a shrinking market. The distribution option is wrong because decline has REDUCED distribution, not full distribution.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-042","concept":"c08-decline","also":["c08-plc-stage-table"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Skyline Audio's portable CD players are in the decline stage. Following Figure 8-3, what should its product strategy be?","options":["Introduce more features to differentiate from rivals","Ensure the full product line is available and innovative","Retain only the best sellers or discontinue the line","Focus on launching the product as a new brand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Figure 8-3's decline product entry is 'retain only best sellers or discontinue', matching the product rationalization objective. Ensuring a full, innovative line is the maturity entry, and introducing more features is the growth entry.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-043","concept":"c08-decline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which practice is NOT part of the decline stage in Figure 8-3?","options":["Very low prices on the remaining items","Distribution reduced to fewer retailers","Frequent price discounts to build brand loyalty","Only minimal promotion, if any at all"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Frequent price discounts aimed at brand loyalty are maturity tactics. In decline, prices are very low, distribution is reduced and promotion is minimal, if any, because the objective is product rationalization.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-044","concept":"c08-deletion-harvesting","also":["c08-decline"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Simon Watercraft expects, and starts to see, decreasing profits for its product line of water ski boats. In response, the company drastically reduces promotional activities to maximize the remaining profit from the remaining sales. This strategy is known as:","options":["line extension","radical innovation","harvesting","commercialization"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Harvesting means keeping the product but reducing marketing support to reap some minor profits during the decline stage, which is exactly what Simon does by slashing promotion. A line extension adds a new item to a line, the opposite of cutting support.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-045","concept":"c08-deletion-harvesting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dropping a product from the company's product line is called:","options":["deletion","harvesting","trading down","cannibalization"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Deletion removes the product from the line entirely. Harvesting is the tempting distractor, but harvesting KEEPS the product and simply cuts its marketing support.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-046","concept":"c08-deletion-harvesting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Grove Foods discontinues its canned lima beans and removes them from its catalogue so it can put those resources into its growing frozen-meal line. This decline-stage strategy is:","options":["harvesting","trading down","market modification","deletion"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Discontinuing a product and removing it from the line is deletion. Harvesting would keep the lima beans on sale with reduced marketing support to collect the remaining profit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-047","concept":"c08-deletion-harvesting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the key difference between harvesting and deletion?","options":["Harvesting keeps the product but cuts support; deletion drops it from the line","Harvesting drops the product; deletion keeps it with reduced support","Harvesting is used in the maturity stage; deletion is used in the growth stage","Harvesting adds new varieties to the line; deletion removes the old varieties"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both are decline-stage strategies: harvesting keeps the product and reduces marketing support to reap minor profits, while deletion removes it from the line. The option claiming harvesting drops the product simply reverses the two definitions, a common mix-up.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-048","concept":"c08-deletion-harvesting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why might a firm choose harvesting rather than deletion for a declining product?","options":["Harvesting revives sales growth by repositioning the product for new buyers","Some loyal buyers remain, so cutting costs still yields minor profits from remaining sales","Harvesting is a required step before a product can be test marketed again","Deletion is only allowed for products that never reached the maturity stage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Harvesting makes sense when enough buyers remain that a low-cost, low-support product still throws off some profit. It does not revive sales; reviving sales is the goal of PLC extension strategies such as repositioning.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-049","concept":"c08-deletion-harvesting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Brava Cosmetics is harvesting its old line of compact powder. Which action would be INCONSISTENT with a harvesting strategy?","options":["Cutting its advertising budget to almost nothing","Keeping the product available for loyal buyers","Reducing the number of retailers that carry it","Launching a national ad campaign to revive sales"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Harvesting means reducing marketing support, so a national campaign to revive sales contradicts it (that would be an attempt to extend the PLC). Cutting ads, keeping the product for loyal buyers and trimming distribution all fit harvesting.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-050","concept":"c08-high-learning","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"When the Swiffer Sweeper was first introduced it required consumers to understand the new product, how to use it, and what to do with their old brooms and mops. This is an example of a _____ product.","options":["learning","new concept","fad","high-learning"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A high-learning product has an extended introductory period because significant effort is required to educate consumers on its usage and benefits, exactly the Swiffer Sweeper situation. A fad is wrong because a fad is defined by a rapid rise and rapid fall, not by consumer education.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-051","concept":"c08-high-learning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A product that requires significant consumer education, and therefore has an extended introductory period, is a:","options":["high-learning product","low-learning product","fashion product","minor innovation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is the definition of a high-learning product. A low-learning product is the opposite: its benefits are self-evident, so the introduction is short and sales begin immediately.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-052","concept":"c08-high-learning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"When induction cooktops first appeared in Canadian stores, buyers had to learn that only magnetic pans work, how the touch controls respond and why the surface stays cool. Sales took years to build. In PLC terms, induction cooktops were a:","options":["fad","low-learning product","high-learning product","fashion product"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Needing to learn about new cookware, controls and benefits stretched out the introductory period, the mark of a high-learning product. A low-learning product would have sold immediately because its benefits are self-evident.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-053","concept":"c08-high-learning","also":["c08-degree-innovation"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"The Swiffer Sweeper is a classic high-learning product, yet Figure 8-5 lists the Swiffer WetJet as a continuous innovation. How can both classifications be valid?","options":["High-learning is a PLC shape (long introduction); continuous innovation rates how much routines change","They contradict each other, so the WetJet must really be classed as a radical innovation","High-learning and continuous innovation are simply two names for the same concept","High-learning refers to the firm's own production learning curve, not to consumers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The two frameworks answer different questions. High- vs low-learning describes the SHAPE of the PLC (how long introduction lasts), while minor/continuous/radical classifies the innovation by how much consumer learning it involves; changing routines without totally new learning is continuous. They are not synonyms.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-054","concept":"c08-high-learning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Vireo Mobility is launching a home exoskeleton that users must learn to put on, calibrate and trust before they will rely on it. What PLC pattern should Vireo plan for?","options":["Immediate sales with very little promotion needed","A rapid rise in sales followed by a rapid fall","Sales that rise, fade away and return in later cycles","An extended introductory period requiring heavy consumer education"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A product that requires significant consumer learning is a high-learning product, so Vireo should budget for a long introduction stage focused on education. Immediate sales with little promotion describes a low-learning product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-055","concept":"c08-low-learning","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dr. Oetker, a popular frozen pizza brand, regularly adds new pizza types and flavours to its existing product mix. Most new pizza products begin selling well immediately with very little promotion or communication about the product. In terms of a product life cycle, the lack of promotion is similar to a _____ product.","options":["high-learning","fashion","low-learning","concept"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A low-learning product has a short introductory stage because its benefits are self-evident and very little learning is required, so sales start right away with little promotion. High-learning is the opposite: an extended introduction because consumers must be educated.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-056","concept":"c08-low-learning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which description fits a low-learning product?","options":["Significant consumer education is required, so the introduction is extended","Benefits are self-evident, little learning is required, and sales begin immediately","Popularity rises, declines and reappears over the years","Sales rise rapidly and then fall just as rapidly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A low-learning product's benefits are obvious, so its introduction is short and sales start immediately. An extended introduction with heavy education describes a high-learning product, while cyclical returns and a rapid rise-and-fall describe fashions and fads.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-057","concept":"c08-low-learning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why is strong distribution especially important for a low-learning product?","options":["It is easily copied, so the firm must reach many buyers quickly before imitators do","Consumers need personal selling to understand how to use it","Its sales return in cycles, so stores must restock it every few years","Test marketing is required before it can be sold nationally"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because a low-learning product is simple to understand, it is also simple for competitors to copy, so the firm needs broad distribution fast to capture sales first. Personal selling to explain usage fits radical or high-learning products, not low-learning ones.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-058","concept":"c08-low-learning","also":["c08-high-learning"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pair correctly contrasts low-learning and high-learning products?","options":["Low-learning: an extended introduction; high-learning: a short introduction","Low-learning: a rapid rise and fall; high-learning: cyclical returns","Low: short introduction, immediate sales; high: extended introduction, heavy education","Low-learning: a radical innovation; high-learning: a minor innovation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Low-learning products sell immediately because their benefits are self-evident; high-learning products need an extended introduction to educate consumers. The option giving low-learning the extended introduction reverses them, and the others confuse PLC shapes with fads, fashions and degrees of innovation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-059","concept":"c08-low-learning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Pantry Pals is launching a new cookie flavour under its well-established brand. Which PLC pattern and priority should it plan for?","options":["A short introduction with immediate sales, so secure strong distribution quickly","A long introduction, so invest in personal selling to educate consumers","A cyclical pattern, so plan to relaunch the flavour in ten years","A slow start, so delay distribution until awareness builds"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A new flavour under a known brand is a low-learning product: buyers understand it instantly, so sales start right away, and because it is easy to copy, strong distribution matters. A long, education-heavy introduction would describe a high-learning product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-060","concept":"c08-fashion-fad","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"In 2017, fidget spinners went from unknown to sold in nearly every corner store within months, then almost vanished a year later. In PLC terms, fidget spinners were a:","options":["fashion product","fad","low-learning product","high-learning product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fad has a rapid rise and an equally rapid fall, giving it a very short life, exactly the fidget-spinner pattern. A fashion product would come back into style in later years, which fidget spinners did not.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-061","concept":"c08-fashion-fad","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Wide-leg jeans were popular in the 1970s, faded, returned in the late 1990s, faded again, and are popular once more. This PLC pattern describes a:","options":["fashion product","fad","high-learning product","low-learning product"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A fashion product is a style that becomes popular, declines and then returns in cycles over the years. A fad rises and falls quickly and does not keep coming back.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-062","concept":"c08-fashion-fad","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What distinguishes a fashion product from a fad?","options":["A fashion rises and falls rapidly; a fad keeps returning in cycles over the years","A fashion requires heavy consumer education; a fad requires almost none","A fashion is a radical innovation; a fad is usually just a minor innovation","A fashion returns in cycles over the years; a fad rises and falls fast with a very short life"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Fashions are cyclical (they return), while fads are brief (fast up, fast down). The option saying a fashion rises and falls rapidly swaps the two definitions, and the others confuse PLC shapes with learning or innovation categories.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-063","concept":"c08-fashion-fad","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A product with a rapid rise and an equally rapid fall in sales, giving it a very short life cycle, is called a(n) _____.","options":["fad","fashion","low-learning product","minor innovation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is a fad. A fashion also loses popularity, but it returns in cycles over the years instead of disappearing after one quick spike.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-064","concept":"c08-fashion-fad","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Blitz Toys expects its viral plush toy to be a fad. Which plan best fits that PLC shape?","options":["Invest in a long consumer-education campaign before sales take off","Plan to relaunch the toy every decade when the style returns","Produce cautiously and sell fast, avoiding big long-term inventory commitments","Harvest the toy for several years to milk a loyal customer base"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A fad's sales rise and fall rapidly, so the firm should move fast and avoid being stuck with stock when demand collapses. Planning periodic relaunches fits a fashion product, and a long education campaign fits a high-learning product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-065","concept":"c08-plc-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which list correctly names the four PLC extension strategies?","options":["Deletion, harvesting, repositioning, line extension","Market penetration, market development, product development, diversification","Modifying the product, modifying the market, repositioning the product, introducing a new product","Product improvement, line extension, trading up, trading down"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The four ways to extend a PLC are modifying the product, modifying the market, repositioning the product and introducing a new product. Market penetration, market development, product development and diversification form the strategic approaches to growth (Ansoff) matrix, and product improvement, line extension, trading up and trading down are sub-tactics, not the four strategies.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-066","concept":"c08-plc-extension","also":["c08-deletion-harvesting"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four strategies for extending a product's life cycle?","options":["Modifying the market","Harvesting the product","Repositioning the product","Introducing a new product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Harvesting is a decline-stage strategy that accepts falling sales and cuts support; it does not extend the PLC. Modifying the market, repositioning and introducing a new product are three of the four extension strategies (modifying the product is the fourth).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-067","concept":"c08-plc-extension","also":["c08-line-extension","c08-market-modification","c08-repositioning"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Brisk Bottling's mature iced-tea brand (1) adds a peach flavour, (2) promotes the tea to seniors, a group that never bought it, and (3) upgrades its ingredients and raises its price to position it as premium. Which extension strategies are these, in order?","options":["Modifying the product; modifying the market; repositioning","Modifying the market; modifying the product; repositioning","Repositioning; modifying the market; introducing a new product","Modifying the product; repositioning; modifying the market"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Adding a flavour is a line extension (modifying the product); reaching seniors is finding new customers (modifying the market); upgrading and raising the price is trading up (repositioning). Mixing up the flavour move (product) and the seniors move (market) is the most common error.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-068","concept":"c08-plc-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do marketers use PLC extension strategies?","options":["To reduce the array of new-product ideas to a manageable list","To test a product in a limited area before launch","To create primary demand for a brand-new product class","To keep a product from sliding into decline by finding new sources of sales"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Extension strategies stretch a product's life, usually in maturity, by changing the product, the market or the positioning, or by replacing it with a new product. Narrowing ideas and test marketing are NPD-process steps, not life-cycle extension.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-069","concept":"c08-plc-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"As sales of its fifth-generation tablet flatten, TechNorth launches a sixth-generation model designed to replace it and carry its customers forward. Which PLC extension strategy is this?","options":["Introducing a new product","Harvesting","Finding new customers","Trading down"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Replacing an ageing product with a new one is the fourth extension strategy, introducing a new product. Harvesting is tempting because the old model is fading, but harvesting keeps the old product with reduced support instead of replacing it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-070","concept":"c08-product-modification","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Altering a product's characteristics, such as its quality, performance or appearance, to increase sales is called:","options":["market modification","product modification","repositioning","market penetration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Changing the product itself (quality, performance, appearance) is product modification, which includes product improvements and line extensions. Market modification leaves the product unchanged and changes who uses it or how it's used.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-071","concept":"c08-product-modification","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"ClearView, a Canadian window-cleaner brand, reformulates its existing spray so it no longer streaks in freezing temperatures and relaunches it as 'new and improved'. This PLC extension tactic is:","options":["a line extension","creating a new use situation","a product improvement","trading down"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Upgrading the performance of the existing item is a product improvement, one form of product modification. It is not a line extension, because no new item was added to the line; the same spray was made better.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-072","concept":"c08-product-modification","also":["c08-line-extension"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Glacier Gum (a) reformulates its mint gum so the flavour lasts longer and (b) adds a new watermelon flavour to the line. Which is correct?","options":["(a) product improvement; (b) line extension; both are product modifications","(a) is a line extension and (b) is a product improvement","(a) is a market modification and (b) is a product modification","Both are brand extensions, because the brand name stays the same"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Improving an existing item is a product improvement; adding a new flavour to the line is a line extension. Both change the product, so both are product modifications. A brand extension would take the Glacier name into a new product class, which neither move does.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-073","concept":"c08-product-modification","also":["c08-market-modification"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a form of product modification?","options":["Improving a product's performance","Adding a new size to an existing line","Promoting an existing product to a new age group","Changing a product's appearance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Promoting the same product to a new age group is finding new customers, a market modification. Improving performance, changing appearance and adding a new size (a line extension) all modify the product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-074","concept":"c08-product-modification","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sales of Prairie Peak's mature ski wax are slipping because competitors' waxes perform better in very cold snow. Which PLC extension option directly addresses this problem?","options":["Creating a new use situation for the existing wax","Harvesting the wax to reap its remaining profit","Test marketing the existing wax in two cities","A product improvement that boosts cold-snow performance"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The problem is the product's performance, so a product improvement (product modification) fixes it directly. Creating a new use situation would change how the wax is used without closing the performance gap, and harvesting gives up on growth.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-075","concept":"c08-line-extension","also":["c08-low-learning"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kellogg's added two new products to its already broad and well-established brands of breakfast cereals. Because Kellogg's was widely recognized and known for high quality, these products required little or no learning by the customers and achieved sales exceeding 3 million units in the first quarter of their availability. Adding these new products to the range of existing ones is known as:","options":["test marketing","dumping","harvesting","line extension"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A line extension is the addition of a new item to an already existing product line, here two new cereals under well-known Kellogg's brands. Harvesting is the opposite move (cutting support for a declining product), and test marketing is a limited trial, not adding items to a line.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-076","concept":"c08-line-extension","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The addition of a new item, such as a flavour, size or variety, to an already existing product line is a(n) _____.","options":["line extension","brand extension","product improvement","diversification"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is a line extension. A brand extension is different: it moves an existing brand name into a NEW product class (Chapter 7) rather than adding an item within the same line.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-077","concept":"c08-line-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario is a line extension rather than a brand extension?","options":["A cereal brand launches a line of frozen pizzas under its name","A running-shoe brand launches branded energy drinks","Banff Tea Co. adds a chai variety to its bagged-tea line","A toothpaste brand launches branded razors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Adding a new variety within the same product line (bagged tea) is a line extension. The other three take an existing brand name into a new product class, which is a brand extension.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-078","concept":"c08-line-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do line extensions launched under a well-known brand often sell quickly?","options":["Consumers already know and trust the brand, so little learning is required","Line extensions are radical innovations that create primary demand","Line extensions skip the introduction stage because they are test marketed","Line extensions are priced using a harvesting strategy"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A trusted brand name carries over to the new item, so buyers understand and accept it immediately, as with Kellogg's new cereals. Line extensions are typically minor, low-learning additions, not radical innovations.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-079","concept":"c08-line-extension","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nordik Granola's best-selling granola is in maturity. Its manager wants a fast, relatively low-risk way to refresh the brand on the shelf while using the trust the brand has already earned. Which tactic fits best?","options":["Diversify into a new product for a new market","Delete the granola from the line","Run a controlled test market of the existing granola","Add new flavours to the existing line"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A line extension (new flavours in the existing line) is a classic maturity tactic that uses brand trust to refresh the line. Diversification is the riskiest growth path, and deletion abandons the product rather than refreshing it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-080","concept":"c08-line-extension","also":["c08-cannibalization"],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"The slide asking 'Why all the varieties? Good idea?' about Tide's many line extensions suggests that adding too many varieties can backfire by confusing consumers and cannibalizing the brand's existing items.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Line extensions keep a mature brand fresh, but too many varieties can confuse shoppers and pull sales from the brand's own existing items (cannibalization) rather than from competitors.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-081","concept":"c08-cannibalization","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When a firm's new product takes sales away from the firm's own existing products, this is called:","options":["diversification","harvesting","cannibalization","market penetration"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cannibalization means the new product 'eats' the firm's own sales. Market penetration is tempting, but it refers to growing a current product's sales among current customers, often at competitors' expense.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-082","concept":"c08-cannibalization","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After Lakeshore Dairy launched a premium lactose-free milk, its overall milk sales barely changed: most buyers had simply switched from Lakeshore's regular lactose-free milk. This is an example of:","options":["market development","cannibalization","the decline stage","a value barrier"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sales shifted from Lakeshore's existing product to its own new product, which is cannibalization. It is not the decline stage, because the drop was caused by the firm's own launch, not by outside technology or environmental change.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-083","concept":"c08-cannibalization","also":["c08-decline"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Sales of Oakridge's original sports drink fell 15% last year. Which explanation indicates cannibalization rather than the decline stage?","options":["Consumers switched to a new rival technology","The whole sports-drink category shrank","Oakridge cut advertising to harvest the product","Customers switched to Oakridge's own new zero-sugar version"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cannibalization is when the firm's OWN new product takes sales from its existing product. A shrinking category or a rival technology points to decline, and cutting advertising describes harvesting.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-084","concept":"c08-cannibalization","also":["c08-line-extension"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is cannibalization a particular concern when a brand adds many line extensions?","options":["New items in the line mostly draw buyers from the brand's existing items, not from rivals","Line extensions require radical new learning and consumption patterns by consumers","Line extensions always shrink the brand's distribution and shelf space","Line extensions automatically move the brand into a brand-new market"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because line extensions are close substitutes for the brand's existing items, many buyers simply switch within the brand, so total sales grow little. Line extensions are typically low-learning additions, not radical innovations.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-085","concept":"c08-cannibalization","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which outcome would NOT be described as cannibalization?","options":["A competitor's new cereal takes sales from a firm's cereal","A new flavour of a firm's yogurt pulls buyers from its original flavour","A firm's new tablet reduces sales of its own laptops","A firm's new mini size reduces sales of its regular size"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cannibalization only happens when a firm's own new product takes its own sales. Losing sales to a competitor's product is ordinary competition, not cannibalization.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-086","concept":"c08-market-modification","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Suzanne's Soup Company noticed that people only ate soup for lunch, so they added recipes to the back of the labels to encourage people to use their soups to make dinner too. This is an example of:","options":["product improvements","creating new use situations","finding new customers","line extensions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Creating a new use situation means finding a new occasion or use for an existing product, here dinner instead of only lunch. Product improvements is wrong because the soup itself did not change; only the occasion being promoted did.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-087","concept":"c08-market-modification","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Petal & Pine, maker of a gentle baby shampoo, launches ads suggesting that adults with sensitive scalps use the same shampoo. This market modification is:","options":["increasing a product's use","creating a new use situation","finding new customers","trading down"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The product and its use (washing hair) are unchanged; the firm is targeting a new group of buyers, adults, so this is finding new customers. It is not a new use situation, because the shampoo is still used for the same purpose.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-088","concept":"c08-market-modification","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Polar Fizz, a sparkling-water brand, runs a campaign encouraging its current drinkers to have three cans a day instead of one. This market modification is:","options":["finding new customers","increasing a product's use","creating a new use situation","a product improvement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Getting existing customers to use the product more often or in larger amounts is increasing a product's use. It is not a new use situation, because the drink is used the same way, just more frequently.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-089","concept":"c08-market-modification","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pairing correctly matches a market modification with its example?","options":["New use situation: promoting baking soda as a fridge deodorizer","Increasing use: marketing a women's razor to men","Finding new customers: label recipes encouraging soup at dinner","New use situation: asking current users to apply sunscreen twice as often"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Baking soda used to deodorize a fridge is a new purpose, so it is a new use situation. A razor marketed to men is finding new customers, soup recipes for dinner are a new use situation, and applying sunscreen more often is increasing use.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-090","concept":"c08-market-modification","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Market modification extends a product's life cycle by:","options":["improving the product's quality or adding line extensions to it","trading the product up or down to change the value it offers","finding new customers, increasing a product's use, or creating new use situations","deleting the product or harvesting it to reap remaining profits"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Market modification changes the market, not the product: new customers, more use, or new use situations. Improving the product or adding line extensions is product modification.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-091","concept":"c08-market-modification","also":["c08-line-extension"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a market modification?","options":["Finding new customers","Increasing a product's use","Creating a new use situation","Adding a new flavour to the line"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Adding a new flavour is a line extension, which is a product modification. The other three change who uses the product or how it is used, so they are market modifications.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-092","concept":"c08-market-modification","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Hearth Oats' rolled oats are bought almost only for breakfast porridge. Management wants growth without changing the product or targeting a new group of customers. Which tactic fits?","options":["Launch a gluten-free oat line (a line extension)","Promote oats as an ingredient in baking and smoothies (a new use situation)","Target university students who don't buy oats (finding new customers)","Upgrade quality and raise the price (trading up)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Promoting oats for baking and smoothies gives current customers a new occasion to use the unchanged product, which is creating a new use situation. Targeting students would be finding new customers, which management ruled out, and a gluten-free line changes the product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-093","concept":"c08-repositioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the slides, a firm repositions a product in order to:","options":["reduce a large list of new-product ideas to a manageable number","meet changing consumer needs, react to competition, or improve value","test the marketing mix on a limited scale in a defined area","reap minor profits while cutting the product's marketing support"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slides list three reasons to reposition: meet changing consumer needs, react to competition, or improve value. Reaping minor profits while cutting support is harvesting, a decline strategy rather than an extension strategy.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-094","concept":"c08-repositioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Fernwood Candles upgrades its existing basic candles with hand-poured soy wax, designer jars and essential oils, and raises their price by 60%. This is:","options":["trading up","trading down","a line extension","market penetration"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Adding value and features to the existing product and raising its price is trading up, a repositioning tactic. It is not a line extension, because Fernwood upgraded its existing candles rather than adding a separate new item to the line.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-095","concept":"c08-repositioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Without changing the price, Crispo Chips reduces its bag from 235 g to 200 g. In PLC repositioning terms, this is:","options":["trading up","a product improvement","harvesting","trading down"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Trading down includes reducing features, quality or price, and also downsizing package contents while keeping the price the same. It is not a product improvement, because consumers get less product rather than a better one.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-096","concept":"c08-repositioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the difference between trading up and trading down?","options":["Trading up lowers the price to gain volume; trading down raises the price to signal quality","Trading up targets new customers; trading down targets the firm's current customers","Up adds value or features and raises price; down cuts features, quality, price or package size","Trading up is a market modification; trading down is a product modification"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Trading up = more value for a higher price; trading down = less (fewer features, lower quality, a lower price, or smaller contents at the same price). Both are repositioning moves, not market or product modifications.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-097","concept":"c08-repositioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hinterland Outfitters repositions its rugged work boots as stylish urban footwear after noticing young city shoppers adopting the look. Which reason for repositioning does this best illustrate?","options":["Catching a rising trend to meet changing consumer needs","Recovering R&D costs through skimming","Reducing cannibalization of its line extensions","Preparing the boots for test marketing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Firms reposition to meet changing consumer needs, react to competition or improve value, which includes catching a rising trend like urban workwear. Skimming is an introduction-stage pricing strategy and has nothing to do with repositioning an established product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-098","concept":"c08-repositioning","also":["c08-line-extension"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Repositioning is one of the four strategies for extending a mature product's life cycle. Which of the following moves by a mature brand is NOT a repositioning move?","options":["A budget hotel chain upgrades its rooms and raises its rates","A cereal maker shrinks box contents but keeps the same price","A juice brand moves from a kids' image to an adult health image","A juice brand adds a new mango flavour to its line"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Adding a new flavour to the line is a line extension (product modification). Upgrading with higher rates is trading up, shrinking contents at the same price is trading down, and moving from a kids' to an adult image changes the product's position, so all three are repositioning.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-099","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","also":["c08-low-learning"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dr. Oetker, a popular frozen pizza brand, regularly adds new pizza types and flavours to its existing product mix. In terms of new product innovation, launching new pizza flavours is a/an:","options":["minor innovation","continuous improvement","radical innovation","mini updates"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Minor innovations are small product modifications that require no adjustment or new learning by consumers; nobody needs to be taught how to eat a new pizza flavour. 'Continuous improvement' sounds like the real term, but even a true continuous innovation would change the consumer's routine, which a new flavour does not.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-100","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"An innovation that changes the consumer's normal routine but does not require totally new learning is a:","options":["minor innovation","continuous innovation","radical innovation","low-learning product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is a continuous innovation, such as the Swiffer WetJet. A radical innovation goes further and requires new learning and new consumption patterns.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-101","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Driverless delivery pods will require Canadians to learn new ways of receiving packages and to adopt entirely new consumption patterns. In terms of degree of innovation, the pods are a:","options":["minor innovation","continuous innovation","line extension","radical innovation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Requiring new learning and new consumption patterns is the definition of a radical innovation, like self-driving cars in Figure 8-5. A continuous innovation changes routines but does not demand totally new learning.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-102","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lumio is launching a home 3D food printer, a radical innovation. According to Figure 8-5, its marketing emphasis should be to:","options":["gain consumer awareness and achieve wide distribution in many stores","advertise its points of difference and benefits versus competitors","educate consumers via advertising, product trial, personal selling and PR","use very low prices and keep promotion to a minimum, if any"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Radical innovations need consumer education through advertising, product trial and personal selling, and public relations can play a major role. Gaining awareness and wide distribution is the emphasis for minor innovations, which require no learning.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-103","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"SparkleBright is launching a new and improved version of its toothpaste. Per Figure 8-5, what should its marketing emphasize?","options":["Gaining consumer awareness and wide distribution","Educating consumers through personal selling and product trial","Public relations to explain new consumption patterns","Advertising that teaches consumers a new daily routine"],"answer":0,"explanation":"New and improved toothpaste is a minor innovation requiring no new learning, so the emphasis is awareness and wide distribution. Personal selling, product trial and PR-led education are for radical innovations.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-104","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pairing of innovation type and marketing emphasis is correct?","options":["Minor innovation: educate consumers through personal selling","Radical innovation: gain consumer awareness and wide distribution","Continuous innovation: use public relations to teach entirely new consumption patterns","Continuous innovation: advertise points of difference and benefits to consumers"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Figure 8-5 pairs continuous innovation with advertising points of difference and benefits. The other options scramble the table: personal selling and PR-led education belong to radical innovation, and awareness plus wide distribution belongs to minor innovation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-105","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which example is NOT listed as a radical innovation in Figure 8-5?","options":["Self-driving cars","Swiffer WetJet","3D printers","Amazon Alexa / Google Home"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Swiffer WetJet is Figure 8-5's example of a continuous innovation: it changes the cleaning routine without requiring totally new learning. Self-driving cars, 3D printers and smart speakers require new learning and consumption patterns, so they are radical.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-106","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"From the consumer's point of view, how new a product is depends on the degree of learning required to use it.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Newness can be judged from the company's view (new to the firm), the market's view (new to the market) or the consumer's view, where it depends on how much learning is required. That consumer view drives the minor, continuous and radical categories.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-107","concept":"c08-degree-innovation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"PulseBand's new wrist tracker has users checking sleep and step data each morning and charging the band nightly. It changes their routine, but anyone who has used a phone app can figure it out. In terms of degree of innovation, this is a:","options":["minor innovation","continuous innovation","radical innovation","fashion product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Wearable technology is Figure 8-5's example of a continuous innovation: it changes the consumer's normal routine without requiring totally new learning. It is more than minor (which requires no new learning) but less than radical (which requires new learning and consumption patterns).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-108","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"What percentage of all adopters are early adopters on the adoption curve?","options":["2.5%","13.5%","34%","16%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Early adopters are 13.5% of adopters. 16% is the tempting distractor: it is the share of laggards, and also the combined total of innovators (2.5%) plus early adopters (13.5%).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-109","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Priya, a popular Edmonton fitness instructor with slightly above-average education, buys the new FormFit smart mirror soon after launch, and many of her followers buy one after seeing her post about it. Priya is best described as:","options":["an innovator","a member of the early majority","an early adopter","a laggard"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Early adopters are leaders in social settings with slightly above-average education, and their adoption influences others. Innovators are the venturesome first 2.5% who use multiple information sources; this scenario stresses Priya's social influence, not risk-taking.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-110","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Gerald finally buys a smartphone in 2026, long after most Canadians, and only after his neighbours and friends convinced him. He avoids buying on credit because he fears debt. Gerald is a:","options":["late majority adopter","laggard","early majority adopter","innovator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Laggards (16%) fear debt and rely on neighbours and friends as information sources. The late majority is skeptical with below-average social status, but the fear of debt and reliance on neighbours point to laggards.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-111","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes innovators from early adopters?","options":["Innovators are leaders in social settings; early adopters are venturesome risk-takers","Innovators are 13.5% of adopters; early adopters are 2.5%","Innovators are skeptical; early adopters are deliberate","Innovators are venturesome and use multiple information sources; early adopters are leaders in social settings"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Innovators (2.5%) are venturesome, higher educated and use multiple information sources; early adopters (13.5%) are the leaders in social settings. Calling innovators the social leaders swaps the descriptors, which is the most common trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-112","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"What cumulative percentage of the market has adopted a new product once the early majority has finished adopting?","options":["34%","16%","50%","84%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Innovators 2.5% + early adopters 13.5% + early majority 34% = 50%. 84% would be the total after the late majority also adopts (50% + 34%).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-113","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pairing of adopter category and descriptor is NOT correct?","options":["Early majority: deliberate; many informal social contacts","Innovators: fear of debt; neighbours and friends are information sources","Late majority: skeptical; below average social status","Early adopters: leaders in social settings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fear of debt and relying on neighbours and friends describe laggards, not innovators. Innovators are venturesome, higher educated and use multiple information sources.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-114","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"On the adoption curve, which adopter category immediately follows the early majority?","options":["Late majority","Early adopters","Laggards","Innovators"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The order is innovators, early adopters, early majority, late majority, laggards. The late majority (34%, skeptical) follows the early majority (34%, deliberate); laggards come last.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-115","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why do marketers of a new product often focus on winning over early adopters to drive mass adoption?","options":["Early adopters make up the largest single group, at 34%","Early adopters are leaders in social settings whose adoption influences the larger majority","Early adopters fear debt, so they respond best to discounts","Early adopters are the most venturesome and use the most information sources"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Early adopters are the leaders in social settings, so their approval influences the deliberate early majority that follows. 'Most venturesome' describes innovators, and 34% describes each of the two majority groups.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-116","concept":"c08-adoption-curve","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The _____, who make up 34% of adopters, are described as skeptical and of below-average social status.","options":["early majority","late majority","laggards","early adopters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The late majority is skeptical and has below-average social status. The early majority is also 34%, but it is described as deliberate, with many informal social contacts.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-117","concept":"c08-adoption-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which list correctly names the four barriers that can deter consumers from adopting a new product?","options":["Price, place, promotion, product","Usage, value, risk, psychological","Awareness, interest, trial, adoption","Usage, price, distribution, timing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The four barriers are usage, value, risk and psychological. Price, place, promotion and product are the 4 Ps, and price, distribution and timing are not on the course's list of adoption barriers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-118","concept":"c08-adoption-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Many Calgary drivers like the idea of an electric pickup truck but worry it could leave them stranded on a highway at -30 degrees and cost a fortune if the battery fails. Which barrier to adoption is at work?","options":["Usage barrier","Value barrier","Risk barrier","Psychological barrier"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fear of physical danger (being stranded) and economic loss (a costly battery failure) is a risk barrier. It is not a usage barrier, because drivers aren't saying the truck clashes with how they drive; they fear what could go wrong.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-119","concept":"c08-adoption-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A new meal-replacement shake is affordable and as nutritious as a regular lunch, but many Canadians resist it because drinking lunch from a bottle clashes with their cultural traditions around sit-down, shared meals. Which barrier is this?","options":["Psychological barrier","Value barrier","Usage barrier","Risk barrier"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Resistance rooted in cultural differences or image concerns is a psychological barrier. It is not a value barrier: the shake is affordable and nutritious, so the issue isn't a lack of incentive but how it fits people's cultural traditions. Nor is it a risk barrier, since nobody fears physical harm or financial loss.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-120","concept":"c08-adoption-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Office workers agree that a new stand-up desk works well and fits easily into their workday, but they see no real advantage over their current desks, so they don't bother switching. Which barrier to adoption is at work?","options":["Usage barrier: the product is incompatible with existing habits","Value barrier: the product offers no incentive to change","Risk barrier: buyers fear physical harm","Psychological barrier: buyers worry about their image"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When a product works and fits in but offers no incentive to switch, it faces a value barrier. A usage barrier would mean the desk clashes with existing habits, which the scenario rules out.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-121","concept":"c08-adoption-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which is NOT one of the four barriers to adoption listed in the course?","options":["Usage","Value","Distribution","Psychological"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The four barriers are usage, value, risk and psychological. Distribution matters for a launch's success, but it is not one of the listed adoption barriers; risk is the missing fourth barrier.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-122","concept":"c08-product-failure","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"GlowBrew launched a coffee pod that customers said tasted 'just like every other pod', and it was pulled within a year. According to Figure 8-7, the main issue was:","options":["bad timing","insignificant point of difference","insufficient market attractiveness","incomplete new concept definition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A product that isn't meaningfully different from competitors fails because of an insignificant point of difference; the fix is a distinctive, meaningful POD found through consumer research and competitor monitoring. Bad timing would mean the product was fine but launched when consumers weren't eager to buy.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-123","concept":"c08-product-failure","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kinetic Kayaks' new folding kayak flopped: it reached only a few stores, its brand name was hard to remember, and its package didn't show what was inside. Which Figure 8-7 issue and solution apply?","options":["Bad timing: launch when consumers are eager to purchase","Insufficient market attractiveness: find a larger target market with growth potential","Poor execution of the marketing mix: align the 4 Ps (incl. name and package) and gain distribution","Insignificant point of difference: research consumers and monitor competitors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Weak distribution, a poor brand name and an unclear package are marketing-mix execution failures, fixed by aligning the 4 Ps and securing sufficient distribution. Nothing suggests the market itself was too small, so insufficient market attractiveness doesn't fit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-124","concept":"c08-product-failure","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Aurora Pools launched a premium backyard pool line in Yellowknife, but too few households there had the need, space or climate for it, and the market showed no growth. Which failure issue does this illustrate?","options":["Bad timing","Poor execution of the marketing mix","Insignificant point of difference","Insufficient market attractiveness"],"answer":3,"explanation":"When the target market lacks the need, size or growth potential, the issue is insufficient market attractiveness; the fix is to identify a target market with a need that is large enough and growing. Bad timing concerns when the product launched, not whether the market was big enough.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-125","concept":"c08-product-failure","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A product fails because its team never clearly defined its features, benefits and positioning. How does this differ from an 'insignificant point of difference' failure?","options":["Incomplete definition: features, benefits and positioning never set; insignificant POD: not meaningfully different","They are the same failure issue, simply described under two different names in the text","Incomplete concept definition is solved by finding a larger market; insignificant POD by better timing","Incomplete concept definition only affects services; insignificant POD only affects goods"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Incomplete new concept definition is fixed by identifying consumer insights, clearly defining features and benefits, and developing a clear positioning. An insignificant POD is fixed by finding a distinctive, meaningful point of difference; they are separate rows in Figure 8-7.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-126","concept":"c08-product-failure","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT listed in the course as a challenge of new product development?","options":["High research costs","Expensive product write-offs if the product fails","Lack of future credibility in the market","Too few sources of new-product ideas"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The listed challenges are an expensive undertaking, high risk of failure, high research costs, extensive time and effort on prototypes and marketing materials, expensive write-offs, and lost credibility. A shortage of ideas is not listed; idea generation draws on many sources.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-127","concept":"c08-product-failure","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the course call new products 'the lifeblood of a company' despite their high risk of failure?","options":["They guarantee higher profits than the company's mature products","They keep the company's products relevant and bring future revenues into the company","They eliminate the need for any PLC extension strategies later on","They are cheaper to develop than marketing the company's existing products"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slide quote says new products help make products relevant and bring future revenues into the company. They don't guarantee profits or come cheaply: NPD is expensive and carries a high risk of failure.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-128","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Well-established as a Canadian brand, Yummy Brand has identified similar market size, makeup and needs in Costa Rica. Taking its existing product line and updating it to align with the cultural preference in Costa Rica, Yummy introduces its product to several large cities in Costa Rica. The growth strategy followed by Yummy is:","options":["market penetration","market development","product development","diversification"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Market development introduces an existing product to a new market or target audience; adapting the existing line to local culture doesn't make it a new product. Diversification is the trap, but it requires BOTH a new product and a new market.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-129","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the strategic approaches to growth matrix, reaching current customers with a new product is called:","options":["market penetration","market development","product development","diversification"],"answer":2,"explanation":"New product + current market = product development. Market development is the mirror image: a current product taken to new customers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-130","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rocky Ridge Coffee launches a punch card offering a free drink after nine purchases, aiming to get its existing customers to buy its existing drinks more often. Which growth strategy is this?","options":["Market development","Diversification","Product development","Market penetration"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Current product + current customers = market penetration: finding ways to make current products appeal more to current customers. No new product or new market is involved, so neither development strategy applies.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-131","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakehead Outfitters, known for canoes sold to Ontario cottage owners, launches a line of paddleboards and sells them to the same cottage owners through its existing stores. Which growth strategy is this?","options":["Product development","Market development","Market penetration","Diversification"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A new product (paddleboards) offered to current customers (cottage owners) is product development. Diversification would also require a new market, but the customers here are the same.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-132","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Credit Union, a Saskatchewan financial co-operative, opens a chain of pet-grooming salons aimed at urban dog owners in Ontario. Which growth strategy is this?","options":["Market development","Product development","Market penetration","Diversification"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pet grooming is a new product for the credit union, and Ontario dog owners are a new market, so this is diversification. Market development would mean taking its existing financial products to the new market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-133","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What distinguishes market development from product development?","options":["Market development creates a new product for new customers; product development sells more to current customers","Market development takes a current product to new customers; product development offers a new product to current customers","Market development is used only internationally; product development only domestically","They are the same strategy described from different viewpoints"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The word before 'development' names what is new: market development = new market, current product; product development = new product, current market. Market development is not limited to other countries; a new domestic segment also counts.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-134","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement about the strategic approaches to growth is NOT correct?","options":["Market penetration means finding ways to make current products appeal to current customers","Market development means reaching new customers with a current product","Diversification means reaching current customers with a new product","Product development means reaching current customers with a new product"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Diversification means reaching NEW customers with a new product; reaching current customers with a new product is product development. The other three statements match Figure 8-8 exactly.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-135","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Pemmican Provisions has saturated its home market in Manitoba with its jerky. Management wants to grow without developing any new products. Which strategy should it consider?","options":["Market development: sell the same jerky in new markets such as B.C. or the U.S.","Product development: launch new snack bars for Manitoba customers","Diversification: enter a new industry in a new region","Product rationalization: retain only the best sellers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With the home market saturated and no new products allowed, the remaining growth path is taking the current product to new markets, which is market development. Product development and diversification both require new products, which management ruled out.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-136","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why is diversification usually seen as the riskiest of the four strategic approaches to growth?","options":["It relies only on current customers, who may grow tired of the firm's products","Both the product and the market are new to the firm, so it has experience with neither","It requires cutting prices on the firm's current products","It cannot be combined with test marketing or a regional rollout"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Diversification pairs a new product with a new market, so the firm can't lean on existing product know-how or customer knowledge. Relying on current customers describes market penetration or product development, not diversification.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-137","concept":"c08-ansoff","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Taking an existing product line into a new country, even after adapting it to local cultural preferences, is still market development rather than diversification.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Adapting an existing line to local tastes doesn't make it a new product, so the move is a current product in a new market: market development. Diversification requires both a new product and a new market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-138","concept":"c08-npd-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the correct order of the first four steps of the NPD process?","options":["Idea generation, new product development strategy, business analysis, screening and evaluation","New product development strategy, idea generation, screening and evaluation, business analysis","Idea generation, screening and evaluation, development, business analysis","New product development strategy, screening and evaluation, idea generation, development"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The process starts with strategy, then idea generation, screening and evaluation, and business analysis (followed by development, test marketing and commercialization). Starting with idea generation is a common slip; the firm first sets a strategic focus so it knows what kinds of ideas to look for.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-139","concept":"c08-npd-process","also":["c08-business-analysis"],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which step comes immediately before development in the NPD process?","options":["Screening and evaluation","Test marketing","Business analysis","Idea generation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Business analysis (step 4) comes right before development (step 5): the firm projects features, marketing strategy and finances before paying to build prototypes. Screening and evaluation is step 3, two steps earlier.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-140","concept":"c08-npd-process","also":["c08-test-marketing"],"type":"fill","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Step 6 of the seven-step NPD process is _____.","options":["commercialization","test marketing","development","business analysis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Test marketing is step 6, between development (step 5) and commercialization (step 7). Commercialization is the final step, the full launch.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-141","concept":"c08-npd-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the NPD process screen out weak ideas before business analysis and development?","options":["Screening is legally required before a patent can be filed","Development must begin before any financial projections are made","Consumers refuse to test more than one prototype","Later steps are far more expensive, so eliminating poor ideas early saves money"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The process is a funnel: early steps like screening and concept testing are cheap, while prototypes, test markets and launches are costly, so weak ideas are dropped as early as possible. Development does not come before financial projections; business analysis precedes it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-142","concept":"c08-npd-process","also":["c08-ansoff"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the seven steps of the NPD process?","options":["Screening and evaluation","Market penetration","Business analysis","Commercialization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Market penetration is a strategic approach to growth (Ansoff matrix), not an NPD step. Screening and evaluation, business analysis and commercialization are steps 3, 4 and 7.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-143","concept":"c08-npd-process","also":["c08-business-analysis"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Aspen Audio has narrowed 200 ideas to three promising earbud concepts and is now projecting each concept's costs, profitability and legal issues. Which step is it in, and what comes next?","options":["Screening and evaluation; next is business analysis","Development; next is test marketing","Business analysis; next is development","Business analysis; next is test marketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Projecting costs, profitability and legal issues is business analysis (step 4), and development (step 5, building prototypes) comes next. 'Business analysis; next is test marketing' names the right step but skips development; test marketing happens only after prototypes exist.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-144","concept":"c08-npd-process","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the NPD process, business analysis comes after development, once a prototype exists that can be costed out.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Business analysis is step 4 and development is step 5, so the firm makes financial projections BEFORE building prototypes. Corrected statement: business analysis comes before development.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-145","concept":"c08-npd-strategy-ideas","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The first step of the NPD process, new product development strategy, aims to:","options":["brainstorm as many new-product ideas as possible from many sources","identify a new-product development focus that meets company objectives","make financial projections for each promising concept","create prototypes and test them internally and with consumers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Step 1 sets the focus: what kinds of new products fit the company's objectives, using company objectives and a SWOT analysis. Brainstorming comes next, in idea generation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-146","concept":"c08-npd-strategy-ideas","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cascade Cosmetics reviews its corporate objectives and conducts a SWOT analysis of the company and its brands to decide what kinds of new products to pursue. Which NPD step is this?","options":["Idea generation","Business analysis","New product development strategy","Screening and evaluation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Using company objectives and a SWOT analysis to set the new-product focus is the new product development strategy step. Business analysis is tempting because it is also analytical, but it evaluates a specific concept's features, marketing mix and finances.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-147","concept":"c08-npd-strategy-ideas","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tundra Tools takes apart a competitor's new cordless drill to see how it works and collects suggestions from its suppliers and front-line staff. These activities belong to which NPD step?","options":["Idea generation","Development","Screening and evaluation","Test marketing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reverse engineering competitors' products and gathering suggestions from suppliers and employees are sources of new ideas, so this is idea generation. Development involves building and testing Tundra's own prototypes, not studying a rival's product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-148","concept":"c08-npd-strategy-ideas","also":["c08-screening"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What distinguishes idea generation from screening and evaluation?","options":["Idea generation uses concept tests; screening and evaluation uses brainstorming","Idea generation makes financial projections; screening and evaluation builds prototypes","Idea generation is step 3; screening and evaluation is step 2","Idea generation brainstorms many ideas; screening and evaluation narrows them to a manageable list"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Idea generation (step 2) widens the funnel by brainstorming; screening and evaluation (step 3) narrows it, using internal evaluation and external concept tests. Saying idea generation uses concept tests reverses their tools.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-149","concept":"c08-npd-strategy-ideas","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a typical source in the idea generation step?","options":["Employees and co-workers","Financial projections from the business analysis step","Consumers, including through crowdsourcing","Competitors, through reverse engineering"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Financial projections come later, in business analysis (step 4), and evaluate concepts rather than generate them. Employees, co-workers, consumers, crowdsourcing and competitors are all idea sources.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-150","concept":"c08-screening","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"The stage of a new product development process called _____ attempts to reduce the array of brainstorming ideas down to a manageable list of promising concepts.","options":["screening and evaluation","idea generation","strategy","development"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The third stage, screening and evaluation, reduces the array of brainstorming ideas to a manageable list of promising concepts. Idea generation is the step before it, which creates that array of ideas in the first place.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-151","concept":"c08-screening","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before building anything, Polaris Pet Co. shows 400 dog owners written descriptions and sketches of five new toy ideas and asks how likely they would be to buy each. This is a:","options":["test market","concept test","prototype test","business analysis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Showing consumers descriptions or sketches of an idea (not an actual product) is a concept test, part of screening and evaluation. A prototype test needs a working model, which comes later in development.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-152","concept":"c08-screening","also":["c08-development"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How does a concept test differ from the consumer testing done in the development step?","options":["Concept tests sell the product in a limited area; development tests use descriptions","Concept tests are internal only; development tests are external only","Concept tests show a description of the idea; development tests let consumers try a prototype","Concept tests make financial projections; development tests check legal issues"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A concept test (screening) presents a description of the idea, while development tests consumers' reactions to a working prototype. Selling in a limited area is test marketing, a later step.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-153","concept":"c08-screening","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the main purpose of the screening and evaluation step?","options":["To eliminate weak ideas, leaving a manageable list of promising concepts","To generate as many new-product ideas as possible from many sources","To set the company's overall new-product focus using a SWOT analysis","To launch the product with full-scale production and marketing support"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Screening and evaluation weeds out weak ideas cheaply, before money goes into business analysis and prototypes. Generating ideas is the previous step, and setting the focus is step 1.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-154","concept":"c08-screening","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which activity does NOT belong in the screening and evaluation step?","options":["Internal evaluation of technical requirements","External concept tests with consumers","Selling finished units in two test cities","Dropping ideas with weak consumer interest"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Selling finished units in defined cities is test marketing (step 6). Screening and evaluation uses internal technical evaluation and external concept tests to drop weak ideas.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-155","concept":"c08-screening","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Screening and evaluation is step _____ of the seven-step NPD process.","options":["two","three","four","five"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Screening and evaluation is step 3, after strategy (1) and idea generation (2) and before business analysis (4). Step 4 is the tempting distractor because both steps evaluate concepts.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-156","concept":"c08-business-analysis","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Specifying a new product's features and marketing strategy and making financial projections happens in which NPD step?","options":["Screening and evaluation","Development","Commercialization","Business analysis"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Business analysis (step 4) identifies the product's key features and marketing strategy and makes financial projections, including production, legal and profitability issues. Screening and evaluation comes earlier and narrows ideas using concept tests.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-157","concept":"c08-business-analysis","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before any prototypes exist, Fjord Foods' team estimates production costs, checks whether its new kelp snack meets food-labelling laws, and projects five-year profits. This is:","options":["development","test marketing","business analysis","screening and evaluation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Assessing production, legal and profitability issues on paper is business analysis. Development would involve building and testing the actual snack prototype.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-158","concept":"c08-business-analysis","also":["c08-development"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which comparison of business analysis and development is accurate?","options":["Business analysis builds prototypes; development makes financial projections","Business analysis follows development so that the prototype can be costed","Both steps involve selling the product on a limited basis in test areas","Business analysis does the paper projections; development builds and tests prototypes"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Business analysis (step 4) is the paper-and-spreadsheet check; development (step 5) turns the idea into a working prototype and tests it. The 'follows development' option reverses their order.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-159","concept":"c08-business-analysis","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT assessed during business analysis?","options":["Production costs and feasibility","Legal and patent issues","Repeat purchase rates in test cities","Projected profitability"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Repeat purchase rates in test cities come from test marketing (step 6), long after business analysis. Business analysis assesses production, legal and profitability issues plus the planned features and marketing mix.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-160","concept":"c08-business-analysis","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Business analysis is done before development so that money isn't spent building prototypes for products that won't be profitable.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Prototypes are expensive, so the firm first checks features, marketing strategy, production, legal and profitability issues. Only concepts that make business sense move on to development.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-161","concept":"c08-development","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the NPD process, the step that turns the idea on paper into a prototype and tests it internally and with consumers is:","options":["development","business analysis","commercialization","idea generation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Development (step 5) creates the prototype and tests it internally (company assessments) and externally (consumer tests on prototypes). Business analysis comes just before it and works on paper, not with physical models.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-162","concept":"c08-development","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Orca Outdoor builds 30 working models of its new insulated water bottle, drop-tests them in its lab, and gives them to hikers for a month of real use. This is the _____ step.","options":["test marketing","screening and evaluation","commercialization","development"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Building working models and testing them in the lab and with consumers is the development step. It is not test marketing, because the bottles are lent out for testing, not sold in a defined market area.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-163","concept":"c08-development","also":["c08-test-marketing"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A student says: 'Development is when we offer the finished product for sale in a couple of cities.' What is the correct contrast?","options":["That is commercialization; development is the concept-testing step","That is test marketing; development creates and tests prototypes","That is business analysis; development is really idea generation","The student is correct; that is exactly what development means"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Selling the finished product on a limited basis in defined areas is test marketing (step 6). Development (step 5) is about creating and testing prototypes; concept tests belong to screening and evaluation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-164","concept":"c08-development","also":["c08-ansoff"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"The word 'development' appears in both the NPD process and the strategic approaches to growth. Which statement is NOT correct?","options":["In the NPD process, development means building and testing prototypes","Product development in the growth matrix means reaching current customers with a new product","In the NPD process, development means taking a current product to a new market","Market development in the growth matrix means reaching new customers with a current product"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Taking a current product to a new market is market development, a growth strategy, not the NPD development step. In the NPD process, development means turning the idea into a prototype and testing it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-165","concept":"c08-development","also":["c08-npd-process"],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which steps come immediately before and immediately after development in the NPD process?","options":["Screening and evaluation before; business analysis after","Test marketing before; commercialization after","Business analysis before; test marketing after","Idea generation before; screening and evaluation after"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The sequence is business analysis (4), development (5), test marketing (6). Putting business analysis after development is the most common ordering mistake.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-166","concept":"c08-test-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Offering a product for sale on a limited basis in a defined area to gauge consumer reactions and test the marketing strategy is:","options":["commercialization","test marketing","a concept test","a regional rollout"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is test marketing (step 6). A regional rollout is tempting, but it is a full launch introduced area by area during commercialization, not a limited trial to gather information.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-167","concept":"c08-test-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bluenose Beverages pays a group of Halifax grocers to stock and display its new energy drink for eight weeks so it can track sales. Which type of test market is this?","options":["Standard test market","Simulated test market","Controlled test market","Concept test"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Paying retailers to stock and display the product is a controlled test market. A standard test market sells the product through regular channels in actual cities without paying stores to carry it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-168","concept":"c08-test-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why might a manufacturer decide to skip test marketing for a new line of washing machines?","options":["Washing machines are low-learning products that need no real launch","Test marketing is expensive, alerts competitors, and is impractical for durable goods","Test marketing is only permitted for services, not for physical goods","Washing machines must be commercialized before business analysis is done"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Test marketing is not always necessary: it is costly, it tips off competitors, and it isn't practical for durable goods like appliances. That is why the step is done only 'if necessary'.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-169","concept":"c08-test-marketing","also":["c08-commercialization"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a type of test market?","options":["Standard test market","Controlled test market","Simulated (laboratory) test market","Regional rollout"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A regional rollout is part of commercialization: a full launch introduced sequentially into geographic areas. Standard, controlled and simulated test markets are the three types of test market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-170","concept":"c08-test-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before deciding whether to go national, Cedar Snacks sells its new maple popcorn through normal grocery channels in Sudbury and Kamloops for six months and tracks repeat purchases. Which type of test market is this?","options":["Standard test market","Controlled test market","Simulated test market","Regional rollout"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Selling through regular channels in actual cities to gauge reactions is a standard test market. It isn't a regional rollout, because Cedar hasn't committed to a full launch; it is still gathering information to decide.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-171","concept":"c08-test-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Shoppers recruited to a research facility watch a set of TV ads, including one for Lumen's new shampoo, then shop in a mock store set up to look like a real drugstore. Which type of test market is this?","options":["Standard test market","Controlled test market","Simulated test market","Concept test"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Exposing consumers to ads and then letting them shop in a simulated store is a simulated (laboratory) test market. A concept test would show only a description of the idea, not a finished product on a shelf.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-172","concept":"c08-commercialization","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ is the step when the new product is brought to market with full-scale production, sales, and marketing support.","options":["Concept test","Test marketing","Commercialization","Business analysis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Commercialization is the final NPD step: the product is brought to market with full-scale production, sales and marketing support. Test marketing is the step before it and involves only limited sales in defined areas.","source":"official"},{"id":"q08-173","concept":"c08-commercialization","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After strong test results, Muskoka Mints ramps up to full production, ships to retailers across Canada and launches a national TV and social media campaign. This is:","options":["test marketing","commercialization","development","business analysis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Full-scale production with all areas of the marketing mix implemented nationally is commercialization. Test marketing has already happened; it was the limited trial that produced the 'strong test results'.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-174","concept":"c08-commercialization","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Wildrose Kombucha launches in Alberta in March, Saskatchewan and Manitoba in June, and B.C. in September so production and marketing can build up gradually. This approach is:","options":["a standard test market","a controlled test market","a regional rollout","harvesting"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Introducing a product sequentially into geographic areas so production and marketing can build is a regional rollout, part of commercialization. It is not a standard test market, because each region receives a full launch rather than a limited trial.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-175","concept":"c08-commercialization","also":["c08-test-marketing"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does a regional rollout differ from test marketing?","options":["A regional rollout is a limited trial; test marketing is a full-scale launch","A rollout is a full launch done area by area; a test market is a limited trial before deciding","A regional rollout happens during development; test marketing during business analysis","They are the same thing, simply described in different words"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A regional rollout is a sequenced full-scale launch (commercialization), while test marketing is a limited sale used to gauge consumer reactions and test the marketing strategy. Calling the rollout a limited trial reverses the two.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q08-176","concept":"c08-commercialization","also":["c08-introduction"],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Once commercialization begins, the new product enters which stage of the product life cycle?","options":["Introduction","Growth","Maturity","Development"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Commercialization is the full launch, which is where the PLC begins with the introduction stage. 'Development' is an NPD step, not a PLC stage, and it happens before commercialization.","source":"generated"}],"color":"#6FA0B8","short":"New products","emoji":"⚔️","tag":"Life cycles and launches","province":"Britannia","numeral":"VIII"},{"chapter":9,"title":"Pricing","topics":[{"id":"t09-1","order":1,"title":"Price, value and the 3 Cs","summary":"Price is whatever a buyer gives up - money or other considerations - to own or use a product, and the price equation shows how a list price becomes the final price. Price also signals quality and value, so it both earns revenue and teaches the firm about its market. Consumer psychology (reference prices, odd/even endings) and the 3 Cs - customers, costs and competition - frame every pricing decision.","concepts":["c09-price","c09-value-pricing","c09-price-importance","c09-consumer-psychology","c09-three-cs"]},{"id":"t09-2","order":2,"title":"Pricing objectives and constraints","summary":"Before picking a number, a firm decides what price must accomplish (objectives such as profit, market share or survival) and recognizes what limits it (constraints such as demand, product newness, costs, the type of competitive market and competitors' prices). Objectives are goals; constraints are boundaries.","concepts":["c09-pricing-objectives","c09-pricing-constraints"]},{"id":"t09-3","order":3,"title":"Demand, revenue, costs and break-even","summary":"A demand curve shows how many units sell at each price, and price elasticity tells you whether a price change will raise or lower total revenue. Costs are fixed or variable; combining revenue and cost gives the profit equation and the break-even point - the quantity where total revenue equals total cost. Expect calculation questions here.","concepts":["c09-demand-estimation","c09-elasticity","c09-revenue-profit","c09-cost-concepts","c09-break-even"]},{"id":"t09-4","order":4,"title":"Step 1: the four approach buckets and demand-oriented pricing","summary":"Step 1 of setting a final price is choosing an approximate price level using one of four approach buckets: demand-, cost-, profit- or competition-oriented (Figure 9-2 - memorize it). Demand-oriented approaches start from what customers want and will pay: skimming, penetration, prestige, odd-even (covered with consumer psychology), target, bundle and yield management.","concepts":["c09-four-approaches","c09-skimming","c09-penetration","c09-prestige","c09-target-pricing","c09-bundle","c09-yield-management"]},{"id":"t09-5","order":5,"title":"Step 1 continued: cost-, profit- and competition-oriented pricing","summary":"Cost-oriented approaches add a markup to cost (standard markup, cost-plus) - know markup on price vs on cost. Profit-oriented approaches solve the profit equation for the price that hits a dollar profit or a percentage return. Competition-oriented approaches benchmark against rivals (customary; above-, at- or below-market; loss leader). Cost-based and value-based pricing run in opposite sequences.","concepts":["c09-markup","c09-cost-plus","c09-target-profit","c09-target-return","c09-customary-market","c09-loss-leader","c09-cost-vs-value"]},{"id":"t09-6","order":6,"title":"Steps 2-4 and the legal limits on pricing","summary":"After choosing an approximate level, the firm sets a list price (one-price or flexible-price policy), makes special adjustments (discounts, allowances, geographic adjustments) and then monitors and adjusts. All of it must respect Canada's Competition Act: no price fixing, illegal price discrimination, deceptive pricing or predatory pricing.","concepts":["c09-final-price-steps","c09-one-flexible","c09-discounts-allowances","c09-geographic","c09-legal"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"c09-price","topic":"t09-1","term":"Price, barter and the price equation","definition":"Price is the money or other considerations, including other goods and services, exchanged for the ownership or use of a product. Final price = List price - Incentives and allowances + Extra fees; barter is exchanging goods or services for other goods or services instead of money.","explain":"Price is not just a sticker number - it is everything the buyer gives up, and it goes by many names (tuition, rent, fare, fee, interest, premium). The price equation turns a list price into what the buyer actually pays: subtract discounts and allowances such as a trade-in, then add extra fees such as financing charges. Barter is the oldest form of price: goods or services are swapped directly with no money changing hands.","example":"A Calgary property manager buys dishwashers listed at $800, gets a $200 discount and a $20 trade-in credit, and pays $30 for financing: $800 - ($200 + $20) + $30 = $610 per unit.","contrast":"Barter (goods or services for goods or services, no money) vs a trade-in allowance (the old item reduces a money price, but money still changes hands). In the price equation, incentives and allowances are SUBTRACTED and extra fees (financing, surcharges, late-payment penalties) are ADDED.","trap":"Calc questions bury the fee: students subtract the financing charge or forget to subtract the trade-in. Group all reductions in brackets, subtract once, then add the fees. 'Other considerations, including other goods and services' is the definition cue for price - not just money.","mnemonic":"List, Less, Plus: list price, less incentives, plus fees.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"The price equation","items":["Final price = List price - Incentives and allowances + Extra fees","Subtract: discounts (quantity, seasonal, trade, cash), coupons, trade-in allowances","Add: financing charges, surcharges, penalties for late payment","Barter: goods or services exchanged for goods or services, with no money"]}},{"id":"c09-value-pricing","topic":"t09-1","term":"Value and value pricing","definition":"Value is the ratio of perceived benefits to price (Value = Perceived benefits / Price). Value pricing is increasing product or service benefits while maintaining or decreasing price.","explain":"Customers judge a price against what they get. Raise benefits or cut price and perceived value rises; raise price without adding benefits and value falls. For some products price also shapes perceived quality, so a very low price can lower perceived value (a cheap diamond seems fake).","example":"A Canadian coffee chain adds a free extra shot and a bigger cup while holding its latte at $4.50 - more benefits, same price, so value rises.","contrast":"Value pricing (more benefits, same or lower price) vs penetration pricing (a low INITIAL price on a new product to win the mass market) vs a plain discount (lower price, same benefits). Value pricing is about the benefits-to-price ratio, not just a low price.","trap":"Watch the formula direction: value = benefits / price, so a price INCREASE with unchanged benefits LOWERS value. In the diamond question (higher price = higher perceived quality), the blank is 'value' - not 'volume' or 'acceptable cost'.","mnemonic":"Value = Benefits over Price: raise the top or cut the bottom.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-price-importance","topic":"t09-1","term":"Importance of price: dual role and the quality signal","definition":"Pricing has a dual purpose or role: (1) generating revenue and (2) learning about the marketplace - for example, the quality heuristic, where consumers use price to judge quality. The other three marketing-mix elements go a long way to create value; pricing is the attempt to capture that value.","explain":"Price is the only marketing-mix element that brings money in; product, place and promotion create value and cost money. Price also teaches the firm about its market: buyers often read a high price as a sign of high quality. Because pricing is the 'moment of truth' - where the focus of every transaction eventually comes to - a poor pricing strategy can make an otherwise reasonable marketing strategy financially ineffective.","example":"An Edmonton craft brewery raises its barrel-aged stout from $14 to $19 and sales climb - buyers took the higher price as a sign of a better beer (the quality heuristic).","contrast":"Price as a quality signal (a heuristic consumers use to judge quality) vs prestige pricing (a deliberate demand-oriented approach of keeping price high for status-conscious buyers). The heuristic is the consumer's shortcut; prestige pricing is a strategy that exploits it.","trap":"The two roles are generating revenue and learning about the marketplace - not 'creating value' (that is the other three Ps) and not 'covering costs' alone. 'Moment of truth' = pricing is where the transaction's focus ends up, not the first decision a firm makes.","mnemonic":"Price earns and learns.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-consumer-psychology","topic":"t09-1","term":"Consumer psychology: reference prices and odd-even pricing","definition":"Reference prices are the prices consumers use to compare against a purchase price (a remembered, 'regular' or competitor price). Odd-even pricing sets prices a few dollars or cents under an even number ($499.99) to suggest a bargain, while even prices ($500) suggest quality; it is one of the demand-oriented approaches.","explain":"Pricing changes behaviour, so first ask what you want consumers to do. Buyers rarely judge a price in isolation - they compare it with a reference price, which is why 'Regular $80, now $59' works. Price endings matter too: odd endings read as a deal, round even numbers read as premium.","example":"A hardware chain tags a drill at $99.99 (odd = bargain feel) while a Banff luxury spa lists its signature treatment at an even $250 (quality feel).","contrast":"Odd-even pricing (small price endings that shape bargain vs quality perceptions) vs prestige pricing (the overall price LEVEL is set high so status-conscious buyers will buy; lowering it can reduce demand). A reference price is a comparison point in the buyer's mind, not a pricing approach.","trap":"Odd endings signal BARGAIN; even endings signal QUALITY - questions flip these. Odd-even sits in the DEMAND-oriented bucket, not the competition-oriented one, because it works on customer perceptions.","mnemonic":"Odd = 'oh, a deal'; Even = elegant.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-three-cs","topic":"t09-1","term":"The 3 Cs of pricing","definition":"The three key considerations when studying pricing: (1) Customers, (2) Costs and (3) Competition. 'Your price' sits among your costs, competitors' prices and what customers want to pay.","explain":"Costs tell you the lowest price you can sustain in the long run; what customers are willing to pay tells you how high you can go; competitors' prices show where you sit relative to alternatives. A price built from only one C - say, cost plus a margin - can land above what customers will pay or far from the competition.","example":"A Calgary window-cleaning start-up checks its labour and supply cost per house, surveys homeowners on what they would pay, and compares two rivals' quotes before settling on $249 per home.","contrast":"The 3 Cs (customers, costs, competition - inputs to the price decision) vs the four approach buckets (demand-, cost-, profit-, competition-oriented - methods for choosing an approximate price level). The 3 Cs do NOT include channels, company or communication.","trap":"Distractors swap in 'channels', 'company' or 'communication' from other C-lists. It is Customers, Costs, Competition only.","mnemonic":"Customers' ceiling, Costs' floor, Competition in between.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"The 3 Cs of pricing","items":["Customers - what the customer wants (is willing) to pay","Costs - your costs to produce and market the product","Competition - competitors' prices"]}},{"id":"c09-pricing-objectives","topic":"t09-2","term":"Pricing objectives","definition":"Pricing objectives specify the role of price in an organization's marketing and strategic plans: profit (managing for long-run profit, current profit or a target return), sales, market share, volume, survival and social responsibility.","explain":"Objectives answer 'what should our price achieve?' A firm chasing market share prices differently from one trying to survive a downturn or one pricing medicine affordably out of social responsibility. Market share is the ratio of a firm's sales revenue to total industry sales; volume objectives focus on units, for example keeping a plant running at capacity.","example":"During a slump, an Alberta RV dealer prices units just high enough to cover costs and keep the doors open - a survival objective.","contrast":"Objectives (goals price must achieve: profit, sales, market share, volume, survival, social responsibility) vs constraints (factors that LIMIT the range of prices: demand, newness, costs, competitive market, competitors' prices). Target return is a profit OBJECTIVE; target return-on-investment pricing is the matching profit-oriented APPROACH.","trap":"'Competitors' prices' and 'cost of producing' are constraints, not objectives. Market share is measured by revenue relative to total industry sales; volume is about units. Survival = covering costs to stay in business in the short run.","mnemonic":"Profit, Sales, Share, Volume, Survival, Social: 'People Sell Shares Very Slowly Sometimes'.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Pricing objectives","items":["Profit - managing for long-run profit, current profit or a target return","Sales - growth in dollar or unit sales","Market share - firm's sales revenue / total industry sales","Volume - unit volume, e.g., keeping production at capacity","Survival - covering costs to stay in business in the short run","Social responsibility - obligations to customers and society (e.g., affordable medicine)"]}},{"id":"c09-pricing-constraints","topic":"t09-2","term":"Pricing constraints (including the type of competitive market)","definition":"Pricing constraints are factors that limit the range of prices a firm may set: demand for the product class, product and brand; newness of the product (stage in the product life cycle); single product vs product line; cost of producing and marketing the product; cost of changing prices and the time period they apply; type of competitive market; and competitors' prices.","explain":"Constraints are the walls around your pricing room. A brand-new product faces few substitutes and can carry a higher price; a product in a line must be priced with its siblings in mind (cannibalization or complementary sales). In the long run costs set a FLOOR under price - a firm may price below full cost for a while, but not indefinitely. Market structure matters: a pure monopoly has wide pricing latitude, an oligopoly (few sellers) risks price wars, monopolistic competition (many sellers, differentiated products) allows a range of prices, and in pure competition (many sellers, identical products) the market sets the price.","example":"A Saskatchewan canola grower must accept the going market price (pure competition), while three national wireless carriers watch each other's every price move (oligopoly).","contrast":"Constraints limit price; objectives are what price should accomplish. Among market types: oligopoly = FEW sellers that react to each other; monopolistic competition = MANY sellers with DIFFERENTIATED products; pure competition = MANY sellers with IDENTICAL products; pure monopoly = ONE seller.","trap":"Costs set a FLOOR (not a ceiling) in the LONG run (not the short run). 'Few sellers' means oligopoly even if the products differ; 'many sellers with slight differences' means monopolistic competition.","mnemonic":"Costs are the floor; customers are the ceiling.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Type of competitive market as a pricing constraint","head":["Market type","Sellers","Product","Pricing latitude"],"rows":[["Pure monopoly","One","Unique, no close substitutes","Wide latitude"],["Oligopoly","Few","Similar or differentiated","Limited - rivals react; price wars possible"],["Monopolistic competition","Many","Differentiated","Some - a range of prices"],["Pure competition","Many","Identical (commodity)","None - the market sets the price"]]}},{"id":"c09-demand-estimation","topic":"t09-3","term":"Demand curve, demand factors and forecasting","definition":"A demand curve is a graph relating the quantity sold to the price, showing the maximum number of units that will be sold at each price. Demand factors - consumer tastes, the price and availability of substitute products, and consumer income - shift the curve; forecasting methods (qualitative, regression, multiple regression, time-series) estimate demand and revenue at a chosen price.","explain":"A change in the product's own price moves buyers ALONG the demand curve; a change in a demand factor SHIFTS the whole curve. Creating the correct price begins the forecasting process: with a price known, estimate how much customers will buy given your marketing efforts and competitors' efforts, then translate that demand into estimated revenue. Accurate forecasts matter because every revenue, break-even and profit calculation rests on them.","example":"When layoffs cut household incomes in an Alberta city, demand for restaurant meals shifts left: fewer meals sell at every price even though menu prices have not changed.","contrast":"Movement along the curve (caused by a change in the product's OWN price) vs a shift of the curve (caused by tastes, substitutes' price or availability, or income). Forecasting: time-series projects past sales patterns over time; regression relates sales to one variable; multiple regression to several; qualitative methods rely on judgment and opinion.","trap":"The product's own price is NOT a demand factor - it causes movement along the curve. A rival's (substitute's) price IS a demand factor.","mnemonic":"Own price = slide along; Tastes, Substitutes, Income = shift.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-elasticity","topic":"t09-3","term":"Price elasticity of demand","definition":"Price elasticity of demand is the percentage change in quantity demanded relative to a percentage change in price (% change in quantity demanded / % change in price). Demand is elastic (greater than 1) when quantity is very responsive to price and inelastic (less than 1) when it is not.","explain":"Elasticity predicts what a price change does to total revenue. With elastic demand (luxuries, many substitutes), a price cut raises quantity so much that total revenue rises, and a price increase lowers revenue. With inelastic demand (necessities, few substitutes - gasoline, cigarettes), a price increase barely dents quantity, so total revenue rises.","example":"If an airline cuts fares 10% and seats sold jump 25%, elasticity = 25% / 10% = 2.5 - elastic, so the fare cut increases total revenue.","contrast":"Elastic: cut price -> total revenue UP; raise price -> total revenue DOWN. Inelastic: raise price -> total revenue UP; cut price -> total revenue DOWN. Skimming relies on an inelastic segment; penetration relies on elastic demand.","trap":"Formula order: % change in QUANTITY on top, % change in PRICE on the bottom. Many substitutes = elastic; necessity with few substitutes = inelastic. Do not assume a price cut always raises revenue.","mnemonic":"Elastic stretches: a small price change, a big quantity change.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Elastic vs inelastic demand","head":["","Elastic (greater than 1)","Inelastic (less than 1)"],"rows":[["Quantity response","Very responsive to price","Not very responsive"],["Typical products","Luxuries, many substitutes","Necessities, few substitutes (gasoline, cigarettes)"],["Price cut","Total revenue rises","Total revenue falls"],["Price increase","Total revenue falls","Total revenue rises"]]}},{"id":"c09-revenue-profit","topic":"t09-3","term":"Revenue concepts and the profit equation","definition":"Total revenue (TR) = unit price x quantity sold; average revenue = TR / quantity (equal to the unit price); marginal revenue = the change in TR from selling one more unit. Profit = Total revenue - Total cost = (P x Q) - [FC + (UVC x Q)].","explain":"Revenue is what comes in; profit is what is left after all costs. The profit equation is the engine behind break-even analysis and the profit-oriented approaches - plug in what you know and solve for the unknown (price, quantity or profit).","example":"A Red Deer bakery sells 8,000 pies at $25: TR = $200,000. With fixed costs of $60,000 and a $12 variable cost per pie, profit = $200,000 - ($60,000 + $96,000) = $44,000.","contrast":"Average revenue (TR divided by units sold = the price) vs marginal revenue (the extra revenue from ONE more unit). Revenue vs profit: revenue ignores costs; profit subtracts both fixed and variable costs.","trap":"Multiply unit variable cost by quantity before adding fixed cost. Average revenue equals price; marginal revenue is about the next unit. At break-even, TR equals total COST, not profit.","mnemonic":"Profit = P x Q minus (FC + UVC x Q).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Revenue and profit formulas","items":["Total revenue = P x Q","Average revenue = TR / Q (equals the unit price)","Marginal revenue = change in TR from selling one more unit","Profit = TR - TC = (P x Q) - [FC + (UVC x Q)]"]}},{"id":"c09-cost-concepts","topic":"t09-3","term":"Cost concepts: fixed, variable, total, unit variable and marginal cost","definition":"Total cost = fixed cost + variable cost. Fixed costs do not change with the quantity produced (rent, executive salaries, insurance); variable costs change directly with quantity (materials, direct labour per unit); unit variable cost = variable cost / quantity; marginal cost = the change in total cost from producing one more unit.","explain":"Whether a cost is fixed or variable depends on whether it moves with output. Rent is the same whether you make 10 frames or 1,000; glass and moulding for each frame rise with every unit. Separating the two is what makes break-even and target-profit calculations possible.","example":"A Winnipeg picture-frame shop pays $32,000 a year in rent and salaries (fixed) and $40 in materials and labour per frame (unit variable cost).","contrast":"Fixed cost (constant in total as volume changes) vs variable cost (rises in total with each unit). Unit variable cost (variable cost per unit) vs marginal cost (the added cost of the NEXT unit).","trap":"Salaries, rent and insurance are fixed; per-unit materials and packaging are variable. Total cost includes BOTH fixed and variable cost.","mnemonic":"Fixed = Frozen; Variable = Varies with volume.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-break-even","topic":"t09-3","term":"Break-even analysis","definition":"Break-even analysis is a technique that examines the relationship between total revenue and total cost to determine profitability at various levels of output. Break-even point (quantity) = Fixed cost / (Unit price - Unit variable cost).","explain":"The break-even point (BEP) is the quantity at which total revenue equals total cost - no profit, no loss. Each unit sold contributes (price - unit variable cost) toward fixed costs; once fixed costs are covered, every further unit adds profit. On a break-even chart, the area between the lines beyond the BEP is profit; before it is loss.","example":"A frame store with $32,000 fixed cost, a $120 price and a $40 unit variable cost breaks even at 32,000 / (120 - 40) = 400 frames (Figure 9-8).","contrast":"Break-even (the quantity where TR = TC and profit = 0) vs target-profit pricing (solve the same profit equation for the PRICE that yields a chosen dollar profit). Raising price or cutting variable cost lowers the BEP; raising fixed cost raises it.","trap":"Divide fixed cost by the contribution per unit (price minus UVC), not by price alone. The answer is in UNITS; multiply by price to get break-even dollars.","mnemonic":"Fixed over (Price minus Variable).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Break-even essentials (Figure 9-8 logic)","items":["BEP (units) = FC / (P - UVC)","Total cost line = fixed cost + variable cost; it starts at the fixed-cost level","Total revenue line starts at zero","Beyond the BEP: profit; before the BEP: loss","Higher price or lower UVC -> lower BEP; higher FC -> higher BEP"]}},{"id":"c09-four-approaches","topic":"t09-4","term":"Four approaches for selecting an approximate price level (Figure 9-2)","definition":"Step 1 of setting a final price uses one of four groups of approaches: demand-oriented (skimming, penetration, prestige, odd-even, target, bundle, yield management), cost-oriented (standard markup, cost-plus), profit-oriented (target profit, target return on sales, target return on investment) and competition-oriented (customary; above-, at- or below-market; loss leader).","explain":"Each bucket starts from a different question. Demand-oriented: what are customers' tastes and preferences, and what will they pay? Cost-oriented: what does it cost, plus a markup? Profit-oriented: what price hits a specific dollar profit or a percentage of sales or investment (this depends on accurate demand estimates)? Competition-oriented: what are competitors doing?","example":"A Vancouver bike maker could price a new e-bike by skimming (demand), adding 40% to cost (cost), solving for $500,000 profit (profit) or pricing just under rivals (competition).","contrast":"Target pricing is DEMAND-oriented (start with what consumers will pay, work backward to cost), while target profit and target return are PROFIT-oriented. Prestige is demand-oriented; above-market is competition-oriented. Loss leader is competition-oriented, not demand-oriented.","trap":"The word 'target' appears in two buckets: target pricing (demand) vs target profit, target return on sales and target ROI (profit). Odd-even and bundle are demand-oriented. Expect 'which is NOT a ___-oriented approach' questions.","mnemonic":"Demand has 7, Cost has 2, Profit has 3, Competition has 3 (7-2-3-3).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 9-2: Four approaches for selecting an approximate price level","head":["Demand-oriented","Cost-oriented","Profit-oriented","Competition-oriented"],"rows":[["Skimming","Standard markup","Target profit","Customary"],["Penetration","Cost-plus","Target return on sales","Above-, at- or below-market"],["Prestige","","Target return on investment","Loss leader"],["Odd-even","","",""],["Target","","",""],["Bundle","","",""],["Yield management","","",""]]}},{"id":"c09-skimming","topic":"t09-4","term":"Skimming pricing","definition":"Skimming pricing sets the highest initial price that customers really desiring the new product are willing to pay, often to recover research and development costs; the price is typically lowered later. It is a demand-oriented approach.","explain":"Skim the cream first: sell to price-insensitive buyers at a high launch price, then lower the price to reach the next segments. It works when (1) enough customers are price-insensitive - a segment with inelastic demand over a broad range of prices, (2) the high price signals quality, (3) competitors cannot enter quickly (patents, high R&D, complex technology) and (4) production costs do not fall much with volume, so a low price would gain little.","example":"A Waterloo tech firm launches a foldable phone at $2,400 to recover R&D, planning to cut the price once rivals catch up.","contrast":"Skimming (high INITIAL price on a NEW product, lowered over time; inelastic segment) vs penetration (low initial price; elastic demand; economies of scale) vs prestige (permanently high price for status; lowering it can reduce demand).","trap":"'Economies of scale are substantial' and 'market is price-sensitive' point to PENETRATION, not skimming. 'Recover R&D' and 'inelastic over a broad range of prices' signal skimming.","mnemonic":"Skim the cream, then lower the stream.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Conditions favouring skimming","items":["Enough customers are price-insensitive (inelastic demand over a broad range of prices)","A high initial price signals high quality","Competitors cannot enter quickly (patents, R&D, technology barriers)","Production costs do not fall much as volume rises","Goal: recover research and development costs early"]}},{"id":"c09-penetration","topic":"t09-4","term":"Penetration pricing","definition":"Penetration pricing sets a low initial price on a new product to appeal immediately to the mass market. It is a demand-oriented approach.","explain":"Instead of skimming the top, penetration grabs the whole market fast. It works when (1) many buyers are price-sensitive (elastic demand), (2) economies of scale and experience lower unit costs as volume grows and (3) a low price discourages competitors from entering. The instructor's example is the Nintendo Wii U.","example":"A new Canadian streaming service launches at $4.99 a month - well below established rivals - to sign up subscribers quickly and spread its content costs.","contrast":"Penetration (low INITIAL price on a NEW product to win the mass market) vs loss leader (an existing item priced below its customary price to pull traffic toward OTHER products) vs below-market pricing (ongoing positioning against competitors' market price).","trap":"'New product' + 'low introductory price' + 'mass market' = penetration. If the low price is on a staple used to bring shoppers into a store, it is a loss leader.","mnemonic":"Penetrate the mass market: price low, scale high.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Conditions favouring penetration","items":["Many segments of the market are price-sensitive (elastic demand)","Economies of scale and experience cut unit costs as volume grows","A low initial price discourages competitors from entering"]}},{"id":"c09-prestige","topic":"t09-4","term":"Prestige pricing","definition":"Prestige pricing sets a high price so that quality- or status-conscious consumers will be attracted to the product and buy it. It is a demand-oriented approach.","explain":"For prestige goods the demand curve bends backward over part of its range: lowering the price can actually reduce the quantity demanded because the product loses its status appeal. The high price is permanent and part of the product's identity, not a temporary launch tactic.","example":"A Montreal jeweller cut prices on its signature watches by 30% and sales fell - buyers stopped seeing them as exclusive.","contrast":"Prestige (permanently high price for status; backward-bending demand) vs skimming (high INITIAL price lowered over time) vs above-market pricing (competition-oriented: priced above competitors' market price, which serves as the benchmark).","trap":"Cue words: 'status-conscious', 'lowering the price reduced sales', 'demand curve bends backward'. A high price on a NEW product to recover R&D is skimming, not prestige.","mnemonic":"Prestige: drop the price, drop the appeal.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-target-pricing","topic":"t09-4","term":"Target pricing","definition":"Target pricing is deliberately adjusting the composition and features of a product to achieve the target price to consumers: estimate the price consumers are willing to pay, then work backward to design and cost the product. It is a demand-oriented approach.","explain":"Target pricing flips the usual order. Rather than build a product and add up its costs, the firm starts with the price customers will accept, subtracts the margins it needs, and engineers the product (materials, features) to hit the resulting cost.","example":"A Quebec furniture maker learns students will pay $199 for a desk, then chooses materials and a flat-pack design so the desk costs $110 to make.","contrast":"Target pricing (demand-oriented: start from consumers' acceptable price and design backward) vs target profit pricing (profit-oriented: solve for the price that delivers a set dollar profit) vs target return pricing (profit as a % of sales or investment).","trap":"The word 'target' tempts students toward the profit-oriented bucket. If the stem starts with what consumers will pay and then adjusts features or costs, it is target pricing (demand-oriented).","mnemonic":"Target pricing: price first, product second.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-bundle","topic":"t09-4","term":"Bundle pricing","definition":"Bundle pricing is the marketing of two or more products in a single package price. It is a demand-oriented approach.","explain":"Buyers see the bundle as a better deal than buying each item separately, and the seller moves more items per sale. Think value meals, internet-plus-TV packages and ski-and-hotel packages.","example":"A Calgary telecom sells home internet, TV and a mobile line together for $120 a month instead of $155 bought separately.","contrast":"Bundle pricing (several different products, ONE package price) vs yield management (different prices for the same capacity at different times) vs a quantity discount (a lower price for buying MORE of the same product).","trap":"Two or more DIFFERENT products for one price = bundle. A price break on a large order of the same product is a quantity discount, not bundle pricing.","mnemonic":"Bundle = many items, one tag.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-yield-management","topic":"t09-4","term":"Yield management pricing","definition":"Yield management pricing is charging different prices to maximize revenue for a set amount of capacity at any given time. It is a demand-oriented approach.","explain":"When capacity is fixed and perishable - airline seats, hotel rooms, concert seats - an unsold unit is lost forever. Yield management raises prices when demand for the remaining capacity is high and lowers them when it is soft, so the firm earns the most from the capacity it has. The instructor's example is airline seats.","example":"A Banff hotel charges $189 for a room on a quiet November Tuesday and $549 for the same room on a ski-season Saturday.","contrast":"Yield management (prices vary with demand for a FIXED CAPACITY over time) vs a flexible-price policy based on the buyer (prices vary by individual BUYER or purchase situation, e.g., age or negotiation) vs bundle pricing (several products, one price).","trap":"Cue words: 'fixed number of seats or rooms', 'maximize revenue from capacity', 'price varies by date or time'. Different prices by age or by haggling is a flexible-price policy, not yield management. 'Adjusting prices in real time based on demand' (Uber surge) is the course's wording for dynamic pricing; a fixed number of seats or rooms sold over time is the yield-management cue.","mnemonic":"Yield: squeeze the most revenue from every seat.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-markup","topic":"t09-5","term":"Standard markup pricing and markup on price vs on cost","definition":"Standard markup pricing is adding a fixed percentage to the cost of all items in a specific product class. Markup on selling price = (price - cost) / price; markup on cost = (price - cost) / cost. Wholesalers and retailers often express markup as a percentage of price; manufacturers often as a percentage of cost.","explain":"It is a cost-oriented approach: start with cost, add a standard percentage. The same dollar markup is a bigger percentage of cost than of price. In Figure 9-3, a $75 selling price (100%), $60 cost (80%) and $15 markup is 20% of price but 25% of cost. When markup is relative to price, price is always 100%.","example":"A Lethbridge hardware store adds a standard 40% markup on cost to every item in its paint department.","contrast":"Standard markup (the same % added to the cost of ALL items in a class; retailers and manufacturers) vs cost-plus (total unit cost of a specific job plus a specific amount or %, common in construction and government contracts). Markup on price (retailers) vs markup on cost (manufacturers).","trap":"Always check the base - % of price or % of cost? Conversions: markup on price = markup on cost / (1 + markup on cost); markup on cost = markup on price / (1 - markup on price). Price from cost and a markup on price: price = cost / (1 - markup %).","mnemonic":"Retailers mark up on Price; Makers mark up on Cost.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 9-3: Same markup, two bases","head":["Item","Dollars","% of price","% of cost"],"rows":[["Selling price","$75","100%","125%"],["Cost","$60","80%","100%"],["Markup","$15","20%","25%"]]}},{"id":"c09-cost-plus","topic":"t09-5","term":"Cost-plus pricing","definition":"Cost-plus pricing involves summing the total unit cost of providing a product or service and adding a specific amount (or percentage) to the cost to arrive at a price. It is a cost-oriented approach common in construction, government contracts and services.","explain":"Cost-plus shows up where each job is different - a building, a custom system, a consulting project. The buyer agrees to cover the total cost of the job plus a fee or percentage, rather than paying a catalogue price.","example":"An Edmonton contractor builds a school gym for the province and bills its total project cost of $2.4 million plus a fixed fee of $180,000.","contrast":"Cost-plus (total unit cost of a specific job + a specific amount or %; contracts, construction, services) vs standard markup (a standard % added to the cost of all items in a product class; retailers, manufacturers). Both are cost-oriented; neither starts from demand.","trap":"If the stem involves a contract, a project, or 'cost plus a fixed fee', think cost-plus. If a retailer or manufacturer applies the same % across a whole product class, think standard markup.","mnemonic":"Cost-plus = contract plus fee.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-target-profit","topic":"t09-5","term":"Target profit pricing","definition":"Target profit pricing sets an annual target of a specific dollar volume of profit and solves the profit equation for price: Profit = (P x Q) - [FC + (UVC x Q)]. It is a profit-oriented approach.","explain":"Pick the dollar profit you want and the volume you expect, then solve: P = (Target profit + FC + UVC x Q) / Q. Like all profit-oriented approaches, it depends on accurate estimates of demand - if the volume does not materialize, neither does the profit.","example":"Charlie Tuna: UVC $40, FC $410,000, target profit $42,000 on 21,000 packages. P = ($42,000 + $410,000 + $840,000) / 21,000 = $61.52.","contrast":"Target profit (a specific DOLLAR profit) vs target return on sales (profit as a % of SALES) vs target return on investment (profit as a % of INVESTMENT) vs target pricing (demand-oriented: start from consumers' acceptable price).","trap":"Multiply UVC by quantity, add fixed cost AND the target profit, then divide by quantity. A dollar figure ($42,000) = target profit; a percentage of sales or investment = target return.","mnemonic":"Dollar goal = target profit; percentage goal = target return.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-target-return","topic":"t09-5","term":"Target return-on-sales and target return-on-investment pricing","definition":"Target return-on-sales pricing sets a price to achieve a profit that is a specified percentage of the sales volume (revenue). Target return-on-investment (ROI) pricing sets a price to achieve an annual target percentage return on investment. Both are profit-oriented approaches.","explain":"Both turn a percentage goal into a profit target, then use the profit equation. Return on sales: Profit = r x (P x Q), so the profit target grows with revenue. ROI: Profit = r x investment, a fixed dollar amount once the investment is known.","example":"A Halifax firm that invested $500,000 in a production line wants a 12% ROI, so it needs $60,000 profit; a grocery chain may aim for profit equal to a set percentage of its sales.","contrast":"Target return on sales (% of SALES revenue) vs target ROI (% of INVESTMENT) vs target profit (a specific DOLLAR amount). All three are profit-oriented; target pricing is demand-oriented.","trap":"Read the base of the percentage: 'of sales' vs 'of investment'. With return on sales, the profit target changes with price, so solve with algebra: P x Q - TC = r x P x Q. The investment itself is not a cost to add into this year's price.","mnemonic":"ROS = slice of Sales; ROI = slice of Investment.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-customary-market","topic":"t09-5","term":"Customary pricing and above-, at- or below-market pricing","definition":"Customary pricing is a method of pricing based on tradition, a standardized channel of distribution or other competitive factors. Above-, at- or below-market pricing uses the 'market price' (what competitors charge) as a benchmark and deliberately prices above, at or below it. Both are competition-oriented approaches.","explain":"Competition-oriented approaches focus on what competitors are doing rather than on demand, cost or profit. Customary prices are hard to change (candy bars, vending items). Market-benchmark pricing positions a brand: luxury brands and the iPhone 17 above market, mainstream brands at market, discounters such as Walmart below market.","example":"A vending company keeps its chocolate bars at the traditional $2.00 (customary); a discount grocer sets everyday prices about 5% below the area's average supermarket prices (below-market).","contrast":"Below-market pricing (ongoing positioning relative to the market price) vs loss leader (a specific item priced below its customary price to draw traffic). Above-market (competition-oriented benchmark) vs prestige (demand-oriented status pricing).","trap":"If the stem says 'tradition' or 'what buyers have always paid' for a low-cost staple, think customary. If it says 'compared with competitors' prices' or 'market price as a benchmark', think above-, at- or below-market.","mnemonic":"The market price is the ruler: above it, at it or below it.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-loss-leader","topic":"t09-5","term":"Loss-leader pricing","definition":"Loss-leader pricing is deliberately selling a product below its customary price to attract attention and store traffic, in the hope that shoppers will buy other products as well. It is a competition-oriented approach.","explain":"The goal is not to make money on the leader item but to bring people through the door. Grocers use milk and eggs; electronics retailers use Black Friday TVs. The profit comes from the other items shoppers add to the cart.","example":"A Calgary supermarket advertises a 4 L jug of milk at $2.99 - well below its usual $5.79 - knowing most shoppers will fill the rest of the cart at regular prices.","contrast":"Loss leader (an existing item below its customary price to drive traffic) vs penetration (a NEW product launched at a low price to win the mass market) vs predatory pricing (an illegal very low price meant to drive competitors out of business).","trap":"The cue is traffic for OTHER products. A low launch price on a new product is penetration; a below-cost price intended to eliminate a rival is predatory pricing.","mnemonic":"Lose on the leader, win on the basket.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-cost-vs-value","topic":"t09-5","term":"Cost-based vs value-based pricing","definition":"Cost-based pricing follows the sequence Product -> Cost -> Price -> Value -> Customers; value-based pricing reverses it: Customers -> Value -> Price -> Cost -> Product (Nagle/Holden).","explain":"Cost-based pricing starts inside the firm: design a product, total its costs, set a price, then convince customers it is worth it. Value-based pricing starts with customers: learn what they value, set a price that captures that value, then determine the costs that can be incurred and design the product to fit. It matches the idea that pricing is the attempt to capture the value the rest of the mix creates.","example":"A Calgary software start-up interviews contractors, finds they would pay $60 a month to save five hours of paperwork, sets that price, then scopes the features it can build profitably at that price (value-based).","contrast":"Cost-based starts with the PRODUCT and ends with CUSTOMERS; value-based starts with CUSTOMERS and ends with the PRODUCT. Value-based pricing is close in spirit to target pricing; standard markup and cost-plus follow the cost-based logic.","trap":"Questions ask what comes first, last or immediately after a step. In value-based pricing, Price comes right after Value and right before Cost; in cost-based pricing, Price comes right after Cost and right before Value.","mnemonic":"Value-based reads cost-based backwards.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Two pricing sequences (Nagle/Holden)","head":["Step","Cost-based","Value-based"],"rows":[["1","Product","Customers"],["2","Cost","Value"],["3","Price","Price"],["4","Value","Cost"],["5","Customers","Product"]]}},{"id":"c09-final-price-steps","topic":"t09-6","term":"Four steps in setting a final price","definition":"Step 1: select an approximate price level (using one of the four approach buckets). Step 2: set the list or quoted price (one-price or flexible-price policy). Step 3: make special adjustments to the list or quoted price (discounts, allowances, geographic adjustments and, in the slides, dynamic pricing). Step 4: monitor and adjust prices.","explain":"The approaches give a ballpark; the list price turns it into a quoted number and a policy; special adjustments tailor it to particular buyers and situations; monitoring keeps it right as costs, competitors and demand change. Pricing is never 'set and forget'. The slides list dynamic pricing (real-time, demand-based changes such as Uber surge) among the Step 3 adjustments, while the textbook treats it as a form of flexible pricing; the one-price vs flexible-price POLICY choice itself is Step 2.","example":"A Calgary bike shop prices a new e-bike near competitors' level (Step 1), lists it at a fixed $2,999 for everyone (Step 2), offers a $300 trade-in allowance (Step 3) and reviews the price after a rival's spring sale (Step 4).","contrast":"The four final-price steps (approximate level -> list price -> special adjustments -> monitor) vs the four approach buckets (demand, cost, profit, competition), which are only the tools used inside Step 1. Identifying objectives and constraints comes before these steps.","trap":"One-price vs flexible-price is decided in Step 2, not Step 3. Discounts, allowances and FOB or delivered pricing are Step 3. 'Monitor and adjust' is the last step.","mnemonic":"Approximate, List, Adjust, Monitor: 'A Little Adjustment Matters'.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Setting a final price","items":["Step 1: Select an approximate price level","Step 2: Set the list or quoted price (one-price vs flexible-price policy)","Step 3: Make special adjustments to the list or quoted price (discounts, allowances, geographic adjustments, dynamic pricing)","Step 4: Monitor and adjust prices"]}},{"id":"c09-one-flexible","topic":"t09-6","term":"One-price vs flexible-price policy (and dynamic pricing)","definition":"A one-price policy sets one price for all buyers of a product or service. A flexible-price policy sets different prices for products and services depending on individual buyers and purchase situations; dynamic pricing - adjusting prices in real time based on demand (e.g., Uber surge pricing) - is a form of flexible pricing.","explain":"Choosing the price policy is part of Step 2 of setting a final price (the slides also show dynamic pricing, such as Uber surge, among the Step 3 special adjustments). One-price policies (no-haggle car brands, Walmart) build trust and simplify selling. Flexible pricing lets a car dealer negotiate, or a transit authority charge seniors, students and adults different fares. Dynamic pricing uses technology to change prices minute to minute as demand rises and falls.","example":"A city transit system charges different fares for youth, seniors and adults (flexible-price policy); a no-haggle car brand posts one sticker price that every buyer pays (one-price policy).","contrast":"Flexible-price policy (different prices for different BUYERS or situations; legal with consumers) vs price discrimination (different prices to COMPETING business buyers for like quantities, illegal when it lessens competition) vs yield management (prices vary with demand for a fixed capacity over time).","trap":"Different prices by age, employment, negotiation or real-time demand = flexible (dynamic) pricing - not penetration, bundle or one-price. 'Same price for everyone' = one-price policy.","mnemonic":"One price fits all; flexible fits each.","figure":null},{"id":"c09-discounts-allowances","topic":"t09-6","term":"Discounts and allowances","definition":"Discounts are reductions from list price that a seller gives a buyer as a reward for some favourable activity: quantity (noncumulative or cumulative), seasonal, trade (functional) and cash discounts. Allowances are reductions from list price such as trade-in allowances (for turning in an old item) and promotional allowances (cash or goods given to resellers for advertising or promoting the product).","explain":"Each discount rewards a specific behaviour: buying more (quantity), buying or stocking earlier than demand requires (seasonal), performing marketing functions as a reseller (trade or functional, often quoted as a chain such as 30/10/5), or paying promptly (cash, e.g., 2/10 net 30). Noncumulative quantity discounts apply to a single order; cumulative ones add up purchases over a period and encourage loyalty. Promotional allowances must be offered proportionally to all competing resellers under the Competition Act.","example":"Molson Coors gives retailers a discount to stock up before Canada Day (seasonal); a supplier bills 2/10 net 30, so a $5,000 invoice paid in 8 days costs $4,900 (cash).","contrast":"Seasonal (buy or stock EARLIER than demand requires) vs quantity (buy MORE) vs trade or functional (resellers rewarded for performing channel functions) vs cash (PAY promptly). Trade-in allowance (an old item reduces the price) vs promotional allowance (a reseller is paid to advertise or promote).","trap":"'Before the season' = seasonal, not quantity or cash. 'Within 10 days' = cash. Chain trade discounts are applied one after another, not added (30/10/5 is not 45% off). Cumulative = over time; noncumulative = one order.","mnemonic":"More, Early, Function, Fast: quantity, seasonal, trade, cash.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Discounts and allowances","head":["Type","Rewards / what it is","Example"],"rows":[["Quantity - noncumulative","A larger single order","10% off any order of 500+ cases"],["Quantity - cumulative","Total purchases over a period (loyalty)","Bigger price break as annual volume grows"],["Seasonal","Buying or stocking earlier than demand requires","Lawnmowers ordered in winter; beer before Canada Day"],["Trade (functional)","Resellers performing marketing functions","30/10/5 off list"],["Cash","Paying bills promptly","2/10 net 30"],["Trade-in allowance","Turning in an old item","$20 credit for an old dishwasher"],["Promotional allowance","Reseller advertises or promotes the product","Free cases for a feature display and flyer ad"]]}},{"id":"c09-geographic","topic":"t09-6","term":"Geographic adjustments: FOB origin and uniform delivered pricing","definition":"Geographic adjustments are made to list or quoted prices to reflect the transportation component. FOB origin pricing quotes a price 'free on board' a vehicle at the seller's location; title passes to the buyer at the point of loading and the buyer pays the freight. Uniform delivered pricing quotes a price that includes all transportation costs.","explain":"Who pays freight, and when ownership changes hands, is the core difference. Under FOB origin the seller covers loading, then the buyer owns the goods and pays shipping from that point, so distant buyers pay more. Under uniform delivered pricing the seller pays shipping and builds it into the quoted price; variations include single-zone (postage-stamp) pricing, multiple-zone pricing, FOB with freight-allowed (freight absorption) and basing-point pricing.","example":"A Red Deer fertilizer plant quotes 'FOB Red Deer' and the Manitoba buyer pays the trucking; a Toronto online retailer charges every Canadian customer the same delivered price (single-zone, a form of uniform delivered pricing).","contrast":"FOB origin (buyer pays freight; title passes at loading) vs uniform delivered (seller pays freight; the quoted price includes all transportation costs).","trap":"FOB origin: title passes at the point of LOADING, not on arrival. 'Price includes all transportation costs' describes uniform delivered pricing - a tempting wrong option in FOB questions.","mnemonic":"FOB origin: Freight On the Buyer.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Geographic adjustments","head":["Method","Who pays freight","Notes"],"rows":[["FOB origin","Buyer","Title passes at the point of loading at the seller's location"],["Uniform delivered","Seller (built into price)","Quoted price includes all transportation costs"],["Single-zone (postage stamp)","Seller","Uniform delivered variation: same delivered price everywhere"],["Multiple-zone","Seller","Uniform delivered variation: one price per zone"],["FOB with freight-allowed","Seller absorbs freight","Uniform delivered variation (freight absorption)"],["Basing-point","Seller","Uniform delivered variation: freight charged from a basing point"]]}},{"id":"c09-legal","topic":"t09-6","term":"Legal and ethical pricing issues (Competition Act)","definition":"Canada's Competition Act, enforced by the Competition Bureau, restricts price fixing (competitors conspiring to set prices - horizontal, or vertical resale price maintenance), price discrimination (different prices to competing buyers for like quantities when it lessens competition), deceptive pricing (price offers that mislead, e.g., bait and switch, double ticketing) and predatory pricing (a very low price set to drive competitors out of business).","explain":"Price fixing is collusion: rivals agree on prices (horizontal), or a manufacturer forces resellers to charge a set price (vertical). Price discrimination is treating competing business buyers unequally for the same quantities. Deceptive pricing misleads consumers - bait and switch (advertise a bargain you do not intend to sell, then push a pricier item), bargains conditional on other purchases, misleading comparable-value or suggested-price comparisons, and double ticketing (the seller must charge the lower of two prices). Predatory pricing charges a very low price to injure or eliminate rivals, planning to raise prices later.","example":"Three Ontario bread makers secretly agree to raise loaf prices together (horizontal price fixing).","contrast":"Price fixing (competitors COLLUDE) vs price discrimination (one seller charges COMPETING buyers different prices) vs predatory pricing (a very low price to drive rivals OUT) vs deceptive pricing (MISLEADING consumers). Flexible pricing to final consumers is legal; discriminating among competing resellers can be illegal.","trap":"Bait and switch and double ticketing are DECEPTIVE pricing, not predatory. With double ticketing the item must be sold at the LOWER price. Vertical price fixing involves a manufacturer and its resellers, not two competitors.","mnemonic":"Fix (collude), Discriminate (unequal), Deceive (mislead), Prey (eliminate rivals).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Pricing practices restricted by the Competition Act","head":["Practice","What it is","Cue"],"rows":[["Price fixing - horizontal","Competitors conspire to set prices","Rival bakeries agree on bread prices"],["Price fixing - vertical","Manufacturer controls resellers' prices (resale price maintenance)","Brand cuts off a store that discounts"],["Price discrimination","Different prices to competing buyers for like quantities, lessening competition","Big chain pays less than independents for the same volume"],["Deceptive pricing","Misleading price offers: bait and switch, conditional bargains, false comparisons, double ticketing","Advertised special is never really available"],["Predatory pricing","Very low price to drive competitors out, then raise prices","Below-cost prices until the local rival closes"]]}}],"activities":[{"id":"a09-01","type":"sort","concept":"c09-four-approaches","title":"Figure 9-2 sort (part 1)","prompt":"Drag each pricing approach into its Figure 9-2 bucket.","buckets":["Demand-oriented","Cost-oriented","Profit-oriented","Competition-oriented"],"items":[{"text":"Skimming","bucket":0},{"text":"Prestige","bucket":0},{"text":"Bundle","bucket":0},{"text":"Yield management","bucket":0},{"text":"Standard markup","bucket":1},{"text":"Target profit","bucket":2},{"text":"Target return on investment","bucket":2},{"text":"Customary","bucket":3}],"explanation":"Demand-oriented approaches start from customer tastes and preferences (skimming, prestige, bundle, yield management). Standard markup adds a percentage to cost. Target profit and target ROI solve for price to hit a profit goal. Customary pricing follows tradition and competitive factors."},{"id":"a09-02","type":"sort","concept":"c09-four-approaches","title":"Figure 9-2 sort (part 2)","prompt":"Drag each remaining pricing approach into its Figure 9-2 bucket.","buckets":["Demand-oriented","Cost-oriented","Profit-oriented","Competition-oriented"],"items":[{"text":"Penetration","bucket":0},{"text":"Odd-even","bucket":0},{"text":"Target","bucket":0},{"text":"Cost-plus","bucket":1},{"text":"Target return on sales","bucket":2},{"text":"Above-, at- or below-market","bucket":3},{"text":"Loss leader","bucket":3}],"explanation":"Penetration, odd-even and target pricing all start from what customers will respond to or pay, so they are demand-oriented (note: target pricing is NOT profit-oriented). Cost-plus adds an amount to total unit cost. Target return on sales is profit-oriented. Market benchmarking and loss leaders focus on competitors. Together with part 1: Demand 7, Cost 2, Profit 3, Competition 3."},{"id":"a09-03","type":"order","concept":"c09-final-price-steps","title":"Four steps to a final price","prompt":"Put the four steps in setting a final price in the correct order.","items":["Select an approximate price level","Set the list or quoted price","Make special adjustments to the list or quoted price","Monitor and adjust prices"],"explanation":"First choose a ballpark using one of the four approach buckets, then set the list or quoted price (one-price or flexible-price policy), then make special adjustments (discounts, allowances, geographic adjustments), and finally monitor and adjust prices as conditions change."},{"id":"a09-04","type":"match","concept":"c09-discounts-allowances","title":"Discounts and allowances in action","prompt":"Match each discount or allowance with the scenario it describes.","pairs":[{"left":"Noncumulative quantity discount","right":"10% off any single order of 500 or more cases of motor oil"},{"left":"Cumulative quantity discount","right":"A cafe chain earns a bigger price break as its total bean purchases for the year grow"},{"left":"Seasonal discount","right":"A garden centre pays less for patio furniture ordered in January"},{"left":"Trade (functional) discount","right":"A wholesaler gets 30/10 off list for warehousing and selling to retailers"},{"left":"Cash discount","right":"2% off an invoice paid within 10 days (2/10 net 30)"},{"left":"Trade-in allowance","right":"$500 off a new snowblower for handing in the old one"},{"left":"Promotional allowance","right":"Free cases of chips for a grocer that runs an end-of-aisle display and flyer ad"}],"explanation":"Each discount rewards a specific behaviour: one big order (noncumulative), loyalty over time (cumulative), buying early (seasonal), performing channel functions (trade), paying promptly (cash). Allowances reduce price for turning in an old item (trade-in) or for promoting the product (promotional)."},{"id":"a09-05","type":"match","concept":"c09-legal","title":"Spot the pricing offence","prompt":"Match each practice restricted by the Competition Act with its example.","pairs":[{"left":"Horizontal price fixing","right":"Two rival gas stations in a small town agree to keep regular gas at the same price"},{"left":"Vertical price fixing","right":"A blender maker stops supplying a retailer that sells below the maker's set price"},{"left":"Price discrimination","right":"A supplier charges a big chain less than an independent store for the same quantity, hurting competition"},{"left":"Bait and switch","right":"A $299 TV is advertised with no real stock, and staff steer shoppers to an $899 model"},{"left":"Double ticketing","right":"An item carries two price stickers, so the store must sell it at the lower one"},{"left":"Predatory pricing","right":"A chain prices far below cost near a new rival until it closes, then raises prices"}],"explanation":"Price fixing is collusion among competitors (horizontal) or a manufacturer controlling resellers' prices (vertical). Price discrimination treats competing buyers unequally. Bait and switch and double ticketing are deceptive pricing. Predatory pricing uses very low prices to drive rivals out."},{"id":"a09-06","type":"order","concept":"c09-cost-vs-value","title":"Value-based pricing sequence","prompt":"Put the elements of VALUE-BASED pricing (Nagle/Holden) in the correct order.","items":["Customers","Value","Price","Cost","Product"],"explanation":"Value-based pricing starts with customers, determines the value they perceive, sets a price that captures it, then works out the costs that can be incurred and the product to build. Cost-based pricing runs the exact reverse: Product, Cost, Price, Value, Customers."}],"questions":[{"id":"q09-001","concept":"c09-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Live 'n' Homes, a company that manages apartments, is renovating 17 units. It has to replace the dishwashers in each of them and is looking to finance the purchase. Each dishwasher is listed at $800. The merchant offers a $200 discount per unit on the dishwashers; a credit of $20 for the dishwashers Live 'n' Homes trades in; and financing for a cost of $30 per unit. What is the net (or final) price Live 'n' Homes will pay for each dishwasher?","options":["$590","$600","$610","$730"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Final price = List price - Incentives and allowances + Extra fees = $800 - ($200 + $20) + $30 = $610. $590 comes from flipping the signs (adding the trade-in credit and subtracting the financing fee); fees such as financing always raise the final price.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-002","concept":"c09-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Peaks Snowmobiles lists a new sled at $14,500. A Grande Prairie buyer receives a $1,200 spring discount and a $3,000 trade-in allowance for an old sled, then pays a $450 financing fee and a $150 delivery surcharge. Using the price equation, what is the final price?","options":["$9,700","$11,500","$10,300","$10,900"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Final price = $14,500 - ($1,200 + $3,000) + ($450 + $150) = $14,500 - $4,200 + $600 = $10,900. $10,300 ignores the extra fees and $9,700 subtracts them, but financing charges and surcharges are ADDED in the price equation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-003","concept":"c09-price","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ is the money or other considerations, including other goods and services, exchanged for the ownership or use of a product.","options":["Price","Value","Barter","Cost"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is the definition of price. Value is the ratio of perceived benefits to price, and barter is one specific exchange of goods or services for goods or services - price is the broader term covering money AND other considerations.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-004","concept":"c09-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A Red Deer dentist agrees to provide a year of check-ups for a local carpenter's family in exchange for the carpenter building new reception cabinets. No money changes hands. This exchange is best described as:","options":["a trade-in allowance","a functional discount","a promotional allowance","barter"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Barter is the exchange of goods or services for other goods or services rather than for money. A trade-in allowance also involves handing over an item, but it only reduces a money price - here no money is exchanged at all.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-005","concept":"c09-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation is barter rather than a trade-in allowance?","options":["A car buyer hands over her old sedan and receives $4,000 off the new car's price","A cellphone shopper gets $150 off a new phone for returning his old one","A dishwasher buyer receives a $20 credit for turning in an old unit","An Edmonton print shop designs a bakery's menus in return for a month of catering"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Barter swaps goods or services directly, with no money price involved - menus for catering. In the other three, the old item merely reduces a money price, which makes it a trade-in allowance (subtracted in the price equation).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-006","concept":"c09-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"When using the price equation (Final price = List price - Incentives and allowances + Extra fees), which of the following would NOT be subtracted from the list price?","options":["A quantity discount","A trade-in allowance","A cash discount","A financing charge"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Incentives and allowances such as quantity discounts, cash discounts and trade-ins are subtracted. Extra fees - financing charges, surcharges, late-payment penalties - are ADDED, so a financing charge raises the final price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-007","concept":"c09-value-pricing","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"Most consumers believe that the higher the price of the diamond, the higher its quality is. This is an example of price influencing the perception of overall quality and _____ to consumers.","options":["acceptable cost","volume","value","trade-in allowances"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For some products, price influences the perception of overall quality and ultimately value; for many consumers a low-priced diamond would imply poor quality and poor perceived value. 'Volume' is a pricing objective and 'trade-in allowances' a price adjustment - neither is what price signals here.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-008","concept":"c09-value-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tundra Outerwear adds a removable fleece liner and a lifetime repair guarantee to its winter parka while keeping the price at $349. Which concept does this illustrate?","options":["Value pricing","Penetration pricing","Prestige pricing","Above-market pricing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Value pricing is increasing product or service benefits while maintaining or decreasing price. Penetration pricing is a low INITIAL price on a new product to win the mass market; here the price did not drop - the benefits rose.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-009","concept":"c09-value-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Halifax gym raises its monthly membership from $50 to $60 without adding any equipment, classes or hours. According to the value equation, what happens to members' perceived value?","options":["It rises, because a higher price always signals higher quality","It stays the same, because benefits are unchanged","It rises, because the gym earns more revenue to reinvest","It falls, because price rose while perceived benefits did not"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Value = perceived benefits / price. With benefits held constant, raising the denominator (price) lowers value. The price-quality link matters for some products like diamonds, but here members know the benefits are explicitly unchanged.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-010","concept":"c09-value-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario is an example of value pricing rather than penetration pricing?","options":["A new energy drink launches at $1.49, half the category average, to win shelf space fast","A streaming service enters Canada at $3.99 a month to sign up subscribers quickly","A new smartphone brand debuts its first model well below rivals to grab market share","A tire retailer adds free seasonal storage and rotation to its tire packages at last year's price"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Value pricing raises benefits while holding or lowering price - the tire package gains storage and rotation at an unchanged price. The other three are low INITIAL prices on NEW offerings to win the mass market quickly: penetration pricing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-011","concept":"c09-value-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why might a jeweller avoid pricing a one-carat diamond far below competitors' prices, even if its costs would allow it?","options":["Because costs set a ceiling over price in the long run","Because the Competition Act prohibits below-market pricing","Because lowering price always reduces total revenue","Because a very low price can signal poor quality and lower perceived value"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For some products price shapes perceived quality and ultimately value, so a cheap diamond implies a poor or fake stone. Costs set a FLOOR, not a ceiling; whether a price cut lowers revenue depends on elasticity; and below-market pricing is legal (predatory pricing is the restricted practice).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-012","concept":"c09-price-importance","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the course, pricing has a dual purpose or role. What are the two roles?","options":["Generating revenue and learning about the marketplace","Creating value and communicating benefits","Covering costs and beating competitors","Building awareness and encouraging trial"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pricing must (1) generate revenue and (2) help the firm learn about the marketplace, as with the quality heuristic. Creating value is mainly the job of the other three marketing-mix elements; pricing is the attempt to capture that value.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-013","concept":"c09-price-importance","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Ridge Winery tests its new Riesling at $16 in some stores and $24 in others. To its surprise, the $24 bottles sell better because shoppers assume the pricier wine must be better. This finding best illustrates which role of pricing?","options":["Generating revenue through penetration","Meeting a survival pricing objective","Creating a reference price through odd-even endings","Learning about the marketplace through the quality heuristic"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Price's second role is learning about the marketplace - here the winery learns that buyers use price as a cue to quality (the quality heuristic). Odd-even pricing concerns endings such as $15.99, which is not what the test revealed.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-014","concept":"c09-price-importance","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"Pricing is called the 'moment of truth' because it is the first marketing-mix decision a firm must make.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Pricing is the 'moment of truth' because it is where the focus of a transaction eventually comes to - every exchange ends at the price paid. The chapter says nothing about price being decided first.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-015","concept":"c09-price-importance","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Glacier Gear has a well-designed backpack, strong Instagram promotion and distribution through major sporting-goods chains, but it priced the pack so low that each sale loses money. What does the chapter predict?","options":["Low prices always create a quality signal that raises demand","The strong product and promotion will make up for the pricing","The firm has used value pricing correctly and will earn long-run profit","Poor pricing can make an otherwise reasonable marketing strategy financially ineffective"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The course stresses that poor pricing strategies can make reasonable marketing strategies financially ineffective - the other Ps create value, but price must capture it. Low prices also tend to signal LOWER quality, not higher.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-016","concept":"c09-price-importance","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the importance of price is NOT consistent with the chapter?","options":["Product, promotion and place go a long way to create value; pricing tries to capture it","Consumers may use price as a heuristic to judge quality","A poor pricing strategy can make a reasonable marketing strategy financially ineffective","Price is the marketing-mix element that creates most of a product's value"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The chapter says the other three marketing-mix elements go a long way to CREATE value, while pricing is the attempt to CAPTURE that value. The quality heuristic and the risk of poor pricing come straight from the slides.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-017","concept":"c09-consumer-psychology","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A shopper sees a shampoo tagged 'Regular $12.99, Sale $8.99' and decides it is a great deal because she compares the sale price with the regular price. The $12.99 is acting as a(n):","options":["reference price","customary price","seasonal discount","loss leader"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A reference price is the price consumers use to compare against a purchase price - here the 'regular' price. A customary price is one set by tradition (like a vending item), not a comparison point in the buyer's mind.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-018","concept":"c09-consumer-psychology","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Northern Sound prices its wireless earbuds at $199.99 instead of $200 so they seem like a better deal. This is:","options":["odd-even pricing","customary pricing","prestige pricing","bundle pricing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Odd-even pricing sets prices a few dollars or cents under an even number to suggest a bargain. Prestige pricing would set a HIGH price level for status-conscious buyers, and even endings would be used to suggest quality.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-019","concept":"c09-consumer-psychology","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why might a luxury restaurant in Vancouver price its tasting menu at an even $180 rather than $179.99?","options":["Odd endings increase price elasticity for luxury goods","Even prices are required by the Competition Act for restaurants","Even prices are a form of loss-leader pricing","Even prices suggest quality, while odd endings suggest a bargain"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In odd-even pricing, odd endings ($179.99) suggest a bargain while even prices ($180) suggest quality - the image a luxury restaurant wants. No law requires even prices, and a loss leader is an item priced below its customary price to build traffic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-020","concept":"c09-consumer-psychology","also":["c09-prestige"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Maison Lux sells handbags at $2,400 and found that a markdown to $1,600 actually reduced sales. ValueMart prices its kettles at $29.97. Which pairing correctly names each store's approach?","options":["Maison Lux: prestige; ValueMart: odd-even","Maison Lux: skimming; ValueMart: customary","Maison Lux: above-market; ValueMart: loss leader","Maison Lux: odd-even; ValueMart: prestige"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Prestige pricing keeps price high for status-conscious buyers, and lowering it can reduce demand (a backward-bending demand curve). $29.97 sits just under an even number to suggest a bargain: odd-even pricing. Skimming would be a high INITIAL price that is later lowered on purpose.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-021","concept":"c09-consumer-psychology","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"An Edmonton boutique hotel wants its signature suite to be seen as premium, not discount. Based on consumer psychology and price endings, which nightly price is the best choice?","options":["$399.99","$399.95","$398.95","$400"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Even prices suggest quality, while odd endings suggest a bargain. $400 supports a premium image; the other three are odd endings that signal a deal.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-022","concept":"c09-three-cs","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The three key considerations when studying pricing are customers, costs and _____.","options":["channels","communication","company","competition"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The 3 Cs of pricing are customers, costs and competition: 'your price' sits among your costs, competitors' prices and what customers want to pay. Channels, communication and company belong to other frameworks.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-023","concept":"c09-three-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"A pricing analyst at Maple Leaf Brews is told to 'cover the 3 Cs' before recommending a price for a six-pack. Which piece of information is NOT one of the 3 Cs?","options":["What customers are willing to pay for a six-pack","The brewery's costs to produce and market a six-pack","The prices rival craft brewers charge","The number of retail outlets carrying the beer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The 3 Cs are customers (what they want to pay), costs (yours) and competition (competitors' prices). Distribution coverage is a place decision, not one of the three key pricing considerations.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-024","concept":"c09-three-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before pricing its new meal kits, PrairieBox surveys 400 Saskatoon households about the most they would pay per week. Which of the 3 Cs is PrairieBox studying?","options":["Customers","Competition","Costs","Channels"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Asking what households would pay studies CUSTOMERS - what the customer wants to pay. Costs would be PrairieBox's own production and marketing costs; competition would be rival meal-kit prices.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-025","concept":"c09-three-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is it risky to set a price by looking only at costs, one of the 3 Cs?","options":["Costs never affect price in the long run","Cost-based prices are illegal under the Competition Act","Looking at costs makes demand perfectly elastic","The price may exceed what customers will pay or sit far from competitors' prices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Your price sits among your costs, competitors' prices and what customers want to pay. Costs tell you the floor, but a cost-only price ignores customers' willingness to pay and the competition. Costs do matter - in the long run they set a floor under price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-026","concept":"c09-three-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Rocky View Roofing's cost per roof is $7,800. Homeowner research shows most will pay at most $11,000, and two rivals charge about $9,500. Applying the 3 Cs, which price range is most defensible?","options":["Below $7,800, undercutting both rivals to win as many roofs as possible","Exactly $7,800, so that the price of each roof just equals its cost","Above $11,000, so that the high price signals superior quality","Between $7,800 and $11,000, positioned relative to the $9,500 rival price"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Costs set the lower limit in the long run ($7,800), customers' willingness to pay sets the upper limit ($11,000), and competitors' prices ($9,500) guide where to sit in between. Pricing below cost is not sustainable, and above $11,000 exceeds what customers will pay.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-027","concept":"c09-pricing-objectives","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ specify the role of price in an organization's marketing and strategic plans.","options":["Pricing objectives","Pricing approaches","Pricing constraints","Price adjustments"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pricing objectives specify the role of price in an organization's marketing and strategic plans (profit, sales, market share, volume, survival, social responsibility). Pricing constraints are the factors that LIMIT the range of prices a firm can set.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-028","concept":"c09-pricing-objectives","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a sharp downturn in Alberta's oil patch, Badlands Equipment Rentals cuts its rates to just cover its costs so it can keep operating until demand returns. Which pricing objective is it pursuing?","options":["Market share","Target return","Social responsibility","Survival"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Survival objectives aim to cover costs and stay in business in the short run, often during hard times. Market share would be about growing sales relative to total industry sales, not merely staying afloat.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-029","concept":"c09-pricing-objectives","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Canadian pharmaceutical firm prices its new malaria drug at cost in low-income countries, recognizing its obligations to patients and society. This reflects which pricing objective?","options":["Social responsibility","Volume","Survival","Current profit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Social responsibility objectives recognize obligations to customers and society - for example, pricing medicines affordably. Survival would mean pricing just to stay in business, which is not the motive here.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-030","concept":"c09-pricing-objectives","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Polar Pop's pricing team lists four items on a whiteboard. Which one is a pricing OBJECTIVE rather than a pricing constraint?","options":["Competitors' prices for similar soft drinks","The product's early stage in the product life cycle","The cost of producing and marketing each case","Growing Polar Pop's share of total industry sales from 8% to 12%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Market share (a firm's sales revenue relative to total industry sales) is a pricing objective - something price should achieve. Competitors' prices, costs and newness (PLC stage) are constraints that limit the range of prices.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-031","concept":"c09-pricing-objectives","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the pricing objectives identified in the chapter?","options":["Managing for long-run profit","Market share","Volume","Yield management"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Yield management is a demand-oriented pricing APPROACH used in Step 1, not an objective. The objectives are profit (long-run, current, target return), sales, market share, volume, survival and social responsibility.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-032","concept":"c09-pricing-constraints","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is TRUE about production cost as a pricing constraint?","options":["In the short run, a firm's price must cover all the costs of producing a product","Covering the firm's costs does not affect pricing the product at all","In the long run, a firm's costs set a floor under its price","In the long run, a firm's costs set a ceiling over its price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In the long run a firm's price must cover all the costs of producing and marketing a product or the firm will fail - so costs set a FLOOR under price. In the short run a firm may price below full cost (for example, to survive), and costs are a floor, not a ceiling.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-033","concept":"c09-pricing-constraints","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aurora Labs launches the first Canadian-made home air-quality sensor, with no direct substitutes yet, and prices it well above what it expects to charge in three years. Which pricing constraint gives Aurora this latitude?","options":["Newness of the product (stage in the product life cycle)","Cost of changing prices and the time period they apply","Pure competition in the home-sensor market","Single product vs product line considerations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newness of the product - its stage in the PLC - affects price: early in the life cycle, with few substitutes, a firm can charge more. Pure competition would do the opposite, forcing the firm to accept the market price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-034","concept":"c09-pricing-constraints","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Canada's national wireless market is dominated by three large carriers. When one cuts its unlimited-data price, the others match within days, and analysts warn of a price war. This type of competitive market is:","options":["oligopoly","monopolistic competition","pure monopoly","pure competition"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An oligopoly has a few sellers that watch and react to each other's prices, which can trigger price wars. Monopolistic competition has MANY sellers with differentiated products, so one firm's price cut does not force everyone to respond.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-035","concept":"c09-pricing-constraints","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which description fits monopolistic competition rather than pure competition?","options":["Thousands of canola growers sell an identical crop at the market price","Three airlines control most domestic routes and match each other's fares","One utility is the only electricity distributor in a city","Many independent coffee shops in Edmonton sell somewhat different drinks at a range of prices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Monopolistic competition = many sellers with differentiated products, so each has some pricing room. Pure competition (the canola growers) = many sellers with identical products, so the market sets the price. One seller is pure monopoly; a few sellers is oligopoly.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-036","concept":"c09-pricing-constraints","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Summit Razors sells a low-priced razor handle and high-margin replacement blades. The pricing manager wants to raise the handle price by 40%. Which pricing constraint should most concern her?","options":["Pure competition: the market sets the handle price","Newness of the product: the handle is in the introduction stage","Cost of production sets a ceiling on the handle price","Single product vs product line: the handle price affects blade sales"],"answer":3,"explanation":"When a firm sells a product line, pricing one item affects others - a pricier handle could reduce complementary blade sales. Costs set a floor, not a ceiling, and nothing in the scenario suggests a commodity market or a brand-new product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-037","concept":"c09-pricing-constraints","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a pricing constraint?","options":["Target return on investment","Demand for the product class, product and brand","Competitors' prices","Type of competitive market"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Target return is a pricing OBJECTIVE (under profit), and target ROI pricing is a profit-oriented approach. Demand, competitors' prices and the type of competitive market are constraints - factors that limit the range of prices.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-038","concept":"c09-pricing-constraints","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a _____, a single seller faces no close competitors and therefore has wide latitude in setting its price.","options":["pure monopoly","oligopoly","monopolistic competition","pure competition"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A pure monopoly has one seller, giving it the most pricing latitude of the four market types. At the other extreme, pure competition has many sellers of identical products that must accept the market price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-039","concept":"c09-demand-estimation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A graph relating the quantity sold to the price, showing how many units will be sold at each price, is called a:","options":["demand curve","break-even chart","total cost curve","product life cycle curve"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A demand curve relates quantity sold to price. A break-even chart plots total revenue against total cost to find the break-even point - it does not show how demand responds to price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-040","concept":"c09-demand-estimation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a major employer in an Alberta city announces layoffs, local car dealers sell fewer vehicles at every price even though sticker prices have not changed. Which demand factor shifted demand?","options":["Consumer tastes","Availability of substitutes","Price of the product itself","Consumer income"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Consumer income is a demand factor; when incomes fall, the whole demand curve shifts left (fewer units at every price). The product's own price is not a demand factor - a change in it moves buyers ALONG the curve, and prices did not change here.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-041","concept":"c09-demand-estimation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which event causes a movement ALONG Fizzco's demand curve rather than a shift of the curve?","options":["A health trend makes consumers prefer sparkling water to soda","A rival cola brand doubles its price","Consumer incomes rise across Canada","Fizzco cuts the price of its own soda from $2.00 to $1.50"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A change in the product's OWN price moves buyers along the demand curve. Changes in demand factors - tastes, the price or availability of substitutes (the rival cola) and consumer income - shift the whole curve.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-042","concept":"c09-demand-estimation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Coastal Kayak Rentals forecasts next summer's demand by studying its weekly rentals over the past eight summers and projecting the pattern forward. Which forecasting method is this?","options":["Time-series analysis","Multiple regression","Qualitative forecasting","Break-even analysis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Time-series analysis projects future demand from the pattern of past sales over time. Multiple regression would relate sales to several explanatory variables (such as weather, tourism and price) rather than to time alone.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-043","concept":"c09-demand-estimation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the chapter, why does creating the correct price begin the forecasting process?","options":["With a price known, the firm can estimate demand and translate it into estimated revenue","Once a price is set, demand no longer needs to be estimated or revised","Forecasting is only needed to calculate the firm's fixed costs for the year","Price determines the break-even point, which replaces the need for a forecast"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With a price in hand, the firm estimates how much customers will buy, considering its marketing efforts and competitors' efforts, then translates that into estimated revenue. Break-even analysis still depends on these estimates; it does not replace them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-044","concept":"c09-elasticity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Westcoast Ferries raises its fare by 10%, and the number of walk-on passengers falls by 4%. What is the price elasticity of demand, and is demand elastic or inelastic?","options":["2.5, elastic","2.5, inelastic","0.4, elastic","0.4, inelastic"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Price elasticity = % change in quantity demanded / % change in price = 4% / 10% = 0.4. Because 0.4 is less than 1, demand is inelastic (quantity is not very responsive to price). 2.5 comes from dividing the wrong way (10 / 4).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-045","concept":"c09-elasticity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Glow Candle Co. sells 1,000 candles a month at $20. When it lowers the price to $18, monthly sales rise to 1,300 candles. What happens to total revenue, and what does that say about demand?","options":["It rises by $3,400; demand is elastic","It falls by $2,000; demand is inelastic","It rises by $6,000; demand is elastic","It stays at $20,000; demand is inelastic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total revenue = P x Q. Before: $20 x 1,000 = $20,000. After: $18 x 1,300 = $23,400, an increase of $3,400. Quantity rose 30% while price fell 10% (elasticity = 3), so demand is elastic and the price cut raised total revenue.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-046","concept":"c09-elasticity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"When gasoline prices in Calgary jumped 15% last spring, the amount of fuel drivers bought barely changed, because most still had to drive to work. Demand for gasoline in this situation is best described as:","options":["elastic","backward-bending","price-sensitive","inelastic"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Inelastic demand means quantity is not very responsive to price - typical of necessities with few substitutes, such as gasoline. Elastic (price-sensitive) demand would show a large drop in quantity; a backward-bending curve describes prestige goods.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-047","concept":"c09-elasticity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A tobacco company faces highly inelastic demand for its cigarette brand. If its goal is to increase total revenue, what should it do with price?","options":["Lower price, because quantity will rise enough to raise revenue","Raise price, because quantity will fall only slightly and revenue will rise","Keep price fixed, because elasticity does not affect revenue","Lower price to the break-even point"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With inelastic demand, raising price increases total revenue because the percentage drop in quantity is smaller than the percentage rise in price. Lowering price works for ELASTIC demand, where the quantity gain outweighs the price cut.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-048","concept":"c09-elasticity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which product is most likely to have ELASTIC rather than inelastic demand?","options":["Insulin for people with diabetes","Home heating during a Winnipeg winter","One brand of potato chips with many close substitutes","Gasoline for commuters with no transit option"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Demand is elastic when there are many substitutes or the product is a luxury - buyers easily switch chip brands if one raises its price. Insulin, winter heating and commuting fuel are necessities with few substitutes, so their demand is inelastic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-049","concept":"c09-revenue-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Banff Trail Tours sells 3,200 guided hikes at $85 each this season. What is its total revenue?","options":["$27,200","$255,000","$272,000","$289,000"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Total revenue = unit price x quantity sold = $85 x 3,200 = $272,000. Revenue counts what comes in before any costs are subtracted; profit would require subtracting fixed and variable costs.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-050","concept":"c09-revenue-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Pie Co. sells 8,000 pies at $25 each. Its fixed costs are $60,000 and its unit variable cost is $12. Using the profit equation, what is its profit?","options":["$44,000","$104,000","$140,000","$200,000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Profit = (P x Q) - [FC + (UVC x Q)] = ($25 x 8,000) - [$60,000 + ($12 x 8,000)] = $200,000 - $156,000 = $44,000. $104,000 forgets the fixed costs and $140,000 forgets the variable costs.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-051","concept":"c09-revenue-profit","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The change in total revenue that results from selling one additional unit of a product is called _____.","options":["average revenue","marginal cost","unit variable cost","marginal revenue"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Marginal revenue is the change in total revenue from selling one more unit. Marginal cost is the change in total COST from producing one more unit - the same 'one more unit' idea, but on the cost side.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-052","concept":"c09-revenue-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"The owner of Bean There Cafe divides last month's total revenue of $36,000 by the 9,000 drinks sold and gets $4.00. What has she calculated?","options":["Marginal revenue","Average revenue","Unit variable cost","Break-even point"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Average revenue = total revenue / quantity sold, which equals the average price per unit ($36,000 / 9,000 = $4.00). Marginal revenue would be the extra revenue from selling just one more drink.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-053","concept":"c09-revenue-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes total revenue from profit?","options":["Total revenue is price times quantity; profit is total revenue minus fixed and variable costs","Total revenue subtracts variable costs; profit subtracts only fixed costs","Total revenue and profit are equal at the break-even point","Profit is price times quantity; total revenue is profit plus fixed costs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"TR = P x Q; Profit = TR - TC, where TC = fixed cost + variable cost. At the break-even point total revenue equals total COST (profit is zero) - not profit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-054","concept":"c09-cost-concepts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fresh Frame Studio pays $2,500 a month in rent and $4,000 a month in manager salaries, regardless of how many frames it makes. These are:","options":["variable costs","marginal costs","fixed costs","unit variable costs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fixed costs, such as rent and salaries, stay the same regardless of the quantity produced. Variable costs (materials, per-unit labour) rise and fall directly with the number of frames made.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-055","concept":"c09-cost-concepts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Lakeside Soap has fixed costs of $50,000 a year and a unit variable cost of $8. If it produces 5,000 bars, what is its total cost?","options":["$40,000","$50,000","$90,000","$400,000"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Total cost = fixed cost + (unit variable cost x quantity) = $50,000 + ($8 x 5,000) = $50,000 + $40,000 = $90,000. $40,000 is only the variable cost and $50,000 only the fixed cost.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-056","concept":"c09-cost-concepts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a Saskatoon furniture maker, which cost is VARIABLE rather than fixed?","options":["Lumber used in each table","Annual property insurance on the factory","The plant manager's salary","Monthly lease payments on the building"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Variable costs change directly with quantity - every extra table uses more lumber. Insurance, salaries and lease payments are fixed: they do not change with the number of tables produced.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-057","concept":"c09-cost-concepts","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ is calculated by dividing total variable cost by the quantity produced.","options":["Marginal cost","Unit variable cost","Average revenue","Fixed cost"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unit variable cost = variable cost / quantity - the variable cost per unit. Marginal cost is the change in total cost from producing ONE more unit, a different measure.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-058","concept":"c09-cost-concepts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about costs is NOT correct?","options":["Total cost equals fixed cost plus variable cost","Fixed costs rise as more units are produced","Variable costs change directly with the quantity produced","Unit variable cost equals variable cost divided by quantity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fixed costs, by definition, do NOT change with the quantity produced - rent is the same at 10 units or 10,000. The other three statements correctly describe total cost, variable cost and unit variable cost.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-059","concept":"c09-cost-concepts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hearth & Grain Bakery's total cost for the day rises from $5,000.00 to $5,003.20 when it bakes one more loaf of sourdough. The $3.20 is best described as the loaf's:","options":["fixed cost","marginal cost","average revenue","marginal revenue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Marginal cost is the change in total cost that results from producing one more unit ($5,003.20 - $5,000.00 = $3.20). Marginal revenue is the matching idea on the revenue side - the change in total revenue from SELLING one more unit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-060","concept":"c09-break-even","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Gallery North, a picture-frame store, has fixed costs of $32,000. It sells each frame for $120, and its unit variable cost is $40. What is its break-even point in frames?","options":["200 frames","267 frames","400 frames","800 frames"],"answer":2,"explanation":"BEP = FC / (P - UVC) = $32,000 / ($120 - $40) = $32,000 / $80 = 400 frames. 267 comes from dividing by the price alone, ignoring that each frame's $40 variable cost must be covered first.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-061","concept":"c09-break-even","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Chinook Bottle Co. sells insulated water bottles for $25. Its unit variable cost is $10 and its annual fixed costs are $90,000. How many bottles must it sell to break even?","options":["3,600","6,000","9,000","2,571"],"answer":1,"explanation":"BEP = $90,000 / ($25 - $10) = $90,000 / $15 = 6,000 bottles. 3,600 divides by price only and 9,000 divides by unit variable cost; the divisor must be the contribution per unit (P - UVC).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-062","concept":"c09-break-even","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Ridge Rider sells snowshoes at $30 with a unit variable cost of $14 and fixed costs of $48,000. If it raises the price to $38 with costs unchanged, how does its break-even point change?","options":["It falls from 3,000 to 2,000 units","It rises from 2,000 to 3,000 units","It falls from 1,600 to 1,263 units","It stays at 3,000 units"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Before: $48,000 / ($30 - $14) = 3,000 units. After: $48,000 / ($38 - $14) = 2,000 units. The higher price raises the contribution per unit from $16 to $24, so fewer units are needed to cover fixed costs. 1,600 and 1,263 wrongly divide by price alone.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-063","concept":"c09-break-even","also":["c09-revenue-profit"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Gallery North (fixed costs $32,000; price $120 per frame; unit variable cost $40) sells 600 frames this year. What is its profit?","options":["$16,000","$40,000","$48,000","$72,000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Profit = (P x Q) - [FC + (UVC x Q)] = ($120 x 600) - [$32,000 + ($40 x 600)] = $72,000 - $56,000 = $16,000. Equivalently, 200 frames beyond the 400-frame BEP x $80 contribution = $16,000. $48,000 forgets the fixed costs.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-064","concept":"c09-break-even","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"The owner of a new Edmonton bakery asks, 'How many loaves do I need to sell before I stop losing money?' Which technique answers this directly?","options":["Break-even analysis","Price elasticity analysis","Yield management","Time-series forecasting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Break-even analysis examines the relationship between total revenue and total cost to find the quantity at which TR = TC - where losses end and profit begins. Price elasticity tells how quantity responds to price, not how many units cover costs.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-065","concept":"c09-break-even","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"If a firm's fixed costs rise while its price and unit variable cost stay the same, its break-even point rises, because more units are needed to cover the larger fixed cost.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. BEP = FC / (P - UVC); fixed cost is the numerator, so raising it raises the BEP while the contribution per unit stays the same. For example, $32,000 / $80 = 400 frames becomes $40,000 / $80 = 500 frames.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-066","concept":"c09-break-even","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement about a break-even chart is NOT correct?","options":["The total cost line begins at the level of fixed costs","The area between the lines to the right of the break-even point represents profit","At the break-even point, total revenue equals total cost","The total revenue line begins at the level of fixed costs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Total revenue starts at zero (no sales, no revenue); it is the total COST line that starts at the fixed-cost level, because fixed costs exist even at zero output. Profit lies beyond the BEP, where TR exceeds TC.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-067","concept":"c09-four-approaches","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Kestrel Cycles sets its new e-bike's price mainly by checking what rival brands charge and pricing just below them. Which group of pricing approaches is Kestrel using?","options":["Demand-oriented","Cost-oriented","Profit-oriented","Competition-oriented"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Competition-oriented approaches focus on what competitors are doing rather than on demand, cost or profit; pricing just below rivals is below-market pricing. Demand-oriented approaches start from customers' tastes and preferences instead.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-068","concept":"c09-four-approaches","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a demand-oriented pricing approach?","options":["Bundle pricing","Odd-even pricing","Target return-on-sales pricing","Yield management pricing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Target return-on-sales pricing is PROFIT-oriented, along with target profit and target ROI. Bundle, odd-even and yield management are all demand-oriented, as are skimming, penetration, prestige and target pricing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-069","concept":"c09-four-approaches","also":["c09-target-pricing","c09-target-profit"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Target pricing and target profit pricing sound alike. Which statement correctly places them in Figure 9-2?","options":["Both are profit-oriented","Target pricing is demand-oriented; target profit pricing is profit-oriented","Target pricing is cost-oriented; target profit pricing is demand-oriented","Both are demand-oriented"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Target pricing starts with the price consumers will pay and works backward, so it is demand-oriented. Target profit pricing solves for the price that yields a specific dollar profit, so it is profit-oriented, along with target return on sales and target ROI.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-070","concept":"c09-four-approaches","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ approaches to pricing emphasize factors underlying expected customer tastes and preferences.","options":["Demand-oriented","Cost-oriented","Profit-oriented","Competition-oriented"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Demand-oriented approaches (skimming, penetration, prestige, odd-even, target, bundle, yield management) weigh customer tastes and preferences. Competition-oriented approaches instead focus on what competitors are doing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-071","concept":"c09-four-approaches","also":["c09-final-price-steps"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does Figure 9-2 describe its four groups of approaches as ways to select only an APPROXIMATE price level?","options":["Because the Competition Bureau must approve the final price","Because the list price, special adjustments and ongoing monitoring still refine the price","Because the approaches apply only to new products","Because costs cannot be known until the product is sold"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Selecting an approximate price level is only Step 1. The firm still sets the list or quoted price (Step 2), makes special adjustments such as discounts, allowances and geographic adjustments (Step 3), and monitors and adjusts prices (Step 4). No regulator approves ordinary prices.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-072","concept":"c09-skimming","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"The manufacturer of a new variety of fat-free ice cream needs to determine a pricing strategy for its new product. Which of the following conditions would suggest using a skimming pricing strategy for the tasty fat-free ice cream?","options":["The ice cream market is highly conservative","A large portion of the market has inelastic demand for fat-free ice cream, over a fairly broad range of prices","Economies of scale in production are substantial","Retailers are willing to pay for new brands of premium ice cream in an extremely overcrowded category"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Skimming works when enough customers are price-insensitive - inelastic demand over a broad range of prices - because they weigh the new product's price, quality and ability to satisfy their needs against substitutes. Substantial economies of scale favour PENETRATION pricing, since lower unit costs reward high volume.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-073","concept":"c09-skimming","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"H&G Co. Ltd. has used a high initial price for its newly launched cellphones. The company hopes to recover its research and development costs with this pricing. This is an example of a:","options":["price lining strategy","price skimming strategy","yield management pricing strategy","prestige pricing strategy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A high initial price used to help recover research and development costs is price skimming. Prestige pricing also uses a high price, but to attract status-conscious buyers permanently - not as a launch tactic for recovering R&D.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-074","concept":"c09-skimming","also":["c09-prestige"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario describes skimming rather than prestige pricing?","options":["A Swiss watch brand keeps prices high year after year because lower prices would hurt its exclusive image","A designer handbag maker found that cutting prices reduced sales","A gaming-console maker launches at $899, planning price cuts as competitors release rival consoles","A luxury vodka is priced well above other vodkas to appeal to status-conscious buyers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Skimming sets a high INITIAL price on a new product and lowers it over time as competitors enter. The other three keep prices high permanently to attract status-conscious buyers - prestige pricing, where cutting price can reduce demand.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-075","concept":"c09-skimming","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which condition does NOT favour a skimming strategy for a new product?","options":["The high price signals high quality to buyers","Competitors cannot enter quickly because of patents","Unit costs drop sharply as production volume grows","Enough buyers are relatively insensitive to price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When unit costs fall sharply with volume (economies of scale), a low price that builds volume pays off - a PENETRATION condition. Skimming favours price-insensitive early buyers, a quality signal and barriers that keep competitors out.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-076","concept":"c09-skimming","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"BioClear has a patented water filter that rivals cannot copy for several years. Research shows a sizable group of health-conscious buyers will pay almost any price, and production costs will not drop much with volume. Which initial pricing approach fits best?","options":["Penetration pricing","Skimming pricing","Loss-leader pricing","Customary pricing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"All the skimming conditions are present: price-insensitive buyers (an inelastic segment), a barrier to entry (the patent) and little cost advantage from volume. Penetration would suit price-sensitive buyers and strong economies of scale - neither is present.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-077","concept":"c09-penetration","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"When Pixel Play launched its new handheld game console, it priced it well below rival systems to appeal immediately to the mass market and build a large user base quickly. This is:","options":["skimming pricing","loss-leader pricing","penetration pricing","prestige pricing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Penetration pricing sets a low initial price on a new product to appeal immediately to the mass market (the instructor's example is the Nintendo Wii U). Loss-leader pricing cuts the price of an existing item to pull traffic toward other products - not a new-product launch strategy.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-078","concept":"c09-penetration","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which set of conditions makes penetration pricing most attractive?","options":["Price-insensitive buyers, high barriers to entry and a high price that signals quality","Price-sensitive buyers, unit costs that fall with volume, and a low price that discourages competitors","A product bought mainly as a status symbol, where a lower price would hurt demand","A fixed, perishable capacity, such as hotel rooms, that must be sold by a set date"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Penetration works when many buyers are price-sensitive (elastic demand), economies of scale lower unit costs as volume grows, and the low price discourages competitors. Price-insensitive buyers plus entry barriers describe skimming; a status symbol calls for prestige pricing; perishable capacity suits yield management.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-079","concept":"c09-penetration","also":["c09-loss-leader"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario is penetration pricing rather than loss-leader pricing?","options":["A new meal-kit company enters Ontario at $5 per meal, far below rivals, to build a subscriber base quickly","A Calgary supermarket sells butter at $2.99, below its usual price, to draw shoppers in","A hardware chain sells snow shovels below their customary price on the first snowy weekend to boost store traffic","An electronics store advertises a Black Friday TV doorbuster to fill the store"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Penetration is a low INITIAL price on a NEW product to win the mass market. The others cut prices on existing items to attract traffic in hopes shoppers buy other products - loss-leader pricing, a competition-oriented approach.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-080","concept":"c09-penetration","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"SnapCharge is launching a phone charger in a market full of price-sensitive buyers. Its factory's unit cost will drop sharply as volume rises, and it wants to discourage rivals from entering. What initial price should it set?","options":["A high initial price to recover R&D, lowered later","A low initial price to win the mass market quickly","A price ending in an even number to signal quality","A price set by tradition for the category"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Elastic demand, economies of scale and a desire to deter competitors are the classic penetration conditions, so a low initial price fits. A high initial price lowered later is skimming, which needs price-insensitive buyers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-081","concept":"c09-penetration","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a condition favouring penetration pricing?","options":["Many segments of the market are price-sensitive","Unit costs fall as production experience accumulates","A low price discourages competitors from entering","A large segment has inelastic demand over a broad range of prices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A large segment with inelastic demand is a SKIMMING condition - those buyers will pay a high initial price. Penetration needs price-sensitive (elastic) buyers, economies of scale or experience, and a low price that keeps competitors out.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-082","concept":"c09-prestige","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aurelia Perfumes cut the price of its signature fragrance from $240 to $150 and was shocked when sales dropped. Buyers had valued the high price as a mark of exclusivity. Which approach should Aurelia have stuck with?","options":["Penetration pricing","Prestige pricing","Odd-even pricing","Below-market pricing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prestige pricing sets a high price so status-conscious consumers will buy; for these goods, lowering the price can reduce demand. Penetration and below-market pricing rely on low prices to win volume - exactly what backfired.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-083","concept":"c09-prestige","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"For a prestige product, why can a price cut REDUCE the quantity demanded?","options":["Because demand for prestige goods is perfectly inelastic at every price","Because fixed costs rise when price falls, so the firm must cut output","Because competitors immediately match the lower price and take the sales","Because the demand curve bends backward: a lower price signals less status"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Prestige goods have a demand curve that bends backward over part of its range: a lower price signals less status, so fewer people want the product. Inelastic demand would mean quantity barely changes - not that it falls when price falls.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-084","concept":"c09-prestige","also":["c09-customary-market"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Luxe Motors prices its sports cars 20% above competitors' average price, deliberately using that market price as its benchmark. Which approach is this, as opposed to prestige pricing?","options":["Above-market pricing (competition-oriented)","Skimming pricing (demand-oriented)","Target pricing (demand-oriented)","Cost-plus pricing (cost-oriented)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Using competitors' market price as a benchmark and pricing above it is above-market pricing, a competition-oriented approach (the instructor's example is the iPhone 17). Prestige pricing is demand-oriented: the high price is set to attract status-conscious buyers, not measured against rivals' prices.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-085","concept":"c09-prestige","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ pricing involves setting a high price so that quality- or status-conscious consumers will be attracted to the product and buy it.","options":["Skimming","Prestige","Above-market","Target"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is the definition of prestige pricing. Skimming also uses a high price, but only initially for a new product, typically to recover R&D before the price is lowered over time.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-086","concept":"c09-prestige","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prestige pricing sets a high initial price that the firm plans to lower as competitors enter the market.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. A high initial price planned to fall as competitors enter describes SKIMMING. Prestige pricing keeps the price high permanently because the high price is part of the product's status appeal; lowering it can actually reduce demand.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-087","concept":"c09-target-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Deliberately adjusting the composition and features of a product to achieve the price consumers are willing to pay is called:","options":["target profit pricing","cost-plus pricing","standard markup pricing","target pricing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Target pricing estimates the price consumers will pay, then works backward to design and cost the product. Target profit pricing instead solves the profit equation for the price that yields a specific dollar profit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-088","concept":"c09-target-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northwind Furniture learns that university students will pay about $199 for a desk. Its engineers then choose particleboard, a flat-pack design and fewer drawers so the desk can be made for $110 and still sell profitably at $199. This is:","options":["target pricing","target return-on-sales pricing","penetration pricing","cost-plus pricing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Target pricing starts with the price consumers are willing to pay and works backward, adjusting the product's composition and features to hit the needed cost. Cost-plus does the reverse - it starts with the cost and adds an amount on top.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-089","concept":"c09-target-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes target pricing from target profit pricing?","options":["Target pricing adds a fixed percentage to cost; target profit pricing benchmarks against competitors","They are two names for the same profit-oriented approach","Target pricing starts from what consumers will pay and designs backward; target profit pricing solves for the price that yields a set dollar profit","Target pricing is profit-oriented; target profit pricing is demand-oriented"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Target pricing is demand-oriented - the consumer's acceptable price comes first and the product is engineered to fit. Target profit pricing is profit-oriented - it plugs a dollar profit goal, costs and volume into the profit equation and solves for price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-090","concept":"c09-target-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In target pricing, what is the FIRST step?","options":["Total the product's costs and add a markup","Estimate the price consumers are willing to pay","Set a dollar profit target","Match the competitors' market price"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Target pricing begins by estimating the price consumers will pay, then works backward through the needed margins to the cost the product must be designed to. Totalling costs first is the cost-oriented logic (standard markup, cost-plus).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-091","concept":"c09-target-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT characteristic of target pricing?","options":["It is a demand-oriented approach","The product's features may be changed to hit a cost","It begins with the firm's total unit cost and adds a set amount","It starts with an estimate of what consumers will pay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Beginning with total unit cost and adding a set amount is cost-plus pricing (cost-oriented). Target pricing begins with consumers' acceptable price and adjusts the product to meet a cost that makes that price work.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-092","concept":"c09-bundle","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Maple Fibre offers home internet, TV and a mobile line for $120 a month, compared with $155 if bought separately. This is:","options":["yield management pricing","bundle pricing","a cumulative quantity discount","customary pricing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bundle pricing markets two or more products in a single package price. A quantity discount rewards buying MORE of the same product, not combining different products.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-093","concept":"c09-bundle","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"Why do fast-food chains use bundle pricing for value meals?","options":["Buyers see the package as a better deal than buying items separately, and the chain sells more items per order","It lets them charge different prices to different customers for the same item","It is required by the Competition Act for restaurants that sell combo meals","It allows them to recover the R&D costs of new menu items quickly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bundle pricing gives buyers perceived savings on a package and lets the seller move more products per sale. Charging different buyers different prices for the same item is a flexible-price policy, not bundling.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-094","concept":"c09-bundle","also":["c09-yield-management"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is bundle pricing rather than yield management?","options":["An airline charges $129 for a Tuesday seat and $389 for a Friday seat on the same route","A ski resort sells a lift ticket, rental skis and a lesson together for one price","A hotel raises room rates as occupancy for a weekend climbs","A theatre charges more for seats to a nearly sold-out Saturday show"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bundle pricing combines two or more products (lift ticket, rentals, lesson) into one package price. The other three vary the price of the same fixed capacity by date or demand - yield management.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-095","concept":"c09-bundle","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ pricing is the marketing of two or more products in a single package price.","options":["Bundle","Customary","Odd-even","Loss-leader"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bundle pricing combines products into one package price (e.g., a value meal). Loss-leader pricing, by contrast, prices ONE item below its customary price to attract traffic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-096","concept":"c09-bundle","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of bundle pricing?","options":["A car dealer includes winter tires, mats and remote start in one package price","A cafe sells a coffee-and-muffin combo for $5","A grocer gives 10% off any single order over $200","A software firm sells three apps together for one suite price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ten percent off a large single order is a noncumulative quantity DISCOUNT, a Step 3 adjustment. The other three sell two or more products together for a single package price - bundle pricing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-097","concept":"c09-yield-management","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peak View Hotel in Banff charges $189 for a standard room on a quiet November Tuesday and $549 for the same room on a ski-season Saturday, adjusting rates to earn the most from its 120 rooms. This is:","options":["skimming pricing","yield management pricing","prestige pricing","bundle pricing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Yield management charges different prices to maximize revenue for a set amount of capacity at a given time - the 120 rooms are fixed and an unsold night is lost forever. Skimming is a high initial price on a new product that falls over time, not a rate that moves with demand for fixed capacity.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-098","concept":"c09-yield-management","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Yield management pricing is the marketing of two or more products in a single package price.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. That describes bundle pricing. Yield management pricing is charging different prices to maximize revenue for a set amount of capacity at any given time, as airlines and hotels do.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-099","concept":"c09-yield-management","also":["c09-one-flexible"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario is yield management pricing, rather than flexible pricing based on the individual buyer?","options":["A transit system charges seniors and students lower fares than adults","A car dealer negotiates a different price with each buyer","An airline raises fares on a flight as its remaining seats fill up","A furniture store lets salespeople haggle with customers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Yield management varies price with demand for a FIXED capacity at a given time (the flight's remaining seats). The others vary price by the individual BUYER - age-based fares and haggling are flexible-price policies.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-100","concept":"c09-yield-management","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is yield management especially suited to airlines and hotels?","options":["Their capacity is fixed, and a seat or room left unsold on a given date can never be sold later","Their products are commodities sold in pure competition at a market price","Their costs are almost entirely variable and rise with each extra passenger or guest","Customary prices in these industries cannot be changed without losing customers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Yield management maximizes revenue from a set amount of capacity at a given time; an empty seat after departure is revenue lost forever, so varying prices to fill capacity pays off. These are differentiated services, not commodities, and their costs are largely fixed.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-101","concept":"c09-yield-management","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"For which business would yield management pricing be LEAST useful?","options":["A Calgary concert venue with 2,000 seats per show","A ferry with a fixed number of car spaces per sailing","A canned-soup manufacturer that can store unsold inventory","A Whistler ski lodge with 60 rooms"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Yield management fits fixed, time-bound capacity that perishes if unsold - seats, car spaces and rooms. A soup maker can store unsold cans and sell them later, so there is no perishable capacity to manage.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-102","concept":"c09-markup","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nature's Taste, a manufacturer of floral jellies made from pansies, honeysuckle, and wisteria, prices its jellies by adding 30 percent to the cost of everything that goes into making the jellies including salaries, jars, sugar, and pectin. Which of the following pricing methods is being used here?","options":["Target return-on-sales pricing","Flexible pricing","Yield management pricing","Standard markup pricing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Adding a set percentage (30%) to cost is standard markup pricing; manufacturers commonly express markup as a percentage of cost - (price - cost) / cost. Target return-on-sales pricing would set price to earn profit equal to a percentage of SALES, not cost.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-103","concept":"c09-markup","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Trailhead Outfitters sells a jacket for $75 that cost it $60. Expressed as a percentage of the SELLING PRICE, what is the markup?","options":["15%","20%","25%","80%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Markup on price = (price - cost) / price = ($75 - $60) / $75 = $15 / $75 = 20%. 25% is the markup on COST ($15 / $60), and 80% is cost as a percentage of price (Figure 9-3).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-104","concept":"c09-markup","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Quebec maple-syrup maker's cost to produce a case is $50, and it sells the case to distributors for $80. Expressed as a percentage of COST, what is the markup?","options":["37.5%","30%","160%","60%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Markup on cost = (price - cost) / cost = ($80 - $50) / $50 = $30 / $50 = 60%. 37.5% is the markup on PRICE ($30 / $80) - the base matters. Manufacturers often express markup on cost; retailers on price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-105","concept":"c09-markup","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Bloom & Co., a Winnipeg flower shop, buys a bouquet for $30 and wants a 40% markup on the SELLING PRICE. What should the selling price be?","options":["$42.00","$50.00","$75.00","$48.00"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When markup is on price, price is 100% and cost is 100% - 40% = 60% of price. Price = $30 / 0.60 = $50.00 (check: $20 markup / $50 = 40%). $42.00 applies 40% to COST ($30 x 1.4), which is only a 28.6% markup on price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-106","concept":"c09-markup","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A manufacturer uses a 50% markup on cost. A retail buyer asks what that markup equals when expressed as a percentage of the selling price. What is the correct answer?","options":["50.0%","66.7%","33.3%","25.0%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Assume cost = $100. A 50% markup on cost adds $50, so price = $150. Markup on price = $50 / $150 = 33.3% (formula: 0.50 / (1 + 0.50)). 66.7% is cost as a share of price ($100 / $150), not the markup, and 50.0% simply keeps cost as the base instead of switching to price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-107","concept":"c09-markup","also":["c09-cost-plus"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which situation is standard markup pricing rather than cost-plus pricing?","options":["A contractor bills a city for a library's total construction cost plus a $250,000 fee","A consulting firm charges a government department its actual costs plus 10% on a one-time project","A Calgary drugstore adds the same 35% to the cost of every item in its cosmetics department","A defence supplier is paid its audited costs plus a fixed fee for a custom radar system"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Standard markup adds a fixed percentage to the cost of ALL items in a product class - typical of retailers. The other three total the cost of a specific job or contract and add a specific amount or percentage: cost-plus pricing, common in construction and government contracts.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-108","concept":"c09-markup","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When markup is expressed relative to selling price, the selling price is always treated as 100 percent.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. In Figure 9-3 the $75 selling price = 100%, the $60 cost = 80% and the $15 markup = 20% of price. When markup is relative to cost, cost becomes the 100% base instead, so the same $15 is 25%.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-109","concept":"c09-cost-plus","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ironbridge Construction agrees to build a new fire hall for the City of Lethbridge. The city will pay Ironbridge's total project costs, whatever they turn out to be, plus a fixed fee of $300,000. This pricing method is:","options":["standard markup pricing","target profit pricing","cost-plus pricing","customary pricing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cost-plus pricing sums the total cost of providing the product or service and adds a specific amount - common in construction and government contracts. Standard markup applies the same percentage to the cost of all items in a product class, as retailers do.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-110","concept":"c09-cost-plus","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Polaris Engineering will design a custom water-treatment system for an Alberta town on a cost-plus basis: total costs plus 8%. If the project's total costs come to $2,400,000, what will the town pay?","options":["$2,208,000","$2,592,000","$2,608,000","$2,400,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cost-plus = total cost + the specified percentage of cost = $2,400,000 + (0.08 x $2,400,000) = $2,400,000 + $192,000 = $2,592,000. $2,208,000 subtracts the 8% instead of adding it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-111","concept":"c09-cost-plus","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cost-plus pricing and standard markup pricing both start with cost. What best distinguishes cost-plus pricing?","options":["It adds a specific amount to the total cost of a particular job or contract; standard markup adds one percentage to a whole product class","It starts with consumer demand, while standard markup starts with cost","It is profit-oriented, while standard markup is cost-oriented","It is used mainly by grocery retailers, while standard markup is used mainly in government contracts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both are cost-oriented, but cost-plus totals the cost of a specific job (construction, government contracts, services) and adds a specific amount, while standard markup adds a fixed percentage to every item in a class (retailers, manufacturers). Claiming grocery retailers rely on cost-plus reverses who typically uses which approach.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-112","concept":"c09-cost-plus","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ pricing involves summing the total unit cost of providing a product or service and adding a specific amount to the cost to arrive at a price.","options":["Target profit","Standard markup","Cost-plus","Loss-leader"],"answer":2,"explanation":"That is cost-plus pricing - total unit cost plus a specific amount. Standard markup is similar but applies a fixed percentage to the cost of all items in a product class rather than to a specific job.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-113","concept":"c09-cost-plus","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cost-plus pricing would be LEAST likely to be used by which organization?","options":["A construction firm building a hospital wing","An engineering firm on a custom government contract","A grocery chain pricing 30,000 everyday items","A consulting firm billing a one-time project"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A grocer pricing thousands of items would use standard markup (a set percentage on the cost of all items in a class). Cost-plus is common in construction, government contracts and services, where each job's total cost is summed and a specific amount added.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-114","concept":"c09-target-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Charlie Tuna Inc. sells five-pound packages of ocean tuna. Its variable cost per package is $40, and its fixed cost is $410,000. It wants a target profit of $42,000 on a volume of 21,000 packages. What should it charge for a five-pound package of tuna?","options":["$50","$40","$61.52","$48.09"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Target profit pricing: Profit = (P x Q) - [FC + (UVC x Q)]. $42,000 = (P x 21,000) - [$410,000 + ($40 x 21,000)], so 21,000P = $42,000 + $410,000 + $840,000 = $1,292,000 and P = $1,292,000 / 21,000 = $61.52 (rounded to the cent). $40 is only the variable cost, which would cover neither fixed costs nor profit.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-115","concept":"c09-target-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Fjord Paddles has fixed costs of $150,000 and a unit variable cost of $12 per paddle. It expects to sell 20,000 paddles and wants a target profit of $50,000. What price should it set?","options":["$19.50","$14.50","$22.00","$10.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"P = (Target profit + FC + UVC x Q) / Q = ($50,000 + $150,000 + $12 x 20,000) / 20,000 = $440,000 / 20,000 = $22.00. $19.50 covers costs but leaves out the $50,000 profit; $10.00 forgets the variable cost per paddle.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-116","concept":"c09-target-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ridgeline Brewing's owners tell the marketing manager: 'Price our new lager so we make exactly $250,000 in profit this year on the volume we expect to sell.' Which approach is being requested?","options":["Target return-on-sales pricing","Target profit pricing","Target pricing","Standard markup pricing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Setting an annual target of a specific DOLLAR amount of profit is target profit pricing. Target return on sales would express the goal as a percentage of sales revenue, and target pricing starts from the price consumers will pay.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-117","concept":"c09-target-profit","also":["c09-target-return"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which goal calls for target profit pricing rather than target return-on-investment pricing?","options":["Earn 15% on the $2 million invested in the new plant","Earn $180,000 in profit this year","Earn profit equal to 10% of sales revenue","Earn a 25% markup on cost on every unit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A specific dollar amount ($180,000) is a target profit goal. A percentage of investment is target ROI, a percentage of sales is target return on sales, and a percentage added to cost is standard markup.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-118","concept":"c09-target-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Charlie Tuna calculated a price of $61.52 assuming it would sell 21,000 packages (fixed cost $410,000; variable cost $40 per package). If it actually sells only 15,000 packages at that price, what happens and why?","options":["It still earns $42,000, because the price already includes the target profit","It misses the target and loses money, because the price depended on an accurate demand estimate to cover fixed costs","It earns more than $42,000, because variable costs fall with lower volume","Its fixed costs fall in proportion to the lower volume, so profit is unchanged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Profit-oriented approaches depend on accurate estimates of demand. At 15,000 packages: TR = $61.52 x 15,000 = $922,800; TC = $410,000 + ($40 x 15,000) = $1,010,000, a loss of $87,200. Fixed costs do not shrink with volume, so the shortfall hits profit directly.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-119","concept":"c09-target-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cascade Plastics invested $4 million in a new moulding line. Head office tells the marketing team to set prices that will earn an annual profit equal to 12% of that investment. Which approach is this?","options":["Target profit pricing","Target return-on-sales pricing","Target return-on-investment pricing","Cost-plus pricing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Setting price to achieve an annual target percentage return on investment is target ROI pricing. Target return on sales would base the percentage on sales revenue, and target profit pricing names a specific dollar amount.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-120","concept":"c09-target-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Harbour Hats wants a profit equal to 20% of its sales revenue. Its fixed costs are $40,000, its unit variable cost is $30, and it expects to sell 2,000 hats. What price achieves this target return on sales?","options":["$50.00","$60.00","$62.50","$70.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Profit = 20% of TR, so (P x 2,000) - ($40,000 + $30 x 2,000) = 0.20 x P x 2,000. Then 2,000P - 400P = $100,000, so P = $100,000 / 1,600 = $62.50. Check: TR $125,000 - TC $100,000 = $25,000 = 20% of sales. $60.00 adds 20% to the $50 average unit COST ($100,000 / 2,000), which earns only $20,000 profit - 16.7% of sales.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-121","concept":"c09-target-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kodiak Tools invested $500,000 in new equipment and wants a 12% annual return on that investment. Its fixed costs are $100,000, its unit variable cost is $20, and expected volume is 10,000 units. What price should it charge?","options":["$26.00","$30.00","$36.00","$86.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Target profit = 12% x $500,000 = $60,000. P = ($60,000 + $100,000 + $20 x 10,000) / 10,000 = $360,000 / 10,000 = $36.00. $30.00 only breaks even, and $86.00 wrongly adds the whole investment as if it were a cost to recover this year.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-122","concept":"c09-target-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the key difference between target return-on-sales pricing and target return-on-investment pricing?","options":["Return on sales sets profit as a percentage of sales revenue; ROI sets profit as a percentage of the money invested","Return on sales is demand-oriented, while ROI is a cost-oriented approach to pricing","Return on sales names a specific dollar profit, while ROI uses a percentage of investment","Return on sales is used only by manufacturers, while ROI is used only by retailers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both are profit-oriented. Target return on sales expresses the profit goal as a percentage of sales volume (revenue); target ROI expresses it as a percentage of investment. A specific dollar profit is target profit pricing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-123","concept":"c09-target-return","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Setting a price to achieve a profit that is a specified percentage of the sales volume is called _____ pricing.","options":["target profit","target return-on-investment","standard markup","target return-on-sales"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Target return-on-sales pricing ties profit to a percentage of sales. Standard markup also uses a percentage, but it is added to COST (a cost-oriented approach) rather than being a profit target measured against sales.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-124","concept":"c09-customary-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"SweetSpot Vending has charged $2.00 for its chocolate bars for years, because that price is set by tradition and is what vending-machine customers expect to pay. This is:","options":["customary pricing","odd-even pricing","loss-leader pricing","prestige pricing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Customary pricing sets a price based on tradition, a standardized channel of distribution or other competitive factors - vending items and candy bars are classic examples. Loss-leader pricing would deliberately price BELOW the customary price to pull traffic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-125","concept":"c09-customary-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Zenith Audio wants its new headphones positioned as the premium choice. Its research shows competitors cluster around $250. Using a competition-oriented approach, what should Zenith do?","options":["Price at $199 as a loss leader","Price above the $250 market price, using rivals' prices as the benchmark","Add a standard 30% to cost","Set the price to earn a 15% return on investment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Above-market pricing uses the market price as a benchmark and deliberately prices above it to signal a premium position (the instructor's example is the iPhone 17). Standard markup and target ROI ignore competitors' prices; they are cost- and profit-oriented.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-126","concept":"c09-customary-market","also":["c09-loss-leader"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which retailer is using below-market pricing rather than loss-leader pricing?","options":["A grocer sells eggs at a deep discount for one weekend to draw shoppers into the store","A discount chain sets everyday prices about 5% under competitors' prices across its whole store","An electronics store advertises a Black Friday TV far below its usual price","A pharmacy features milk below its customary price to build traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Below-market pricing is an ongoing positioning choice that uses competitors' market price as the benchmark (Walmart-style). The other three temporarily price a specific item below its customary price to attract traffic for other purchases - loss leaders.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-127","concept":"c09-customary-market","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Above-, at- or below-market pricing uses the _____ as the benchmark for setting a price.","options":["firm's total unit cost","market price set by competitors","price consumers are willing to pay","firm's target profit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Above-, at- or below-market pricing is competition-oriented: the 'market price' (what competitors charge) is the benchmark. Starting from consumers' willingness to pay would be target pricing, and starting from cost would be a cost-oriented approach.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-128","concept":"c09-customary-market","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a competition-oriented pricing approach?","options":["Customary pricing","Loss-leader pricing","Prestige pricing","At-market pricing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prestige pricing is DEMAND-oriented - it targets status-conscious consumers. Customary, above/at/below-market and loss-leader pricing are the competition-oriented approaches, which focus on what competitors are doing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-129","concept":"c09-loss-leader","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Every Thursday, Bow Valley Foods advertises a 4 L jug of milk for $2.99, well below its customary $5.79, expecting shoppers who come for the milk to fill their carts with regularly priced items. This is:","options":["penetration pricing","loss-leader pricing","seasonal discount","customary pricing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Loss-leader pricing deliberately sells a product below its customary price to attract attention and traffic, in hopes shoppers buy other products. Penetration pricing applies to a NEW product's low introductory price, not to a staple like milk.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-130","concept":"c09-loss-leader","also":["c09-legal"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which of the following is loss-leader pricing rather than predatory pricing?","options":["A big-box chain prices far below cost near a new independent rival until the rival closes, then raises prices","A Saskatoon grocer sells turkeys below their customary price before Thanksgiving to draw shoppers who will buy side dishes too","A dominant airline floods a new competitor's routes with below-cost fares to force it out","A cement maker undercuts a small rival's prices until the rival leaves the region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Loss-leader pricing prices one item below its customary price to attract shoppers who will buy other products - a legal competition-oriented approach. The other three set very low prices to drive competitors out of business: predatory pricing, restricted by the Competition Act.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-131","concept":"c09-loss-leader","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the main purpose of loss-leader pricing?","options":["To recover R&D costs quickly","To drive competitors out of business permanently","To reward buyers for paying their bills promptly","To attract store traffic in hopes customers buy other products as well"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Loss leaders draw attention and traffic; the retailer profits on the other items shoppers buy. Driving competitors out of business is predatory pricing, recovering R&D is skimming, and rewarding prompt payment is a cash discount.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-132","concept":"c09-loss-leader","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Deliberately selling a product below its customary price to attract attention to it is called _____ pricing.","options":["penetration","customary","loss-leader","below-market"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Loss-leader pricing sells an item below its customary price to attract attention and traffic. Below-market pricing is an ongoing, store-wide positioning against competitors' prices, not a single item used to lure shoppers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-133","concept":"c09-loss-leader","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about loss-leader pricing is NOT correct?","options":["It is a competition-oriented approach","Milk and eggs are common loss leaders","It is used to launch new products with a low introductory price","The retailer hopes shoppers will buy other products too"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A low introductory price on a new product is PENETRATION pricing. Loss leaders are usually familiar items (milk, eggs, Black Friday TVs) priced below their customary price to drive traffic - a competition-oriented approach.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-134","concept":"c09-cost-vs-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"In value-based pricing (Nagle/Holden), what is the FIRST element in the sequence?","options":["Product","Cost","Customers","Price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Value-based pricing runs Customers -> Value -> Price -> Cost -> Product, so it starts with customers. Cost-based pricing is the reverse and starts with the product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-135","concept":"c09-cost-vs-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In cost-based pricing, which element comes immediately AFTER price?","options":["Cost","Value","Customers","Product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cost-based pricing runs Product -> Cost -> Price -> Value -> Customers: after setting the price, the firm must convince buyers of the value. In value-based pricing it is the reverse - value comes immediately BEFORE price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-136","concept":"c09-cost-vs-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peak Tech designs a new trail camera, adds up its manufacturing and marketing costs, sets a price that covers them plus a margin, and then asks its ad agency to convince hunters the camera is worth it. Which pricing logic is this?","options":["Value-based pricing","Target pricing","Cost-based pricing","Yield management pricing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cost-based pricing follows Product -> Cost -> Price -> Value -> Customers: the product and costs come first, and value must be 'sold' afterward. Value-based (and target) pricing would start with what customers value and will pay.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-137","concept":"c09-cost-vs-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts cost-based and value-based pricing?","options":["Cost-based starts with the product and ends with customers; value-based starts with customers and ends with the product","Cost-based starts with customers and ends with the product; value-based starts with the product","Both sequences start with cost and set price second, but they end at different points","Value-based pricing ignores costs entirely and sets price from customer value alone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The two sequences are mirror images: cost-based = Product, Cost, Price, Value, Customers; value-based = Customers, Value, Price, Cost, Product. Value-based pricing does not ignore costs - cost comes after price, as the amount the firm can spend to deliver that value.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-138","concept":"c09-cost-vs-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does value-based pricing begin with customers rather than with the product?","options":["So the firm can guarantee a fixed margin over its total unit cost on every sale","Because customers determine the firm's fixed costs and therefore its price floor","So the price reflects the value customers perceive, with costs and the product then designed to fit that price","So the firm never needs to consider competitors' prices when it sets a price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pricing is the attempt to capture the value the marketing mix creates. Starting with customers and value means the price reflects what buyers perceive, and costs and the product are then held to what that price allows. Guaranteeing a margin over unit cost is the cost-based logic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-139","concept":"c09-final-price-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In setting a final price, which step comes immediately after 'set the list or quoted price'?","options":["Select an approximate price level","Make special adjustments to the list or quoted price","Monitor and adjust prices","Identify pricing constraints"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The four steps are: (1) select an approximate price level, (2) set the list or quoted price, (3) make special adjustments - discounts, allowances, geographic adjustments, (4) monitor and adjust prices. Identifying constraints happens earlier in the pricing process, not within these four steps.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-140","concept":"c09-final-price-steps","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"The first step in setting a final price is to set the list or quoted price.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Step 1 is to select an approximate price level using one of the four approach buckets (demand-, cost-, profit- or competition-oriented). Setting the list or quoted price is Step 2.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-141","concept":"c09-final-price-steps","also":["c09-discounts-allowances"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Having already set a list price, Prairie Paint's sales manager now offers retailers 2/10 net 30 payment terms, a discount for orders placed before spring, and free freight to Saskatchewan stores. Which step of setting a final price is he working on?","options":["Step 1: Select an approximate price level","Step 2: Set the list or quoted price","Step 3: Make special adjustments to the list or quoted price","Step 4: Monitor and adjust prices"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Discounts (cash, seasonal) and geographic adjustments (freight) are special adjustments made in Step 3. Step 2 is where the list price and the one-price vs flexible-price policy are set.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-142","concept":"c09-final-price-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four steps in setting a final price?","options":["Identify pricing objectives","Monitor and adjust prices","Set the list or quoted price","Make special adjustments to the list or quoted price"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Identifying pricing objectives (and constraints) comes earlier in the pricing process. The four final-price steps are: select an approximate price level, set the list or quoted price, make special adjustments, and monitor and adjust prices.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-143","concept":"c09-final-price-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Six months after launching its fitness app at $12.99 a month, Pulse Fitness sees subscriptions slip after a rival cuts its price to $8.99. Which step of setting a final price should Pulse now carry out?","options":["Select an approximate price level for the first time","Monitor and adjust prices","Choose a geographic adjustment","Calculate a cash discount"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Step 4 - monitor and adjust prices - responds to changes in competition, demand and costs after launch. Pricing is not 'set and forget'; Step 1 was completed at launch.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-144","concept":"c09-one-flexible","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"City transit services often offer different riders a unique price to ride based on age, employment, etc. This is an example of a _____ policy.","options":["penetration strategy","flexible price","one-price policy","bundle-pricing policy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A flexible-price policy sets different prices for products and services depending on individual buyers and purchase situations - here, riders' age or employment. A one-price policy would charge every rider the same fare.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-145","concept":"c09-one-flexible","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"At Clearwater Motors, every customer pays the posted sticker price - no haggling and no special deals - whether they are a first-time buyer or a returning customer. This is a:","options":["flexible-price policy","yield management approach","one-price policy","form of price discrimination"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A one-price policy sets one price for all buyers of a product (no-haggle car brands and Walmart are examples). A flexible-price policy would let the price vary by buyer or situation, as in traditional dealer negotiation.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-146","concept":"c09-one-flexible","also":["c09-legal"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which situation reflects a legal flexible-price policy rather than illegal price discrimination?","options":["A manufacturer charges a large chain less than competing independent retailers for identical quantities, lessening competition","A Calgary museum charges students, seniors and adults different admission prices","A supplier secretly gives one distributor lower prices than its competing distributors for the same volume","A wholesaler charges two competing grocers different prices for the same order size, harming the smaller one"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Charging consumers different prices based on age or status is a legal flexible-price policy. Price discrimination involves charging COMPETING business buyers different prices for like quantities in a way that lessens competition - restricted under the Competition Act.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-147","concept":"c09-one-flexible","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"When a big concert ends in downtown Edmonton, a rideshare app's fares jump to 2.5 times normal for 30 minutes. What explains this dynamic pricing?","options":["Prices are adjusted in real time based on demand","The app is using a one-price policy","The fare includes a uniform delivered charge","The app is practising customary pricing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dynamic pricing adjusts prices in real time based on demand - like Uber surge pricing - and is a form of flexible pricing. A one-price policy would keep the fare the same regardless of demand, and customary pricing keeps a traditional price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-148","concept":"c09-one-flexible","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ is the practice of adjusting prices in real time based on changes in demand.","options":["Customary pricing","Cost-plus pricing","Standard markup pricing","Dynamic pricing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Dynamic pricing adjusts prices in real time as demand changes (for example, Uber surge pricing); it is a form of flexible pricing. Customary pricing does the opposite - it keeps a price fixed by tradition.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-149","concept":"c09-discounts-allowances","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the spring and early summer, before Canada Day, Molson Coors offers retailers a discount on Molson Canadian to see their product lead long-weekend sales. This is known as a:","options":["seasonal discount","cash discount","pre-buy discount","functional discount"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Seasonal discounts encourage buyers to buy an item or stock inventory earlier than their normal demand would require - retailers stock up before the Canada Day long weekend. A functional (trade) discount rewards resellers for performing marketing functions, not for timing.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-150","concept":"c09-discounts-allowances","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northern Supply invoices a Kelowna restaurant $5,000 on terms of 2/10 net 30. If the restaurant pays on day 8, how much does it pay?","options":["$4,000","$4,900","$5,000","$4,980"],"answer":1,"explanation":"2/10 net 30 means 2% off if paid within 10 days; otherwise the full amount is due within 30 days. Paying on day 8 qualifies: $5,000 - (0.02 x $5,000) = $5,000 - $100 = $4,900. The full $5,000 applies only if it pays after day 10.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-151","concept":"c09-discounts-allowances","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A manufacturer quotes a list price of $200 with a chain trade (functional) discount of 30/10/5 to wholesalers. What does a wholesaler pay?","options":["$110.00","$126.00","$119.70","$140.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chain discounts are applied one after another: $200 x 0.70 = $140; $140 x 0.90 = $126; $126 x 0.95 = $119.70. Adding the discounts together (45% off = $110) is the classic error - each discount applies to the already-reduced price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-152","concept":"c09-discounts-allowances","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Red Deer cafe chain earns larger price reductions from its coffee roaster as its total purchases over the year add up, regardless of the size of any single order. This is a:","options":["noncumulative quantity discount","cumulative quantity discount","seasonal discount","cash discount"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cumulative quantity discounts apply to total purchases over a period of time and encourage repeat buying and loyalty. Noncumulative quantity discounts are based on the size of a SINGLE order.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-153","concept":"c09-discounts-allowances","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Crunchy Prairie Snacks gives Valley Fresh Grocers free cases of chips in return for running an end-of-aisle display and featuring the chips in its weekly flyer. This is a:","options":["trade-in allowance","functional discount","cumulative quantity discount","promotional allowance"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Promotional allowances are cash payments or extra goods given to resellers for advertising or promoting the product; under the Competition Act they must be offered proportionally to all competing resellers. A functional (trade) discount rewards general channel functions such as warehousing and selling, not a specific promotion.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-154","concept":"c09-discounts-allowances","also":["c09-geographic"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the discounts a seller might offer as a special adjustment to the list price?","options":["Quantity discount","FOB origin pricing","Seasonal discount","Cash discount"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FOB origin pricing is a GEOGRAPHIC adjustment - it determines who pays freight and when title passes - not a discount. The four discounts are quantity, seasonal, trade (functional) and cash.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-155","concept":"c09-discounts-allowances","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Discounts are reductions from list price that a seller gives a buyer as a reward for some favourable activity, such as paying promptly or buying in large quantities.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True - this is the course definition of discounts. Each type rewards a different favourable activity: buying more (quantity), buying early (seasonal), performing marketing functions (trade or functional) or paying promptly (cash).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-156","concept":"c09-geographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"Free-on-board (FOB) origin pricing is a method of pricing where the:","options":["title of goods remains with the manufacturer until sold to the ultimate consumer","title of goods passes to the buyer upon arrival at the final destination","title of goods passes to the buyer at the point of loading","price the seller sets includes all transportation costs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FOB means 'free on board' some vehicle at some location: the seller pays to load the product, and title passes to the buyer at the point of loading, after which the buyer pays the freight. A price that includes all transportation costs describes uniform delivered pricing.","source":"official"},{"id":"q09-157","concept":"c09-geographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Gold Fertilizer quotes a Manitoba co-op a price at its Red Deer plant. Once the product is loaded onto the co-op's hired truck, the co-op owns it and pays the freight. This is:","options":["uniform delivered pricing","FOB origin pricing","single-zone pricing","basing-point pricing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under FOB origin pricing the buyer takes title at the point of loading and pays transportation from the seller's location. Uniform delivered pricing (and its variations such as single-zone and basing-point) builds freight into the seller's quoted price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-158","concept":"c09-geographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts FOB origin pricing with uniform delivered pricing?","options":["Under FOB origin the seller pays freight; under uniform delivered the buyer pays","Both make distant buyers pay more than nearby buyers","Under uniform delivered pricing, title passes at the point of loading","Under FOB origin the buyer pays freight; under uniform delivered the price includes all transportation costs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"FOB origin: the buyer pays freight and takes title at the point of loading, so distant buyers pay more. Uniform delivered: the seller quotes a price that includes all transportation costs. Saying the seller pays freight under FOB origin reverses the roles.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-159","concept":"c09-geographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Maple Lane Books, an online retailer in Toronto, charges every customer in Canada the same delivered price, whether they live in Ottawa or Iqaluit. This form of uniform delivered pricing is called:","options":["multiple-zone pricing","FOB origin pricing","single-zone (postage-stamp) pricing","basing-point pricing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Single-zone (postage-stamp) pricing charges all buyers the same delivered price, like a stamp that costs the same to mail anywhere - a variation of uniform delivered pricing. Multiple-zone pricing would set different prices for different geographic zones.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-160","concept":"c09-geographic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a variation of uniform delivered pricing?","options":["FOB origin pricing","Single-zone pricing","Basing-point pricing","FOB with freight-allowed pricing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"FOB origin pricing is the opposite of uniform delivered pricing - the buyer pays freight from the seller's location. Single-zone, multiple-zone, FOB with freight-allowed (freight absorption) and basing-point pricing are all variations of uniform delivered pricing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-161","concept":"c09-legal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Executives from three Ontario bakeries meet secretly and agree to raise the wholesale price of a loaf of bread by $1.50 at the same time. This is:","options":["horizontal price fixing","price discrimination","predatory pricing","vertical price fixing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Competitors colluding or conspiring to set prices is price fixing; because the conspirators are rivals at the same level of the channel, it is HORIZONTAL price fixing. Vertical price fixing involves a manufacturer controlling the prices its own resellers charge.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-162","concept":"c09-legal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"BlendPro, a kitchen-appliance maker, tells retailers they must sell its blenders for no less than $199 and stops shipping to a Winnipeg store that advertised them at $159. This is an example of:","options":["horizontal price fixing","vertical price fixing","predatory pricing","bait and switch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When a manufacturer controls the resale price its retailers charge, it is vertical price fixing (resale price maintenance), restricted under the Competition Act. Horizontal price fixing would require competing firms at the same level to collude.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-163","concept":"c09-legal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Discount Electronics Depot advertises a 65-inch TV for $299 but keeps only one in stock. When shoppers arrive, staff say it is sold out, criticize the model anyway and push an $899 TV instead. This practice is:","options":["loss-leader pricing","double ticketing","price discrimination","bait and switch"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Bait and switch is a form of deceptive pricing: advertising a bargain the seller does not intend to sell, then steering buyers to a pricier item. A genuine loss leader is actually available at the low price to draw traffic.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-164","concept":"c09-legal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"A shopper at a Halifax hardware store finds a drill with two price stickers: $89.99 and $119.99. Under Canada's rules on double ticketing, what price must the store charge?","options":["$119.99, the higher price","$89.99, the lower price","The average of the two prices","Whatever price is in the store's computer system"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Double ticketing is a deceptive pricing practice; when a product carries two prices, the seller must sell it at the LOWER price. The store cannot charge the higher sticker or default to its system price.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-165","concept":"c09-legal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which situation is price discrimination rather than price fixing?","options":["Two competing cement makers secretly agree to charge all contractors the same high price","Rival airlines agree with one another to add identical fuel surcharges to every fare","A manufacturer charges a big chain less than competing small retailers for the same quantity, hurting competition","Three gas stations in a small town agree with each other not to undercut one another's prices"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Price discrimination is one seller charging COMPETING buyers different prices for like quantities in a way that lessens competition. Price fixing is competitors colluding to set prices - the cement makers, the airlines and the gas stations each reach an agreement with rivals rather than treating buyers unequally.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-166","concept":"c09-legal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a form of deceptive pricing?","options":["Bait and switch","Double ticketing","Predatory pricing","Misleading comparisons with a 'suggested' price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Predatory pricing - a very low price set to drive competitors out of business - is a separate legal issue, not deceptive pricing. Bait and switch, double ticketing, bargains conditional on other purchases and misleading comparable-value or suggested-price comparisons are all deceptive pricing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-167","concept":"c09-legal","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In Canada, pricing practices such as price fixing and deceptive pricing are governed by the _____, which is enforced by the Competition Bureau.","options":["Competition Act","Consumer Packaging and Labelling Act","Weights and Measures Act","Food and Drugs Act"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Competition Act, enforced by the Competition Bureau, covers price fixing, price discrimination, deceptive pricing and predatory pricing. The Consumer Packaging and Labelling Act governs the information on labels, not pricing conduct.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q09-168","concept":"c09-legal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"When an independent pharmacy opens in a small Alberta town, a national chain drops prices at its nearby store far below cost, planning to raise them again once the independent closes. This is:","options":["loss-leader pricing","penetration pricing","predatory pricing","below-market pricing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Predatory pricing charges a very low price to injure or drive out competitors, with the plan of raising prices afterward. Loss-leader pricing also cuts prices, but to draw traffic for other products - not to eliminate a rival.","source":"generated"}],"color":"#B08968","short":"Pricing","emoji":"🪙","tag":"Capture the value","province":"Germania","numeral":"IX"},{"chapter":10,"title":"Marketing Channels and Supply Chains","topics":[{"id":"t10-1","order":1,"title":"Marketing channels and the value of intermediaries","summary":"A marketing channel is the set of people and firms that get a product from its maker to the buyer. Intermediaries such as agents, wholesalers, retailers and distributors earn their place by adding value: they cut the number of transactions, create time, place, form and possession utility, and perform transactional, logistical and facilitating functions. Every channel decision balances cost against level of service.","concepts":["c10-marketing-channel","c10-channel-tradeoffs","c10-intermediary-terms","c10-intermediary-value","c10-channel-functions"]},{"id":"t10-2","order":2,"title":"Channel structures and multiple channels","summary":"Channels can be direct (no intermediaries) or indirect, and consumer and business markets use different typical structures. Firms also combine channels: dual distribution, strategic channel alliances, multichannel marketing across the marketplace and marketspace, and seamless omni-channel retailing. Global channels add links between and within nations.","concepts":["c10-direct-indirect","c10-consumer-channels","c10-business-channels","c10-electronic-direct-channels","c10-dual-distribution","c10-strategic-alliance","c10-multichannel-omni","c10-global-channels"]},{"id":"t10-3","order":3,"title":"Vertical marketing systems","summary":"A vertical marketing system replaces independent channel members with a professionally managed, centrally coordinated channel. The three types differ by how coordination is achieved: corporate (ownership through forward or backward integration), contractual (voluntary chains, cooperatives and franchises) and administered (the size and influence of one member). A horizontal marketing system, by contrast, links firms at the same level.","concepts":["c10-vms","c10-corporate-vms","c10-contractual-vms","c10-administered-vms"]},{"id":"t10-4","order":4,"title":"Choosing and managing channels","summary":"Marketers choose channels based on market, product and company factors, then design them around target market coverage (intensive, selective or exclusive), buyer requirements and profitability. Once running, channels must be managed: members are motivated through sources of power, led by a channel captain, evaluated on performance, and watched for vertical and horizontal conflict, including disintermediation.","concepts":["c10-channel-choice-factors","c10-coverage-intensity","c10-buyer-requirements","c10-channel-power","c10-channel-conflict","c10-disintermediation"]},{"id":"t10-5","order":5,"title":"Logistics and supply chain management","summary":"Logistics gets the right products to the right place at the right time at the lowest total cost for the level of customer service buyers expect. Supply chain management integrates information and logistics across suppliers, producers and the marketing channel. Key functions are transportation, order processing (EDI), inventory management (JIT, VMI, ECR/QR) and warehousing (including cross-docking), plus reverse logistics and outsourcing to 3PLs.","concepts":["c10-logistics","c10-scm","c10-transportation","c10-order-inventory","c10-warehousing","c10-reverse-logistics","c10-3pl"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"c10-marketing-channel","topic":"t10-1","term":"Marketing channel and intermediaries","definition":"A marketing channel consists of the individuals and firms involved in the process of making a product or service available for use or consumption by consumers or industrial users. Intermediaries are the individuals or firms performing a role in the marketing channel, involved in making a product available.","explain":"The channel is the whole route a product travels from maker to buyer, including every firm that helps along the way. Intermediaries are the in-between members (agents, wholesalers, retailers, distributors). Reaching potential buyers is essential to successful marketing, and buyers benefit from well-structured, efficient distribution systems. Channels can be direct (no intermediaries) or indirect (one or more intermediaries).","example":"A Quebec maple syrup producer sells to a food wholesaler that supplies Sobeys stores, where shoppers buy the syrup. The producer, wholesaler and Sobeys form the marketing channel; the wholesaler and Sobeys are intermediaries.","contrast":"The marketing channel covers the parties that distribute and promote the finished product toward buyers (downstream). The supply chain is broader: it adds the supplier network upstream (raw materials and parts). Logistics is the management of the physical flow, not the set of firms.","trap":"'Individuals and firms involved in making a product available' = marketing channel. 'Performing a role IN the channel' = intermediaries. If a stem includes raw-material or parts suppliers, it is describing the supply chain, not the marketing channel.","mnemonic":"Channel = the whole pipe; intermediaries = the joints in the pipe.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-channel-tradeoffs","topic":"t10-1","term":"Two key trade-offs in channel decisions (cost vs level of service; every partner adds value)","definition":"The instructor's framing: every channel decision requires weighing two elements, cost and level of service, and every channel partner must provide value by performing its functions more efficiently or effectively than the producer could alone.","explain":"More service (faster delivery, more outlets, expert advice, after-sale support) usually costs more, so channel design is a balancing act between what buyers expect and what the firm can afford. An intermediary only earns its place, and its margin, if it adds value, for example by reducing contacts, holding inventory or extending credit more cheaply than the producer could. A partner that adds no value is a candidate for removal, but its functions must then be performed by someone else.","example":"A Kelowna winery could ship every order by overnight courier (high service, high cost) or sell through BC Liquor Stores and private wine shops that hold stock locally (lower cost per bottle). Each shop it uses must add value by reaching buyers the winery could not reach as efficiently itself.","contrast":"These two elements are not the three channel design considerations (target market coverage, buyer requirements, profitability), nor the total logistics cost trade-offs (e.g., faster transport vs lower inventory). The instructor's pair is specifically COST and LEVEL OF SERVICE; what every partner must provide is VALUE.","trap":"'What must every channel partner provide?' = value (not lowest price, not exclusivity, not financing). Distractor pairs such as 'price and promotion' or 'coverage and exclusivity' sound plausible, but the trade-off is cost vs level of service.","mnemonic":"C-S-V: Cost vs Service, and every partner adds Value.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-intermediary-terms","topic":"t10-1","term":"Terms used for marketing intermediaries (Figure 10-1)","definition":"Middleman: another name for intermediary. Agent or broker: an intermediary with legal authority to act on behalf of another channel member (e.g., a manufacturer); does not take title. Wholesaler: sells to other intermediaries, usually retailers (usually consumer goods). Retailer: sells to consumers. Distributor: general term for an intermediary performing a variety of functions (selling, maintaining inventories, extending credit), usually in business markets. Dealer: general term that can mean distributor, retailer or wholesaler.","explain":"Each label tells you WHO the intermediary sells to and WHETHER it owns the goods. Agents and brokers negotiate on someone else's behalf and never take title. Wholesalers sell to businesses that resell (usually retailers), retailers sell to final consumers, and distributors are the business-market version of a full-function intermediary. 'Dealer' and 'middleman' are loose catch-all words.","example":"A Toronto food broker negotiates shelf space for a small salsa maker with Loblaws but never owns the salsa (agent/broker); a wholesaler buys pallets of salsa and resells them to independent grocers (wholesaler); the grocer sells jars to shoppers (retailer).","contrast":"Agent/broker vs wholesaler: the agent does NOT take title; the wholesaler does. Wholesaler vs distributor: 'wholesaler' usually refers to consumer goods sold to other intermediaries, 'distributor' to business markets and many functions. Retailer sells to ultimate consumers; wholesaler sells to other intermediaries.","trap":"'Legal authority to act on behalf of' or 'does not take title' = agent/broker. 'Sells to other intermediaries, usually retailers' = wholesaler. 'Business market, extends credit, maintains inventory' = distributor. 'Can mean distributor, retailer or wholesaler' = dealer. 'Another name for intermediary' = middleman.","mnemonic":"Agents Act for others (no title); Wholesalers sell to those Who resell; Retailers sell to the Real user.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 10-1 Terms used for marketing intermediaries","head":["Term","Description","Exam cue"],"rows":[["Middleman","Another name for intermediary","Generic synonym"],["Agent or broker","Has legal authority to act on behalf of another channel member (e.g., a manufacturer)","Does NOT take title"],["Wholesaler","Sells to other intermediaries, usually retailers","Usually consumer goods"],["Retailer","Sells to consumers","Final buyer is the consumer"],["Distributor","Performs a variety of functions: selling, maintaining inventories, extending credit, etc.","Usually business markets"],["Dealer","General term; can mean distributor, retailer or wholesaler","Catch-all term"]]}},{"id":"c10-intermediary-value","topic":"t10-1","term":"How intermediaries create value: fewer transactions and utilities","definition":"Intermediaries minimize the number of transactions: without an intermediary every producer must contact every buyer (producers x buyers contacts); with one intermediary each party contacts only the intermediary (producers + buyers contacts). Intermediaries also create time, place, form and possession utility.","explain":"In Figure 10-2, four producers selling to four buyers need 4 x 4 = 16 contacts; routing everything through one intermediary (Best Buy) cuts this to 4 + 4 = 8. Fewer contacts mean lower selling and buying costs for everyone. Intermediaries also make products available when buyers want them (time), where they want them (place), in a usable form (form) and easy to acquire or own, for example through credit (possession).","example":"Five Alberta craft breweries each selling to six Calgary restaurants would need 5 x 6 = 30 relationships; one beverage distributor in between reduces this to 5 + 6 = 11.","contrast":"Contact efficiency is about the NUMBER of transactions, while the channel functions (transactional, logistical, facilitating) are the WORK intermediaries do. Time utility (available when wanted) is not place utility (available where wanted); possession utility is about ease of ownership (credit, transfer of title).","trap":"Calculations: MULTIPLY for no intermediary, ADD for one intermediary; contacts saved = (P x B) - (P + B). Utility cue words: 'when' = time, 'where' = place, 'credit / easy to own' = possession, 'altered or assembled' = form.","mnemonic":"Without = Multiply; With = Add. Utilities: Time, Place, Form, Possession.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 10-2 How intermediaries minimize transactions","head":["Situation","Formula","4 producers, 4 buyers"],"rows":[["No intermediary","Producers x Buyers","4 x 4 = 16 contacts"],["One intermediary","Producers + Buyers","4 + 4 = 8 contacts"]]}},{"id":"c10-channel-functions","topic":"t10-1","term":"Channel functions performed by intermediaries (transactional, logistical, facilitating)","definition":"Intermediaries perform three families of functions. Transactional: buying, selling and risk-taking. Logistical: selection (assorting), storing, sorting and transporting. Facilitating: financing, marketing information and research, and grading.","explain":"Ask what the intermediary is DOING. Ownership and the deal (buying to resell, promoting and seeking orders, bearing the risk that inventory spoils or goes out of style) = transactional. The physical product (gathering an assortment, holding it, breaking bulk, moving it) = logistical. Making the exchange easier without handling the goods or the deal (credit, market intelligence, grading) = facilitating. A producer can eliminate an intermediary but not its functions; someone must still perform them.","example":"A Winnipeg grocery wholesaler buys cereal by the truckload (buying), breaks it into cases for corner stores (sorting), keeps it in its warehouse (storing), delivers it (transporting), lets stores pay in 30 days (financing) and shares sales-trend reports with manufacturers (marketing information and research).","contrast":"Risk-taking is TRANSACTIONAL (it comes from owning inventory), not facilitating. Financing is FACILITATING, not transactional, even though money is involved. Sorting (buy in bulk, divide into smaller amounts) and selection (gather products from several sources) are both LOGISTICAL. 'Informational function' is not one of the three family names.","trap":"Stems mix verbs: a supermarket that 'buys produce from different sources, transfers it to its stores and arranges it' is performing the LOGISTICAL function (assorting, transporting, displaying), even though 'buys' appears. Look for the dominant activity. 'Extends credit' = facilitating; 'promotes and seeks orders' = transactional; 'divides large lots into smaller amounts' = logistical sorting; 'assigns quality grades' = facilitating grading.","mnemonic":"Transactional = BSR (Buy, Sell, Risk). Logistical = Select, Store, Sort, Transport. Facilitating = FIG (Financing, Information, Grading).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 10-3 Marketing channel functions performed by intermediaries","head":["Function family","Activity","What it involves"],"rows":[["Transactional","Buying","Purchasing products for resale"],["Transactional","Selling","Contacting potential customers, promoting products and seeking orders"],["Transactional","Risk-taking","Assuming business risks in the ownership of inventory"],["Logistical","Selection (assorting)","Putting together a selection of products from several different sources"],["Logistical","Storing","Assembling and protecting products at a convenient location"],["Logistical","Sorting","Purchasing in large quantities and dividing into smaller amounts"],["Logistical","Transporting","Physically moving a product to customers"],["Facilitating","Financing","Extending credit to customers"],["Facilitating","Marketing information and research","Providing information to customers and suppliers, including competitive conditions and trends"],["Facilitating","Grading","Inspecting, testing or judging products and assigning quality grades"]]}},{"id":"c10-direct-indirect","topic":"t10-2","term":"Direct vs indirect channels","definition":"A direct channel is one in which the producer and the ultimate consumer deal directly with each other, with no intermediaries. An indirect channel inserts intermediaries (agents, wholesalers, retailers, distributors) between the producer and consumers, and they perform numerous channel functions.","explain":"Direct channels give the producer full control and direct customer contact but force it to perform every channel function itself. Indirect channels hand functions such as storing, sorting, selling and financing to intermediaries that can often perform them more efficiently. Whether the sale happens online is irrelevant: the only test is whether an intermediary sits between producer and consumer.","example":"Mary Kay and Dell sell directly to consumers (direct). A Saskatoon jam maker that sells through a wholesaler to Co-op grocery stores uses an indirect channel.","contrast":"Direct vs indirect is about whether intermediaries exist, not whether the sale is online. A brand selling on its own website is direct; a brand selling through Amazon or Best Buy's website uses an intermediary (indirect). Dual distribution means using BOTH kinds of channel for the same product.","trap":"'Uses intermediaries like wholesalers and retailers' = indirect. 'Online' is a distractor because online sales can be direct or indirect. Removing intermediaries does not remove their functions; the producer must take them on.","mnemonic":"Direct = nobody in the middle.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-consumer-channels","topic":"t10-2","term":"Consumer (B2C) channel structures","definition":"Four common consumer channels: A Producer -> Consumer (direct, e.g., Mary Kay, Dell); B Producer -> Retailer -> Consumer (large retailers buying straight from producers, e.g., Walmart, Canadian Tire); C Producer -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer (most common for low-cost, low-unit-value items such as candy and magazines); D Producer -> Agent -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer (agents bring producers and wholesalers together, e.g., Mars candy).","explain":"Each step from A to D adds one more intermediary. Big retailers buy in volumes large enough to deal straight with producers (B). Low-value items sold in thousands of small stores need a wholesaler to break bulk and reach those stores economically (C). When a producer needs help reaching many wholesalers, an agent, who never takes title, is added in front of the wholesaler (D).","example":"A Winnipeg candy maker selling through a confectionery wholesaler to convenience stores uses channel C; Canadian Tire buying barbecues straight from the manufacturer is channel B.","contrast":"Channel B skips the wholesaler because the retailer is big enough to buy direct; channel C adds a wholesaler for low-unit-value goods sold through many small retailers; channel D adds an agent in front of the wholesaler. The agent does not take title; the wholesaler does. Only channel A is direct.","trap":"Stems describe a situation and ask for the structure or the missing member. 'Large retailer buys direct from the manufacturer' = B; 'candy or magazines through wholesalers to many small stores' = C; 'agent connects producer with wholesalers' = D. Industrial distributors never appear in consumer channels.","mnemonic":"A = Alone (direct), B = Big retailer, C = Candy through a wholesaler, D = aDD an agent.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Consumer (B2C) channel structures","head":["Channel","Structure","Typical use / example"],"rows":[["A","Producer -> Consumer","Direct; e.g., Mary Kay, Dell"],["B","Producer -> Retailer -> Consumer","Large retailers buy direct from producers; e.g., Walmart, Canadian Tire"],["C","Producer -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer","Most common for low-cost, low-unit-value items; e.g., candy, magazines"],["D","Producer -> Agent -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer","Agents bring producers and wholesalers together; e.g., Mars candy"]]}},{"id":"c10-business-channels","topic":"t10-2","term":"Business (B2B) channel structures and industrial distributors","definition":"Four common business channels: A Producer -> Industrial user (direct, e.g., IBM); B Producer -> Industrial distributor -> Industrial user (e.g., Caterpillar); C Producer -> Agent -> Industrial user; D Producer -> Agent -> Industrial distributor -> Industrial user. An industrial distributor performs functions similar to a wholesaler in business markets; agents do not take title or physical possession of the goods.","explain":"Business channels tend to be shorter than consumer channels because buyers are fewer and larger and often need technical selling. Direct (A) suits expensive, complex products sold to a few large buyers. Industrial distributors (B) stock and resell standardized items and equipment to many users. Agents (C) represent the producer in negotiations without owning or holding goods, which is useful when the firm lacks its own sales force; adding a distributor after the agent (D) supplies inventory and local delivery.","example":"A Calgary valve maker sells custom valves directly to a large oil sands producer (A) but sells standard fittings through an industrial supply distributor to hundreds of small contractors (B).","contrast":"Industrial distributor = the B2B equivalent of a wholesaler (takes title, holds inventory). Agent = no title and no physical possession. Consumer channels end with a consumer and can include retailers; business channels end with an industrial user and never include a retailer.","trap":"'Industrial distributor' only appears in business channels. If a stem says the intermediary 'never handles the goods' or 'earns a commission for arranging sales', it is an agent, not a distributor.","mnemonic":"B2B: the Distributor stocks and Delivers; the Agent only Arranges.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Business (B2B) channel structures","head":["Channel","Structure","Notes / example"],"rows":[["A","Producer -> Industrial user","Direct; e.g., IBM"],["B","Producer -> Industrial distributor -> Industrial user","Distributor buys, stocks and resells; e.g., Caterpillar"],["C","Producer -> Agent -> Industrial user","Agent represents the producer; no title or physical possession"],["D","Producer -> Agent -> Industrial distributor -> Industrial user","Agent arranges sales; distributor holds inventory"]]}},{"id":"c10-electronic-direct-channels","topic":"t10-2","term":"Electronic and direct marketing channels (and e-commerce trends)","definition":"Electronic marketing channels use the Internet to make goods and services available to consumers or business buyers (e.g., Amazon, Expedia). Direct marketing channels allow consumers to buy products by interacting with various advertising media without a face-to-face meeting with a salesperson (catalogues, telemarketing, direct mail, TV home shopping, online).","explain":"Electronic channels can perform many channel functions online; Expedia, for example, is an intermediary between travellers and airlines or hotels. Direct marketing channels are about buying through a medium (catalogue, phone, mail, TV, screen) rather than through a person. The instructor's e-commerce trends slide adds: top categories are fashion and electronics; AI personalization, AR and VR; CRM and customer support; hybrid commerce; multiple payment options such as buy now, pay later; voice activation; social selling; data privacy; sustainability; and fast, free delivery.","example":"Expedia (electronic marketing channel) lets travellers book flights and hotels from many suppliers; a TV shopping channel where viewers phone in orders is a direct marketing channel.","contrast":"An electronic channel can be indirect (Expedia and Amazon are intermediaries), so 'online' does not automatically mean 'direct channel'. A direct MARKETING channel (buying through media without a salesperson) differs from a DIRECT CHANNEL (no intermediaries): a TV shopping network selling many brands is direct marketing but is still an intermediary.","trap":"'Without a face-to-face meeting with a salesperson', catalogue, telemarketing or TV home shopping = direct marketing channel. 'Uses the Internet to make goods available' = electronic marketing channel. In trends questions, options like 'dropping free delivery' or 'eliminating customer support' are the planted wrong answers.","mnemonic":"Electronic = Internet; direct MARKETING = Media, no salesperson.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"E-commerce trends (instructor slide)","items":["Top categories: fashion and electronics","Use of AI (personalization), AR and VR","CRM and customer support","Hybrid commerce","Multiple payment options (e.g., buy now, pay later)","Voice activation","Social selling","Data privacy","Sustainability","Fast, free delivery"]}},{"id":"c10-dual-distribution","topic":"t10-2","term":"Dual distribution","definition":"Dual distribution is an arrangement whereby a firm reaches buyers by using two or more different types of channels for the same basic product.","explain":"Dual distribution lets a firm reach different segments, for example large accounts through its own sales force and small ones through distributors, or shoppers through both retailers and its own website. A website can play several roles at once: transactional (taking orders) and promotional (information and brand building). The main risk is conflict when the channels compete for the same customers.","example":"Prairie Peak Outerwear (a fictional Alberta brand) sells the same parkas through Sport Chek stores and through its own online store.","contrast":"Dual distribution = ONE firm using two or more of its own channel TYPES for the same product. Strategic channel alliance = one firm's products sold through ANOTHER firm's channel. Multichannel marketing = blending communication AND delivery channels so they reinforce each other. Omni-channel = a seamless experience across channels. Intensive/selective/exclusive = number of outlets, not channel types.","trap":"Cue: 'same basic product' + 'two or more different types of channels'. If the stem stresses mutual reinforcement of communication and delivery channels, think multichannel; if it stresses a seamless customer experience, think omni-channel; if another company's channel is used, think strategic alliance.","mnemonic":"Dual = same product, two channel types.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-strategic-alliance","topic":"t10-2","term":"Strategic channel alliances","definition":"A strategic channel alliance is an arrangement whereby one firm's marketing channel is used to sell another firm's products.","explain":"Instead of building its own distribution, a firm borrows a partner's established channel. This gives quick, low-cost access to buyers, as when Starbucks coffee is sold in Safeway. The partner benefits by adding products its customers want. No ownership is involved.","example":"Starbucks coffee sold in Safeway stores; a small B.C. kombucha brand delivered and sold by a national juice company's trucks and sales force.","contrast":"Dual distribution = one firm using its own two or more channel types. Strategic channel alliance = one firm's channel sells another firm's products. Horizontal marketing system = unrelated firms at the same level pooling resources. Franchising = a contractual VMS. Owning the partner's outlets = corporate VMS.","trap":"Cue: 'one firm's channel is used to sell another firm's products' or 'uses a partner's distribution network'. If no second company's channel is involved, it is not an alliance.","mnemonic":"Alliance = borrow a partner's channel.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-multichannel-omni","topic":"t10-2","term":"Multichannel marketing (marketplace vs marketspace) and omni-channel retailing","definition":"Multichannel marketing is the blending of different communication and delivery channels that are mutually reinforcing in attracting, retaining and building relationships with consumers who shop in the traditional marketplace (face-to-face) and the online marketspace (face-to-screen). Omni-channel retailing creates a seamless shopping experience among all available shopping channels (online, catalogue, in-store).","explain":"Multichannel marketing uses several channels (stores, catalogues, websites, apps) that support each other, leveraging the value-adding capabilities of each: the store lets people touch the product, the website offers convenience and information. Omni-channel retailing goes a step further: the customer experiences all channels as one seamless system, with a shared cart, inventory, loyalty account and returns.","example":"A shopper checks stock on a retailer's app, buys online, picks up in store and returns the item at a different location, all as one continuous omni-channel experience.","contrast":"Omni-channel is NOT just another term for multichannel marketing (official question). Multichannel = several mutually reinforcing channels; omni = those channels feel like one seamless experience. Dual distribution = two channel types for the same product, with no requirement that they be integrated.","trap":"'Seamless' = omni-channel. 'Mutually reinforcing communication and delivery channels' or 'leverage the value-adding capabilities of each' = multichannel. 'Face-to-face' = marketplace; 'face-to-screen' = marketspace.","mnemonic":"Omni = One seamless experience. MarketPLACE = a physical place; MarketSPACE = cyberSPACE.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-global-channels","topic":"t10-2","term":"Global channel strategy (Figure 10-7)","definition":"In global marketing the channel runs Seller -> Seller's international marketing headquarters -> Channels between nations -> Channels within foreign nations -> Final consumer. Channel sophistication increases as a country's economic infrastructure develops.","explain":"The international marketing headquarters manages the global effort; channels between nations move the product across borders (e.g., export agents, freight forwarders); channels within foreign nations distribute it inside the market (local wholesalers and retailers). Where economic infrastructure is less developed, intermediaries are fewer and less specialized, so marketers must adapt.","example":"A Banff skincare brand's international marketing team ships through an export agent and freight forwarder (between nations) to a Tokyo wholesaler that supplies department stores (within the foreign nation), which sell to Japanese consumers.","contrast":"Channels BETWEEN nations (crossing borders) vs channels WITHIN foreign nations (local distribution inside the market). Global channel strategy is about the sequence of links across countries, not about the number of channel types (dual distribution) or blending channels (multichannel).","trap":"Order questions: immediately after the international marketing headquarters = channels between nations; immediately before the final consumer = channels within foreign nations. Sophistication grows with economic infrastructure, not with distance or population.","mnemonic":"Seller, HQ, Between, Within, Consumer: 'Sellers Head Beyond borders, Within markets, to Consumers.'","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Figure 10-7 Channels of distribution in global marketing","items":["1. Seller","2. Seller's international marketing headquarters","3. Channels between nations","4. Channels within foreign nations","5. Final consumer"]}},{"id":"c10-vms","topic":"t10-3","term":"Vertical marketing systems (VMS) vs horizontal marketing systems","definition":"Vertical marketing systems are professionally managed and centrally coordinated marketing channels designed to achieve channel economies and maximum marketing impact. The three types are corporate (ownership), contractual (contracts) and administered (size and influence of one member). Contrast: a horizontal marketing system is two or more unrelated companies at the same channel level pooling resources.","explain":"In a conventional channel each member acts independently to maximize its own profit; a VMS coordinates the levels (producer, wholesaler, retailer) as one system. The three types differ only in HOW that coordination is achieved. 'Vertical' means up and down the levels; 'horizontal' means side by side at the same level.","example":"Apple Stores (corporate), IGA and Home Hardware (contractual), Procter & Gamble's influence over retailers (administered); two unrelated producers sharing a delivery van (horizontal marketing system, not a VMS).","contrast":"VMS coordinates DIFFERENT levels; a horizontal marketing system combines firms at the SAME level. Within VMS: corporate (owns), contractual (signs), administered (dominates).","trap":"Cue: 'professionally managed and centrally coordinated' = VMS. 'Two or more unrelated companies at the same level pool resources' = horizontal marketing system, a frequent distractor (e.g., in the Mocha Jive official question). 'Horizontal' is never one of the three VMS types.","mnemonic":"Own it, Sign it, or Sway it: Corporate, Contractual, Administered.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Types of vertical marketing systems","head":["Type","Coordination achieved through","Examples"],"rows":[["Corporate VMS","Ownership of successive stages (forward or backward integration)","Ralph Lauren/Polo stores; Apple Stores; a coffee chain owning its cookie bakery"],["Contractual VMS","Contracts among independently owned firms","IGA (voluntary chain); Home Hardware (cooperative); franchises such as Ford dealers, Coca-Cola bottlers, Canadian Tire, McDonald's"],["Administered VMS","Size and influence of one channel member","Procter & Gamble; Walmart"],["Contrast: horizontal marketing system","Two or more unrelated firms at the SAME level pool resources","Not a VMS: side by side, not up and down"]]}},{"id":"c10-corporate-vms","topic":"t10-3","term":"Corporate VMS (forward and backward integration)","definition":"A corporate VMS combines successive stages of production and distribution under a single ownership. Forward integration: a producer owns intermediaries downstream (e.g., Apple Stores, Ralph Lauren's Polo stores). Backward integration: a retailer or wholesaler owns upstream manufacturing or wholesaling (e.g., a coffee chain owning the bakery that makes its cookies).","explain":"One company owns two or more successive levels of the channel, so it can coordinate them directly. Integration can go forward (toward the consumer) or backward (toward the source). Ownership gives maximum control but requires major investment.","example":"Ralph Lauren operating its own Polo stores and Apple running Apple Stores (forward integration); Mocha Jive coffee shops owning Perfect Cookies, which bakes for its cafés (backward integration).","contrast":"Forward = toward the consumer (a manufacturer owns stores); backward = toward the source (a retailer owns its supplier). Corporate (ownership) vs contractual (independent firms bound by contracts, e.g., franchises) vs administered (no ownership, no contract, only influence).","trap":"Ask which direction the OWNER is reaching. Coffee shop owning its cookie maker = backward (official question). Designer opening its own boutiques = forward. Buying from a supplier without owning it is not integration. A franchise is contractual, not corporate.","mnemonic":"Forward = toward the Front door (consumer); Backward = Back toward the factory.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-contractual-vms","topic":"t10-3","term":"Contractual VMS (voluntary chains, cooperatives, franchises)","definition":"A contractual VMS is one in which independently owned firms at different levels of production and distribution integrate their efforts on a contractual basis to obtain greater functional economies and marketing impact than they could alone. Forms: wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chains (IGA), retailer-sponsored cooperatives (Home Hardware) and franchise programs (manufacturer-sponsored retail, manufacturer-sponsored wholesale, service-sponsored retail, service-sponsored franchise systems).","explain":"Firms stay independently owned but sign agreements that coordinate buying, merchandising and branding. The voluntary chain vs cooperative distinction is about who starts it: a WHOLESALER signs up independent retailers (voluntary chain), or independent RETAILERS band together to run their own wholesale operation (cooperative). Franchises are classified by who sponsors them (a manufacturer or a service company) and at what level (retail or wholesale).","example":"IGA grocers coordinated by a wholesaler (voluntary chain); Home Hardware stores that jointly own their wholesale operation (cooperative); Ford dealers, Coca-Cola bottlers, Canadian Tire, H&R Block, Holiday Inn and McDonald's (franchises).","contrast":"Voluntary chain (wholesaler-sponsored) vs cooperative (retailer-sponsored). Manufacturer-sponsored RETAIL franchise (Ford dealers sell to consumers) vs manufacturer-sponsored WHOLESALE franchise (Coca-Cola bottlers sell to retailers). Contractual (independent owners plus contracts) vs corporate (one owner) vs administered (influence only).","trap":"IGA = wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain; Home Hardware = retailer-sponsored cooperative. Coca-Cola bottlers = manufacturer-sponsored WHOLESALE franchise. A franchise system looks like one company, but it is CONTRACTUAL, not corporate.","mnemonic":"Voluntary chain = a Wholesaler woos retailers; Cooperative = retailers Club together.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Forms of contractual VMS","head":["Form","How it works","Example"],"rows":[["Wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","A wholesaler contracts with independent retailers to standardize and coordinate buying and merchandising","IGA"],["Retailer-sponsored cooperative","Independent retailers band together to own and operate a wholesale operation","Home Hardware"],["Manufacturer-sponsored retail franchise system","A manufacturer licenses independent retailers (dealers) to sell its product","Ford dealers"],["Manufacturer-sponsored wholesale franchise system","A manufacturer licenses independent wholesalers who sell to retailers","Coca-Cola bottlers"],["Service-sponsored retail franchise system","A service company sponsors a franchised system of retail outlets","Canadian Tire, H&R Block"],["Service-sponsored franchise system","A service company licenses franchisees to operate under its name and system","Holiday Inn, McDonald's"]]}},{"id":"c10-administered-vms","topic":"t10-3","term":"Administered VMS","definition":"An administered VMS achieves coordination at successive stages of production and distribution through the size and influence of one channel member, rather than through ownership or contracts (e.g., Procter & Gamble, Walmart).","explain":"No one owns anyone and no franchise-type contract binds them, yet the channel behaves as a coordinated system because one member is so large and important that the others go along with its programs (displays, pricing, inventory systems). It rests on channel power.","example":"Procter & Gamble gets retailers to cooperate on displays and promotions; Walmart's buying power leads suppliers to adopt its inventory and delivery systems.","contrast":"Administered (influence, no ownership, no contract) vs corporate (ownership) vs contractual (formal agreements such as franchises, voluntary chains, cooperatives).","trap":"Cue: 'because of its size or market power, it gets others to cooperate' with no ownership and no contract = administered. 'Administered' here has nothing to do with government administration.","mnemonic":"Administered = Authority by size (no deed, no contract).","figure":null},{"id":"c10-channel-choice-factors","topic":"t10-4","term":"Factors affecting channel choice (market, product, company)","definition":"Market factors: geographic concentration, number of potential customers, type of market (consumer vs business) and order size. Product factors: technical factors, perishability, unit value and product life cycle stage. Company factors: financial resources and ability of management, and desire for channel control.","explain":"Market factors ask who and where the buyers are; product factors ask what is being sold; company factors ask what the firm can afford and how much control it wants. Rules of thumb: few, concentrated buyers placing large orders -> shorter or direct channels; many dispersed buyers placing small orders -> indirect. Technical or perishable products -> short or direct; low unit value -> long and indirect. Strong resources and a strong desire for control -> direct.","example":"Live Nova Scotia lobster (perishable) moves through a short channel; a granola bar (low unit value, millions of buyers) moves through wholesalers and retailers.","contrast":"Keep the groups straight: geographic concentration, number of customers, type of market and order size are MARKET factors; technical factors, perishability, unit value and PLC stage are PRODUCT factors; financial resources/management ability and desire for control are COMPANY factors. These differ from the three channel design considerations (coverage, buyer requirements, profitability).","trap":"Questions ask you to classify a factor or predict channel length. 'Perishability' is a product factor (not a transportation criterion); 'order size' is a market factor (not a product factor); 'desire for control' is a company factor.","mnemonic":"Market = who, where, how many, how much; Product = tech, spoil, value, life cycle; Company = cash and control.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Factors affecting channel choice","head":["Factor group","Factor","Typical channel implication"],"rows":[["Market","Geographic concentration","Concentrated buyers -> direct is practical; dispersed buyers -> intermediaries"],["Market","Number of potential customers","Few customers -> direct; many customers -> indirect"],["Market","Type of market","Consumer and business markets use different channel structures"],["Market","Order size","Large orders -> direct; small orders -> intermediaries"],["Product","Technical factors","Complex, technical products -> direct"],["Product","Perishability","Perishable products -> short or direct"],["Product","Unit value","Low unit value -> long and indirect; high unit value -> short"],["Product","Product life cycle stage","Channel needs change as the product moves through its life cycle"],["Company","Financial resources and ability of management","Strong resources and expertise -> can perform functions itself"],["Company","Desire for channel control","Strong desire for control -> direct or tightly managed channel"]]}},{"id":"c10-coverage-intensity","topic":"t10-4","term":"Target market coverage: intensive, selective and exclusive distribution","definition":"Intensive distribution places products in as many outlets as possible (convenience goods). Selective distribution uses a few retailers in a specific geographic area (shopping goods such as appliances and apparel). Exclusive distribution uses only one retailer in a specific geographic area (specialty goods such as luxury cars, yachts and some fragrances).","explain":"Coverage answers 'how many outlets in an area?' Convenience goods are bought often with little effort, so they must be everywhere (intensive). Shopping goods are compared across a few stores (selective). Specialty goods are sought out by brand-loyal buyers, so one dealer per area is enough and protects image and service (exclusive).","example":"Gum and soft drinks in every gas station and grocery store (intensive); appliances at a few Edmonton retailers (selective); one luxury sports car dealer in Calgary (exclusive).","contrast":"Intensive vs selective vs exclusive = number of outlets per area. Dual distribution = number of channel TYPES, not coverage intensity, even though it also ends in 'distribution'.","trap":"Cue words: 'as many outlets as possible' = intensive; 'a few retailers in an area' = selective; 'only one retailer in a specified geographic area' = exclusive. Product cues: convenience -> intensive, shopping -> selective, specialty -> exclusive. 'Dual distribution' is a planted distractor (official question 1).","mnemonic":"Convenience-Intensive, Shopping-Selective, Specialty-Exclusive (C-I, S-S, S-E).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Target market coverage","head":["Coverage","Outlets per area","Product type","Examples"],"rows":[["Intensive","As many as possible","Convenience goods","Gum, soft drinks, batteries"],["Selective","A few retailers","Shopping goods","Appliances, apparel"],["Exclusive","Only one retailer","Specialty goods","Luxury cars, yachts, some fragrances"]]}},{"id":"c10-buyer-requirements","topic":"t10-4","term":"Channel design: buyer requirements and profitability","definition":"Besides target market coverage, channel design considers (2) satisfying buyer requirements: information, convenience, variety, and pre- and/or post-sale services; and (3) profitability: each channel's distribution, advertising and selling expenses relative to the revenue and margins it generates.","explain":"Information: buyers of technical or unfamiliar products need knowledgeable salespeople. Convenience: easy-to-reach locations, longer hours, online ordering, pickup options. Variety: breadth and depth of assortment. Pre- and post-sale services: delivery, installation, repair, training, maintenance. Profitability: a channel that satisfies buyers but loses money for its members will not last.","example":"A Calgary hot-tub dealer that delivers, installs and services every tub meets post-sale service requirements, but the channel must still be profitable once its distribution, advertising and selling costs are counted.","contrast":"Coverage = how many outlets; buyer requirements = what buyers need from the channel; profitability = whether the channel makes money. Buyer requirements are not the transportation criteria (cost, time, capability, dependability, accessibility, frequency) or the criteria for evaluating channel members (sales, inventory levels, treatment of damaged goods).","trap":"List cues: 'information, convenience, variety, pre- and post-sale services' = buyer requirements; 'distribution, advertising and selling expenses' = profitability. A scenario where customers are delighted but members lose money is a profitability failure, not a buyer-requirements failure.","mnemonic":"ICVS: Information, Convenience, Variety, Service; then ask, is it Profitable?","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Channel design considerations","items":["1. Target market coverage: intensive, selective or exclusive distribution","2. Satisfying buyer requirements: information, convenience, variety, pre- and/or post-sale services","3. Profitability: distribution, advertising and selling expenses vs margins"]}},{"id":"c10-channel-power","topic":"t10-4","term":"Channel power, the channel captain and evaluating channel members","definition":"Channel members are motivated through sources of power: coercive (threat of punishment or withdrawal of support), reward (incentives), legitimate (authority from position or contract), expert (specialized knowledge others value) and referent (others want to be identified with a respected member). A channel captain is a channel member that coordinates, directs and supports other members; it can be a producer, wholesaler or retailer (e.g., Walmart). Members are evaluated on sales, inventory levels, treatment of damaged/lost goods and others (customer service, cooperation in promotion).","explain":"Channel members are independent businesses, so the stronger ones motivate the others with power. Coercive (sticks) and reward (carrots) are the most direct; legitimate power comes from a formal position or contract; expert power from know-how; referent power from others wanting to be associated with a respected brand. The member that uses its power to lead the channel is the channel captain. At review time, members are judged on sales, inventory levels, how they treat damaged or lost goods, and other factors such as customer service and promotional cooperation.","example":"Walmart acts as channel captain, using its buying power to set delivery and inventory standards for its suppliers.","contrast":"Coercive (threat) vs reward (incentive): same goal, opposite tools. Legitimate (contract or position) vs referent (admiration). Expert (knowledge) vs referent (identification). Channel captain is a ROLE (leader of the channel); power is the means it uses.","trap":"'Or we will stop supplying you' = coercive; 'bonus or allowance' = reward; 'the agreement entitles us' = legitimate; 'because of their know-how' = expert; 'want to be associated with the prestige' = referent. The channel captain does not have to be the manufacturer.","mnemonic":"Stick, Carrot, Contract, Know-how, Admiration = Coercive, Reward, Legitimate, Expert, Referent.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Sources of channel power","head":["Power source","Basis","Example cue"],"rows":[["Coercive","Threat of punishment or withdrawal of support","'Give us more shelf space or we stop supplying you'"],["Reward","Incentives for cooperating","Bonuses, allowances, display funding"],["Legitimate","Authority from position or contract","A franchise agreement requires compliance"],["Expert","Specialized knowledge others value","Partners follow the firm's technical advice"],["Referent","Others want to be identified with a respected member","Retailers want the prestige of carrying the brand"]]}},{"id":"c10-channel-conflict","topic":"t10-4","term":"Channel conflict: vertical vs horizontal","definition":"Channel conflict arises when one channel member believes another channel member is engaged in behaviour that prevents it from achieving its goals. Vertical conflict occurs between different levels of a marketing channel (e.g., manufacturer vs wholesaler or retailer). Horizontal conflict occurs between intermediaries at the same level (e.g., two dealers of the same brand).","explain":"Channel members are independent businesses with their own goals, so friction is natural. Conflict is vertical when it runs up and down the channel: a producer arguing with its wholesaler over price, squeezing retailers' margins, or bypassing them (disintermediation). It is horizontal when it is between members at the same level, such as two dealers of the same brand fighting over territory.","example":"Tez energy drinks arguing with a major wholesaler over price (vertical); two Edmonton dealers of the same truck brand poaching each other's customers (horizontal).","contrast":"Vertical = different levels; horizontal = same level. Disintermediation is a specific cause of vertical conflict (bypassing a member), not a synonym for channel conflict in general.","trap":"Check the LEVELS of the two parties named in the stem. Producer vs wholesaler or retailer = vertical, whatever the topic (price, territory, margins), and a producer bypassing a member to sell direct is disintermediation (vertical). One course nuance: the slides list a retailer competing with the maker's own online store under dual distribution as a horizontal-conflict example, because both outlets compete at the retail level. Retailer vs retailer of the same brand = horizontal. 'Selective conflict' and 'disintegration' are not real terms.","mnemonic":"Vertical = up and down the ladder; Horizontal = across the same rung.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-disintermediation","topic":"t10-4","term":"Disintermediation","definition":"Disintermediation is a channel conflict that arises when a channel member bypasses another member and sells or buys products directly from others (e.g., a manufacturer selling direct to consumers online, bypassing its retailers).","explain":"Disintermediation literally means removing the intermediary. A manufacturer might start selling directly online, cutting out retailers who then feel threatened; or a large buyer might stop using a distributor and buy straight from producers. Because the parties are at different levels, it is a form of vertical conflict. The bypassed member's functions do not disappear; someone else must now perform them.","example":"A mattress maker that always sold through furniture stores launches a direct-to-consumer website, and its retailers protest.","contrast":"Disintermediation (bypassing a member, no ownership) vs forward/backward integration (owning another stage) vs dual distribution (adding a second channel type, which can trigger disintermediation-type conflict) vs horizontal conflict (members at the same level).","trap":"Cue: 'bypasses', 'cuts out the middleman', 'sells or buys directly instead of through'. It works on the buying side too. Do not choose horizontal conflict, because the bypassed member is at a different level.","mnemonic":"Dis-INTERMEDIARY-ation = taking the intermediary out.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-logistics","topic":"t10-5","term":"Logistics, customer service and total logistics cost","definition":"Logistics: activities that focus on getting the right amount of the right products to the right place at the right time at the lowest possible cost. Logistics is built around the expected level of customer service (time or order cycle, dependability, communication, convenience) and aims to minimize total logistics cost: the expenses of transportation, materials handling and warehousing, inventory, stockouts, order processing, and return goods handling.","explain":"Logistics requires collaboration, coordination and information sharing among manufacturers, suppliers and distributors so the flow is seamless. Its costs trade off against each other: faster shipping raises transportation cost but can lower inventory and stockout costs; fewer warehouses lower warehousing cost but raise transport cost. The goal is the lowest TOTAL cost for the level of service customers expect, not the lowest cost of any single activity. The four key logistics functions are transportation, order processing, inventory management and warehousing.","example":"A Calgary office-supply distributor promises next-day delivery (time), meets that promise 99% of the time (dependability), sends tracking alerts (communication) and offers easy online ordering (convenience), while weighing faster trucking costs against lower inventory costs.","contrast":"Logistics (moving goods efficiently) vs supply chain management (integrating information and logistics across all firms in the chain) vs marketing channel (the parties that distribute and promote). Within customer service, 'time' is the order cycle or lead time, while 'dependability' is how consistently that time is met.","trap":"Definition cue: 'right amount, right products, right place, right time, lowest possible cost' = logistics. Total cost questions: add every cost that changes (transportation, warehousing, inventory, stockouts, order processing, returns) and never judge by one cost alone. Advertising is not a logistics cost.","mnemonic":"Four rights at the lowest cost; service = Time, Dependability, Communication, Convenience.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Key logistics functions in a supply chain","head":["Function","Key ideas"],"rows":[["Transportation","Criteria: cost, time, capability, dependability, accessibility, frequency; modes: rail, truck, air, water, pipeline, intermodal"],["Order processing","Electronic data interchange (EDI)"],["Inventory management","Just-in-time (JIT); also vendor-managed inventory (VMI) and ECR/quick response"],["Warehousing","Public or private; traditional storage warehouses vs distribution centres with cross-docking; or a combination"]]}},{"id":"c10-scm","topic":"t10-5","term":"Supply chain management (supply chain vs marketing channel, Figure 10-9)","definition":"Supply chain management is the integration and organization of information and logistics activities across firms in a supply chain for the purpose of creating and delivering goods and services that provide value to consumers. A supply chain runs Suppliers -> Producer -> Consumers and includes the supplier network (all parties supplying raw materials and parts) plus the marketing channel (all parties involved in distributing and promoting the product).","explain":"The supply chain is the whole sequence from raw-material suppliers through the producer to consumers. SCM manages it as one system, sharing information (forecasts, inventory data) and coordinating logistics across company lines. The marketing channel is only the downstream part; the supplier network is the upstream part. The supply chain must be harmonized with the marketing strategy, built to deliver the value the target market expects.","example":"A bike maker shares real-time sales data and forecasts with its tire suppliers and its retailers so that production and deliveries match demand.","contrast":"Supply chain (supplier network + producer + marketing channel) vs marketing channel (distributing and promoting only). Logistics management coordinates the movement of goods throughout; supply chain management integrates information AND logistics across all parties.","trap":"If a stem mentions raw-material or parts suppliers, it is describing the supply chain, not just the marketing channel. 'Integration and organization of information and logistics activities across firms' = SCM; 'right products, right place, right time, lowest cost' = logistics.","mnemonic":"Supply chain = Supplier network + Marketing channel.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 10-9 Supply chain vs marketing channel","head":["Element","What it covers"],"rows":[["Supplier network","All parties that supply raw materials and parts to the producer"],["Producer","Makes the product"],["Marketing channel","All parties involved in distributing and promoting the product to consumers"],["Logistics management","Coordinates the movement of goods throughout the chain"],["Supply chain management","Integrates information and logistics activities across all parties, from suppliers to consumers"]]}},{"id":"c10-transportation","topic":"t10-5","term":"Transportation: criteria and modes","definition":"Transportation alternatives are evaluated on six criteria: cost, time, capability, dependability, accessibility and frequency. The modes are rail, truck, air, water, pipeline and intermodal (combinations of modes).","explain":"Cost = what the carrier charges; time = speed of delivery; capability = ability to provide the right equipment and conditions (e.g., refrigeration, oversized loads); dependability = reliability in delivering on time without loss or damage; accessibility = ability to reach the needed locations or routes; frequency = how often shipments can be scheduled. Air is fastest but costliest; water and rail move bulky goods cheaply but slowly; trucks are flexible and accessible; pipelines carry liquids and gases; intermodal combines modes.","example":"A vaccine maker that needs -20 C refrigeration focuses on capability; a jeweller shipping small, high-value parcels across the country chooses air for speed.","contrast":"Capability (right equipment) vs accessibility (can reach the place); dependability (consistency) vs frequency (how often). Transportation criteria differ from the product factors in channel choice (perishability, unit value).","trap":"Swapped definitions are the main trick. 'Perishability' is a channel-choice product factor, not one of the six transportation criteria. Intermodal is a combination of modes, not a warehousing practice like cross-docking.","mnemonic":"'Can The Carrier Deliver Any Freight?' = Cost, Time, Capability, Dependability, Accessibility, Frequency.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-order-inventory","topic":"t10-5","term":"Order processing (EDI) and inventory management (JIT, VMI, ECR/QR)","definition":"Order processing increasingly uses electronic data interchange (EDI): computers in different firms exchange documents such as purchase orders and invoices electronically. Firms hold inventory to buffer variations in supply and demand, provide better service, promote efficiency and speculate. Just-in-time (JIT): an inventory supply system with very low inventories that requires fast, on-time delivery; suppliers deliver less merchandise more frequently. Vendor-managed inventory (VMI): the supplier determines the product amount and assortment a customer needs and automatically delivers it. Efficient consumer response (ECR) / quick response (QR): inventory management systems designed to reduce the retailer's lead time for receiving merchandise, lowering inventory investment and improving customer service.","explain":"Inventory is useful but expensive, so modern systems try to keep less of it without running out. EDI speeds up and automates ordering. JIT shrinks stock by having suppliers deliver small amounts often. VMI hands the reorder decision to the supplier. ECR/QR shortens how long a retailer waits for replenishment, so it can carry less stock while keeping shelves full.","example":"A pharmacy whose supplier reads its sales data and restocks automatically (VMI); a car plant receiving parts several times a day (JIT); a hardware chain exchanging purchase orders and invoices computer-to-computer (EDI).","contrast":"JIT = low inventory, small and frequent deliveries; VMI = the SUPPLIER decides and replenishes; ECR/QR = shorten the RETAILER's lead time; EDI = electronic exchange of order documents; cross-docking = a warehousing practice with little or no storage.","trap":"'Less merchandise on a more frequent basis' = JIT (official question). 'Supplier determines amount and assortment and automatically delivers' = VMI. 'Computer-to-computer purchase orders and invoices' = EDI. 'Reduce the retailer's lead time' = ECR/QR. Do not pick 'time utility' just because JIT is about timing.","mnemonic":"JIT = Just In Time; VMI = the Vendor Minds the Inventory; EDI = Electronic Documents Instantly.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-warehousing","topic":"t10-5","term":"Warehousing: public vs private, storage warehouses vs distribution centres, cross-docking","definition":"Warehouses can be public (space and services rented from independent operators) or private (owned or operated by the firm), or a combination. Traditional storage warehouses hold goods for moderate to long periods; distribution centres are designed to facilitate the timely movement of goods. Cross-docking unloads inbound shipments and loads them onto outbound vehicles with little or no storage.","explain":"The ownership decision (public vs private) is separate from the purpose decision (store vs keep moving). Public space suits seasonal or uncertain needs; private space suits steady, high volumes. Distribution centres and cross-docking keep products flowing quickly toward customers, which reduces inventory.","example":"A grocery chain's distribution centre cross-docks produce from inbound trucks to store-bound trucks within hours; a toy company rents public warehouse space only for the Christmas peak.","contrast":"Storage warehouse (hold) vs distribution centre (move). Public (rent) vs private (own). Cross-docking (warehousing practice) vs JIT (inventory system) vs VMI (who decides replenishment).","trap":"'Little or no storage' or 'straight from the inbound to the outbound dock' = cross-docking. 'Rent space from an independent operator' = public warehouse. Long-term holding = storage warehouse, not distribution centre.","mnemonic":"Storage warehouse = Store; Distribution centre = Dispatch.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-reverse-logistics","topic":"t10-5","term":"Reverse logistics","definition":"Reverse logistics is a process of reclaiming recyclable and reusable materials, returns and reworks from the point of consumption or use for repair, remanufacturing, redistribution or disposal.","explain":"Most logistics moves goods forward to the customer; reverse logistics moves them back: returns, worn-out products, packaging and recyclable materials, so they can be repaired, remanufactured, redistributed or disposed of responsibly. It recovers value and supports sustainability goals.","example":"H&M accepts used clothing in its stores and recycles it into new textiles.","contrast":"Reverse logistics (products flowing back FROM the consumer) vs backward integration (OWNERSHIP of upstream suppliers) vs disintermediation (bypassing a channel member).","trap":"Pick the option where products flow back from the point of consumption. Shipping to customers, buying from local farmers or a promotional event are forward-channel or promotion activities (official question 8).","mnemonic":"Reverse = back from the customer: Return, Repair, Remanufacture, Recycle.","figure":null},{"id":"c10-3pl","topic":"t10-5","term":"Third-party logistics providers (3PLs)","definition":"Third-party logistics providers are firms that perform most or all of the logistics functions that manufacturers, suppliers and distributors would normally perform themselves.","explain":"Many firms outsource logistics (warehousing, transportation, order fulfilment, returns) to specialists that serve many clients and can do the work more efficiently. This follows the principle that whoever performs a channel function should add value; the functions are still performed, just by a specialist.","example":"An online clothing start-up hands warehousing, order picking, shipping and returns to an outside logistics firm that serves dozens of brands.","contrast":"3PL (logistics service for a fee, typically without taking title) vs wholesaler or distributor (takes title and resells) vs agent or broker (negotiates sales) vs VMI (supplier manages a customer's inventory) vs strategic channel alliance (selling through another firm's marketing channel).","trap":"Cue: 'an outside firm performs most or all logistics functions for other firms'. Do not confuse with a strategic channel alliance, which is about selling, not outsourcing logistics.","mnemonic":"Third party = a logistics specialist that is neither the buyer nor the seller.","figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"a10-01","type":"sort","concept":"c10-channel-functions","title":"Sort the intermediary functions","prompt":"Drag each intermediary activity into the function family it belongs to.","buckets":["Transactional","Logistical","Facilitating"],"items":[{"text":"A wholesaler purchases 500 cases of soup to resell to corner stores","bucket":0},{"text":"A distributor's reps call on retailers, promote new products and take orders","bucket":0},{"text":"A wholesaler absorbs the loss when unsold Halloween costumes go out of season","bucket":0},{"text":"A hardware wholesaler gathers tools from 12 manufacturers into one catalogue","bucket":1},{"text":"A wholesaler keeps snowblowers in a warehouse near its dealers until winter","bucket":1},{"text":"A produce wholesaler buys potatoes by the truckload and repacks them into 5 kg bags","bucket":1},{"text":"A distributor's trucks deliver orders to stores across Saskatchewan","bucket":1},{"text":"A wholesaler lets small retailers pay 60 days after delivery","bucket":2},{"text":"A wholesaler shares sales-trend and competitor-pricing reports with its suppliers","bucket":2},{"text":"A grain handler inspects wheat and assigns it a quality grade","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Transactional = buying, selling and risk-taking (ownership and the deal). Logistical = selection (assorting), storing, sorting (bulk breaking) and transporting (the physical product). Facilitating = financing, marketing information and research, and grading (making exchange easier). Watch the traps: risk-taking is transactional and grading is facilitating, not logistical."},{"id":"a10-02","type":"sort","concept":"c10-vms","title":"Corporate, contractual or administered?","prompt":"Drag each example to the type of vertical marketing system it illustrates.","buckets":["Corporate VMS","Contractual VMS","Administered VMS"],"items":[{"text":"Apple sells through its own Apple Stores","bucket":0},{"text":"A coffee chain owns the bakery that makes its cookies","bucket":0},{"text":"Ralph Lauren operates its own Polo stores","bucket":0},{"text":"IGA: a wholesaler coordinates independently owned grocers","bucket":1},{"text":"Home Hardware: independent retailers jointly own their wholesale operation","bucket":1},{"text":"Coca-Cola licenses independent bottlers in each territory","bucket":1},{"text":"Independently owned Ford dealerships","bucket":1},{"text":"McDonald's franchised restaurants","bucket":1},{"text":"Procter & Gamble's influence gets retailers to follow its display plans","bucket":2},{"text":"Walmart's buying power leads suppliers to adopt its delivery standards","bucket":2},{"text":"A dominant toy maker persuades independent stores to follow its merchandising guidelines, with no ownership or contract","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Corporate = one firm OWNS successive stages (forward integration like Apple or Ralph Lauren stores; backward integration like a coffee chain owning its bakery). Contractual = independent firms bound by CONTRACTS (voluntary chains, cooperatives, franchises). Administered = coordination through the SIZE AND INFLUENCE of one member (P&G, Walmart)."},{"id":"a10-03","type":"match","concept":"c10-coverage-intensity","title":"Coverage, product type and example","prompt":"Match each coverage strategy or product type with the example or description that fits it.","pairs":[{"left":"Intensive distribution","right":"Gum and batteries stocked in every gas station, grocery store and drugstore in Alberta"},{"left":"Selective distribution","right":"A washer-dryer line carried by three appliance retailers in Edmonton"},{"left":"Exclusive distribution","right":"A single Calgary dealer authorized to sell a luxury sports car brand"},{"left":"Convenience goods","right":"Product type matched with intensive coverage: bought often, with little shopping effort"},{"left":"Shopping goods","right":"Product type matched with selective coverage: buyers compare a few stores on price and features"},{"left":"Specialty goods","right":"Product type matched with exclusive coverage: buyers will seek out the one dealer that carries the brand"}],"explanation":"Coverage intensity follows product type: convenience goods -> intensive (as many outlets as possible), shopping goods -> selective (a few retailers per area), specialty goods -> exclusive (only one retailer per area)."},{"id":"a10-04","type":"order","concept":"c10-global-channels","title":"Build the global channel","prompt":"Put the links of the global channel of distribution (Figure 10-7) in order, from the seller to the final consumer.","items":["Seller","Seller's international marketing headquarters","Channels between nations","Channels within foreign nations","Final consumer"],"explanation":"The seller's international marketing headquarters manages the global effort, channels between nations move the product across borders, and channels within foreign nations distribute it locally to the final consumer. Sophistication increases as a country's economic infrastructure develops."},{"id":"a10-05","type":"order","concept":"c10-consumer-channels","title":"Order consumer channel D","prompt":"Put the members of the longest consumer channel (channel D) in order, from producer to consumer.","items":["Producer","Agent","Wholesaler","Retailer","Consumer"],"explanation":"Channel D is Producer -> Agent -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer. The agent brings producers and wholesalers together without taking title (e.g., Mars candy); the wholesaler then supplies retailers, which sell to consumers."},{"id":"a10-06","type":"sort","concept":"c10-channel-choice-factors","title":"Market, product or company factor?","prompt":"Drag each factor affecting channel choice into the correct group.","buckets":["Market factors","Product factors","Company factors"],"items":[{"text":"Geographic concentration of buyers","bucket":0},{"text":"Number of potential customers","bucket":0},{"text":"Type of market (consumer or business)","bucket":0},{"text":"Order size","bucket":0},{"text":"Technical complexity","bucket":1},{"text":"Perishability","bucket":1},{"text":"Unit value","bucket":1},{"text":"Product life cycle stage","bucket":1},{"text":"Financial resources and ability of management","bucket":2},{"text":"Desire for channel control","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Market factors describe the buyers (where they are, how many, what kind of market, how much they order). Product factors describe the offering (technical, perishable, unit value, PLC stage). Company factors describe the firm (resources, management ability, desire for control)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q10-001","concept":"c10-marketing-channel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The individuals and firms involved in the process of making a product or service available for use or consumption by consumers or industrial users make up a:","options":["supply chain","supplier network","vertical marketing system","marketing channel"],"answer":3,"explanation":"This is the textbook definition of a marketing channel. 'Supply chain' is tempting but broader: it also includes the supplier network of raw-material and parts suppliers upstream of the producer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-002","concept":"c10-marketing-channel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northern Harvest, a Nova Scotia blueberry grower, sells frozen berries through a food broker, who arranges sales to a wholesaler, which supplies Sobeys stores. In channel terms, the broker, the wholesaler and Sobeys are all:","options":["intermediaries in the marketing channel","members of the supplier network","third-party logistics providers","channel captains"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Intermediaries are individuals or firms performing a role in the marketing channel, involved in making a product available. These firms are downstream of the grower, so they are not the supplier network (which provides raw materials and parts), and nothing says any of them leads the whole channel.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-003","concept":"c10-marketing-channel","also":["c10-scm"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Velo Laurentien, a Quebec bicycle maker, buys steel tubing from a mill and sells finished bikes through sporting goods retailers. Which statement correctly separates its marketing channel from its supply chain?","options":["The retailers are part of the supply chain but not the marketing channel; the mill is part of both","The marketing channel and the supply chain are identical and include exactly the same firms","The mill is part of the supply chain but not the marketing channel; the retailers are part of both","Only the mill and the bike maker belong to the marketing channel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In Figure 10-9 the supply chain = supplier network (raw materials and parts, like the mill) + marketing channel (parties that distribute and promote the product, like retailers). So retailers sit in both, while the mill sits only in the supply chain.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-004","concept":"c10-marketing-channel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the chapter, why do buyers benefit from a well-structured, efficient marketing channel?","options":["A longer channel always lowers the final price because each intermediary adds a discount","It makes products available where and when buyers want them while keeping the cost of reaching them down","It removes the need for channel functions such as storing and transporting","It guarantees that every product is sold directly by its producer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reaching buyers efficiently is the point of a channel: intermediaries cut the number of contacts and perform functions that make products conveniently available. Channel functions never disappear, and efficient channels are often indirect rather than direct.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-005","concept":"c10-marketing-channel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an intermediary in the marketing channel of Crunchwell, a Manitoba snack maker?","options":["The farm co-operative that supplies Crunchwell with corn and sunflower oil","The food broker that negotiates listings with grocery chains on Crunchwell's behalf","The wholesaler that resells cases of Crunchwell snacks to convenience stores","The grocery chain that sells Crunchwell snacks to shoppers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A raw-material supplier belongs to the supplier network (the upstream part of the supply chain), not the marketing channel. The broker, wholesaler and grocer all help make the finished snack available to buyers, so they are intermediaries.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-006","concept":"c10-channel-tradeoffs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the instructor's framing of channel decisions, which two elements must always be traded off against each other?","options":["Price and promotion","Coverage and exclusivity","Cost and level of service","Ownership and control"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The instructor's 'two key trade-offs' are cost and level of service: more service (speed, more outlets, support) usually costs more. Coverage (intensive, selective, exclusive) is a separate channel design consideration.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-007","concept":"c10-channel-tradeoffs","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"The instructor asks, 'What must every channel partner provide?' The answer is _____: an intermediary earns its place only if it performs its functions more efficiently or effectively than the producer could alone.","options":["exclusivity","value","financing","ownership"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every channel partner must add value. Financing is one function some partners provide, but not every partner must provide it; exclusivity and ownership are not requirements of channel membership.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-008","concept":"c10-channel-tradeoffs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Glacier Gear, an Edmonton maker of ski goggles, can ship every online order overnight from its own warehouse with a personal follow-up call, or sell through regional sporting goods stores that hold stock. The overnight option delights customers but costs far more per unit. Which instructor concept is Glacier Gear wrestling with?","options":["The choice between forward and backward integration","The difference between vertical and horizontal conflict","The choice between selective and exclusive distribution","The trade-off between cost and level of service"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Higher service (overnight shipping, personal follow-up) comes at a higher cost, which is the instructor's core channel trade-off. Integration would mean owning another channel stage, and selective vs exclusive concerns how many outlets per area, neither of which is the dilemma described.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-009","concept":"c10-channel-tradeoffs","also":["c10-channel-functions"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Regina furniture maker reviews the regional wholesaler that buys, warehouses and resells its products to retailers. It finds it could do the same storage and delivery itself at lower cost and with better service to the same retailers. Based on the instructor's channel principle, what follows?","options":["The wholesaler must be kept, because every channel needs at least one wholesaler","The maker should grant the wholesaler exclusive distribution to motivate it","The wholesaler is not adding value, so the maker should consider taking over its functions","Once the wholesaler is removed, its storage and delivery functions will no longer be needed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Every channel partner must provide value; if the producer can perform the functions more efficiently, the intermediary is a candidate for removal. The functions themselves never vanish: the maker would now perform the storage and delivery.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-010","concept":"c10-channel-tradeoffs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the instructor's key channel trade-offs is NOT correct?","options":["Raising the level of service usually raises channel cost","Every channel partner is expected to add value","Channel choices balance what buyers expect against what it costs to provide it","The lowest-cost channel is always the best choice, regardless of the service buyers expect"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Channel design balances cost against level of service; choosing purely on lowest cost ignores the service side of the trade-off. The other statements restate the instructor's two trade-offs and the value requirement.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-011","concept":"c10-intermediary-terms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Coastal Reps, a Vancouver firm, negotiates sales of a seafood processor's products to restaurant chains and earns a commission. It has legal authority to act for the processor but never owns the seafood. Coastal Reps is best described as a(n):","options":["agent or broker","wholesaler","distributor","retailer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An agent or broker has legal authority to act on behalf of another channel member and does not take title. A wholesaler or distributor buys (takes title to) the goods it resells.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-012","concept":"c10-intermediary-terms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fraser Valley Supply buys canned goods from processors and resells them in case lots to independent grocery and convenience stores across B.C. Which intermediary term fits best?","options":["Agent","Wholesaler","Retailer","Broker"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A wholesaler sells to other intermediaries, usually retailers, and the term usually applies to consumer goods. It is not a retailer because it does not sell to consumers, and not an agent or broker because it takes title to the goods.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-013","concept":"c10-intermediary-terms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Industrial Supply stocks bearings, belts and hydraulic hoses, sells them to farm-equipment repair shops and mining companies, and lets customers pay on 60-day terms. Which term is usually used for this kind of intermediary?","options":["Retailer","Broker","Distributor","Agent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A distributor performs many functions (selling, maintaining inventory, extending credit), and the term is usually used for business markets. A broker or agent would not hold inventory or take title, and a retailer sells to final consumers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-014","concept":"c10-intermediary-terms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a distributor from a wholesaler as the terms are used in Figure 10-1?","options":["A distributor never takes title to the goods it sells, whereas a wholesaler always takes title","A distributor sells only to final consumers, whereas a wholesaler sells only to manufacturers","Both terms mean an intermediary with legal authority to act on a manufacturer's behalf","'Distributor' usually means a multi-function intermediary in business markets; 'wholesaler' usually means consumer goods"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Figure 10-1: a distributor performs a variety of functions and the term is usually used for business markets, while a wholesaler sells to other intermediaries (usually retailers) and usually refers to consumer goods. Legal authority to act on another's behalf describes an agent or broker.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-015","concept":"c10-intermediary-terms","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In Figure 10-1, 'dealer' is a general term that can mean the same as a distributor, a retailer or a wholesaler.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. 'Dealer' is the catch-all term in Figure 10-1; 'middleman', the other generic word, simply means an intermediary.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-016","concept":"c10-intermediary-terms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about marketing intermediary terms is NOT correct?","options":["An agent takes title to the goods it sells on a manufacturer's behalf","'Middleman' is another name for intermediary","A retailer sells to consumers","A wholesaler sells to other intermediaries, usually retailers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Agents and brokers act on behalf of another channel member but do NOT take title; that is their defining feature. The other statements match Figure 10-1.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-017","concept":"c10-intermediary-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"As in Figure 10-2, four producers each want to sell to four buyers. How many contacts are needed with no intermediary, and how many when all trade goes through one intermediary?","options":["8 without; 4 with","16 without; 8 with","16 without; 4 with","8 without; 16 with"],"answer":1,"explanation":"No intermediary: producers x buyers = 4 x 4 = 16. One intermediary: producers + buyers = 4 + 4 = 8. Answering 4 with the intermediary counts only one side, and 8 without / 16 with reverses the two formulas.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-018","concept":"c10-intermediary-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Six Okanagan wineries each want to sell to five Calgary restaurants. How many contacts are ELIMINATED if all sales go through one wine distributor?","options":["11","19","30","25"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without the distributor: 6 x 5 = 30 contacts. With it: 6 + 5 = 11 contacts. Contacts eliminated = 30 - 11 = 19. Choosing 11 confuses the contacts that remain with the contacts saved.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-019","concept":"c10-intermediary-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does inserting one intermediary between many producers and many buyers reduce the total number of transactions?","options":["Each party contacts only the intermediary, so contacts equal producers + buyers rather than producers x buyers","The intermediary takes title to fewer products than the producers did, so fewer sales are recorded","Buyers purchase less often when an intermediary is present, so fewer orders are placed","The intermediary eliminates transporting and storing, so fewer shipments are needed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The saving comes from the structure of contacts: everyone connects to one hub instead of to each other. Intermediaries do not eliminate logistical functions such as transporting and storing; they perform them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-020","concept":"c10-intermediary-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rather than ordering parkas only when customers ask, Northwind Outfitters in Yellowknife keeps a deep stock all year so a customer can buy one the moment a cold snap hits. Which utility is Northwind mainly creating?","options":["Form utility","Place utility","Possession utility","Time utility"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Time utility means having the product available WHEN the buyer wants it, here the instant a cold snap hits. Place utility is about WHERE it is available; the stem stresses timing, not location.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-021","concept":"c10-intermediary-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Halifax appliance retailer offers in-store financing so a young couple can take home a $2,400 fridge today and pay over 18 months. Which utility does the financing create, and how does it differ from place utility?","options":["Time utility: it delivers the fridge quickly; place utility would be about the credit terms","Form utility: it changes the fridge's features; place utility would be about the payment plan","Possession utility: it eases ownership; place utility would be having the fridge at a convenient location","Place utility: the couple takes the fridge home; possession utility would be about warehousing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Possession utility is created by making it easier to acquire and own a product, for example through credit. Place utility is having the product where the buyer wants it, which has nothing to do with payment terms.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-022","concept":"c10-intermediary-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about how intermediaries create value is NOT correct?","options":["Adding an intermediary always increases the number of transactions in the channel","With one intermediary, contacts equal the number of producers plus the number of buyers","Without an intermediary, contacts equal the number of producers multiplied by the number of buyers","Intermediaries can create time, place, form and possession utility"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Figure 10-2 shows the opposite: one intermediary cuts 4 x 4 = 16 contacts to 4 + 4 = 8. The other statements are the contact formulas and the four utilities.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-023","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A supermarket chain buys fruits and vegetables from different sources, transfers them to its stores, and arranges them for its customers. Which of the following functions is the store performing as an intermediary?","options":["Transactional function","Informational function","Logistical function","Facilitating function"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Gathering products from different sources (selection/assorting), moving them (transporting) and arranging them for shoppers are logistical activities. 'Buys' tempts students toward transactional, but the dominant activity described is gathering, moving and arranging the produce; 'informational function' is not one of the three families.","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-024","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Leaf Hardware Supply buys hammers from six manufacturers, drills from four others and paint from three more, so a contractor can find everything in one catalogue. Which specific activity is it performing?","options":["Sorting","Risk-taking","Selection (assorting)","Selling"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Selection (assorting) is putting together a selection of products from several different sources, a logistical activity. Sorting is different: buying large quantities of a product and dividing them into smaller amounts.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-025","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Lethbridge produce wholesaler buys potatoes by the truckload from growers and repacks them into 10-pound bags for small grocers. This is an example of:","options":["selection, a transactional function","sorting, a logistical function","buying, a facilitating function","storing, a transactional function"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Purchasing in large quantities and dividing into smaller amounts is sorting (bulk breaking), which is logistical. Selection would be gathering products from many sources, and neither selection nor storing is transactional.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-026","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northern Plains Distributors lets its rural hardware-store customers pay for spring inventory 90 days after delivery. Which function family does this represent?","options":["Transactional","Logistical","Promotional","Facilitating"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Extending credit to customers is financing, a facilitating function. It is not transactional (buying, selling, risk-taking) even though money is involved.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-027","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Montreal clothing wholesaler buys 20,000 winter jackets in March for next season and will absorb the loss if a warm winter leaves them unsold. Which activity, and which function family, does this illustrate?","options":["Storing, a logistical function","Financing, a facilitating function","Risk-taking, a transactional function","Sorting, a logistical function"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Assuming the business risk of owning inventory is risk-taking, a transactional function. Students often file risk under facilitating, but Figure 10-3 places risk-taking with buying and selling because it comes from ownership.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-028","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"TrueNorth Foods Wholesale sends its suppliers monthly reports on which snack flavours are gaining share in Atlantic Canada and what competitors are discounting. This is:","options":["selling and promotion, a transactional function","marketing information and research, a facilitating function","selection and assorting, a logistical function","risk-taking on inventory, a transactional function"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Providing information to customers and suppliers, including competitive conditions and trends, is marketing information and research, a facilitating function. It is not selling, because no products are being promoted and no orders sought.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-029","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"An electronics distributor's sales reps call on retailers, demonstrate new products and take orders. Which function family is being performed?","options":["Facilitating","Logistical","Informational","Transactional"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Contacting potential customers, promoting products and seeking orders is selling, a transactional function. Demonstrations share information, but the purpose is to sell; 'informational' is not one of the three families.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-030","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Before reselling, a Prairie grain handler inspects each farmer's wheat, tests its protein content and assigns it a quality grade. In Figure 10-3 terms, this is:","options":["grading, a facilitating function","sorting, a logistical function","buying, a transactional function","storing, a logistical function"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inspecting, testing and assigning quality grades is grading, listed with the facilitating functions. In channel vocabulary 'sorting' does not mean sorting by quality; it means buying in bulk and dividing into smaller amounts.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-031","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Thunder Bay wholesaler keeps snowblowers in a regional warehouse close to its dealers and runs its own trucks to deliver them when dealers reorder. Both activities belong to which function family?","options":["Transactional","Facilitating","Logistical for storing, but transactional for transporting","Logistical"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Storing (assembling and protecting products at a convenient location) and transporting (physically moving products to customers) are both logistical functions in Figure 10-3. Transporting is not transactional; transactional covers buying, selling and risk-taking.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-032","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pair places both activities in the SAME function family?","options":["Risk-taking and financing","Buying and storing","Sorting and selection (assorting)","Transporting and marketing research"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sorting and selection are both logistical. Risk-taking (transactional) and financing (facilitating) is the classic trap pair; buying is transactional while storing is logistical; transporting is logistical while marketing research is facilitating.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-033","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a logistical function?","options":["Risk-taking","Storing","Sorting","Transporting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Risk-taking is transactional because it comes from owning inventory. The logistical functions are selection (assorting), storing, sorting and transporting.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-034","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"All of the following are transactional functions EXCEPT:","options":["buying","financing","selling","risk-taking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Financing (extending credit to customers) is a facilitating function. The transactional functions are buying, selling and risk-taking.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-035","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Purchasing products for resale is called _____, while purchasing in large quantities and dividing them into smaller amounts is called _____.","options":["sorting; buying","selection; storing","buying; sorting","buying; selection"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Buying (transactional) is purchasing products for resale; sorting (logistical) is buying in large quantities and dividing into smaller amounts. Selection means gathering products from several different sources.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-036","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":["c10-direct-indirect"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Alder Furniture cuts out its wholesaler and begins selling directly to retailers. What happens to the storing, sorting and financing functions the wholesaler used to perform?","options":["They disappear, because no intermediary remains to perform them","They must still be performed, by Alder, by the retailers or by someone else","They automatically shift to consumers","They become transactional functions once the wholesaler is gone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You can eliminate an intermediary, but not its functions: products still have to be stored, broken into smaller lots and financed. The work shifts to another channel member, which is why removing a partner only pays if someone else can do the job more efficiently.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-037","concept":"c10-channel-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cold Lake Seafoods is choosing a channel partner. It most needs a partner that will extend credit to small restaurant customers and supply data on menu and competitor trends. Which type of functions is it primarily looking for?","options":["Logistical functions","Transactional functions","Sorting functions","Facilitating functions"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Financing (credit) and marketing information and research are both facilitating functions. Logistical functions (selection, storing, sorting, transporting) handle the physical product, which is not the need described.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-038","concept":"c10-direct-indirect","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When a company uses intermediaries like wholesalers and retailers to distribute its products, it is following which distribution strategy?","options":["Direct","Indirect","Online","Indeterminate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In indirect marketing channels, intermediaries are inserted between the producer and consumers and perform numerous channel functions. 'Online' is not a channel type: online sales can be direct (a brand's own site) or indirect (through Amazon).","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-039","concept":"c10-direct-indirect","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aurora Skincare sells only through its own website and ships every order from its Kelowna facility to the customer's door. No other company resells its products. Which channel is Aurora using?","options":["Direct channel","Indirect channel","Dual distribution","Strategic channel alliance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A direct channel is one in which the producer and the ultimate consumer deal directly with each other, with no intermediaries. Dual distribution would require two or more types of channels; Aurora uses only one.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-040","concept":"c10-direct-indirect","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts direct and indirect channels?","options":["A direct channel is any channel that uses the Internet; an indirect channel uses physical stores","Direct: producer and ultimate consumer deal with each other; indirect: intermediaries are inserted between them","A direct channel always has lower total costs than an indirect channel for the same product","In an indirect channel the producer still performs all of the channel functions itself"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The test is whether intermediaries exist, not the medium used. Direct channels are not automatically cheaper because intermediaries often perform functions more efficiently, and in indirect channels the intermediaries take on many of the functions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-041","concept":"c10-direct-indirect","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A small Ontario jam maker that sells its jars through Amazon's online store is using a direct channel because the sale happens online.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Corrected statement: the jam maker is using an indirect channel because Amazon is an intermediary between it and the consumer. A channel is direct only when producer and consumer deal with each other with no intermediaries, online or offline.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-042","concept":"c10-direct-indirect","also":["c10-channel-choice-factors"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Peak Analytics makes complex, custom-configured laboratory instruments bought by a small number of hospitals and universities, each needing extensive technical consultation before purchase. Which channel approach fits best?","options":["An indirect channel through wholesalers and retailers","Intensive distribution through as many outlets as possible","A long channel with an agent, a wholesaler and a retailer","A direct channel using the company's own technical sales force"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Technical complexity, high unit value and few buyers all point to a short, direct channel where the producer can provide expert information. Long indirect channels suit low-value, standardized products sold to many buyers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-043","concept":"c10-consumer-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Polar Pops, a small Winnipeg candy maker, wants its chocolate bars in thousands of convenience stores across Canada. According to the chapter, which consumer channel is most common for low-cost, low-unit-value items like this?","options":["Producer -> Consumer","Producer -> Retailer -> Consumer","Producer -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer","Producer -> Industrial distributor -> Industrial user"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Channel C (producer, wholesaler, retailer, consumer) is the most common for low-cost, low-unit-value items such as candy and magazines: the wholesaler breaks bulk and reaches many small stores economically. Producer -> Retailer -> Consumer works when a large retailer buys directly, which thousands of small convenience stores cannot do.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-044","concept":"c10-consumer-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Canadian Tire buys barbecues directly from the manufacturer in huge volumes and sells them to shoppers in its stores. Which consumer channel structure is this?","options":["Producer -> Retailer -> Consumer","Producer -> Consumer","Producer -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer","Producer -> Agent -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Channel B is used when a large retailer such as Walmart or Canadian Tire buys directly from the producer, so no wholesaler is needed. Channel C adds a wholesaler, typical for low-unit-value items sold through many small retailers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-045","concept":"c10-consumer-channels","also":["c10-intermediary-terms"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Sweet Prairie, a large candy maker, uses independent agents who earn commissions for connecting it with regional wholesalers; the wholesalers then supply retailers. Which channel is this, and what role does the agent play?","options":["Producer -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer; 'agent' is just another name for the wholesaler","Producer -> Agent -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer; the agent links producer and wholesalers, without title","Producer -> Retailer -> Consumer; the agent acts as the retailer selling to shoppers","Producer -> Agent -> Consumer; the agent takes title and sells directly to shoppers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Channel D adds an agent in front of the wholesaler; agents bring producers and wholesalers together (Mars candy is the chapter's example) and, unlike wholesalers, do not take title. An agent is not just another name for a wholesaler.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-046","concept":"c10-consumer-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which consumer channel example is NOT correctly matched with its structure?","options":["Mary Kay: Producer -> Consumer","Walmart buying from manufacturers: Producer -> Retailer -> Consumer","Dell selling computers on its own website: Producer -> Agent -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer","Magazines sold in corner stores: Producer -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dell selling on its own site is the chapter's example of the direct channel A (Producer -> Consumer), not the longest channel D. The other three pairings match the chapter's figure.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-047","concept":"c10-consumer-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In consumer channel D, which member comes immediately after the producer?","options":["Wholesaler","Retailer","Industrial distributor","Agent"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Channel D runs Producer -> Agent -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer, so the agent comes right after the producer. Industrial distributors appear only in business (B2B) channels.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-048","concept":"c10-business-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rockies Heavy Equipment buys loaders and excavators from a manufacturer, holds them in inventory, and sells and services them for construction firms in its region. In a business channel, Rockies is acting as a(n):","options":["industrial distributor","agent","retailer","broker"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An industrial distributor performs functions similar to a wholesaler in business markets: taking title, holding inventory and reselling to industrial users (the chapter's Caterpillar example). Agents and brokers do not take title or possession, and retailers sell to consumers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-049","concept":"c10-business-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"In business channel D (Producer -> Agent -> Industrial distributor -> Industrial user), how do the agent and the industrial distributor differ?","options":["The agent takes title and holds the inventory; the industrial distributor only negotiates sales for a commission","Both take title to the goods, but only the agent extends credit to industrial users","The agent arranges sales without taking title or possession; the distributor takes title and holds inventory","The agent sells to final consumers; the industrial distributor sells mainly to retailers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Agents do not take title or physical possession of goods; they negotiate on the producer's behalf. The industrial distributor, like a wholesaler, buys, stocks and resells to industrial users. Neither sells to consumers in a B2B channel.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-050","concept":"c10-business-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hartland Sensors, a small Ontario maker of industrial sensors, cannot afford its own sales force but wants to reach manufacturing plants across Canada. Sensors ship straight from its factory to each plant. Which business channel fits best?","options":["Producer -> Industrial user, using its own sales force","Producer -> Wholesaler -> Retailer -> Consumer","Producer -> Retailer -> Consumer","Producer -> Agent -> Industrial user"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An agent can represent the producer and negotiate sales without the firm building a sales force, and because goods ship directly from the factory no distributor is needed to hold inventory. The direct option requires the sales force Hartland cannot afford, and the other two are consumer channels.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-051","concept":"c10-business-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which is NOT one of the business (B2B) channel structures described in the chapter?","options":["Producer -> Industrial user","Producer -> Industrial distributor -> Retailer -> Consumer","Producer -> Industrial distributor -> Industrial user","Producer -> Agent -> Industrial user"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Business channels end with an industrial user, never a consumer, and never include a retailer. The other three are channels A, B and C of the B2B figure.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-052","concept":"c10-business-channels","also":["c10-channel-choice-factors"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does a company like IBM sell large computing systems to major corporations through a direct channel rather than through distributors?","options":["Complex, expensive systems sold to a few large buyers justify a direct sales force with technical expertise","Business law requires producers of all industrial products to sell through direct channels","Industrial distributors are not permitted to stock or resell technology products","Direct channels always cost less than indirect channels, whatever the product or buyer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Business channels tend to be direct when products are technical and high in unit value and customers are few and large, because the producer's own sales force can supply the information buyers need. Nothing requires direct channels, and direct is not automatically cheaper.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-053","concept":"c10-electronic-direct-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Expedia lets travellers compare and book flights and hotels from many suppliers on its website. Expedia is an example of a(n):","options":["direct channel","corporate vertical marketing system","horizontal marketing system","electronic marketing channel"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Electronic marketing channels use the Internet to make goods and services available. Expedia is an intermediary between travellers and the airlines and hotels, so it is not a direct channel from the producers' point of view.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-054","concept":"c10-electronic-direct-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northern Home Shopping, a TV channel, airs product demonstrations and viewers phone in their orders; no salesperson ever meets the buyer. This is an example of a:","options":["strategic channel alliance","dual distribution system","direct marketing channel","retailer-sponsored cooperative"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Direct marketing channels let consumers buy by interacting with advertising media (catalogues, telemarketing, direct mail, TV home shopping, online) without a face-to-face meeting with a salesperson.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-055","concept":"c10-electronic-direct-channels","also":["c10-direct-indirect"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a direct marketing channel from a direct channel?","options":["Direct marketing is buying via media (catalogue, TV, online) with no salesperson; a direct channel has no intermediaries","They are identical terms for a producer selling straight to consumers with no intermediaries involved","A direct marketing channel always includes a retailer, while a direct channel always includes a wholesaler","A direct channel uses only the Internet, while a direct marketing channel uses only telephone sales"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The terms answer different questions: direct marketing is HOW the buyer purchases (through media, no salesperson); a direct channel is about WHO is in the channel (no intermediaries). A TV shopping network that sells many brands is a direct marketing channel but is still an intermediary.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-056","concept":"c10-electronic-direct-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the e-commerce trends highlighted in the course?","options":["Using AI for personalization, along with AR and VR","Moving away from free delivery to cut costs","Offering multiple payment options such as buy now, pay later","Social selling and voice activation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The trends slide lists fast, free delivery as a trend, the opposite of dropping it. AI, AR and VR, multiple payment options, social selling and voice activation are all on the list, along with CRM, hybrid commerce, data privacy and sustainability.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-057","concept":"c10-electronic-direct-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the course's e-commerce trends slide, which two product categories lead online sales?","options":["Fashion and electronics","Groceries and pharmaceuticals","Furniture and automotive parts","Books and music"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The slide names fashion and electronics as the top e-commerce categories. The other pairs are plausible guesses but are not the categories the course identifies.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-058","concept":"c10-dual-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A firm that reaches buyers by using two or more different types of channels for the same basic product is using:","options":["a strategic channel alliance","dual distribution","omni-channel retailing","intensive distribution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is the definition of dual distribution. A strategic channel alliance uses another firm's channel, and intensive distribution is about how many outlets carry the product, not how many channel types.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-059","concept":"c10-dual-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Peak Outerwear sells the same parkas through Sport Chek stores and through its own online store. Which channel arrangement is this?","options":["Strategic channel alliance","Backward integration","Exclusive distribution","Dual distribution"],"answer":3,"explanation":"One firm, one basic product, two different channel types (an indirect retail channel and its own direct online channel) = dual distribution. It is not a strategic channel alliance, because Prairie Peak is not using another producer's distribution network.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-060","concept":"c10-dual-distribution","also":["c10-strategic-alliance"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario is dual distribution rather than a strategic channel alliance?","options":["A small tea brand's products ride on a large cookie maker's delivery trucks to the cookie maker's grocery accounts","A regional airline's flights are sold through a larger airline's booking system","A cereal maker sells its granola through grocery chains and through its own subscription website","A snack brand uses a soft-drink company's vending-machine network to reach consumers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dual distribution is one firm using two or more of its own channel types for the same product. In the other three, one firm's marketing channel is used to sell another firm's products, which is the definition of a strategic channel alliance.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-061","concept":"c10-dual-distribution","also":["c10-channel-conflict"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is a common risk of dual distribution?","options":["Losing the ability to reach more than one market segment","Being forced to give up ownership of the product","Eliminating the need for any intermediary functions","Channel conflict, since the channels may compete for the same customers"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Using two channel types for the same product can pit them against each other, for example a retailer resenting the maker's own online store, which is a source of channel conflict. Dual distribution is used precisely to reach more segments, not fewer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-062","concept":"c10-dual-distribution","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When a firm uses dual distribution, its website can play multiple roles, such as taking orders (a transactional role) and providing product information (a promotional role).","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The chapter notes that websites can play multiple roles, transactional and promotional, when a firm uses them alongside its other channels.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-063","concept":"c10-strategic-alliance","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"An arrangement whereby one firm's marketing channel is used to sell another firm's products is called a:","options":["strategic channel alliance","dual distribution system","wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","corporate vertical marketing system"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is the definition of a strategic channel alliance (e.g., Starbucks coffee sold in Safeway). Dual distribution involves one firm using two or more of its own channel types.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-064","concept":"c10-strategic-alliance","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nordic Chips, a Quebec snack maker, signs an agreement under which a national soft-drink company sells Nordic's chips through the soft-drink company's own vending-machine route network. This is a:","options":["dual distribution system","retailer-sponsored cooperative","strategic channel alliance","case of disintermediation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One firm's marketing channel (the soft-drink company's vending routes) is used to sell another firm's products. It is not disintermediation, because no channel member is being bypassed.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-065","concept":"c10-strategic-alliance","also":["c10-dual-distribution","c10-vms"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario is best classified as a strategic channel alliance?","options":["A paint maker sells the same paint through national hardware chains and through its own online store","A B.C. kombucha maker's drinks ride on a national juice company's trucks and sales force to the juice company's stores","Two unrelated bakeries in Moncton share the cost of one delivery van and a joint market booth","A tire maker opens its own chain of tire and service centres in major cities across Canada"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In an alliance, one firm's channel sells another firm's products. The paint maker is dual distribution, the bakeries pooling resources at the same level form a horizontal marketing system, and the tire maker owning outlets is forward integration.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-066","concept":"c10-strategic-alliance","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why might a small brand enter a strategic channel alliance with a larger company?","options":["It gains legal ownership of the partner's retail stores and warehouses","It is guaranteed exclusive distribution in every market it enters","It eliminates all possibility of channel conflict with retailers","It gains established distribution that would be costly or slow to build itself"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Using another firm's channel lets the smaller brand reach buyers quickly without building its own sales force and delivery network. Owning the partner's outlets would be integration (a corporate VMS), which an alliance does not involve.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-067","concept":"c10-strategic-alliance","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a strategic channel alliance, the firm whose products are being sold must own the partner's distribution channel.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Corrected statement: in a strategic channel alliance one firm's marketing channel is used to sell another firm's products, with no ownership required. Owning successive channel stages would be a corporate VMS.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-068","concept":"c10-multichannel-omni","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Omni-channel retailing is:","options":["another term for multichannel marketing.","the concept of a seamless shopping experience among all shopping channels.","a historical term describing retailing before technology revolutionized the shopping process.","when a retailer closes either its online or offline marketing channel to focus on the remaining channel."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Omni-channel retailing creates a seamless experience among all available shopping channels: online, catalogue and in-store. It is not just another word for multichannel marketing, which blends channels so they reinforce each other; omni-channel goes further by making them feel like one experience.","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-069","concept":"c10-multichannel-omni","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Huron Home, a home decor retailer, lets shoppers start a cart in its app, check live in-store stock, pick up at any store, return online orders in store and earn the same loyalty points everywhere, all as one continuous experience. This best illustrates:","options":["dual distribution","omni-channel retailing","a strategic channel alliance","selective distribution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A single, seamless experience across every shopping channel is omni-channel retailing. Dual distribution only requires two channel types for the same product; it says nothing about integrating them into one experience.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-070","concept":"c10-multichannel-omni","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario reflects multichannel marketing but NOT yet omni-channel retailing?","options":["A retailer's catalogue drives web visits and its website drives store traffic, but carts and returns stay separate by channel","A retailer's app, website and stores share one cart, one loyalty account and one live inventory system","A retailer closes all of its physical stores to focus only on selling through its website","A manufacturer sells its products through one exclusive dealer per city across the country"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Multichannel marketing blends communication and delivery channels so they reinforce each other; omni-channel requires the customer to experience them seamlessly. Separate carts and returns mean the channels support each other but are not yet seamless.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-071","concept":"c10-multichannel-omni","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Maya tries on running shoes at a Toronto store, then orders them later on her phone. In multichannel terms, the store visit took place in the _____ and the phone order in the _____.","options":["marketspace; marketplace","supply chain; marketing channel","marketplace; marketspace","direct channel; indirect channel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The marketplace is the traditional face-to-face environment; the marketspace is the online, face-to-screen environment. Reversing them is the usual mistake: think of 'space' as in cyberspace.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-072","concept":"c10-multichannel-omni","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do firms practise multichannel marketing?","options":["To make each channel fully independent so that the channels compete for the same customers","To avoid all face-to-face contact with consumers by moving every sale to the marketspace","To cut the number of channels down to the single channel that is most profitable","To leverage each channel's strengths so the channels reinforce one another in building relationships"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Multichannel marketing blends communication and delivery channels that are mutually reinforcing, leveraging what each does best. Letting channels compete for the same customers is the conflict that multichannel marketing tries to avoid.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-073","concept":"c10-global-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the global channel of distribution (Figure 10-7), what comes immediately after the seller's international marketing headquarters?","options":["Channels within foreign nations","Channels between nations","Final consumer","The seller's domestic sales force"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The sequence is Seller -> Seller's international marketing headquarters -> Channels between nations -> Channels within foreign nations -> Final consumer. The product must cross borders before it can be distributed inside the foreign market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-074","concept":"c10-global-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which link in the global channel of distribution comes immediately before the final consumer?","options":["Channels between nations","Seller's international marketing headquarters","Channels within foreign nations","Seller"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Channels within foreign nations (local wholesalers and retailers) deliver the product to the final consumer. Channels between nations come earlier, moving the product from the home country into the foreign market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-075","concept":"c10-global-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the chapter, channel sophistication in a foreign market tends to increase as:","options":["the country's economic infrastructure develops","the distance from the seller's home country increases","the seller opens more international headquarters","the number of competing sellers declines"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The chapter states that channel sophistication increases as a country's economic infrastructure develops, since developed economies have more specialized intermediaries. Distance and number of competitors are not the stated driver.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-076","concept":"c10-global-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Banff Botanicals ships its skincare products to a freight forwarder and export agent who move them from Canada into Japan. These firms belong to which link of the global channel?","options":["Channels within foreign nations","Seller's international marketing headquarters","Channels between nations","Final consumer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Firms that move products from the home country to the foreign country form the channels between nations. Channels within foreign nations handle distribution once the product is inside Japan.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-077","concept":"c10-global-channels","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Once Banff Botanicals' products reach Japan, a Tokyo wholesaler supplies them to department stores across the country. How does this wholesaler's role differ from the export agent's?","options":["The Tokyo wholesaler is part of channels within foreign nations; the export agent was part of channels between nations","Both are part of channels between nations, since both handle imported goods","The Tokyo wholesaler is the seller's international marketing headquarters","The export agent is part of channels within foreign nations; the wholesaler is between nations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Distribution inside the foreign market (wholesaler to department stores) is channels within foreign nations; moving goods across the border is channels between nations. The international marketing headquarters belongs to the seller, not to a foreign intermediary.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-078","concept":"c10-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Professionally managed and centrally coordinated marketing channels designed to achieve channel economies and maximum marketing impact are called:","options":["horizontal marketing systems","vertical marketing systems","strategic channel alliances","multichannel marketing systems"],"answer":1,"explanation":"This is the definition of a vertical marketing system (VMS). A horizontal marketing system links firms at the same level instead of centrally coordinating the levels of a channel.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-079","concept":"c10-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the key difference between a vertical marketing system and a horizontal marketing system?","options":["A VMS always involves franchising; a horizontal system always involves ownership","A VMS is used only in business markets; a horizontal system only in consumer markets","A VMS links competitors at the same level; a horizontal system links suppliers and retailers","A VMS coordinates different channel levels; a horizontal system pools unrelated firms at the same level"],"answer":3,"explanation":"'Vertical' means up and down the channel (producer, wholesaler, retailer); 'horizontal' means side by side at the same level. Franchising is only one form of contractual VMS, so 'always franchising' is wrong.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-080","concept":"c10-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two unrelated Moncton businesses, a craft brewery and an artisan cheese maker, share the cost of a refrigerated delivery van and a joint booth at farmers' markets. This is best described as a:","options":["corporate vertical marketing system","horizontal marketing system","administered vertical marketing system","retailer-sponsored cooperative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two or more unrelated companies at the same channel level (both are producers) pooling resources form a horizontal marketing system. A VMS would coordinate different levels, and a retailer-sponsored cooperative is formed by retailers, not producers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-081","concept":"c10-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which is NOT one of the three types of vertical marketing systems?","options":["Corporate","Contractual","Administered","Horizontal"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The three VMS types are corporate (ownership), contractual (contracts) and administered (size and influence). A horizontal marketing system is the contrasting idea of firms at the same level pooling resources.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-082","concept":"c10-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do channel members form a vertical marketing system instead of operating as independent firms?","options":["Central coordination achieves channel economies and maximum marketing impact","So each member can maximize its own profit regardless of the others","To eliminate the need for logistics activities","To bring unrelated firms at the same level together to share resources"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A VMS is professionally managed and centrally coordinated precisely to gain channel economies and marketing impact. Independent members chasing their own profit describes the conventional channel a VMS replaces; pooling at the same level is horizontal.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-083","concept":"c10-corporate-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mocha Jive coffee shops operate their own cafés and have a subsidiary company, Perfect Cookies, that produces a wide variety of cookies and snacks for their stores. This is an example of:","options":["dual distribution","selective distribution","backward integration","horizontal marketing system"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A retailer owning the manufacturing operation that supplies it is backward integration, which creates a corporate VMS. It is not a horizontal marketing system, which would require unrelated firms at the same level pooling resources.","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-084","concept":"c10-corporate-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Laurentian Leather, a Quebec handbag maker, buys a chain of 30 boutiques so that it controls how its bags are displayed and sold. This is:","options":["backward integration","a retailer-sponsored cooperative","forward integration","an administered VMS"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A producer acquiring intermediaries downstream (closer to the consumer) is forward integration, creating a corporate VMS. Backward integration runs the other way: a retailer or wholesaler acquiring production.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-085","concept":"c10-corporate-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes forward from backward integration?","options":["Forward: a producer owns intermediaries closer to the consumer; backward: a retailer or wholesaler owns production","Forward: a retailer owns the manufacturer that supplies it; backward: a manufacturer opens its own stores","Forward integration relies on contracts between firms, while backward integration relies on ownership","Both terms describe two firms at the same channel level merging to pool their resources"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Direction is measured from the firm doing the acquiring: toward the consumer = forward, toward the source = backward. Both involve ownership (corporate VMS); the reversed definitions are the classic trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-086","concept":"c10-corporate-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A corporate vertical marketing system is characterized by:","options":["contracts among independently owned firms at different levels","coordination through the size and influence of one dominant member","unrelated firms at the same level pooling resources","ownership of successive stages of production and distribution by one firm"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Corporate VMS = ownership, achieved through forward or backward integration. Contracts describe a contractual VMS; size and influence describe an administered VMS.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-087","concept":"c10-corporate-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Harvest Moon Grocers, a Saskatchewan supermarket chain, is frustrated by inconsistent supply of its private-label bread and wants permanent control over production. Which move would create a corporate VMS through backward integration?","options":["Signing a franchise agreement with a national bakery brand","Joining a retailer-sponsored cooperative with other grocers","Buying the bakery that produces its private-label bread","Letting the largest bread brand manage its shelf displays"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Owning the upstream supplier is backward integration, which creates a corporate VMS. A franchise agreement or cooperative would be contractual, and letting a dominant brand manage the shelves would be an administered arrangement.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-088","concept":"c10-corporate-vms","also":["c10-contractual-vms"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which is NOT an example of a corporate VMS?","options":["Apple selling its products through its own Apple Stores","An independent hardware store joining Home Hardware's retailer-owned cooperative","Ralph Lauren operating its own chain of Polo stores","A coffee chain owning the bakery that makes its cookies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A retailer-sponsored cooperative is a contractual VMS: the stores stay independently owned and coordinate by agreement. The other three involve one firm owning successive stages (forward or backward integration).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-089","concept":"c10-contractual-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Valley Fresh, a Nova Scotia food wholesaler, signs up 60 independently owned grocers who agree to buy through Valley Fresh, use a common store banner and follow its merchandising program. This is a:","options":["retailer-sponsored cooperative","wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","corporate VMS","manufacturer-sponsored wholesale franchise system"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A wholesaler that contracts with independent retailers to standardize and coordinate buying and merchandising sponsors a voluntary chain (IGA is the chapter's example). If the retailers had banded together to own the wholesale operation, it would be a cooperative.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-090","concept":"c10-contractual-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two hundred independent hardware store owners jointly own and operate a wholesale buying organization that supplies all their stores under a shared banner. This is a:","options":["wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","corporate VMS","horizontal marketing system","retailer-sponsored cooperative"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Independent retailers banding together to run their own wholesale operation form a retailer-sponsored cooperative (Home Hardware is the chapter's example). It is a contractual VMS, not corporate, because each store remains independently owned. Although all the owners are retailers, they jointly run the wholesale stage that supplies them, so this is a vertical (contractual) arrangement, not a horizontal marketing system of unrelated firms.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-091","concept":"c10-contractual-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What is the key difference between a wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain and a retailer-sponsored cooperative?","options":["The sponsor: a wholesaler organizes a voluntary chain, while independent retailers organize a cooperative","A voluntary chain is coordinated through ownership, while a cooperative is coordinated through influence","A voluntary chain charges franchise fees, while a cooperative operates with no agreements at all","There is no real difference, since both are examples of an administered VMS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both are contractual VMSs; the difference is the sponsor. Wholesaler-sponsored = IGA-style voluntary chain; retailer-sponsored = Home Hardware-style cooperative. Neither is based on ownership or on mere influence.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-092","concept":"c10-contractual-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Coca-Cola licenses independent bottlers in each territory, who buy syrup, bottle the drink and sell it to retailers. This is an example of a:","options":["manufacturer-sponsored retail franchise system","service-sponsored franchise system","manufacturer-sponsored wholesale franchise system","wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The bottlers are franchised wholesalers (they sell to retailers) under a manufacturer's sponsorship. A manufacturer-sponsored RETAIL franchise, like Ford dealers, licenses outlets that sell to final consumers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-093","concept":"c10-contractual-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kodiak Motors authorizes independently owned dealerships across Canada to sell and service its vehicles under the Kodiak brand. This is a:","options":["manufacturer-sponsored wholesale franchise system","corporate VMS using forward integration","manufacturer-sponsored retail franchise system","retailer-sponsored cooperative"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A manufacturer licensing independent retailers (dealers) to sell its product to consumers is a manufacturer-sponsored retail franchise system, like Ford dealers. It is not forward integration, because Kodiak does not own the dealerships.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-094","concept":"c10-contractual-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which example is NOT correctly matched with its contractual VMS type?","options":["IGA: wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","Home Hardware: retailer-sponsored cooperative","Ford dealers: manufacturer-sponsored retail franchise system","Coca-Cola bottlers: service-sponsored franchise system"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Coca-Cola bottlers are a manufacturer-sponsored wholesale franchise system. Service-sponsored franchise systems are illustrated by firms such as Holiday Inn and McDonald's.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-095","concept":"c10-contractual-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the chapter's classification of franchise programs, McDonald's and Holiday Inn are examples of a:","options":["manufacturer-sponsored retail franchise system","service-sponsored franchise system","manufacturer-sponsored wholesale franchise system","retailer-sponsored cooperative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"McDonald's and Holiday Inn are the chapter's examples of service-sponsored franchise systems, in which a service company licenses franchisees to operate under its name. They do not manufacture a product for dealers or bottlers to resell.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-096","concept":"c10-administered-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In an administered vertical marketing system, coordination among successive stages is achieved through:","options":["the size and influence of one channel member","ownership of the stages by one firm","formal franchise contracts","a wholesale operation owned by retailers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Administered VMS = coordination through one member's size and influence (e.g., Procter & Gamble), with no ownership and no binding contracts. Ownership is corporate; franchise contracts and retailer-owned wholesalers are contractual.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-097","concept":"c10-administered-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Because its brands are must-haves for grocers, Brightway, a giant household products maker, gets independent supermarket chains to follow its shelf-layout plans and promotion calendars, even though it owns no stores and has no franchise contracts with them. This is a(n):","options":["corporate VMS","administered VMS","contractual VMS","horizontal marketing system"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Coordination that comes from a dominant member's size and influence, without ownership or contracts, is an administered VMS. Corporate would require ownership of the stores; contractual would require formal agreements such as franchises.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-098","concept":"c10-administered-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A national retailer dictates package sizes, delivery schedules and pricing terms to hundreds of independent suppliers simply because of its enormous purchase volumes. It owns none of them and has no franchise or cooperative agreements with them. Why is this an administered rather than a corporate or contractual VMS?","options":["The retailer owns the successive stages of production and distribution","The suppliers have signed franchise agreements with the retailer","The suppliers and the retailer are at the same channel level","Coordination comes from the retailer's size and influence, not ownership or contracts"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Administered VMSs rely on size and power (Walmart and P&G are the classic examples). Ownership would make it corporate and franchise or cooperative agreements would make it contractual; suppliers and retailer are at different levels, so it is vertical, not horizontal.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-099","concept":"c10-administered-vms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation is NOT an example of an administered VMS?","options":["A grocery chain buys the dairy that supplies its private-label milk","Procter & Gamble's influence leads retailers to cooperate on displays and promotions","Walmart's buying power leads suppliers to adopt its inventory systems","A dominant toy maker persuades independent stores to follow its merchandising guidelines"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Buying the supplier is ownership, which is backward integration in a corporate VMS. The other three achieve coordination through size and influence alone, the mark of an administered VMS.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-100","concept":"c10-administered-vms","also":["c10-channel-power"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Brandt Appliances, the market-leading appliance maker, wants independent dealers to adopt its display standards and sales training, but it does not want to buy stores or sign franchise contracts. What approach is it relying on?","options":["Backward integration into dealer ownership","A retailer-sponsored cooperative","An administered VMS, using its size and influence to secure cooperation","A horizontal marketing system with competing appliance makers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without ownership (corporate) or contracts (contractual), Brandt's only coordinating mechanism is its market power, which makes this an administered VMS. Owning dealers would in any case be forward, not backward, integration.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-101","concept":"c10-channel-choice-factors","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Fresh Catch, a Nova Scotia exporter of live lobster, uses the shortest possible channel so its lobsters reach restaurants alive, before they can die and spoil. Which factor most directly drives this choice?","options":["Unit value, a product factor","Order size, a market factor","Perishability, a product factor","Desire for channel control, a company factor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Perishable products need short or direct channels so they reach buyers before they spoil. Unit value is also a product factor, but the stem's cue is that the product is live and spoils quickly.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-102","concept":"c10-channel-choice-factors","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which is NOT a product factor affecting channel choice?","options":["Technical factors","Geographic concentration","Perishability","Unit value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Geographic concentration of buyers is a market factor. The product factors are technical factors, perishability, unit value and product life cycle stage.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-103","concept":"c10-channel-choice-factors","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pairing of channel-choice factor and factor group is correct?","options":["Perishability: company factor","Desire for channel control: product factor","Number of potential customers: product factor","Order size: market factor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Market factors are geographic concentration, number of potential customers, type of market and order size. Perishability is a product factor, desire for channel control is a company factor, and number of potential customers is a market factor.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-104","concept":"c10-channel-choice-factors","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Snackwise sells low-priced granola bars bought in small quantities by millions of consumers across Canada. Based on market and product factors, which channel is most appropriate?","options":["A longer, indirect channel using wholesalers and retailers","A direct channel with its own sales force calling on each consumer","Exclusive distribution through one retailer per province","A short direct channel justified by high unit value"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Many dispersed customers, small order sizes and low unit value all point to a longer, indirect channel in which intermediaries spread the cost of reaching buyers. High unit value is the opposite of this product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-105","concept":"c10-channel-choice-factors","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does low unit value generally push a producer toward a longer, indirect channel?","options":["Low-value products are always perishable, so they must pass through many intermediaries","Intermediaries refuse to carry high-value products, so only low-value ones are left for them","Each sale is too small to pay for direct contact, so intermediaries spread costs over many products","Low-value products are always technically complex and need many specialists to sell them"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With low unit value, the margin on each sale cannot cover the cost of direct contact, while intermediaries carrying many products can reach buyers more cheaply. Technical complexity points the other way, toward direct channels.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-106","concept":"c10-channel-choice-factors","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bytewise, a young Ottawa start-up with limited cash and little distribution experience, relies on established distributors instead of building its own warehouses and sales force. Which group of channel factors is driving this?","options":["Company factors: financial resources and ability of management","Market factors: geographic concentration of buyers","Product factors: perishability of the goods","Market factors: type of market served"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A firm's own financial resources and management ability are company factors, and limited resources push it toward intermediaries. Nothing in the stem concerns where customers are or how perishable the product is.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-107","concept":"c10-coverage-intensity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following channel designs are chosen for specialty products or services like high-end sports vehicles, exclusive women's jewellery, and yachts because only one retail outlet in a specified geographical area carries these products?","options":["Intensive distribution","Selective distribution","Exclusive distribution","Dual distribution"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Exclusive distribution means only one retailer in a specified geographic area carries the product, and it is typically chosen for specialty products such as specialty automobiles, some fragrances and yachts. Selective distribution uses a few retailers per area (shopping goods); dual distribution is about channel types, not coverage.","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-108","concept":"c10-coverage-intensity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which product category is MOST likely to use exclusive distribution?","options":["Specialty products.","Unsought products.","Everyday products.","Convenience products."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Exclusive distribution suits specialty products, which buyers will make a special effort to find (luxury cars, yachts, high-end fragrances). Convenience products are the opposite: they need intensive distribution in as many outlets as possible.","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-109","concept":"c10-coverage-intensity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fizzi, a Canadian sparkling water brand, wants its cans in every grocery store, gas station, vending machine and convenience store in the country. This coverage strategy is:","options":["selective distribution","intensive distribution","exclusive distribution","dual distribution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Placing a product in as many outlets as possible is intensive distribution, typical for convenience goods. Dual distribution concerns using two or more channel types, not the number of outlets.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-110","concept":"c10-coverage-intensity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Whirlwind, a mid-priced appliance brand, is carried by only a few retailers in each city, for example three stores in Edmonton, so shoppers can compare models while dealers earn enough to provide good service. This is:","options":["intensive distribution","exclusive distribution","a strategic channel alliance","selective distribution"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Using a few retailers in a specific geographic area is selective distribution, typical for shopping goods such as appliances and apparel. Exclusive distribution would mean only one retailer in Edmonton.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-111","concept":"c10-coverage-intensity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pairing of target market coverage strategy and product type is correct?","options":["Intensive distribution: specialty goods such as yachts","Selective distribution: shopping goods such as appliances and apparel","Exclusive distribution: convenience goods such as gum","Selective distribution: convenience goods such as batteries"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The course mapping is convenience -> intensive, shopping -> selective, specialty -> exclusive. The wrong options swap the extremes, which is the most common trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-112","concept":"c10-coverage-intensity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why would a luxury fragrance maker choose exclusive rather than intensive distribution?","options":["Few outlets protect the brand's prestige and dealer service, and buyers will seek the brand out","Exclusive distribution maximizes the number of outlets and encourages impulse purchases","Exclusive distribution guarantees consumers the lowest possible retail price for the fragrance","Intensive distribution is not permitted for premium consumer products such as fragrances"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Specialty goods are bought by customers willing to make a special effort, so one outlet per area is enough and protects image and dealer service. Maximizing outlets and impulse purchases describes intensive distribution for convenience goods.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-113","concept":"c10-coverage-intensity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which is NOT one of the target market coverage options considered in channel design?","options":["Intensive distribution","Selective distribution","Exclusive distribution","Dual distribution"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Target market coverage is intensive, selective or exclusive. Dual distribution is a different decision (two or more channel types for the same product) and appears as a distractor in the official question.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-114","concept":"c10-buyer-requirements","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the chapter, which buyer requirements should a channel be designed to satisfy?","options":["Cost, time, capability and dependability","Sales, inventory levels and treatment of damaged goods","Information, convenience, variety, and pre- and/or post-sale services","Coercive, reward and legitimate power"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Satisfying buyer requirements means providing information, convenience, variety and pre- and/or post-sale services. Cost, time, capability and dependability are transportation criteria; sales and inventory levels are criteria for evaluating channel members.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-115","concept":"c10-buyer-requirements","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"TechNest, a Winnipeg electronics retailer, stocks 40 brands of headphones at every price point so shoppers can compare a wide assortment of models. Which buyer requirement is it addressing?","options":["Information","Variety","Post-sale service","Convenience"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Variety refers to the breadth and depth of products available, here 40 brands across price points. Convenience would be about ease of access (location, hours, online ordering), not assortment.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-116","concept":"c10-buyer-requirements","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Glow Spas, a Calgary hot-tub dealer, delivers and installs every tub it sells and offers a five-year maintenance plan. Which buyer requirement does this address?","options":["Variety","Convenience","Pre- and post-sale services","Information"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Delivery, installation and ongoing maintenance are pre- and post-sale services. Information would involve explaining the product to buyers before they purchase.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-117","concept":"c10-buyer-requirements","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Prairie Pantry extends its store hours to 24/7 and adds curbside pickup so busy parents can shop whenever and however suits them. Which buyer requirement is it improving?","options":["Variety","Information","Pre-sale service","Convenience"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Convenience covers ease of access: locations, hours, parking and pickup options. Variety would involve expanding the assortment, which the stem does not mention.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-118","concept":"c10-buyer-requirements","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A home-security company's new dealer channel delights customers with expert advice and installation, but the dealers' distribution, advertising and selling expenses exceed what they earn on each sale. Which channel design consideration is failing?","options":["Profitability","Satisfying buyer requirements","Target market coverage","Selective distribution"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Buyer requirements are being met (advice and installation), but profitability, meaning distribution, advertising and selling expenses relative to revenue, is not. A channel that satisfies buyers but loses money for its members will not last.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-119","concept":"c10-buyer-requirements","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sun Valley Solar sells complex rooftop solar systems to homeowners who are unfamiliar with the technology and need detailed explanations before buying. Which buyer requirement should its channel design emphasize most?","options":["Variety: carrying many unrelated product lines","Convenience: stocking panels in convenience stores","Intensive coverage: as many outlets as possible","Information: knowledgeable salespeople who can explain the product"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Buyers of technical, unfamiliar products most need information from knowledgeable salespeople. Intensive coverage and convenience-store placement suit simple convenience goods, not complex systems.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-120","concept":"c10-channel-power","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Titan Tools tells a Calgary hardware chain it will stop supplying its best-selling drills unless the chain gives Titan more shelf space. Which source of channel power is Titan using?","options":["Reward power","Coercive power","Legitimate power","Referent power"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Coercive power rests on the threat of punishment or withdrawal of support. Reward power would offer an incentive for cooperating rather than threatening a penalty.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-121","concept":"c10-channel-power","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Crispy Crunch Snacks pays retailers a $2 bonus for every case sold above target and funds their in-store displays. Which source of channel power is it using?","options":["Coercive power","Expert power","Reward power","Legitimate power"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reward power uses incentives such as bonuses, allowances and display funding to gain cooperation. Coercive power would threaten a penalty instead.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-122","concept":"c10-channel-power","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Boutiques across Canada compete to carry LuxeNord parkas because being associated with the prestigious brand enhances their own image. LuxeNord's influence over these retailers is mainly:","options":["referent power","expert power","reward power","legitimate power"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Referent power exists when other members want to be identified with a respected or admired channel member. Expert power would come from specialized knowledge, which is not why these boutiques cooperate.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-123","concept":"c10-channel-power","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Resellers of DataForge's enterprise software follow its recommendations closely because DataForge has unmatched technical knowledge of how to configure the system. Which power source is this?","options":["Referent power","Coercive power","Reward power","Expert power"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Expert power comes from specialized knowledge that others value. Referent power is about wanting to be associated with an admired brand, not about relying on its know-how.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-124","concept":"c10-channel-power","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A franchisee that follows head office's pricing rules because its signed franchise agreement gives head office that authority is responding to _____ power, whereas a boutique that cooperates because it admires a designer's brand is responding to _____ power.","options":["coercive; reward","expert; legitimate","legitimate; referent","reward; coercive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Legitimate power is authority that comes from position or contract (the franchise agreement). Referent power is the desire to be identified with an admired member. Coercive power would require a threat, which neither case involves.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-125","concept":"c10-channel-power","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A channel member that coordinates, directs and supports other channel members is called a(n):","options":["channel captain","agent","middleman","third-party logistics provider"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is the definition of a channel captain, which can be a producer, wholesaler or retailer. 'Middleman' is just another name for any intermediary.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-126","concept":"c10-channel-power","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the channel captain is NOT correct?","options":["It coordinates, directs and supports other channel members","It must always be the manufacturer of the product","It can be a producer, a wholesaler or a retailer","Walmart is an example of a retailer acting as a channel captain"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The channel captain can be any member with the power to lead: producer, wholesaler or retailer (Walmart is the classic retailer example). Assuming it must be the manufacturer is the trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-127","concept":"c10-channel-power","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northern Lights Cosmetics is reviewing its retail partners at year-end. Which set of criteria matches the chapter's guidance for evaluating channel members?","options":["Cost, time, capability, dependability, accessibility and frequency of each partner's deliveries","Geographic concentration, number of customers, type of market and the partner's order size","Sales, inventory levels, treatment of damaged or lost goods, customer service and promotional cooperation","Information, convenience, variety and pre- and post-sale services offered to buyers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Channel members are evaluated on sales, inventory levels, treatment of damaged or lost goods, and other factors like customer service and cooperation in promotion. The distractors are the transportation criteria, the market factors and the buyer requirements.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-128","concept":"c10-channel-conflict","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ arises when one channel member believes another channel member is engaged in behaviour that prevents it from achieving its goals.","options":["Disintermediation","Disintegration","Channel designing","Channel conflict"],"answer":3,"explanation":"This is the definition of channel conflict. Disintermediation is one specific cause of conflict (bypassing a member), not the general term.","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-129","concept":"c10-channel-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Tez, an energy drink brand, has a disagreement with one of the major wholesalers of its products over the price of its drinks. What kind of conflict is reflected in this scenario?","options":["Disintermediation","Vertical conflict","Horizontal conflict","Selective conflict"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vertical conflict occurs between different levels of a marketing channel, here a producer and its wholesaler. It is not disintermediation, because Tez is not bypassing the wholesaler, and not horizontal conflict, which would be between two wholesalers.","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-130","concept":"c10-channel-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two Edmonton dealerships that both sell Kodiak trucks accuse each other of advertising in each other's territory and undercutting prices. This is:","options":["vertical conflict","disintermediation","dual distribution","horizontal conflict"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Horizontal conflict occurs between intermediaries at the same level of the channel, such as two dealers of the same brand. Vertical conflict would be between Kodiak and one of its dealers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-131","concept":"c10-channel-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts vertical and horizontal channel conflict?","options":["Vertical conflict occurs between different levels of a channel; horizontal conflict occurs between intermediaries at the same level","Vertical conflict occurs between competitors; horizontal conflict occurs between a manufacturer and its retailers","Vertical conflict happens only online; horizontal conflict happens only in stores","Vertical conflict involves only agents; horizontal conflict involves only wholesalers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Vertical = up and down the channel (e.g., manufacturer vs wholesaler or retailer); horizontal = side by side (e.g., two retailers of the same brand). Saying vertical conflict is between competitors and horizontal conflict is between a manufacturer and its retailers reverses the two definitions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-132","concept":"c10-channel-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kodiak's head office learns that two of its Halifax dealers are fighting over customers because their sales territories overlap. Which action best addresses this horizontal conflict?","options":["Bypassing both dealers to sell only through its own website","Clearly defining and enforcing each dealer's sales territory","Raising the wholesale price to both dealers","Adding a third dealer in the same overlapping area"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The conflict is between members at the same level over territory, so clarifying territories removes its cause. Bypassing the dealers would create vertical conflict (disintermediation), and adding another dealer would intensify the overlap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-133","concept":"c10-channel-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of vertical conflict?","options":["A manufacturer and its wholesaler disagree over price","A manufacturer bypasses its retailers to sell directly to consumers","Two retailers of the same brand fight over the same territory","A manufacturer squeezes its retailers' margins"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Two retailers at the same level is horizontal conflict. The others pit members at different levels against each other; bypassing retailers is disintermediation, a form of vertical conflict.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-134","concept":"c10-disintermediation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeshore Mattress has always sold through furniture retailers. It now launches a website that ships mattresses straight to consumers at lower prices, and its retailers protest. What is the specific term for what Lakeshore is doing?","options":["Disintermediation","Backward integration","Strategic channel alliance","Selective distribution"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disintermediation occurs when a channel member bypasses another member and sells or buys directly, which is what Lakeshore does by selling around its retailers. It is not backward integration, which would mean Lakeshore acquiring an upstream supplier such as a foam maker; disintermediation involves no ownership.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-135","concept":"c10-disintermediation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Channel conflict that arises when a channel member bypasses another member and sells or buys products directly from others is called:","options":["dual distribution","disintermediation","backward integration","horizontal conflict"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is the definition of disintermediation. Backward integration involves owning an upstream supplier, not simply bypassing an intermediary.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-136","concept":"c10-disintermediation","also":["c10-channel-conflict"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Disintermediation is best classified as a form of:","options":["horizontal conflict, because it involves intermediaries at the same level","strategic channel alliance, because firms share a channel","administered VMS, because one member dominates the others","vertical conflict, because it involves members at different levels when one bypasses another"],"answer":3,"explanation":"When a producer bypasses its retailers (or a buyer bypasses its distributor), the conflict is between different channel levels, so disintermediation is a type of vertical conflict.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-137","concept":"c10-disintermediation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Urban Plate, a large Toronto restaurant group, stops buying produce through its foodservice distributor and begins buying directly from Ontario farms. It does not own any farms. The distributor is upset. This is:","options":["disintermediation","horizontal conflict","backward integration","a strategic channel alliance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disintermediation covers bypassing a member to BUY directly as well as to sell directly. It is not backward integration, because Urban Plate is buying from the farms, not owning them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-138","concept":"c10-disintermediation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about disintermediation is NOT correct?","options":["It can occur when a manufacturer sells directly online, bypassing retailers","It can create conflict with the intermediary that is bypassed","It can occur on the buying side, when a buyer bypasses its distributor","It eliminates the channel functions the bypassed intermediary used to perform"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Bypassing an intermediary removes the member, not the functions: storing, transporting, selling and financing must still be done by someone. The other statements are accurate.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-139","concept":"c10-logistics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Activities that focus on getting the right amount of the right products to the right place at the right time at the lowest possible cost are called:","options":["supply chain management","logistics","the marketing channel","reverse logistics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is the definition of logistics. Supply chain management is broader: it integrates information AND logistics activities across all firms in the chain.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-140","concept":"c10-logistics","also":["c10-scm"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does logistics differ from supply chain management?","options":["Logistics covers raw-material suppliers, while SCM covers only activities inside the producer's own warehouse","Logistics is mainly about promotion decisions, while SCM is mainly about pricing decisions in the channel","Logistics manages the flow of goods at lowest cost; SCM integrates information and logistics across all firms in the chain","The two terms are interchangeable, since both describe moving goods from producers to consumers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logistics is the movement-of-goods piece; SCM integrates information and logistics across suppliers, the producer and the marketing channel. Assigning raw-material suppliers to logistics while confining SCM to one warehouse reverses their scope: SCM is the broader, cross-firm concept.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-141","concept":"c10-logistics","also":["c10-transportation"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Boreal Books switches its shipping from rail to air. Transportation costs rise by $40,000 a year, but faster delivery lets it cut inventory costs by $55,000 and stockout costs by $10,000. What is the change in total logistics cost?","options":["An increase of $40,000","A decrease of $65,000","An increase of $15,000","A decrease of $25,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Change in total logistics cost = +$40,000 - $55,000 - $10,000 = -$25,000. Looking only at transportation (+$40,000) ignores the trade-offs, and -$65,000 forgets to offset the higher transportation cost.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-142","concept":"c10-logistics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do logistics managers focus on TOTAL logistics cost rather than minimizing each cost separately?","options":["Total cost is always lowest when each separate cost is minimized on its own","Customer service levels have no effect on any of the logistics costs","Costs trade off: cheaper, slower transport can raise inventory and stockout costs","Only transportation costs matter when evaluating logistics decisions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Total logistics cost covers transportation, materials handling and warehousing, inventory, stockouts, order processing and return goods handling, and these move against each other. Minimizing one in isolation often raises the total.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-143","concept":"c10-logistics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cascade Office Supply promises that orders placed by 2 p.m. arrive the next business day, and it keeps that promise on 99% of orders. The 99% figure measures which customer service factor?","options":["Dependability","Time","Communication","Convenience"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dependability is the consistency of service, meaning delivering as promised. Time (order cycle or lead time) is the next-day promise itself; the 99% describes how reliably that promise is met.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-144","concept":"c10-logistics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which is NOT a component of total logistics cost?","options":["Inventory","Advertising","Stockouts","Return goods handling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total logistics cost includes transportation, materials handling and warehousing, inventory, stockouts, order processing and return goods handling. Advertising is a promotion cost, not a logistics cost.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-145","concept":"c10-logistics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which is NOT one of the four key logistics functions in a supply chain shown in the chapter?","options":["Transportation","Order processing","Market segmentation","Warehousing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The four key logistics functions are transportation, order processing, inventory management and warehousing. Market segmentation is a targeting decision, not a logistics function.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-146","concept":"c10-scm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The integration and organization of information and logistics activities across firms in a supply chain, for the purpose of creating and delivering goods and services that provide value to consumers, is:","options":["logistics","supply chain management","a vertical marketing system","multichannel marketing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"This is the definition of supply chain management. Logistics alone is the narrower activity of getting the right products to the right place at the right time at the lowest cost.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-147","concept":"c10-scm","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a car manufacturer, the steel mills and parts makers that supply it belong to the _____, while the dealers that sell its cars belong to the _____ (Figure 10-9).","options":["supplier network; marketing channel","marketing channel; supplier network","marketing channel; reverse logistics network","supplier network; horizontal marketing system"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Figure 10-9: the supplier network is all parties supplying raw materials and parts; the marketing channel is all parties distributing and promoting the product. Together they make up the supply chain.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-148","concept":"c10-scm","also":["c10-marketing-channel"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement about the supply chain and the marketing channel is correct?","options":["The marketing channel is broader: it includes the supply chain plus promotion","The supply chain includes only raw-material suppliers, not distributors","The two terms are interchangeable","The supply chain is broader: it includes the supplier network as well as the marketing channel"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A supply chain runs from suppliers through the producer to consumers, so it contains the marketing channel as its downstream part. The marketing channel does not include upstream suppliers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-149","concept":"c10-scm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"The chapter says a firm must harmonize its supply chain with its marketing strategy. What does this mean in practice?","options":["Always minimize supply chain cost, whatever the firm's chosen marketing strategy","Change the marketing strategy to fit whatever supply chain the firm already has","Design the supply chain to deliver what the target market values, such as speed or low cost","Leave all supply chain decisions to suppliers so marketing can focus on promotion"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Supply chain management exists to create and deliver value to consumers, so its design must support the chosen marketing strategy. Always minimizing cost could undercut a strategy built on fast, reliable service.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-150","concept":"c10-scm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Glacier Bikes sets up shared demand forecasts, real-time inventory data and coordinated production schedules with its tire and frame suppliers, its own plants and the retailers that sell its bikes. This is an example of:","options":["supply chain management","dual distribution","multichannel marketing","a horizontal marketing system"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Integrating information and logistics activities across suppliers, the producer and channel members is supply chain management. Multichannel marketing concerns blending communication and delivery channels toward consumers, not coordinating suppliers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-151","concept":"c10-transportation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which is NOT one of the criteria used to evaluate transportation alternatives?","options":["Cost","Dependability","Accessibility","Perishability"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The six transportation criteria are cost, time, capability, dependability, accessibility and frequency. Perishability is a product factor affecting channel choice, not a transportation criterion.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-152","concept":"c10-transportation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Polar Pharma must move vaccines that need to stay at -20 C, so it only considers carriers with refrigerated equipment. Which transportation criterion is it focusing on?","options":["Frequency","Capability","Accessibility","Cost"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Capability is the ability of a mode or carrier to provide the equipment and conditions needed to move a particular kind of good, such as refrigeration. Accessibility is about whether the carrier's routes reach the needed locations.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-153","concept":"c10-transportation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Emerald Gems must ship small, very high-value diamond parcels from Yellowknife to Toronto as quickly as possible. Which transportation mode fits best?","options":["Water","Pipeline","Air","Rail"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Air is the fastest mode and, despite its high cost, suits small, high-value, urgent shipments. Water and rail are slower modes suited to bulky goods, and pipelines carry only liquids and gases.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-154","concept":"c10-transportation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about transportation criteria is correct?","options":["Frequency is the reliability of delivery; dependability is how often shipments can be scheduled","Frequency is how often shipments can be scheduled; dependability is reliable, on-time, damage-free delivery","Accessibility is the price the carrier charges; cost is the set of routes the carrier serves","Capability is the speed of delivery; time is the availability of special equipment such as refrigeration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Frequency = how often pickups or shipments can be scheduled; dependability = consistency of on-time, damage-free service. The distractors swap pairs of criteria, a common exam trick.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-155","concept":"c10-transportation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Combining transportation modes, for example moving truck trailers on railway flatcars, is called:","options":["cross-docking","pipeline transportation","electronic data interchange","intermodal transportation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Intermodal transportation combines two or more modes to gain the advantages of each. Cross-docking is a warehousing practice, not a transportation mode.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-156","concept":"c10-order-inventory","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"TrackMart's suppliers supply less merchandise on a more frequent basis. This is called a _____ inventory system.","options":["facilitating","transactional","time utility","just-in-time"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A just-in-time (JIT) inventory system delivers less merchandise on a more frequent basis than traditional systems, keeping inventories very low. Facilitating and transactional are channel function families, and time utility is a type of value, not an inventory system.","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-157","concept":"c10-order-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Shoreline Pharmacy lets its main supplier monitor its sales data, decide how much of each product to restock, and ship replacements automatically. This is:","options":["vendor-managed inventory","just-in-time inventory","cross-docking","electronic data interchange"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In vendor-managed inventory (VMI) the supplier determines the product amount and assortment a customer needs and automatically delivers it. JIT is about small, frequent deliveries; it does not require the supplier to make the reorder decision.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-158","concept":"c10-order-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Big North Hardware's computers send purchase orders to, and receive invoices from, its suppliers' computers with no paper forms. This order-processing technology is:","options":["vendor-managed inventory","efficient consumer response","third-party logistics","electronic data interchange (EDI)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"EDI links computers in different firms to transmit documents such as purchase orders and invoices electronically, speeding up order processing. VMI goes further by handing the reorder decision to the supplier.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-159","concept":"c10-order-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What is the key difference between just-in-time (JIT) and vendor-managed inventory (VMI)?","options":["JIT keeps stock very low with small, frequent deliveries; under VMI the supplier decides what to restock and sends it","JIT hands reorder decisions to the supplier, while VMI requires the customer to hold large safety stocks","JIT is a warehousing method with no storage time, while VMI is a mode of transportation","They are identical inventory systems that simply go by two different names"],"answer":0,"explanation":"JIT is defined by low inventory and frequent delivery; VMI is defined by who decides, namely the vendor. 'Warehousing with no storage' describes cross-docking, not JIT.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-160","concept":"c10-order-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"Inventory management systems designed to reduce a retailer's lead time for receiving merchandise, thereby lowering its inventory investment and improving customer service, are known as:","options":["reverse logistics and recycling programs","selective distribution systems for retailers","efficient consumer response or quick response systems","cross-docking warehouse operations"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ECR/QR systems shorten the retailer's lead time so it can carry less inventory while keeping shelves stocked. Cross-docking is a warehousing technique that may support them, but it is not the name of the inventory system.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-161","concept":"c10-order-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the main risk of operating a just-in-time inventory system?","options":["Inventory carrying costs become much higher than in traditional systems","Little stock on hand means a late delivery can quickly cause stockouts or stoppages","Suppliers must deliver larger shipments on a less frequent basis","It removes the need for fast, dependable transportation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"JIT works only with fast, on-time delivery, because there is little stock to absorb a delay. It lowers carrying costs and relies on smaller, more frequent shipments, the opposite of the distractors.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-162","concept":"c10-order-inventory","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which is NOT one of the reasons the chapter gives for holding inventory?","options":["To buffer against variations in supply and demand","To provide better customer service","To speculate on future price changes","To eliminate the need for order processing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Firms hold inventory to buffer variations in supply and demand, give better service, promote efficiency and speculate. Holding inventory does nothing to remove the need to process orders.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-163","concept":"c10-warehousing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a Fleetway Grocers facility, inbound trucks are unloaded and the pallets are moved straight across the dock onto outbound trucks headed to stores, spending only a few hours in the building. This practice is:","options":["cross-docking","traditional storage warehousing","vendor-managed inventory","reverse logistics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cross-docking means unloading inbound shipments and loading them onto outbound vehicles with little or no storage. A traditional storage warehouse holds goods for longer periods.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-164","concept":"c10-warehousing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does a distribution centre differ from a traditional storage warehouse?","options":["A distribution centre holds goods for years, while a storage warehouse ships everything the same day","A distribution centre is always public, while a storage warehouse is always private","A distribution centre keeps goods moving; a storage warehouse holds them for longer periods","There is no difference; both are built only for long-term storage of goods"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Storage warehouses hold goods for moderate to long periods; distribution centres are designed to keep goods moving and often use cross-docking. Public vs private is about ownership, which is a separate warehousing decision.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-165","concept":"c10-warehousing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A firm that rents space and services from an independent warehouse operator, rather than owning its own facility, is using:","options":["private warehousing","public warehousing","cross-docking","vendor-managed inventory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Public warehouses are operated by independent firms that rent space and services to others; private warehouses are owned or operated by the firm itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-166","concept":"c10-warehousing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Harbourview Toys' volume triples for the six weeks before Christmas, and its small private warehouse is enough for the rest of the year. Which warehousing approach fits best?","options":["Build a private warehouse large enough for the Christmas peak","Eliminate all warehousing and cross-dock every product year-round","Combine its private warehouse with rented public warehouse space for the peak season","Replace warehousing with a reverse logistics program"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The chapter notes that firms can use public, private or a combination of warehousing. Renting public space only for the peak avoids paying year-round for capacity that sits empty most of the year.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-167","concept":"c10-warehousing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement about warehousing is NOT correct?","options":["Warehouses can be public or private","Cross-docking means storing goods for long periods before shipping them","Distribution centres are designed to facilitate the timely movement of goods","Many firms use a combination of warehousing approaches"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cross-docking involves little or no storage: goods move from inbound to outbound vehicles quickly. Long-term storage describes a traditional storage warehouse.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-168","concept":"c10-reverse-logistics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which is the BEST example of reverse logistics?","options":["H&M accepts used clothing in its stores. H&M then recycles the clothing to make new textiles.","Co-op purchases products from local farmers to sell in its grocery stores.","Apple ships its products directly to customers from its online store.","Zara hosts a promotional event to launch the opening of a new store where guests are given a tote bag."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reverse logistics reclaims recyclable and reusable materials, returns and reworks from the point of consumption for repair, remanufacturing, redistribution or disposal, which is exactly what H&M does with used clothing. Apple shipping to customers is forward (outbound) logistics through a direct channel.","source":"official"},{"id":"q10-169","concept":"c10-reverse-logistics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A process of reclaiming recyclable and reusable materials, returns and reworks from the point of consumption or use for repair, remanufacturing, redistribution or disposal is called:","options":["disintermediation","backward integration","cross-docking","reverse logistics"],"answer":3,"explanation":"That is the definition of reverse logistics. Backward integration is a retailer or wholesaler owning upstream production; 'backward' there refers to ownership, not to products flowing back.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-170","concept":"c10-reverse-logistics","also":["c10-corporate-vms"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A student confuses reverse logistics with backward integration. Which explanation correctly separates them?","options":["Reverse logistics moves used goods back from consumers; backward integration means owning upstream production","Reverse logistics is a retailer buying its suppliers; backward integration is recycling used products","Both terms mean returning defective goods to the manufacturer for a refund or a replacement","Reverse logistics is part of channels between nations; backward integration is part of channels within nations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The two share a 'backward' feel but are unrelated: reverse logistics is the return flow of products and materials, while backward integration is ownership in a corporate VMS (e.g., a coffee chain owning its cookie bakery).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-171","concept":"c10-reverse-logistics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Voltway, an e-bike maker, collects worn batteries from customers, refurbishes the usable cells into new packs and safely disposes of the rest. This is an example of:","options":["reverse logistics","vendor-managed inventory","backward integration","disintermediation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reclaiming used products from customers for remanufacturing and disposal is reverse logistics. Backward integration would mean Voltway owning its battery supplier.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-172","concept":"c10-reverse-logistics","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why might a retailer invest in a reverse logistics program?","options":["To shorten its forward channel by removing wholesalers and selling direct","To recover value from returns and used products and meet sustainability expectations","To get newly launched products to consumers faster than competitors","To gain control over its suppliers by taking ownership of them"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reverse logistics turns returns and used goods into value through repair, remanufacturing, redistribution or responsible disposal, which also supports sustainability. Removing wholesalers or owning suppliers are forward-channel decisions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-173","concept":"c10-3pl","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Firms that perform most or all of the logistics functions that manufacturers, suppliers and distributors would normally perform themselves are called:","options":["industrial distributors","agents or brokers","channel captains","third-party logistics providers"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Third-party logistics providers (3PLs) perform most or all logistics functions for other firms. Industrial distributors buy and resell goods, and agents negotiate sales rather than handle logistics.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-174","concept":"c10-3pl","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Muskeg Apparel, an online clothing start-up, hands its warehousing, order picking, shipping and returns handling to LogiPro, an outside firm that does the same for dozens of other brands. LogiPro is a:","options":["wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","strategic channel alliance partner","third-party logistics provider","vendor-managed inventory supplier"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An outside firm performing most or all logistics functions for other companies is a 3PL. A strategic channel alliance would involve selling Muskeg's products through another firm's marketing channel, not outsourcing its logistics.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-175","concept":"c10-3pl","also":["c10-intermediary-terms"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How does a third-party logistics provider typically differ from a wholesaler?","options":["A 3PL always takes title to the goods it handles, whereas a wholesaler never does","A 3PL sells mainly to final consumers, whereas a wholesaler sells only to manufacturers","A 3PL stores and moves goods for a fee without buying them; a wholesaler takes title and resells","The two terms are interchangeable, since both store and move goods for other firms"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A 3PL is a logistics service firm that moves and stores other firms' goods. A wholesaler buys (takes title to) goods and resells them, performing transactional as well as logistical functions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-176","concept":"c10-3pl","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why would a manufacturer outsource its logistics to a 3PL?","options":["A specialist can do the work more efficiently, letting the manufacturer focus on its core business","Outsourcing removes the need for any logistics functions to be performed at all","A 3PL guarantees the manufacturer exclusive distribution in each of its markets","A 3PL takes ownership of the manufacturer's retail customers and their accounts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Like any channel partner, a 3PL must add value by doing the work better or more cheaply. The functions still have to be performed; outsourcing only changes who performs them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q10-177","concept":"c10-3pl","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which task would a third-party logistics provider be LEAST likely to perform for a client?","options":["Warehousing the client's products","Designing the client's national advertising campaign","Transporting orders to customers","Processing returned goods"],"answer":1,"explanation":"3PLs handle logistics functions such as warehousing, transportation, order fulfilment and returns. Advertising is a promotion activity outside logistics.","source":"generated"}],"color":"#A98BC9","short":"Channels","emoji":"⛵","tag":"Channels and supply chains","province":"Graecia","numeral":"X"},{"chapter":11,"title":"Retailing and Wholesaling","topics":[{"id":"t11-1","order":1,"title":"Retailing and how retail outlets are classified","summary":"Retailing is every activity that gets goods and services to ultimate consumers for their own use, and it creates time, place, form and possession utility. Stores can be classified three separate ways: by who owns them (independent, corporate chain, contractual system), by how much service they give (self, limited, full), and by their merchandise line (deep and narrow vs broad). Exam questions love to mix these three schemes up.","concepts":["c11-retailing","c11-retail-utilities","c11-independent-chain","c11-contractual-system","c11-level-of-service","c11-depth-of-line","c11-breadth-of-line","c11-scrambled-merchandising"]},{"id":"t11-2","order":2,"title":"Retailer marketing decisions: target market and positioning","summary":"A retailer makes a long list of marketing decisions, and selecting a target market comes first. Next it decides how it wants to be perceived (retail positioning), which can be mapped on the retail positioning matrix of breadth of line versus value added. Shopper marketing studies how people behave as shoppers across every channel, starting from the moment they first think about buying.","concepts":["c11-retailer-decisions","c11-retail-positioning","c11-positioning-matrix","c11-shopper-marketing"]},{"id":"t11-3","order":3,"title":"The retailing mix","summary":"The retailing mix is the set of tools a retailer controls, arranged in four quadrants around the target market: products and services, retail pricing, physical location, and communications. Inside those quadrants sit the merchandise mix (breadth and depth), store atmosphere, markups, markdowns, shrinkage, off-price formats and the different kinds of shopping locations. Category management is how many retailers run each product category like its own business.","concepts":["c11-retailing-mix","c11-merchandise-mix","c11-category-management","c11-store-atmosphere","c11-retail-pricing","c11-off-price-retailing","c11-retail-location"]},{"id":"t11-4","order":4,"title":"Non-store, online and multichannel retailing","summary":"A lot of retailing happens outside stores: vending machines, catalogues, TV shopping, online stores, telemarketing and direct selling. Consumers shop online for six reasons (the six Cs), and scenario questions test whether you can tell them apart. Multichannel retailers combine stores with non-store formats and serve cross-channel shoppers who showroom or webroom.","concepts":["c11-non-store-retailing","c11-online-six-cs","c11-multichannel-retailing"]},{"id":"t11-5","order":5,"title":"The changing retail landscape","summary":"Face-to-face retail is not dead, but omnichannel shopping, technology and a squeezed middle market are reshaping it, and the 2025 Retail Council of Canada / Adyen survey adds hard numbers to memorize. Two classic theories explain retail change: the wheel of retailing (low-price entrants trade up and get replaced) and the retail life cycle (formats grow and then decline).","concepts":["c11-modern-retail-environment","c11-wheel-of-retailing","c11-retail-life-cycle"]},{"id":"t11-6","order":6,"title":"Wholesaling","summary":"Wholesaling is selling to buyers who will resell or use the goods in a business. The must-memorize figure splits wholesalers into merchant wholesalers (who take title; full-service and limited-service types), agents and brokers (no title; commission), and manufacturer's branches and offices (owned by the producer). Wholesalers earn their place by performing nine functions, from bulk breaking to management counselling.","concepts":["c11-wholesaling","c11-merchant-wholesalers","c11-limited-service-wholesalers","c11-agents-brokers","c11-manufacturer-branches","c11-wholesaler-functions"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"c11-retailing","topic":"t11-1","term":"Retailing","definition":"All activities involved in selling, renting, and providing goods and services to ultimate consumers for personal, family, or household use.","explain":"Retailing is the final step in the channel, where products reach the person who will actually use them. The test is WHO buys and WHY: an ultimate consumer buying for personal, family or household use. Renting (tools, tuxedos) and providing services (haircuts, car washes) count too, not just selling goods. The instructor also stresses that shopping is not only a way to acquire necessities but also a social activity and sometimes an adventure.","example":"Sport Chek selling a hockey stick to a parent, or a Calgary tuxedo shop renting a suit to a wedding guest, are both retailing.","contrast":"Wholesaling sells to buyers who purchase for resale or business use. The same box of printer paper is retailing when a family buys it for home and wholesaling when a law firm buys it for its office. Order size and whether the item is sold or rented do not matter; the buyer's purpose does.","trap":"Cue words 'ultimate consumers' and 'personal, family, or household use' = retailing; 'resale or business use' = wholesaling. Do not be fooled by renting or services: they are still retailing. Official distractors: manufacturing, wholesaling, facilitating.","mnemonic":"Retail = rent, sell or serve to the REAL end user.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-retail-utilities","topic":"t11-1","term":"Value of retailing: time, place, form and possession utility","definition":"Retailers create value by providing time utility (products available when consumers want them), place utility (where they want them), form utility (producing or altering products to suit consumers) and possession utility (making it easier to take ownership or use of products).","explain":"Utility is the value a retailer adds beyond the product itself. Each utility answers a different question: WHEN can I get it (time), WHERE can I get it (place), is it in the SHAPE I need (form), and can I actually OWN or use it (possession). One store usually creates several utilities, so exam questions isolate one practice and ask which utility it creates.","example":"Shoppers Drug Mart's late hours (time), a Tim Hortons in nearly every neighbourhood (place), a tailor hemming new pants (form), and no-payment-for-12-months furniture financing (possession).","contrast":"Time is about hours and availability; place is about location and access. Form changes the product itself (alterations, assembly), while possession only eases ownership (credit, layaway) without changing the product.","trap":"'Open 24 hours / holiday hours' = time; 'convenient locations / near home' = place; 'alterations, assembly, mixing paint' = form; 'credit, financing, layaway' = possession. A common trick is offering place utility as the answer for an hours scenario.","mnemonic":"When, Where, What shape, Whose: time, place, form, possession.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Utilities created by retailing","head":["Utility","What the retailer provides","Typical practice"],"rows":[["Time","Products available WHEN consumers want them","Long or 24-hour store hours, holiday hours"],["Place","Products available WHERE consumers want them","Convenient locations, stores in every neighbourhood"],["Form","Producing or altering products to suit the consumer","Alterations, assembly, custom fitting"],["Possession","Making it easier to take ownership or use","Credit, financing, layaway"]]}},{"id":"c11-independent-chain","topic":"t11-1","term":"Independent retailers and corporate chains (form of ownership)","definition":"An independent retailer is owned by an individual and usually operates one store; it is the most common form of retail ownership. A corporate chain is multiple outlets under common ownership, which allows centralized buying and greater bargaining power.","explain":"Form of ownership is the first of three ways to classify outlets (the others are level of service and merchandise line). Independents are flexible and personal but lack buying clout. Corporate chains make decisions centrally and buy in huge volumes, so they can negotiate better terms from suppliers. The third ownership form, the contractual system, sits between the two.","example":"A family-owned bike shop in Canmore is an independent retailer; Loblaws and Canadian Tire's corporate-owned stores are corporate chains.","contrast":"A contractual system can look exactly like a chain (one banner, shared ads), but its stores are independently OWNED and cooperate by agreement. Corporate chain = one owner of many outlets; independent = one owner, usually one store.","trap":"'Multiple outlets under common ownership', 'centralized buying', 'bargaining power' = corporate chain. 'Independently owned stores banding together' = contractual system, even if they share a banner. Do not assume the corporate chain is the most common form; the independent is.","mnemonic":"One owner, one store = independent; one owner, many stores = chain; many owners acting as one = contractual.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-contractual-system","topic":"t11-1","term":"Contractual systems (co-ops, voluntary chains, franchises)","definition":"A contractual system is a group of independently owned stores that band together to act like a chain. The three types are retailer-sponsored cooperatives, wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chains, and franchises.","explain":"Contractual systems let independents get chain-like advantages, such as concentrated buying power and coordinated promotion and pricing, while keeping their own ownership. To tell the three types apart, ask who organized the group: the retailers themselves (co-op), a wholesaler (voluntary chain), or a franchisor licensing its name and operating system in exchange for a fee plus royalties (franchise).","example":"Home Hardware dealers own their stores but jointly run the co-op that buys for them; IGA grocers are organized by a wholesaler; Tim Hortons and McDonald's franchisees pay fees and royalties to use the brand and system.","contrast":"A corporate chain has common ownership of all outlets; a contractual system has many separate owners. Within contractual systems: retailer-sponsored co-op = retailers' initiative; voluntary chain = wholesaler's initiative; franchise = franchisor grants name and system for fees and royalties.","trap":"Cue words 'independent members', 'concentrate buying power', 'collaborative promotion and pricing' = contractual system (official Bright @ Night question). Distractors include corporate chain, manufacturer's agent and joint venture. If a wholesaler organizes it, it is a voluntary chain, not a co-op.","mnemonic":"Co-op = retailers' idea; Voluntary = wholesaler's idea; Franchise = franchisor's name for a fee.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Types of contractual systems","head":["Type","Who organizes it","Example"],"rows":[["Retailer-sponsored cooperative","Independent retailers band together and jointly run a buying/wholesale operation","Home Hardware"],["Wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","A wholesaler links independent retailers that buy from it under a common banner","IGA"],["Franchise","Franchisor provides name and operating system; franchisee pays a fee plus royalties","Tim Hortons, McDonald's"]]}},{"id":"c11-level-of-service","topic":"t11-1","term":"Level of service (self, limited, full)","definition":"Retail outlets can be classified by the degree of service provided: self-service (customers perform most functions), limited-service (some services such as credit and merchandise returns) and full-service (many services and personalized assistance).","explain":"Service level is a separate classification scheme from ownership and merchandise line. In the retailing mix, retailers also choose their services mix: prepurchase services (help before the sale, such as information and demonstrations), postpurchase services (after the sale, such as delivery, returns and repairs) and ancillary services (extras such as experiences and dining). More service usually means higher costs and prices, so the level must fit the target market.","example":"Self: Costco, a pay-at-the-pump gas station, online stores. Limited: Walmart and many department stores. Full: Holt Renfrew with personal shoppers. Ancillary: West Edmonton Mall's water park.","contrast":"Self-service means almost no help; limited-service adds a few services such as credit and returns; full-service offers trained salespeople, personalized help and many services. A boutique with salespeople is limited-service, not self-service, before it upgrades to full service.","trap":"Official Kyra question: trained salespeople + personalized experience + a database of customer preferences = moving from LIMITED to FULL service. Distractors 'from self-service to full service' and 'full service at self-service prices' are traps. Do not mix service level with merchandise line (category killer) or ownership terms.","mnemonic":"Self = you do it; Limited = credit and returns; Full = they do it for you.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Levels of service","head":["Level","What the customer gets","Examples"],"rows":[["Self-service","Customer performs most functions; few services","Warehouse clubs, gas stations, online stores"],["Limited-service","Some services, such as credit and merchandise returns","Walmart, many department stores"],["Full-service","Many services and personalized assistance","Specialty stores, luxury department stores (Holt Renfrew)"]]}},{"id":"c11-depth-of-line","topic":"t11-1","term":"Depth of line: specialty outlets and category killers","definition":"Stores classified by depth of line carry a large assortment within a narrow range of product lines. Specialty outlets (single-line stores) offer a deep assortment in one line; category killers are specialty discount outlets that focus on one product category, offer a deep assortment and sell at very low prices.","explain":"Depth means how many items are carried within a line. Both specialty outlets and category killers are deep and narrow; what separates them is price and scale. A specialty outlet competes on expertise and selection, often at regular or premium prices, while a category killer is a big-box discounter that tries to dominate (kill) its category.","example":"Specialty outlets: Lululemon, Foot Locker. Category killers: Best Buy, Staples, Home Depot.","contrast":"Depth-of-line stores are the opposite of breadth-of-line stores (department stores, hypermarkets) that carry many lines. Category killer vs specialty outlet = low prices and big-box scale vs regular prices. Category killer (a store format) is also different from category management (an internal management practice).","trap":"'Very low prices + huge selection in ONE category' = category killer. 'Narrow line, deep assortment, expert service' with no discount cue = specialty outlet. 'Power centre' is a LOCATION full of category killers, not the store itself.","mnemonic":"Deep and narrow: specialty = boutique; category killer = big box that kills the category on price.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-breadth-of-line","topic":"t11-1","term":"Breadth of line: general merchandise stores, department stores and hypermarkets","definition":"Stores classified by breadth of line carry a wide range of different product lines. General merchandise stores such as department stores carry many lines; a hypermarket (supercentre) is a very large store combining general merchandise with a full grocery line.","explain":"Breadth means the number of different product lines. Department stores organize many lines (clothing, cosmetics, housewares, furniture) into departments. Hypermarkets and supercentres take breadth to the extreme by putting a full supermarket and a general merchandise store under one huge roof.","example":"Department stores such as Hudson's Bay; supercentres such as Walmart Supercentre and the Real Canadian Superstore.","contrast":"Breadth (many lines) vs depth (many items within a line). A hypermarket is a store FORMAT designed around groceries plus general merchandise, whereas scrambled merchandising is a STRATEGY any store can use by adding unrelated lines. A power centre is a location, not a store.","trap":"'Many different product lines under one roof' = breadth/general merchandise. 'Combines a full grocery line with general merchandise in a very large store' = hypermarket/supercentre. Watch for the reversed breadth/depth definition as a distractor.","mnemonic":"Breadth = how WIDE the store is; depth = how DEEP each aisle goes.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-scrambled-merchandising","topic":"t11-1","term":"Scrambled merchandising and intertype competition","definition":"Scrambled merchandising is offering several unrelated product lines in a single store. It creates intertype competition: competition between very dissimilar types of retail outlets.","explain":"When a drugstore starts selling groceries or a tire retailer sells food, the store is scrambling its merchandise to capture more of each shopper's spending. The side effect is that very different kinds of stores now chase the same customers, which is intertype competition. Think of scrambled merchandising as the cause and intertype competition as the result.","example":"Shoppers Drug Mart selling milk and frozen dinners; Canadian Tire selling food; Walmart competing with Loblaws for grocery shoppers.","contrast":"Scrambled merchandising = the store's assortment strategy (adding unrelated lines). Intertype competition = the rivalry between dissimilar outlet types that results. Two rival supermarkets competing is NOT intertype competition (same type). A hypermarket is a store format, not the act of scrambling.","trap":"'Unrelated product lines in one store' = scrambled merchandising. 'Very different types of retailers competing for the same shoppers' = intertype competition. Distractors include category management, assortment building (a wholesaler function) and the wheel of retailing.","mnemonic":"Scramble the shelves, and different store types collide.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-retailer-decisions","topic":"t11-2","term":"Retailer marketing decisions","definition":"The marketing decisions a retailer makes: target market, channels, product assortment, procurement, prices, services, store atmosphere, store activities and experiences, communications, and location. Selecting a target market is the FIRST item.","explain":"Every other retail decision depends on who the store is trying to serve, so target market selection comes first and retail positioning follows. The remaining decisions are largely organized into the four quadrants of the retailing mix. Procurement, a term students often skip, means how and from whom the retailer buys its merchandise.","example":"A new Edmonton outdoor store first decides it serves serious backcountry hikers, then chooses a deep assortment, premium prices, expert staff and a location near trailhead traffic.","contrast":"The decision list is broader than the retailing mix: target market selection and positioning come first, and the retailing mix (products and services, pricing, location, communications) is then built around that target market.","trap":"If a question asks what comes FIRST, the answer is selecting a target market, not location or pricing. Wholesaler functions such as bulk breaking or risk bearing are NOT on the retailer decision list.","mnemonic":"Target first, then everything else.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Retailer marketing decisions","items":["Target market (selected FIRST)","Channels","Product assortment","Procurement","Prices","Services","Store atmosphere","Store activities and experiences","Communications","Location"]}},{"id":"c11-retail-positioning","topic":"t11-2","term":"Retail positioning","definition":"How a retailer wants to be perceived by consumers relative to competitors. Like marketers of packaged goods, retailers position themselves to differentiate from competitors.","explain":"Once a retailer chooses its target market, it must decide what it stands for in shoppers' minds: luxury, value, Canadian pride, expertise. Every retailing-mix decision should reinforce that position. Stocking merchandise that contradicts the position confuses and drives away loyal customers.","example":"Canadian Tire positioning itself around buying Canadian; the official Raphael & Sons case, where a high-end clothier lost loyal customers by adding cheap, low-quality suits.","contrast":"Retail positioning is about the retailer's image versus rivals. Shopper marketing is about understanding how consumers behave as shoppers across channels. Target market selection is WHO you serve; positioning is how you want THEM to see you.","trap":"A high-end retailer adding low-quality, low-priced goods = wrong retail positioning (official question). Distractors: cross-channel shopping, location, shopper marketing. In a downturn, the course's answer is to keep quality consistent: loyal customers may buy slightly less, but they are not looking for cheaper products.","mnemonic":"Position = the picture of your store in the shopper's head.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-positioning-matrix","topic":"t11-2","term":"Retail positioning matrix","definition":"A framework that positions retailers on two dimensions: breadth of product line (narrow to broad) and value added (low to high), where value added includes elements such as location, product reliability, prestige and service.","explain":"The matrix produces four quadrants. Broad line with high value added describes department stores; broad line with low value added describes mass merchandisers and warehouse clubs; narrow line with high value added describes luxury specialists; narrow line with low value added describes discount specialty stores. Retailers use it to see where they stand and which direction to move.","example":"Holt Renfrew or Hudson's Bay (broad/high), Walmart or Costco (broad/low), Tiffany (narrow/high), Payless ShoeSource-style stores (narrow/low).","contrast":"The axes are breadth of product line and value added, NOT price and quality, and NOT depth of line or level of service. Service is one element of value added, while breadth is the other axis entirely.","trap":"Read the scenario twice: count the product lines (one = narrow, many = broad), then look for prestige, location, reliability and service cues (high vs low value added). A question may list breadth of line as a 'value added' element; it is not.","mnemonic":"Two questions: how many lines? how much extra value?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Retail positioning matrix quadrants","head":["Quadrant","Breadth of line","Value added","Course examples"],"rows":[["Broad line / high value added","Broad","High","Department stores such as Holt Renfrew, Hudson's Bay"],["Broad line / low value added","Broad","Low","Walmart, Costco"],["Narrow line / high value added","Narrow","High","Luxury specialists such as Tiffany"],["Narrow line / low value added","Narrow","Low","Payless ShoeSource, Dollarama-style discount stores"]]}},{"id":"c11-shopper-marketing","topic":"t11-2","term":"Shopper marketing","definition":"A discipline designed to understand how consumers behave as shoppers in different channels and formats. It extends outside the store to the time and place a consumer first thinks about purchasing, and is by necessity multichannel.","explain":"Shopper marketing follows the shopper's journey from the first thought of buying (seeing a social post, reading a flyer) to the shelf. Because that journey crosses many touchpoints, it uses traditional media, new media, direct marketing, loyalty programs, trade promotion and in-store tools together.","example":"A spice company tracks when and where customers first think about buying spices and coordinates digital ads, loyalty offers and shelf displays (official Graham's Spice question).","contrast":"Shopper marketing studies shopper behaviour across channels; retail positioning is the retailer's desired image; category management gives one manager profit responsibility for a product category; multichannel retailing is about the retailer's own selling channels.","trap":"Cue words 'traditional media, new media, direct marketing, loyalty, trade promotion' and 'when and where customers first think about purchasing' = shopper marketing. It is NOT limited to in-store activity. Distractors: understanding consumers, retail positioning, purchase matrix.","mnemonic":"Shopper marketing starts at the first thought, not the front door.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-retailing-mix","topic":"t11-3","term":"Retailing mix (four quadrants)","definition":"The strategic components a retailer manages to satisfy its target market: the products and services mix, the retail pricing mix, the physical location mix and the communications mix, arranged around the target market and consumers and surrounded by information.","explain":"The retailing mix is the retailer's version of the marketing mix. Each quadrant has specific elements that exam questions ask you to place, for example shrinkage in pricing, number of stores in location, guarantees in products and services, and database marketing in communications. The communications mix exists to communicate the retailer's unique benefits through integrated, multiple channels, as in the Indigo example.","example":"Indigo coordinates its stores, website, email, loyalty program and social media so every channel communicates the same benefits.","contrast":"The retailing mix (four quadrants) is narrower than the full list of retailer marketing decisions, which starts with target market selection. Catalogues appear in the communications quadrant even though catalogues are also a non-store retailing format.","trap":"Quadrant placement traps: shrinkage and 'image, quality and value' are PRICING; logistics, size and layout, format and number of stores are LOCATION; guarantees and exchanges, sales assistance and product-service trade-offs are PRODUCTS AND SERVICES; displays and signage, catalogues, website and database marketing are COMMUNICATIONS.","mnemonic":"P-P-L-C around the customer: Products, Pricing, Location, Communications.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"The retailing mix","head":["Quadrant","Elements in the course figure"],"rows":[["Products and services mix","Variety and assortment; sales functions and assistance; customer service; guarantees and exchanges; product-service trade-offs"],["Retail pricing mix","Image, quality and value; markups; markdowns; shrinkage"],["Physical location mix","Store location; logistics; size and layout; format; number of stores"],["Communications mix","Personal selling; advertising; displays and signage; public relations; catalogues; telephone sales and follow-up; website; direct marketing; database marketing"],["Centre and surround","Target market and consumers at the centre; information surrounds the mix"]]}},{"id":"c11-merchandise-mix","topic":"t11-3","term":"Merchandise mix (breadth and depth)","definition":"How many different types of products a store carries and in what assortment. Breadth is the number of different product lines carried; depth is the number of items within each product line.","explain":"The merchandise mix sits in the products and services quadrant. A store carrying shoes, appliances, CDs and men's clothing has a breadth of four lines; carrying 60 shoe styles and sizes is depth in the shoe line. Retailers adjust breadth and depth to match their target market and positioning.","example":"A sporting goods store adding a new camping line increases breadth; adding 40 new fishing lures to its fishing line increases depth.","contrast":"Merchandise mix (which products and how much assortment) differs from store atmosphere (how the store looks, sounds and smells) and level of service (how much help customers get), although all three are part of the products and services mix.","trap":"Official definition: 'how many different products a store carries, and in what assortment'. The distractor 'and how they are displayed' is wrong, because display is atmosphere. Do not reverse breadth (lines) and depth (items per line).","mnemonic":"Breadth = how many aisles of different lines; depth = how many choices in each aisle.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-category-management","topic":"t11-3","term":"Category management","definition":"Assigning a manager responsibility for the profitability of a product category (for example, shoes) and managing that category as a strategic business unit; it aligns the assortment with the target market.","explain":"Instead of buying and pricing items one at a time, the retailer gives a category manager ownership of a whole category: which brands to carry, shelf space, pricing and promotion, and the category's profit. This keeps each category focused on what the target market wants and makes performance accountable.","example":"At a grocery chain, one manager runs the entire snack category, choosing brands, shelf space and promotions and answering for its profit.","contrast":"Category management is an internal management practice; a category killer is a type of discount store. Shopper marketing studies shopper behaviour across channels; category management organizes the assortment and profit of a product category.","trap":"The word 'category' tempts students toward category killer. Look for cues such as 'manager responsible for the profitability of a category' and 'strategic business unit'. Category management is NOT one of the nine wholesaler functions.","mnemonic":"Category management = a CEO for each category.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-store-atmosphere","topic":"t11-3","term":"Store atmosphere (atmospherics)","definition":"The overall impression a store conveys, created by atmospherics such as layout, colours, lighting, music and scent; it shapes the retail image.","explain":"Store atmosphere is part of the products and services mix. It works on shoppers' senses to signal what kind of store this is (calm and upscale, fast and bargain-focused, playful and social) and should reinforce the retailer's positioning.","example":"A home décor store using warm lighting, soft acoustic music and a subtle cedar scent to feel calm and upscale.","contrast":"Atmosphere is how the store FEELS; merchandise mix is WHAT it carries; level of service is HOW MUCH HELP customers get. Changing layout and lighting without changing products or staff changes only the atmosphere.","trap":"Sensory cues (music, lighting, scent, colour, layout) = store atmosphere. Markups and markdowns are pricing, not atmosphere. The official merchandise-mix distractor about 'how products are displayed' is really an atmosphere idea.","mnemonic":"Atmosphere = what you see, hear and smell.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-retail-pricing","topic":"t11-3","term":"Retail pricing: markup, markdown and shrinkage","definition":"Markup is the difference between the retailer's cost and its selling price, usually expressed by retailers as a percentage of selling price. A markdown is a reduction in the original selling price to move slow-selling merchandise. Shrinkage is inventory lost to theft, breakage and paperwork errors.","explain":"The retail pricing mix covers image, quality and value, markups, markdowns and shrinkage. Price signals the store's image, so it must fit the positioning. Markups must be high enough to cover expected markdowns and shrinkage, which is why shrinkage is treated as a pricing issue.","example":"A jacket bought for $60 and sold for $75 has a $15 markup, which is 20% of the selling price. Cutting last season's snowboards by 40% is a markdown.","contrast":"A markdown is a temporary cut on a retailer's own slow stock; off-price retailing is an ongoing business model of selling brand names below regular prices by buying excess inventory. Shrinkage is the retail term for these inventory losses; risk bearing is the WHOLESALER function of absorbing ownership risk.","trap":"Markup on price = (price - cost) / price; markup on cost = (price - cost) / cost. For markdown percentages, divide by the ORIGINAL price. Shoplifting, breakage and paperwork errors = shrinkage, and it sits in the PRICING quadrant.","mnemonic":"Markup goes up, markdown goes down, shrinkage goes missing.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-off-price-retailing","topic":"t11-3","term":"Off-price retailing","definition":"Selling brand-name merchandise at lower than regular prices, made possible largely by buying manufacturers' excess inventory. Types include warehouse clubs, outlet stores, single-price (extreme-value) stores and off-price chains.","explain":"Off-price retailers do not just run sales; their whole model is built on buying cheaply (overruns, cancelled orders, end-of-season goods) and passing the savings on. Each type has a distinctive cue: membership and bulk for warehouse clubs, a manufacturer's own excess stock for outlet stores, fixed low price points for single-price stores, and treasure-hunt brand-name fashion for off-price chains.","example":"Costco (warehouse club), factory outlet stores, Dollarama (single-price / extreme value), Winners (off-price chain).","contrast":"Off-price retailing vs markdown: a markdown is a one-time cut on a store's own slow stock. Off-price vs a hypermarket: a hypermarket combines groceries and general merchandise without a membership or excess-inventory model.","trap":"'Membership fee + bulk' = warehouse club; 'manufacturer sells its own excess or discontinued stock' = outlet store; 'one or a few fixed low price points' = single-price/extreme value; 'brand-name excess inventory bought from manufacturers, sold below department-store prices' = off-price chain. A full-service department store is not off-price.","mnemonic":"Off-price = brand names, off the regular price, from excess inventory.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Off-price retailers","head":["Type","How it works","Example"],"rows":[["Warehouse club","No-frills warehouse stores selling food and general merchandise in bulk to members who pay a fee","Costco"],["Outlet store","Stores (often manufacturer-owned) selling a brand's excess or discontinued stock at reduced prices","Factory outlet stores"],["Single-price / extreme-value store","Small no-frills stores selling at one or a few low price points","Dollarama"],["Off-price chain","Buys brand-name apparel and home goods from manufacturers' excess inventory and sells below regular prices","Winners"]]}},{"id":"c11-retail-location","topic":"t11-3","term":"Physical location: shopping areas, number of stores, non-store","definition":"Physical location decisions cover where to locate (central business district, regional shopping centre, community shopping centre, strip location, power centre or lifestyle centre), how many stores to operate, and whether to use non-store locations such as mail or online.","explain":"Each location type has a signature: downtown for the central business district; 50-150 stores with department-store anchors for a regional centre; one primary store plus 20-40 smaller stores for a community centre; a cluster of stores on a busy street for a strip; several national big-box anchors or category killers in a huge strip for a power centre; and an open-air, upscale village of shops, dining and entertainment for a lifestyle centre.","example":"A downtown Calgary bookstore (central business district); a giant open-air strip of big-box anchors on the edge of Edmonton (power centre); a neighbourhood row of a dry cleaner, pizza place and pharmacy (strip location).","contrast":"Power centre vs regional centre: big-box national anchors or category killers in a strip vs 50-150 stores anchored by department stores. Community centre vs strip: one primary anchor plus 20-40 stores vs a simple cluster of neighbourhood stores. Lifestyle centre vs power centre: both open-air, but lifestyle is upscale and leisure-oriented.","trap":"Count the stores and identify the anchor. 'Downtown/office towers' = CBD; '50-150 stores, department-store anchors' = regional; 'one primary store + 20-40 smaller' = community; 'busy street cluster' = strip; 'several big-box national anchors' = power centre; 'open-air upscale with dining and entertainment' = lifestyle centre. Markdowns are pricing, not location.","mnemonic":"C-R-C-S-P-L: City core, Regional giant, Community anchor, Strip street, Power boxes, Lifestyle village.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Retail location types","head":["Location type","Description"],"rows":[["Central business district","The downtown area of a city, with heavy foot traffic from office workers"],["Regional shopping centre","50-150 stores anchored by department stores, drawing from a wide area"],["Community shopping centre","One primary store plus 20-40 smaller stores serving nearby neighbourhoods"],["Strip location","A cluster of stores along a busy street serving everyday neighbourhood needs"],["Power centre","A huge shopping strip with several national anchor stores, often category killers"],["Lifestyle centre","An open-air, upscale centre mixing specialty shops, dining and entertainment"]]}},{"id":"c11-non-store-retailing","topic":"t11-4","term":"Non-store retailing and click-and-mortar","definition":"Retailing that occurs outside a retail outlet through activities involving varying levels of customer and retailer involvement: automatic vending, direct mail and catalogues, television home shopping, online retailing, telemarketing and direct selling. Bricks-and-mortar stores that also engage in non-store retailing become click-and-mortar retailers.","explain":"Non-store forms range from very low involvement (a vending machine with no staff) to very high personal involvement (a consultant hosting an in-home party). Many traditional retailers now combine stores with non-store channels, which makes them click-and-mortar.","example":"Avon and Tupperware (direct selling through in-home parties), snack machines in hospitals (automatic vending), shopping channels (TV home shopping), a decades-old bookstore chain adding an app (click-and-mortar).","contrast":"Direct selling = face-to-face (door-to-door, home parties); telemarketing = by phone; direct mail and catalogues = printed offers; TV home shopping = televised products ordered by phone or online; automatic vending = machines. A strip location is a store-based location, not non-store retailing.","trap":"Official question: the TRUE statement is 'activities of varying levels of customer and retailer involvement, outside the store environment'. It is NOT limited to small mobile activities like food carts, NOT about weather, and NOT 'specifically automated or electronic' (direct selling and telemarketing are non-store but involve people).","mnemonic":"Non-store = Vending, Mail, TV, Online, Phone, Person (direct selling).","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Forms of non-store retailing","items":["Automatic vending","Direct mail and catalogues","Television home shopping","Online retailing","Telemarketing","Direct selling (door-to-door, in-home parties: Avon, Tupperware)","Bricks-and-mortar stores that add non-store channels = click-and-mortar"]}},{"id":"c11-online-six-cs","topic":"t11-4","term":"Online retailing and the six Cs","definition":"Online retailing lets consumers search, evaluate (compare) and purchase. Consumers shop online for six reasons: convenience, choice, communication, customization, cost and control.","explain":"Each C answers a different 'why online?' Convenience is shopping any time, anywhere; choice is a broad selection plus assistance; communication is two-way interaction through email, chat, reviews and social media; customization is tailoring the product to the individual; cost is lower prices and easy comparison; control is empowered consumers deciding at their own pace and on their own terms. Reviews create smarter, more informed and more demanding consumers, and influencers' opinions matter.","example":"Dell letting shoppers choose their computer's components (customization); a shopper researching cameras for weeks and deciding without a salesperson (control).","contrast":"Control vs convenience: control is about who runs the decision (the empowered consumer, at their own pace); convenience is about when and where. Customization vs choice: customization changes the product for you; choice is picking from a bigger existing range. Communication vs control: communication is exchanging messages; control is deciding for yourself.","trap":"Scenario cues: '3 a.m. / from home / no store open' = convenience; '40 styles no local store carries' = choice; 'chatted with a rep / emailed a question' = communication; 'chose the components / designed my own' = customization; 'lowest price / saved $40' = cost; 'researched at my own pace, decided on my own terms' = control (official: empowered consumers).","mnemonic":"When/where = convenience; how many = choice; talk = communication; made for me = customization; money = cost; my pace = control.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Six Cs of online shopping","head":["C","Meaning","Scenario cue"],"rows":[["Convenience","Shop 24/7 from anywhere, no travel or store hours","Middle of the night, from home"],["Choice","Broad selection plus assistance in finding products","More styles and sizes than any local store"],["Communication","Two-way interaction: email, live chat, reviews, social media","Chatted with a rep, asked a question"],["Customization","Product or experience tailored to the individual","Chose the components, designed my own"],["Cost","Lower prices and easy price comparison","Lowest price, saved money"],["Control","Empowered consumers search, evaluate and decide on their own time and terms","Own pace, decided without a salesperson"]]}},{"id":"c11-multichannel-retailing","topic":"t11-4","term":"Multichannel retailers and cross-channel shoppers","definition":"Multichannel retailers use and integrate a combination of traditional store and non-store formats, such as stores, catalogues and online retailing. Cross-channel shoppers research in one channel and buy in another: showrooming (examine in store, buy online) and webrooming (research online, buy in store).","explain":"Using several channels lets retailers offer a broader spectrum of benefits and experiences and gain synergy from sharing information among channel operations. For example, a store can take an order for an out-of-stock item through the catalogue. Retailers must also design channels to win back cross-channel shoppers rather than lose them to rivals.","example":"A shopper tries on ski boots at a Banff shop, then orders them online (showrooming); another reads blender reviews online, then buys in store that afternoon (webrooming).","contrast":"Showrooming and webrooming run in opposite directions. Multichannel retailing is about the retailer's integrated channels; shopper marketing is about understanding shopper behaviour; click-and-mortar describes a store-based retailer that has added non-store channels.","trap":"Official Jimmy's question: ordering an out-of-stock item through the catalogue = synergy of sharing information among channel operations. Distractors include 'the Internet is a relationship-building medium' and 'reduces the size of its target markets'. Do not reverse showrooming and webrooming.","mnemonic":"SHOWroom = the store is the showroom, buy online; WEBroom = the web is the research room, buy in store.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-modern-retail-environment","topic":"t11-5","term":"Modern retail environment and retail trends","definition":"Today's retail environment: face-to-face (F2F) retail is not dead (instant gratification, convenience, experience, savings); omnichannel and social media purchasing are growing; middle-market retailers are declining; technology and retail optimization (e.g., Shopify) matter; and consumers dislike long lines, poor customer service and stock-outs.","explain":"The 2025 Retail Council of Canada / Adyen survey adds facts to memorize: mobile banking is part of the shift; nearly two-thirds of online shoppers reach checkout and then leave to compare shipping costs and prices on other sites (a cart-abandonment threat); only 9% use a digital wallet, while most use debit or credit cards; and retailers can improve online shopping with experiential or interactive tools such as Instagram storefronts, QR codes and GPS.","example":"A small Edmonton boutique uses Shopify to sync inventory across its store, website and Instagram shop, so customers can buy from a post and pick up in store.","contrast":"The decline of middle-market retailers is a market squeeze on mid-priced chains, not the wheel of retailing (which describes one entrant trading up over time). The 9% figure is digital-wallet use; nearly two-thirds is the checkout-abandonment figure, so do not swap them.","trap":"Number traps: two-thirds (abandon at checkout to compare shipping and prices) vs 9% (digital wallet). A statement that most shoppers pay with digital wallets is false. Reasons F2F survives: instant gratification, convenience, experience, savings.","mnemonic":"Two-thirds bail at checkout; 9% tap a wallet.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Modern retail environment and 2025 RCC / Adyen survey facts","items":["F2F not dead yet: instant gratification, convenience, experience, savings","Omnichannel and social media purchasing","Decline of middle-market retailers","Role of technology; retail optimization (e.g., Shopify)","Consumers dislike long lines, poor customer service, stock-outs","Survey: mobile banking is part of the shift","Survey: nearly two-thirds of online shoppers reach checkout, then leave to compare shipping costs and prices (cart-abandonment threat)","Survey: 9% use a digital wallet; most use debit or credit cards","Improve online retailing with experiential or interactive tools: Instagram storefronts (online social shopping), QR codes, GPS"]}},{"id":"c11-wheel-of-retailing","topic":"t11-5","term":"Wheel of retailing","definition":"A concept that describes how new forms of retail outlets enter the market as low-status, low-margin, low-price outlets, then gradually add services and raise their prices and status, leaving room for new low-price entrants.","explain":"The wheel keeps turning: today's discounter becomes tomorrow's full-service, higher-priced retailer, which opens the bottom of the market to the next wave of discounters. It explains why no low-price position stays empty for long.","example":"McDonald's began as a cheap walk-up burger stand, then added seating, drive-thrus, salads and premium coffee, and raised prices, leaving room for new low-price food options.","contrast":"Wheel of retailing = the trade-up dynamic in price, status and service. Retail life cycle = stages of growth and decline (early growth, accelerated development, maturity, decline). Intertype competition is rivalry between dissimilar store types.","trap":"Cue words 'started cheap, added services, raised prices, undercut by new discounters' = wheel. 'Grew, peaked, declined' = retail life cycle. In ordering questions, trading up comes BEFORE new low-price entrants appear.","mnemonic":"The wheel turns upward, and a new discounter climbs on at the bottom.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-retail-life-cycle","topic":"t11-5","term":"Retail life cycle","definition":"The process of growth and decline that retail outlets, like products, experience. The stages are early growth, accelerated development, maturity and decline.","explain":"Retail formats rise and fall much like products: market share and profit climb through early growth and accelerated development, level off in maturity, and fall in decline. Retailers must adapt or be replaced as their format ages.","example":"Video-rental chains spread rapidly, peaked, then declined and closed as streaming arrived.","contrast":"Retail life cycle (growth and decline stages) vs wheel of retailing (low-price entrants trade up and are replaced). Its stages differ from the product life cycle's introduction, growth, maturity and decline.","trap":"Order trap: early growth, accelerated development, maturity, decline. 'Introduction' is a product life cycle stage, not a retail life cycle stage.","mnemonic":"Early, Accelerated, Mature, Decline: 'Every Album Must Drop.'","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Retail life cycle stages","items":["1. Early growth","2. Accelerated development","3. Maturity","4. Decline"]}},{"id":"c11-wholesaling","topic":"t11-6","term":"Wholesaling and the types of wholesalers","definition":"Wholesaling includes all the activities in selling goods or services to those who buy for resale or business use. Wholesalers fall into three main categories: merchant wholesalers, agents and brokers, and manufacturer's branches and offices.","explain":"Wholesaling is defined by the buyer's purpose, not the order size or the product. The course figure (memorize it) splits wholesalers by ownership and title: merchant wholesalers are independent and take title; agents and brokers are independent but do not take title; manufacturer's branches and offices are owned by the producer.","example":"An office-supply distributor selling copy paper to hospitals for their operations is wholesaling; a food distributor selling to restaurants is wholesaling.","contrast":"Retailing sells to ultimate consumers for personal, family or household use; wholesaling sells for resale or business use. A business that is the final user (a dental clinic buying gloves) is still a wholesale customer.","trap":"Do not let 'final user' fool you: business use = wholesaling. Retailer-sponsored cooperatives are contractual RETAIL systems, not one of the three wholesaler categories.","mnemonic":"Wholesale = for Resale or for the Business.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Types of wholesalers (course figure)","items":["Merchant wholesalers (independently owned; TAKE TITLE)","Full-service merchant wholesalers: general merchandise wholesalers; specialty merchandise wholesalers","Limited-service merchant wholesalers: rack jobbers; cash and carry wholesalers; drop shippers; truck jobbers","Agents and brokers (do NOT take title; earn commissions): manufacturers' agents; selling agents; brokers","Manufacturer's branches and offices (owned by the manufacturer): branch office (carries inventory); sales office (no inventory)"]}},{"id":"c11-merchant-wholesalers","topic":"t11-6","term":"Merchant wholesalers (full-service: general and specialty merchandise)","definition":"Merchant wholesalers are independently owned firms that take title to the merchandise they handle. Full-service merchant wholesalers provide a wide range of services and include general merchandise wholesalers (a broad range of products with little depth) and specialty merchandise wholesalers (a narrow range of products with a deep assortment).","explain":"Taking title means the wholesaler buys and owns the goods, so it also bears the risks of ownership. Full-service wholesalers offer services such as credit, delivery and sales support; limited-service merchant wholesalers (rack jobbers, cash and carry, drop shippers, truck jobbers) offer fewer.","example":"A distributor carrying hardware, cleaning supplies, small appliances and toys for small retailers (general merchandise); a seafood distributor with a very deep fish assortment for restaurants (specialty merchandise).","contrast":"Merchant wholesalers take title; agents and brokers do not. General merchandise = broad but shallow; specialty merchandise = narrow but deep. Full-service vs limited-service is about how many services are offered; both take title.","trap":"'Takes title / buys and owns' = merchant wholesaler. Breadth vs depth cues decide general vs specialty merchandise. A merchant wholesaler is independently owned, so 'owned by the manufacturer' points to branches and offices instead.","mnemonic":"Merchant = owns the merchandise.","figure":null},{"id":"c11-limited-service-wholesalers","topic":"t11-6","term":"Limited-service merchant wholesalers","definition":"Merchant wholesalers that take title but offer fewer services: rack jobbers (furnish and stock display racks, own the inventory, bill only for items sold), cash and carry wholesalers (customers pay cash and provide their own transportation), drop shippers or desk jobbers (own the goods but never physically handle them; the producer ships directly to the customer) and truck jobbers (small warehouse; sell perishables for cash from the truck).","explain":"All four take title because they are merchant wholesalers. Each has one signature feature: racks and billing only for items sold (rack jobber), no credit and no delivery (cash and carry), title without physical handling (drop shipper), and perishables sold from the truck (truck jobber).","example":"Rack jobbers for magazines and hosiery; cash and carry for restaurant supplies; drop shippers for coal, lumber and chemicals; truck jobbers for bakery, dairy and meat.","contrast":"Drop shipper vs broker: both may never handle the goods, but the drop shipper TAKES TITLE and the broker does not. Rack jobber vs truck jobber: in-store racks billed on sales vs perishables sold for cash from a truck.","trap":"'Bills only for items sold' = rack jobber; 'no credit, no delivery, haul it yourself' = cash and carry; 'owns it but never touches it' = drop shipper; 'bread/milk sold for cash from the truck' = truck jobber. A selling agent is NOT a limited-service wholesaler.","mnemonic":"Racks, Cash, Drop, Truck: 'Really Cool Delivery Trucks.'","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Limited-service merchant wholesalers (all take title)","head":["Type","Distinctive feature","Typical products"],"rows":[["Rack jobber","Furnishes and stocks racks in stores, owns the inventory, bills only for items sold","Hosiery, magazines"],["Cash and carry wholesaler","Customers pay cash and furnish their own transportation; no credit or delivery","Supplies for small retailers and restaurants"],["Drop shipper (desk jobber)","Owns the goods but never handles, stores or delivers them; producer ships directly","Coal, lumber, chemicals"],["Truck jobber","Small warehouse; sells perishables for cash from the truck","Bakery, dairy, meat"]]}},{"id":"c11-agents-brokers","topic":"t11-6","term":"Agents and brokers (manufacturers' agents, selling agents, brokers)","definition":"Agents and brokers do not take title to merchandise and typically earn commissions. Manufacturers' agents work for several producers, carrying non-competitive lines in an exclusive territory; selling agents represent a single producer and are responsible for its entire marketing function; brokers bring buyers and sellers together to make sales, usually with no continuous relationship.","explain":"Because they never own the goods, agents and brokers earn commissions rather than resale margins. To tell them apart, count producers and scope: several producers with complementary lines in a territory (manufacturers' agent), one producer with the whole marketing job (selling agent), or one-off matchmaking between a buyer and a seller (broker).","example":"A rep selling cutlery, cookware and linens from different makers to Alberta kitchen stores (manufacturers' agent); a firm handling all marketing for a small winery (selling agent); a real estate, food or insurance broker.","contrast":"Agents and brokers vs merchant wholesalers: no title vs title. Manufacturers' agent vs selling agent: several producers and partial selling vs one producer and the entire marketing function. Broker vs drop shipper: neither handles goods, but only the drop shipper takes title.","trap":"'Several non-competing producers + exclusive territory' = manufacturers' agent; 'entire marketing function for one producer' = selling agent; 'brings buyers and sellers together, no ongoing relationship' = broker. Any option saying an agent or broker takes title is wrong.","mnemonic":"Agents and brokers never own; they only connect.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Agents and brokers (no title)","head":["Type","Represents","Role"],"rows":[["Manufacturers' agent","Several producers","Sells non-competitive, complementary lines in an exclusive territory"],["Selling agent","One producer","Responsible for the producer's entire marketing function"],["Broker","Buyer or seller, per transaction","Brings buyers and sellers together; usually no continuous relationship (real estate, food, insurance)"]]}},{"id":"c11-manufacturer-branches","topic":"t11-6","term":"Manufacturer's branches and offices","definition":"Wholesaling operations owned by the manufacturer, a form of vertical integration in wholesaling. A branch office carries inventory and performs wholesaler functions; a sales office does not carry inventory and performs only a sales function.","explain":"Some producers prefer to control wholesaling themselves rather than rely on independent intermediaries. A branch office stocks and distributes the manufacturer's products like a wholesaler, while a sales office is a selling base whose products ship from elsewhere.","example":"An appliance maker's regional Winnipeg facility that stores and delivers inventory (branch office); a chipmaker's downtown office where salespeople take orders but hold no stock (sales office).","contrast":"Branch office = inventory; sales office = no inventory. Both are owned by the manufacturer, unlike merchant wholesalers (independent, take title) and agents and brokers (independent, no title).","trap":"'Owned by the manufacturer' rules out merchant wholesalers and agents. Then check for inventory: stocks goods = branch office; only sells = sales office.","mnemonic":"Branch holds the Boxes; Sales office only Sells.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Manufacturer's branches and offices","items":["Owned by the manufacturer (vertical integration in wholesaling)","Branch office: carries inventory and performs wholesaler functions","Sales office: no inventory; performs only a sales function"]}},{"id":"c11-wholesaler-functions","topic":"t11-6","term":"Wholesaler functions (nine)","definition":"The nine functions wholesalers perform: selling and promoting; buying and assortment building; bulk breaking; warehousing; transportation; financing; risk bearing; market information; and management services and counselling.","explain":"Wholesalers earn their place by doing these jobs more efficiently than producers or retailers could. Bulk breaking splits large lots into small ones; assortment building gathers a variety of products; financing extends credit; risk bearing absorbs ownership losses; market information passes along news about competitors, products and prices; management services and counselling helps retailers run their businesses (training, layout, systems).","example":"A grocery wholesaler buys pasta by the truckload and resells it to small grocers in cases of 12 (bulk breaking) and lets them pay in 60 days (financing).","contrast":"Bulk breaking (quantity) vs assortment building (variety). Financing (giving customers credit) vs risk bearing (absorbing losses from owning goods). Market information (reporting trends) vs management services and counselling (hands-on operational help).","trap":"Category management, shrinkage and procurement are RETAILER terms, not wholesaler functions. Scenario cues: 'truckload into cases' = bulk breaking; 'pay in 60 days' = financing; 'spoiled stock, wholesaler absorbs the loss' = risk bearing; 'helped redesign layout and train clerks' = management services and counselling.","mnemonic":"Sell, Buy, Break, Store, Move, Fund, Risk, Inform, Advise.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Nine wholesaler functions","items":["Selling and promoting","Buying and assortment building","Bulk breaking","Warehousing","Transportation","Financing","Risk bearing","Market information","Management services and counselling"]}}],"activities":[{"id":"a11-01","type":"sort","concept":"c11-wholesaling","title":"Sort the wholesalers","prompt":"Drag each wholesaler type into its main category from the course figure.","buckets":["Merchant wholesalers (take title)","Agents and brokers (no title)","Manufacturer's branches and offices (manufacturer-owned)"],"items":[{"text":"General merchandise wholesaler","bucket":0},{"text":"Specialty merchandise wholesaler","bucket":0},{"text":"Rack jobber","bucket":0},{"text":"Cash and carry wholesaler","bucket":0},{"text":"Drop shipper (desk jobber)","bucket":0},{"text":"Truck jobber","bucket":0},{"text":"Manufacturers' agent","bucket":1},{"text":"Selling agent","bucket":1},{"text":"Broker","bucket":1},{"text":"Branch office","bucket":2},{"text":"Sales office","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Merchant wholesalers are independent and take title: full-service (general and specialty merchandise) and limited-service (rack jobbers, cash and carry, drop shippers, truck jobbers). Agents and brokers never take title and earn commissions. Branch offices (with inventory) and sales offices (no inventory) are owned by the manufacturer."},{"id":"a11-02","type":"sort","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","title":"Six Cs, part 1: convenience, choice or cost?","prompt":"Drag each online shopper's experience to the benefit it best illustrates.","buckets":["Convenience","Choice","Cost"],"items":[{"text":"Orders groceries at 2 a.m. without leaving home","bucket":0},{"text":"Shops from a remote northern town with no mall nearby","bucket":0},{"text":"Finds 40 styles of size-15 boots that no local store carries","bucket":1},{"text":"Uses a size-finder tool to narrow down a huge online selection","bucket":1},{"text":"Uses a price-comparison site to find the cheapest TV","bucket":2},{"text":"Saves $30 because the online price beats the mall's","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Convenience is about WHEN and WHERE you can shop (any time, anywhere). Choice is the broad selection, plus assistance in finding products. Cost is lower prices and easy price comparison."},{"id":"a11-03","type":"sort","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","title":"Six Cs, part 2: communication, customization or control?","prompt":"Drag each online shopper's experience to the benefit it best illustrates.","buckets":["Communication","Customization","Control"],"items":[{"text":"Chats live with a company rep before ordering","bucket":0},{"text":"Emails a question and gets replies from the brand on social media","bucket":0},{"text":"Chooses the processor, memory and graphics card for a new laptop","bucket":1},{"text":"Designs custom sneakers with her own colours and initials","bucket":1},{"text":"Researches cameras for two weeks and decides at his own pace","bucket":2},{"text":"Evaluates options and decides on her own terms with no salesperson","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Communication is two-way interaction (chat, email, reviews, social media). Customization tailors the product itself to the buyer (the Dell example). Control describes empowered consumers who search, evaluate and decide on their own time and terms (the official camera example)."},{"id":"a11-04","type":"match","concept":"c11-depth-of-line","title":"Match the retail format","prompt":"Match each retail format with its description.","pairs":[{"left":"Specialty outlet","right":"Deep assortment in one narrow line at regular prices (e.g., Lululemon)"},{"left":"Category killer","right":"Deep assortment in one category at very low prices (e.g., Best Buy)"},{"left":"Department store","right":"Many different product lines organized into departments"},{"left":"Hypermarket / supercentre","right":"Very large store combining a full grocery line with general merchandise"},{"left":"Scrambled merchandising","right":"A drugstore adding groceries and patio furniture (unrelated lines)"},{"left":"Warehouse club","right":"Membership fee, bulk packages, no-frills warehouse (e.g., Costco)"},{"left":"Off-price chain","right":"Brand names bought from manufacturers' excess inventory, sold below regular prices (e.g., Winners)"},{"left":"Click-and-mortar retailer","right":"A bricks-and-mortar store that also sells through non-store channels such as online"}],"explanation":"Depth-of-line formats (specialty outlets, category killers) go deep in a narrow line, and category killers add discount prices. Breadth-of-line formats (department stores, hypermarkets) carry many lines. Scrambled merchandising adds unrelated lines to any store. Warehouse clubs and off-price chains are off-price formats, and click-and-mortar describes a store that adds non-store retailing."},{"id":"a11-05","type":"match","concept":"c11-retail-location","title":"Match the store location","prompt":"Match each location type with its description.","pairs":[{"left":"Central business district","right":"Downtown area among office towers with heavy foot traffic"},{"left":"Regional shopping centre","right":"50-150 stores anchored by department stores, drawing from a wide area"},{"left":"Community shopping centre","right":"One primary store plus 20-40 smaller stores"},{"left":"Strip location","right":"A cluster of neighbourhood stores along a busy street"},{"left":"Power centre","right":"A huge strip anchored by several national big-box stores or category killers"},{"left":"Lifestyle centre","right":"An open-air, upscale village of shops, restaurants and entertainment"}],"explanation":"Count stores and identify anchors: department-store anchors with 50-150 stores = regional; one primary store with 20-40 others = community; several big-box national anchors = power centre. Downtown = central business district; a simple street cluster = strip; open-air upscale with leisure = lifestyle centre."}],"questions":[{"id":"q11-001","concept":"c11-retailing","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ includes all activities involved in selling, renting, and providing goods and services to ultimate consumers for personal, family, or household use.","options":["manufacturing","wholesaling","retailing","facilitating"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retailing covers every activity (selling, renting, providing goods and services) aimed at ULTIMATE consumers for personal, family, or household use. Wholesaling is the tempting distractor, but it sells to those who buy for resale or business use, not to end consumers.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-002","concept":"c11-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Black Tie Rentals, a downtown Edmonton shop, rents tuxedos and suits to grooms and wedding guests for weekend events and returns them to its racks on Monday. How is Black Tie Rentals' core activity classified?","options":["Wholesaling, because the suits are rented rather than sold","Brokering, because no ownership passes to the customer","Retailing, because it provides goods to ultimate consumers for personal use","Non-store retailing, because customers do not keep the goods"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retailing includes renting as well as selling, as long as the customer is an ultimate consumer using the item for personal, family, or household use. Wholesaling is wrong because it is defined by the buyer's purpose (resale or business use), not by whether goods are sold or rented.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-003","concept":"c11-retailing","also":["c11-wholesaling"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Paper Co. sells cases of printer paper to families buying school supplies and to a Calgary accounting firm that uses the paper in its office. Which statement is correct?","options":["Both are retailing because the same product is sold in the same store","Both are wholesaling because the paper is sold by the case","Sales to families are retailing; sales to the accounting firm are wholesaling","Sales to the accounting firm are retailing because the firm is the final user"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The buyer's purpose decides it: ultimate consumers buying for personal or household use = retailing; buyers purchasing for resale or BUSINESS use = wholesaling. The accounting firm is the final user, but it uses the paper for business, which the definition places in wholesaling; order size (cases) is irrelevant.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-004","concept":"c11-retailing","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"Retailers invest in social and entertainment experiences partly because shopping is not only a way to acquire necessities but also a social activity and sometimes an adventure.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The instructor stresses that shopping is also a social activity and sometimes an adventure, which is why retailers add experiences such as dining and entertainment (e.g., West Edmonton Mall's water park) instead of only stocking necessities.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-005","concept":"c11-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of retailing?","options":["A Banff ski shop renting snowboards to vacationers","A food distributor selling cases of frozen fries to restaurants","A hair salon providing haircuts to local residents","An online store selling phone cases to students"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Restaurants buy the fries to use in their business (and resell them as meals), so that sale is wholesaling. Renting snowboards and providing haircuts are still retailing because the customers are ultimate consumers; renting and providing services are explicitly part of the retailing definition.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-006","concept":"c11-retail-utilities","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northside Pharmacy keeps its Regina location open 24 hours a day, seven days a week, so customers can fill prescriptions at 3 a.m. Which utility does this primarily create?","options":["Time utility","Place utility","Form utility","Possession utility"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Time utility means having products available WHEN consumers want them, and round-the-clock hours do exactly that. Place utility is the tempting choice, but it is about WHERE products are available (convenient locations), not the hours.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-007","concept":"c11-retail-utilities","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Summit Furniture lets customers take a sofa home today and pay nothing for 12 months through in-store financing, or put it on layaway. Which utility is Summit creating?","options":["Time utility","Possession utility","Form utility","Place utility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Possession utility makes it easier for consumers to take ownership or use of a product; credit, financing and layaway are the classic examples. Time utility is wrong because the issue is not when the store is open but how easily the customer can afford to own the sofa.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-008","concept":"c11-retail-utilities","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"At Northern Threads, a Winnipeg menswear store, in-house tailors hem trousers and adjust jacket sleeves so each suit fits the buyer before he leaves the store. Which utility does this create?","options":["Possession utility","Form utility","Place utility","Time utility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Form utility comes from producing or altering a product to suit the consumer, and tailoring changes the product itself. Possession utility is tempting because the customer takes the suit home, but possession utility is about easing ownership (credit, layaway), not changing the product.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-009","concept":"c11-retail-utilities","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pairing of retailer practice and utility is correct?","options":["Extended holiday shopping hours: place utility","Stores in nearly every neighbourhood: place utility","A layaway plan for winter coats: form utility","Assembling bicycles before pickup: possession utility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Convenient locations put products WHERE consumers want them, which is place utility. Extended hours create time utility (when), layaway creates possession utility (easier ownership), and assembling a product creates form utility (altering the product).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-010","concept":"c11-retail-utilities","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Possession utility is created when a retailer:","options":["makes it easier for consumers to take ownership or use of a product","has products available when consumers want to buy them","alters or assembles a product to suit the consumer","locates stores close to where consumers live and work"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Possession utility = easing ownership or use (credit, financing, layaway). The other options describe time utility (when), form utility (altering the product) and place utility (where).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-011","concept":"c11-retail-utilities","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Saskatoon appliance store finds that many shoppers love its fridges but leave without buying because they cannot pay the full price at once. Which utility should the store strengthen to win these sales?","options":["Time utility, by extending store hours","Possession utility, by offering credit or instalment plans","Place utility, by opening more locations","Form utility, by customizing fridge finishes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The barrier is affordability of ownership, so the fix is possession utility: credit, financing or instalment payments. Longer hours (time) or more stores (place) would not solve the shopper's inability to pay up front.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-012","concept":"c11-independent-chain","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Rosa owns and operates a single bakery-café in Lethbridge, making all buying and pricing decisions herself. Classified by form of ownership, her business is a(n):","options":["independent retailer","corporate chain","contractual system","franchise"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An independent retailer is owned by an individual and usually operates one store; it is the most common form of retail ownership. It is not a franchise or other contractual system because Rosa has not joined a group or licensed another firm's name and system.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-013","concept":"c11-independent-chain","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Grocers operates 140 stores across Canada, all owned by the same parent company, which negotiates purchases centrally for every store. Maple Grocers is a:","options":["retailer-sponsored cooperative","corporate chain","wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","hypermarket"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Multiple outlets under COMMON ownership with centralized buying define a corporate chain. Co-ops and voluntary chains are contractual systems made up of independently owned stores, and 'hypermarket' describes a merchandise-line format, not a form of ownership.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-014","concept":"c11-independent-chain","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the main advantage a corporate chain has over an independent retailer?","options":["Each outlet is independently owned, so local owners share promotion costs","Centralized buying gives it greater bargaining power with suppliers","It is automatically classified as a full-service retailer","It is protected from intertype competition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because a corporate chain buys for many outlets at once, it can negotiate better terms; centralized buying and bargaining power are its hallmark. Independently owned outlets sharing promotion describes a contractual system, not a corporate chain.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-015","concept":"c11-independent-chain","also":["c11-contractual-system"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Store A is one of 300 hardware stores under one banner; each is owned by a local entrepreneur, and the owners jointly run the group's buying operation. Store B is one of 300 stores under another banner, all owned by a single corporation. Which classification is correct?","options":["Store A is part of a corporate chain; Store B is part of a contractual system","Both are corporate chains because each operates under a common banner","Store A is part of a contractual system; Store B is part of a corporate chain","Store A is an independent retailer; Store B is a franchise"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ownership, not the banner, decides: independently owned stores banding together (here a retailer-sponsored co-op) form a contractual system, while outlets with common ownership form a corporate chain. Sharing a banner does not make Store A a corporate chain.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-016","concept":"c11-independent-chain","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The corporate chain is the most common form of retail ownership.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. The INDEPENDENT retailer, owned by an individual and usually operating one store, is the most common form of retail ownership. Corporate chains (common ownership of many outlets) and contractual systems are the other two ownership forms.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-017","concept":"c11-contractual-system","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Bright @ Night allows its independent members, retailers of lighting fixtures, to concentrate their buying power and to plan collaborative promotional and pricing activities. Bright @ Night is a _____.","options":["manufacturer's agent","contractual system","corporate chain","joint venture"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A contractual system is independently owned stores that band together to act like a chain, pooling buying power and coordinating promotion and pricing. It is not a corporate chain, because the members remain independently owned rather than sharing one owner.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-018","concept":"c11-contractual-system","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Pharmacy Partners was formed by 60 independent pharmacists, who jointly own and run the buying and warehousing operation that supplies their stores. This arrangement is a:","options":["retailer-sponsored cooperative","wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","franchise","corporate chain"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When independent RETAILERS themselves band together and run a shared buying or wholesale operation, it is a retailer-sponsored cooperative (like Home Hardware). A wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain is organized by a wholesaler, not by the retailers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-019","concept":"c11-contractual-system","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Westfield Foods, a grocery wholesaler, invites independent grocers to operate under a common store banner, buy their stock from Westfield and share joint advertising. The grocers remain independently owned. This is a:","options":["retailer-sponsored cooperative","wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","corporate chain","manufacturer's sales office"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A wholesaler organizing independent retailers into a chain-like group that buys from it is a wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain (IGA is the course example). It is not a retailer-sponsored co-op, because the wholesaler, not the retailers, sponsors the group.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-020","concept":"c11-contractual-system","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a franchise system such as Tim Hortons, the franchisee typically:","options":["owns all outlets in the system and buys centrally for them","pays a fee plus royalties to use the franchisor's name and operating system","takes title to goods but never physically handles them","earns a commission for bringing buyers and sellers together"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A franchise is a contractual system in which the franchisor supplies the name and system and the independently owning franchisee pays a fee plus royalties. Owning all outlets describes a corporate chain, taking title without handling the goods describes a drop shipper, and earning commissions for matching buyers and sellers describes a broker.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-021","concept":"c11-contractual-system","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which feature BEST distinguishes a contractual system from a corporate chain?","options":["Contractual-system stores are independently owned but band together to act like a chain","Contractual systems always offer full service, while chains offer self-service","Corporate chains cannot use centralized buying","Contractual systems sell only through non-store formats"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The dividing line is ownership: corporate chain outlets share common ownership, while contractual-system stores keep separate owners and cooperate by agreement. Level of service and store vs non-store format are separate classification schemes, and corporate chains are known FOR centralized buying.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-022","concept":"c11-contractual-system","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a type of contractual system?","options":["Retailer-sponsored cooperative","Corporate chain","Wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","Franchise"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The three contractual systems are retailer-sponsored cooperatives, wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chains and franchises, all built on independently owned stores. A corporate chain is a separate form of ownership in which one company owns all the outlets.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-023","concept":"c11-level-of-service","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kyra aims to expand her women's clothing boutique across Canada and make it a high-end store. Well-trained, informed salespeople in each store will give customers a personalized shopping experience, using a database of regular customers' preferred colours, textures and sizes so customers do not have to repeat these details every visit. Customers' needs will be addressed in-store. Kyra is aiming to make her boutique:","options":["move from limited service to full service","move from self-service to full service","become a more self-service store","offer full-service at self-service prices"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Trained salespeople, personalized help and a customer-preference database are hallmarks of FULL service. A boutique that already has salespeople offers limited service, not self-service, so 'from self-service to full service' misstates the starting point, and nothing in the plan lowers prices to self-service levels.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-024","concept":"c11-level-of-service","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"At CostLess Warehouse, shoppers find products on bare steel shelving, load their own carts and bag their own purchases; there are almost no salespeople on the floor. Classified by level of service, CostLess is a:","options":["limited-service retailer","full-service retailer","category killer","self-service retailer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"When the customer performs most of the functions, the store is self-service; warehouse clubs, pay-at-the-pump gas stations and online stores are course examples. 'Category killer' classifies a store by merchandise line, a different scheme from level of service.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-025","concept":"c11-level-of-service","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pairing of retailer and level of service is correct?","options":["Warehouse club: self-service","Pay-at-the-pump gas station: full-service","Holt Renfrew: limited-service","Online store: full-service"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Warehouse clubs are self-service: customers do most of the work. Gas stations and online stores are also self-service, while Holt Renfrew, with personal shoppers and attentive staff, is the course's full-service example.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-026","concept":"c11-level-of-service","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Wildrose Mall adds a water park, a food hall and live weekend concerts to give shoppers reasons to stay longer. In the products and services mix, these additions are best described as:","options":["ancillary services","prepurchase services","postpurchase services","self-service features"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Ancillary services are extras beyond the core shopping transaction, such as experiences and dining (the course's West Edmonton Mall water park example). Prepurchase services help before a sale (information, demonstrations) and postpurchase services follow it (delivery, returns, repairs).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-027","concept":"c11-level-of-service","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT typical of a full-service retailer?","options":["Personalized assistance from knowledgeable salespeople","Records of each regular customer's preferences","Customers locate, select and carry products with little staff help","Services such as alterations and delivery"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Customers doing most of the work describes self-service. Full-service retailers provide many services and personal attention: knowledgeable staff, preference records (as in Kyra's boutique), alterations and delivery.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-028","concept":"c11-level-of-service","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A retailer that offers some services, such as credit and merchandise returns, but leaves most of the shopping to customers is a _____ retailer.","options":["self-service","limited-service","full-service","specialty"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Limited-service retailers (e.g., Walmart, many department stores) offer some services such as credit and returns. Self-service offers almost none and full-service offers many personalized services; 'specialty' describes merchandise line, not service.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-029","concept":"c11-depth-of-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peak Running sells only running shoes, apparel and accessories. It stocks dozens of brands in every size and width, charges regular prices and offers expert gait analysis. Classified by merchandise line, Peak Running is a:","options":["category killer","general merchandise store","specialty outlet","hypermarket"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A specialty outlet offers a deep assortment within a narrow line (Lululemon, Foot Locker). It is not a category killer because nothing suggests very low prices or big-box scale; category killers combine depth with discount pricing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-030","concept":"c11-depth-of-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"HomeBuild Depot operates enormous stores carrying a vast assortment of home-improvement products at very low prices, and many small local hardware stores close after it enters a market. HomeBuild Depot is a:","options":["specialty outlet","department store","power centre","category killer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A category killer is a specialty discount outlet that focuses on one category with deep assortment and very low prices (Home Depot, Best Buy, Staples), 'killing' smaller competitors. A power centre is a location type (a big strip of anchor stores), not the store format itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-031","concept":"c11-depth-of-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What BEST distinguishes a category killer from a typical specialty outlet?","options":["The category killer pairs a deep assortment in one category with very low prices","The category killer carries many unrelated product lines","The specialty outlet carries broad lines with little depth","The specialty outlet is always part of a contractual system"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both are deep-and-narrow (depth of line) formats; the category killer adds discount pricing and big-box scale. Carrying many unrelated lines is scrambled merchandising, and ownership (contractual system) is a different classification scheme.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-032","concept":"c11-depth-of-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Stores classified by depth of line, such as Lululemon and Foot Locker, offer:","options":["a large assortment within a narrow range of product lines","a wide range of product lines with limited assortment in each","several unrelated product lines under one roof","full grocery lines combined with general merchandise"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Depth = how many items are carried within a line, so depth-of-line stores go deep in a narrow range. A wide range of lines describes breadth (general merchandise stores); unrelated lines describe scrambled merchandising; grocery plus general merchandise describes a hypermarket.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-033","concept":"c11-depth-of-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which retailer is LEAST likely to be classified as a category killer?","options":["A big-box electronics chain with a huge selection at low prices","A department store carrying clothing, cosmetics, housewares and furniture","A big-box office-supply chain with a deep assortment at low prices","A home-improvement warehouse with a vast selection at low prices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Category killers focus on ONE category with deep assortment and low prices (Best Buy, Staples, Home Depot). A department store carries many different lines, so it is classified by breadth of line as a general merchandise store.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-034","concept":"c11-breadth-of-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hartwell's carries cosmetics, women's and men's clothing, housewares, luggage and furniture, each in its own department, with moderate selection in each. Classified by merchandise line, Hartwell's is a:","options":["specialty outlet","department store","category killer","warehouse club"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Carrying many different product lines (breadth) organized into departments makes Hartwell's a department store, the classic general merchandise (breadth-of-line) store. Specialty outlets and category killers are classified by depth: a narrow line with a deep assortment.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-035","concept":"c11-breadth-of-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northland Supercentre is a 200,000-square-foot store that sells a full line of groceries alongside clothing, electronics, toys and hardware under one roof. Northland is a:","options":["category killer","department store","power centre","hypermarket (supercentre)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A hypermarket or supercentre is a very large store combining general merchandise with a full grocery line (Walmart Supercentre, Real Canadian Superstore). A power centre is a location, a large strip of several big-box anchors, not a single store.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-036","concept":"c11-breadth-of-line","also":["c11-depth-of-line"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts the two ways of classifying stores by merchandise line?","options":["Breadth-of-line stores carry many different product lines; depth-of-line stores carry a deep assortment within few lines","Breadth-of-line stores carry a deep assortment within few lines; depth-of-line stores carry many different lines","Breadth refers to the level of service; depth refers to the form of ownership","Breadth refers to the number of stores; depth refers to store size"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Breadth = number of different lines (department stores, hypermarkets); depth = assortment within a line (specialty outlets, category killers). The reversed option is the classic trap, and service level and ownership are separate classification schemes.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-037","concept":"c11-breadth-of-line","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A very large store that combines a full grocery offering with general merchandise in one location is called a(n) _____.","options":["hypermarket (supercentre)","category killer","lifestyle centre","specialty outlet"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hypermarkets and supercentres combine groceries and general merchandise in one very large store. A lifestyle centre is an open-air upscale shopping location, not a store, and a category killer focuses on a single category.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-038","concept":"c11-breadth-of-line","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT classified by breadth of line?","options":["Department store","Specialty outlet","Hypermarket","General merchandise store"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Specialty outlets are classified by DEPTH: a deep assortment in a narrow line. Department stores, hypermarkets and other general merchandise stores are breadth-of-line formats that carry many different product lines.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-039","concept":"c11-scrambled-merchandising","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Corner Rx, a pharmacy chain, now sells milk, frozen dinners, greeting cards, phone chargers and seasonal patio furniture alongside prescriptions. This practice is called:","options":["intertype competition","category management","scrambled merchandising","assortment building"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Offering several unrelated product lines in a single store is scrambled merchandising (e.g., drugstores selling groceries, Canadian Tire selling food). Intertype competition is the RESULT, as the pharmacy now competes with supermarkets, not the assortment practice itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-040","concept":"c11-scrambled-merchandising","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"As a discount general-merchandise chain expands its grocery aisles, a regional supermarket chain finds it is now fighting the discounter for the same weekly grocery shoppers. The rivalry between these two very different types of retailers is called:","options":["intertype competition","scrambled merchandising","category management","the wheel of retailing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Competition between very dissimilar types of retail outlets is intertype competition (e.g., Walmart vs Loblaws in groceries). Scrambled merchandising is the discounter's strategy that CAUSED the rivalry, not the rivalry itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-041","concept":"c11-scrambled-merchandising","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does scrambled merchandising lead to intertype competition?","options":["It forces retailers to raise service levels and prices over time","Dissimilar types of outlets end up selling the same products to the same customers","It makes franchisees compete with corporate-owned stores","It lets manufacturers bypass retailers and sell direct"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When a drugstore adds groceries or a discounter adds food, very different outlet types now carry overlapping products, so they compete; that is intertype competition. Raising service and prices over time describes the wheel of retailing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-042","concept":"c11-scrambled-merchandising","also":["c11-breadth-of-line"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A mid-sized hardware store in Red Deer adds snack foods, pet food and phone chargers near its tills. A classmate says the store has 'become a hypermarket.' Why is the classmate wrong?","options":["It is intertype competition, which by definition occurs only between stores of the same type","It is a category killer, because it now covers more product categories at low prices","It is a specialty outlet, because hardware is still its main line of merchandise","It is scrambled merchandising; a hypermarket is a very large store combining groceries and general merchandise"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Scrambled merchandising is a strategy any store can use by adding unrelated lines. A hypermarket is a specific, very large store format built around groceries plus general merchandise. The intertype-competition option is wrong on its own terms, because intertype competition is between DISSIMILAR outlets.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-043","concept":"c11-scrambled-merchandising","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Intertype competition is competition between retail outlets of the same type, such as two rival supermarket chains.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Intertype competition is competition between VERY DISSIMILAR types of retail outlets, such as a drugstore and a supermarket both selling groceries. It is usually caused by scrambled merchandising.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-044","concept":"c11-retailer-decisions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the course, which retailer marketing decision comes FIRST?","options":["Choosing store locations","Setting prices and markups","Designing the store atmosphere","Selecting a target market"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The instructor flags selecting a target market as the FIRST item, followed by positioning. Location, pricing and atmosphere are all decisions that must be designed around the chosen customers, so they come later.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-045","concept":"c11-retailer-decisions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT on the course's list of retailer marketing decisions?","options":["Bulk breaking","Procurement","Store activities and experiences","Product assortment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bulk breaking is one of the nine WHOLESALER functions. The retailer decision list is target market, channels, product assortment, procurement, prices, services, store atmosphere, store activities and experiences, communications and location.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-046","concept":"c11-retailer-decisions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why must target market selection come before decisions about assortment, prices, atmosphere and location?","options":["Location must be fixed before a target market can be identified","Prices must be set first because they define the target market","Atmosphere decisions are made by wholesalers, not retailers","Every other retailing decision is designed to appeal to the chosen customers"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A retailer cannot choose the right assortment, price level, atmosphere or location until it knows WHO it is serving, which is why target market selection is the first item. The other options reverse the logic: location and price follow from the target market, not the other way around.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-047","concept":"c11-retailer-decisions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before opening, the owners of Kettle & Crumb, a café-bookshop, negotiate with publishers and coffee roasters about which titles and beans to buy, in what quantities and on what terms. Which retailer marketing decision does this reflect?","options":["Communications","Store atmosphere","Retail positioning","Procurement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Procurement is how the retailer sources and buys its merchandise: suppliers, quantities and terms. Retail positioning is how the store wants to be perceived, and communications is how it tells customers about its benefits.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-048","concept":"c11-retailer-decisions","also":["c11-retail-positioning"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A retail consultant tells a client: 'Before you touch assortment, prices or decor, decide exactly who you are serving and how you want them to see you compared with rivals.' Which decisions is the consultant telling the client to make first?","options":["Target market selection and retail positioning","Procurement and channels","Markups and markdowns","Store atmosphere and communications"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Choosing who to serve (target market) and how to be perceived relative to competitors (retail positioning) come first. Markups, atmosphere and communications are retailing-mix tools that are tailored afterward.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-049","concept":"c11-retail-positioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Raphael & Sons is a high-end men's clothing retailer. In times of recession, the retailer began to carry lower-quality, low-priced suits. This made Raphael & Sons lose a significant number of its loyal customers. This is an example of wrong:","options":["cross-channel shopping","location","shopper marketing","retail positioning"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Retail positioning is how a retailer wants to be perceived relative to competitors. Raphael & Sons' loyal customers expect high quality and calibre, so stocking cheap suits clashed with its high-end image. Shopper marketing is about understanding shopper behaviour across channels, which is not what went wrong here.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-050","concept":"c11-retail-positioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Canadian Tire features Canadian-made products and its long history in Canadian communities in its advertising, aiming to be seen as the store that 'buys Canadian.' This effort is best described as:","options":["shopper marketing","retail positioning","category management","scrambled merchandising"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shaping how the retailer is perceived relative to competitors, here as the Canadian choice, is retail positioning (the course's 'buy Canadian?' example). Shopper marketing studies how consumers behave as shoppers across channels and formats; it is not about the store's image.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-051","concept":"c11-retail-positioning","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Velour, an upscale Montreal boutique, is losing sales during an economic downturn. Based on the Raphael & Sons case, which response BEST protects its retail positioning?","options":["Add low-quality, low-priced lines to attract bargain hunters","Switch to self-service and cut prices deeply to match discounters","Move into a power centre next to category killers","Keep its merchandise quality consistent, even if loyal customers buy slightly less"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The official explanation notes that high-end customers may buy slightly less in a recession but are not looking for lower quality, so the retailer should stay consistent with its high-end position. Adding cheap lines is exactly the mistake that cost Raphael & Sons its loyal customers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-052","concept":"c11-retail-positioning","also":["c11-shopper-marketing"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes retail positioning from shopper marketing?","options":["Retail positioning studies how consumers behave as shoppers; shopper marketing is how a retailer wants to be perceived","Retail positioning is how a retailer wants to be perceived relative to competitors; shopper marketing studies how consumers behave as shoppers across channels and formats","Both refer to choosing between downtown and suburban locations","Retail positioning applies only to non-store retailers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Positioning is about the retailer's desired image versus rivals; shopper marketing is a multichannel discipline for understanding shopper behaviour from the moment a purchase is first considered. The reversed option swaps the two definitions, and neither concept is a location decision.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-053","concept":"c11-retail-positioning","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Just as marketers of packaged goods position their products, retailers position themselves to differentiate from competitors in the minds of consumers.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Retail positioning is how a retailer wants to be perceived relative to competitors; the official Raphael & Sons explanation makes this exact comparison to packaged-goods marketers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-054","concept":"c11-positioning-matrix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The retail positioning matrix positions retailers on which two dimensions?","options":["Breadth of product line and value added","Price and quality","Depth of line and level of service","Location and number of stores"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The matrix crosses breadth of product line (narrow vs broad) with value added (low vs high: location, product reliability, prestige, service). 'Price and quality' is the trap answer; price is not one of the axes.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-055","concept":"c11-positioning-matrix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Laurier & Co. is a department store that carries many product lines, features prestigious brands in elegant locations and is known for attentive service. On the retail positioning matrix, it sits in the:","options":["broad line, high value added quadrant","broad line, low value added quadrant","narrow line, high value added quadrant","narrow line, low value added quadrant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Many product lines = broad line; prestige, elegant locations and service = high value added, the quadrant of department stores like Holt Renfrew and Hudson's Bay. Narrow line/high value added would be a single-line luxury specialist such as Tiffany.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-056","concept":"c11-positioning-matrix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"ValuMax sells groceries, clothing, toys and hardware in no-frills stores with minimal service, competing mainly on low prices. On the retail positioning matrix, ValuMax sits in the:","options":["broad line, high value added quadrant","narrow line, high value added quadrant","broad line, low value added quadrant","narrow line, low value added quadrant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Many lines = broad; minimal service and no-frills stores = low value added, the Walmart/Costco quadrant. It is not narrow line/low value added, because ValuMax carries many different lines rather than one.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-057","concept":"c11-positioning-matrix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aurora Jewellers sells only fine jewellery from prestigious downtown locations, offering expert advice, lifetime cleaning and elegant presentation. On the retail positioning matrix, Aurora sits in the:","options":["narrow line, high value added quadrant","broad line, high value added quadrant","broad line, low value added quadrant","narrow line, low value added quadrant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"One product line (jewellery) = narrow; prestige, location and service = high value added, the Tiffany quadrant. Broad line/high value added is for department stores carrying many lines.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-058","concept":"c11-positioning-matrix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"ShoeSaver sells only discount footwear from plain stores in low-rent plazas, with self-service racks and no fitting help. On the retail positioning matrix, ShoeSaver sits in the:","options":["broad line, high value added quadrant","broad line, low value added quadrant","narrow line, low value added quadrant","narrow line, high value added quadrant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A single line (shoes) = narrow; plain stores, low-rent locations and no service = low value added, the Payless ShoeSource-style quadrant. Broad line/low value added (Walmart, Costco) requires many product lines.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-059","concept":"c11-positioning-matrix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A classmate says the retail positioning matrix compares retailers on price and quality. Which correction is accurate?","options":["The axes are depth of line and markup percentage","The axes are breadth of product line and value added","The axes are level of service and form of ownership","The axes are target market size and number of stores"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Breadth of product line and value added (location, product reliability, prestige and service) are the matrix dimensions; price is an outcome, not an axis. Service is part of value added, but form of ownership is a separate way of classifying outlets, not a matrix dimension.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-060","concept":"c11-positioning-matrix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Northern Lux, a narrow-line luxury handbag retailer with high value added, wants to move into the broad line, high value added quadrant. What should it do?","options":["Add many more product lines while keeping its prestige and service","Cut service and prices while continuing to sell only handbags","Cut service and prices while adding many product lines","Narrow its line further to a single handbag brand"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moving from narrow to broad means adding product lines; staying high value added means keeping prestige, location and service. Cutting service and prices while adding lines would land Northern Lux in the broad line, low value added quadrant instead.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-061","concept":"c11-positioning-matrix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an element of 'value added' on the retail positioning matrix?","options":["Breadth of product line","Location","Prestige","Product reliability"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Value added includes elements such as location, product reliability, prestige and service. Breadth of product line is the matrix's OTHER axis, not part of value added.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-062","concept":"c11-shopper-marketing","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Graham's Spice LLC makes use of _____, which includes use of traditional media, new media, direct marketing, loyalty, and trade promotion to understand when and where its customers first think about purchasing its spices.","options":["understanding consumers","retail positioning","shopper marketing","purchase matrix"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shopper marketing is a discipline for understanding how consumers behave as shoppers in different channels and formats; it reaches back to the time and place a consumer first thinks about buying and is by necessity multichannel. Retail positioning is how a retailer wants to be perceived, not a study of when and where shoppers start their purchase journey.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-063","concept":"c11-shopper-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Shopper marketing is best defined as:","options":["a discipline designed to understand how consumers behave as shoppers in different channels and formats","how a retailer wants to be perceived by consumers relative to competitors","assigning a manager responsibility for the profitability of a product category","selling brand-name merchandise below regular prices"],"answer":0,"explanation":"That is the course definition of shopper marketing. The distractors define retail positioning, category management and off-price retailing, three neighbouring retail concepts often confused with it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-064","concept":"c11-shopper-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is shopper marketing described as 'by necessity' a multichannel practice?","options":["It can only be used by retailers that also operate catalogues","It focuses solely on in-store displays and signage","It requires manufacturers to sell through several types of wholesalers","It extends beyond the store to wherever consumers first think about buying"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Because shopper marketing follows the shopper from the first thought of buying (on social media, in a flyer, at the shelf), it relies on traditional media, new media, direct marketing, loyalty, trade promotion and in-store tools. Saying it focuses solely on in-store displays contradicts its defining feature of extending outside the store.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-065","concept":"c11-shopper-marketing","also":["c11-category-management"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Brightwater Foods studies where customers are when they first think about buying granola (scrolling social media, reading a flyer, standing at the shelf) and coordinates digital ads, loyalty offers and in-store displays accordingly. A colleague calls this category management. Why is the colleague wrong?","options":["It is retail positioning, because it concerns how the brand is perceived","It is shopper marketing; category management gives one manager profit responsibility for a product category","It is the wheel of retailing, because the firm is adding services","It is intertype competition, because several channels are involved"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Studying shopper behaviour across channels from the first moment of purchase consideration is shopper marketing. Category management is an internal structure in which a manager runs a category (e.g., snacks) as a strategic business unit; it does not describe tracking shoppers across media.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-066","concept":"c11-shopper-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about shopper marketing is NOT accurate?","options":["It extends to the time and place a consumer first thinks about purchasing","It uses traditional media, new media, direct marketing, loyalty and trade promotion","It examines how consumers behave in different channels and formats","It is limited to activities that take place inside the retail store"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Shopper marketing deliberately extends WELL OUTSIDE the store, to the point where a consumer first thinks about buying. The other three statements come straight from the course definition.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-067","concept":"c11-retailing-mix","also":["c11-retail-pricing"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A retailer's year-end review shows $85,000 lost to shoplifting, damaged goods and paperwork errors. In the retailing mix figure, this issue falls in which quadrant?","options":["Products and services mix","Physical location mix","Communications mix","Retail pricing mix"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Shrinkage (theft, breakage, paperwork errors) is listed in the retail pricing mix alongside markups, markdowns and image/quality/value, because losses must be covered through pricing. It is not part of the products and services mix, even though the lost items are products.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-068","concept":"c11-retailing-mix","also":["c11-retail-location"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Urban Pantry is deciding whether to open 12 small-format stores or 4 large ones and how to route deliveries from its warehouse to each store. These decisions fall in which part of the retailing mix?","options":["Physical location mix","Retail pricing mix","Products and services mix","Communications mix"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Number of stores, store size, format and logistics are all elements of the physical location mix. The products and services mix covers assortment, service and guarantees, not how many stores to run or how goods reach them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-069","concept":"c11-retailing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"BrightNest Home introduces a 90-day no-questions exchange policy and debates dropping free in-home design consultations to keep costs down. These decisions belong to which retailing mix quadrant?","options":["Products and services mix","Retail pricing mix","Physical location mix","Communications mix"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Guarantees and exchanges and product-service trade-offs are listed under the products and services mix. The pricing mix is tempting because cost is mentioned, but the decisions themselves are about which services to offer, not markups or markdowns.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-070","concept":"c11-retailing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the chapter, what is the main purpose of a retailer's communications mix?","options":["To set markups, markdowns and allowances for shrinkage","To choose between strip locations and power centres","To communicate the retailer's unique benefits using integrated, multiple channels","To determine the breadth and depth of the merchandise mix"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The communications mix exists to communicate unique benefits, ideally through integrated, multiple channels (the Indigo example: stores, website, email, loyalty and social media working together). The other options belong to the pricing, location and products and services quadrants.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-071","concept":"c11-retailing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which element does NOT belong in the communications mix quadrant of the retailing mix?","options":["Markdowns","Displays and signage","Database marketing","Telephone sales and follow-up"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Markdowns belong in the retail pricing mix. The communications quadrant includes personal selling, advertising, displays and signage, public relations, catalogues, telephone sales and follow-up, website, direct marketing and database marketing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-072","concept":"c11-retailing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which element is correctly matched with its retailing mix quadrant?","options":["Catalogues: physical location mix","Logistics: communications mix","Sales functions and assistance: retail pricing mix","Image, quality and value: retail pricing mix"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The pricing quadrant lists image, quality and value along with markups, markdowns and shrinkage, because price signals image. Catalogues are in the communications mix, logistics in the physical location mix, and sales functions and assistance in the products and services mix.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-073","concept":"c11-retailing-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the course's retailing mix figure, what is at the centre of the four quadrants, and what surrounds the whole mix?","options":["The target market and consumers are at the centre, surrounded by information","Price is at the centre, surrounded by the other three quadrants","The wholesaler is at the centre, surrounded by suppliers","Store atmosphere is at the centre, surrounded by communications"],"answer":0,"explanation":"All four quadrants (products and services, pricing, physical location, communications) are arranged around the target market and consumers, and information surrounds the mix. Price is only one quadrant, not the hub.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-074","concept":"c11-merchandise-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A retailer's merchandise mix is best described as:","options":["how many different products a store carries, and in what assortment","how many different types of products a store carries, and how they are displayed","what assortment of products a store expects to stock annually","the products a store is considering purchasing for the coming quarter of the business cycle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The merchandise mix describes how many different types of products a store carries and in what assortment. How products are displayed is a store atmosphere idea, so the 'and how they are displayed' option is wrong; the annual-stocking and coming-quarter options describe purchase planning, not the merchandise mix.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-075","concept":"c11-merchandise-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"TrendHouse carries four product lines: shoes (60 items), appliances (25 items), CDs (300 titles) and men's clothing (90 items). What is the BREADTH of TrendHouse's merchandise mix?","options":["475 items","300 items","60 items","4 product lines"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Breadth = the number of different product lines carried = 4 (shoes, appliances, CDs, men's clothing). 475 is the total item count (60 + 25 + 300 + 90 = 475), and 300 is the depth of the CD line, not breadth.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-076","concept":"c11-merchandise-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Store X carries 3 product lines with about 500 items in each. Store Y carries 40 product lines with about 10 items in each. Which statement is correct?","options":["Store X has the deeper merchandise mix; Store Y has the broader one","Store X has the broader merchandise mix; Store Y has the deeper one","Both stores have the same breadth because their totals are similar","Store Y is deeper because it carries more product lines"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Breadth counts the number of product lines (Y = 40 vs X = 3), while depth counts items within each line (X = 500 vs Y = 10). So X is deeper and Y is broader; mixing these up is the most common trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-077","concept":"c11-merchandise-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tackle Box Outdoors adds 40 new fishing lures to its existing fishing line without adding any new product lines. Which aspect of its merchandise mix has changed?","options":["Breadth","Depth","Store atmosphere","Level of service"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adding items within an existing line increases depth, the number of items in each product line. Breadth would change only if Tackle Box added a new line (e.g., camping gear).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-078","concept":"c11-merchandise-mix","also":["c11-store-atmosphere"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"A retailer's merchandise mix does NOT describe which of the following?","options":["How products are displayed and lit in the store","The number of different product lines carried","The number of items within each product line","The assortment offered within its lines"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Display and lighting are store atmosphere decisions. The merchandise mix covers how many types of products (breadth) and in what assortment (depth) a store carries.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-079","concept":"c11-category-management","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Category management involves:","options":["a big-box retailer dominating one category with deep assortment and low prices","assigning a manager profit responsibility for a product category, run as a strategic business unit","classifying retail outlets by the breadth of lines they carry","understanding how consumers behave as shoppers across channels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Category management makes one manager accountable for a category's profitability (e.g., shoes) and runs it like a strategic business unit. A big-box store dominating one category at low prices is a category killer (a store format), and understanding how consumers behave as shoppers across channels is shopper marketing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-080","concept":"c11-category-management","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"At FreshCo Markets, one manager is responsible for the entire snack category: choosing brands, allocating shelf space, planning promotions and answering for the category's profit. This approach is called:","options":["category killer","shopper marketing","category management","scrambled merchandising"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Giving one manager profit responsibility for a whole product category and running it as a strategic business unit is category management. 'Category killer' sounds similar but is a type of discount specialty store, not an internal management approach.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-081","concept":"c11-category-management","also":["c11-depth-of-line"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes category management from a category killer?","options":["A category killer is how a retailer manages product categories internally; category management is a store format","Both are store formats classified by depth of line","Category management is an internal way to manage product categories; a category killer is a store format with deep, low-priced assortment in one category","Both are forms of retail ownership"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Category management is a management practice (one manager, one category, profit responsibility); a category killer (Best Buy, Staples) is a type of store. The reversed option swaps them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-082","concept":"c11-category-management","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is a key benefit of category management for a retailer?","options":["It aligns each category's assortment with the target market","It eliminates shrinkage losses from every product category","It removes the need to select a target market first","It turns the retailer into a merchant wholesaler for that category"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By making a manager accountable for a category's profitability, category management helps align the assortment with what the target market wants. It does not eliminate shrinkage or replace target market selection, which still comes first.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-083","concept":"c11-category-management","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Calgary grocer notices its baby-products aisle is cluttered with slow-selling brands, and no one can say whether the aisle makes money. Applying category management, the grocer should:","options":["open a separate big-box baby store with very low prices","add unrelated products such as hardware to the aisle","appoint one manager to run the baby category and answer for its profit","replace the aisle with self-service vending machines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Category management means one manager runs the category as a strategic business unit, owning its profitability and tuning its assortment to the target market. Opening a big-box discount baby store would be launching a category killer, a different concept entirely.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-084","concept":"c11-store-atmosphere","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cedar & Sage, a home decor store, plays soft acoustic music, uses warm lighting and diffuses a faint cedar scent to create a calm, upscale impression. Which element of the retailing mix is Cedar & Sage managing?","options":["Merchandise mix","Level of service","Shopper marketing","Store atmosphere"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Music, lighting, scent and layout are atmospherics that shape the overall impression of the store. The merchandise mix is about which product lines and assortments are carried, which this scenario does not change.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-085","concept":"c11-store-atmosphere","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Store atmosphere refers to:","options":["the overall impression a store conveys through elements such as layout, lighting, music and scent","the number of product lines a store carries and its assortment","the services a store offers before and after a purchase","a store's position on the breadth-of-line and value-added matrix"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Store atmosphere (atmospherics) is the overall impression created by sensory and design elements. The distractors describe the merchandise mix, prepurchase and postpurchase services, and the retail positioning matrix.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-086","concept":"c11-store-atmosphere","also":["c11-merchandise-mix"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A clothing retailer changes its layout, colour scheme and lighting but keeps exactly the same products and staffing. Which element of its products and services mix has changed?","options":["Store atmosphere","Merchandise mix","Level of service","Category management"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Layout, colour and lighting are atmospheric elements, so store atmosphere has changed. The merchandise mix (types and assortment of products) and level of service (staffing and services) were explicitly left unchanged.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-087","concept":"c11-store-atmosphere","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do retailers invest in store atmosphere?","options":["It creates an overall impression that supports the store's image","It reduces the number of product lines the store must carry","It replaces the need for a communications mix","It allows the store to take title to goods it never handles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Atmosphere provides the overall impression customers form of the store, reinforcing its retail image and positioning (calm and upscale vs fast and bargain-focused). It does not change the merchandise mix or substitute for communications.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-088","concept":"c11-store-atmosphere","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an atmospheric element of a store?","options":["Background music","Lighting","Markup percentage","Scent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Markup is a retail pricing decision. Atmospherics are sensory and design elements such as layout, music, lighting and scent that create the overall impression of a store.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-089","concept":"c11-retail-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ridgeview Sports cuts the price of last season's unsold snowboards by 40% to clear them out before spring. This price reduction is a:","options":["markdown","markup","shrinkage allowance","off-price retailing strategy"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A markdown is a reduction in the selling price to move slow or seasonal merchandise. Off-price retailing is a whole strategy of selling brand names below regular prices using manufacturers' excess inventory, not a one-time cut on a store's own stock.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-090","concept":"c11-retail-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A convenience store's year-end count shows $3,200 less merchandise than its records, due to shoplifting, broken items and recording errors. This loss is called:","options":["shrinkage","markdown","bulk breaking","risk bearing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Shrinkage is inventory lost to theft, breakage and paperwork errors, and it is part of the retail pricing mix. Risk bearing is a WHOLESALER function (absorbing ownership risk), not the retail term for these losses.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-091","concept":"c11-retail-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A retailer buys a jacket for $60 and sells it for $75. What is the markup as a percentage of the selling price?","options":["20%","25%","15%","80%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Markup on selling price = (price - cost) / price = ($75 - $60) / $75 = $15 / $75 = 20%. 25% is the markup on COST ($15 / $60), and 80% is cost as a share of price ($60 / $75).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-092","concept":"c11-retail-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Harbour Home marks down a coffee maker from $120 to $90. By what percentage of the original price was it reduced?","options":["33%","30%","75%","25%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Markdown = $120 - $90 = $30; $30 / $120 (original price) = 25%. 33% is the trap of dividing by the new price ($30 / $90), and 75% is the new price as a share of the original ($90 / $120).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-093","concept":"c11-retail-pricing","also":["c11-off-price-retailing"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How does a markdown differ from off-price retailing?","options":["A markdown sells brand names from manufacturers' excess inventory; off-price retailing reduces prices on slow stock","Both are forms of shrinkage caused by theft and breakage","A markdown reduces the price of a retailer's own slow-moving stock; off-price retailing sells brand names below regular prices by buying manufacturers' excess inventory","A markdown raises the selling price; off-price retailing lowers it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Markdowns are price cuts to move slow merchandise; off-price retailing (Winners, Costco, outlets) is an ongoing business model based on buying excess inventory. The reversed option swaps the two, and neither is shrinkage.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-094","concept":"c11-retail-pricing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT listed in the retail pricing mix?","options":["Markups","Shrinkage","Image, quality and value","Store layout"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The retail pricing mix lists image, quality and value; markups; markdowns; and shrinkage. Store layout belongs elsewhere: it is part of store atmosphere and of the size-and-layout decisions in the physical location mix.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-095","concept":"c11-off-price-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Off-price retailing is best described as:","options":["selling brand-name merchandise below regular prices, often via excess inventory","temporarily reducing the price of a store's own slow-moving stock","adding several unrelated product lines to a single store","selling only private-label goods at full price"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Off-price retailers (warehouse clubs, outlet stores, single-price/extreme-value stores, off-price chains) build their model on brand names below regular prices. A temporary price cut on slow stock is a markdown, not off-price retailing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-096","concept":"c11-off-price-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bargain Boulevard buys designer-brand clothing and home goods that manufacturers overproduced or other retailers cancelled, and sells them at 20-60% below department-store prices. Bargain Boulevard is a(n):","options":["warehouse club","single-price (extreme-value) store","category killer","off-price chain"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Off-price chains such as Winners buy brand-name excess inventory and sell it below regular prices. A warehouse club also sells for less, but it charges a membership fee and focuses on food and general merchandise in bulk.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-097","concept":"c11-off-price-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"BulkWay charges an annual membership fee and sells food and general merchandise in large package sizes from no-frills warehouse stores. BulkWay is a(n):","options":["off-price chain","outlet store","warehouse club","hypermarket"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Membership fees, bulk packages and no-frills warehouse stores mark a warehouse club (Costco). A hypermarket also combines groceries and general merchandise, but it has no membership model and is not an off-price format.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-098","concept":"c11-off-price-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Northern Parka operates its own stores in a factory outlet mall, selling its excess inventory and discontinued styles at reduced prices. These stores are:","options":["off-price chains","outlet stores","warehouse clubs","sales offices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Outlet stores let a manufacturer sell its OWN excess and discontinued stock at reduced prices. Off-price chains buy other manufacturers' excess inventory; a sales office is a manufacturer's wholesaling unit that sells to businesses, not consumers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-099","concept":"c11-off-price-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Loonie Land sells party supplies, kitchen gadgets and snacks at one or a few fixed low price points, with nothing over $5. Loonie Land is a(n):","options":["warehouse club","off-price chain","single-price (extreme-value) store","category killer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Single-price/extreme-value stores (Dollarama is the course example) sell at one or a few fixed low price points. An off-price chain sells brand-name apparel and home goods below regular prices but not at fixed price points.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-100","concept":"c11-off-price-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a type of off-price retailer?","options":["Warehouse club","Outlet store","Off-price chain","Full-service department store"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The four off-price types are warehouse clubs, outlet stores, single-price/extreme-value stores and off-price chains. A full-service department store sells at regular prices with many services.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-101","concept":"c11-off-price-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What BEST distinguishes a warehouse club from an off-price chain?","options":["A warehouse club is full-service; an off-price chain is self-service","An off-price chain is a contractual system; a warehouse club is a corporate chain","A warehouse club sells in bulk to fee-paying members; an off-price chain resells brand-name excess inventory","A warehouse club is a category killer; an off-price chain is a hypermarket"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both are off-price formats, but Costco-style clubs rely on memberships and bulk food and general merchandise, while Winners-style chains sell brand-name fashion and home goods from manufacturers' excess inventory. The other options mix in unrelated classification schemes (service level, ownership, merchandise line).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-102","concept":"c11-retail-location","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Stephen Avenue Books is located in downtown Calgary among office towers, banks and transit stops, where thousands of workers walk past daily. This location type is a:","options":["strip location","lifestyle centre","central business district","community shopping centre"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The central business district is the downtown area of a city, with heavy foot traffic from office workers. A strip location is a cluster of stores along a busy street serving a neighbourhood.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-103","concept":"c11-retail-location","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kingsgate Centre is a large mall of about 120 stores anchored by two department stores, drawing shoppers from across the city and surrounding towns. Kingsgate is a:","options":["community shopping centre","power centre","regional shopping centre","strip location"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Regional shopping centres have roughly 50-150 stores with department-store anchors and draw from a wide area. A community centre is smaller: one primary store plus 20-40 smaller stores.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-104","concept":"c11-retail-location","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maplewood Plaza has one primary store, a large supermarket, plus about 30 smaller shops serving several nearby neighbourhoods. Maplewood Plaza is a:","options":["regional shopping centre","lifestyle centre","power centre","community shopping centre"],"answer":3,"explanation":"One primary store plus 20-40 smaller stores defines a community shopping centre. A regional centre is larger (50-150 stores) and anchored by department stores.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-105","concept":"c11-retail-location","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A dry cleaner, a pizza place, a nail salon, a convenience store and a pharmacy sit side by side along a busy neighbourhood street, each with parking out front. This is a:","options":["community shopping centre","central business district","strip location","power centre"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A strip location is a cluster of stores along a busy street serving everyday neighbourhood needs. It is not a community shopping centre because there is no primary anchor store with 20-40 smaller stores.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-106","concept":"c11-retail-location","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"South Ridge Commons is a huge open-air strip of big-box stores anchored by several national retailers, including an electronics category killer, a home-improvement warehouse and a discount supercentre. South Ridge is a:","options":["regional shopping centre","lifestyle centre","power centre","strip location"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A power centre is a huge shopping strip with several national anchor stores, often category killers. A plain strip location has small neighbourhood stores rather than multiple national big-box anchors.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-107","concept":"c11-retail-location","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"The Promenade is an open-air centre with upscale boutiques, restaurants, landscaped walkways and a cinema, designed to feel like a pedestrian village. The Promenade is a:","options":["power centre","lifestyle centre","regional shopping centre","central business district"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lifestyle centres are open-air, upscale centres mixing specialty shops with dining and entertainment. A power centre is also open-air, but it is built around big-box national anchors and category killers, not upscale boutiques and leisure.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-108","concept":"c11-retail-location","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What distinguishes a power centre from a regional shopping centre?","options":["A power centre has one primary store and 20-40 smaller stores; a regional centre is a downtown business district","A power centre is a huge strip anchored by national big-box stores or category killers; a regional centre has 50-150 stores with department-store anchors","A power centre is an upscale open-air village; a regional centre is a small neighbourhood strip","Both are names for downtown shopping areas"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Anchors are the key: power centres cluster big-box national anchors and category killers in a giant strip, while regional centres have 50-150 stores anchored by department stores. One primary store plus 20-40 smaller ones describes a community centre.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-109","concept":"c11-retail-location","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a physical location decision in the retailing mix?","options":["Choosing the type of shopping area to locate in","Setting markdown percentages","Deciding how many stores to operate","Deciding whether to sell through non-store channels such as mail"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Markdowns belong to the retail pricing mix. Physical location covers where to locate, the number of stores, store size, format and logistics, and non-store options such as mail.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-110","concept":"c11-retail-location","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Lumen Home, an upscale decor boutique, targets affluent shoppers who like to browse, dine and linger. It wants to sit among similar upscale shops and restaurants in an attractive open-air setting. Which location type fits Lumen Home BEST?","options":["Power centre","Strip location","Community shopping centre","Lifestyle centre"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A lifestyle centre is an open-air, upscale centre mixing specialty shops with dining and entertainment, a match for Lumen's target market. A power centre is also open-air, but it is built around big-box national anchors and category killers that attract bargain-focused trips, not lingering affluent browsers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-111","concept":"c11-non-store-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement is TRUE of non-store retailing?","options":["Non-store retailing describes small, mobile retail activities like door-to-door sales and food carts","Non-store retailing occurs outside and may be impacted by weather or other environmental forces","Non-store retailing comprises activities of varying levels of customer and retailer involvement, outside the store environment","Non-store retailing refers specifically to buying goods and services via automated or electronic means"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Non-store retailing occurs outside a retail outlet through activities involving varying levels of customer and retailer involvement: automatic vending, direct mail and catalogues, TV home shopping, online retailing, telemarketing and direct selling. The 'automated or electronic' option is too narrow, because direct selling and telemarketing are non-store but involve people, and the 'small, mobile retail activities' option wrongly limits it to door-to-door sales and food carts; weather has nothing to do with the definition.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-112","concept":"c11-non-store-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Glow Beauty sells skin-care products through independent consultants who host parties in customers' homes and demonstrate the products. This form of non-store retailing is:","options":["telemarketing","television home shopping","direct selling","direct mail and catalogues"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Direct selling involves personal contact with consumers in their homes or offices, door-to-door or through in-home parties (Avon, Tupperware). Telemarketing also involves personal contact, but by phone, not face to face.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-113","concept":"c11-non-store-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Huckleberry Books, which has run stores across Canada for decades, launches a website and app so shoppers can order books for home delivery. Huckleberry has become a:","options":["category killer","wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chain","drop shipper","click-and-mortar retailer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A traditional bricks-and-mortar retailer that also engages in non-store retailing (here online) becomes a click-and-mortar retailer. A category killer is about deep, low-price assortment in one category, which the scenario does not mention.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-114","concept":"c11-non-store-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"SnackSmart places machines in Edmonton hospitals and campus buildings that sell healthy snacks and drinks 24 hours a day with no staff present. This form of retailing is:","options":["direct selling","automatic vending","television home shopping","telemarketing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Automatic vending is non-store retailing through machines that sell without salespeople. Direct selling requires personal, face-to-face contact with consumers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-115","concept":"c11-non-store-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a form of non-store retailing?","options":["Operating a store in a strip location","Television home shopping","Telemarketing","Automatic vending"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A strip location is a type of physical store location, so it is store-based retailing. Non-store forms are automatic vending, direct mail and catalogues, TV home shopping, online retailing, telemarketing and direct selling.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-116","concept":"c11-non-store-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which pairing of non-store retailing form and example is correct?","options":["Television home shopping: a channel showcasing products that viewers order by phone or online","Telemarketing: a consultant demonstrating cookware at a home party","Direct selling: a call centre phoning households to sell insurance","Direct mail and catalogues: a machine dispensing phone chargers in an airport"],"answer":0,"explanation":"TV home shopping displays products on television for viewers to order. The other pairings are swapped: a home party is direct selling, a call centre is telemarketing, and a machine is automatic vending.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-117","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fred uses the internet to research, compare, and decide on a new camera at his own pace. This demonstrates the online shopping benefit of:","options":["Limited control","Forced decision-making","Empowered consumers","Reliance on in-store salespeople"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Online shoppers are empowered consumers: they seek information, evaluate alternatives and decide on their own time, terms and conditions, which is the CONTROL benefit of the six Cs. 'Limited control' and 'reliance on in-store salespeople' describe the opposite of what Fred is doing.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-118","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dell Computers allows online shoppers to choose the components in their computer instead of buying one off the shelf. This exemplifies:","options":["communication benefits of online shopping","control benefits of online shopping","customization benefits of online shopping","cost benefits of online shopping"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Choosing your own components tailors the product to you, which is the customization benefit. Control is the tempting distractor, but control is about deciding at your own pace and on your own terms, not configuring the product itself.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-119","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Priya, a nurse working night shifts, orders groceries online at 3 a.m. from home because no store is open when she is free. Which of the six Cs does this illustrate?","options":["Control","Choice","Cost","Convenience"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Convenience means shopping 24/7 from anywhere, without store hours or travel. Control is about researching and deciding at your own pace and on your own terms; Priya's motive is timing and access, not decision power.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-120","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Marcus could not find size-15 hiking boots in any Calgary store, but online he found 40 styles from dozens of brands, plus a size-finder tool to help him narrow them down. Which of the six Cs does this illustrate?","options":["Customization","Communication","Choice","Convenience"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Choice is the broad selection (plus assistance) available online, far beyond any local store's shelves. Customization would mean the boots were configured or personalized for Marcus, not simply picked from a larger range.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-121","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Before buying a stroller online, Lena chats live with a company representative, emails a question about safety ratings and reads the brand's replies to her comments on Instagram. Which of the six Cs does this illustrate?","options":["Control","Communication","Cost","Choice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Communication covers two-way interaction online: email, live chat, reviews and social media. Control is tempting, but Lena's experience centres on exchanging messages with the company, not on deciding alone at her own pace.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-122","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ahmed uses a price-comparison site to find the lowest price on a gaming headset and saves $40 compared with the mall. Which of the six Cs does this illustrate?","options":["Cost","Choice","Control","Convenience"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cost refers to lower prices and the ease of comparing them online. Choice is about the breadth of selection, while Ahmed's goal is clearly to save money.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-123","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario BEST illustrates the CONTROL benefit of online shopping rather than CONVENIENCE?","options":["Jen orders a birthday gift at midnight because the website never closes","Tasha shops online from her cottage, hours from the nearest mall","Ben buys a desk lamp on his phone while riding the bus","Omar compares laptops across sites for two weeks and decides on his own terms"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Control = empowered consumers who search, evaluate and decide at their own pace and on their own terms. The other three are about WHEN and WHERE someone can shop, which is convenience.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-124","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which scenario illustrates CUSTOMIZATION rather than CHOICE?","options":["A shopper browses 3,000 sneaker styles from 50 brands","A shopper filters a huge catalogue by size to find in-stock styles","A shopper uses an online fit assistant to pick from hundreds of models","A shopper designs her own sneakers, choosing colours, materials and initials"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Customization tailors the product itself to the individual (like Dell letting buyers choose components). Browsing or filtering a large existing selection, even with help, is the choice benefit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-125","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six Cs that explain why consumers shop online?","options":["Choice","Community","Cost","Communication"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The six Cs are convenience, choice, communication, customization, cost and control. 'Community' sounds plausible, but it is not on the course list.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-126","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the chapter, how do online reviews affect consumers?","options":["They make consumers rely more heavily on in-store salespeople","They create smarter, more informed and more demanding consumers","They reduce the influence of other people's opinions","They make consumers less sensitive to price differences"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slides state that reviews create smarter, more informed and demanding consumers, and that influencers' opinions are important. Reviews increase, not reduce, the role of other people's opinions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-127","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the slides, online retailing allows consumers to do which three things?","options":["Browse, negotiate and return","Advertise, sell and deliver","Warehouse, transport and finance","Search, evaluate (compare) and purchase"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Online retailing supports search, evaluation (comparison) and purchase. 'Warehouse, transport and finance' are wholesaler functions, not consumer activities.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-128","concept":"c11-online-six-cs","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"FitWear, an online activewear retailer, finds that visitors leave because they are unsure whether products will suit them and have no one to ask. Which change MOST directly strengthens the COMMUNICATION benefit of shopping online?","options":["Cutting prices 15% below competitors","Adding live chat with fit specialists and customer reviews","Letting shoppers design their own custom leggings","Adding hundreds of new styles from other brands"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Communication covers two-way interaction (chat, email, reviews, social media), which directly answers 'no one to ask.' The other options strengthen cost, customization and choice, which do not address the communication gap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-129","concept":"c11-multichannel-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Jimmy's, a multichannel retailer, assists customers to order items that may be out of stock at one of its retail stores. The customers may use the catalogue to place the order. This provision BEST illustrates that Jimmy's:","options":["mainly has its retail outlets in the central business district","recognizes that the Internet is an effective relationship-building medium among different channel operations","benefits from the synergy of sharing information among its different channel operations","typically reduces the size of its target markets"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Multichannel retailers integrate store and non-store formats (catalogue, online); because the channels share information, a store can fill an out-of-stock order through the catalogue, which is the synergy of sharing information. The Internet option is tempting, but the scenario involves a catalogue and is about channels working together, not the Internet as a relationship medium.","source":"official"},{"id":"q11-130","concept":"c11-multichannel-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kai tries on ski boots at a Banff ski shop and gets fitted by the staff, then orders the same boots online from a cheaper seller. This behaviour is called:","options":["showrooming","webrooming","scrambled merchandising","telemarketing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Showrooming = examining products in a store, then buying online. Webrooming is the reverse: researching online and then buying in a physical store.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-131","concept":"c11-multichannel-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Danielle reads reviews and compares blender prices online, then drives to a nearby store and buys the model she chose that afternoon. This behaviour is called:","options":["showrooming","automatic vending","retail positioning","webrooming"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Webrooming = researching online, then buying in store; Danielle is a cross-channel shopper. Showrooming runs in the opposite direction (look in store, buy online).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-132","concept":"c11-multichannel-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes showrooming from webrooming?","options":["Showrooming means researching online and buying in a store; webrooming means examining in a store and buying online","Showrooming is done by retailers; webrooming is done by manufacturers","Showrooming means examining a product in a store and buying it online; webrooming means researching online and buying in a store","Both describe consumers who use only a single channel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both are cross-channel shopping behaviours: showrooming uses the store as a showroom for an online purchase; webrooming uses the web to research an in-store purchase. They are consumer behaviours, and by definition involve more than one channel.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-133","concept":"c11-multichannel-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Retailers that use and integrate a combination of traditional store and non-store formats, such as stores, catalogues and online retailing, are called:","options":["multichannel retailers","category killers","independent retailers","drop shippers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Multichannel retailers integrate store and non-store formats to offer a broader spectrum of benefits and experiences. A drop shipper is a limited-service wholesaler, and a category killer is a deep, low-price store format.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-134","concept":"c11-multichannel-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do many retailers adopt a multichannel strategy?","options":["Multiple channels allow them to shrink the size of their target markets","Integrated channels offer broader benefits and synergy from shared information","Multiple channels eliminate the need to operate any physical stores","Multiple channels prevent consumers from shopping across channels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Using several integrated channels lets retailers offer a broader spectrum of benefits and experiences and gain synergy from sharing information among channel operations (as in the Jimmy's example). Shrinking the target market is the distractor from the official question; multichannel retailing typically widens reach.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-135","concept":"c11-modern-retail-environment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Despite fast home delivery, Olivia drives to a store for a new phone charger because she wants to walk out with it right now rather than wait two days. Which reason that face-to-face (F2F) retail is 'not dead yet' does this illustrate?","options":["Savings","Experience","Decline of middle-market retailers","Instant gratification"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The slides list instant gratification, convenience, experience and savings as reasons F2F retail survives; wanting the item immediately is instant gratification. Nothing in the scenario involves saving money or an in-store experience.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-136","concept":"c11-modern-retail-environment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the 2025 Retail Council of Canada / Adyen survey discussed in class, roughly what share of online shoppers reach checkout and then leave to compare shipping costs and prices on other sites?","options":["About 9%","About one-quarter","Nearly two-thirds","Over 95%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nearly two-thirds of online shoppers make it to checkout and then leave to compare shipping costs and prices, a serious cart-abandonment threat. The 9% figure is the share who use a digital wallet.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-137","concept":"c11-modern-retail-environment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a finding or recommendation from the retail trends survey (Retail Council of Canada / Adyen) discussed in class?","options":["Most Canadian shoppers now pay with digital wallets rather than cards","Nearly two-thirds of online shoppers leave at checkout to compare shipping costs and prices","Only 9% use a digital wallet; most use debit or credit cards","Experiential tools such as Instagram storefronts, QR codes and GPS can improve online retailing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Only 9% use a digital wallet and most pay by debit or credit card, so the claim that most use digital wallets is false. The other three statements match the survey slide.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-138","concept":"c11-modern-retail-environment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Pine & Ply, an online furniture retailer, sees many shoppers reach checkout and then leave after seeing shipping costs. Based on the retail trends discussed in class, what threat is this, and what is a sensible response?","options":["Showrooming; open more physical stores in power centres","Shrinkage; add security tags to inventory","Cart abandonment; show competitive, transparent shipping costs early","Intertype competition; add unrelated product lines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The survey found nearly two-thirds of online shoppers reach checkout, then leave to compare shipping costs and prices, so competitive, transparent shipping directly addresses the threat. Shrinkage is loss from theft, breakage and errors, which has nothing to do with abandoned carts.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-139","concept":"c11-modern-retail-environment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Marco leaves a store without buying because the checkout line was 20 minutes long and the jacket he wanted was out of stock. Which point about the modern retail environment does this illustrate?","options":["Consumers dislike long lines and stock-outs","The decline of middle-market retailers","The growth of social media purchasing","Retail optimization through technology"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The slides note that consumers dislike long lines, poor customer service and stock-outs, exactly what drove Marco away. The decline of middle-market retailers describes a market-wide squeeze on mid-priced chains, not one shopper's bad visit.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-140","concept":"c11-modern-retail-environment","also":["c11-wheel-of-retailing"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Mid-priced chains that are neither the cheapest option nor a premium experience are closing stores, while discounters and luxury retailers grow. Which modern retail trend does this describe?","options":["The wheel of retailing","Decline of middle-market retailers","Scrambled merchandising","Showrooming"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slides list the decline of middle-market retailers as a feature of today's environment, as shoppers gravitate to either low prices or high-value experiences. The wheel of retailing describes how a single low-price entrant trades up over time, not a squeeze on the middle of the market.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-141","concept":"c11-modern-retail-environment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the chapter recommend experiential or interactive online features such as Instagram storefronts, QR codes and GPS tools?","options":["To improve the online retailing experience and engage shoppers","To reduce the retailer's breadth of product line","To eliminate the need for debit and credit cards","To avoid holding inventory, as drop shippers do"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The survey slide suggests improving online retailing through experiential or interactive activities such as online social shopping (Instagram storefronts), QR codes and GPS. These tools engage shoppers; they do not change the product line or replace card payments (most shoppers still use debit or credit).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-142","concept":"c11-modern-retail-environment","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A small Edmonton boutique uses Shopify to sync inventory across its store, website and Instagram shop, so a customer can buy a sweater from an Instagram post and pick it up in store. Which modern retail theme does this BEST illustrate?","options":["Off-price retailing","The wheel of retailing","Retail optimization through technology","Scrambled merchandising"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The slides highlight omnichannel and social media purchasing, the role of technology and retail optimization (Shopify is the course example). Nothing here involves selling brand names below regular prices (off-price) or adding unrelated lines (scrambled merchandising).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-143","concept":"c11-wheel-of-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The wheel of retailing describes how:","options":["retail outlets pass through early growth, accelerated development, maturity and decline","very dissimilar types of retail outlets compete for the same customers","new retail forms enter at low prices, trade up over time and leave room for new low-price entrants","retailers gradually move from narrow product lines to broad, general merchandise lines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The wheel of retailing describes new forms entering as low-status, low-margin, low-price outlets, then adding services and raising prices until new discounters fill the gap below them. Moving through early growth, accelerated development, maturity and decline is the retail life cycle (the most commonly confused concept), and dissimilar outlets competing for the same customers is intertype competition.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-144","concept":"c11-wheel-of-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"QuickBurger began as a cheap walk-up burger stand. Over 30 years it added seating, drive-thrus, salads, premium coffee and renovated dining rooms, and its prices rose. Now no-frills food trucks undercut it on price. This pattern illustrates:","options":["the retail life cycle","scrambled merchandising","intertype competition","the wheel of retailing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Entering at low prices, trading up with services and higher prices, then being undercut by new low-price entrants is the wheel of retailing (the course's McDonald's example). The retail life cycle tracks the growth and decline of a format, not this trade-up-and-replace pattern.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-145","concept":"c11-wheel-of-retailing","also":["c11-retail-life-cycle"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which observation fits the wheel of retailing rather than the retail life cycle?","options":["A retail format's market share rises quickly, levels off and then falls","A format moves from early growth into accelerated development","Video-rental stores grow, mature and eventually close as streaming arrives","A discount chain adds services and raises prices, opening a gap for new discounters"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The wheel is about trading UP in price, status and service, which creates room for new low-price entrants. Growth, levelling off and decline over time are stages of the retail life cycle.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-146","concept":"c11-wheel-of-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the wheel of retailing, what happens immediately after a new retailer enters the market as a low-status, low-margin, low-price outlet?","options":["It gradually adds services, raising its prices and status","New low-price retailers enter beneath it","It cuts services further to become a hard discounter","It enters the decline stage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The sequence is: enter at low prices, then trade up (add services, raise prices and status), which leaves a gap that new low-price entrants fill. New entrants come only AFTER the incumbent has traded up.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-147","concept":"c11-wheel-of-retailing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the wheel of retailing, why do opportunities keep opening up for new discount retailers?","options":["Established retailers trade up, leaving a low-price gap","Consumers eventually refuse to shop at full-service stores","Category killers are forced out by regulation","Markups shrink to zero as retailers mature"],"answer":0,"explanation":"As incumbents add services and raise prices and status, they abandon the low-price position, and new low-margin entrants step into that gap. The other options are not part of the wheel concept.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-148","concept":"c11-retail-life-cycle","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The retail life cycle refers to:","options":["new retail forms entering as low-price outlets and gradually trading up","the process of growth and decline that retail outlets, like products, experience","the steps a shopper follows from search to purchase","the sequence of functions a wholesaler performs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The retail life cycle applies life-cycle thinking to retail outlets: they grow and then decline. Entering at low prices and trading up describes the wheel of retailing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-149","concept":"c11-retail-life-cycle","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the correct sequence of the retail life cycle stages?","options":["Introduction, growth, maturity, decline","Early growth, accelerated development, maturity, decline","Accelerated development, early growth, maturity, decline","Early growth, maturity, accelerated development, decline"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The retail life cycle runs early growth, accelerated development, maturity, then decline. 'Introduction, growth, maturity, decline' is the PRODUCT life cycle, a common trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-150","concept":"c11-retail-life-cycle","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Video-rental chains spread rapidly in the 1980s and 1990s, peaked, then saw market share and profits collapse as streaming arrived, and most stores closed. This pattern best illustrates:","options":["the wheel of retailing","scrambled merchandising","intertype competition","the retail life cycle"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A retail format growing, maturing and declining over time is the retail life cycle. Nothing here involves trading up in price and service, which is the wheel of retailing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-151","concept":"c11-retail-life-cycle","also":["c11-wheel-of-retailing"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts the retail life cycle with the wheel of retailing?","options":["The retail life cycle explains how low-price entrants trade up; the wheel of retailing tracks a format's growth and decline","The retail life cycle tracks a format's growth and decline over time; the wheel of retailing explains how low-price entrants trade up and get replaced","Both describe how individual consumers move from search to purchase","Both apply only to wholesalers, not retailers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Life cycle = stages of growth and decline (early growth, accelerated development, maturity, decline). Wheel = the price and status trade-up dynamic that leaves room for new discounters. The reversed option swaps them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-152","concept":"c11-retail-life-cycle","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a stage of the retail life cycle?","options":["Early growth","Accelerated development","Maturity","Introduction"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The retail life cycle stages are early growth, accelerated development, maturity and decline. 'Introduction' is the first stage of the product life cycle, not the retail life cycle.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-153","concept":"c11-wholesaling","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"_____ includes all the activities in selling goods or services to those who buy for resale or business use.","options":["Wholesaling","Retailing","Shopper marketing","Non-store retailing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wholesaling is defined by the buyer's purpose: resale or business use. Retailing sells to ultimate consumers for personal, family or household use.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-154","concept":"c11-wholesaling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Canuck Office Supply sells pallets of copy paper, toner and office furniture to schools, hospitals and law firms for use in their operations. Canuck Office Supply is engaged in:","options":["wholesaling","retailing","direct selling","telemarketing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Selling to organizations that buy for business use is wholesaling, even though the schools and hospitals are the final users. Retailing requires ultimate consumers buying for personal, family or household use.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-155","concept":"c11-wholesaling","also":["c11-retailing"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which purchase is wholesaling rather than retailing?","options":["A parent buying latex gloves for household cleaning","A dentist buying boxes of latex gloves for her clinic","A student buying winter gloves for a ski trip","A retiree renting a snowblower to clear his driveway"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The dentist buys for business use, which makes the sale wholesaling. The other buyers are ultimate consumers buying or renting for personal or household use, which is retailing even when the item is the same (gloves).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-156","concept":"c11-wholesaling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three main categories of wholesalers in the course figure?","options":["Merchant wholesalers","Agents and brokers","Manufacturer's branches and offices","Retailer-sponsored cooperatives"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The figure's three categories are merchant wholesalers, agents and brokers, and manufacturer's branches and offices. Retailer-sponsored cooperatives are a type of contractual retail system (e.g., Home Hardware).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-157","concept":"c11-wholesaling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"What ultimately determines whether a sale is classified as wholesaling or retailing?","options":["The size of the order being placed","The buyer's purpose for making the purchase","Whether the seller operates a physical store","Whether the price is above or below the regular price"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The definitions hinge on why the buyer purchases: resale or business use = wholesaling; personal, family or household use = retailing. A large order by a family is still retailing, and many retailers sell without stores.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-158","concept":"c11-merchant-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Merchant wholesalers are independently owned firms that:","options":["never take title and earn commissions on sales","take title to the merchandise they handle","are owned and operated by the manufacturer","only bring buyers and sellers together for single transactions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Taking title (ownership) is the defining feature of merchant wholesalers. Agents and brokers do not take title and earn commissions; manufacturer's branches and offices are owned by the manufacturer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-159","concept":"c11-merchant-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Western Supply Co. carries a broad range of products (hardware, cleaning supplies, small appliances and toys) with only a few items in each line. It buys and owns the goods and offers credit, delivery and sales support to small retailers. Western Supply is a:","options":["specialty merchandise wholesaler","rack jobber","selling agent","general merchandise wholesaler"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A full-service merchant wholesaler carrying a broad range with little depth is a general merchandise wholesaler. A specialty merchandise wholesaler does the opposite: a narrow range with a deep assortment.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-160","concept":"c11-merchant-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Pacific Seafood Distributors carries only seafood but offers an extremely deep assortment of fresh, frozen and specialty fish. It owns its stock and provides credit, delivery and merchandising advice to restaurants and grocers. It is a:","options":["general merchandise wholesaler","truck jobber","broker","specialty merchandise wholesaler"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A narrow range with a deep assortment, plus full services, marks a specialty merchandise wholesaler. A truck jobber also sells perishables, but it is a limited-service wholesaler selling for cash from a truck, not offering credit and advice.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-161","concept":"c11-merchant-wholesalers","also":["c11-agents-brokers"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A merchant wholesaler and a manufacturers' agent both sell a producer's goods to retailers. What is the key difference?","options":["The agent takes title to the goods; the merchant wholesaler earns a commission","The merchant wholesaler is owned by the manufacturer; the agent is independent","The merchant wholesaler takes title to the goods; the agent does not and earns a commission","The agent always holds inventory in a branch office"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Title is the dividing line: merchant wholesalers buy and own the goods, while agents and brokers sell on commission without taking title. Ownership by the manufacturer describes branches and offices, not merchant wholesalers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-162","concept":"c11-merchant-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Because merchant wholesalers take title to the goods they handle, they:","options":["earn only a commission on each sale","bear the risks of ownership, such as theft, damage, spoilage and obsolescence","cannot resell the goods to retailers","are considered vertical integration by the manufacturer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Owning the goods means owning the risk, which is why risk bearing is a wholesaler function. Earning only commissions is what agents and brokers do, precisely because they do NOT take title.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-163","concept":"c11-merchant-wholesalers","also":["c11-limited-service-wholesalers"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What distinguishes full-service merchant wholesalers from limited-service merchant wholesalers?","options":["Full-service wholesalers do not take title, while limited-service wholesalers do","Limited-service wholesalers are owned by manufacturers","Full-service wholesalers sell only to ultimate consumers","Full-service wholesalers provide a wide range of services, while limited-service wholesalers offer only a few"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both groups are merchant wholesalers and both take title; they differ in how many services they perform. General and specialty merchandise wholesalers are full-service, while rack jobbers, cash and carry wholesalers, drop shippers and truck jobbers are limited-service.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-164","concept":"c11-limited-service-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Glossy Racks Inc. installs and stocks magazine and greeting-card displays in pharmacies. It owns the merchandise on the racks and bills each store only for the items that sell. Glossy Racks is a:","options":["truck jobber","drop shipper","rack jobber","broker"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rack jobbers furnish and stock display racks in stores, own the inventory and bill only for items sold. A truck jobber sells perishables for cash from a truck rather than maintaining racks.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-165","concept":"c11-limited-service-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Restaurant owners visit Metro Depot's warehouse, pay for supplies on the spot and haul them away in their own vans; Metro offers no credit and no delivery. Metro Depot is a:","options":["truck jobber","general merchandise wholesaler","cash and carry wholesaler","rack jobber"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Customers paying cash and furnishing their own transportation, with no credit or delivery, defines cash and carry wholesaling. A general merchandise wholesaler is full-service, offering credit and delivery.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-166","concept":"c11-limited-service-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"TimberLink takes orders from builders for carloads of lumber and has the sawmill ship directly to each job site. TimberLink owns the lumber while it is in transit but never stores or handles it. TimberLink is a:","options":["drop shipper","broker","rack jobber","cash and carry wholesaler"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Drop shippers (desk jobbers) take title but never physically handle, store or deliver the goods; the producer ships directly to the customer, typical for bulky goods like lumber. A broker also never handles goods, but a broker does not take title.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-167","concept":"c11-limited-service-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Fresh Daily Dairy runs a small warehouse and sends trucks along regular routes each morning, selling milk and yogurt to convenience stores for cash right off the truck. Fresh Daily is a:","options":["truck jobber","rack jobber","drop shipper","specialty merchandise wholesaler"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Truck jobbers are small wholesalers with a small warehouse that sell perishables (bakery, dairy, meat) for cash from their trucks. A rack jobber stocks and owns in-store displays and bills only for items sold.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-168","concept":"c11-limited-service-wholesalers","also":["c11-agents-brokers"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Both a drop shipper and a broker can arrange a sale without ever physically handling the goods. What distinguishes them?","options":["The drop shipper takes title to the goods; the broker does not","The broker takes title to the goods; the drop shipper does not","The drop shipper is owned by the manufacturer; the broker is independent","The broker always stores inventory in a warehouse"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A drop shipper is a merchant wholesaler, so it owns the goods even though the producer ships them directly. A broker is an agent intermediary: it brings buyers and sellers together for a commission without taking title.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-169","concept":"c11-limited-service-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a limited-service merchant wholesaler?","options":["Cash and carry wholesaler","Truck jobber","Rack jobber","Selling agent"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Selling agents are agents (no title) that handle a single producer's entire marketing function. The four limited-service merchant wholesalers are rack jobbers, cash and carry wholesalers, drop shippers and truck jobbers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-170","concept":"c11-limited-service-wholesalers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A rack jobber is best defined as a wholesaler that:","options":["owns goods but ships them directly from producer to customer without handling them","furnishes and stocks racks in stores, owns the merchandise displayed and bills only for items sold","sells perishable goods for cash directly from a truck","requires customers to pay cash and provide their own transportation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is the rack jobber definition (common for hosiery and magazines). The distractors define the drop shipper, truck jobber and cash and carry wholesaler, the other three limited-service types.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-171","concept":"c11-agents-brokers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ana represents four non-competing producers (a cutlery maker, a cookware maker, a linen maker and a candle maker), selling their lines to kitchen stores in an exclusive Alberta territory for a commission. Ana is a:","options":["selling agent","broker","general merchandise wholesaler","manufacturers' agent"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Manufacturers' agents work for several producers, carrying non-competitive, complementary lines in an exclusive territory, and do not take title. A selling agent represents only ONE producer and handles its entire marketing function.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-172","concept":"c11-agents-brokers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Ridge, a small winery, contracts with Vino Sales Group to handle its entire marketing function (pricing, promotion and selling in all markets) for a commission. Vino Sales Group is a:","options":["manufacturers' agent","selling agent","broker","sales office"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A selling agent represents a single producer and is responsible for that producer's entire marketing function. A sales office is owned by the manufacturer itself, not an independent agent.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-173","concept":"c11-agents-brokers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hana connects a potato farm with a food processor for a one-time sale of surplus potatoes and earns a commission. She has no ongoing relationship with either party and never owns the potatoes. Hana is acting as a:","options":["broker","manufacturers' agent","drop shipper","truck jobber"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Brokers bring buyers and sellers together to make a sale, usually with no continuous relationship (common in real estate, food and insurance). A drop shipper would take title to the potatoes, which Hana does not.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-174","concept":"c11-agents-brokers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a manufacturers' agent from a selling agent?","options":["A selling agent sells non-competing lines for several producers in an exclusive territory; a manufacturers' agent handles the entire marketing function for one producer","A manufacturers' agent takes title; a selling agent does not","A manufacturers' agent sells non-competing lines for several producers in an exclusive territory; a selling agent handles the entire marketing function for one producer","A selling agent is owned by the manufacturer; a manufacturers' agent is independent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The key differences are how many producers they represent and how much of the marketing they handle. Neither takes title (both are agents earning commissions), and neither is owned by the manufacturer.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-175","concept":"c11-agents-brokers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Agents and brokers differ from merchant wholesalers because they:","options":["take title to the goods and bear ownership risk","do not take title to the goods and typically earn commissions","are owned and operated by the manufacturer","sell mainly to ultimate consumers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Agents and brokers facilitate sales without owning the goods, earning commissions instead of resale margins. Taking title and bearing risk is what merchant wholesalers do.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-176","concept":"c11-agents-brokers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about brokers is NOT accurate?","options":["Their main function is bringing buyers and sellers together","They usually have no continuous relationship with their clients","They are common in real estate, food and insurance","They take title to the goods they sell"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Brokers never take title; they are agent intermediaries paid commissions. The other statements match the course description of brokers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-177","concept":"c11-manufacturer-branches","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Manufacturer's branches and offices are wholesaling operations that are:","options":["independently owned and take title to goods","independent agents that earn commissions","owned jointly by independent retailers","owned and operated by the manufacturer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Manufacturer's branches and offices are a form of vertical integration in wholesaling: the producer owns them. Independent firms that take title are merchant wholesalers; independent commission earners are agents and brokers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-178","concept":"c11-manufacturer-branches","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Polar Appliances, a manufacturer, owns a regional facility in Winnipeg that holds inventory and sells and delivers appliances to retailers across the Prairies. This facility is a:","options":["sales office","selling agent","drop shipper","branch office"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A manufacturer-owned unit that carries inventory and performs wholesaling functions is a branch office. A sales office does not carry inventory; it only performs a sales function.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-179","concept":"c11-manufacturer-branches","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"TechCore Chips, a manufacturer, opens a small downtown Toronto office where its salespeople meet business customers and take orders. All products ship from the factory; the office holds no inventory. This is a:","options":["branch office","broker","sales office","rack jobber"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A manufacturer-owned sales office carries no inventory and performs only a sales function. A branch office would also store inventory and perform other wholesaling functions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-180","concept":"c11-manufacturer-branches","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What is the key difference between a manufacturer's branch office and its sales office?","options":["A sales office carries inventory; a branch office does not","A branch office is independently owned; a sales office is owned by the manufacturer","A sales office takes title to goods; a branch office earns commissions","A branch office carries inventory; a sales office does not and performs only a sales function"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both are owned by the manufacturer; inventory is the difference. Branch = inventory plus wholesaling functions; sales office = no inventory, selling only.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-181","concept":"c11-manufacturer-branches","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why might a manufacturer set up its own branch offices instead of relying on merchant wholesalers?","options":["To avoid ever holding inventory","To let independent agents take title to its goods","To control wholesaling directly through ownership","To form a retailer-sponsored cooperative"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Manufacturer's branches and offices represent vertical integration in wholesaling: the producer owns and controls the wholesale operation. Avoiding inventory describes a sales office, not the reason to open a branch office, which carries inventory.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-182","concept":"c11-wholesaler-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Grocery Wholesale buys pasta by the truckload from manufacturers and resells it to small independent grocers in cases of 12. Which wholesaler function is this?","options":["Bulk breaking","Buying and assortment building","Warehousing","Risk bearing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bulk breaking means buying in large quantities and dividing them into smaller lots that small customers can use. Assortment building is about bringing together a VARIETY of products, not splitting one product into smaller quantities.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-183","concept":"c11-wholesaler-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A wholesaler lets small retailers pay for their orders 60 days after delivery. Which wholesaler function is this?","options":["Risk bearing","Financing","Market information","Transportation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Extending credit to customers is the financing function. Risk bearing is absorbing the risks of owning goods (theft, damage, spoilage, obsolescence), not giving customers time to pay.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-184","concept":"c11-wholesaler-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A heat wave spoils a wholesaler's stock of chocolate before it can be resold, and the wholesaler absorbs the loss because it owns the goods. This reflects which wholesaler function?","options":["Financing","Risk bearing","Warehousing","Bulk breaking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By taking title, wholesalers absorb risks such as spoilage, theft, damage and obsolescence; that is the risk-bearing function. Warehousing (storing goods) is where the loss happened, but absorbing the financial loss is risk bearing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-185","concept":"c11-wholesaler-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A wholesaler helps an independent hardware store redesign its layout, train its clerks and set up an inventory-control system. Which wholesaler function is this?","options":["Market information","Management services and counselling","Selling and promoting","Buying and assortment building"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Helping retailers improve operations (training, layout, accounting or inventory systems) is management services and counselling. Market information is passing along facts about competitors, new products and prices, not hands-on operational help.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-186","concept":"c11-wholesaler-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A wholesaler tells its suppliers which competing products are gaining popularity with retailers and how prices are trending. Which wholesaler function is this?","options":["Management services and counselling","Selling and promoting","Risk bearing","Market information"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Sharing information about competitors, new products and price developments is the market information function. Management services and counselling involves helping customers run their businesses, not reporting market trends.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-187","concept":"c11-wholesaler-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the nine wholesaler functions?","options":["Bulk breaking","Financing","Market information","Category management"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Category management is a retailer practice (managing a product category as a strategic business unit). The nine wholesaler functions are selling and promoting, buying and assortment building, bulk breaking, warehousing, transportation, financing, risk bearing, market information, and management services and counselling.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-188","concept":"c11-wholesaler-functions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes bulk breaking from buying and assortment building?","options":["Bulk breaking divides large quantities into smaller lots; assortment building brings together a variety of products customers need","Bulk breaking brings together a variety of products; assortment building divides large quantities into smaller lots","Both refer to storing goods until customers need them","Both refer to extending credit to customers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bulk breaking is about QUANTITY (a truckload into cases); assortment building is about VARIETY (gathering many items so customers can buy from one source). Storing goods is warehousing and extending credit is financing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q11-189","concept":"c11-wholesaler-functions","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Warehousing, transportation, financing and risk bearing are all among the nine wholesaler functions.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The nine functions are selling and promoting; buying and assortment building; bulk breaking; warehousing; transportation; financing; risk bearing; market information; and management services and counselling.","source":"generated"}],"color":"#D28C3C","short":"Retail","emoji":"🏪","tag":"Retailing and wholesaling","province":"Asia","numeral":"XI"},{"chapter":12,"title":"Integrated Marketing Communications: The Mix, Trends, and Industry","topics":[{"id":"t12-1","order":1,"title":"How communication works","summary":"Before any marketing message can work, it has to travel from a sender to a receiver. This topic covers the parts of the communication process (source, encoding, message, channel, receiver, decoding, feedback, noise, field of experience) and the four barriers that stop marketing messages from getting through.","concepts":["c12-communication-process","c12-feedback-noise-experience","c12-barriers"]},{"id":"t12-2","order":2,"title":"Marketing communications, IMC, and the promotional mix","summary":"Marketing communications inform, persuade, and remind. IMC is the idea that every tool (ads, personal selling, sales promotion, PR, direct marketing, sponsorship, social media, email, customer service) must deliver one consistent, measured message. Here you learn each tool, what it is good and bad at, and whether it is mass or customized.","concepts":["c12-marketing-communications","c12-imc","c12-promotional-mix","c12-advertising","c12-personal-selling","c12-sales-promotion","c12-public-relations","c12-direct-marketing","c12-sponsorship-placement"]},{"id":"t12-3","order":3,"title":"Designing programs: the advocacy funnel and push vs pull","summary":"Marketers design communications to move people from first hearing about a brand all the way to recommending it: the seven-stage customer advocacy funnel. They also decide whether to aim their efforts at the channel (push) or at consumers (pull), and the two usually work together.","concepts":["c12-advocacy-funnel","c12-push-pull"]},{"id":"t12-4","order":4,"title":"The six-step marketing communications process","summary":"Planning an IMC program follows six steps: specify objectives, identify the target audience, set the budget, design the program, schedule and run it, then evaluate and recommend changes. This topic covers each step and its tools: communication vs behavioural objectives, touch points, budgeting methods, the PLC table, scheduling patterns, and pretests/posttests.","concepts":["c12-imc-steps","c12-objectives","c12-target-audience","c12-budget-methods","c12-plc-promotion","c12-scheduling","c12-evaluation"]},{"id":"t12-5","order":5,"title":"Digital channels and the changing landscape","summary":"Communication is shifting toward content customers find on their own (inbound) and toward digital channels classified as paid, owned, or earned. Connected consumers and evolving media are reshaping where messages appear, and storytelling is a powerful way to engage people emotionally.","concepts":["c12-inbound-outbound","c12-paid-owned-earned","c12-trends","c12-storytelling"]},{"id":"t12-6","order":6,"title":"The industry, regulators, and internal marketing","summary":"Advertisers, agencies, media, suppliers, measurement companies, and regulators together make up the marketing communications industry. Six bodies (ASC, Competition Bureau, CRTC, CMA, CWTA, MMA) guide and regulate it, and internal marketing reminds firms that their own employees deliver the brand promise.","concepts":["c12-industry-agencies","c12-regulators","c12-internal-marketing"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"c12-communication-process","topic":"t12-1","term":"Communication process (source, message, channel, receiver, encoding, decoding)","definition":"Communication is the process of conveying a message to others; it requires a source (sender), a message (information to be transmitted), a channel of communication, a receiver (consumers who read, hear, or see the message), and the processes of encoding and decoding.","explain":"Think of it as a relay. The source has an idea and encodes it into words, images, or sounds (the message), sends it through a channel (TV, a salesperson, Instagram), and the receiver decodes it back into meaning. Encoding is done by the sender; decoding is done by the receiver. If any link is weak, the meaning the receiver takes away can differ from what the sender intended.","example":"Lululemon (source) encodes the idea 'gear that moves with you' into a video of a runner (message), posts it on Instagram (channel), and a follower in Calgary (receiver) decodes it as 'these leggings won't ride up on my runs.'","contrast":"Encoding vs decoding is the classic trap: encoding = the SENDER turning an idea into symbols; decoding = the RECEIVER interpreting those symbols. The channel is the vehicle that carries the message (radio station, salesperson, app), not the message itself and not the company behind it (the source).","trap":"Stems describe a creative team 'turning an idea into an ad' (encoding) or a viewer 'interpreting' or 'concluding' something (decoding). Don't confuse the channel (the medium) with the source (who is speaking) or the message (the content).","mnemonic":"S-M-C-R: Sender Makes it, Channel Carries it, Receiver Reads it. ENcode = ENter the idea into symbols; DEcode = DEcipher them.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Elements of the communication process","items":["Source (sender): the company or person with information to convey","Encoding: the sender turns the idea into symbols (words, pictures, sounds)","Message: the information to be transmitted","Channel: the means of conveying the message (TV, radio, salesperson, social media)","Receiver: consumers who read, hear, or see the message","Decoding: the receiver interprets the symbols back into meaning","Feedback: the receiver's response, communicated back to the sender","Noise: extraneous factors that distort the message or the feedback","Field of experience: the shared understanding sender and receiver need for the message to be decoded as intended"]}},{"id":"c12-feedback-noise-experience","topic":"t12-1","term":"Feedback, noise, and field of experience","definition":"Feedback is the receiver's response to a message, communicated back to the sender; noise is any extraneous factor that works against effective communication by distorting the message or the feedback; field of experience is the shared understanding and knowledge sender and receiver need for the message to be decoded as intended.","explain":"These three explain why communication succeeds or fails once a message is sent. Feedback (comments, clicks, purchases, questions) tells the sender whether the message landed. Noise is interference anywhere in the system: static, a typo, a cluttered page, a noisy room. Field of experience is common ground: if the receiver doesn't share the sender's language, culture, or knowledge, they decode the message differently.","example":"A Vancouver brand's English slogan translated word for word confuses its newcomer audience (field of experience problem); a cable glitch that garbles its TV spot is noise; the drop in calls to its sales line afterward is feedback.","contrast":"Noise distorts the transmission (something gets in the way); field of experience is a mismatch in understanding (the message arrives clearly but means something different to the receiver). Personal bias, a barrier, is different again: the receiver understands the message but rejects it because of their values.","trap":"Jargon, slang, inside jokes, and literal translations = field of experience, not noise. Static, typos, poor reception, background distractions = noise. Comments, clicks, questions, or purchases flowing back to the sender = feedback.","mnemonic":"Feedback flows BACK; Noise is a Nuisance; Field of experience = common ground.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-barriers","topic":"t12-1","term":"Barriers to effective communication","definition":"Four common barriers keep marketing messages from working: poor awareness, selective inattention, selective inexposure, and personal bias (e.g., principles).","explain":"Poor awareness: the audience doesn't know the brand or product exists, so messages have nothing to attach to. Selective inattention: people are exposed to the message but tune it out. Selective inexposure: people avoid being exposed at all (ad blockers, ad-free subscriptions, 'no flyers' signs). Personal bias: people's beliefs or principles lead them to reject or discount the message.","example":"A Toronto shopper pays for ad-free streaming (inexposure), scrolls past sponsored posts without noticing them (inattention), has never heard of a new earbud brand (poor awareness), and dismisses a fast-fashion ad because she opposes fast fashion (personal bias).","contrast":"Inexposure vs inattention is the key split: inexposure = the message never reaches the person because they avoid it; inattention = it reaches them but they don't pay attention. Personal bias differs from field of experience: bias is rejecting a message you understand; field of experience is failing to understand it.","trap":"'Installs an ad blocker', 'pays for ad-free', 'opts out' = inexposure. 'Sees but ignores', 'scrolls past', 'tunes out' = inattention. 'Values', 'principles', 'beliefs' = personal bias. 'Never heard of the brand' = poor awareness. Selective retention or distortion are perception terms from consumer behaviour, not on this list.","mnemonic":"A-I-E-B: Awareness is poor, Ignore it (inattention), Evade it (inexposure), Bias against it.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Common barriers to effective communication","items":["Poor awareness: the audience doesn't know the brand, product, or message exists","Selective inattention: exposed to the message but tunes it out","Selective inexposure: avoids exposure to the message altogether","Personal bias (e.g., principles): beliefs or values lead the receiver to reject the message"]}},{"id":"c12-marketing-communications","topic":"t12-2","term":"Marketing communications","definition":"The means by which firms attempt to inform, persuade, and remind consumers about the products and brands they sell (Kotler/Keller).","explain":"Every ad, post, coupon, sales call, or sponsorship is marketing communications, and each has three possible jobs: inform (tell people something new), persuade (convince them to choose you), and remind (keep the brand top of mind). The course also stresses that today 'the lines are blurred between reality, entertainment, self-expression, and marketing communications': brand messages often look like content people watch and share for its own sake.","example":"WestJet informs travellers about a new route, persuades them its fares beat rivals', and reminds past flyers with holiday-season emails.","contrast":"Marketing communications is the broad activity (inform, persuade, remind); IMC is the discipline of coordinating all of it so every element delivers one consistent message. The marketing mix (4 Ps) is broader still: promotion is only one P.","trap":"The three verbs are inform, persuade, remind; watch for swaps like 'segment', 'price', 'distribute', or 'regulate'. The same verbs reappear in the PLC table (inform in introduction, persuade in growth, remind in maturity).","mnemonic":"I-P-R: Inform, Persuade, Remind.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-imc","topic":"t12-2","term":"Integrated marketing communications (IMC)","definition":"The concept of designing marketing communications programs that coordinate all promotional activities to provide a consistent message across all audiences (a target audience of consumers, intermediaries, or both), relying on metrics and analytics to measure and evaluate success and make improvements.","explain":"IMC means every touch (ads, social posts, salespeople, packaging, events, customer service) works together and says the same thing. The marketer must consider two issues: (1) the balance of the promotional elements, and (2) coordinating all promotional activities to provide a consistent message to the target audience. Because different elements are often run by different departments, coordinating a consistent effort is a key challenge, so marketers use a process (the six steps) that makes programs easy to design and evaluate.","example":"A Calgary hockey team's promotion night bundles discounted tickets, a collectible T-shirt giveaway, a website landing page, radio ads, and a ticket phone line, all built around one theme and message.","contrast":"Balance = how much of each tool to use (the proportions of the mix). Consistency = whether every tool says the same thing. Plain 'marketing communications' just means informing, persuading, and reminding; IMC adds the coordination and measurement.","trap":"Answers like 'each department develops its own message' or 'focus solely on the most cost-effective element' contradict IMC. The key challenge is coordinating a consistent promotional effort across the departments responsible for different elements. The audience can be consumers OR intermediaries OR both.","mnemonic":"IMC = One Voice: Balance the tools, Coordinate the message, Measure the results.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-promotional-mix","topic":"t12-2","term":"Promotional mix (IMC components)","definition":"The combination of communication tools a firm uses (advertising, personal selling, public relations/publicity, sales promotion, and direct marketing), expanded in IMC to include digital/social media, email, event marketing and sponsorship, product placement, and customer service.","explain":"Each tool has strengths and weaknesses, so firms combine them. Mass elements (advertising, sales promotion, public relations) send the same message to large audiences; customized elements (personal selling, direct marketing) tailor the message to individuals. The course's IMC components figure shows advertising, PR/publicity, customer service, direct marketing, personal selling, sponsorship, digital/social media, email, and sales promotion all feeding one integrated message.","example":"Canadian Tire combines TV ads (advertising), weekly flyers with discounts (sales promotion), in-store staff help (personal selling and customer service), Triangle Rewards emails (direct marketing/email), and sports sponsorships.","contrast":"The promotional mix is only the Promotion P; the marketing mix is all 4 Ps (product, price, place, promotion). Mass vs customized: advertising, sales promotion, and PR are mass; personal selling and direct marketing are customized.","trap":"'Pricing strategy' or 'distribution intermediaries' are not IMC components. Customer service DOES count as an IMC component in the course figure. Never call personal selling a mass element, and never give it a low cost per contact.","mnemonic":"Mass = A-S-P (Advertising, Sales promotion, PR); Customized = P-D (Personal selling, Direct marketing).","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Promotional mix elements","head":["Element","Mass or customized","Key strength","Key weakness"],"rows":[["Advertising","Mass","Efficient reach; control over the message","High absolute cost; hard to get feedback"],["Personal selling","Customized","Immediate feedback; persuasive; tailored","Very high cost per contact"],["Public relations / publicity","Mass","High credibility","Little control over the message"],["Sales promotion","Mass","Changes behaviour in the short run","Easily abused; can trigger promotion and price wars"],["Direct marketing","Customized","Targeted and measurable","Cost (databases); privacy concerns"]]}},{"id":"c12-advertising","topic":"t12-2","term":"Advertising","definition":"Any paid form of non-personal communication about an organization, good, service, or idea by an identified sponsor.","explain":"Advertising is mass communication you pay for and put your name on: TV, radio, print, out-of-home, and digital ads. Because you buy the space, you control exactly what is said and when, and you can reach huge audiences efficiently. The downsides are a high absolute cost and difficulty getting feedback on whether the message worked.","example":"Tim Hortons pays for national TV spots announcing its Roll Up To Win contest, clearly branded as Tim Hortons.","contrast":"Advertising vs publicity: both are non-personal, but advertising is paid by an identified sponsor who controls the message, while publicity is indirectly paid and largely uncontrolled. Advertising vs personal selling: one-way mass communication vs two-way customized communication.","trap":"Cue words: 'paid', 'identified sponsor', 'non-personal'. If the firm didn't pay the media outlet and can't control the story, it's publicity. Clearly labelled, paid 'sponsored content' is advertising. Advertising does NOT give immediate two-way feedback.","mnemonic":"Advertising = P-I-N: Paid, Identified sponsor, Non-personal.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-personal-selling","topic":"t12-2","term":"Personal selling","definition":"The two-way flow of communication between a buyer and seller, designed to influence a person's or group's purchase decision.","explain":"Personal selling happens face to face, by phone, or by video, with a salesperson who can listen, answer questions, and adjust the pitch on the spot. That makes it the most customized and persuasive element, with immediate feedback, which suits complex, expensive, or B2B products. Its weakness is cost: it is extremely expensive per contact, so it can't reach mass audiences efficiently.","example":"A rep for an Edmonton farm-equipment dealer visits a grain farm, asks about acreage, and recommends a combine configuration that fits the farmer's budget.","contrast":"Personal selling vs direct marketing: both are customized, but personal selling is a live two-way dialogue with immediate feedback, while direct marketing sends targeted messages (mail, email, catalogues, telemarketing) to generate a response. Personal selling vs customer service: selling aims to influence a purchase decision; service supports customers, often after the sale.","trap":"Cue words: 'two-way', 'adjusts the pitch', 'demonstrates', 'answers objections'. Personal selling appears in BOTH the push and pull columns of Figure 12-2. Its weakness is HIGH cost per contact, never low.","mnemonic":"Personal selling = Pricey but Precise.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-sales-promotion","topic":"t12-2","term":"Sales promotion","definition":"A short-term inducement of value offered to arouse interest in buying a good or service, such as coupons, samples, contests, and rebates (consumer promotions) or allowances and co-op funds (trade promotions).","explain":"Sales promotion gives people an extra reason to act now. Consumer promotions (coupons, free samples, contests, rebates) target end users and support a pull strategy; trade promotions (trade allowances, trade shows, merchandising allowances, co-op advertising funds) target channel members and support a push strategy. It is very effective at changing behaviour in the short run, but it is easily abused and can trigger promotion and price wars.","example":"Shoppers Drug Mart offers bonus PC Optimum points this weekend only (consumer promotion); a snack maker gives Loblaws a merchandising allowance for an end-aisle display (trade promotion).","contrast":"Sales promotion vs advertising: advertising builds awareness and attitudes with a message; sales promotion adds tangible short-term value (a deal) to trigger action. Consumer promotion (pull, aimed at shoppers) vs trade promotion (push, aimed at the channel).","trap":"Cue words: 'limited time', 'coupon', 'free sample', 'contest', 'rebate', 'buy one get one'. A news story about the product is publicity, not sales promotion. In the PLC table, free samples = introduction; discounts, coupons, and events = maturity.","mnemonic":"Sales promotion = 'Act NOW' value.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-public-relations","topic":"t12-2","term":"Public relations and publicity","definition":"Public relations is a form of communication management that seeks to influence the feelings, opinions, or beliefs held by customers, potential customers, shareholders, employees, and other stakeholders; publicity is a non-personal, indirectly paid presentation of an organization, good, or service, such as a news story.","explain":"PR tools include news releases, press conferences, community involvement, and reputation management (for example, during a recall). When media cover the company because the story is newsworthy, that coverage is publicity: the firm didn't buy the space. Publicity is highly credible because it comes from a third party, but the firm has little control over what is said or whether it runs at all.","example":"After a Canadian outerwear brand donates parkas to northern communities, CBC runs a story about it; the company paid nothing for the airtime.","contrast":"Publicity vs advertising: publicity is indirectly paid and uncontrolled; advertising is paid, with an identified sponsor and full control. In the digital channels framework, public relations and media coverage are earned media.","trap":"'The company paid nothing to the outlet', 'a news story', 'media covered' = publicity. A labelled, paid 'sponsored' article is advertising. Strength = credibility; weakness = lack of control. A mail-in rebate is sales promotion, not PR.","mnemonic":"Publicity: Praised by others, Paid indirectly, Poor control.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-direct-marketing","topic":"t12-2","term":"Direct marketing","definition":"Using direct communication with consumers to generate a response in the form of an order, a request for further information, or a visit to a retail outlet, through tools such as direct mail, catalogues, telemarketing, direct response TV, and email.","explain":"Direct marketing talks to specific people (usually from a database) and asks them to respond, so results can be measured precisely (responses, orders, redemptions by code). It is customized and targeted. Its weaknesses include the cost of building and maintaining databases and consumers' privacy concerns.","example":"Mark's mails a catalogue and sends a personalized email with a unique order code to past customers, then counts the orders each code produces.","contrast":"Direct marketing vs advertising: direct marketing targets known individuals and seeks a measurable response; advertising broadcasts to a mass audience. Direct marketing vs personal selling: targeted messages vs a live two-way conversation. On the course's digital channels slide, email marketing is classified as PAID media.","trap":"Cue words: 'catalogue', 'direct mail', 'telemarketing', 'respond', 'order code', 'database'. A press release is PR, not direct marketing. Email marketing = paid media on the slide, not owned.","mnemonic":"Direct marketing: Direct to you, Data-driven, Demands a response.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-sponsorship-placement","topic":"t12-2","term":"Event marketing, sponsorship, and product placement","definition":"Event marketing involves a brand in an experience or occasion that brings consumers and the brand together; sponsorship is when a company pays a fee to be associated with an event, team, cause, or individual in exchange for exposure; product placement is the paid inclusion of a brand in entertainment content such as TV shows, movies, video games, or online videos.","explain":"These tools reach consumers in settings they chose to be in (a festival, a game, a favourite show), so the brand feels less intrusive than a commercial. All three appear on the PULL side of Figure 12-2, aimed at consumers. The trade-off is less control over exactly how and when the brand is seen, and effects that can be harder to measure.","example":"A telecom pays to put its name on a hockey arena (sponsorship); a Canadian beer brand runs its own pop-up summer festival (event marketing); a streaming series shows characters using a specific phone brand under a paid deal (product placement).","contrast":"Event marketing = the brand creates or is woven into an experience; sponsorship = the brand pays to attach its name to someone else's event, team, or cause; product placement = the brand appears inside entertainment content. None of these is publicity, because the firm pays.","trap":"A logo on arena boards or a festival stage = sponsorship, not product placement. A product used on screen by characters = product placement. These are pull tools, not push tools.","mnemonic":"Sponsor = Sign on their event; Event marketing = your own Experience; Placement = Product on screen.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-advocacy-funnel","topic":"t12-3","term":"Customer advocacy funnel","definition":"A model (Figure 12-1) of how marketing communications move consumers through seven stages: awareness, interest, engagement, trial, purchase, loyalty, and advocacy, where loyal customers recommend the product to others.","explain":"The funnel shows that communications don't stop at the sale. Awareness (a website, SEO, online video, and display ads drive people to an online destination) leads to interest (attributes are highlighted so people want to learn more), engagement (people participate in the product experience and interact with its marketing), trial (free samples, limited trials, downloads), purchase (positive experiences lead to buying), loyalty (repeat purchases, encouraged by CRM/loyalty programs), and advocacy (loyal customers are rewarded with extra experiences and recommend the product).","example":"A Calgary meal-kit brand runs YouTube ads (awareness), highlights 15-minute recipes (interest), hosts an Instagram cook-along (engagement), offers a free first box (trial), converts to a paid order (purchase), rewards repeat orders with points (loyalty), and gives top fans perks so they recommend it (advocacy).","contrast":"Loyalty = repeat purchasing, often driven by reward programs; advocacy = recommending to others. Engagement = participating and interacting with the brand and its marketing; trial = getting the product itself to try (sample, limited trial, download). Don't confuse the funnel with the hierarchy of effects (awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption).","trap":"Membership cards, points, and coupons for continued purchases = LOYALTY (official question). 'Recommend', 'refer friends', 'rave reviews' = ADVOCACY. 'Free sample', '14-day free trial', 'download' = TRIAL. 'Evaluation' and 'adoption' are hierarchy-of-effects stages, not funnel stages.","mnemonic":"A-I-E-T-P-L-A: 'All Interested Eager Testers Purchase, Love, Advocate.'","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Figure 12-1 The customer advocacy funnel (top to bottom)","items":["1. Awareness: a website, SEO, online video, and display ads drive consumers to an online destination","2. Interest: interesting product attributes are highlighted so potential customers want to learn more","3. Engagement: potential customers participate in the product experience and interact with its marketing","4. Trial: customers obtain free samples or get the product as a limited trial or download","5. Purchase: positive product experiences lead to purchase","6. Loyalty: ongoing positive experiences lead to repeat purchases, encouraged by CRM/loyalty programs","7. Advocacy: loyal customers are rewarded with additional experiences and recommend the product to others"]}},{"id":"c12-push-pull","topic":"t12-3","term":"Push vs pull strategies","definition":"A push strategy directs communications at channel members (distributors, wholesalers, retailers) to gain their support in stocking and selling the product; a pull strategy directs communications at ultimate consumers (and media and the public) to build awareness, trial, and demand so consumers ask for the product.","explain":"Push: the manufacturer pushes the product through the channel using direct response, personal selling, and trade promotions (trade allowances, trade shows, merchandising allowances, co-op advertising funds) to win listings, sales, merchandising displays, feature pricing, and inclusion in flyers. Pull: the manufacturer creates consumer demand that pulls the product through the channel, using advertising, publicity, consumer promotion, product placement, event marketing, social media and sponsorship, and personal selling, aiming for awareness, interest, recognition, trial, purchase, loyalty, and advocacy. The course stresses that the two work together.","example":"A Quebec cheese maker pays Metro a merchandising allowance for a feature display (push) while running Instagram recipes and in-store sampling for shoppers (pull).","contrast":"Push targets the trade (intermediaries); pull targets consumers. Incentives and training for retailers' sales staff = push. Push is NOT the same as outbound, and pull is NOT the same as inbound: TV ads aimed at consumers are outbound AND pull.","trap":"Ask WHO receives the communication: retailers, wholesalers, distributors, or their sales staff = push; consumers = pull. Results like listings, displays, feature pricing, and flyer inclusion = push; awareness, trial, loyalty, and advocacy = pull. Personal selling appears in both columns.","mnemonic":"PUSH = Persuade Us (the Stores) to Handle it; PULL = People Urge Local stores to List it.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 12-2 Push and pull communication strategies","head":["Aspect","Push strategy","Pull strategy"],"rows":[["Focus","Distributors, wholesalers, retailers (the channel)","Consumers, media, the public"],["Tools","Direct response; personal selling; trade promotions (trade allowances, trade shows, merchandising allowances, co-op advertising funds)","Advertising; publicity; consumer promotion; product placement; event marketing; social media and sponsorship; personal selling"],["Results sought","Listings, sales, merchandising displays, feature pricing, inclusion in flyers","Awareness, interest, recognition, trial, purchase, loyalty, advocacy"]]}},{"id":"c12-imc-steps","topic":"t12-4","term":"Six steps in the marketing communications process","definition":"The planning process for an IMC program (Figure 12-3): (1) specify the IMC objectives, (2) identify the target audience, (3) set the promotional budget, (4) design the promotional program, (5) schedule and run the IMC elements, and (6) evaluate the program and recommend changes.","explain":"The steps flow logically: decide what you want to achieve, then who you need to reach, then how much you will spend to get there, then which tools and messages to use, then when and how often each runs, and finally whether it worked and what to change. Customer touch points are considered when identifying the target audience (step 2); the product life cycle is considered when designing the program (step 4).","example":"A Winnipeg gym (1) aims for 2,000 trial memberships, (2) targets students near campus and maps their touch points, (3) budgets $60,000, (4) chooses TikTok creators, campus posters, and a free-week offer, (5) runs them in late August and January, and (6) reviews sign-ups and engagement to adjust the next campaign.","contrast":"Designing the program (step 4: which tools and messages) vs scheduling and running (step 5: timing, frequency, duration). Specifying objectives (step 1: what to achieve) vs evaluating (step 6: did we achieve it, and what should change).","trap":"Official questions: defining goals before launch = step 1; deciding the specific timing of TV ads and online videos = step 5; reviewing sales data, feedback, and digital engagement afterward = step 6. Touch points go with step 2; the PLC goes with step 4. Selecting intermediaries is a distribution decision, not one of the six steps.","mnemonic":"'Only Target Buyers Deserve Scheduled Evaluations': Objectives, Target audience, Budget, Design, Schedule, Evaluate.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Figure 12-3 Steps in the marketing communications process","items":["1. Specify the IMC objectives","2. Identify the target audience (consider customer touch points)","3. Set the promotional budget","4. Design the promotional program (consider the product life cycle)","5. Schedule and run the IMC elements","6. Evaluate the program and recommend changes"]}},{"id":"c12-objectives","topic":"t12-4","term":"Communication vs behavioural objectives (and the hierarchy of effects)","definition":"Communication objectives lay the foundation to build customer relationships (e.g., awareness, knowledge, attitudes); behavioural objectives relate to outcomes that are action-oriented and necessary for business success (e.g., trial, purchase, repeat purchase). The hierarchy of effects is the sequence of stages a prospective buyer goes through: awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption.","explain":"Step 1 of the process means setting both kinds of objectives. Communication objectives work on the mind: getting people to know, understand, and feel good about a brand. Behavioural objectives work on actions: trying, buying, and buying again. The hierarchy of effects reminds marketers that buyers move from awareness and interest through evaluation before they try and adopt a product, so early objectives tend to be communication-based and later ones behavioural.","example":"A new Alberta oat milk sets a communication objective (raise brand awareness among Edmonton coffee drinkers to 40%) and a behavioural objective (10,000 trial purchases in three months).","contrast":"Awareness, knowledge, attitudes, recall = communication. Trial, purchase, repeat purchase, sign-ups = behavioural. The hierarchy of effects (awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption) is not the advocacy funnel, which has engagement, purchase, loyalty, and advocacy and no 'evaluation' stage.","trap":"A number doesn't make an objective behavioural: '30% awareness' is still a communication objective. 'Evaluation' and 'adoption' belong to the hierarchy of effects; 'loyalty' and 'advocacy' belong to the funnel.","mnemonic":"Communication = Head (know, feel); Behavioural = Hands (do, buy). Hierarchy of effects: A-I-E-T-A.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Communication vs behavioural objectives","head":["Type","Purpose","Examples"],"rows":[["Communication","Lay the foundation to build customer relationships","Awareness, knowledge, attitudes"],["Behavioural","Action-oriented outcomes necessary for business success","Trial, purchase, repeat purchase"]]}},{"id":"c12-target-audience","topic":"t12-4","term":"Target audience and customer touch points","definition":"The target audience is the group of prospective buyers toward which a promotional program is directed; customer touch points are all the points of contact between a customer and a brand (ads, packaging, the store, the website, service calls), considered when identifying the target audience.","explain":"Step 2 asks: who exactly are we talking to, and where do they meet our brand? Knowing the audience's demographics, lifestyle, and media habits tells you which tools and channels can reach them. Mapping touch points shows every moment that shapes their impression, so each can carry a consistent message. In IMC the audience can be consumers, intermediaries, or both.","example":"A Halifax dental clinic identifies young families as its audience and maps their touch points: Google search results, the booking app, the reminder text, the waiting room, and the follow-up email.","contrast":"Touch points (where the customer contacts the brand) are not channels of distribution (how the product gets to market) or the promotional schedule (when tools run). The target audience can include intermediaries, such as retailers in a push program, not only end consumers.","trap":"Touch points are considered at step 2 (identify the target audience), not step 4 (where the PLC is considered). Internal documents customers never see are not touch points.","mnemonic":"Touch points = every place the customer 'touches' the brand.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-budget-methods","topic":"t12-4","term":"Promotional budgeting methods","definition":"Ways to set the promotional budget (step 3): percentage of sales (a fixed percentage of past or anticipated sales), competitive parity (matching competitors' spending or share of market), all-you-can-afford (money left after other budget items), and objective and task (determine the objectives, outline the tasks needed to reach them, and total their costs).","explain":"Percentage of sales is simple but backwards: it makes sales the cause of promotion instead of the result, so spending falls just when sales need help. Competitive parity keeps pace with rivals but ignores the firm's own objectives. All-you-can-afford is common in small firms but has no link to goals. Objective and task is considered the best approach because spending is tied directly to what the firm wants to achieve, though estimating the tasks requires judgment.","example":"A Kelowna winery sets an objective (5,000 trial bottles), lists the tasks (tastings, Instagram ads, a food-show booth), and adds up their costs: objective and task.","contrast":"Percentage of sales = based on sales; competitive parity = based on rivals; all-you-can-afford = based on leftover cash; objective and task = based on goals. Only objective and task starts from objectives.","trap":"'Matches the rival's spending' or 'share of market' = competitive parity. 'Whatever is left over' = all-you-can-afford. 'X% of last year's sales' = percentage of sales. 'Lists the tasks and adds up the cost' = objective and task.","mnemonic":"Sales, Rivals, Leftovers, Goals: only Goals is the gold standard.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Promotional budgeting methods","items":["Percentage of sales: a fixed % of past or anticipated sales (simple, but backwards logic)","Competitive parity: match competitors' spending or share of market (ignores own objectives)","All-you-can-afford: money left after other budget items (common in small firms)","Objective and task: set objectives, outline tasks, total their cost (best approach; needs judgment)"]}},{"id":"c12-plc-promotion","topic":"t12-4","term":"Product life cycle and promotional programs","definition":"The promotional objective and the composition of the promotional mix change across the product life cycle, which is considered when designing the promotional program (step 4): introduction, to inform; growth, to persuade; maturity, to remind; decline, to phase out (Figure 12-4).","explain":"In introduction, people must learn the product exists: publicity in magazines, advertising, a salesforce calling on intermediaries, and free samples. In growth, competitors arrive, so the job is to persuade: personal selling to intermediaries and advertising that differentiates the brand from competitors. In maturity, the goal is to maintain existing buyers: reminder advertising, sales promotion such as discounts, coupons, and events, limited personal selling, and direct-mail reminders. In decline, little money is spent on promotion as the product is phased out.","example":"A new Canadian electric snowblower is reviewed in magazines (introduction), later runs 'quieter than the competition' ads (growth), then relies on coupons and reminder ads (maturity), and finally gets almost no promotion before it is discontinued (decline).","contrast":"Maturity = maintain existing buyers with reminder advertising and sales promotion; 'reduce promotional spending to maximize profit' describes decline, not maturity. Free samples = introduction; discounts and coupons = maturity.","trap":"Official question: the PLC helps marketers choose the appropriate communication tools at each phase. Map the verbs: inform/persuade/remind/phase out = introduction/growth/maturity/decline. 'Differentiate from competing brands' = growth.","mnemonic":"I-P-R-P: Inform, Persuade, Remind, Phase out.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Figure 12-4 Product life cycle considerations for promotional programs","head":["Stage","Promotional objective","Promotional activities"],"rows":[["Introduction","To inform","Publicity in magazines; advertising; salesforce calling on intermediaries; sales promotion in the form of free samples"],["Growth","To persuade","Personal selling to intermediaries; advertising to differentiate the product from competing brands"],["Maturity","To remind","Reminder advertising; sales promotion in the form of discounts, coupons, and events; limited personal selling; direct-mail reminders"],["Decline","To phase out","Little money spent on promotion"]]}},{"id":"c12-scheduling","topic":"t12-4","term":"Scheduling the IMC elements (continuous, flighting, pulse)","definition":"The promotional schedule describes the order in which each promotional tool is introduced and the frequency and duration of its use (step 5); common patterns are continuous (steady activity), flighting (intermittent periods of activity and no activity), and pulse (a continuous base plus periodic bursts).","explain":"Once the program is designed, the marketer decides timing: which tool launches first, how often each runs, and for how long. Continuous scheduling suits products bought steadily all year. Flighting concentrates spending in bursts when demand is seasonal and goes dark in between. Pulse combines both: an always-on presence with extra pushes at peak times.","example":"Canadian Tire keeps weekly flyers running all year and adds heavy TV bursts before winter and the holidays: a pulse schedule.","contrast":"Flighting has gaps with NO activity; pulse never drops to zero. Scheduling (when and how often) is step 5; designing the program (which tools and messages) is step 4.","trap":"Official question: deciding the specific timing of TV ads and online video placements = scheduling the IMC elements, not designing the program. 'Heavy, then nothing' = flighting; 'steady base plus spikes' = pulse.","mnemonic":"Continuous = Constant; Flighting = Flies, then Lands; Pulse = a heartbeat on a steady line.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-evaluation","topic":"t12-4","term":"Evaluating the program and recommending changes","definition":"The final step (6) of the marketing communications process: measuring results with pretests (before elements run) and posttests (after) and with metrics such as sales data, customer feedback, and digital engagement (click-through, conversion, and engagement rates), then recommending changes.","explain":"IMC relies on metrics and analytics to measure success and make improvements, so evaluation is built into the process. Pretests check ads or concepts before money is spent; posttests measure what happened afterward (recall, attitude change, sales). Results are compared with the objectives set in step 1 and drive changes to the next program. Because different departments often run different elements, coordinating a consistent promotional effort, and consistent measurement, is a practical challenge.","example":"After its spring campaign, an Ottawa bike shop compares its sales lift, survey feedback, and Instagram engagement against its goals and shifts budget from flyers to short-form video.","contrast":"Pretest = before launch; posttest = after. Evaluating (step 6) vs specifying objectives (step 1): objectives are the yardstick, and evaluation measures against it. Deciding when ads run is scheduling (step 5), not evaluation.","trap":"Official question: reviewing sales data, customer feedback, and digital engagement after the campaign = evaluating the program and recommending changes. 'Recommend changes' means results feed back into the next plan.","mnemonic":"Pre = Preview; Post = Post-mortem.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-inbound-outbound","topic":"t12-5","term":"Inbound vs outbound communications","definition":"Inbound communications draw customers to the brand; they require excellent content and the ability to be seen in the right places (search, social, content marketing) and can be considerably more cost-effective than outbound. Outbound communications are the more traditional approach of sending messages out to audiences (advertising, cold calls, direct mail).","explain":"Inbound flips the direction: instead of interrupting people, you create useful content and make sure it shows up where they are already looking (search results, social feeds), so they come to you. Because interested people find you, inbound can be much more cost-effective. Outbound is the traditional broadcast or interruption approach: ads, telemarketing, and direct mail sent to audiences whether or not they are looking.","example":"A Calgary renovation firm publishes basement-development cost guides that rank on Google (inbound) and also mails postcards to new homeowners (outbound).","contrast":"Inbound/outbound is about direction (they come to you vs you go to them); push/pull is about the target (the trade vs consumers). A TV ad aimed at consumers is outbound but pull. Inbound overlaps with owned and earned media but is a different classification.","trap":"Inbound cue words: 'content', 'SEO', 'be found', 'searching', 'how-to guides'. Outbound cue words: 'cold calls', 'mailed flyers', 'radio spots', 'traditional'. Don't equate inbound with pull.","mnemonic":"Inbound = they come IN; Outbound = you send OUT.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-paid-owned-earned","topic":"t12-5","term":"Paid, owned, and earned media (digital channels)","definition":"Paid media are channels the company pays to use (search advertising, display advertising, social media advertising, email marketing, paid bloggers/influencers, mobile in-app advertising); owned media are channels the company controls at least in part (company websites, company-owned social media such as Facebook pages and YouTube channels, official blogs, mobile apps); earned media is exposure generated by others (review websites such as Amazon reviews and Yelp, public relations, media coverage).","explain":"Ask two questions: did we pay for it, and do we control it? Paid = we pay for placement. Owned = we run the channel ourselves. Earned = other people (customers, journalists) create or spread it, which makes it credible but uncontrollable. The course slide (Keller/Swaminathan) specifically classifies email marketing and paid influencers as PAID.","example":"Roots runs Instagram ads (paid), posts on its own Instagram account and website (owned), and gets a fashion-magazine feature and customer reviews (earned).","contrast":"A brand's own Facebook PAGE is owned; Facebook ADS are paid. Influencers the brand pays are paid; unpaid customer reviews and media stories are earned. 'Organic media' is not one of the three categories.","trap":"Email marketing = paid (per the course slide), even though it goes to the company's own list. Official question: regularly updating the official blog and social profiles = owned. Reviews, PR, and media coverage = earned.","mnemonic":"Paid = you Pay; Owned = you Own; Earned = others give it to you.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Digital channels (Keller/Swaminathan)","head":["Type","Examples from the course slide"],"rows":[["Paid","Search advertising; display advertising; social media advertising; e-mail marketing; paid bloggers/influencers; mobile in-app advertising"],["Owned","Company websites; company-owned social media (e.g., Facebook pages, YouTube channel); official blog; mobile apps"],["Earned","Review websites (Amazon reviews, Yelp); public relations; media coverage"]]}},{"id":"c12-trends","topic":"t12-5","term":"Changing landscape: connected consumers and evolving media","definition":"Connected consumers: affordable and fast Internet technology provides consumers with easy-to-use services and devices that facilitate marketing communications. Evolving media: content produced strictly for streaming; traditional media creating their own online assets to remain competitive and relevant; news organizations delivering content through YouTube channels and apps; radio stations streaming content and creating podcasts; out-of-home being replaced with digital boards; digital advertising shifting to mobile.","explain":"Two forces are reshaping marketing communications. Consumers are always connected, so they can search, compare, review, and share instantly, and brands can reach them anywhere. Meanwhile, media themselves are changing form: TV and news move online and to streaming, radio becomes podcasts, billboards go digital, and ad spending follows audiences to mobile.","example":"A Toronto shopper compares prices on her phone in a store (connected consumer) while a TV network releases a streaming-only series and a downtown billboard switches to a digital screen (evolving media).","contrast":"Connected consumers is about consumers' technology and behaviour; evolving media is about how media outlets and formats are changing. Neither is the same as inbound communications, which is a firm's strategy.","trap":"Every example on the evolving media list moves TOWARD digital, streaming, and mobile. Anything describing a return to print only, or a move away from mobile, is not on the list.","mnemonic":"Consumers CONNECT; Media MORPH.","figure":{"type":"list","title":"Evolving media (course list)","items":["Content produced strictly for streaming","Traditional media creating their own online assets to remain competitive and relevant","News organizations using YouTube channels and apps to deliver content","Radio stations streaming content and creating podcasts","Out-of-home being replaced with digital boards","Digital advertising shifting to mobile"]}},{"id":"c12-storytelling","topic":"t12-5","term":"Storytelling","definition":"Storytelling builds relationships by conveying events using images and words; stories engage people through their emotions and experiences, which motivates them to act and to more readily accept a concept or idea.","explain":"Instead of listing features, a brand tells a story with characters, a challenge, and a resolution. Because stories trigger emotion and connect to people's own experiences, audiences remember them, relate to the brand, and are more open to its message. Dove's campaigns about real women's self-image are the course example.","example":"A Canadian bank's video follows a newcomer family's first year in Saskatoon, from opening an account to buying a first home, instead of quoting interest rates.","contrast":"Storytelling vs feature or price advertising: storytelling persuades through emotion and experience; feature ads persuade through facts, specs, and deals. Storytelling vs product placement: placement puts the product inside someone else's story; storytelling is the brand's own narrative.","trap":"Cue words: 'journey', 'follows a family', 'emotions', 'images and words'. Statistics, ingredient lists, and coupons are not storytelling.","mnemonic":"Facts tell; stories SELL (through emotion).","figure":null},{"id":"c12-industry-agencies","topic":"t12-6","term":"Marketing communications industry and agency types","definition":"The marketing communications industry is made up of areas (advertisers or clients, agencies, media, suppliers, regulators, and measurement companies) that work together to form an industry that is ethical, trustworthy, cohesive, and measurable. Agencies may be full-service (research, creative, media, production), limited-service or specialty (e.g., creative boutiques, media-buying services), or in-house (a department inside the advertiser's company).","explain":"Advertisers hire agencies to create and place communications, media companies sell the space and time, suppliers provide production services, measurement companies track audiences and results, and regulators set the rules. Agencies differ by scope: full-service agencies do it all, limited-service agencies specialize in one function, and in-house agencies are departments within the advertiser itself.","example":"A Canadian grocery chain uses a full-service Toronto agency for its national campaign, a media-buying specialist for digital placements, and its in-house team for weekly flyers.","contrast":"Full-service = one-stop shop; limited-service = one specialty (creative only, media buying only); in-house = owned by the advertiser. Measurement companies measure audiences and results; regulators set and enforce rules.","trap":"Distribution intermediaries (wholesalers, retailers) belong to marketing channels, not the marketing communications industry. The four qualities are ethical, trustworthy, cohesive, and measurable.","mnemonic":"Industry players: Advertisers, Agencies, Media, Suppliers, Regulators, Measurement.","figure":null},{"id":"c12-regulators","topic":"t12-6","term":"Regulatory bodies in Canadian marketing communications","definition":"Six bodies guide or regulate Canadian marketing communications: Advertising Standards Canada (ASC), the Competition Bureau, the Canadian Radio-television and Telecommunications Commission (CRTC), the Canadian Marketing Association (CMA), the Canadian Wireless Telecommunications Association (CWTA), and the Mobile Marketing Association (MMA).","explain":"ASC is the industry's self-regulatory body; it administers the Canadian Code of Advertising Standards and handles complaints about ads. The Competition Bureau is the federal agency that enforces the Competition Act, including rules against misleading advertising and deceptive marketing. The CRTC regulates broadcasting and telecommunications and enforces Canada's anti-spam legislation (CASL). The CMA is the marketers' association, with a code of ethics and standards of practice. The CWTA represents the wireless (mobile) industry, for example through guidelines for text-message short-code campaigns, and the MMA sets guidelines and best practices for mobile marketing.","example":"A misleading 'was $899, now $399' claim could draw a Competition Bureau investigation; a consumer complaint about an exaggerated ad goes to ASC; a new broadcast licence comes from the CRTC.","contrast":"ASC = voluntary industry self-regulation through its Code; Competition Bureau = law enforcement under the Competition Act; CRTC = broadcasting and telecom regulator; CMA = marketers' code of ethics; CWTA and MMA = the wireless and mobile specialists.","trap":"Don't mix up the ASC Code (self-regulation) with the Competition Act (federal law). Bodies such as the Better Business Bureau are not on the course's list of six.","mnemonic":"ASC = Ad Standards Code; Competition Bureau = Competition Act; CRTC = broadcasting and telecom; CMA = Marketers' ethics; CWTA = Wireless; MMA = Mobile.","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Regulatory bodies","head":["Body","What it does"],"rows":[["Advertising Standards Canada (ASC)","Industry self-regulatory body; administers the Canadian Code of Advertising Standards and handles complaints about ads"],["Competition Bureau","Federal agency that enforces the Competition Act, including misleading advertising and deceptive marketing practices"],["CRTC","Regulates broadcasting and telecommunications; enforces anti-spam legislation (CASL)"],["Canadian Marketing Association (CMA)","Marketers' association with a code of ethics and standards of practice"],["Canadian Wireless Telecommunications Association (CWTA)","Represents the wireless (mobile) industry, e.g., guidelines for text-message short codes"],["Mobile Marketing Association (MMA)","Sets guidelines and best practices for mobile marketing"]]}},{"id":"c12-internal-marketing","topic":"t12-6","term":"Internal marketing and the employer brand manager","definition":"Internal marketing treats the organization's own employees (its people) as an audience to inform, train, and engage so they can deliver the brand promise; the course calls it a key to competitive advantage. An emerging role is the employer brand manager, who shapes how current and prospective employees see the organization as a place to work.","explain":"External campaigns promise an experience, but employees deliver it. Internal marketing makes sure staff understand, believe in, and can deliver what the brand promises. The course notes that social media is important for employee engagement and content development, and that the ultimate goal is to create experiences.","example":"Before a new service campaign launches, WestJet shares the ads with crews first, trains them on the promise, and features employee stories on its social channels.","contrast":"Internal marketing targets the firm's OWN employees; a push strategy targets channel members (retailers, wholesalers) and their staff; external IMC targets customers. An employer brand manager manages the company's image as an employer, not a product brand.","trap":"Training and incentives for a retailer's sales staff = push strategy, not internal marketing. Internal marketing supports external IMC; it does not replace it.","mnemonic":"Market INSIDE before you market OUTSIDE.","figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"a12-01","type":"order","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","title":"Build the customer advocacy funnel","prompt":"Put the seven stages of the customer advocacy funnel (Figure 12-1) in order, from the top of the funnel to the bottom.","items":["Awareness","Interest","Engagement","Trial","Purchase","Loyalty","Advocacy"],"explanation":"Customers first learn the brand exists (awareness), find attributes worth learning about (interest), participate and interact (engagement), try a sample or limited version (trial), buy (purchase), keep buying thanks to positive experiences and rewards (loyalty), and finally recommend it to others (advocacy)."},{"id":"a12-02","type":"order","concept":"c12-imc-steps","title":"Six steps of the marketing communications process","prompt":"Put the six steps of the marketing communications process (Figure 12-3) in order.","items":["Specify the IMC objectives","Identify the target audience","Set the promotional budget","Design the promotional program","Schedule and run the IMC elements","Evaluate the program and recommend changes"],"explanation":"Decide what to achieve before whom to reach, whom to reach before how much to spend, the budget before designing the program, the design before scheduling it, and evaluate only after it runs. Touch points inform step 2; the PLC informs step 4."},{"id":"a12-03","type":"sort","concept":"c12-paid-owned-earned","title":"Paid, owned, or earned?","prompt":"Sort each digital channel example from the course slide into paid, owned, or earned media.","buckets":["Paid","Owned","Earned"],"items":[{"text":"Search advertising","bucket":0},{"text":"Display banner ads on a news site","bucket":0},{"text":"Email marketing to subscribers","bucket":0},{"text":"Paid influencer posts","bucket":0},{"text":"Mobile in-app advertising","bucket":0},{"text":"Company website","bucket":1},{"text":"Brand's own YouTube channel","bucket":1},{"text":"Brand's mobile app","bucket":1},{"text":"Company Facebook page","bucket":1},{"text":"Customer reviews on Yelp or Amazon","bucket":2},{"text":"Public relations","bucket":2},{"text":"News media coverage of a launch","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Paid = the company buys the placement (including email marketing and paid influencers, per the course slide). Owned = channels the company controls (its website, its own social pages and channels, its apps). Earned = exposure created by others (reviews, PR, media coverage)."},{"id":"a12-04","type":"sort","concept":"c12-push-pull","title":"Push tool or pull tool?","prompt":"Sort each promotional tool from Figure 12-2 into the strategy it supports.","buckets":["Push (aimed at the channel)","Pull (aimed at consumers)"],"items":[{"text":"Trade allowances","bucket":0},{"text":"Trade shows","bucket":0},{"text":"Merchandising allowances","bucket":0},{"text":"Co-op advertising funds","bucket":0},{"text":"Incentives and training for retailers' sales staff","bucket":0},{"text":"Advertising","bucket":1},{"text":"Publicity","bucket":1},{"text":"Consumer promotion (coupons, samples)","bucket":1},{"text":"Product placement","bucket":1},{"text":"Event marketing","bucket":1},{"text":"Social media and sponsorship","bucket":1}],"explanation":"Push tools are trade promotions and selling efforts aimed at distributors, wholesalers, and retailers to win listings, displays, feature pricing, and flyer space. Pull tools are aimed at consumers, media, and the public to build awareness, trial, loyalty, and advocacy. Personal selling appears in both columns, so it is left out here."},{"id":"a12-05","type":"match","concept":"c12-regulators","title":"Who regulates what?","prompt":"Match each regulatory body with what it does.","pairs":[{"left":"Advertising Standards Canada (ASC)","right":"Industry self-regulation; administers the Canadian Code of Advertising Standards"},{"left":"Competition Bureau","right":"Enforces the Competition Act, including misleading advertising and deceptive marketing"},{"left":"CRTC","right":"Regulates broadcasting and telecommunications; enforces anti-spam legislation"},{"left":"Canadian Marketing Association (CMA)","right":"Marketers' association with a code of ethics and standards of practice"},{"left":"Canadian Wireless Telecommunications Association (CWTA)","right":"Represents Canada's wireless (mobile) industry"},{"left":"Mobile Marketing Association (MMA)","right":"Sets guidelines and best practices for mobile marketing"}],"explanation":"ASC is voluntary industry self-regulation through its Code, while the Competition Bureau enforces federal law. The CRTC is the broadcasting and telecom regulator, the CMA is the marketers' ethics body, and the CWTA and MMA cover the wireless and mobile space."},{"id":"a12-06","type":"match","concept":"c12-plc-promotion","title":"PLC stage to promotional focus","prompt":"Match each product life cycle stage with its promotional objective and signature activities (Figure 12-4).","pairs":[{"left":"Introduction","right":"To inform: magazine publicity, advertising, salesforce calling on intermediaries, free samples"},{"left":"Growth","right":"To persuade: personal selling to intermediaries, advertising that differentiates from competing brands"},{"left":"Maturity","right":"To remind: reminder advertising, discounts, coupons and events, limited personal selling, direct-mail reminders"},{"left":"Decline","right":"To phase out: little money spent on promotion"}],"explanation":"The promotional objective moves from inform to persuade to remind to phase out as the product ages, and the tools change with it: samples when new, differentiation when rivals arrive, reminders and deals to keep buyers in maturity, and minimal spending in decline."}],"questions":[{"id":"q12-001","concept":"c12-communication-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Brightwater Bank's creative team takes the idea 'banking that respects your time' and turns it into a 15-second video of a customer finishing a mortgage application on her phone before her coffee cools. In the communication process, this translation of an idea into words and images is:","options":["decoding","encoding","feedback","the channel"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encoding is the sender's job of transforming an idea into symbols such as words, pictures, and sounds. Decoding is the opposite step, done by the receiver when interpreting those symbols, so it doesn't fit the sender's creative team.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-002","concept":"c12-communication-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A viewer in Edmonton watches Brightwater Bank's video and concludes, 'This bank will approve my mortgage quickly without a branch visit.' The viewer's interpretation of the ad's symbols is called:","options":["encoding","noise","decoding","field of experience"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Decoding is the receiver taking the sender's symbols and transforming them back into an idea. Encoding is done by the sender, not the viewer; field of experience is the shared understanding that makes accurate decoding possible, not the act of interpreting itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-003","concept":"c12-communication-process","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Prairie Roast Coffee buys a 30-second spot on a Regina morning radio show. In the communication process, the radio station is the _____.","options":["channel","source","receiver","message"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The channel is the means of conveying the message, here the radio station's broadcast. Prairie Roast is the source (sender), the listeners are the receivers, and the ad's content is the message.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-004","concept":"c12-communication-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northline Outfitters creates and pays for a campaign in which the company, through its spokesperson, tells Canadians its parkas are rated to -40. In the communication process, Northline is the:","options":["receiver","channel","message","source"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The source (sender) is the company or person who has information to communicate: Northline and its spokesperson. The channel is the medium carrying the message (TV, Instagram), not the organization behind it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-005","concept":"c12-communication-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the communication process, the receiver is best described as:","options":["the consumers who read, hear, or see the message","the medium, such as TV or a salesperson, that carries the message","the company that encodes and sends the message","the information the sender wants to transmit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The course defines receivers as the consumers who read, hear, or see the message. The medium that carries it is the channel, the company sending it is the source, and the information itself is the message.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-006","concept":"c12-communication-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Juniper Telecom posts an in-app notice reading 'Unlimited data now $10 cheaper' for its customers. Which option correctly maps the communication-process elements?","options":["Source: the app; message: Juniper; channel: the customers; receiver: the offer","Source: Juniper; message: the app; channel: the offer; receiver: customers who read it","Source: Juniper; message: the offer; channel: the app; receiver: customers who read it","Source: the customers; message: the offer; channel: Juniper; receiver: the app"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Juniper is the source (sender), the $10-cheaper offer is the message (information to be transmitted), the app is the channel that carries it, and customers who read it are the receivers. The most tempting error is calling the app the message; the app is the vehicle, not the content.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-007","concept":"c12-communication-process","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the course slide, communication requires all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["a source and a receiver","a channel of communication","a promotional budget","the processes of encoding and decoding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The slide lists a source, a message, a channel, a receiver, and the processes of encoding and decoding. A promotional budget is step 3 of the marketing communications planning process, not an element of communication itself.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-008","concept":"c12-feedback-noise-experience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a playoff broadcast, a regional cable glitch turns GlacierGear's commercial into pixelated video with garbled audio. In the communication process, the glitch is an example of:","options":["feedback","noise","selective inattention","decoding"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Noise is any extraneous factor that distorts the message or the feedback, and technical interference is a textbook case. Selective inattention is a receiver tuning out a message that arrives clearly; here the message itself was distorted.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-009","concept":"c12-feedback-noise-experience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"After Prairie Pantry posts a new recipe reel, hundreds of followers comment with questions, share it, and click through to buy the featured spice blend. In the communication process, these responses back to the company are:","options":["decoding","the channel","noise","feedback"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Feedback is the receiver's response communicated back to the sender; comments, clicks, and purchases all tell Prairie Pantry how the message landed. Decoding happens inside the receiver's head before any response is sent back.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-010","concept":"c12-feedback-noise-experience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Maple Crest Insurance translated its slogan 'We've got your back' word for word into another language for a newcomer audience; many readers understood it as an ad about back-injury coverage. The breakdown occurred mainly because sender and receivers lacked a shared:","options":["field of experience","channel","feedback loop","personal bias"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Field of experience is the common understanding (language, culture, knowledge) sender and receiver need for a message to be decoded as intended, and a literal idiom translation breaks it. Personal bias is a barrier in which receivers understand a message but reject it because of their values; these readers simply decoded it differently.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-011","concept":"c12-feedback-noise-experience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Extraneous factors that work against effective communication by distorting a message or the feedback received are called:","options":["selective inexposure","field of experience","noise","decoding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"That is the definition of noise. Field of experience refers to the shared understanding between sender and receiver, and selective inexposure is a barrier in which people avoid messages altogether.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-012","concept":"c12-feedback-noise-experience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which situation reflects a field-of-experience problem rather than noise?","options":["A bus-shelter poster for a gym is half covered by graffiti","A TV ad's soundtrack is drowned out by a crying baby in the living room","A printed flyer contains a typo in the store's phone number","A podcast ad for an investing app uses crypto slang its retired listeners don't understand"],"answer":3,"explanation":"When the message arrives intact but the receiver lacks the knowledge to interpret it (unfamiliar jargon), sender and receiver don't share a field of experience. Graffiti, background crying, and typos are noise: factors that distort the message in transmission.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-013","concept":"c12-feedback-noise-experience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is feedback valuable to the sender in the communication process?","options":["It shows whether the message was received and understood as intended","It removes noise from the channel before the message is sent","It translates the sender's idea into words and images","It guarantees that sender and receiver share a field of experience"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Feedback (responses, questions, purchases) lets the sender judge whether the receiver decoded the message as intended and adjust. Translating an idea into symbols is encoding, which happens before the message is sent, not after.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-014","concept":"c12-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Jonah pays extra for the ad-free tier of his streaming service and runs an ad blocker on his laptop so commercials never reach him. Which barrier to effective communication does this illustrate?","options":["selective inattention","selective inexposure","poor awareness","noise"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Selective inexposure means avoiding exposure to messages altogether; ad-free subscriptions and ad blockers keep ads from ever reaching Jonah. Selective inattention would mean he sees the ads but tunes them out.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-015","concept":"c12-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Every morning, Priya scrolls her social feed. Sponsored posts appear on her screen, but she glides past them without registering a single brand name. This barrier is:","options":["selective inexposure","personal bias","noise","selective inattention"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Selective inattention occurs when people are exposed to a message but don't pay attention to it. Priya isn't avoiding exposure (inexposure), since the posts are right on her screen; she simply tunes them out.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-016","concept":"c12-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lena has been vegan for ten years. When a dairy marketing board's ad claims milk is the best source of calcium, she dismisses the claim immediately because it conflicts with her principles. Which barrier is at work?","options":["personal bias","selective inattention","field of experience","poor awareness"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Personal bias (e.g., principles) leads receivers to reject a message because it conflicts with their beliefs. Lena understood the ad perfectly, so it isn't a field-of-experience problem, and she paid attention, so it isn't inattention.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-017","concept":"c12-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"HushPod, a new Winnipeg startup, launched premium earbuds with superior noise cancelling. Its message isn't getting through because most shoppers have never heard of the brand and don't know the product exists. The main barrier is:","options":["selective inexposure","poor awareness","personal bias","selective inattention"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Poor awareness means the audience doesn't know the brand or product, so messages have nothing to attach to. Nothing suggests shoppers are actively avoiding the ads (inexposure) or rejecting them on principle (bias).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-018","concept":"c12-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What is the key difference between selective inexposure and selective inattention?","options":["Inexposure is tuning out a message; inattention is avoiding it altogether","Inexposure stems from personal principles; inattention stems from low brand awareness","Inexposure is avoiding the message altogether; inattention is being exposed but tuning it out","Inexposure is caused by the sender's encoding; inattention by the receiver's decoding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With selective inexposure the message never reaches the person because they avoid it (ad blockers, ad-free plans); with selective inattention it reaches them but they don't pay attention. Defining inexposure as tuning out and inattention as avoiding reverses the two, which is exactly the trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-019","concept":"c12-barriers","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four common barriers to effective communication listed in the course?","options":["poor awareness","selective inattention","personal bias","selective retention"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The four barriers are poor awareness, selective inattention, selective inexposure, and personal bias. Selective retention is a perception concept from consumer behaviour (remembering only some information), not one of the four communication barriers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-020","concept":"c12-barriers","also":["c12-inbound-outbound"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Regina meal-kit company learns that its target customers block banner ads and pay for ad-free streaming. Which response best works around this selective inexposure?","options":["Create useful recipe content that customers find through search and social on their own","Double the number of banner ads so customers see them more often","Rerun the same pre-roll video ads with a louder soundtrack","Lower prices and announce the discount in banner and pre-roll ads"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When people avoid exposure to ads, more of the same ads won't reach them; inbound content that is seen in the right places (search, social) lets customers come to the brand. Doubling banner ads fails because ad blockers keep those ads from ever appearing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-021","concept":"c12-marketing-communications","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Kotler and Keller define marketing communications as the means by which firms attempt to inform, _____, and remind consumers about the products and brands they sell.","options":["segment","persuade","price","distribute"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The three purposes are inform, persuade, and remind. Segmenting, pricing, and distributing are other marketing activities (STP and the 4 Ps), not purposes of marketing communications.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-022","concept":"c12-marketing-communications","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over one year, Arctic Spring Water ran ads announcing its new sparkling line, then ads arguing it tastes better than rival brands, then short 'still here, still refreshing' spots. These three kinds of messages reflect:","options":["awareness, interest, and evaluation: the hierarchy of effects","balance, consistency, and metrics: the issues in IMC","inform, persuade, and remind: the purposes of marketing communications","encode, transmit, and decode: the communication process"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Announcing (inform), arguing superiority (persuade), and 'still here' spots (remind) match the Kotler/Keller definition of marketing communications. The hierarchy of effects describes stages a buyer goes through, not the purposes of a firm's messages.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-023","concept":"c12-marketing-communications","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A pharmacy chain's flyer announces that its flu-shot clinics open next week and explains who is eligible and how to book. Which purpose of marketing communications does this message mainly serve?","options":["remind","persuade","phase out","inform"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Telling people about something new (dates, eligibility, how to book) is informing. Persuading would mean arguing why to choose this pharmacy over competitors, which the flyer doesn't do.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-024","concept":"c12-marketing-communications","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does integrated marketing communications (IMC) differ from marketing communications in general?","options":["Marketing communications informs, persuades, and reminds; IMC coordinates all of those activities to deliver one consistent message","Marketing communications uses only advertising and publicity; IMC adds personal selling and sales promotion to the mix","IMC targets only intermediaries such as retailers, while marketing communications targets only end consumers","IMC is the regulatory code that governs all marketing communications and advertising claims in Canada"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Marketing communications is the broad means of informing, persuading, and reminding; IMC is the concept of coordinating all promotional activities so they provide a consistent message. IMC audiences can be consumers, intermediaries, or both, so saying IMC targets only intermediaries is wrong.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-025","concept":"c12-marketing-communications","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three purposes in the course's definition of marketing communications?","options":["inform","remind","regulate","persuade"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Marketing communications attempt to inform, persuade, and remind consumers. Regulating marketing communications is the job of bodies such as Advertising Standards Canada and the Competition Bureau, not a purpose of the communications themselves.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-026","concept":"c12-imc","also":["c12-promotional-mix","c12-evaluation"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"In integrated marketing communications, what is a key challenge to address when putting together the promotional mix?","options":["Focusing solely on the most cost-effective promotional element","Ignoring the stage of the product's life cycle and the characteristics of the product","Limiting the promotional mix to only one or two traditional methods","Coordinating a consistent promotional effort across various departments responsible for different elements"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Because the various promotional elements are often the responsibility of different departments, coordinating a consistent promotional effort is a key IMC challenge. Focusing on only the cheapest element or ignoring the PLC contradicts IMC, which balances the elements and considers the product's life cycle.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-027","concept":"c12-imc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Bow River Ski Resort makes sure its TV spots, Instagram reels, ticket-window signage, email newsletter, and even staff greetings all use the same 'Your mountain, your pace' theme and visual style. This approach is the essence of:","options":["storytelling","integrated marketing communications","a push strategy","direct marketing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"IMC coordinates all promotional activities to provide a consistent message across audiences, which is exactly what Bow River does across every element. A push strategy targets channel members such as retailers, not the resort's own customers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-028","concept":"c12-imc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about IMC is NOT consistent with the course?","options":["Each department should develop its own distinct message for the element it controls","The marketer must consider the balance of the promotional elements","The target audience can be consumers, intermediaries, or both","Programs should rely on metrics and analytics to measure success and make improvements"],"answer":0,"explanation":"IMC requires all promotional activities to be coordinated to provide one consistent message, so separate departmental messages contradict it. Balance of elements, consumer and/or intermediary audiences, and reliance on metrics are all stated in the course.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-029","concept":"c12-imc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the course, why must all marketing communications rely on metrics and analytics?","options":["To satisfy the Competition Bureau's campaign-reporting requirements","To replace the need to set objectives before a campaign","To measure and evaluate success and make improvements","To ensure every promotional element receives an equal share of the budget"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The slide says marketing communications must work together and rely on metrics and analytics to measure and evaluate success and make improvements. Metrics don't replace objectives; objectives (step 1) are the yardstick the metrics are measured against.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-030","concept":"c12-imc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A marketer must consider two IMC issues: balance and consistency. Which decision is mainly about BALANCE rather than consistency?","options":["Making sure the slogan on the package matches the slogan in TV ads","Training customer-service reps to repeat the campaign's key promise","Ensuring the website uses the same colours and tone as the flyers","Deciding how much weight to give advertising versus personal selling versus sales promotion"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Balance is about the proportions of each promotional element in the mix. Matching slogans, colours, and service scripts are all about providing a consistent message across elements, which is the other IMC issue (consistency).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-031","concept":"c12-promotional-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The combination of advertising, personal selling, public relations, sales promotion, and direct marketing that a firm uses to communicate with its audiences is called the:","options":["marketing mix","promotional mix","product mix","media schedule"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The promotional (marketing communications) mix is the set of communication tools. The marketing mix is broader, covering all 4 Ps, with promotion as only one of them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-032","concept":"c12-promotional-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which promotional elements are classified as customized rather than mass forms of communication?","options":["advertising and sales promotion","public relations and advertising","personal selling and direct marketing","sales promotion and publicity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Personal selling and direct marketing tailor messages to specific individuals, so they are customized. Advertising, sales promotion, and public relations send the same message to large audiences, so they are mass elements.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-033","concept":"c12-promotional-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Coastal Bike Co. answers warranty claims and repair questions on its live-chat line within minutes and follows up after every service visit, treating each exchange as a chance to reinforce its brand promise. In the course's IMC components figure, this is:","options":["customer service","personal selling","direct marketing","public relations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The course's IMC components figure includes customer service as a communication element, since every support interaction carries the brand's message. Personal selling is two-way communication designed to influence a purchase decision, whereas these post-purchase warranty and repair exchanges are service.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-034","concept":"c12-promotional-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the components shown in the course's 'Concept of Integrated Marketing Communications' figure?","options":["Sponsorship","Email","Customer service","Pricing strategy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The figure shows advertising, PR/publicity, customer service, direct marketing, personal selling, sponsorship, digital/social media, email, and sales promotion. Pricing strategy belongs to a separate P of the marketing mix, not the communication components.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-035","concept":"c12-promotional-mix","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why do most marketers combine several promotional elements instead of relying on just one?","options":["The Competition Bureau requires every Canadian campaign to use at least two different promotional elements","Each element has distinct strengths and weaknesses, so combining them balances reach, cost, credibility, and feedback","Mass elements cannot legally carry a brand message unless customized elements support them at the same time","Customized elements always cost less per contact than mass elements, so adding them lowers total spending"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Advertising gives reach and control but little feedback, personal selling gives feedback but costs a lot per contact, and publicity gives credibility but little control, so a balanced mix offsets weaknesses. Customized elements actually cost MORE per contact, so the claim that they always cost less is wrong.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-036","concept":"c12-advertising","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Any paid form of non-personal communication about an organization, good, service, or idea by an identified sponsor is:","options":["publicity","sales promotion","advertising","direct marketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Paid, non-personal, and an identified sponsor are the three hallmarks of advertising. Publicity is also non-personal, but it is indirectly paid and not controlled by an identified sponsor.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-037","concept":"c12-advertising","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Rocky Ridge Motors pays a Calgary radio station to air a 30-second spot, written by its own team, that names the dealership and urges listeners to visit this weekend. This is an example of:","options":["advertising","publicity","personal selling","direct marketing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rocky Ridge pays for the airtime, controls the message, and is identified as the sponsor, which makes it advertising. It isn't publicity, because the station was paid and didn't choose to cover the dealership as news.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-038","concept":"c12-advertising","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What best distinguishes advertising from publicity?","options":["Advertising is two-way, personal communication; publicity is one-way, non-personal communication","Advertising is a short-term inducement of value; publicity builds long-term relationships with stakeholders","Advertising can be used only in the introduction stage; publicity can be used in any stage of the life cycle","Advertising is paid by an identified sponsor who controls the message; publicity is indirectly paid and largely uncontrolled"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both are non-personal, but advertising space is bought by an identified sponsor who controls the content, while publicity (such as a news story) is indirectly paid and the firm has little control. A short-term inducement describes sales promotion, not advertising.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-039","concept":"c12-advertising","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is a commonly cited weakness of advertising as a promotional element?","options":["Little control over what the message says","High absolute cost and difficulty getting good feedback","Tendency to trigger promotion and price wars","Very high cost per contact because each contact is one-to-one"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Advertising's strengths are reach and control; its weaknesses are high absolute cost and difficulty getting feedback. Little control is publicity's weakness, price wars are sales promotion's, and very high cost per contact is personal selling's.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-040","concept":"c12-advertising","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of advertising?","options":["It provides immediate two-way feedback","It is paid for","It is non-personal","It has an identified sponsor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Advertising is paid, non-personal communication by an identified sponsor; because it is one-way mass communication, feedback is hard to get. Immediate two-way feedback is the main strength of personal selling.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-041","concept":"c12-personal-selling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The two-way flow of communication between a buyer and seller, designed to influence a person's or group's purchase decision, is:","options":["direct marketing","personal selling","customer service","public relations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A two-way flow designed to influence a purchase decision defines personal selling. Direct marketing is also customized, but it sends targeted messages to generate a response rather than holding a live two-way dialogue.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-042","concept":"c12-personal-selling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A rep from Lakehead Medical Supply visits a Thunder Bay clinic, asks about patient volumes, demonstrates a new ultrasound unit, and reworks her proposal on the spot when the doctor raises budget concerns. This is:","options":["direct marketing","sales promotion","event marketing","personal selling"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A live, two-way exchange in which the seller listens, demonstrates, and adapts the pitch to influence a purchase is personal selling. Direct marketing would send targeted mail, email, or calls seeking a response, without this tailored face-to-face dialogue.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-043","concept":"c12-personal-selling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the main strength of personal selling compared with other promotional elements?","options":["It has the lowest cost per contact of any element","It is the most credible because the firm doesn't pay for it","It offers immediate feedback and lets the message be tailored to each buyer","It reaches a mass audience with one identical message"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Personal selling is customized and two-way, so the seller gets immediate feedback and can persuade by tailoring the message. It does not have the lowest cost per contact; its main weakness is an extremely high cost per contact.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-044","concept":"c12-personal-selling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Personal selling and direct marketing are both customized elements. Which statement correctly contrasts them?","options":["Personal selling is a live two-way dialogue with immediate feedback; direct marketing sends targeted communications to generate a response","Personal selling is paid by an identified sponsor; direct marketing is indirectly paid, like publicity in the news","Direct marketing offers the most immediate feedback of any element; personal selling offers the least feedback","Personal selling is a mass element that reaches large audiences; direct marketing is a customized element"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both tailor messages, but personal selling is interactive in real time, while direct marketing (mail, catalogues, email, telemarketing) reaches targeted individuals and asks them to respond. Personal selling is customized, not a mass element, so the statement calling it mass is wrong.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-045","concept":"c12-personal-selling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Glacier Systems sells $250,000 custom water-treatment systems to Canadian municipalities. Each buyer has unique technical requirements and a committee that raises detailed questions. Which promotional element should carry the most weight in its mix?","options":["advertising","sales promotion","publicity","personal selling"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Complex, high-priced, customized B2B purchases call for personal selling, which allows two-way discussion, immediate feedback, and tailored solutions; the high cost per contact is justified by the deal size. Advertising reaches many people but can't answer each committee's technical questions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-046","concept":"c12-sales-promotion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A short-term inducement of value offered to arouse interest in buying a good or service is:","options":["sales promotion","advertising","direct marketing","personal selling"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sales promotion is defined as a short-term inducement of value, such as a coupon, sample, contest, or rebate. Advertising communicates a message but doesn't itself add short-term value to trigger the purchase.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-047","concept":"c12-sales-promotion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"For two weeks only, Northern Pantry offers buy-one-get-one-free on its granola bars and hands out $1-off coupons at store entrances in Red Deer. This is an example of:","options":["public relations","sales promotion","advertising","personal selling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Time-limited deals like BOGO offers and coupons are short-term inducements of value, which is sales promotion. Advertising would deliver a paid message about the brand without the added incentive.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-048","concept":"c12-sales-promotion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which is a key weakness of sales promotion?","options":["It cannot change buying behaviour in the short run","It has an extremely high cost per contact","It gives the firm little control over the message","It can be easily abused and can lead to promotion and price wars"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Sales promotion is effective at changing behaviour in the short run, but it is easily abused (and copied) and can spark promotion wars. Little control over the message is publicity's weakness, and high cost per contact belongs to personal selling.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-049","concept":"c12-sales-promotion","also":["c12-push-pull"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts consumer sales promotion with trade promotion?","options":["Consumer promotions such as allowances target retailers; trade promotions such as coupons target shoppers in the store","Trade promotions are a form of publicity earned from trade media; consumer promotions are a form of paid advertising","Consumer promotions such as coupons and samples target end users (pull); trade promotions such as allowances and co-op funds target channel members (push)","Consumer promotions are used only in the decline stage; trade promotions are used only in the introduction stage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Figure 12-2 places trade promotions (trade allowances, trade shows, merchandising allowances, co-op advertising funds) in the push column aimed at the channel, and consumer promotion in the pull column aimed at consumers. Saying consumer promotions (allowances) target retailers and trade promotions (coupons) target shoppers simply reverses the two audiences.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-050","concept":"c12-sales-promotion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a form of sales promotion?","options":["a mail-in rebate","a newspaper story about the product's launch","a free in-store sample","a social media contest with prizes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rebates, samples, and contests are short-term inducements of value, which makes them sales promotion. A newspaper story the firm didn't pay for is publicity, part of public relations.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-051","concept":"c12-public-relations","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A non-personal, indirectly paid presentation of an organization, good, or service, such as a news story, is called:","options":["advertising","product placement","sponsorship","publicity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Publicity is non-personal and indirectly paid: the firm doesn't buy the media space. Advertising and product placement are directly paid, and sponsorship involves paying a fee for association with an event.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-052","concept":"c12-public-relations","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After Ember Stoves donated 200 wood stoves to families displaced by northern Alberta wildfires, CBC News ran a feature about the donation. Ember paid nothing to the broadcaster for the coverage. The coverage is:","options":["publicity","advertising","sponsorship","sales promotion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Unpaid news coverage of a company's actions is publicity, a public relations outcome (and earned media). It isn't advertising, because Ember didn't buy the airtime or control the story.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-053","concept":"c12-public-relations","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pairing correctly states a strength and a weakness of publicity?","options":["Strength: complete control of the message; weakness: low credibility","Strength: high credibility; weakness: little control over the message","Strength: immediate two-way feedback; weakness: high cost per contact","Strength: changes behaviour in the short run; weakness: can trigger price wars"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because publicity comes from an independent source such as a journalist, it is highly credible, but the firm can't control what is said or whether it runs. Immediate feedback with high cost per contact describes personal selling.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-054","concept":"c12-public-relations","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A skincare brand pays a magazine to run a two-page, article-style feature clearly labelled 'Sponsored content.' Why is this advertising rather than publicity?","options":["Because it appears in a magazine, which is a paid medium, rather than on television","Because it is non-personal communication delivered to a large audience at once","Because the brand paid for the space and, as the identified sponsor, controls the content","Because it is long enough to tell a detailed story about the brand's products"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Advertising is paid communication by an identified sponsor who controls the message, so buying labelled space makes this advertising. Being non-personal doesn't decide it, because publicity is non-personal too.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-055","concept":"c12-public-relations","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT typically a public relations activity?","options":["Issuing a news release about a new product","Holding a press conference to announce a factory opening","Managing stakeholders' opinions during a product recall","Offering a mail-in rebate on a new appliance"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PR manages the feelings, opinions, and beliefs of customers and other stakeholders through tools like news releases and press conferences. A mail-in rebate is a short-term inducement of value, which is sales promotion.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-056","concept":"c12-direct-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Using direct communication with consumers to generate a response (an order, a request for more information, or a store visit) is called:","options":["inbound marketing","personal selling","direct marketing","advertising"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Direct marketing's defining feature is direct communication aimed at generating a measurable response. Advertising speaks to a mass audience without a direct response mechanism tied to individuals.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-057","concept":"c12-direct-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ridgeline Garden Supply mails its spring catalogue to past customers, follows up with an email containing a personal order code, and counts how many orders each code generates. This is:","options":["direct marketing","public relations","advertising","personal selling"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Catalogues and targeted email asking for a trackable response are classic direct marketing tools, and the order codes show its measurability. There is no live two-way conversation, so it isn't personal selling.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-058","concept":"c12-direct-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which description best captures the strengths and weaknesses of direct marketing?","options":["Highly credible but hard to control","Reaches mass audiences cheaply but gives little feedback","Changes behaviour quickly but invites price wars","Targeted and measurable, but costly and raises privacy concerns"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Direct marketing reaches specific individuals and its responses can be counted, but building and maintaining databases is costly and consumers worry about privacy. 'Highly credible but hard to control' describes publicity.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-059","concept":"c12-direct-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a direct marketing tool?","options":["telemarketing","a press release","direct response TV","a mailed catalogue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Direct mail, catalogues, telemarketing, direct response TV, and email are direct marketing tools that seek a response. A press release is a public relations tool aimed at earning media coverage.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-060","concept":"c12-direct-marketing","also":["c12-paid-owned-earned"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Strata Sneakers sends promotional emails to its opted-in subscriber list. How is this classified in the course materials?","options":["It is a direct marketing tool and, on the digital channels slide, paid media","It is personal selling and, on the digital channels slide, owned media","It is public relations and, on the digital channels slide, earned media","It is sales promotion and, on the digital channels slide, owned media"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Email is one of the direct marketing tools in the textbook, and the Keller/Swaminathan digital channels slide lists e-mail marketing under PAID media. Many students pick owned because the list belongs to the company, but the course slide classifies email marketing as paid.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-061","concept":"c12-sponsorship-placement","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a hit Canadian streaming comedy, the main characters always drink Polar Peak iced tea with the label clearly visible; Polar Peak paid the producers for this exposure. This is:","options":["sponsorship","publicity","product placement","event marketing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Paid inclusion of a brand within entertainment content is product placement. Sponsorship would mean paying to attach the brand's name to an event, team, or cause, such as a festival stage.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-062","concept":"c12-sponsorship-placement","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Summit Credit Union pays a fee to have its name on the Edmonton Folk Festival's main stage and its logo on all festival materials. This is:","options":["product placement","sponsorship","publicity","sales promotion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Paying a fee to be associated with someone else's event in exchange for exposure is sponsorship. Product placement puts a brand inside entertainment content such as a show or movie, not on an event stage.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-063","concept":"c12-sponsorship-placement","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What best distinguishes event marketing from sponsorship?","options":["Event marketing builds an experience that brings consumers and the brand together; sponsorship pays a fee for association with an event, team, or cause","Event marketing is always unpaid publicity earned through news coverage; sponsorship is always paid advertising bought in mass media","Event marketing targets channel members such as retailers; sponsorship targets ultimate consumers, media, and the public","Event marketing is used only in the decline stage to clear stock; sponsorship is used only during the introduction stage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Event marketing puts the brand at the centre of an experience (often one the brand creates, like a pop-up), while sponsorship buys association with an event or property someone else owns. Both are pull tools aimed at consumers in Figure 12-2, so saying event marketing targets channel members is wrong.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-064","concept":"c12-sponsorship-placement","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do marketers use product placement?","options":["It guarantees the brand full control over how, when, and how often it appears on screen","It persuades retailers and wholesalers to give the brand more shelf space and feature pricing","It is required by the CRTC for any brand that also advertises on Canadian television","It shows the brand within entertainment people choose to watch, which feels less intrusive than a commercial"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Product placement reaches consumers inside content they've chosen to watch, so it can get past ad avoidance and feels natural. It does not guarantee control, since producers decide how the scene plays, and persuading retailers is a push objective.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-065","concept":"c12-sponsorship-placement","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of product placement?","options":["A laptop brand used by the hero in a Canadian film, paid for by the maker","A car brand driven by characters in a racing video game under a paid deal","A bank's logo painted on the boards of a hockey arena it pays to sponsor","A paid soft-drink can placed on the desk of a late-night talk-show host"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Product placement is paid inclusion of a brand inside entertainment content. A logo on arena boards in exchange for a fee is sponsorship (signage at a sponsored venue), not placement within a show, film, or game.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-066","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Laila purchases groceries from FoodMart. FoodMart offers her a membership card, which entitles Laila to coupons, lucky draws, and discounts on her continued purchases at FoodMart. Which of the following tools is FoodMart using to drive the customer through the customer advocacy funnel?","options":["Engagement","Loyalty","Trial","Awareness"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Customer loyalty is encouraged through CRM programs that reward continued purchases, so a membership card with coupons, draws, and discounts puts FoodMart in the loyalty stage. Engagement would involve inviting her to participate in the product experience and interact with its marketing, not rewarding repeat buying.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-067","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"At a Vancouver mall pop-up, Alpine Audio invites passers-by to step into a soundproof booth, remix a track using its headphones, and post the result to the brand's social media challenge. Which advocacy funnel stage is Alpine targeting?","options":["Trial","Interest","Engagement","Advocacy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Engagement invites potential customers to participate in the product experience and interact with its marketing: remixing in the booth and joining a branded challenge. Trial would mean obtaining a free sample or getting the product as a limited trial or download.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-068","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Zenith Meditation lets new users download its app and use every premium feature free for 14 days before any billing begins. Which stage of the customer advocacy funnel does this offer target?","options":["Trial","Engagement","Purchase","Interest"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Trial is when customers obtain free samples or get the product as a limited trial or download. Purchase comes next, when positive experiences during the trial lead users to pay.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-069","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nomad Coffee gives its longest-standing subscribers early access to rare roasts and a behind-the-scenes farm tour. Many then post glowing recommendations and refer friends. Which funnel stage do these actions reflect?","options":["Loyalty","Engagement","Purchase","Advocacy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In the advocacy stage, loyal customers are rewarded with additional experiences and become advocates who recommend the product to others. Loyalty is repeat purchasing; the recommending is what marks advocacy.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-070","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Trailhead Tents uses search engine optimization, online video, and display ads to drive people who have never heard of it to its website. Which advocacy funnel stage is this?","options":["Interest","Awareness","Engagement","Trial"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The course's awareness example is a company using a website, SEO, online video, and display ads to drive consumers to an online destination. Interest comes after people know the brand, when attributes are highlighted so they want to learn more.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-071","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"On its product page, KettleCraft highlights its kettle's 60-second boil time and stay-cool handle so curious visitors want to learn more. Which funnel stage is this designed for?","options":["Awareness","Purchase","Interest","Engagement"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Interest is the stage where interesting product attributes are highlighted to prompt potential customers to learn more. Engagement goes further, inviting people to participate in the product experience and interact with marketing.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-072","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a two-week free trial, Mei enjoys Zest Meal Kits so much that she pays for her first full month of deliveries. Which advocacy funnel stage has Mei just reached?","options":["Loyalty","Trial","Advocacy","Purchase"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Purchase is the stage where positive product experiences (here, during the trial) lead to buying the product. Loyalty comes later, when ongoing positive experiences lead to repeat purchases over time.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-073","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which sequence correctly lists the LAST four stages of the customer advocacy funnel, in order?","options":["purchase, trial, loyalty, advocacy","trial, purchase, advocacy, loyalty","engagement, trial, loyalty, purchase","trial, purchase, loyalty, advocacy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The full funnel is awareness, interest, engagement, trial, purchase, loyalty, advocacy. Trial comes before purchase, and loyalty (repeat purchase) comes before advocacy (recommending to others).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-074","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What distinguishes the loyalty stage from the advocacy stage of the customer advocacy funnel?","options":["Loyalty is repeat purchasing driven by ongoing positive experiences; advocacy is loyal customers recommending the product to others","Loyalty is obtaining free samples or a limited trial; advocacy is making the first full-price purchase","Loyalty is recommending the product to friends and family; advocacy is buying the product again and again","Loyalty is the final stage of the funnel and comes after advocacy, once customers stop recommending"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Ongoing positive experiences lead to repeat purchases (loyalty), often encouraged by CRM programs; loyal customers who are rewarded with additional experiences then recommend the product (advocacy). Calling loyalty 'recommending to friends' and advocacy 'buying again and again' swaps the two definitions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-075","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a stage of the customer advocacy funnel?","options":["Engagement","Trial","Evaluation","Loyalty"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The funnel's seven stages are awareness, interest, engagement, trial, purchase, loyalty, and advocacy. Evaluation belongs to the hierarchy of effects (awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-076","concept":"c12-advocacy-funnel","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Halifax skincare brand's online quiz and video tutorials attract many active participants, but analytics show few go on to buy because they're unsure the product suits their skin. According to the advocacy funnel, which tactic targets the NEXT stage?","options":["Run more display ads to build awareness","Launch a points program that rewards repeat purchases","Offer free trial-size samples matched to quiz results","Give referral rewards to customers who recommend the brand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Customers are already at engagement (participating and interacting), so the next stage is trial; free samples let them test the product before buying. A points program targets loyalty, which only makes sense after people have purchased.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-077","concept":"c12-push-pull","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ginger Inc. is focusing its communication efforts to build awareness and create demand for its products amongst consumers. Ginger, Inc. is adopting a(n):","options":["personal selling strategy","push strategy","social network strategy","pull strategy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A pull strategy focuses communication efforts on ultimate consumers to build awareness, trial, and demand, so consumers pull the product through the channel. A push strategy would aim the effort at distributors, wholesalers, and retailers instead.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-078","concept":"c12-push-pull","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"A tech company develops a new smartphone and decides to promote it by offering incentives and training to mobile phone retailers and sales staff. This approach is designed to:","options":["Focus communication efforts on ultimate consumers to build awareness, trial, and demand for a product","Influence the distribution channel to support and sell the product","Create a direct-to-consumer sales channel","Build a direct relationship with customers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Incentives and training for retailers and their sales staff are a push strategy: communication aimed at the distribution channel to gain support through listings, sales, merchandising, feature pricing, and inclusion in flyers. Focusing on ultimate consumers to build awareness, trial, and demand describes a pull strategy, which targets consumers rather than the channel.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-079","concept":"c12-push-pull","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Harvest Gold Foods offers grocery chains a trade allowance, pays for end-aisle displays, and contributes co-op advertising funds so retailers feature its new soup in their weekly flyers. Harvest Gold is using:","options":["a push strategy","a pull strategy","inbound communications","earned media"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Trade allowances, merchandising (display) allowances, and co-op advertising funds are trade promotions directed at channel members, which is a push strategy. A pull strategy would aim advertising and consumer promotions at shoppers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-080","concept":"c12-push-pull","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lumen Skincare runs Instagram ads, mails free samples to consumers, and places its serum in a popular YouTube beauty series so shoppers start asking pharmacies to stock it. Lumen is using:","options":["a push strategy","a trade promotion strategy","a pull strategy","a personal selling strategy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Advertising, consumer promotion, and product placement aimed at end consumers create demand that pulls the product through the channel. A push strategy would target the pharmacies themselves with trade promotions and personal selling.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-081","concept":"c12-push-pull","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts push and pull strategies?","options":["Push focuses on consumers, media, and the public; pull focuses on distributors, wholesalers, and retailers","Push focuses on distributors, wholesalers, and retailers; pull focuses on consumers, media, and the public","Push uses only advertising; pull uses only trade shows","Push and pull are two names for the same strategy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Figure 12-2 shows push aimed at the channel (distributors, wholesalers, retailers) and pull aimed at consumers, media, and the public. Saying push focuses on consumers and pull on the channel reverses them, which is the most common trap.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-082","concept":"c12-push-pull","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A brand manager reports that her campaign won new listings at two national chains, better merchandising displays, feature pricing, and inclusion in retailer flyers. These results are associated with:","options":["a pull strategy","the advocacy stage of the funnel","earned media","a push strategy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Figure 12-2 lists listings, sales, merchandising displays, feature pricing, and inclusion in flyers as the results sought by a push strategy. Pull results are consumer outcomes such as awareness, trial, loyalty, and advocacy.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-083","concept":"c12-push-pull","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to Figure 12-2, which result is NOT typically sought by a pull strategy?","options":["trial","awareness","retailer listings","advocacy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pull results are consumer outcomes: awareness, interest, recognition, trial, purchase, loyalty, and advocacy. Retailer listings are a push result, gained by influencing the channel.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-084","concept":"c12-push-pull","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does Figure 12-2 note that push and pull strategies should 'work together'?","options":["Pull-created demand is wasted if stores don't stock the product, and push-won shelf space needs consumer demand to sell through","The Competition Act requires every manufacturer to run both strategies at once whenever it launches a product","Push and pull use exactly the same tools, so running both together adds almost nothing to the budget","A firm can use only one strategy per PLC stage, so it must alternate between push and pull each year"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pull creates consumer demand and push secures distribution and in-store support; each is weaker without the other. The tools differ (trade promotions vs advertising and consumer promotion), so the claim that push and pull use exactly the same tools is wrong.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-085","concept":"c12-push-pull","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"In Figure 12-2, personal selling appears as a tool for both push and pull strategies.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Personal selling is listed in the push column (aimed at channel members) and in the pull column alongside advertising, publicity, consumer promotion, product placement, event marketing, and social media and sponsorship. So 'personal selling' alone doesn't tell you the strategy; look at who is being targeted.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-086","concept":"c12-imc-steps","also":["c12-objectives"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Before launching a new advertising campaign, a company first defines what they hope to achieve, such as increasing brand awareness or boosting sales. This initial step in the marketing communications process is:","options":["specifying the IMC objectives","evaluating the program and recommending changes","identifying the target audience","scheduling and running the IMC elements"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Step 1 of the six-step process is specifying the IMC objectives: deciding what the program should achieve before anything else. Evaluating the program is the final step (6), when results are compared against those objectives.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-087","concept":"c12-imc-steps","also":["c12-target-audience"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before writing any ads, Fjord Outdoor profiles the 25-to-40-year-old urban hikers it wants to reach: their lifestyle, media habits, and every place they encounter the brand. Which step of the marketing communications process is this?","options":["Specifying the IMC objectives","Identifying the target audience","Designing the promotional program","Evaluating the program and recommending changes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Step 2, identifying the target audience, means understanding who you're trying to reach (demographics, lifestyle, media habits) and considering their customer touch points. Designing the program (step 4) uses this knowledge to choose tools and messages.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-088","concept":"c12-imc-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mosaic Mobile's team decides that its phone launch will combine TikTok videos, influencer partnerships, in-store displays, and a sampling event, and develops the creative message for each. Which step of the process is this?","options":["Scheduling and running the IMC elements","Designing the promotional program","Specifying the IMC objectives","Setting the promotional budget"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Choosing which promotional tools to use and developing their messages is designing the promotional program (step 4). Scheduling (step 5) comes after and decides when, how often, and how long each element runs.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-089","concept":"c12-imc-steps","also":["c12-budget-methods"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Saskatoon furniture retailer determines that it will spend $180,000 on promotion next year after costing out the tasks needed to reach its goals. Which step of the marketing communications process is this?","options":["Designing the promotional program","Specifying the IMC objectives","Evaluating the program","Setting the promotional budget"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Deciding how much to spend is step 3, setting the promotional budget, here using the objective-and-task method. Designing the program (step 4) then uses that budget to choose tools and messages.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-090","concept":"c12-imc-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which step immediately precedes 'schedule and run the IMC elements' in the marketing communications process?","options":["Design the promotional program","Set the promotional budget","Evaluate the program and recommend changes","Identify the target audience"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Step 4, designing the promotional program, comes right before step 5, scheduling and running the elements: you must choose the tools before deciding their timing. Setting the budget is step 3.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-091","concept":"c12-imc-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":3,"stem":"In Figure 12-3, customer touch points and the product life cycle are flagged as considerations at which steps, respectively?","options":["Step 1 (objectives) and step 3 (budget)","Step 4 (design the program) and step 2 (target audience)","Step 2 (target audience) and step 4 (design the program)","Step 5 (schedule and run) and step 6 (evaluate)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Customer touch points are considered when identifying the target audience (step 2), and the product life cycle is considered when designing the promotional program (step 4). Pairing touch points with step 4 and the PLC with step 2 reverses them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-092","concept":"c12-imc-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six steps in the marketing communications process?","options":["Set the promotional budget","Select distribution intermediaries","Evaluate the program and recommend changes","Specify the IMC objectives"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Selecting intermediaries is a distribution (place) decision. The six steps are specify objectives, identify the target audience, set the budget, design the program, schedule and run, and evaluate and recommend changes.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-093","concept":"c12-imc-steps","also":["c12-scheduling"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Marketer A is choosing between a continuous and a flighting schedule and fixing the exact weeks each video runs. Marketer B is choosing whether to use influencers or printed flyers to carry the message. Which steps are they in, respectively?","options":["A: designing the promotional program; B: scheduling and running the IMC elements","A: setting the budget; B: identifying the target audience","A: evaluating the program; B: specifying the IMC objectives","A: scheduling and running the IMC elements; B: designing the promotional program"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Timing decisions (continuous vs flighting, which weeks) belong to step 5, scheduling and running; choosing which tools carry the message belongs to step 4, designing the program. Labelling A as designing the program and B as scheduling swaps the two.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-094","concept":"c12-objectives","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Polaris Bank sets a goal to raise unaided awareness of its new no-fee student account from 10% to 30% among Calgary post-secondary students by spring. This is:","options":["a behavioural objective","a communication objective","a promotional schedule","a customer touch point"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Awareness is a communication objective, which builds the foundation for customer relationships. Being measurable (10% to 30%) doesn't make it behavioural; behavioural objectives involve actions such as trial or purchase.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-095","concept":"c12-objectives","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which option correctly classifies these two objectives? (1) Improve consumers' attitudes toward the brand. (2) Increase repeat purchases by 15%.","options":["(1) behavioural; (2) communication","Both are communication objectives","(1) communication; (2) behavioural","Both are behavioural objectives"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Attitudes are a communication objective (laying the foundation for relationships); repeat purchase is an action-oriented behavioural objective. Calling attitudes behavioural and repeat purchases communication reverses them.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-096","concept":"c12-objectives","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the hierarchy of effects, which stage comes between interest and trial?","options":["Evaluation","Engagement","Adoption","Awareness"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The hierarchy of effects runs awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption. Engagement sits between interest and trial in the customer advocacy funnel, a different model and a common mix-up.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-097","concept":"c12-objectives","also":["c12-advocacy-funnel"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a stage in the hierarchy of effects?","options":["Awareness","Adoption","Evaluation","Loyalty"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The hierarchy of effects is awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption. Loyalty is a stage of the customer advocacy funnel, not the hierarchy of effects.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-098","concept":"c12-objectives","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does the course describe communication objectives as laying 'the foundation to build customer relationships'?","options":["Because they are measured in dollars of revenue, which shows directly how much each relationship is worth","Because people generally need to know about and feel positively toward a brand before they try it and buy it","Because they replace behavioural objectives once sales begin, so the firm can stop tracking purchases","Because the Competition Act requires every campaign to state an awareness target before it runs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Awareness, knowledge, and attitudes set up the later action-oriented outcomes (trial, purchase, repeat purchase), mirroring the hierarchy of effects. They don't replace behavioural objectives; firms typically set both.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-099","concept":"c12-target-audience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"All the points of contact between a customer and a brand (ads, packaging, the store, the website, service calls) are called:","options":["channels of distribution","the promotional schedule","the media mix","customer touch points"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Customer touch points are every place a customer comes into contact with the brand, considered when identifying the target audience. Channels of distribution are the routes a product takes to market, not points of communication contact.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-100","concept":"c12-target-audience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Brightline Optometry maps every place a patient encounters its brand: its Google listing, the booking app, the reminder text, the waiting room, the eyewear bag, and the follow-up survey. Brightline is identifying its:","options":["customer touch points","communication objectives","promotional schedule","pretest results"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Listing every point of contact between customer and brand is mapping customer touch points, which supports step 2 (identify the target audience). A promotional schedule sets the timing and frequency of promotional tools, not points of contact.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-101","concept":"c12-target-audience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why should marketers consider customer touch points when identifying the target audience?","options":["Touch points determine which stage of the product life cycle the product is currently in","Knowing where and how the audience encounters the brand shows which channels and moments can carry a consistent message","Touch points are the inputs needed to calculate a percentage-of-sales promotional budget","Advertising Standards Canada requires a complete touch-point map before any campaign can run"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Understanding the audience's points of contact reveals where messages will actually reach them and where consistency matters, which is central to IMC. The PLC is considered at step 4 and isn't determined by touch points.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-102","concept":"c12-target-audience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"IMC messages can be directed at consumers, intermediaries, or both. Which promotional program has a target audience of INTERMEDIARIES rather than consumers?","options":["A paint maker's Instagram tutorials aimed at do-it-yourself homeowners","A paint maker's TV ads aimed at young families","A paint maker's trade-show booth and dealer incentives aimed at hardware-store owners","A paint maker's in-store coupons aimed at shoppers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hardware-store owners are channel members, so a trade show and dealer incentives target intermediaries. In-store coupons may be redeemed in the store, but they're aimed at shoppers, who are consumers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-103","concept":"c12-target-audience","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a customer touch point for a restaurant chain?","options":["The chain's mobile ordering app","The takeout packaging","A server's greeting at the table","An internal spreadsheet of supplier costs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Touch points are points of contact between the customer and the brand; the app, packaging, and service staff all shape customer impressions. An internal cost spreadsheet is never seen by customers, so it isn't a touch point.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-104","concept":"c12-budget-methods","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Every year, Chinook Hardware sets its promotional budget at 4% of the previous year's sales. Which budgeting method is this?","options":["Competitive parity","Percentage of sales","All-you-can-afford","Objective and task"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Setting the budget as a fixed percentage of past or anticipated sales is the percentage-of-sales method. Competitive parity would base the figure on what rivals spend.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-105","concept":"c12-budget-methods","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"After learning that its main rival spends about $2 million a year on advertising, Lakeshore Mattress sets its own budget at $2 million. Which method is Lakeshore using?","options":["Objective and task","All-you-can-afford","Competitive parity","Percentage of sales"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Matching competitors' spending (or share of market) is competitive parity. Its weakness is that it ignores Lakeshore's own objectives, unlike objective and task.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-106","concept":"c12-budget-methods","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"The owner of a two-location bakery in Red Deer pays rent, wages, and suppliers first; whatever money is left at the end of the month goes to promotion. Which budgeting method is this?","options":["All-you-can-afford","Percentage of sales","Competitive parity","Objective and task"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Funding promotion with money left after other budget items is the all-you-can-afford method, common in small businesses. It has no link to goals or to sales levels.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-107","concept":"c12-budget-methods","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aurora Fitness wants 3,000 new trial memberships. It lists the tasks needed (six weeks of social ads, a sampling event, and a direct-mail drop) and totals their costs to set its budget. Which method is this?","options":["Percentage of sales","Competitive parity","All-you-can-afford","Objective and task"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Objective and task sets objectives, outlines the tasks required to meet them, and totals the cost. It's considered the best approach because spending is tied to what the firm wants to accomplish.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-108","concept":"c12-budget-methods","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Last year Kestrel Outfitters had sales of $3,200,000. If it sets its promotional budget at 3.5% of last year's sales, how much will it budget for promotion?","options":["$11,200","$112,000","$1,120,000","$320,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Percentage of sales: $3,200,000 x 0.035 = $112,000. $1,120,000 comes from multiplying by 0.35 (35%) and $11,200 from 0.0035 (0.35%), both decimal-place errors.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-109","concept":"c12-budget-methods","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"What is the main criticism of the percentage-of-sales budgeting method?","options":["It treats sales as the cause of promotion rather than its result, so spending drops just when sales are weak","It copies competitors' spending levels and so ignores the firm's own objectives for the campaign","It can be used only by small firms that fund promotion from whatever cash is left over","It requires detailed judgment about the cost of every task needed to reach each objective"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Percentage of sales has backwards logic: sales determine promotion, when promotion is supposed to drive sales, so a sales slump automatically cuts the budget. Copying competitors is the flaw of competitive parity, and needing judgment about task costs is objective and task's drawback.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-110","concept":"c12-budget-methods","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about promotional budgeting methods is NOT correct?","options":["Objective and task starts with objectives and costs out the tasks needed to reach them","Competitive parity matches competitors' spending or share of market","Percentage of sales bases the budget on the firm's communication objectives","All-you-can-afford is common among small firms"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Percentage of sales bases the budget on past or anticipated sales, not on objectives; only objective and task starts from objectives. The other three statements are accurate descriptions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-111","concept":"c12-plc-promotion","also":["c12-imc-steps"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"The product life cycle is useful in describing the phases a product moves through from launch to discontinuation, but the PLC also helps marketers in:","options":["choosing the appropriate communication tools at each phase of the cycle","budgeting the marketing spend","meeting product demand","creating the most effective messaging for the product"],"answer":0,"explanation":"All products have a life cycle, and the composition of the promotional mix changes from one phase to the next, which is why the PLC is considered at step 4, designing the promotional program. Budgeting is a separate step (3) using methods such as objective and task.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-112","concept":"c12-plc-promotion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the maturity stage of a popular line of kitchen appliances, the company's marketing objective is most likely to:","options":["Increase consumer awareness of the product category","Persuade consumers to choose their brand over competitors","Maintain existing buyers with reminder advertising and sales promotions","Reduce promotional spending to maximize profits"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In maturity the goal is to maintain existing buyers: advertising reminds buyers of the product and sales promotions help maintain loyalty. Reducing promotional spending describes the decline stage (phase out), and persuading against competitors is the growth-stage objective.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-113","concept":"c12-plc-promotion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Orbit Robotics has just launched Canada's first window-cleaning robot. It sends units to tech magazines for reviews, runs ads explaining what the product does, and has its salesforce call on electronics retailers. What is Orbit's promotional objective at this stage?","options":["To persuade","To inform","To remind","To phase out"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Magazine publicity, explanatory advertising, and salesforce calls on intermediaries are introduction-stage activities, where the objective is to inform. Persuading comes in growth, once competitors arrive.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-114","concept":"c12-plc-promotion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two years later, several rivals sell robot window cleaners. Orbit's ads now stress why its model cleans faster than competing brands, and its reps work hard to keep intermediaries carrying it. What is Orbit's promotional objective now?","options":["To inform","To remind","To phase out","To persuade"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In growth, personal selling to intermediaries and advertising that differentiates the product from competing brands serve the objective to persuade. Reminding is the maturity-stage objective, after the market has settled.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-115","concept":"c12-plc-promotion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Sales of Vista Electronics' DVD players have dwindled, and the company plans to discontinue them soon. It now spends very little money on promoting them. What is its promotional objective?","options":["To phase out","To remind","To persuade","To inform"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Little money spent on promotion is the decline-stage activity, where the objective is to phase out. Reminder advertising belongs to maturity, when the firm is still actively maintaining buyers.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-116","concept":"c12-plc-promotion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to Figure 12-4, which activity is NOT associated with the introduction stage?","options":["Publicity in magazines","Salesforce calling on intermediaries","Sales promotion in the form of free samples","Reminder advertising"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Introduction activities are publicity in magazines, advertising, the salesforce calling on intermediaries, and free samples. Reminder advertising is a maturity-stage activity, when the goal is to remind.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-117","concept":"c12-plc-promotion","also":["c12-sales-promotion"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Figure 12-4 uses sales promotion in two different stages. Which pairing is correct?","options":["Introduction: discounts and coupons; maturity: free samples","Growth: free samples; decline: discounts and coupons","Introduction: free samples; maturity: discounts, coupons, and events","Growth: contests; introduction: rebates"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Free samples help new buyers try a product in introduction; discounts, coupons, and events help keep existing buyers in maturity. Pairing discounts and coupons with introduction and free samples with maturity reverses the two stages.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-118","concept":"c12-scheduling","also":["c12-imc-steps"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Amy is in the midst of deciding on specific timing of TV advertisements and online video placements. She is most likely engaged in:","options":["scheduling the IMC elements","setting the promotional budget","designing the promotional program","evaluating the promotional program"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The promotional schedule describes when each promotional tool is used and for what frequency and duration, so deciding the timing of TV ads and video placements is scheduling (step 5). Designing the program (step 4) decides which tools and messages to use, not when they run.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-119","concept":"c12-scheduling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Banff ski resort advertises heavily from October to February and runs no advertising at all from March to September. Which scheduling approach is this?","options":["Continuous","Pulse","Flighting","Competitive parity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Flighting alternates periods of advertising with periods of no advertising. Pulse would keep a steady base of advertising all year and add bursts at peak times, but the resort goes completely dark.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-120","concept":"c12-scheduling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A national grocery chain runs a steady base of weekly flyers all year, then adds bursts of heavy TV advertising before Thanksgiving, Christmas, and back-to-school. Which scheduling approach is this?","options":["Flighting","Continuous","Objective and task","Pulse"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pulse scheduling combines a continuous base level of promotion with periodic bursts. It isn't flighting, because flighting includes periods with no activity at all.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-121","concept":"c12-scheduling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What is the difference between flighting and pulse schedules?","options":["Flighting keeps a continuous base with periodic bursts; pulse alternates periods of activity with none","Flighting alternates periods of activity with periods of none; pulse keeps a continuous base with periodic bursts","Flighting is used for push strategies; pulse is used for pull strategies","Flighting runs at the same level every week; pulse runs only once a year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In flighting, advertising stops completely between flights; in pulse, there is always a base level of activity, with spikes at key times. Describing flighting as a continuous base with bursts and pulse as on-and-off periods swaps the two definitions.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-122","concept":"c12-scheduling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the marketing communications process, the promotional schedule describes:","options":["the percentage of last year's sales assigned to each promotional tool in the budget","the stages a customer moves through, from first awareness of the brand to advocacy","the order in which each promotional tool is introduced and the frequency and duration of its use","the regulatory approvals that ASC and the CRTC require before a campaign can run"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The promotional schedule sets the order, frequency, and duration of each tool, as part of step 5, scheduling and running the IMC elements. The stages from awareness to advocacy describe the customer advocacy funnel.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-123","concept":"c12-scheduling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Frostline Tire sells winter tires that Canadians buy almost entirely in October and November. Which scheduling approach fits best?","options":["Continuous","Flighting","Pulse","Percentage of sales"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When demand is concentrated in a short season, flighting puts spending into that window and goes dark the rest of the year. Continuous scheduling would waste money advertising steadily when almost no one is buying.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-124","concept":"c12-evaluation","also":["c12-imc-steps"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Following the completion of a marketing campaign, a company reviews metrics such as sales data, customer feedback, and digital engagement to understand the effectiveness of the campaign and areas for improvement. This final step in the marketing communications process is:","options":["specifying the IMC objectives","evaluating the program and recommending changes","designing the promotional program","scheduling and running the IMC elements"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Step 6, evaluating the program and recommending changes, compares results such as sales, feedback, and engagement with the objectives and identifies improvements. Specifying objectives is step 1, which sets the targets this evaluation measures against.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-125","concept":"c12-evaluation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Halcyon Tea shows two versions of a new commercial to a consumer panel to choose which one to air. After the campaign runs, it surveys viewers to measure recall. These two research activities are, respectively:","options":["a posttest and a pretest","a pretest and a pretest","a communication objective and a behavioural objective","a pretest and a posttest"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pretests evaluate promotional elements before they run (choosing between ad versions); posttests evaluate them after (measuring recall). Calling the panel test a posttest and the recall survey a pretest reverses the order.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-126","concept":"c12-evaluation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Halo Fitness tracks click-through rates, conversion rates, and engagement rates on its campaign posts to judge how well the campaign is working. These measures are examples of:","options":["digital engagement metrics used in program evaluation","customer touch points mapped when identifying the audience","barriers to effective communication with the audience","results sought by a push strategy aimed at retailers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Click-throughs, conversions, and engagement rates are digital metrics used in step 6 to evaluate the program. Push results are trade outcomes such as listings and displays, not consumer engagement measures.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-127","concept":"c12-evaluation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which activity is NOT part of the 'evaluate the program and recommend changes' step?","options":["Comparing campaign sales results with the objectives","Running posttests to measure ad recall","Deciding which weeks the TV ads will air","Reviewing customer feedback and engagement rates"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deciding when ads air is scheduling (step 5). Comparing results with objectives, posttesting, and reviewing feedback and engagement all belong to evaluation (step 6).","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-128","concept":"c12-evaluation","also":["c12-objectives"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Cycle's posttest shows strong ad recall, meeting its communication objective, but trial purchases are far below its behavioural objective. What should the team recommend?","options":["Declare the campaign a success, because high ad recall shows the program fully worked","Cut nearly all promotion, since weak trial means the product must be in decline","Switch to the competitive-parity method so the budget simply matches rivals' spending","Revise the program, for example by adding trial-driving tools such as samples or a limited-time offer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Evaluation exists to recommend changes; awareness without action calls for tools that move people to trial, such as sales promotion. Declaring success ignores the unmet behavioural objective, and cutting promotion is a decline-stage response with no evidence of decline.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-129","concept":"c12-inbound-outbound","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Loft & Grain, a Halifax furniture maker, publishes room-design guides, ranks near the top of Google for 'small apartment storage ideas,' and posts how-to videos so buyers find it while searching. This is an example of:","options":["outbound communications","inbound communications","a push strategy","flighting"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inbound communications require excellent content and the ability to be seen in the right places, so customers come to the brand. Outbound would send messages out through ads, cold calls, or direct mail.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-130","concept":"c12-inbound-outbound","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Granite Roofing buys radio spots, cold-calls homeowners, and mails flyers to every house in a northwest Calgary neighbourhood. This approach is:","options":["inbound communications","earned media","outbound communications","storytelling"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Advertising, cold calls, and direct mail sent out to audiences are traditional outbound communications. Inbound relies on content that customers find on their own.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-131","concept":"c12-inbound-outbound","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the course, why can inbound communications be considerably more cost-effective than outbound?","options":["Because interested consumers find the content themselves, so the firm isn't paying to push messages at audiences who may not care","Because inbound content never requires any investment in writing, video, design, or search optimization","Because the CRTC subsidizes Canadian content marketing that appears in search results and on social media","Because inbound is funded by trade allowances that retailers pay manufacturers for featured placement"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inbound requires excellent content and visibility in the right places; when people who are already interested come to you, spending isn't wasted on broad outbound reach. It still requires investment in content (writing, video, search optimization), so the claim that inbound needs no investment is wrong.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-132","concept":"c12-inbound-outbound","also":["c12-push-pull"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A soft-drink brand runs national TV commercials aimed at consumers to build demand. How is this best classified?","options":["Inbound communication supporting a push strategy","Inbound communication supporting a pull strategy","Outbound communication supporting a push strategy","Outbound communication supporting a pull strategy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"TV advertising is traditional outbound communication, and because it targets consumers to create demand it supports a pull strategy. Inbound and pull are not synonyms: inbound describes customers finding the brand, while pull describes who the communication targets.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-133","concept":"c12-inbound-outbound","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of inbound communication?","options":["Search-optimized blog posts answering common customer questions","A telemarketing campaign calling numbers from a purchased list","A how-to YouTube series that people find through search","A free downloadable guide promoted through the brand's social profiles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Telemarketing sends messages out to people who didn't seek them, which is outbound. Search-optimized posts, findable tutorials, and downloadable guides all draw interested customers in.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-134","concept":"c12-paid-owned-earned","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A brand regularly updates its official blog and social media profiles with new content about its products and services. This approach to marketing primarily uses:","options":["Earned media","Owned media","Paid media","Organic media"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Owned media refers to the channels a company controls, at least in part, typically its website, blog, and social media profiles. Earned media would be coverage or reviews created by others, such as journalists and customers.","source":"official"},{"id":"q12-135","concept":"c12-paid-owned-earned","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Willow Bakery's sourdough has collected dozens of five-star Google and Yelp reviews written by customers. In the digital channels framework, these reviews are:","options":["paid media","owned media","earned media","outbound media"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Review websites such as Yelp and Amazon reviews are earned media, generated by others rather than paid for or controlled by the bakery. Owned media would be Willow's own website or social pages.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-136","concept":"c12-paid-owned-earned","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Velo Coffee pays three Toronto lifestyle creators to post about its new cold brew, and separately sends a weekly promotional email to its subscriber list. Using the course's digital channels slide, how should both activities be classified?","options":["Influencer posts: earned; email: owned","Influencer posts: paid; email: paid","Influencer posts: paid; email: owned","Influencer posts: owned; email: earned"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Keller/Swaminathan slide lists both paid bloggers/influencers and e-mail marketing under paid media. The tempting answer calls email owned because the subscriber list belongs to the company, but the course classifies e-mail marketing as paid.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-137","concept":"c12-paid-owned-earned","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which set contains ONLY owned media?","options":["Company website, Yelp reviews, mobile app","Company Facebook page, Facebook ads, company website","Company website, company YouTube channel, mobile app","Mobile app, mobile in-app ads, company blog"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Websites, company-owned social channels such as a YouTube channel, and mobile apps are owned media. Facebook ads and in-app ads are paid, and Yelp reviews are earned, which rules out the other sets.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-138","concept":"c12-paid-owned-earned","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of paid media?","options":["Search advertising","Media coverage of a product launch","Display advertising","Mobile in-app advertising"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Media coverage is earned media, because journalists choose to cover the brand. Search, display, and mobile in-app advertising all appear under paid media on the course slide.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-139","concept":"c12-paid-owned-earned","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Exposure generated by others (such as customer reviews, public relations, and media coverage) that the company neither buys nor controls is called:","options":["owned media","paid media","outbound media","earned media"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Earned media comes from third parties such as customers and journalists. Owned media is controlled by the company (its website, social pages, apps), and paid media is bought.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-140","concept":"c12-paid-owned-earned","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A new Calgary kombucha brand has no budget for advertising but wants a channel it fully controls, where it can tell its story and take orders. What should it build first?","options":["A campaign of Google search ads","A set of paid influencer partnerships","A strategy for collecting Yelp reviews","Its own website and mobile-friendly online store"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A company website is owned media: the firm controls it and doesn't have to buy ad space. Search ads and paid influencers are paid media, and reviews are earned media the brand can't control.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-141","concept":"c12-trends","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A Calgary radio station now streams its morning show live online and releases each segment as a podcast. Which trend in the changing communications landscape does this illustrate?","options":["Connected consumers","Evolving media","Internal marketing","Storytelling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The course's evolving media list includes radio stations streaming content and creating podcasts. Connected consumers refers to consumers' access to fast, affordable Internet services and devices, not to how media outlets change.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-142","concept":"c12-trends","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The trend in which affordable and fast Internet technology provides consumers with easy-to-use services and devices that facilitate marketing communications is called:","options":["evolving media","inbound communications","connected consumers","owned media"],"answer":2,"explanation":"That is the course's definition of connected consumers. Evolving media describes changes in media outlets and formats, such as streaming-only content and digital boards.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-143","concept":"c12-trends","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT listed in the course as an example of evolving media?","options":["Content produced strictly for streaming","Out-of-home advertising being replaced with digital boards","News organizations moving content back to print-only editions","Digital advertising shifting to mobile"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Every evolving-media example moves toward digital: streaming-only content, traditional media building online assets, news on YouTube and apps, radio streaming and podcasts, digital boards, and mobile ads. News organizations actually deliver content through YouTube channels and apps, not by returning to print only.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-144","concept":"c12-trends","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the course, why are traditional media outlets such as TV networks and newspapers creating their own online assets?","options":["To remain competitive and relevant as audiences shift online","To comply with CRTC rules that ban print and broadcast advertising","To reduce the number of connected consumers","To replace the need for measurement companies"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The evolving media slide says traditional media are creating their own online assets to remain competitive and relevant. There is no rule banning print or broadcast; the shift is driven by audience behaviour.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-145","concept":"c12-trends","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which scenario illustrates connected consumers rather than evolving media?","options":["A TV network launches a new drama series produced strictly for streaming on its own app","A national newspaper starts delivering its daily stories through a YouTube channel and a news app","A downtown billboard owner replaces printed posters with a digital screen that rotates several ads","A shopper in a store uses her phone to compare prices, read reviews, and watch a product demo"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Connected consumers is about consumers using affordable, fast Internet services and devices, like a smartphone in a store. The other three describe media outlets and formats changing, which is evolving media.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-146","concept":"c12-storytelling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Instead of listing policy features, Northern Hearth Insurance releases a short film that follows a Fort McMurray family rebuilding their home after a wildfire, with their agent helping at every step. Northern Hearth is using:","options":["product placement","storytelling","publicity","sales promotion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Storytelling builds relationships by conveying events with images and words, engaging people through emotions and experiences. It isn't product placement, because this is the brand's own narrative, not a paid appearance in someone else's entertainment content.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-147","concept":"c12-storytelling","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the course, storytelling builds relationships by conveying events using _____ and words.","options":["statistics","prices","images","coupons"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Storytelling conveys events using images and words, and stories engage people through emotions and experiences. Statistics and prices belong to feature-and-price messaging, which storytelling deliberately moves away from.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-148","concept":"c12-storytelling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the course, why is storytelling an effective marketing communications approach?","options":["Stories pack in more technical specifications and product detail than any other message format","Stories engage people through their emotions and experiences, motivating them to act and to more readily accept an idea","Stories are exempt from the Canadian Code of Advertising Standards, so their claims face no review","Stories guarantee earned media coverage because journalists are expected to report on brand narratives"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The course says stories engage people through emotions and experiences, which motivates them to act and to more readily accept a concept or idea. Storytelling is not exempt from advertising standards; ASC's Code applies to advertising generally.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-149","concept":"c12-storytelling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which campaign uses storytelling rather than conventional feature-based advertising?","options":["A lotion ad that lists its seven active ingredients, its SPF rating, and its dermatologist approval","A banner ad that reads 'Now 30% more moisturizing than our original formula' beside the bottle","A flyer offering 20% off all body care products this weekend only at every store location","A video following a real woman's journey to self-confidence, inviting viewers to rethink beauty"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A narrative about a real person's experience that engages emotions is storytelling, like the course's Dove example. Ingredient lists and claims are feature-based advertising, and a weekend discount is sales promotion.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-150","concept":"c12-storytelling","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Saskatoon animal shelter's flyers listing adoption fees and opening hours aren't increasing adoptions. Applying the course's view of storytelling, what should the shelter do?","options":["Share the story of one rescued dog's journey from intake to a new family, using photos and video","Add more statistics about shelter capacity and intake numbers to the existing flyer","Offer a coupon for half-price adoption fees to anyone who visits this month","Print twice as many flyers and distribute them more often around the city"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Storytelling uses images and words to engage emotions and experiences, motivating action, and one dog's journey does that far better than facts. A fee coupon is sales promotion, which may spark short-term action but isn't storytelling.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-151","concept":"c12-industry-agencies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Prairie Harvest Foods hires TrueNorth Collective to handle its market research, creative development, media planning and buying, and ad production. TrueNorth is a:","options":["creative boutique","full-service agency","in-house agency","measurement company"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A full-service agency offers the complete range of services: research, creative, media, and production. A creative boutique is a limited-service agency that handles only the creative work.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-152","concept":"c12-industry-agencies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Coastline Telecom set up its own internal department that writes, designs, and places all of its advertising instead of hiring an outside firm. This department is a(n):","options":["limited-service agency","full-service agency","media-buying service","in-house agency"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An in-house agency is an advertising department within the advertiser's own company. A full-service agency is an outside firm offering all services to many clients.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-153","concept":"c12-industry-agencies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the course, the areas of the marketing communications industry work together to form an industry that is:","options":["ethical, trustworthy, cohesive, and measurable","low-cost, fast, global, and unregulated","creative, entertaining, viral, and emotional","competitive, independent, self-funded, and confidential"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The slide states that the industry's areas work together to form an industry that is ethical, trustworthy, cohesive, and measurable. Regulators and measurement companies are part of the industry precisely so it is not unregulated or unmeasured.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-154","concept":"c12-industry-agencies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the areas that make up the marketing communications industry?","options":["Advertisers (clients) who pay for campaigns","Measurement companies that track audiences","Distribution intermediaries such as wholesalers","Regulators such as ASC and the CRTC"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The industry consists of advertisers, agencies, media, suppliers, regulators, and measurement companies. Wholesalers are marketing channel intermediaries that move products, not part of the communications industry.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-155","concept":"c12-industry-agencies","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What distinguishes a full-service agency from a limited-service agency?","options":["A full-service agency is a department inside the advertiser's own company; a limited-service agency is an independent outside firm","A full-service agency offers research, creative, media, and production; a limited-service agency specializes in one area, such as creative or media buying","A full-service agency only plans and buys media; a limited-service agency only measures audiences and campaign results","A full-service agency regulates advertising content for the industry; a limited-service agency creates the ads themselves"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Full-service agencies provide the whole package, while limited-service (specialty) agencies such as creative boutiques or media-buying services focus on one function. Working inside the advertiser's company describes an in-house agency.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-156","concept":"c12-regulators","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A consumer thinks a TV ad for a skin cream exaggerates its results and files a complaint under the Canadian Code of Advertising Standards. Which body administers this code?","options":["Competition Bureau","Canadian Marketing Association (CMA)","Advertising Standards Canada (ASC)","CRTC"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ASC is the advertising industry's self-regulatory body and administers the Canadian Code of Advertising Standards, including consumer complaints. The Competition Bureau enforces the federal Competition Act, a law rather than an industry code.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-157","concept":"c12-regulators","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"An Ontario electronics retailer advertises a TV as 'Regular $899, now $399' even though it never sold at $899. Which body enforces the federal law that prohibits this kind of misleading advertising?","options":["Competition Bureau","Advertising Standards Canada (ASC)","Mobile Marketing Association (MMA)","Canadian Marketing Association (CMA)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Competition Bureau enforces the Competition Act, which prohibits misleading advertising and deceptive marketing practices such as inflated 'regular' prices. ASC handles ad complaints under the voluntary industry Code, not federal law.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-158","concept":"c12-regulators","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A company wants to launch a new Canadian specialty TV channel and must obtain a broadcasting licence. Which body on the course's regulator list will it deal with?","options":["Canadian Wireless Telecommunications Association (CWTA)","Advertising Standards Canada (ASC)","Competition Bureau","CRTC"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Canadian Radio-television and Telecommunications Commission (CRTC) regulates broadcasting and telecommunications, and also enforces Canada's anti-spam legislation. The CWTA is an industry association for wireless companies, not the broadcasting regulator.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-159","concept":"c12-regulators","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The Canadian Marketing Association (CMA) is best described as:","options":["the federal agency that enforces the Competition Act","the regulator that licenses broadcasters and enforces anti-spam rules","the national association of marketers, which sets a code of ethics and standards of practice","the self-regulatory body that administers the Canadian Code of Advertising Standards"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The CMA is the marketing profession's association, known for its code of ethics and standards of practice. The self-regulatory body that administers the Code of Advertising Standards is ASC.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-160","concept":"c12-regulators","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"What is the key difference between Advertising Standards Canada (ASC) and the Competition Bureau?","options":["ASC is the industry's self-regulatory body administering the Code of Advertising Standards; the Competition Bureau is a federal agency enforcing the Competition Act","ASC enforces the federal Competition Act; the Competition Bureau administers the industry's Canadian Code of Advertising Standards","ASC regulates broadcasting licences and anti-spam rules; the Competition Bureau represents Canada's wireless carriers","ASC handles only mobile marketing complaints; the Competition Bureau handles only misleading claims in print advertising"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ASC is voluntary industry self-regulation through its Code, while the Competition Bureau enforces federal law against misleading advertising and deceptive marketing. Saying ASC enforces the Competition Act while the Competition Bureau administers the Code swaps their roles.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-161","concept":"c12-regulators","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six regulatory bodies listed on the course slide?","options":["Canadian Wireless Telecommunications Association (CWTA)","Mobile Marketing Association (MMA)","Better Business Bureau (BBB)","Canadian Marketing Association (CMA)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The six bodies are ASC, the Competition Bureau, the CRTC, the CMA, the CWTA, and the MMA. The Better Business Bureau is not on the course's list.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-162","concept":"c12-regulators","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Montreal retailer plans a campaign in which shoppers text a keyword to a five-digit short code to join a VIP list. Which two bodies on the course list focus specifically on the wireless and mobile marketing space?","options":["CRTC and ASC","CMA and the Competition Bureau","ASC and the CMA","CWTA and MMA"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Canadian Wireless Telecommunications Association represents the wireless industry (including guidelines for text-message short codes), and the Mobile Marketing Association sets mobile marketing guidelines. The CRTC regulates broadcasting and telecom broadly, and ASC covers advertising in general.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-163","concept":"c12-internal-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before launching its 'Fresh in 60 seconds' campaign, Prairie Burger trains every crew member on the promise, shows staff the ads before customers see them, and rewards teams that deliver. This is an example of:","options":["a push strategy","internal marketing","personal selling","public relations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Internal marketing treats the company's own employees as an audience so they can deliver the brand promise. A push strategy aims incentives and training at channel members such as independent retailers, not the firm's own crew.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-164","concept":"c12-internal-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A Calgary software company hires someone to shape how current and prospective employees see the firm as a place to work, including running its careers social accounts and employee-story videos. This emerging role is the:","options":["media buyer","product manager","employer brand manager","sales manager"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The course identifies the employer brand manager as an emerging role that manages the organization's image as an employer. A product manager manages a product brand's marketing, not the company's reputation with employees.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-165","concept":"c12-internal-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the course describe internal marketing as a key to competitive advantage?","options":["Because employees deliver the brand experience, so informed, engaged people make the external promise real","Because internal communications to staff are exempt from all Canadian advertising regulation","Because it removes the need to spend money on external IMC programs","Because employees count as earned media in the paid, owned, and earned framework"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The course stresses people as the source of competitive advantage: staff who understand and believe in the brand deliver the experience customers were promised. Internal marketing supports external IMC; it doesn't replace it.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-166","concept":"c12-internal-marketing","also":["c12-push-pull"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Internal marketing and a push strategy can both involve training and incentives. What distinguishes them?","options":["Internal marketing targets channel members such as retailers; push targets the firm's own employees and managers","Internal marketing targets the firm's own employees; push targets channel members such as retailers and wholesalers","Internal marketing is used only in the decline stage; push is used only in the introduction stage of the PLC","Internal marketing is a form of earned media; push is a form of owned media that the firm controls"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The audience decides it: internal marketing is aimed inside the organization at its own people, while push is aimed at intermediaries and their sales staff. Saying internal marketing targets channel members and push targets employees reverses the two audiences.","source":"generated"},{"id":"q12-167","concept":"c12-internal-marketing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the course's points about internal marketing?","options":["Internal marketing is a key to competitive advantage through people","The employer brand manager is an emerging role in organizations","Social media is important for employee engagement and content development","Internal marketing replaces the need for external marketing communications"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The course notes internal marketing's role in competitive advantage, the emerging employer brand manager role, and social media's importance for engagement and content (with the ultimate goal of creating experiences). Nothing says it replaces external communications; it supports them.","source":"generated"}],"color":"#4FA3A5","short":"IMC","emoji":"📯","tag":"Integrated communications","province":"Aegyptus","numeral":"XII"}],"stats":{"questions":1308,"concepts":230,"activities":47},"version":"0137cb42bb"};
